  periods. Choose<nbsp><math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>1> large enough
  for the initial coupling requirements, including
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|224>>, so that
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/128>
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-coordinate>, and
  for<nbsp><math|t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> at every
  level.>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|Choose entry levels and periods to
  satisfy the remaining level and geometric requirements, including the scale
  restrictions<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> and comparison
  intervals<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>. For each incoming
  stage<nbsp><math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>, fix integers<nbsp><math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>,h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>\<geqslant\>1>,
  depending only on the stage data, and require at every step and
  every<nbsp><math|p> that <math|<with|math-display|true|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>><space|0.17em>.>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|This
  bound is met with the existing periods by postponing stage entry. The
  marked construction uses Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs>, with
  weight restrictions<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> and entry
  restrictions<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-entrance>. Impose also the
  support-enlargement and occupancy entry requirements in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-lattice>, and the regular marked weight
  inequalities stated before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs>.
  Delaying entry preserves earlier requirements.>>|<row|<cell|8>|<cell|<math|J<rsub|m>,\<ell\><rsub|m>,D<rsub|m>>,
  <math|a<rsub|m>,L<rsub|p>>, <math|<reflevel|d>>>|<cell|Depend on rows 1--7.
  Set <math|D<rsub|0>=1>, <math|D<rsub|m+1>=\<ell\><rsub|m>*D<rsub|m>>,
  <math|a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>, <math|s<rsub|m>=log D<rsub|m>>, with
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>=\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>> and
  <math|4\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1>,
  <math|J<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>. Require
  <math|0\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>. Choose strictly
  increasing<nbsp><math|L<rsub|p>> with<nbsp><math|4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>> and
  every positive integer dividing infinitely many<nbsp><math|L<rsub|p>>.
  Require <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>>
  and <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|J<rsub|m+1>+3|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>+1|)>>.
  Choose <math|J<rsub|<reflevel|d>>\<geqslant\>d+3>, with
  <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d+1>,
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d> for
  <math|m\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>.>>|<row|<cell|9>|<cell|Derived level
  scales>|<cell|The scales are determined by earlier choices. Set
  <math|<with|math-display|true|<refbeta><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<slope>s<rsub|m>\<gtr\>1,<space|2em><cutoffsize><rsub|m>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|)><space|0.17em>,t<rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2><cutoffsize><rsub|m>,<space|2em>B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>><space|0.17em>,\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>,<space|2em>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>=e<rsup|-<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|1/5>><space|0.17em>,\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=2<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>,<space|2em>\<frak-B\><rsub|m>=<around|[|<refbeta><rsub|m>/2,2<refbeta><rsub|m>|]><space|0.17em>.>>These
  give the field thresholds, activity bounds and coupling bands. The
  quantity<nbsp><math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>> bounds the
  weighted quadratic loop sum in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>.>>|<row|<cell|10>|<cell|Actions,
  classes, recursion, trajectories, couplings;
  width<nbsp><math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>>>|<cell|Depend on rows 1--9. Choose
  the path actions and admissible subsets, the specified recursion and its
  trajectories for every finite<nbsp><math|N,p>, all with the common
  level-zero endpoint (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>).
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fixed-level> gives the fixed-level
  convergence used in the ultraviolet limit;
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:endpoint-tuning> records their analytic
  variation in the endpoint; it is not used
  later.>>|<row|<cell|11>|<cell|Marked transfers, terminal maps,
  insertions>|<cell|Depend on rows 1--10; impose reference normalization
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>). Fix them before any prescribed
  volume sequence.>>|<row|<cell|12>|<cell|<math|<math-ss|p>>, then
  <math|<math-ss|q>,P,S,F<rsub|d>,\<partial\><rsub|a>,\<cal-L\>>>|<cell|Prescribe
  arbitrary increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>> first.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV> and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>
  give the retained volume indices<nbsp><math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>>
  and continuum fields. Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV> retains the full
  inner limit.>>|<row|<cell|13>|<cell|Comparison radii, frames, <math|J>,
  charges, <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>,R,T>>|<cell|Depend on row 12 and the fixed
  lattice family. Use Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>, then
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST> and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>.
  Refine only comparison radii.>>|<row|<cell|14>|<cell|Slab operators,
  <math|<phases>,\<nu\>,S<rsup|\<xi\>>>, then<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>|<cell|Depend
  on earlier data. Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH> gives the phase
  decomposition. Choose<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> from the
  full-measure set with one-dimensional time-axis OS ground space
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>). This choice precedes
  reconstruction.>>|<row|<cell|15>|<cell|Every-phase theories and
  continuations; <math|\<Theta\>,<tracespectrum>>>|<cell|Depend on earlier
  data. Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC> reconstructs the theories.
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16> centers the trace in the already
  selected phase; Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR> gives its short-distance
  law; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> supplies its spectral
  measure.>>|<row|<cell|16>|<cell|OPE prescription; contact projections,
  distributions, bases, scales>|<cell|Depend on rows 12--14 and the fixed
  family. Theorems<nbsp><reference|prop:OP> and<nbsp><reference|prop:CT> use
  the same original fields and correlation table. The contact projections,
  separating functionals and clearance are chosen separately from those used
  for the product coefficients.>>|<row|<cell|17>|<cell|Estimate constants and
  thresholds>|<cell|Depend only on the fixed data preceding them in each
  statement. Their dependence is uniform in positions, volumes and states
  wherever asserted.>>>>>|Choices in dependence order. Stage choices precede
  entry levels at each fixed<nbsp><math|j\<geqslant\>4>.>

  <part|Proofs>

  <section|Local polynomial fields><label|sec:partII-A>

  The polynomial labels of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07> specify
  continuum Taylor coefficients, whereas the Wilson measure averages
  functions of group-valued links. We relate them by local sections:
  polynomials in the real and imaginary parts of finitely many link entries
  that reproduce each label on radial test holonomies through its engineering
  degree. They initialize the renormalized insertions in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, hence the Wilson family of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>, independently of the lattice volume and
  later block ratios.

  We must recover the connection coefficients visible to a label from
  finitely many measurements. Transported plaquette curvatures have
  linearizations given by ordinary square circulations. Vanishing of all
  these circulations forces the highest homogeneous curvature to vanish;
  radial gauge then forces the corresponding potential to vanish. Descending
  in degree proves injectivity. Finite dimensionality reduces this to
  finitely many plaquettes. A local inverse of their joint map, followed by
  Taylor expansion, gives the required polynomials. Averaging over signed
  permutations imposes covariance without changing the Taylor coefficients.

  Each curvature factor also contributes one small-field threshold factor to
  the bounds in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-bounds>. For the equation
  projections in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4> constructs auxiliary sections with exact
  exterior zero moments. Their labels undergo a fixed invertible triangular
  change. We use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D02>,
  <reference|def:D05a> and<nbsp><reference|def:D07>, the
  chord<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-price-chord>, and the order of choices in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>; no earlier Part<nbsp>II result is
  used.

  <paragraph|Labels and test holonomies.>

  Write <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j>> for the real space of homogeneous radial
  potentials of degree<nbsp><math|j+1> in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>. Every label in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>>
  is a polynomial on the finite-dimensional space

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>=<big|oplus><rsub|j+2\<leqslant\>d>\<cal-J\><rsub|j><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  A coordinate of<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|j>> has weight<nbsp><math|j+2>,
  so only finitely many monomials have weight at most<nbsp><math|d>. Adjoint
  action and conjugation preserve both gradings. Real and imaginary parts
  span each invariant component over<nbsp><math|<C>>, giving bihomogeneous
  bases as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>. Conjugation in this real
  basis is coefficientwise and agrees with conjugation of polynomial values
  on real jets. Write <math|k<rsub|i>> for the number of jet-coordinate
  factors in<nbsp><math|b<rsub|d,i>>. We identify a coefficient vector with
  its polynomial:

  <\equation*>
    \<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*b<rsub|d,i><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|d,0>=\<b-1\><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  We use the test holonomies<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-test-holonomy>, on the
  lattice<nbsp><math|<Z><rsup|4>> covering the torus. With
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>=<big|sum><rsub|j+2\<leqslant\>d>\<cal-A\><rsub|j>>,
  their integral equation is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:A-holonomy>U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)><around|(|x,a|)>=u<around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>u<around|(|\<tau\>|)>=1+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<tau\>>u<around|(|\<eta\>|)>*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*\<cal-A\><rsub|a><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*<around|(|x+\<eta\>*e<rsub|a>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<eta\><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The defining linear matrix equation has a unique global solution for
  continuous coefficients<nbsp><cite-detail|Teschl2012|Theorem<nbsp>3.9>.
  Piecewise constant Lie-algebra coefficients give products of exponentials
  in<nbsp><math|G>; uniform convergence on compact intervals, by Gronwall's
  inequality<nbsp><cite-detail|Teschl2012|Lemma<nbsp>2.7>, and closedness
  of<nbsp><math|G> put the solution in<nbsp><math|G>. Homogeneity and
  uniqueness give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:A-dilation>U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>=U*<around|(|1,<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>=<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|j+2>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j>|)><rsub|j>,<space|2em>U*<around|(|0,<math-ss|j>|)>=1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  For a cover plaquette<nbsp><math|\<square\>> based at<nbsp><math|z> and a
  path<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>> from<nbsp><math|0> to<nbsp><math|z>, write
  <math|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>> for the ordered product of successive oriented
  links, read from the initial point to the endpoint. Define its transported
  curvature by

  <\equation*>
    \<sigma\><rsub|\<gamma\>,\<square\>><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>*F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><frac|U<rsub|\<square\>>-U<rsub|\<square\>><rsup|-1>|2><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The projection is orthogonal for the normalized Frobenius metric of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>. These measurements transform by the
  adjoint action at<nbsp><math|0>. They are real polynomial functions of the
  real and imaginary parts of link entries, since
  <math|U<rsub|e><rsup|-1>=U<rsub|e><rsup|\<ast\>>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Local sections><label|def:D19> For a finite site stencil
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>\<subset\><Z><rsup|4>>, a local section has the
    form

    <\equation*>
      <localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>,\<mu\>>p<rsub|\<nu\>*\<mu\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|\<b-up-c\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|e,\<alpha\>,\<delta\>>U<rsub|e>*<around|(|\<alpha\>,\<delta\>|)><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|e*\<alpha\>*\<delta\>>><wide|U<rsub|e>*<around|(|\<alpha\>,\<delta\>|)>|\<bar\>><rsup|\<mu\><rsub|e*\<alpha\>*\<delta\>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The product uses positive links with both endpoints
    in<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and matrix indices
    <math|1\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>,\<delta\>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|G>>. The sum is
    finite over nonnegative multi-indices; each coefficient is complex linear
    in<nbsp><math|\<b-up-c\>>. The construction below chooses such a map to
    represent each continuum label.
  </definition>

  For a torus of side<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>> (the
  side<nbsp><math|s> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D02>), write
  <math|<around|[|x|]>> for reduction of <math|x\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>
  modulo<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>>. Placement at a torus
  site<nbsp><math|y> means evaluation on the periodic extension
  <math|U<rsup|<around|[|y|]>>*<around|(|x,a|)>=U*<around|(|y+<around|[|x|]>,a|)>>;
  denote it by <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|y;U|)>>.
  On the cover, <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|0;U|)>=<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>>.
  For <math|\<rho\>\<in\><hypercubic>>, define the configuration and jet
  actions by

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><around|(|x,a|)>>|<cell|=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|U*<around|(|\<rho\>*x,b|)>,>|<cell|\<rho\>*e<rsub|a>=e<rsub|b>,>>|<row|<cell|U*<around|(|\<rho\>*x-e<rsub|b>,b|)><rsup|-1>,>|<cell|\<rho\>*e<rsub|a>=-e<rsub|b>,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<rho\><math-ss|j>|)><rsub|j,a><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|b>\<rho\><rsub|a*b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,b>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|-1>*z|)>,<space|2em><around|(|\<rho\>*\<b-up-c\>|)><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=\<b-up-c\><around|(|\<rho\><rsup|-1><math-ss|j>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  This action preserves homogeneity and radial gauge. Reality and covariance
  mean

  <\equation*>
    <localsection|d><around|[|<wide|\<b-up-c\>|\<bar\>>|]><around|(|U|)>=<wide|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>|\<bar\>>,<space|2em><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>=<localsection|d><around|[|\<rho\>*\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<rho\>\<in\><hypercubic>|)>,
  </equation*>

  with conjugation as fixed above.

  The following result supplies the sections specified in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>. The unit normalization is
  exact at every configuration; the Taylor assertion concerns the test
  configurations near zero spacing.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Taylor-normalized sections><label|lem:A1> For every<nbsp><math|d>,
    a box stencil <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>=<around|[|-\<varrho\><rsub|d>,\<varrho\><rsub|d>|]><rsup|4>\<cap\><Z><rsup|4>>
    and sections can be chosen gauge invariant, real and <math|<hypercubic>>
    covariant, with <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-1\>|]>=1>, so that,
    for every <math|\<b-up-c\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, radial
    jet<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>> and <math|0\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>d>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-Taylor><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>*<around|(|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>>*\<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  Fix the sections constructed in the following proof.

  <\proof>
    We use the metric and lattice transports of
    Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>\U<reference|def:D02> and the labels
    of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>. If <math|d\<less\>2>, the labels
    are constant and their constant sections suffice. Fix
    <math|d\<geqslant\>2>.

    <em|Step 1: Curvatures determine the truncated jet.>

    Use the <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant coefficient norm
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> obtained from the metric of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>. The Volterra term of
    order<nbsp><math|l> is bounded by <math|<around|(|C<rsub|x,a,d><around|\||<math-ss|j>|\|>|)><rsup|l>/l>!,
    giving locally uniform convergence with all parameter derivatives and
    smooth measurements. The first variation of a link solution is its edge
    integral; that of a plaquette measurement is their circulation. Transport
    along<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>> contributes nothing at first order, because
    <math|F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|1|)>=0>.

    Suppose all these linearized measurements vanish on
    <math|\<cal-A\>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<cal-A\><rsub|j>>.
    For <math|a\<less\>b> set

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|x,\<tau\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><space|-0.17em><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,a>|)>*<around|(|x+\<tau\>*<around|(|\<xi\>*e<rsub|a>+\<eta\>*e<rsub|b>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<xi\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<eta\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The fundamental theorem of calculus on each edge identifies the
    unit-square circulation with <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>>N<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|x,1|)>>.
    It is zero for every <math|x\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>. Homogeneity gives
    <math|N<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|\<tau\>*x,\<tau\>|)>=\<tau\><rsup|j>*N<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|x,1|)>>
    and hence

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<tau\><rsup|j<rsub|\<ast\>>-j>*N<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|\<tau\>*x,\<tau\>|)>=0*<space|2em><around|(|x\<in\><Z><rsup|4>,\<tau\>\<gtr\>0|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|z\<in\><R><rsup|4>>, set <math|x<rsub|\<tau\>>=<around|(|<around|\<lfloor\>|z<rsup|a>/\<tau\>|\<rfloor\>>|)><rsub|a>>,
    so <math|<around|\||\<tau\>*x<rsub|\<tau\>>-z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>\<tau\>>.
    Continuity as <math|\<tau\>*\<downarrow\>*0> leaves only the term
    <math|j=j<rsub|\<ast\>>>, giving <math|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,a>|)><around|(|z|)>=0>.
    Antisymmetry covers the other ordered pairs. Differentiating the radial
    condition and using Euler's identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|j<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>z<rsup|a>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,a>|)><around|(|z|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Remove this component and descend in<nbsp><math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The
    common kernel is zero. Adding a measurement whenever the kernel is
    nonzero decreases its dimension, so finitely many measurements
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<sigma\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>>
    give a joint map

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|S>:\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>\<longrightarrow\><g><rsup|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>,<space|2em><math-ss|S><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|l>*<around|(|U*<around|(|1,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>|)><rsub|l=1><rsup|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>
    </equation*>

    with injective derivative<nbsp><math|D<math-ss|S><around|(|0|)>>.

    <em|Step 2: A polynomial inverse to the required order.> Uniqueness of
    the link equation shows that a constant gauge transformation takes the
    test links of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>> to those
    of<nbsp><math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)><math-ss|j>>.
    Thus<nbsp><math|<math-ss|S>> and its derivative at zero are adjoint
    equivariant. The range of <math|D<math-ss|S><around|(|0|)>> and its
    orthogonal complement in<nbsp><math|<g><rsup|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>> are
    adjoint invariant. Compose its inverse on the range with orthogonal
    projection to obtain <math|C:<g><rsup|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>\<to\>\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>.
    Then

    <\equation*>
      C*D<math-ss|S><around|(|0|)>=I,<space|2em>C<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)>*C*<space|1em><around|(|v\<in\>G|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply the inverse function theorem<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorem<nbsp>9.24>
    to<nbsp><math|C<math-ss|S>> on this finite-dimensional real space: it is
    smooth near zero, sends zero to zero and has derivative<nbsp><math|I>.
    Its local inverse<nbsp><math|\<varphi\>> is smooth, by differentiating
    <math|D*\<varphi\>=<around|(|D<around|(|C<math-ss|S>|)>\<circ\>\<varphi\>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Choose an invariant source ball on which <math|C<math-ss|S>> is
    injective, and then an invariant target ball whose inverse image lies in
    that source ball. Both exist because the adjoint actions are isometric.
    Put <math|f=\<varphi\>\<circ\>C>. Equivariance of <math|C<math-ss|S>> and
    injectivity on the source ball give, near zero,

    <\equation*>
      f<math-ss|S><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=<math-ss|j>,<space|2em>f<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em>f*<around|(|<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)>*z|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)>*f<around|(|z|)>*<space|1em><around|(|v\<in\>G|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently the Taylor polynomial

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|d><frac|1|k!>*D<rsup|k>*f<around|(|0|)><around|[|z,\<ldots\>,z|]>
    </equation*>

    is adjoint equivariant and has no constant term. Define

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=b<rsub|d,i>*<around|(|T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|z|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<Psi\><rsub|\<b-up-c\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|1><around|(|U|)>,\<ldots\>,\<sigma\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>><around|(|U|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The polynomials<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>> are invariant and real on real
    arguments, and <math|Q<rsub|0>=1>. If <math|b<rsub|d,i>> has factor
    count<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>>, every monomial of<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>> has
    at least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>> factors.

    Taylor's formula gives <math|T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|<math-ss|S><around|(|X|)>|)>=X+O<around|(|<around|\||X|\|><rsup|d+1>|)>>
    near<nbsp><math|0>. Substitute <math|X=<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>>
    truncated to<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>, and
    use<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-dilation>. Both sides are smooth
    in<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>>, and

    <\equation*>
      \<Psi\><rsub|\<b-up-c\>>*<around|(|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>=\<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>+O<rsub|\<b-up-c\>,<math-ss|j>><around|(|<around|\||h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>|\|><rsup|d+1>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Their derivatives through order<nbsp><math|d> at zero agree. The
    polynomial<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>> itself need not have degree at
    most<nbsp><math|d> in the curvature measurements.

    <em|Step 3: Covariance and a common stencil.> Average the section by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-average><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><hypercubic>>\<Psi\><rsub|\<rho\><rsup|-1>*\<b-up-c\>>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Changing the summation variable to <math|\<omega\>*\<rho\>> proves
    <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>*<around|(|\<omega\><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>=<localsection|d><around|[|\<omega\>*\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>>.
    Simultaneous adjoint covariance of the measurements gives gauge
    invariance. The real basis and signed-permutation action give reality;
    the constant column remains one. The action preserves factor count, so
    averaging retains the lower bound on the number of curvature factors.

    For a positive image direction, the pulled-back link equation has
    coefficient

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*<big|sum><rsub|b>\<rho\><rsub|b*a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|b><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*\<rho\>*<around|(|x+\<tau\>*e<rsub|a>|)>|)>,
    </equation*>

    the coefficient for<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|-1><math-ss|j>>. Reversing
    the parameter and inverting the transport covers a negative image
    direction. Uniqueness gives <math|\<rho\><rsup|\<ast\>>*U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>=U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,\<rho\><rsup|-1><math-ss|j>|)>>.
    Each term of<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-average> therefore has Taylor
    polynomial<nbsp><math|\<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>>
    through order<nbsp><math|d>. A signed image of a transported curvature is
    again a transported curvature, up to sign: reversal changes
    <math|F<rsub|\<square\>>> to <math|-F<rsub|\<square\>>>, and a change of
    base point gives conjugation by the intervening boundary transport.
    Enclose the finitely many resulting paths and plaquettes in a box
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>=<around|[|-\<varrho\><rsub|d>,\<varrho\><rsub|d>|]><rsup|4>\<cap\><Z><rsup|4>>.
    This stencil contains zero and is signed-permutation invariant. Expanding
    the finite polynomial in matrix entries gives
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D19>. This
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-section-Taylor>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Bounds and lattice symmetries.>

  We bound normalized initial insertions using the ordered left derivatives
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. At a placement<nbsp><math|y>, set
  <math|Y=y+<around|[|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|]>>; being healthy on<nbsp><math|Y>
  means that every plaquette meeting<nbsp><math|Y> has chord at
  most<nbsp><math|t>. A direction is permitted when

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsup|->,y|)>|)><rsup|\<varpi\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<around|[|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|]>> is the torus image of the
  stencil, and <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>=min<rsub|z\<in\>n<rsub|<math-up|tor>><Z><rsup|4>><around|\||<wide|x|~>-<wide|y|~>+z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>
  is the periodic maximum-coordinate distance, for any lifts
  <math|<wide|x|~>,<wide|y|~>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Section bounds><label|lem:A-bounds> Each basis column is a
    polynomial in transported curvatures with at least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>>
    factors per monomial. For all <math|d,k,\<varpi\>\<in\><N>> there are
    finite constants <math|C<rsub|d,k,\<varpi\>>> and <math|C<rsub|d>> such
    that, on every torus, for every placement<nbsp><math|y>,
    <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>, configuration healthy on<nbsp><math|Y>,
    and permitted direction list of length <math|q\<leqslant\>k>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-section-bound><around*|\||D<rsup|L><rsub|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*D<rsup|L><rsub|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|q>><localsection|d><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]><around|(|y;U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,k,\<varpi\>>*t<rsup|k<rsub|i>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For arbitrary configurations,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-global-bound>sup<rsub|i,y,U><around|\||<localsection|d><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]><around|(|y;U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  The proof uses the curvature polynomials constructed in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>.

  <\proof>
    First note that <math|<around|\||F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>>:
    orthogonal projection is contractive and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|\<square\>>-U<rsub|\<square\>><rsup|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|\<square\>>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>>.
    Adjoint transport is isometric, so an undifferentiated curvature
    measurement is bounded by<nbsp><math|t>. An order-<math|q> left
    derivative of a fixed path product is a finite sum of products of unitary
    matrices and exactly<nbsp><math|q> direction matrices. On the fixed
    stencil their norms are at most a constant times<nbsp><math|t>, since
    periodic distance is bounded by cover distance. Thus each differentiated
    measurement is <math|O<around|(|t<rsup|q>|)>>, and hence
    <math|O<around|(|t|)>> when <math|q\<geqslant\>1>. This argument also
    applies when cover links coincide after placement: one first
    differentiates the cover expression and then assigns the same direction
    to coincident links.

    Apply the product rule to the curvature polynomials
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-average>. Every differentiated monomial still has at
    least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>> factors, each bounded by a constant
    times<nbsp><math|t>. There are finitely many terms; since
    <math|t\<leqslant\>1>, their sum gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-section-bound>,
    including order zero. Each entry of a unitary matrix has absolute value
    at most one, so the finite matrix-entry polynomial
    gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-global-bound>. The formula also gives smoothness,
    measurability and locality. The constants depend on the sections,
    <math|G,d,k,\<varpi\>> as applicable, and finite maxima make them
    nondecreasing in the integer parameters. They are fixed before the stage
    block ratios in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>.
  </proof>

  By linearity, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-bounds> bounds permitted
  derivatives of <math|<localsection|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsup|-1>>\<b-up-c\>|]>>
  by <math|C<rsub|d,k,\<varpi\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>>
  on the healthy set: the factor <math|t<rsup|-k<rsub|i>>> cancels
  <math|t<rsup|k<rsub|i>>>. Taking <math|t=t<rsub|N>>,
  <math|k=\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>> and
  <math|\<varpi\>=\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>> bounds the initial marked
  density of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-evolution> on the healthy set, in the
  sense of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. On unhealthy configurations
  the global bound gives <math|C<rsub|d>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>*t<rsup|-k<rsub|i>>>;
  the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> places this contribution in a
  merged forced remainder of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation>.

  <paragraph|Finite-lattice invariance.> For the identities deferred from
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D02>, telescoping the path products shows
  that gauge transformations act on plaquette holonomies by conjugation. An
  affine isometry <math|h<rsub|<math-up|iso>><around|(|x|)>=\<rho\>*x+b>,
  with <math|\<rho\>\<in\><hypercubic>>, changes an image plaquette holonomy
  by conjugation, possibly composed with inversion. Both operations
  preserve<nbsp><math|<wilsonterm>>, hence the Wilson energy. Fixed left and
  right multiplication, inversion and permutation preserve product
  probability Haar measure<nbsp><cite-detail|Folland2015|Corollary<nbsp>2.28
  and Proposition<nbsp>2.31>. Consequently, for every integer side
  <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>1>, real<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, site
  gauge transformation<nbsp><math|\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>>> and bounded
  measurable<nbsp><math|F>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:A-Wilson-invariance><big|int>F<around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>>>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|iso>><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  All integrals exist: <math|0\<leqslant\><wilsonterm>\<leqslant\>2> gives
  <math|0\<leqslant\>E<rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>><rsup|<math-up|W>>\<leqslant\>12*n<rsub|<math-up|tor>><rsup|4>>
  and <math|0\<less\>Z<rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  The terminal transfer normalization in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>
  is proved in Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>\U<reference|lem:E2.1a>.

  <paragraph|Auxiliary equation sections.>

  Exact lattice equation identities require different sections, still
  polynomial and local with the same small-field bounds.
  On<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j>> define the Maxwell subspace and its
  polynomial ideal by

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|j>=<around*|{|\<cal-A\><rsub|j>:<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,a>|)>=0<text|for
    every >b|}>,<space|2em>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>=<around|{|p\<in\>V<rsub|d>:p|\|><rsub|<big|prod><rsub|j>K<rsub|j>>=0}<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here restriction uses only <math|j+2\<leqslant\>d>. Substitution into
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|j>K<rsub|j>> preserves bidegree, so
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> is the direct sum of its bihomogeneous parts.
  Order bidegrees<nbsp><math|<around|(|w,k|)>> by the lexicographic order
  of<nbsp><math|<around|(|k,w|)>>. A regrading is a complex-linear map whose
  difference from the identity sends each bihomogeneous polynomial into
  strictly later bidegrees. Such a map preserves the filtration by minimum
  factor count. For a torus site<nbsp><math|y>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<Omega\><rsub|r><around|(|y|)>=<around|{|x:<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>\<gtr\>r|}><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  An exterior spectator is a bounded measurable function of the positive
  links having both endpoints in<nbsp><math|\<Omega\><rsub|r><around|(|y|)>>;
  it need not be gauge invariant.

  The following identity supplies the auxiliary equation observables used in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>. Its proof constructs them from
  derivatives of the Wilson energy with multipliers independent of the varied
  link.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Auxiliary equation sections><label|lem:A-R4> For
    every<nbsp><math|d> there are a local
    section<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>>> as in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D19>, on its own box stencil, gauge
    invariant, real and <math|<hypercubic>> covariant, with
    <math|V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|\<b-1\>|]>=1>,
    satisfying<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-Taylor> and, with its own constants,
    <eqref|eq:A-section-bound>\U<eqref|eq:A-global-bound>, an invertible
    regrading<nbsp><math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>> fixing<nbsp><math|\<b-1\>> and
    commuting with conjugation and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, and
    <math|r<rsub|d>,M<rsub|d>\<in\><N>> such that every
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>M<rsub|d>>,
    <math|\<beta\>\<ne\>0>, placement<nbsp><math|y>,
    <math|p\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> and exterior spectator<nbsp><math|S>
    on<nbsp><math|\<Omega\><rsub|r<rsub|d>><around|(|y|)>> satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-aux-zero><big|int>V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>p|]><around|(|y;U|)>*S<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  The exact-equation subspace for these sections is
  <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>|)>>. Its comparison
  with the selected primary family is deferred to
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>. All choices in the following proof
  depend only on<nbsp><math|G,d> and the label basis.

  <\proof>
    We use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D02>,
    <reference|def:D07> and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>\U<reference|lem:A-bounds>.

    <em|Step 1: One-link equations.> Extend the curvature
    <math|F<rsub|\<square\>>> defined above to
    <math|F<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U;x|)>> for ordered axes by antisymmetry,
    with <math|F<rsub|a*a>=0>. For a positive link <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>>
    set

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|e><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b\<ne\>a><around*|(|F<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U;x|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|U*<around|(|x-e<rsub|b>,b|)><rsup|-1>|)>*F<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U;x-e<rsub|b>|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    On a torus of side at least two, direct differentiation gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-link-equation>D<rsup|L><rsub|e,A>*E<rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>=<around|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-E\><rsub|e><around|(|U|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Indeed, the forward plaquette contributes
    <math|-n<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><around|(|A*U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|A,F<rsub|\<square\>>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>>.
    The backward plaquette, rebased at<nbsp><math|x>, contributes the
    negative of the transported curvature in the display. These are the two
    incidences for each transverse axis; antisymmetry gives the signs for the
    opposite ordering.

    If a continuous multiplier<nbsp><math|g> is independent
    of<nbsp><math|U<rsub|e>>, and a bounded measurable<nbsp><math|S> is also
    independent of<nbsp><math|U<rsub|e>>, left invariance of Haar measure
    gives

    <\equation*>
      0=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*\<epsilon\>>*<big|int>g<around|(|U|)>*S<around|(|U|)>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U<rsup|e,A,\<epsilon\>>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*U|\|><rsub|\<epsilon\>=0>=-\<beta\>*Z<rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>>*<big|int><around|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-E\><rsub|e>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>*g*S*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|U<rsup|e,A,\<epsilon\>><rsub|e>=e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*A>*U<rsub|e>>
    and all other links are fixed. Compactness bounds the derivative of the
    smooth density uniformly, justifying differentiation even for
    measurable<nbsp><math|S>.

    Choose a path<nbsp><math|\<gamma\><rsub|e>> from<nbsp><math|0>
    to<nbsp><math|e<rsup|->> avoiding<nbsp><math|e>. If<nbsp><math|g> also
    avoids<nbsp><math|e>, then

    <\equation*>
      \<Theta\><around|(|U|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<xi\>,<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|e>>|)>*\<cal-E\><rsub|e><around|(|U|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>*g<around|(|U|)>
    </equation*>

    has zero moment for every <math|\<xi\>\<in\><g>> against every exterior
    spectator. To see this, expand <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|e>><rsup|-1>|)>*\<xi\>>
    in a fixed orthonormal basis of<nbsp><math|<g>> and apply the preceding
    identity to each coefficient times<nbsp><math|g*S>. These multipliers are
    independent of<nbsp><math|U<rsub|e>>. There is one radius and one minimum
    torus side for any finite collection of such expressions: enlarge the
    radius to contain both endpoints of every varied link, and the side until
    distinct links in their finite cover supports remain distinct after
    wrapping. The same choices work at every placement.

    <em|Step 2: Enough measurements with avoidance.> Any path can avoid one
    specified link: replace a traversal of that link by three sides of a
    square in a transverse direction. For each plaquette orientation only
    finitely many base points give a plaquette containing that link. Removing
    these points does not affect the shrinking-square argument in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>: for <math|z\<ne\>0>, the integer points
    <math|x<rsub|\<tau\>>=<around|\<lfloor\>|z/\<tau\>|\<rfloor\>>>
    eventually avoid any finite set, and continuity gives the conclusion
    at<nbsp><math|z=0>. Thus curvature measurements whose paths and
    plaquettes avoid<nbsp><math|e> still have injective joint linearization.
    Finite-dimensional duality therefore realizes every linear functional
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> by finitely many components.

    For every linear functional<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j>> vanishing on<nbsp><math|K<rsub|j>>, a
    finite combination of force linearizations equals
    <math|\<ell\>\<circ\><math-up|pr><rsub|j>> on
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j+2>=<big|oplus><rsub|i\<leqslant\>j>\<cal-J\><rsub|i>>.
    Write <math|N<rsub|j;a*b>> for the square averages in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>, extended antisymmetrically. The force
    linearization at<nbsp><math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>> is

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|a>*<around|(|<math-ss|j>;x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i,b><around*|(|N<rsub|i;a*b>*<around|(|x,1|)>-N<rsub|i;a*b>*<around|(|x-e<rsub|b>,1|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For a homogeneous component define

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|z,\<tau\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|<around|[|0,1|]><rsup|3>>\<partial\><rsub|b>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,a>|)>*<around*|(|z+\<tau\>*<around|(|\<xi\>*e<rsub|a>+<around|(|\<eta\>+\<zeta\>-1|)>*e<rsub|b>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<xi\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<eta\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<zeta\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The fundamental theorem of calculus in the backward direction gives

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|z,\<tau\>|)>-N<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|z-\<tau\>*e<rsub|b>,\<tau\>|)>=\<tau\>*H<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|z,\<tau\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    If all <math|L<rsub|a>*<around|(|<math-ss|j>;x|)>> vanish for jets
    through degree<nbsp><math|j=j<rsub|\<ast\>>>, homogeneity gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>\<tau\><rsup|j<rsub|\<ast\>>-j>*H<rsub|j;a*b>*<around|(|\<tau\>*x,\<tau\>|)>=0>
    for <math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>. Taking <math|x=x<rsub|\<tau\>>> and
    <math|\<tau\>*\<downarrow\>*0> shows <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>\<partial\><rsub|b>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,a>|)>=0>,
    hence <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<in\>K<rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
    A functional of this top component annihilating<nbsp><math|K<rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
    therefore annihilates the common kernel of the force measurements.
    Finite-dimensional duality puts it in their linear span. Transport paths
    do not enter the linearization and can be chosen to avoid their varied
    links. On<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> the same combination
    can have additional linear terms only at engineering weights
    above<nbsp><math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>+2>.

    <em|Step 3: Polynomial lifts and the triangular map.> Choose linear
    projections <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>:\<cal-J\><rsub|j>\<to\>K<rsub|j>> and
    put <math|\<pi\>=<big|prod><rsub|j>\<pi\><rsub|j>>. A polynomial belongs
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> precisely when <math|p\<circ\>\<pi\>=0>.
    For a product of linear coordinates,

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>>\<ell\><rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<circ\>\<pi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|l\<less\>i><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|l>\<circ\>\<pi\>|)>|]>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>-\<ell\><rsub|i>\<circ\>\<pi\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l\<gtr\>i>\<ell\><rsub|l><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently every bihomogeneous equation label is a finite sum of
    products with one linear factor vanishing on some<nbsp><math|K<rsub|j>>,
    with the same engineering weight and factor count. The individual factors
    need not be invariant. For an observable<nbsp><math|O>, its Taylor
    polynomial through engineering weight<nbsp><math|d> means the
    degree-<math|d> Taylor polynomial at zero of
    <math|<math-ss|j>\<mapsto\>O<around|(|U*<around|(|1,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>>
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>, with monomials of weight
    above<nbsp><math|d> discarded. Every coordinate has weight at least two.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-dilation>, matching this polynomial is equivalent to
    matching the spacing derivatives through order<nbsp><math|d>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-Taylor>.

    Lift that distinguished factor by a finite combination of transported
    forces from Step<nbsp>2. For each summand, lift the other factors by
    curvature measurements avoiding its varied link, matching their full
    linear parts through engineering weight<nbsp><math|d>. The resulting
    expression has the zero-moment property of Step<nbsp>1 and at
    least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>> curvature factors. Indeed, every
    force is a difference of two transported curvatures per transverse axis.
    Its Taylor polynomial through engineering weight<nbsp><math|d> equals the
    prescribed product plus terms later in<nbsp><math|<around|(|k,w|)>>: the
    additional linear terms of the force have greater engineering weight, and
    every nonlinear correction increases factor count. The square-measurement
    lifts have their prescribed full linear parts, so introduce only the
    latter kind of correction. Taylor polynomials exist by the smooth
    Volterra dependence used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>.

    Average over constant gauge transformations. All factors depend on a
    gauge transformation only at the origin, where they transform by adjoint
    matrices. Their orbit therefore lies in the finite-dimensional span of
    finitely many fixed-degree colour-coordinate monomials on the same
    measurements. Each orbit function retains zero moments, with colour
    vectors rotated by <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|v<rsup|-1>|)>>. The Haar
    average is a finite linear combination of orbit functions, so preserves
    zero moments, minimum factor count and triangular Taylor identity, and is
    gauge invariant. The test-holonomy identity makes its Taylor polynomial
    invariant too.

    In each bidegree extend a basis of<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> to a
    basis of the invariant label space in that bidegree, lifting the
    complementary vectors by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>.
    Lift<nbsp><math|\<b-1\>> to<nbsp><math|1>, since <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>
    contains no nonzero constant. Summing gives a linear
    lift<nbsp><math|p\<mapsto\>\<cal-Y\><rsub|p>>. Replace this lift by the
    two successive averages

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-Y\><rprime|'><rsub|p><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<frac|1|<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<rho\>\<in\><hypercubic>>\<cal-Y\><rsub|\<rho\><rsup|-1>*p>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Y\><rprime|''><rsub|p>>|<cell|=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|\<cal-Y\><rprime|'><rsub|p>+<wide|\<cal-Y\><rprime|'><rsub|<wide|p|\<bar\>>>|\<bar\>>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The Taylor map commutes with both operations;
    write<nbsp><math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|p>> for the final lift. The real Maxwell
    equations and the chain rule make <math|K<rsub|j>>, and
    hence<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, stable under signed permutations,
    adjoint transformations and conjugation. The zero-moment property
    survives the signed average by<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-Wilson-invariance>,
    changing the spectator by the inverse isometry; the exterior set is
    preserved. Conjugation uses<nbsp><math|<wide|S|\<bar\>>>. A reversed
    varied link uses a right derivative of the Wilson energy, whose Haar
    identity is identical. Thus the averaged equation representatives remain
    finite sums of one-link action derivatives with link-independent
    multipliers and avoiding transport paths.

    Let<nbsp><math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>p> be the Taylor polynomial of the
    resulting<nbsp><math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|p>> through engineering
    weight<nbsp><math|d>. Then <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>-I> strictly
    raises<nbsp><math|<around|(|k,w|)>>. There are finitely many bidegrees of
    weight at most<nbsp><math|d>, so <math|<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>-I|)><rsup|h<rsub|0>>=0>
    for some integer<nbsp><math|h<rsub|0>>, and

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|R><rsub|d><rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|l=0><rsup|h<rsub|0>-1><around|(|-1|)><rsup|l>*<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>-I|)><rsup|l>,<space|2em>V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|c|]>=\<cal-Y\><rsub|<math-ss|R><rsub|d><rsup|-1>c><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This gives invertibility and Taylor normalization; the averages give the
    symmetry and unit identities. The inverse preserves minimum factor count,
    so Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-bounds> applies to these sections with
    their own constants. A signed-invariant box enclosing all paths and
    plaquettes gives a common stencil. Take <math|r<rsub|d>,M<rsub|d>> as
    maxima for an ideal basis; linearity gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-aux-zero>.
  </proof>

  <section|Gauges and block integration><label|sec:partII-B>

  The block map of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> averages fine parallel
  transports in local frames. A fixed axial frame would distinguish an
  ordering of the axes. We instead minimize a symmetric average of transport
  energies when the field is sufficiently small, and average over
  curvature-controlled axial frames elsewhere. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>
  and the paragraph following its proof show that these choices give a
  measurable, covariant probability kernel. The normalization preserves the
  partition function under the block integration in
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>.

  The remaining lemmas prepare the local integration used in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C> and<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>.
  The free block problem determines the quadratic action and the linear
  interpolation. An axial coercivity estimate then gives the nonlinear
  stationary branch. Its Gaussian integral contributes a determinant and the
  Haar-coordinate density; together with the inherited loop action they give
  the one-loop action. The third and fourth derivatives have bounds that
  improve the input-dependent part by one power of the block ratio. A
  stationary gauge identity removes the predictor contact term from the
  tested quadratic coupling coefficient. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> give the local large-field energy and the
  simultaneous differentiated integral used in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>.

  Three uniformities require care. The frame cutoffs must work for
  arbitrarily many sites, and the free kernels must remain controlled after
  arbitrarily many integrations. The first follows from a contraction after
  centering the frame minimizers. For the second we separate the principal
  Fourier pole before inversion. In the determinant comparison, the fine cell
  contains<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> sites. Weighted inverses and the
  summable cube of a regulator prevent this number from entering the
  comparison constant.

  Throughout, use the group and metric of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, the Wilson
  term<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-price-chord>, and the level
  choices<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-levels>. At a step<nbsp><math|m+1\<to\>m>, put
  <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>, <math|\<ell\>=\<ell\><rsub|m>>, and
  <math|R=<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\>>. Unless stated otherwise, periods satisfy
  the minimum-period requirement in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>.
  All finite operators use counting inner products and are periodizations of
  the kernels described below. An adjoint at complex momentum means the
  analytic continuation of the real-momentum adjoint. Bounds for an input
  path action refer to Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> and
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> at the finer level. They
  are used in a descending induction through
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B>\U<reference|sec:partII-D>, starting
  from the Wilson representation (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>). At a step
  <math|m+1\<to\>m> its hypothesis is <math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>:
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> at level <math|m+1>, and
  an input curve satisfying Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25a>
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps>. The joint proof after
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> gives <math|<around|(|I<rsub|m>|)>>;
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> give the output
  activities; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> gives the coupling (its proof
  uses Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>\U<reference|lem:D6>, none of which uses
  it); Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> closes the step.

  <paragraph|Frames and predictors.>

  Put <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>=<around|{|-R,\<ldots\>,R|}><rsup|4>>. For
  <math|o\<in\>S<rsub|4>>, let <math|T<rsub|U>*<around|(|y\<to\>z;o|)>> be
  the ordered product along the path that changes the four coordinates in the
  order<nbsp><math|o>, using inverse links for negative steps.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Axial frames><label|def:D20a> The frame tagged by
    <math|<around|(|z,o|)>>, its normalization and its link coordinates are

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>=T<rsub|U>*<around|(|z\<to\>y;o|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>=<around|(|g<rsub|0><rsup|z,o>|)><rsup|-1>*g<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>,<space|2em>U<rsub|<around|(|y,a|)>><rsup|z,o>=g<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>*U*<around|(|y,a|)><around|(|g<rsub|y+e<rsub|a>><rsup|z,o>|)><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    These frames provide the finite alternatives for the symmetric gauge
    choice.
  </definition>

  For an origin-based axial tag <math|o<rsub|0>>, the route matrix tested
  below is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-T\><rsub|x*y;o><rsup|o<rsub|0>>=g<rsub|x><rsup|0,o<rsub|0>>*T<rsub|U>*<around|(|x\<to\>y;o|)><around|(|g<rsub|y><rsup|0,o<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  The common tag set on both branches is <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>\<times\>S<rsub|4>>;
  on the minimizing branch the frame is independent of its tag. For the patch
  centred at <math|\<ell\>*y>, the notation
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-framed-connector> is <math|h<rsub|y,r>=h<rsub|r>>.
  Choose <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>\<gtr\>0> small enough as in the
  proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>, and
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>\<geqslant\>16>. Patch sums below count internal
  positive links and plaquettes.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Symmetric frame choice><label|def:D20b> When
    <math|<chord><around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|x*y;o><rsup|o<rsub|0>>|)>\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>>
    for every <math|x,y,o,o<rsub|0>>, use the minimizer, with
    <math|h<rsub|0>=1>, of

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|24<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>|\|><rsup|2>>*<big|sum><rsub|y,z\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>><big|sum><rsub|o\<in\>S<rsub|4>><wilsonterm><around|(|h<rsub|y>*T<rsub|U>*<around|(|y\<to\>z;o|)>*h<rsub|z><rsup|-1>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Otherwise use normalized axial frames with

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|patch>><chord><around|(|U<rsub|e><rsup|z,o>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*<around|(|2*R+1|)>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\><math-up|patch>><chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Tags have equal mass over all tags on the first branch and eligible tags
    on the second. This makes the choice symmetric.
  </definition>

  For a coarse edge use the framed connector<nbsp><math|Y<rsub|e,z>>
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-framed-connector>. The offset weight is supported
  in<nbsp><math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|j*<around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2>\<subset\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Coarse predictors><label|def:D21> Let
    <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>=<around|(|<math-up|Unif><around|(|\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2>|)>|)><rsup|\<ast\>j>>.
    Define

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>=<math-up|argmin><rsub|u\<in\>G><big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>><wilsonterm><around|(|Y<rsub|e,z>*u<rsup|-1>|)><space|1em><text|if
      the minimum is below >\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Give predictor tags uniform mass on <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>> on this
    branch. Otherwise choose <math|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>=Y<rsub|e,z>>
    with tag mass <math|w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>>. The product of frame
    and predictor masses is <math|\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)>>.
    These choices define every summand of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>.
  </definition>

  The branch choices require constants independent of the patch radius. Their
  normalization then follows from finite probability sums and Haar
  invariance.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Branches and normalization><label|lem:B1> For the chosen frame and
    predictor cutoffs and axial factor <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>\<geqslant\>16>,
    every stated minimizer is unique on its branch, eligible axial tags are
    nonempty, and

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>>\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)>=1,<space|2em><big|int><blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  The proof below establishes the cutoffs first, then the two normalizations.

  <\proof>
    We use<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-price-chord> and
    Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D20a>\U<reference|def:D21>. Work in the
    real Euclidean space of complex matrices with Frobenius norm. The tubular
    projection lemma of<nbsp><cite-detail|Foote1984|p.<nbsp>153, Lemma>,
    applied to the compact smooth embedded submanifold <math|G>, gives a
    neighbourhood with unique smooth nearest-point
    projection<nbsp><math|\<pi\>>. At the identity its derivative is the
    orthogonal projection<nbsp><math|P<rsub|<g>>> onto<nbsp><math|<g>>.
    Consequently, for any <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, shrinking this
    neighbourhood gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-projection><around|\<\|\|\>|\<pi\><around|(|A|)>-\<pi\><around|(|B|)>-P<rsub|<g>>*<around|(|A-B|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A-B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>
    </equation>

    when <math|A,B> lie in a sufficiently small ball about<nbsp><math|1>.
    Integrate <math|D*\<pi\>-P<rsub|<g>>> along their joining segment.

    For the predictor put <math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*Y<rsub|e,z>>.
    Expansion of the square gives

    <\equation*>
      2*n<rsub|G>*<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>><wilsonterm><around|(|Y<rsub|e,z>*u<rsup|-1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>\<\|\|\>Y<rsub|e,z>-<wide|Y|\<bar\>>\<\|\|\><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>-u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the minimizers are precisely the nearest group points
    to<nbsp><math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>>. If the minimum is
    below<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>, then
    <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>,G|)>\<less\><sqrt|2*n<rsub|G>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>>.
    Choose this smaller than a tubular radius. This choice uses neither the
    weights nor their number.

    For the frame, first make an origin-based axial change of gauge. Its
    route matrices, denoted <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|x*y,o>>, satisfy
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|x*x,o>=1> and <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|y*x,o<rprime|'>>=\<cal-T\><rsub|x*y,o><rsup|-1>>
    for the reversed order<nbsp><math|o<rprime|'>>. On the minimizing branch
    choose <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>> so that
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|x*y,o>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>d>,
    where <math|d\<leqslant\>1/4> will be fixed below. Write
    <math|<math-up|av>> for the uniform averages over the indicated indices.
    A global minimizer<nbsp><math|h> exists by compactness. Varying its
    coordinate<nbsp><math|h<rsub|x>> and pairing the two orientations shows
    that <math|h<rsub|x>> is a nearest group point to

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>=<math-up|av><rsub|y,o><space|0.17em>h<rsub|y>*\<cal-T\><rsub|x*y,o><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The self term is harmless: replacing it by its value at a proposed
    competitor adds a nonnegative square. Comparison with the constant frame
    and the triangle inequality give

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|av><rsub|x,y><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|x>-h<rsub|y>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4*d<rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Left-multiply<nbsp><math|h> so that <math|1> is a nearest point to its
    centroid <math|<wide|h|\<bar\>>=<math-up|av><rsub|y><space|0.17em>h<rsub|y>>.
    Jensen's inequality then gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>2*d>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>3*d>.

    Center two minimizers<nbsp><math|h,k> in this way, and put
    <math|u=max<rsub|y><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|y>-k<rsub|y>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>>.
    Their centroids are normal to<nbsp><math|G> at<nbsp><math|1>, so
    <math|P<rsub|<g>>*<around|(|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-<wide|k|\<bar\>>|)>=0>.
    Moreover,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|k|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>u,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|x><around|(|h|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|k|)>-<around|(|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-<wide|k|\<bar\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>d*u<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-projection> with <math|\<epsilon\>=1/4>, choosing
    <math|3*d> inside its ball. At a coordinate attaining<nbsp><math|u> it
    yields <math|u\<leqslant\><around|(|d+1/4|)>*u\<leqslant\>u/2>. Hence the
    centered minimizers coincide. All minimizers differ by a common left
    multiple, which <math|h<rsub|0>=1> fixes uniquely.

    For the axial alternatives let <math|E*<around|(|X,U|)>> and
    <math|F*<around|(|X,U|)>> be the sums of link and plaquette chords
    internal to a rectangular slice<nbsp><math|X>. Induct on the
    number<nbsp><math|r> of varying axes in that slice. Choose a layer
    perpendicular to the last axis whose plaquette sum is at most the slice
    average, and apply the induction hypothesis there. Extend its frame along
    the last axis, making those links equal to<nbsp><math|1>. A link in
    another layer differs from its transported link in the chosen layer by a
    product of intervening plaquettes. Each plaquette lies between at most
    <math|2*R> such pairs. The chord triangle inequality and conjugation
    invariance, summed over the links, give

    <\equation*>
      E*<around|(|X,U<rsup|<math-up|ax>>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*R+1|)>*<around|{|E*<around|(|X<rsub|0>,U<rsup|<math-up|ax>>|)>+F*<around|(|X,U|)>|}>\<leqslant\>4*r*<around|(|2*R+1|)>*F*<around|(|X,U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the second inequality use <math|<around|(|2*R+1|)>*F*<around|(|X<rsub|0>,U|)>\<leqslant\>F*<around|(|X,U|)>>
    in the induction bound. At <math|r=4> this proves eligibility, for each
    axis ordering, with factor<nbsp><math|16>.

    Each frame mass therefore sums to one; each predictor mass sums to one
    since <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>=1>. Successively
    summing the finite tag choices proves the first identity. For fixed tags,
    every coarse-link factor in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>
    integrates to one by right invariance of Haar measure. Their product and
    the tag sum prove the second identity.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The kernel assertions in Part<nbsp>I.> The branch tests are
  Borel; unique compact minimizers depend continuously on their data. Axial
  eligibility is a closed inequality. Thus the finite tag sum is Borel, and
  it is bounded for fixed<nbsp><math|\<beta\>> since
  <math|0\<leqslant\><wilsonterm>\<leqslant\>2> and the Haar denominator is
  positive. Gauge transformations act by conjugation on the rooted transports
  and transform both ends of a connector; lattice isometries permute the
  roots, orders and offsets, including inversion of reversed transports. The
  objective functions, eligibility inequalities and tag masses are preserved.
  Uniqueness on the minimizing branches and reindexing on the other branches
  therefore give

  <\equation*>
    <blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\>>,V<rsup|\<gamma\>\<circ\>\<iota\>>|)>=<blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>,<space|2em><blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|s<rsub|\<ell\>*b>*U,s<rsub|b>*V|)>=<blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>,<space|2em>\<iota\><around|(|y|)>=\<ell\>*y<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|s<rsub|b>> is the pullback by <math|x\<mapsto\>O*x+b>, with
  <math|O\<in\>H<rsub|4>>; <math|s<rsub|\<ell\>*b>> uses the same <math|O> on
  fine sites. Fubini's theorem and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>
  give<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-partition-preservation>. Thus the block kernel is
  measurable, covariant and normalized.

  <paragraph|The free action.>

  Write <math|d<rsub|r>> for the forward lattice exterior derivative on
  <math|r>-forms. Thus <math|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>=a<rsub|b>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>-a<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>-a<rsub|a>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|b>|)>+a<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>>.
  A history at level <math|m> is the finite sequence
  <math|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>,J<rsub|i+1>|)><rsub|m\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>>,
  read from fine to coarse. Its stages decrease by at most one. Let
  <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>> be the derivative of the predictor at the flat
  field. To specify its free metric, set

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|q<rsub|a><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<math-up|e><rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|a>>-1,<space|1em>\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a><around|(|\<theta\>|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-\<theta\>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|t|)>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|s=0><rsup|\<ell\>-1><math-up|e><rsup|i*s*t>,<space|1em>b<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2><math-up|e><rsup|i*s*\<theta\><rsub|a>>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Starting from <math|c<rsub|a>=\<lambda\><rsup|-1>>, update these auxiliary
  diagonal covariances at each step <math|i+1\<to\>i> of the history, with
  <math|\<ell\>=\<ell\><rsub|i>> and <math|j=J<rsub|i+1>>, by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-cov-recursion>c<rprime|'><rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>+\<ell\><rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|z<bmod>\<ell\>>b<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)><rsup|2*j>*r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)><rsub|a>|)>*r<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|-<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)><rsub|a>|)>*c<rsub|a><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>,<space|2em>\<theta\><rsub|z>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/\<ell\>.
  </equation>

  For <math|p\<ne\>0>, define the unmodified two-form metric and the chosen
  metric by

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|p|)>=<frac|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|(|a,b|)>=<around|(|c,d|)>>|c<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*c<rsub|b><around|(|p|)>*<big|sum><rsub|h>q<rsub|h><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|h>*<around|(|-p|)>/c<rsub|h><around|(|p|)>>,<space|2em><wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><around|(|0|)>=I,>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|H><rsup|-1>=<around|(|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>+\<zeta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><around*|(|d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  This modification permits positive square completion on all two-form
  coordinates, including those outside the curl range. Fix
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|k>=max <around|(|\<zeta\><rsup|0><rsub|k,k>,\<zeta\><rsup|0><rsub|k+1,k>|)>>,
  where the positive thresholds for the two incoming stage pairs are chosen
  in the proof below. These constants are fixed before
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|k>> and the clipping coefficients. The ratio threshold
  also depends on the incoming <math|\<zeta\>>. The free action is

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><rsup|<math-up|free>><rsub|N,m><around|(|a|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|K><rsub|N,m>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>d<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a<around|(|e|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|\<ell\>-1>a*<around|(|\<ell\>*e<rsup|->+z+r*e<rsub|<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>>,<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>|)>>
  be straight-link averaging. The smooth interpolation <math|W<rsub|0>>
  minimizes the fine free action plus <math|<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a-v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>,
  with the remaining gradient fixed to match the predictor; the tree
  representative is obtained afterwards.

  Let <math|T> be the free two-form transfer (the lift of the straight-link
  block average): a periodized analytic alias kernel from coarse to fine
  two-forms which on curls satisfies

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1>*\<cal-M\><rsub|m>,<space|2em>T<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>=<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>*W<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Its extension to all forms is given by the same alias formula, with a
  straight geometric-sum factor for each of the two indices. The two
  conditions are compatible: <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>-\<cal-M\><rsub|m>> takes
  values in gradients, <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|c>d<rsub|0>=0> and
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>W<rsub|0>=\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|c>>,
  so

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>*v|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|a,\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|c>v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|a,<math-ss|K><rsub|f>W<rsub|0>*v|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>*W<rsub|0>*v|\<rangle\>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> shows that the adjacent defect factors
  through<nbsp><math|d<rsub|2>> on both sides,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-free-defect><math-ss|H><rsub|c><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>-T<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|-1>T-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>d<rsub|1>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><math-ss|H><rsub|c>=d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-W\><rsub|3>*d<rsub|2>,
  </equation>

  with the three-form kernel <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|3>> given in its proof.

  Write <math|k=J<rsub|m>>, and let <math|C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>> be
  the stage-pair transfer bound obtained below. Include the transfer
  inequality in the next lemma among the ratio requirements of row<nbsp>5 of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. At <math|m=N> the free form is the
  identity on curls. This free integration fixes the normalization of the
  kinetic coefficient in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>. The constants
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> below are uniform over histories. The
  coefficient arrays considered below are those of
  <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>>, <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>>, together with <math|W<rsub|0>> and <math|T> at
  the step <math|m+1\<to\>m>. Strip and coefficient bounds are uniform in
  <math|N,m,p>; the constant <math|c<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> depends only on
  <math|k>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free quadratic forms><label|lem:B2> For histories satisfying
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>*\<zeta\><rsub|j>>
    and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,k>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|k>>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|N,m+1>W<rsub|0>+<blockpenalty><around|(|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>-I|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>-I|)>=<math-ss|K><rsub|N,m>,>>|<row|<cell|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsub|N,m><around|(|0|)>=I,<space|2em><math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>\<geqslant\>2*\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*I,<space|2em><math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*I=\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<rho\><rsub|N,m><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The adjacent defect<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-free-defect> has
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|3>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k>*I>. At fixed <math|m> these
    arrays converge as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> in every smaller exponentially
    weighted coefficient norm. They and their limits have uniform analytic
    strip bounds and exponential coefficient decay.
  </lemma>

  We prove the history bounds before the energy identity, so that inversion
  at zero momentum is justified.

  <\proof>
    Use the averaging weights of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D21>. The
    straight-link multiplier is <math|\<ell\>*b<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>*r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|a>|)>>.
    The frame adds a gradient and <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>=d<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<phi\>\<circ\>\<iota\>|)>>.
    Gauge reduction therefore commutes with the block step. Before reduction
    the Gaussian marginal is

    <\equation*>
      v=\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a+<blockpenalty><rsup|-1/2>\<xi\>,<space|2em><math-up|Cov><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|m><math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|a>|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>+<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>I,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<xi\>> is a standard Gaussian and <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>>
    is straight-link averaging. Taking aliases
    gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-cov-recursion>;
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>=\<ell\><rsup|-4>*\<ell\><rsup|2>> combines
    counting adjoints with the two straight connectors. For
    <math|w<rsub|a>=1/c<rsub|a>>, minimization of the diagonal precision over
    gradients gives

    <\equation*>
      min<rsub|\<phi\>> <big|sum><rsub|a>w<rsub|a>*<around|\||a<rsub|a>+q<rsub|a>*\<phi\>|\|><rsup|2>=<frac|<big|sum><rsub|a\<less\>b>w<rsub|a>*w<rsub|b>*<around|\||q<rsub|a>*a<rsub|b>-q<rsub|b>*a<rsub|a>|\|><rsup|2>|<big|sum><rsub|d>w<rsub|d><around|\||q<rsub|d>|\|><rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed the minimizer is <math|\<phi\>=-<big|sum><rsub|a>w<rsub|a>*<wide|q|\<bar\>><rsub|a>*a<rsub|a>/<big|sum><rsub|a>w<rsub|a><around|\||q<rsub|a>|\|><rsup|2>>;
    expanding its square proves the identity. This is precisely the two-form
    metric defined above. For total history dilation<nbsp><math|M> at
    stage<nbsp><math|j>, the cumulative averaging filter is

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|\<theta\>|)>=b<rsub|M><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>*<big|prod><rsub|<text|stage
      increases at dilation >M<rprime|'>>b<rsub|M<rprime|'>><around|(|\<theta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Successive dilations<nbsp><math|M<rprime|'>> have ratio at
    least<nbsp><math|3>. On the enlarged coarse cube
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>5*\<pi\>/4>,
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>>,
    the geometric-sum formula gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-filter><around|\||f*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/M|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-j>,<space|2em><around|\||f*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0<space|1em><around|(|p<text|
      real>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the extra product, use <math|<around|\||b<rsub|M<rprime|'>>*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-up|e><rsup|4*\<eta\>*M<rprime|'>/M>>
    and the corresponding real lower bound
    <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-C*M<rprime|'>/M>>. The sum
    of<nbsp><math|M<rprime|'>/M> is bounded by a geometric series. The
    identities <math|b<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>*b<rsub|\<ell\><rprime|'>>*<around|(|\<ell\>*\<theta\>|)>=b<rsub|\<ell\>*\<ell\><rprime|'>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>>
    and <math|r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|t|)>*r<rsub|\<ell\><rprime|'>>*<around|(|\<ell\>*t|)>=r<rsub|\<ell\>*\<ell\><rprime|'>><around|(|t|)>>
    follow by writing each integer in its quotient and residue classes. They
    give the cumulative filter. The basic <math|b<rsub|M><rsup|j>> factor
    supplies the displayed alias decay. It also bounds
    <math|r<rsub|M>*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)><rsub|a>/M|)>> by
    <math|C/<around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)>>, with a positive
    principal lower bound.

    Separate the principal alias in the iterated<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-cov-recursion>.
    With <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>=M<rsup|2>*\<lambda\>*<around|(|p/M|)>>
    this reads

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<frac|A<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>>+r<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=f*<around|(|p/M|)>*f*<around|(|-p/M|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|p<rsub|a>/M|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|-p<rsub|a>/M|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The remainder<nbsp><math|r<rsub|a>> contains nonprincipal aliases,
    bounded by<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter> and
    <math|<around|\||M<rsup|2>*\<lambda\>*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>c<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>
    for <math|z\<ne\>0> on a smaller strip. Each earlier noise contributes
    <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|M<rprime|'>|)><rsup|-2>> by the squared filter
    bound. Summing these geometric contributions makes <math|r<rsub|a>>
    analytic and uniformly bounded; it is nonnegative on real momenta.

    Put <math|B<rsub|a>=A<rsub|a>+\<lambda\><rsub|M>*r<rsub|a>> and
    <math|F=f*<around|(|p/M|)>*f*<around|(|-p/M|)>>, so
    <math|c<rsub|a>=B<rsub|a>/\<lambda\><rsub|M>>. The identity for a
    straight geometric sum implies

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>*F=M<rsup|2>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|p/M|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p/M|)>*A<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It removes the apparent singularity in

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-h\><around|(|p|)>=F<rsup|-1>-<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>*r<rsub|a>|A<rsub|a>*B<rsub|a>>,<space|2em>\<lambda\><rsub|M>*\<frak-h\>=<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>|B<rsub|a>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    On the real enlarged cube <math|B<rsub|a>> is bounded above and below by
    positive constants. Since <math|\<lambda\><around|(|p|)>> and
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>> are uniformly comparable there,
    the last identity bounds<nbsp><math|\<frak-h\>> above and below,
    including <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|0|)>=1>. Thin the strip using the
    uniform analytic bounds. The unmodified plaquette metric is then

    <\equation*>
      <wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|p|)>=<frac|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|(|a,b|)>=<around|(|c,d|)>>|\<frak-h\><around|(|p|)>*B<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*B<rsub|b><around|(|p|)>>*<space|2em><around|(|a\<less\>b,c\<less\>d|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Substitution of <math|w<rsub|a>=\<lambda\><rsub|M>/B<rsub|a>> in the
    preceding minimization gives exactly <math|1/<around|(|\<frak-h\>*B<rsub|a>*B<rsub|b>|)>>.
    Thus this formula extends the defined metric analytically to zero. It and
    its inverse are uniformly positive on real momenta, with
    <math|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><around|(|0|)>=I>.

    The inverse modification defining <math|<math-ss|H>> has fixed stage
    coefficient <math|\<zeta\><rsub|j>>. This positive addition has uniform
    analytic bounds. Since <math|d<rsub|2>*d<rsub|1>=0>, it gives
    <math|<math-ss|H>d<rsub|1>=<math-ss|H><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>>; it also leaves
    <math|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><around|(|0|)>=I>.

    The defect annihilates curls, for both the unmodified and the modified
    metrics: by the two conditions on<nbsp><math|T> and
    <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|-1><math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1>>,
    its value on <math|d<rsub|1>*v> is

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|H><rsub|c>d<rsub|1>*<around|(|v-\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>*v-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|K><rsub|c>v|)>=0,
    </equation*>

    since <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>=I-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|K><rsub|c>>
    and <math|d<rsub|1>*d<rsub|0>=0>. For the unmodified metrics, divide this
    closed two-form defect by the two exterior derivatives, coordinate by
    coordinate, using <math|<around|(|f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|p\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>|)>|)>/q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x>f<rsub|x><math-up|e><rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x><around|(|1-<math-up|e><rsup|-i*p<rsub|a>*x<rsub|a>>|)>/q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>>.
    The quotient is a finite geometric sum for each Fourier coefficient. The
    two divisions lose two powers of path length; the exponential bounds
    leave a smaller strip. Symmetrize the resulting three-form kernel and
    call it <math|F<rsub|0>>. Thus <math|F<rsub|0>> factors the defect formed
    with <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>,<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>>. If
    <math|S<rsub|0>> is the corresponding lift of
    <math|d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>*T<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>>,
    Woodbury inversion gives <math|d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|H><rsub|c>=D<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2>>.
    Substituting the two inverse modifications into the defect, the outgoing
    addition is <math|\<zeta\><rsub|k>*D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|c>> and
    the incoming subtraction is <math|-\<zeta\><rsub|j>*D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*S<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*S<rsub|0>*D<rsub|c>>.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\><rsub|3>=D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|F<rsub|0>+\<zeta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*I-\<zeta\><rsub|j>*S<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*S<rsub|0>|)>*D<rsub|c>,<space|2em>D<rsub|c>=<around|(|I+\<zeta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>*d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    The pole-free alias estimates give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>/\<ell\><rsup|2>>
    on real momenta: the lift has per-residue order <math|\<ell\><rsup|-3>>,
    whose square is summed over <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> residues. Choose
    <math|\<zeta\><rsup|0><rsub|j,k>=C+2> and then
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|j>*C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>>
    for the stage pair <math|<around|(|j,k|)>>. The parenthesis is at least
    <math|I>. Both <math|D<rsub|c>> and its inverse have uniform analytic
    bounds at the fixed stages. Consequently
    <math|c*I\<leqslant\>\<cal-W\><rsub|3>\<leqslant\>C*I> on real momenta,
    uniformly in the history. This proves the required positive defect; it is
    the input for the square rows in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>.

    Choose <math|2*\<gamma\><rsub|k>> below the lower bounds for the top and
    the two incoming possibilities at stage<nbsp><math|k>. Then
    <math|<math-ss|H>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,k>*I\<geqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|k>*I>.
    Its positive square root<nbsp><math|\<rho\>> is analytic on a smaller
    common strip: expand the square root about a scalar exceeding the common
    upper bound. The resulting convergent matrix power series agrees with the
    positive root on the real torus. Contour displacement in Fourier
    inversion gives exponential coefficient decay and the same bound for all
    periodizations.

    Finally, the normal equations for<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-cov-recursion>, with
    the gradient chosen to match the rooted predictor, give

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|K><rsub|f>W<rsub|0>=\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|c>,<space|2em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>=I-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|K><rsub|c><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    At nonzero momentum write <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|f>=<math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|a>|)>>
    and <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|c>=\<cal-M\><rsub|m><math-ss|C><rsub|f>\<cal-M\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>+<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>I>.
    Gaussian conditioning gives <math|W<rsub|0>=<math-ss|C><rsub|f>\<cal-M\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|C><rsub|c><rsup|-1>>
    modulo gradients and <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>=I-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|C><rsub|c><rsup|-1>>.
    Reduce these identities over gradients and select the gradient matching
    <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>. This gives the two normal equations. The
    pole-free formulas above extend them to zero; the constant fine potential
    is the coarse potential divided by<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>. Expanding the
    square gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|a,<math-ss|K><rsub|f>a|\<rangle\>>+<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a-v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|v,<math-ss|K><rsub|c>v|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|a-W<rsub|0>*v,<around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>+<blockpenalty>\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|a-W<rsub|0>*v|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Set <math|a=W<rsub|0>*v> and polarize. This is the asserted adjacent
    relation. The empty history gives <math|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>=I> and hence
    the Wilson form on curls, also after the inverse modification.

    For the cutoff comparison use the exponential coefficient norm

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c>=max<rsub|a\<less\>b,<space|0.17em>d\<less\>e>
      <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>e<rsup|c<around|\||z|\|>><around|\||K<rsub|a*b,d*e><around|(|z|)>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    For rectangular kernels the maximum runs over their finite component and
    residue indices. Choose <math|c\<gtr\>0> below the common decay rate of
    the forms, inverses, row factors and the kernels <math|W<rsub|0>,T>. For
    fixed <math|m> let <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, so the cumulative dilation
    <math|M\<to\>\<infty\>>. At each nonzero real momentum <math|p>,
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>\<to\><around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>;
    <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>> converges as a product of filter factors
    whose deviations from one are summable; the nonprincipal aliases converge
    by dominated convergence with<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>; and the noise
    contributions form a convergent series since <math|M> at least doubles at
    each step. Hence the symbols of <math|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>>,
    <math|<math-ss|H><rsup|\<pm\>1>>, <math|W<rsub|0>> and <math|T> converge
    at every such <math|p>, and so does that of <math|\<rho\>> by continuity
    of the positive square root. The uniform strip bounds make the
    coefficient arrays precompact under diagonal extraction; an array with
    these bounds is determined by its symbol at nonzero real momenta, so all
    subsequential limits coincide, and dominated convergence gives
    convergence at every smaller width. Absolute summation also gives
    convergence of every fixed periodization.

    To display the cutoff dependence, write <math|W<rsub|0;N,m>> and
    <math|T<rsub|N,m>> for the kernels at the step <math|m+1\<to\>m>. Writing
    their limiting kernels with the subscript <math|\<infty\>,m>, we have,
    for <math|0\<less\>c<rprime|'>\<less\>c>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-free-limit>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-<math-ss|H><rsub|\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m><rsup|-1>-<math-ss|H><rsub|\<infty\>,m><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>-\<rho\><rsub|\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>+<around|\<\|\|\>|W<rsub|0;N,m>-W<rsub|0;\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|N,m>-T<rsub|\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>\<longrightarrow\>0.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The stationary configuration.>

  Root the axial tree in the block <math|\<ell\>*y+\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2>>
  at <math|\<ell\>*y>, and set its tree links to<nbsp><math|1>. The remaining
  logarithmic link coordinates are<nbsp><math|z>. For a fine edge
  <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>> put

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|V<rsub|<around|(|y,a|)>>,>|<cell|y=<math-up|parent><around|(|x|)>,<math-up|parent><around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>=y+e<rsub|a>,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|<math-up|parent><around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>=<math-up|parent><around|(|x|)>,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|U*<around|(|v,z|)><rsub|e>=exp
    <around|(|<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*z|)><rsub|e>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>exp
    v|)><rsub|e>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Here <math|<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>=a> for <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>>,
  <math|<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>=<math-up|parent><around|(|x|)>>, and
  <math|<math-up|parent>> takes the centred block containing a site, and
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>> extends by zero on tree edges. The
  copied field is one on every tree edge. The fine leading action is the
  hypercubic average of

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|f><around|(|U|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\>><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|x,\<frak-p\>><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|U,x|)>|\|><rsup|2>+<big|sum><rsub|x,i><around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|3|)>>+\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>|)>*<around|(|U,x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The slots and coefficients are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, with masks and clipping removed. It
  excludes the inherited quadratic loop action and the common inverse
  coupling. Let <math|S*<around|(|v,z|)>> be this action at
  <math|U*<around|(|v,z|)>> plus

  <\equation*>
    <blockpenalty><big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|coarse>><wilsonterm><around*|(|<around|(|exp
    v|)><rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>*<around|(|U*<around|(|v,z|)>|)><rsup|-1>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Omit the common inverse coupling and put
  <math|<math-ss|Q>=D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|0,0|)>>. The path bounds
  make these unclipped sums analytic near zero.

  We specify the norms used for Fourier estimates. Put
  <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>, <math|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<around|(|\<xi\>+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/\<ell\>>,
  with <math|x,z\<in\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,\<ell\>-1|}><rsup|4>>, and

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<omega\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>+<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||q<rsub|a><around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsub|x*z>=\<ell\><rsup|-2><math-up|e><rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<cdot\>x>,<space|1em>T<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><math-up|diag><around|(|\<omega\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|-1>|)>*\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|0|)><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Use <math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>> on fine links or sites, the identity on coarse
  coordinates, direct sums on products, and
  <math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|-\<xi\>|)><rsup|-T>> on dual fields. Axial
  variables inherit the norm of their slice embedding. For kernels set

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>><rsup|<math-up|bl>>=sup
    <around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|<math-up|out>><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|out>>|)><rsup|-1>*K*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|out>>,\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>*T<rsub|<math-up|in>><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The momenta and all their indicated shifts lie in
  <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|\<eta\>>=<around|{|\<xi\>\<in\><C><rsup|4>:<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>\<xi\><rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>|}>>.
  Use tensor products for several inputs; field insertions are normalized
  coarse directions. The weights themselves need not be analytic. A fine
  Bloch field obeys <math|u*<around|(|x+\<ell\>*n|)>=<math-up|e><rsup|i*\<xi\>\<cdot\>n><space|0.17em>u<around|(|x|)>>;
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>>> identifies its aliases with the fixed cell
  at <math|\<xi\>=0>. The last factor in <math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>> fixes that
  reference cell. Write <math|W<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*v=<around|(|v,D*z<around|(|0|)>*v|)>>
  for the axial tangent, and retain <math|W<rsub|0>> for the smooth free
  interpolation before axial gauge. They are distinct coordinate
  representatives.

  The next lemma supplies the stationary configuration and identifies its
  first derivative with the free solution. For its kernel bounds fix an
  integer <math|d\<geqslant\>0> with <math|d\<leqslant\>2> or
  <math|j\<geqslant\>d+3>; let <math|a> be any centered fine residue. The
  constants <math|c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> and <math|C<rsub|j,r>> depend on the
  stage and derivative order, but not on the ratio, history or period.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stationary interpolation><label|lem:B3> Near zero there is an
    analytic stationary branch with

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|z>*S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>=0,<space|1em>z<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|1em><math-ss|Q>\<geqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>*I<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>\<gtr\>0> is volume independent. The
    axial representative of the free interpolation is
    <math|D*z<around|(|0|)>>. The averaging symbol equals one at zero and
    vanishes to order at least <math|j> at every nonzero alias. For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>d>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+a,y<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The second bound holds for every bounded coarse field.
  </lemma>

  The proof uses a local axial estimate for coercivity and a
  finite-dimensional implicit equation for the branch.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D20a>\U<reference|def:D21> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>\U<reference|lem:B2>. At the flat field the
    frame objective has quadratic part <math|<math-up|av><rsub|x,y><around|\||h<rsub|x>-h<rsub|y>|\|><rsup|2>/2>.
    On <math|h<rsub|0>=0> this is positive definite. The predictor objective
    has Hessian<nbsp><math|I> in its group variable. Their chart equations
    are real analytic. Their local complexifications and the analytic
    implicit function theorem make both minimizing choices real analytic near
    zero. Apply<nbsp><cite-detail|Lebl2026|Theorem<nbsp>1.3.8> to the
    complexified chart equations, which vanish at zero and have the displayed
    invertible Hessians. The neighbourhoods may depend on the period.

    For a fine infinitesimal field<nbsp><math|a>, let
    <math|I<rsub|a>*<around|(|x\<to\>y;o|)>> be its signed sum along the
    route. Differentiating the frame equation gives

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)>=-<math-up|av><rsub|z,o><space|0.17em>I<rsub|a>*<around|(|\<ell\>*x+y\<to\>\<ell\>*x+z;o|)>,<space|1em>h<rsub|x><rprime|'><around|(|y|)>=\<tau\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)>-\<tau\><rsub|x><around|(|0|)>,<space|1em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a=\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a+d<rsub|0>*\<cal-F\><rsub|m>*a<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a> is the
    <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>>-average of straight length-<math|\<ell\>>
    sums, and <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|m>*a<around|(|x|)>=\<tau\><rsub|x><around|(|0|)>-<big|sum><rsub|y>w<rsub|y><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<tau\><rsub|x><around|(|y|)>>.
    In particular <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>=d<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<phi\>\<circ\>\<iota\>|)>>.

    We next bound a field on the axial slice. Telescoping the boundary of
    each coordinate rectangle expresses a free link as a copied coarse-link
    value plus curls in its enlarged block. Averaging the straight connectors
    replaces the copied value by<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a>; the frame
    differences are route sums of the same curls. For example, interchanging
    two positive steps gives

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>+a<rsub|b>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>-a<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>-a<rsub|a>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|b>|)>=<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    Reverse steps change the sign and translate this identity. Repeated
    interchanges compare the tree route, a straight connector and its
    averaged translates. Thus, at each free edge<nbsp><math|e>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||a<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\>\<in\>\<ell\><space|0.17em><math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>+\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|j+7|)>*\<ell\>>><around|\||<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|\<square\>>|\|>+<around|\||<around|(|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a|)><rsub|<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>,<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>>|\|><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This is an identity followed by a triangle inequality: tree-edge
    coordinates are zero, and every interchange of two successive route steps
    contributes exactly one signed curl. Cauchy\USchwarz and the bounded
    number of enlarged blocks containing a given plaquette give constants
    <math|A<rsub|j,\<ell\>>,B<rsub|j,\<ell\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>, independent
    of the period, with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|1>*a*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|2><rsup|2>+B<rsub|j,\<ell\>>|\<\|\|\>>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The row construction under <em|Fourth-order matching> in
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>, at the finer input level,
    identifies the leading Hessian with <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|f>d<rsub|1>>;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> fixes its normalization at zero momentum.
    Cubic and quartic curvature atoms have zero quadratic derivative.
    Consequently, for the slice embedding<nbsp><math|\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|z,<math-ss|Q>z|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*z,<math-ss|H><rsub|f>d<rsub|1>*\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*z|\<rangle\>>+<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>><around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    One can take <math|\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>=min
    <around|(|2*\<gamma\><rsub|j>,<blockpenalty>|)>/<around|(|1+A<rsub|j,\<ell\>>+B<rsub|j,\<ell\>>|)>>.

    The map <math|\<cal-E\><around|(|v,z|)>=D<rsub|z>*S*<around|(|v,z|)>> is
    analytic, <math|\<cal-E\><around|(|0,0|)>=0>, and
    <math|D<rsub|z>*\<cal-E\><around|(|0,0|)>=<math-ss|Q>>. The same implicit
    function theorem gives <math|z<around|(|v|)>>. Differentiation yields

    <\equation*>
      D*z<around|(|0|)>=-<math-ss|Q><rsup|-1>D<rsub|v>*D<rsub|z>*S*<around|(|0,0|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The normal equations in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> identify this with
    the free interpolation expressed relative to the copied field and the
    tree slice. The implicit neighbourhood may depend on the volume; the
    preceding coercivity constant does not.

    Finally <math|<wide|w|^><rsup|<math-up|av>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=b<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>>.
    At a nonzero alias <math|2*\<pi\>*z/\<ell\>>, at least one centered
    geometric sum has a simple zero, so the product has a zero of order at
    least<nbsp><math|j\<geqslant\>4>. At zero it equals one. For a word of
    <math|r> fine differences the alias multiplier is a product of <math|r>
    factors <math|q<rsub|a<rsub|h>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>>, each
    bounded by <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)>>.
    This includes a difference crossing a fine cell boundary: its Bloch phase
    shifts the coarse base and gives exactly the same multiplier. After
    separating the principal pole as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, the
    interpolation alias has the bound <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-j>>
    on a common smaller strip. The centered-residue exponential is bounded
    there. Consequently the sum of absolute alias terms after <math|r>
    differences is at most

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r>*<big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>><around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|r>*<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-j>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'><rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r>*<space|2em><around|(|r\<leqslant\>j-2|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|r>*<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|r>>
    and each remaining exponent is at least two. The pole-free principal term
    has the same bound. This proves the required symbol estimate through
    order two for <math|j\<geqslant\>4>, and through order <math|d> for
    <math|j\<geqslant\>d+3>. There are finitely many difference words at a
    fixed stage; take a common smaller strip for them. Shifting the four
    coarse Fourier contours towards the sign of the displacement gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+a,y<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>d.
    </equation*>

    The bound includes all link components and is uniform in the centered
    fine residue<nbsp><math|a>. Summing over <math|y<rprime|'>> proves the
    assertion for bounded coarse fields. Periodization preserves the row
    bound by the triangle inequality.

    The same kernel bound permits polynomially growing inputs. To specify the
    exponential and polynomial weights, for
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<frak-a\>\<less\>c<rsub|j>> and <math|h\<geqslant\>0>
    put

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|\<frak-a\>,h><around|(|y|)>=e<rsup|\<frak-a\><around|\||y|\|>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||y|\|>|)><rsup|h>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<frak-a\>,h>=sup<rsub|y>
      <frac|<around|\||v<around|(|y|)>|\|>|w<rsub|\<frak-a\>,h><around|(|y|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The triangle inequality gives <math|w<rsub|\<frak-a\>,h><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>/w<rsub|\<frak-a\>,h><around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<frak-a\>*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>|)><rsup|h>>.
    Thus absolute kernel summation yields

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|y,a> <frac|<around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*v*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+a|)>|\|>|w<rsub|\<frak-a\>,h><around|(|y|)>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r,\<frak-a\>,h>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<frak-a\>,h>.
    </equation*>

    The sum converges since <math|\<frak-a\>\<less\>c<rsub|j>>. In particular
    it applies to polynomial jets of any fixed degree and to a cubic
    remainder when <math|j\<geqslant\>6>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Gaussian coordinates.>

  Write <math|U<rsub|v>=U*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>> and
  <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>=D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>>.
  Let <math|s> change axial logarithmic increments to left increments
  at<nbsp><math|U<rsub|v>>, and let <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>> be the
  product Haar density in these coordinates divided by its value at zero. For
  <math|x=\<ell\>*y+r> in its centered block, set
  <math|\<Psi\>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)><around|(|x|)>=h<rsub|<math-up|fr>><around|(|y|)>*h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U|)>>,
  using the patch frame of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D20b>. This extends
  a coarse frame<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>> over each fine block. The
  joint variable is <math|<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>>, with gauge
  action <math|<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\>>,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>*<around|(|\<gamma\>\<circ\>\<iota\>|)><rsup|-1>|)>>.
  Let <math|\<cal-R\>> insert fine site gauge parameters, and let
  <math|\<cal-I\>> embed free-edge increments, with zero coarse-frame
  component. Thus <math|<around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)>:<g><rsup|<math-up|fine
  sites>>\<oplus\><g><rsup|<math-up|free edges>>\<to\><g><rsup|<math-up|fine
  edges>>\<oplus\><g><rsup|<math-up|coarse sites>>> is square: the number of
  tree edges is the number of fine sites minus the number of block roots.
  Write <math|<math-ss|J><around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>> for the joint
  left Hessian of the leading action plus block penalty, with the coarse
  field fixed. We evaluate it along the stationary background; its value at
  the flat field has fine kinetic part <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>>. Put

  <\equation*>
    X=D<rsub|w=0>*<around|[|-log <around|(|\<Psi\>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)><rsup|-1>*\<Psi\><around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|w><around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>|)>|)>|]>,<space|2em>\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1>=<big|sum><rsub|x,i>\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|2|)>><around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>,x|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The last sum is the inherited quadratic loop action of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>. Define its stationary Gaussian
  correction by

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det <math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>-log J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is the negative logarithm of the field-dependent Gaussian factor.

  The determinant and coordinate density must be compared in the same
  counting coordinates.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Gaussian coordinates><label|lem:B4> The stationary Gaussian
    integral, including the inherited loop action and Haar density, has
    field-dependent factor <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-<math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>>>.
    Moreover,

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>=<around|\||det
      D*s|\|>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|0|)>=1,<space|2em>X*\<cal-R\>=I,<space|2em>det
      <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)><rsup|2>=1.
    </equation*>

    The Hessian <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>> is positive near zero.
  </lemma>

  The proof identifies the determinant in the coordinates used in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C5>. The sector version is given in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D04> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>. Tree gauge is an exact Haar change of
    variables. In tree order, each tree link is a Haar translate of its child
    gauge variable, whose integral is one. For fixed <math|V>, gauge
    transformations fixing every block root leave the kernel invariant.
    Integration of these gauge variables reduces its integral to product Haar
    measure on free links; root transformations are not integrated here. In
    exponential coordinates write this measure as
    <math|J<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|n<rsub|e>>*<big|prod><rsub|e>J<around|(|z<rsub|e>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z>,
    with <math|J<around|(|0|)>=1> and <math|n<rsub|e>> the number of free
    links.

    At a fixed stationary axial point<nbsp><math|y=z<around|(|v|)>> the
    coordinate change on each free link is

    <\equation*>
      s<rsub|y><around|(|z|)><rsub|e>=log <around|(|exp z<rsub|e>*exp
      <around|(|-y<rsub|e>|)>|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Right invariance of Haar measure gives
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|e>J<around|(|z<rsub|e>|)>=<around|\||det
    D*s<rsub|y><around|(|z|)>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|e>J<around|(|s<rsub|y><around|(|z|)><rsub|e>|)>>.
    Evaluate at<nbsp><math|z=y> to obtain
    <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)>=<around|\||det
    D*s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)>|\|>>. Since <math|D*s<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=I>,
    its value at zero is one, and it stays positive nearby. Stationarity
    removes the second-derivative term in the Hessian chain rule, giving
    <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>=<around|(|D*s|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>|)>*D*s>
    for the joint left Hessian<nbsp><math|<math-ss|J>>.

    Gauge covariance of<nbsp><math|\<Psi\>> gives <math|X*\<cal-R\>=I>. In
    tree-height order, each tree-edge row
    of<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)>> has its child gauge
    coordinate<nbsp><math|\<pm\>I> and earlier gauge coordinates; each
    coarse-frame row has its root gauge coordinate <math|-I>, and every
    free-edge slice column has its identity entry. Thus <math|det
    <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)><rsup|2>=1>. Positivity of
    <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|0|)>> from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>
    persists near zero.

    Integrate the positive stationary quadratic form with
    covariance<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)><rsup|-1>>. Its
    field-dependent factor is <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>><space|0.17em>J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|det
    <math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>>. Its negative logarithm proves
    the asserted action; the other factors enter the vacuum scalar.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Weighted inverses and insertions.>

  For a fine site field put

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|d<rsub|U>*\<phi\>|)><rsub|e>=\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|->|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|e>|)>*\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|+>|)>,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rsub|U>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>,<space|1em>P<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The joint gauge completion is <math|<math-ss|J>+F<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|\<mu\>>>,
  where <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X>
  and <math|P<rsub|f>> projects to fine links. Remove its predictor contact
  term to obtain one choice of<nbsp><math|A<rsub|\<mu\>>>. The other is the
  folded fine operator <math|<math-ss|A>+d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>+<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>>,
  where <math|<math-ss|A><around|(|U|)>> is the fine leading Hessian and
  <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>> applies <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>> to
  each link component. For weighted bounds use the nearest
  projection<nbsp><math|\<pi\>> from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1> and
  centered coarse frames: <math|c<rsub|y><around|(|U|)>=\<pi\><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|av>>*h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U|)>|)>>,
  <math|<wide|h|~><rsub|<math-up|fr>>=h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>*c<around|(|U|)>>.
  Pull back the joint quadratic forms by this change. Its tangent is
  triangular with identity diagonal, so it preserves determinants. These are
  the operators compared in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C6>; derivatives are at
  zero in free interpolated coarse directions. The constants in the following
  lemma depend on the free metric, frame and penalty bounds. After that lemma
  fix <math|E=max <around|(|1,C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>> for
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>.

  For inserted inverse bounds, let <math|M<rsub|r>> be the supremum of
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>> from the primal
  block space to its dual, in normalized smooth coarse directions, for
  <math|r=1,2>. Use any common smaller strip for these inserted kernels.
  Define

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|0><around|(|C|)>=C,<space|2em>Q<rsub|1><around|(|C|)>=C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|1>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|2><around|(|C|)>=2*C<rsup|3>*M<rsub|1><rsup|2>+C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|2>.
  </equation*>

  The norms <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>> retain the actual cubic and quartic
  path contributions. On a smaller common strip, the bounds for the joint
  Hessians through two insertions give

  <\equation*>
    sup<rsub|\<ell\>> M<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>\<cal-P\><rsub|j,r><around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>,<space|2em>r=1,2.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>> is the stage path bound fixed in
  row<nbsp>4 of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>; the fixed polynomials
  also depend on the interpolation, free metric, frame and penalty bounds.
  The derivation is included below.

  These estimates control the determinant comparison without a factor equal
  to the number of fine sites.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Scaled inverse insertions><label|lem:B7> There are
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>\<less\>\<infty\>>, fixed before the
    ratio and path bounds, such that, for <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>2>
    and every admitted odd <math|\<ell\>>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|sup<rsub|\<xi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>>><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|3>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*P<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>><rsup|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r><around|(|A<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|dual>\<to\><math-up|primal>>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|r><around|(|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsup|2>*D<rsup|r><around|(|A<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|r><around|(|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    On the common smaller strip, block-penalty and frame insertions have
    norms at most <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>>.
  </lemma>

  We first prove real coercivity in block units, then extend it to a strip
  and differentiate the inverse.

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>\U<reference|lem:B3>. Constants depend on
    the free bounds, stage and<nbsp><math|<blockpenalty>>.

    At zero background, <math|\<Delta\><rsub|0>> has
    symbol<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>>. With
    <math|x=\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-mass-floor>2<rsup|-10>*<around|(|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>x+\<mu\><rsup|2><around*|(|<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>>|)><rsup|9>\<leqslant\>x+\<mu\><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For <math|x\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>> the first term supplies the lower
    bound; for <math|x\<less\>\<mu\><rsup|2>> the parenthesis is at
    least<nbsp><math|1/2>. On links, the exterior identity
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|1>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> give the same lower bound, with a fixed
    positive factor, for the auxiliary fine operator. Changing its quadratic
    form by<nbsp><math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>> therefore gives a positive lower
    bound independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

    In centered coordinates fine gauge insertion has coarse component
    <math|-<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+r|)>>:
    differentiate <math|h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\>>|)>=\<gamma\>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y|)>*h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U|)>*\<gamma\>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+r|)><rsup|-1>>
    and the centroid. The uncompleted joint form is

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*u,<math-ss|H><rsub|f>d<rsub|1>*u|\<rangle\>>+<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*u-d<rsub|0>*\<vartheta\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Modulo a joint gauge transformation choose <math|\<vartheta\>=0> and make
    every nonprincipal fine alias transverse. This is possible by dividing
    its longitudinal part by<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>>;
    the remaining principal gauge parameter fixes<nbsp><math|\<vartheta\>>,
    since the principal averaging multiplier is bounded below
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>. Write <math|u<rsub|z>> for the resulting
    normalized alias coordinates and <math|H<rsub|\<gtr\>>=<big|sum><rsub|z\<ne\>0>\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2><around|\||u<rsub|z>|\|><rsup|2>>.
    The fine curvature energy bounds <math|c*H<rsub|\<gtr\>>>. The averaging
    multiplier on the principal alias is at least <math|c<rsub|j>*\<mu\>> in
    unitary cell coordinates; the weighted norm of all other alias columns is
    at most<nbsp><math|C<rsub|j>>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|c<rsub|j>*\<mu\><around|\||u<rsub|0>|\|>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2\<\|\|\>\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*u\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+2*C<rsub|j><rsup|2>*H<rsub|\<gtr\>>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Combining these inequalities gives a uniform positive bound on the
    distance, in the weighted norm, to the joint gauge subspace.

    Let <math|\<cal-R\>*\<phi\>> be joint gauge insertion. Frame covariance
    gives <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-R\>=\<Delta\><rsub|0>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-mass-floor>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\>*\<phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|0>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>*\<phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the second bound one must also estimate
    <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X> on arbitrary fine increments. In the
    centred frame, <math|X> is a sum of normalized averages of routes of
    length at most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>>. Its row and column sums are at
    most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>>, so

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<mu\><rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>.
    </equation*>

    The coarse-frame part satisfies the same inequality since its embedding
    has <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>> as counting-norm factor and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>>.
    Also <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>.
    For the first bound a fine gauge component contributes
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|z>*<around|\||q<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*\<phi\><rsub|z>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2><around|\||\<phi\><rsub|z>|\|>>,
    and its averaged coarse component is bounded by the summable alias filter
    times<nbsp><math|\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2><around|\||\<phi\><rsub|z>|\|>>.
    For <math|w=w<rsub|\<perp\>>+\<cal-R\>*\<phi\>>, the completed energy
    bounds <math|c\<\|\|\>w<rsub|\<perp\>>\<\|\|\><rsub|<math-up|bl>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>.
    The last two inequalities give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|\<perp\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>+C<around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*w*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|2>+C|\<\|\|\>>*w<rsub|\<perp\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Squaring proves real coercivity of the completed form with a constant
    independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

    The uninserted quadratic symbols and the geometric frame and penalty
    kernels have bounded weighted norms on a fixed coarse strip. To verify
    this, each fine derivative is bounded by<nbsp><math|C*\<omega\><rsub|z>>,
    a free interpolated field by<nbsp><math|C*\<mu\>>, and normalized frame
    or predictor averages by the summable filter<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>. At
    the flat field these operators depend only on the free quadratic action,
    the interpolation, the frame and the penalty. Their defining inserted
    kernels use the smooth interpolation; its fine differences of order
    <math|s\<leqslant\>2> are <math|O<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-1-s>|)>> by the
    alias estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>. For complex momenta, freeze the
    weights at a real momentum: if the coarse displacement has norm at most
    one, the geometric-sum formula and <math|<around|\||q<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>-q<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\><rprime|'>|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\>*<around|\||\<xi\>-\<xi\><rprime|'>|\|>>
    make the two weights comparable by a fixed factor. Cauchy's
    estimate<nbsp><cite-detail|Lebl2026|Proposition<nbsp>1.2.2> on a smaller
    disc bounds the change of the conjugated symbol by<nbsp><math|C*\<eta\>>.
    Choose<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>> so this is less
    than half the real lower bound. A Neumann series gives the weighted
    inverse on that strip.

    For inserted directions <math|v,w>, write
    <math|A<rsub|v>=D*A<around|[|v|]>> and <math|<math-ss|G>=A<rsup|-1>>.
    Direct differentiation gives

    <\equation*>
      D<math-ss|G><around|[|v|]>=-<math-ss|G>A<rsub|v><math-ss|G>,<space|2em>D<rsup|2><math-ss|G><around|[|v,w|]>=<math-ss|G>A<rsub|v><math-ss|G>A<rsub|w><math-ss|G>+<math-ss|G>A<rsub|w><math-ss|G>A<rsub|v><math-ss|G>-<math-ss|G>D<rsup|2>*A*<around|[|v,w|]><math-ss|G><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The geometric insertions have norm at most <math|C>: each smooth field is
    <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>>, each derivative on it is
    <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>, and
    <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|z>>. With two arbitrary fine legs,
    Cauchy\USchwarz accounts for their two factors
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|-1>>; a difference on either leg supplies
    its corresponding <math|\<omega\><rsub|z>>. This bounds the block
    penalty, frame and <math|\<Delta\><rsub|U>> insertions independently of
    the nonlinear path coefficients. For the path contribution, expand each
    transported curvature through total degree four. Its third derivative has
    one curl and its fourth has zero or more differences after two smooth
    insertions. In either case two arbitrary fine legs contribute at most
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|-2>>, while the two smooth insertions in the fourth
    derivative contribute <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>. For the third derivative a
    smooth insertion and the remaining curl give the same bound. Summation by
    parts moves a difference off a smooth leg when necessary; its path-length
    loss is summable against the exponential path bound. Each coefficient is
    a product of at most two path-row coefficients, or one atom coefficient.
    The row and atom sums therefore give a fixed polynomial
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j,r><around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>>,
    uniformly in <math|\<ell\>>. Adding the geometric insertions proves the
    displayed bounds for <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>>. The inverse identities
    therefore give dual-to-primal norms <math|C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|1>> and
    <math|2*C<rsup|3>*M<rsub|1><rsup|2>+C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|2>>. The inclusion
    of a primal source in the dual space has norm at most
    <math|C*\<mu\><rsup|-2>>; include its fixed factor in
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>>. Multiplication by
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> gives precisely <math|Q<rsub|1>,Q<rsub|2>>.
    Applying the same identities to <math|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I>,
    with the geometric insertion bounds, gives the stated bounds for
    <math|D<rsup|r>*P<rsub|\<mu\>>>. No derivative of a weight is taken.

    Finally, in centered alias representatives,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The complex denominator differs from its real value by a small multiple
    of<nbsp><math|\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2>>, and the Bloch Fourier matrices
    have bounded norms. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<dimG><big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>><around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-3>\<less\>\<infty\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9>=<around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>,
    two insertions leave one undifferentiated cube, whose trace norm bounds
    the regulator products; the remaining factors have bounded operator
    norms.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Vertices and the stationary gauge identity.>

  For a translation-invariant Taylor kernel, momentum order at
  least<nbsp><math|s> means that every coefficient of total momentum degree
  less than<nbsp><math|s> vanishes at zero. Let <math|V<rsub|r>> be the
  degree-<math|r> Taylor kernel of the fine leading action minus Wilson, for
  <math|r=2,3,4>. Use smooth free directions
  <math|\<cal-W\>*v=<around|(|v,W<rsub|0>*v,0|)>> in the coarse-link,
  fine-link and centred coarse-frame variables. The joint action in these
  variables is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-S\><around|(|v,u,\<vartheta\>|)>=\<cal-A\><rsub|f><around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|u>|)>+<blockpenalty><big|sum><rsub|e><wilsonterm><around*|(|<around|(|exp
    v|)><rsub|e><around*|[|Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|u>|)><rsup|h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>>|]><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>=exp
    \<vartheta\>*<space|0.17em>c<around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|u>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Here the superscript is the coarse gauge action; <math|c> is the centring
  map defined before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>. Denote its third and
  fourth derivatives at zero by <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|3>,\<cal-V\><rsub|4>>.
  On a smaller strip <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|\<eta\>>>, define <math|C<rsub|r>>
  as the supremum of their absolute scalar Fourier entries on
  momentum-conserving tuples, with either all legs
  <math|\<cal-W\>*v<rsub|i>>, <math|<around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>,
  or one fine leg of block norm at most one. The remaining coarse legs are
  normalized. This definition includes the predictor and frame derivatives.
  Put <math|S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|v|)>=S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>>.
  For two mixed cubic functionals annihilating joint gauge directions, put

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|h>=\<cal-V\><rsub|3>*<around|[|\<cal-W\>*v<rsub|h,1>,\<cal-W\>*v<rsub|h,2>,\<cdot\>|]>,<space|2em>h=1,2.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|b<rsub|1,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>>,b<rsub|2,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>>>
  for their axial restrictions and <math|<math-ss|G>> for the completed
  inverse. These pairings express the stationary fourth derivative in smooth
  coordinates. The constants <math|a<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>> below depend only on
  preceding stage data; <math|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>> may
  also depend on the input path bound.

  The extra momentum order of the difference makes the classical matching
  bounds independent of the input amplitude after
  enlarging<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Vertex bounds><label|lem:B5> The kernels
    <math|V<rsub|2>,V<rsub|3>,V<rsub|4>> have momentum orders at least
    <math|3,2,1>. For <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>>, a threshold
    depending on the input path data,

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|3>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|3>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,<space|2em>C<rsub|4>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|4>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|4><rprime|'><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In normalized coarse directions,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||D<rsup|4>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|4>+3*C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E,<space|2em>E=max
      <around|(|1,C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Gauge-annihilating mixed functionals satisfy
    <math|b<rsub|1><math-ss|G>b<rsub|2>=b<rsub|1,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>><math-ss|Q><rsup|-1>b<rsub|2,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>>>.
  </lemma>

  We first prove the low-momentum cancellations, then count powers under
  interpolation.

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, its input bounds in
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>, and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>\U<reference|lem:B3>. The quadratic
    difference is

    <\equation*>
      <wide|V|^><rsub|2><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><wide|d|^><rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*<around|(|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsub|f><around|(|p|)>-I|)>*<wide|d|^><rsub|1><around|(|p|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its middle factor vanishes at zero and its two curls each supply one
    momentum, proving order three.

    To include the nonlinear transport terms, expand a connection with
    connection components of weight one and coordinate derivatives of weight
    one. A plaquette curvature starts at weight two, with leading term
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*A<rsub|a>+<around|[|A<rsub|a>,A<rsub|b>|]>>.
    Changing its transport path changes this term only at higher weight. The
    row part at weight four is therefore its curvature square with
    coefficient<nbsp><math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<gamma\>*\<delta\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>s<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<gamma\>>*s<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<delta\>>=\<delta\><rsub|\<gamma\>*\<delta\>>>
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> at the finer input level;
    <math|s<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<gamma\>>> are the path-row coefficients of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D22>. Cubic and quartic curvature atoms
    start at weights six and eight. The Wilson action has the same
    weight-four term. All contributions of weight at most four consequently
    cancel in their difference. Polynomial connection jets vary
    independently. Expand their finite parallel transports through weight
    four and sum using the exponential path bound. A degree-<math|r> field
    term of weight at least five has at least <math|5-r> coordinate
    derivatives, equivalently that many momentum powers. This proves the
    three claimed orders, including cubic and quartic terms arising inside a
    quadratic curvature row.

    For the quantitative bound, an analytic coefficient <math|f> vanishing at
    zero factors as

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>p<rsub|a>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>\<partial\><rsub|a>*f*<around|(|t*p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply this to the vanishing Taylor coefficients, successively, on a
    smaller strip. Equivalently in position space, write
    <math|<math-up|e><rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>-1> as a sum of coordinate
    differences; the losses are fixed powers of the true path length. The
    exponential path norm bounds their sums. Wilson's degrees <math|r=2,3,4>
    have orders <math|4-r>; the differences have orders <math|5-r>.

    Use the fine-difference bound for <math|W<rsub|0>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>. For one arbitrary fine leg use
    Cauchy\USchwarz over the block. If it carries <math|s=0> or <math|1>
    differences, its <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm on the block is at most
    <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|3-s><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>:
    there are <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> sites,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>,
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>.
    Pair this with the other bounded legs. Summation by parts places at most
    one difference on the arbitrary leg. This gives the same bound in the
    dual weighted norm for a mixed vertex.

    There are <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> sites per block. A term with <math|r>
    fields and <math|s> fine differences thus has scale
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4-r-s>>. Wilson gives scale<nbsp><math|1> at its
    leading orders, and the differences give<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>>.
    The penalty has a fixed bound: normalized route and offset averages have
    mass one, straight sums cancel the factor<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>> in
    a smooth input, and implicit differentiation of the centered frame and
    predictor equations bounds their derivatives through order four. In
    centroid-normalized frame variables the linear equation is identity minus
    the constant projection; every remaining term is a normalized average, so
    these bounds have no factor counting patch sites. These contributions
    define <math|a<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>>; the path-dependent sums define
    <math|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>>. Choose the stated ratio
    threshold so that <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>*max
    <around|(|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>1>,
    giving <math|C<rsub|3>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|3>+1> and
    <math|C<rsub|4>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|4>+1> independently of the input path
    bound.

    For the mixed pair in the fourth stationary derivative, use the joint
    vertices just defined. In the joint coordinates, the exact nonlinear tree
    gauge change and the stationary chain rule give

    <\equation*>
      D<rsup|4>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>=\<cal-V\><rsub|4><around|[|\<cal-W\><rsup|4>|]>-<big|sum><rsub|<text|three
      pairings>>\<cal-V\><rsub|3>*<around|[|\<cal-W\>,\<cal-W\>,\<cdot\><around|]|<space|0.17em><math-ss|G><space|0.17em>\<cal-V\><rsub|3>|[>\<cdot\>,\<cal-W\>,\<cal-W\>|]>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<math-ss|G>> is the inverse after gauge completion.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> identifies the axial tangent with the tree
    representative of <math|W<rsub|0>>: their difference is an infinitesimal
    joint gauge direction, including its coarse-frame component. A joint
    gauge transformation of the axial stationary curve remains stationary and
    can be chosen with tangent <math|\<cal-W\>*v>. Differentiating this curve
    gives the displayed minimum formula. Each paired functional annihilates
    the infinitesimal gauge directions by differentiated stationarity and
    gauge invariance. It may therefore be paired through the completed
    inverse of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>. If <math|b*\<cal-R\>=0>, the
    shift <math|x\<mapsto\>x-\<cal-R\>*<around|(|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-R\>|)><rsup|-1>*F<rsub|\<mu\>>*x>
    sets <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*x> to zero and preserves both the uncompleted
    energy and <math|b<around|(|x|)>>. Thus its pairing through the completed
    inverse equals its pairing through the slice inverse. That lemma gives a
    constant<nbsp><math|E> independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>> and the input
    path bound. Duality then bounds the pairing by
    <math|C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E>. The three pairings in a symmetric fourth
    derivative have identical bounds. This proves both estimates and the
    mixed-insertion assertion.
  </proof>

  Denote the contact-removed completed Hessian by
  <math|A<rsup|<math-up|rem>><around|(|v|)>> and its inverse by
  <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>> on the smooth interpolated
  field. In joint left coordinates let <math|p<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|w|)>>
  be the <math|\<lambda\>> component of the logarithm of the framed
  predictor. The index <math|\<lambda\>> comprises a coarse link and a colour
  index; let <math|C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>> be its second derivative
  at that field. Thus the full Hessian differs by
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>d<rsup|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>>,
  where <math|d<around|(|v|)>> is the penalty discrepancy and

  <\equation*>
    d<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em>D*d<around|(|0|)>=-<math-ss|K><rsub|c>.
  </equation*>

  To keep the derivatives of the contact insertion as well as those of the
  inverse, introduce an independent coarse link source <math|u> and put

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|R><around|(|v,u|)>=<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>*<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>u<rsup|\<lambda\>>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|F><around|(|v,u|)>=<tr><math-ss|R><around|(|v,u|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|F><rsub|2><around|(|v,u|)>=<tr><math-ss|R><around|(|v,u|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The traces sum colour and cell coordinates. Their translation-invariant
  kernels, with internal momentum integrated using
  <math|d*\<theta\>/<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>>, are

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|b><rsub|i*\<lambda\>>=\<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>>*\<partial\><rsub|u<rsub|\<lambda\>>><math-ss|F><around|(|0,0|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|c><rsub|\<lambda\>*\<rho\>>=\<partial\><rsub|u<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*\<partial\><rsub|u<rsub|\<rho\>>><math-ss|F><rsub|2><around|(|0,0|)>.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|<math-ss|c><rsub|\<lambda\>*\<rho\>>> includes the two
  product-rule terms. Put <math|<math-ss|a><rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=-<math-ss|K><rsub|c><around|(|\<xi\>|)>*e<rsub|i>>.
  The contact difference <math|Y<rsup|<math-up|con>>> is the Hessian kernel
  of one half of the full minus contact-removed logarithmic determinant,
  evaluated on the stationary branch. Use the simple Lie algebra of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01> and the same stationary and analytic
  data as above.

  The cancellation below is exactly the coefficient needed in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C6>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stationary Ward estimates><label|lem:B6> The first penalty
    derivative has momentum order two; <math|<math-ss|b>> vanishes at zero
    and <math|<math-ss|c>> is analytic there. For a fixed colour and
    transverse link index <math|i=<around|(|2,\<alpha\>|)>>, the tested
    diagonal entry satisfies

    <\equation*>
      Y<rsup|<math-up|con>><rsub|i*i>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>=O<around|(|t<rsup|3>|)>,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsup|<math-up|con>>|)>=0.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> takes twice the real coefficient of
    <math|t<rsup|2>> in this entry.
  </lemma>

  The proof derives the trace contraction before extracting this coefficient.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D23> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>\U<reference|lem:B4>, <reference|lem:B7>.
    The free normal equation gives <math|<blockpenalty><around|(|I-\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>|)>=<math-ss|K><rsub|c>>.
    With the chosen discrepancy sign its first derivative is
    <math|<math-ss|a>=-<math-ss|K><rsub|c>>, of order two because
    <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|c>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|c>d<rsub|1>>.

    The determinant difference along the stationary curve is

    <\equation*>
      <tfrac|1|2>log det <around|(|I+<math-ss|R><around|(|v,d<around|(|v|)>|)>|)>=<tfrac|1|2><math-ss|F><around|(|v,d<around|(|v|)>|)>-<tfrac|1|4><math-ss|F><rsub|2><around|(|v,d<around|(|v|)>|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||v|\|><rsup|3>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first derivatives of each invariant scalar determinant vanish at the
    flat field: each is an adjoint-invariant linear functional on <math|<g>>,
    hence zero because <math|<g>=<around|[|<g>,<g>|]>>. Therefore its Hessian
    is unchanged on replacing the stationary curve by its tangent, by the
    second-derivative chain rule. Also <math|\<partial\><rsub|u><math-ss|F><around|(|0,0|)>=0>
    by the same argument. The potentially remaining chain-rule term
    consequently vanishes exactly:

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|u><math-ss|F><around|(|0,0|)>*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|2>*d<around|(|0|)><around|[|v,w|]>=0.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|<math-ss|F>> is linear in <math|u>, its pure <math|v> and
    pure <math|u> Hessians vanish at <math|<around|(|0,0|)>>. The mixed
    derivative retains both contributions

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>><tr><around|(|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>=<tr><around|(|-<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>>*A<rsup|<math-up|rem>>|)><math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>+<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>\<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The two mixed chain-rule terms of <math|<math-ss|F>> and the pure
    <math|u> Hessian of <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|2>> now give

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Y<rsup|<math-up|con>><rsub|i*i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|{|W<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>+W<rsub|i>*<around|(|-\<xi\>|)>-<tfrac|1|2>V<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|W<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>><math-ss|a><rsub|i><rsup|\<lambda\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><math-ss|b><rsub|i*\<lambda\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>,<space|2em>V<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>,\<rho\>><math-ss|a><rsub|i><rsup|\<lambda\>><around|(|-\<xi\>|)><math-ss|c><rsub|\<lambda\>*\<rho\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><math-ss|a><rsub|i><rsup|\<rho\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    These identities hold first for finite momentum sums.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7> bounds their traces on a common strip, so
    period limits give the indicated integrals and their derivatives.

    Write <math|y<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>=<tr><around|(|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>|)>>.
    The stationary gauge identity is <math|D*y<around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>=0>:
    gauge covariance of the stationary field gives
    <math|y<around|(|<around|(|exp 0|)><rsup|\<gamma\>>|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>*y<around|(|0|)>=0>,
    and differentiation gives this identity since <math|y<around|(|0|)>=0>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> identifies the stationary tangent with the
    smooth one, so it applies to the kernels just defined. In Fourier
    coordinates it gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-\<xi\>|)><math-ss|b><rsub|<around|(|a,\<alpha\>|)>,\<lambda\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    for each colour <math|\<alpha\>> and source index <math|\<lambda\>>.
    Restrict to <math|\<xi\>=t*e<rsub|a>>. Only
    <math|q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-t*e<rsub|a>|)>> remains, hence the
    corresponding component is zero for <math|t\<ne\>0>. Continuity gives
    <math|<math-ss|b><around|(|0|)>=0> for every component. Analyticity
    implies <math|<math-ss|b>=O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>|)>> and
    <math|<math-ss|c>=O<around|(|1|)>>. On the tested ray
    <math|W<rsub|i>=O<around|(|t<rsup|3>|)>> and
    <math|V<rsub|i>=O<around|(|t<rsup|4>|)>>. The displayed diagonal identity
    therefore has zero second Taylor coefficient, proving the assertion.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Local quadratic energy.>

  The Gaussian integral is performed only on part of each field
  configuration. Its complement must retain a positive energy per selected
  large-field test. We define this energy as the local sum of the completed
  squares and their signed error rows, on a consistent selection of tests.

  Fix one hypercubic orientation. Set <math|x=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>,
  <math|s=t<rsub|m>>, <math|t=t<rsub|m+1>> (in this subsection only; in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>, <math|t=t<rsub|m>>),
  <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>,
  <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>>, and

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>=<around*|(|x+<slope>log \<ell\>|)><rsup|-49/100>.
  </equation*>

  Increase the coupling threshold so that <math|B\<geqslant\>j+4>. In this
  and the remaining subsections, the estimates hold on every coarse torus of
  period <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B+1|)>>, with
  fine period <math|\<ell\>*n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>>. These periods also
  satisfy <math|\<ell\>*n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\>+1|)>>,
  so the patch-size requirement used in Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>\U<reference|lem:B4>
  holds. Use the same periodized kernels and local sector conditions. The
  threshold <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> distinguishes very large tests from
  tests at scale <math|t>. Here <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>=x+<slope>log
  \<ell\>>. By the formula for <math|t<rsub|m>> in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization> and
  <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|)>> in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, <math|t/s\<to\>1> and
  <math|B<rsup|n>*t/t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>\<to\>0> as <math|x\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  with <math|\<ell\>> fixed, for every fixed <math|n>. The input and output
  row maps on two-forms are <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|f>=<around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>>*I,\<rho\><rsub|f>|)><rsup|T>>
  and <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|c>=<around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>>*I,\<rho\><rsub|c>|)><rsup|T>>.
  They satisfy <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\><rsub|f>=<math-ss|H><rsub|f>>
  and <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\><rsub|c>=<math-ss|H><rsub|c>>.
  The free square-completion maps are

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M<rsub|f>=\<cal-B\><rsub|f><math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|-1>T*\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|n>=<blockpenalty><rsup|-1/2>d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>,>>|<row|<cell|N=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I-\<cal-B\><rsub|c><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-W\><rsub|3><rsup|1/2>*d<rsub|2><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>>>>>>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-free-defect> gives
  <math|M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|f>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>+N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=I>.
  For an infinitesimal pair of fine and coarse fields, let
  <math|X<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|lin>>,X<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|lin>>> be their
  curls followed by <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|f>,\<cal-B\><rsub|c>>, and let
  <math|Z<rsup|<math-up|lin>>> be <math|<sqrt|<blockpenalty>>> times their
  linear predictor discrepancy. The free interpolation identities give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-flat-rows>M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|lin>>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<rsup|<math-up|lin>>=X<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|lin>>.
  </equation>

  These identities apply to the untruncated flat kernels. We retain them when
  estimating the errors below.

  For the actual rows, transport every kernel coefficient along the fixed
  coordinate route joining its endpoints, using the replicated coarse
  configuration <math|V>. Retain a coefficient only when that route, measured
  in fine units, has length at most <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>>. For a
  fine-to-coarse coefficient also retain its residue and all the endpoints of
  that route. Denote the resulting maps, respectively truncating
  <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|n>,N>, by <math|\<Phi\>,\<Phi\><rsub|n>,\<cal-N\>>.
  Write <math|L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>> and <math|L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
  for the path rows of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> truncated by their
  true lengths in fine units. At a plaquette <math|p> put

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>>*F<rsub|p><around|(|U|)>,L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|)>,<space|1em>X<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>>*F<rsub|p><around|(|V|)>,L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|min
    <around|(|1,s/<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>|)><sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>>*F<rsub|p><around|(|V|)>,\<chi\><rsub|p><around|(|V|)>*L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|Z<around|(|e|)>=<sqrt|<blockpenalty>>*<space|0.17em>F*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>Q=Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Here <math|F<around|(|g|)>=P<rsub|<g>>*<around|(|g-g<rsup|-1>|)>/2>, the
  ratio in <math|v<rsub|c>> is one at zero chord, and <math|\<chi\><rsub|p>>
  is the output health test at the plaquette base. These thinned coordinates
  are bounded by <math|C*t> everywhere. Put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|clip><rsub|t><around|(|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsub|p><around|(|U|)>*<around|\||L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|1em>K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|clip><rsub|s><around|(|<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsub|p><around|(|V|)>*<around|\||L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|\|><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The clips use the respective input and output coefficients. The six local
  rows are

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=K<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>\|<rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,X<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|n><around|(|e|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|<blockpenalty>|2><chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>\|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c><around|(|e|)>\|<rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c><around|(|e|)>,Z<around|(|e|)>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,X<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>-<tfrac|1|2>\|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>\|<rsup|2>-K<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|N><around|(|q|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><around|\||\<cal-N\>*v<rsub|c><around|(|q|)>|\|><rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|L><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>+\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<around|(|p|)>-X<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|D><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,<around|(|I-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>-\<cal-N\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-N\>|)>*v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  A doubled plaquette row is summed over its two components. The index
  <math|q> runs over the two-form complement and three-form components of
  <math|N>. Summing over every anchor cancels all cross terms and squares,
  exactly:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-global-rows><big|sum>E<rsub|f>+<big|sum>E<rsub|n>+<big|sum>E<rsub|c>+<big|sum>E<rsub|N>+<big|sum><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>=<big|sum>K<rsub|f>+<tfrac|<blockpenalty>|2><big|sum><rsub|e><chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>-<big|sum>K<rsub|c>.
  </equation>

  The adjoints in this identity are those of the truncated transported
  matrices, not the flat matrices. The right side is the truncated clipped
  kinetic energy difference; the higher atoms and omitted tails are separate
  factors in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>.

  Fix a mismatch multiplier <math|W\<gtr\>0>, chosen after <math|\<ell\>>
  below. In this subsection <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,f>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>>
  and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,c>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>>, with
  the same convention for <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>>. A selection
  <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|f>,A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|c>|)>> consists of fine
  plaquettes, coarse mismatch edges and coarse flagged sites; set
  <math|<around|\||A|\|>=<around|\||A<rsub|f>|\|>+<around|\||A<rsub|n>|\|>+<around|\||A<rsub|c>|\|>>.
  Its location includes every projected plaquette corner and both mismatch
  endpoints. Write <math|A<rsup|<around|[|r|]>>> for its radius-<math|r>
  coarse neighbourhood. Consistency means that all tests meeting
  <math|A<rsup|<around|[|5*B|]>>> obey

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|p\<in\>A<rsub|f>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<geqslant\>t/4,>|<cell|p\<nin\>A<rsub|f>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>t/2,>>|<row|<cell|e\<in\>A<rsub|n>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<geqslant\>W*t,>|<cell|e\<nin\>A<rsub|n>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*W*t.>>>>
  </align*>

  In this neighbourhood <math|A<rsub|c>> is the set of coarse sites touched
  by an output plaquette with <math|<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<gtr\>s>,
  and every chosen frame tag has positive mass. The fine field is in the
  actual tree slice. An allocation sector fixes these selected tests, their
  assignment to connected components of their neighbourhoods, a block tree
  and the frame and predictor tags. Its consistency conditions are exactly
  the inequalities just displayed and the coarse flag and positive-mass
  requirements. Define <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>> by summing the six rows above
  over anchors in <math|A<rsup|<around|[|2*B|]>>>, projecting fine anchors to
  coarse sites. This fixes the energy whose lower bound is needed.

  Let <math|D> select the fine or mismatch tests exceeding
  <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>, and let <math|H> be the output anchors within
  <math|4*B/5> of <math|D>. Put <math|r<rsub|p>=<chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>>
  or <math|<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>> according to the lattice and
  <math|n<rsub|e>=<chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>>. All sums
  below are restricted to the local anchors defining
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>>.

  The positive fine and mismatch rows absorb the signed-row losses estimated
  below. The order of choices in the next lemma leaves its energy constant
  independent of the later coupling threshold.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local large-field energy><label|lem:B-D1> Fix the stage path
    bounds, free interpolation and penalty. For sufficiently large odd
    <math|\<ell\>>, and then sufficiently large <math|W>, there is
    <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> such that, for all sufficiently large reference
    couplings and every coarse period <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B+1|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|A><around|(|U,V|)>\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>
    </equation*>

    for every rooted block-tree gauge, allocation sector and consistent
    selection <math|A>. The coupling threshold and <math|\<kappa\>> are
    uniform in the period and local sector.
  </lemma>

  We first bound the fine, mismatch and coarse rows, then the affected and
  unaffected signed rows, and finally combine their contributions.

  <\proof>
    Use the path and cancellation bounds of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>,
    and the free kernels of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, all at the input
    and output levels already fixed in the descending construction.
    Exponential row bounds, together with unitary parallel transport, give
    row and column sums for <math|\<Phi\>> bounded by
    <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|-2>> and <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|2>> respectively. Both
    sums for <math|\<Phi\><rsub|n>,\<cal-N\>> are at most <math|C>.
    Truncation restricts every coarse displacement to <math|B/50>. The
    thinned coordinates give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*t>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*t>.

    Write <math|R<rsub|f>=<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|f>:r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>t>r<rsub|p>>
    and <math|R<rsub|n>=<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e>>. For the direct
    component, <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|g|)>>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|dir>><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,f>>*r<rsub|p>-<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>*<around|(|p,0|)>|\|>|)><rsup|2>*<space|1em><around|(|r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t|)>.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|\<ell\>> so <math|<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>*t>,
    with <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,f>>/8>.
    Each selected <math|t/4\<leqslant\>r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t> contributes
    <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,f>*t<rsup|2>/128>. For
    <math|r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>t>, the linear clip and
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,f>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,f>/4>
    leave <math|3*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,f>*t*r<rsub|p>/4>.

    The second component is a square when its health test holds. When it
    fails it has a cross term of either sign, which must be summed. If a row
    based at <math|p> is dropped, some plaquette <math|q> obeys
    <math|r<rsub|q>\<gtr\>t*exp <around|(|<math-up|dist><around|(|p,q|)>/R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>|)>>.
    For a fixed <math|q>, the number of possible <math|p> is at most
    <math|C<rsub|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>*<around|(|r<rsub|q>/t|)>>:
    its radius is <math|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*log
    <around|(|r<rsub|q>/t|)>>, and <math|<around|(|1+R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*log
    y|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>*y> for
    <math|y\<geqslant\>1>. Since <math|r<rsub|q>\<leqslant\>2>, each witness
    is within <math|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*log
    <around|(|2/t|)>\<leqslant\>B/50> for large <math|x>. It therefore lies
    in the neighbourhood where consistency applies and belongs to
    <math|A<rsub|f>>. Split a path row into endpoints with chord at most
    <math|t> and those above <math|t>. The row bound controls the first part
    by <math|C*t> times the number of dropped rows; the column bound controls
    the second by <math|C*R<rsub|f>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|<text|dropped >p><around|\||L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|C<rsub|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>+1|)>*R<rsub|f>.
    </equation*>

    Multiplication by <math|<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>*t>
    loses at most <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,f>*t*R<rsub|f>/4> after
    increasing <math|\<ell\>>. Therefore, for
    <math|d=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,f>/128>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum>E<rsub|f>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,f>*t*R<rsub|f>+d*t<rsup|2>*<around|\||A<rsub|f>\<setminus\><around|{|r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>t|}>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    For a selected mismatch <math|n<rsub|e>\<geqslant\>W*t>,
    <math|<around|\||\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*t>. Choose
    <math|W> so this is at most <math|<sqrt|<blockpenalty>>*<space|0.17em>n<rsub|e>/2>.
    The same square inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum>E<rsub|n>\<geqslant\><tfrac|<blockpenalty>|8><big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|E<rsub|c>> the path-row component cancels exactly, including
    its health test. Put <math|\<alpha\><rsub|p>=min
    <around|(|1,s/r<rsub|p>|)>>. Its direct component is

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,c>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|p>-\<alpha\><rsub|p><rsup|2>/2|)>\|F<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\|<rsup|2>-<math-up|clip><rsub|s><around|(|r<rsub|p>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The nearest-point Taylor expansion gives
    <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\><chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>-C<chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|4>>
    for small chord: the deficit is the squared normal displacement of
    <math|g-1>, whose normal part is <math|O<around|(|<chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>|)>>.
    Fix <math|\<delta\>> small enough that also
    <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>3<chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>/4>
    below <math|\<delta\>>. Since <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,c>\<gtr\>16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,c>>,
    the three ranges yield

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-C*s<rsup|4>,>|<cell|r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>s,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,c>*s*r<rsub|p>/4,>|<cell|s\<less\>r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>,>>|<row|<cell|-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,c>*s*r<rsub|p>,>|<cell|r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>.>>>>>
    </equation*>

    The last range uses only nonnegativity of the first term.

    Fix a constant <math|a> with

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>a\<leqslant\>min <around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,f>/8,d,<blockpenalty>/32,c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,c>/64|)>.
    </equation*>

    and set <math|\<epsilon\>=a/4>.

    Here is the comparison for large output plaquettes. Let
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|q>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)><chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>>
    be the fine plaquette sum in the lifted macro-patch centred at
    <math|\<ell\>*y>, with <math|m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>> its actual
    multiplicity there. The macro radius is <math|<around|(|j+7|)>*\<ell\>>,
    so <math|<big|sum><rsub|y>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j>>
    independently of <math|\<ell\>> and the period. On its axial frame,
    denote a straight connector by <math|Y<rsub|z>> and its two root
    adjustments by <math|p<rsub|0>,p<rsub|1>>. The averaging weight satisfies
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Telescoping each length-<math|\<ell\>> connector and the two end routes,
    then summing their crossings, gives

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>><chord><around|(|p<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|z>*p<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>.
    </equation*>

    For the straight segment, a fine edge occurs in at most <math|\<ell\>>
    translated segments, and the eligible axial link sum is at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>>. The two
    end-frame sums obey the same <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>>
    bound: write their coordinate routes, count each link at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>> times after the normalized transverse average,
    and use the nearest-point comparison for the minimizing frame. The axial
    alternatives use their defining link-sum bound instead. The predictor
    branch comparison bounds its adjusted chord by
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|pred>>> times this weighted chord sum, on either
    branch. Around the four sides of an output plaquette the root adjustments
    cancel. Comparing its four links with <math|Q> gives

    <\equation*>
      <chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>\<partial\>*p>n<rsub|e>+C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>.
    </equation*>

    This proves both the inverse-square factor and its summable multiplicity.
    In an eligible axial frame a fine link differs from its replicated coarse
    link by at most <math|C> times the macro-patch plaquette sum, plus the
    mismatch on a boundary edge. Compare the four sides of a coarse
    plaquette, average the straight paths, and use the predictor equation.
    Each fine plaquette occurs with total coefficient at most
    <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|-2>>; mismatch chords enter linearly. Split each
    fine chord into its part at most <math|t> and its selected excess, and
    each mismatch into its part at most <math|2*W*t> and its selected part.
    The resulting bound has the form

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t+H<rsub|p>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|p>H<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*<around*|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|f>:r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>t>r<rsub|p>+<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The excess constant depends on the fixed stage, predictor and penalty
    bounds and is fixed before <math|\<ell\>,W>; only the bounded-field term
    may depend on them. This follows by summing the actual route
    multiplicities before enlarging a macro-patch. Applying the same bound to
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>> and
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z>, with the column estimates above,
    bounds their sums on <math|H> by <math|C> times that excess sum plus
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<around|\||H|\|>>. Since
    <math|<around|\||v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*t>, these estimates give

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|H><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>\<geqslant\>-C*t*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>-C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsup|2><around|\||H|\|>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|E<rsub|D>>, expand its four operator terms. The bounds on
    <math|v<rsub|c>,\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>,\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>,\<cal-N\>*v<rsub|c>>,
    together with both row and column sums of the three maps, bound each term
    by <math|C*t<rsup|2>> per anchor. The constants also depend on the
    predictor and penalty bounds.

    Every output plaquette with <math|r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>> lies in
    <math|H> for large <math|x>: outside <math|H> the macro-patch comparison
    has no fine or mismatch test above <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>, so
    <math|r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>\<less\>\<delta\>>.
    On <math|H> the same comparison and <math|s\<leqslant\>2*t> give

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,c>*s*<big|sum><rsub|r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>r<rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsup|2>\|H\|+C*t*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Add this inequality to the previous lower bound. There is a geometric
    constant <math|K>, fixed before the coupling threshold, with
    <math|<around|\||H|\|>\<leqslant\>K*B<rsup|4><around|\||D|\|>> and at
    most <math|K*B<rsup|4><around|\||A|\|>> local output anchors. Increasing
    the constants defines <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>=C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*K>
    and gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|H><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,c>*s*<big|sum><rsub|r<rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>r<rsub|p>-C*t*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>-Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>*t<rsup|2>*B<rsup|4><around|\||D|\|>.
    </equation*>

    This is the positive term that cancels the third coarse-plaquette range.

    For an anchor outside <math|H>, no fine chord or mismatch above
    <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> occurs in the surrounding macro-patches. The
    preceding plaquette comparison gives coarse plaquette chords at most
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> on this region. Choose an
    axial gauge in a radius-<math|O<around|(|B|)>> coarse cube.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1> bounds its link chords by <math|C*B> times
    the sum of its <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|4>|)>> plaquette chords, hence by
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*B<rsup|5>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. Its
    block-constant extension acts on the fine field. Comparing a fine link
    with its replicated coarse link uses the local block-tree route; its fine
    plaquette sum is <math|O<rsub|j,\<ell\>><around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>|)>>
    and its boundary mismatch is at most <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. Both
    fields are consequently small in this common gauge, with the convenient
    bound

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>=C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*B<rsup|10>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>
    </equation*>

    through distance <math|2*B/5>. In this region compare each transported
    kernel with its flat uncut kernel. A path of length <math|n> has
    transport error at most <math|C*n*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>>, by
    telescoping its product. Let <math|\<tau\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> be a common
    exponential decay rate of the fixed-stage kernels. Their tails are at
    most <math|C*e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|0>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>. Each curvature or
    predictor differs from its linearization by
    <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>><rsup|2>>. With
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>=<around|(|1+R<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>+<math-up|e><rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|0>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>,
    subtract<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-flat-rows> and
    <math|M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|f>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>+N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=I>
    before applying the triangle inequality. This gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>+\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-X<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|I-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>-\<cal-N\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-N\>|)>*v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*t*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>.
    </equation*>

    The assertion is local: extend the small logarithms by zero off the
    neighbourhood; the cut rows do not change there. Their range fits inside
    <math|2*B/5> for sufficiently large <math|x>. Thus each signed row is
    bounded below by <math|-C*<around|(|t*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>+t<rsup|2>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>>.
    Since <math|B<rsup|n>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>><rsup|2>/t\<to\>0> and
    <math|B<rsup|n>*e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|0>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>/t\<to\>0> for
    every fixed <math|n>, their total is at least
    <math|-\<epsilon\>*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>.

    The fine and mismatch bounds leave <math|a*t<rsup|2>> per selected fine
    or mismatch test, <math|a*t*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> per test in <math|D>,
    and still retain the losses <math|C*t*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>>
    from the signed rows. First increase <math|\<ell\>> to handle the fine
    loss, then <math|W> to handle the mismatch loss. Coarse flags can be
    assigned to incident output plaquettes with chord above <math|s>, with at
    most four flags per plaquette. Plaquettes above <math|\<delta\>> number
    at most <math|K<around|\||D|\|>> by the macro-patch comparison; all local
    output anchors number at most <math|K*B<rsup|4><around|\||A|\|>>. Using
    <math|t\<leqslant\>2*s>, the middle range above leaves
    <math|4*a*t<rsup|2>> per remaining such plaquette and so covers every
    other coarse flag.

    The large output plaquette loss cancels against the positive term in the
    affected-row estimate; <math|E<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>0>. The residual losses
    are at most <math|<around|(|4*a*K+Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsup|2>\|D*<around|\||+C*s<rsup|4>*K*B<rsup|4>|\|>*A\|+a*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>/4>.
    Increase the coupling threshold, with <math|\<ell\>,W,a> fixed, until

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|4*a*K+Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a*t*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>,<space|2em>C*s<rsup|4>*K*B<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>a*t<rsup|2>/4.
    </equation*>

    The limits stated before the row construction justify both choices. At
    least <math|a*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>/2> remains. Take
    <math|\<kappa\>=a/2>. Every multiplicity used a fixed-radius local count
    or a row or column sum; none counts the period. This proves the asserted
    uniformity and the stated order of choices.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Sector covariances.>

  In this subsection put <math|t=t<rsub|m+1>>. We now specify the Gaussian
  law for simultaneous local integration. Fix the allocation, its actual
  block trees, the selected tests, all tags, and the frozen fine links
  <math|Y>. Active coordinates are the remaining free-edge logarithms in the
  complement of the selected radius-<math|10*B> neighbourhoods. The copied
  coarse field is the replica defined before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>.
  Linearize the retained fine and mismatch square rows at this field,
  applying their actual cutoffs and stencils. Their stacked affine map is
  <math|a\<mapsto\><math-ss|D>a+b<rsub|Y>>, so their quadratic energy is

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|Y><around|(|a|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\||<math-ss|D>a+b<rsub|Y>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|2em><math-ss|A>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|D>,<space|2em><math-ss|b>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>>b<rsub|Y>.
  </equation*>

  The stack includes the hypercubic average with its square-root
  normalization. A stencil for a row anchored at <math|z> contains every fine
  edge with projected distance at most <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>+5+r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>>
  from <math|z>; <math|r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>> is the maximum coarse distance
  read by a patch predictor. Thus no stencil is chosen by testing whether its
  coefficient vanishes.

  For an edge-coordinate <math|i> write <math|E<rsub|i>> for the two
  endpoints projected to the coarse lattice. The change of sector and the
  differentiated local integral use, respectively, the symmetric windows

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|sec>>>|<cell|=<around|{|j:max<rsub|u\<in\>E<rsub|i>,v\<in\>E<rsub|j>><math-up|dist><around|(|u,v|)>\<leqslant\>B/20|}>,>>|<row|<cell|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>|<cell|=<around|{|j:min<rsub|u\<in\>E<rsub|i>,v\<in\>E<rsub|j>><math-up|dist><around|(|u,v|)>\<leqslant\>B|}>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Each window is restricted to the coordinates of its sector; unused
  coordinates may be completed by identity modes. For either prescription put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-window>P<rsub|j*i>=\<b-1\><rsub|j\<in\>\<Omega\><rsub|i>><around|(|<math-ss|A>\|<rsub|\<Omega\><rsub|i>><rsup|-1>|)><rsub|j*i>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|P+P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>,<space|2em>m<rsub|Y>=-P<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|b>.
  </equation>

  These are compressed inverse columns, symmetrized for the covariance. Both
  windows contain a radius-<math|B/60> ball for large <math|B>, since the two
  projected endpoints are at distance at most one. The following argument
  applies to both prescriptions, with their own retained affine rows and
  mean. The Schur norm of a kernel is the maximum of its absolute row and
  column sums.

  Fix a coarse root <math|o> for the derivative directions. Here the coarse
  flag tests remain the correct ones at the base field; derivatives hold
  their selected index sets, <math|Y> and tags fixed, in coarse directions of
  size at most <math|s*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,e|)>|)><rsup|q>>;
  <math|\<upsilon\><rsub|i>=<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,i|)>|)><rsup|q*k>>.
  Assume <math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>
  and put <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>. All bounds below hold for
  sufficiently large <math|x>.

  For any principal coordinate set <math|\<Omega\>>, write
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>>=<math-ss|A><around|[|\<Omega\>,\<Omega\>|]>>
  and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<Omega\>>=\<Gamma\>*<around|[|\<Omega\>,\<Omega\>|]>>;
  their inverses are extended by zero outside <math|\<Omega\>>. They control
  the Gaussian marginals and the compressed columns
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window>. In the next statement, <math|K> ranges over
  <math|<math-ss|A>>, <math|<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>>, <math|\<Gamma\>>,
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>>, and <math|<math-ss|E>> over
  <math|<math-ss|A>\<Gamma\>-I> and <math|\<Gamma\>-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>>. At
  derivative order zero, exponential entry bounds give exponential Schur
  bounds by summing over coordinate locations.

  The following estimate justifies differentiating the common Gaussian law
  and replacing its window covariance by the full inverse.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Window covariance bounds><label|lem:B-windows> For each fixed
    derivative order <math|k> and direction power <math|q>, there are
    <math|c\<gtr\>0> and <math|C<rsub|k>\<less\>\<infty\>>, depending only on
    the fixed stage and ratio data and <math|k,q>, such that, uniformly in
    the period, sector and <math|\<Omega\>>, for
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|A>\<geqslant\>c*I,<space|2em>\<Gamma\>\<geqslant\>c*I,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||D<rsup|r>*K<rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><math-up|e><rsup|-c<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>><space|0.17em>\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>,<space|2em><around|\||D<rsup|r><math-ss|E><rsub|i*j>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><math-up|e><rsup|-c*B-c<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>><space|0.17em>\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||D<rsup|r>*m<rsub|Y><around|(|i|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k>*t*\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>,<space|2em><around|\||D<rsup|r>*<around|[|g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|A>m<rsub|Y>+<math-ss|b>|)>|]><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><math-up|e><rsup|-c*B><space|0.17em>\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>.>>>>
    </gather*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D23>, the axial
    estimate in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>, and the square rows above. The
    linearized square rows and an eligible predictor control a fine link by
    those row values in a fixed macro-patch plus <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|x>>
    times the surrounding fine increments, where
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>=C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*s> tends to zero. Summing
    squares, using the bounded number of macro-patches meeting an edge, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|D>a*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|2><rsup|2>+C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*\<delta\><rsub|x><rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    For large <math|x> the last coefficient is at most <math|1/2>, proving
    <math|<math-ss|A>\<geqslant\>c*I>. All-frozen sectors have
    zero-dimensional active space and the assertion is vacuous there. Adding
    identity coordinates for inactive links preserves this lower bound.

    Exponential path coefficients bound the weighted row and column sums of
    <math|<math-ss|D>>. Products give the same Schur bound for
    <math|<math-ss|A>>. More explicitly, for sufficiently small fixed
    <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0>, multiply coordinates by
    <math|<math-up|e><rsup|\<theta\><math-up|dist><around|(|i,\<cdot\>|)>>>
    on the left and its inverse on the right. The resulting matrix differs
    from <math|<math-ss|A>> by at most <math|C*\<theta\>> in operator norm:
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|e><rsup|\<theta\>*d>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>*d<math-up|e><rsup|\<theta\>*d>>
    is summable against the exponential coefficient bound. Choose
    <math|C*\<theta\>\<less\>c/2>. The inverse Neumann series now gives
    exponential row and column bounds for <math|<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>> and
    every principal compression, with constants independent of the number of
    coordinates.

    A compressed inverse column solves <math|<math-ss|A>P*e<rsub|i>=e<rsub|i>>
    inside its window. Outside that window, its residual is a sum of products
    whose combined length crosses the boundary. Every window contains a
    radius-<math|B/60> ball and there are only <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>>
    coordinates per coarse site. The exponential Schur bounds therefore give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|Schur>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-c*B>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\>-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|Schur>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-c*B>.
    </equation*>

    The entrywise column and inverse bounds retain exponential decay in
    <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>>, while the preceding difference has
    the factor <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-c*B>>. Taking the geometric mean of
    these bounds and decreasing <math|c> gives both factors together. For
    sufficiently large <math|B>, the uniform upper bound on
    <math|<math-ss|A>> and <math|<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|A>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|-1>*I>
    give <math|\<Gamma\>\<geqslant\>c<rprime|'>*I> for a fixed
    <math|c<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>. Its row and column sums are bounded by a
    fixed constant. The same estimate gives
    <math|<math-ss|A>\<Gamma\>-I=O<around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|-c*B>|)>>. Apply
    the exponential conjugation argument to <math|\<Gamma\>> and its
    principal compressions; their common lower bound gives the stated bounds
    for <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>>.

    Differentiate <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>> by
    <math|D<around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>|)>=-<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1><around|(|D<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>>|)><math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>>
    and iterate through order <math|k>. The index sets <math|\<Omega\>> stay
    fixed. Each derivative of a path coefficient is bounded by a fixed power
    of the path length and by the direction weight;
    <math|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,j|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,i|)>|)>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|i,j|)>|)>>
    moves it to <math|\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>>. A smaller exponential
    rate sums all remaining powers. In every differentiated residual, at
    least one chain still crosses the window boundary. This proves the
    displayed derivative bounds for <math|<math-ss|A>> and
    <math|<math-ss|A>\<Gamma\>-I>. For the inverse difference, use

    <\equation*>
      \<Gamma\>-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>=<math-ss|A><rsup|-1><around|(|<math-ss|A>\<Gamma\>-I|)>.
    </equation*>

    Every differentiated product retains the exponentially small residual
    factor. The same inverse differentiation formula, applied to
    <math|<math-ss|A>>, <math|\<Gamma\>> and their principal compressions,
    proves the remaining bounds for <math|K>. The spatial weights are moved
    to the first index as above; reducing the decay rate sums the intervening
    polynomial factors.

    The affine source is formed from the same retained rows and from a
    bounded smooth extension of the frozen logarithms. Its derivatives are
    <math|O<around|(|t|)>> with the same spatial weights. Multiplication by
    <math|P<rsup|\<ast\>>> proves the mean bound. Subtracting the exact
    inverse solution gives <math|<math-ss|A>m<rsub|Y>+<math-ss|b>=<around|(|I-<math-ss|A>P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)><math-ss|b>>,
    whose derivatives are <math|O<around|(|t*e<rsup|-c*B>|)>>. Finally
    <math|g<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>C*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|1/2>> and
    <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log x|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>; this
    fixed power is absorbed by decreasing <math|c>. The estimates refer to
    the actual <math|<math-ss|A>,\<Gamma\>,m<rsub|Y>>
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window> throughout.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|One differentiated local integral.>

  In this subsection put <math|t=t<rsub|m+1>>. We state the local estimate
  for the finite products used in the expansion. In a fixed allocation
  sector, a selected large-field test is called a seed. Join seeds when their
  fixed enlarged neighbourhoods intersect; their connected components
  <math|A<rsub|\<eta\>>> are called compulsory components. Write
  <math|A<around|(|M|)>> for the union of the fine-plaquette, mismatch and
  coarse-site selections belonging to the components chosen in a product
  <math|M>. Fix the support enlargement <math|h<rsub|<math-up|loc>>> once for
  the local factors. Compatibility means that the
  radius-<math|h<rsub|<math-up|loc>>*B> neighbourhoods of the distinct chosen
  components are disjoint.

  A factor has a complete read support <math|S<rsub|i>> containing the
  projected endpoints of every link it uses, every path and health test in
  its formula, every tag and frozen link, and the entire window
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> for each Gaussian coordinate
  <math|a> it reads. Thus a change outside <math|S<rsub|i>> leaves its
  formula and its Gaussian marginal unchanged. A finite product <math|M> is
  called a molecule when the intersection graph of its supports is connected.
  Choose <math|o> in their union. Its size <math|s<rsub|M>> is the sum of the
  numbers of radius-<math|B> blocks covering these supports, including
  retained path lengths in the same units.

  For an old forced activity retain its support <math|Y<rsub|i>> and
  inventory <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|i>> from
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>. The admitted products satisfy

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Y<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y<rsub|i<rprime|'>>=\<varnothing\>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>i<rprime|'>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-I\><rsub|i>\<subset\><big|cup><rsub|p\<in\>A<rsub|f><around|(|M|)>><math-up|corners><around|(|p|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>\<cap\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>M>S<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  They also satisfy the component compatibility just specified. These
  conditions retain exactly the incidence and separation used in local
  integration.

  Here are the factors and their envelopes. The retained square corrections
  are the exponential differences between the exact fine or mismatch rows and
  their affine squares <math|<around|\||<math-ss|D>a+b<rsub|Y>|\|><rsup|2>/2>.
  The other smooth factors are inherited regular activities, chart densities,
  and determinant and Gaussian-density corrections. Old forced activities are
  bounded by their measurable supremum envelopes. For these factors let
  <math|a<rsub|i>> include their absolute coefficient bound, the selected
  seed or path suppression, and the Gaussian exponential bound described
  below.

  For the chart cutoffs choose a smooth <math|\<chi\>:<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>\<to\><around|[|0,1|]>>
  equal to one on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and zero on
  <math|<around|[|2,\<infty\>|)>>. On an active logarithmic coordinate put
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|u<rsub|e>|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|<around|\||u<rsub|e>|\|>/<around|(|a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t|)>|)>>.
  The fixed mean bound is <math|<around|\||m<rsub|Y><around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>*t>,
  and choose <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>+1>. Fix a logarithm
  radius <math|r<rsub|log>\<gtr\>0> and take <math|x> large enough that
  <math|4*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t\<less\>r<rsub|log>/2>. The smaller chart is
  <math|<around|\||u<rsub|e>|\|>\<less\>a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t>; the broader
  cutoff equals one for <math|<around|\||u<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t>.
  A protected link is an active link accompanied by the broader cutoff
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|2*a<rsub|\<ast\>>>> whenever an old measurable factor
  reads it. Nonactive free links are the frozen links <math|Y>; their
  physical values are held fixed. The nesting
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<ne\>0\<Rightarrow\>\<chi\><rsub|2*a<rsub|\<ast\>>>=1>
  is what allows the physical-link change below on all derivative supports.

  Warning factors are the departures from one of the fine-plaquette and
  mismatch cutoffs, the frame and predictor tag likelihoods, the inventory
  tests, or the active chart cutoffs. The addresses are fine plaquettes for
  fine cutoffs, coarse edges for mismatches and predictor tags, coarse sites
  for frame tags, fine sites for inventory tests, and fine edges for chart
  cutoffs. A site's location is its coarse projection; an edge or plaquette
  is located at its projected initial vertex. Near a compulsory component the
  corresponding departure is included in its component factor. Away from
  those components, each remaining warning and all its derivatives are
  supported on

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|w>=<around|{|z:\<exists\>i,<math-up|dist><around|(|i,<math-up|loc><around|(|w|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*B,<space|1em><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<xi\>*t/<around|(|2*g|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, if all these coordinates are below that threshold, then
  <math|<around|\||m<rsub|Y><around|(|e|)>+g*z<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|c<rsub|\<ast\>>+\<xi\>/2|)>*t\<less\>a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t>
  after reducing <math|\<xi\>>, so both chart cutoffs equal one. The local
  predictor bound then makes the fine chord less than <math|t/4> and the
  mismatch less than <math|W*t>, and both tag likelihoods and the inventory
  test equal one. The strict margin persists on derivative supports. Choose
  the fixed <math|\<xi\>\<gtr\>0> small enough for these finitely many local
  implications. Let <math|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>> be the set of distinct
  selected warning addresses and <math|E<rsub|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>=<big|cap><rsub|w\<in\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>E<rsub|w>>
  their simultaneous event. Their amplitudes are bounded by the fixed
  stage-and-ratio constant

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|w>=2+<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>\<times\>S<rsub|4>|\|>+<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  This includes the likelihood relative to uniform tags. Fix any
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|w>\<gtr\>0> before the coupling threshold. With
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|w>>>,
  the weights used below are

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i><space|1em><around|(|i<text|
    nonwarning>|)>,<space|2em>f<rsub|w>=M<rsub|w>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|w>*<space|1em><around|(|w\<in\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each distinct address contributes once.

  Assume <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>/2\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>.
  Use the local prescription <math|\<Omega\><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window>, throughout this local integral. Conditional on
  the fixed sector and tags the reference law is

  <\equation*>
    d*\<nu\>=d*H<rsub|F><around|(|Y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|H<rsub|F>> is product Haar measure on the frozen tree-slice
  links and <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\>>> is the centered Gaussian.
  Reference tags are independent uniform tags; their actual likelihoods are
  included among the factors. All estimates below are uniform in those tags.
  A finite set of Gaussian reads <math|I> has marginal covariance
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I>=\<Gamma\>*<around|[|I,I|]>>.

  For a finite selection write its product as

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|M>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>M\<setminus\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|w\<in\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>W<rsub|w>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|F<rsub|i>> are the nonwarning factors just specified and
  <math|W<rsub|w>> the warning factors. The transported product will use one
  change of Gaussian coordinates on their common set of reads.

  To differentiate while holding physical fine links fixed, fix a base coarse
  field <math|V<rsub|0>> and write <math|u=m<rsub|Y><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>+g*z>.
  For a nearby <math|V>, set

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|V><around|(|e|)>=log <around*|(|exp
    <around|(|u<around|(|e|)>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V<rsub|0>|)><rsub|e><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|V>=g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|u<rsub|V>-m<rsub|Y><around|(|V|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Use a fixed smooth extension of this formula outside
  <math|<around|\||u<around|(|e|)>|\|>\<less\>r<rsub|log>/2>, equal to the
  identity plus a bounded correction there. Its derivative is invertible for
  <math|V> sufficiently near <math|V<rsub|0>>, uniformly over the chart
  argument; the extension is used only with the stated protected cutoff. On
  active protected links,

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|(|u<rsub|V><around|(|e|)>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e>=exp
    <around|(|u<around|(|e|)>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V<rsub|0>|)><rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  Every old measurable activity evaluated on those links is therefore
  constant under this differentiation. It is bounded by its supremum majorant
  and no field derivative is applied to it.

  The density of the changed marginal, relative to the base one on the union
  <math|I> of all reads, is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|I>*<around|(|V,z|)>=<around|\||det
    D<rsub|z>*z<rsub|V,I>|\|><space|0.17em><frac|<around|(|det
    \<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|[|-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsub|V,I><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V|)><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|V,I>|]>|<around|(|det
    \<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|[|-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsub|I><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|I>|]>>.
  </equation*>

  Define the transported integrand by

  <\equation*>
    <wide|F|~><rsub|M>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>=\<cal-R\><rsub|I>*<around|(|V,z|)>*F<rsub|M>*<around|(|V,Y,z<rsub|V>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The next lemma bounds its ordered derivatives at <math|V<rsub|0>> under the
  same base law, including products containing old measurable forced
  activities. It is the second local input to
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>. Its bounds may depend on the fixed
  mismatch multiplier<nbsp><math|W> and energy constant<nbsp><math|\<kappa\>>
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>.

  For an admitted molecule put <math|A=A<around|(|M|)>>. For a compulsory
  component with selection <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|f>,A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|c>|)>>
  and <math|A<rsub|c>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, let <math|F<rsub|A>> contain its
  full local sector factor and every read meeting it. Adjoin every seed
  component met by those reads. In this value estimate only, replace the
  healthy-support condition within this component by the consistent flag
  condition of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>. Retain the old-support
  disjointness and inventory incidence. The other factors attached to
  <math|F<rsub|A>> have the bounds <math|f<rsub|i>> above; their product
  excludes the component's seed suppression and warnings already included in
  that component. Fix <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>\<less\>\<kappa\>>
  before the coupling threshold. Let <math|s<rsub|A>> count the
  radius-<math|B> blocks of its complete read support, including retained
  path lengths in those units. The component itself is estimated in value;
  derivatives fall only on attached smooth factors and the common Gaussian
  density. Fix the orders <math|k,q> and take odd <math|\<ell\>> above the
  stage-data threshold. The factor <math|P> has scale rate zero:
  <math|P<around|(|x|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<epsilon\>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1><rsup|\<epsilon\>>>
  for every <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> once <math|x> is large. Write
  <math|P=P<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q>> and <math|d=d<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q>>; the
  compulsory value bound may also depend on
  <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>>. Directions and sector parameters have
  the restrictions of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Simultaneous local integration><label|lem:B-D2> Admitted molecules
    <math|M> have integrable majorants <math|g<rsub|M>> through order
    <math|k>:

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>g<rsub|M>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>M>f<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|A<rsub|c>=\<varnothing\>>, its seed envelopes satisfy, through
    order <math|k>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|i<math-up|seed>>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>.
    </equation*>

    A component with <math|A<rsub|c>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>> has a value
    majorant satisfying

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>g<rsub|A>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|A>|)><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|i<math-up|attached>>f<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    Simultaneous warnings at <math|r> distinct addresses have probability at
    most <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w><rsup|2*r>>. Disjoint complete read
    supports factorize, including Haar and tag coordinates.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D23>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>, <reference|lem:B4>, <reference|lem:B-D1>,
    and <reference|lem:B-windows>. First retain the exact affine Gaussian
    substitution. For any chart-supported <math|F<rsub|Y>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><math-up|e><rsup|-\<beta\>*Q<rsub|Y><around|(|a|)>><space|0.17em>F<rsub|Y><around|(|a|)>*<space|0.17em>d*a=>|<cell|<around|(|2*\<pi\>*g<rsup|2>|)><rsup|n/2><around|(|det
      \<Gamma\>|)><rsup|1/2><math-up|e><rsup|-\<beta\>*Q<rsub|Y><around|(|m<rsub|Y>|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<times\><big|int>F<rsub|Y>*<around|(|m<rsub|Y>+g*z|)>*exp
      <around*|{|<around|\<langle\>|\<eta\><rsub|Y>,z|\<rangle\>>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|z,<around|(|\<Gamma\><rsup|-1>-<math-ss|A>|)>*z|\<rangle\>>|}>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|z|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<eta\><rsub|Y>=>|<cell|-g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|A>m<rsub|Y>+<math-ss|b>|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Here <math|n> counts active real coordinates; the Haar density and chart
    cutoffs are included in <math|F<rsub|Y>>. Expansion of
    <math|Q<rsub|Y>*<around|(|m<rsub|Y>+g*z|)>> proves the identity,
    including its determinant and residual linear term. The identity holds
    for either window prescription. The local expansion in
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D> uses
    <math|\<Omega\><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>.

    For the common change of coordinates defined above, the first logarithmic
    derivative of the Gaussian density is

    <\equation*>
      -<tfrac|1|2><tr><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<dot\>><rsub|I>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>z<rsub|V><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<dot\>><rsub|I>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|V>-z<rsub|V><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|z|\<dot\>><rsub|V>.
    </equation*>

    Add the logarithmic Jacobian derivative. Repeated differentiation bounds
    every score through order <math|k> by a polynomial in the absolute values
    of the marginal Gaussian coordinates, with coefficients controlled by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> and the fixed smooth chart
    extension. The number of placements is a fixed polynomial in
    <math|1+s<rsub|M>>, since <math|k> is fixed and
    <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*B<rsup|4>*s<rsub|M>>.
    Connectivity and the chosen root bound all distances from <math|o> by
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*B*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)>>, so the spatial
    direction weights give another fixed polynomial. This explicitly includes
    covariance changes before any factors are multiplied.

    The normalized coarse directions have size <math|O<around|(|s|)>> with
    their stated spatial weights. On a fixed chart, derivatives of its
    multiplication, logarithm and bounded extension are bounded. The mean
    derivatives are <math|O<around|(|t|)>>, while <math|s/t> stays bounded.
    In <math|z<rsub|V>> the additional normalization is <math|g<rsup|-1>>.
    Thus the score coefficients are bounded by a fixed power of
    <math|g<rsup|-1>*s>, <math|B> and <math|1+s<rsub|M>>. Since

    <\equation*>
      g<rsup|-1>*s\<leqslant\>C*exp <around|{|<around|(|log log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>|}>,
    </equation*>

    all fixed powers have scale rate zero. The small inverse and stationarity
    residuals in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> have the same property
    after this normalization. The chart-supported square corrections have
    deterministic envelopes: on their support all logarithms are
    <math|O<around|(|t|)>>, the affine remainder is
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsup|2>|)>>, and <math|<around|\||<tfrac|1|2>|\|>*r+e*<around|\||<rsup|2>-<tfrac|1|2>|\|>*r<around|\||<rsup|2>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||r|\|>\|e\|+<around|\||e|\|><rsup|2>/2>
    gives an energy correction <math|O<around|(|\<beta\>*t<rsup|3>|)>>. The
    exponentially summable path coefficients bound the same quantities with
    fixed path-length powers after differentiation. These bounds are included
    in <math|a<rsub|i>>.

    The unbounded exponentials come from the residual Gaussian density and
    its local covariance comparisons. Write their upper exponents as
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>z+z<rsup|T>*K<rsub|i>*z/2>, with
    <math|K<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. At the base field the Gaussian likelihood
    is one; its derivatives are the polynomial scores already estimated. Its
    exponent tends to zero with <math|V-V<rsub|0>>, so on a sufficiently
    small neighbourhood its weighted row sums are at most
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|B>=C<rsub|j,k>*B<rsup|d><math-up|e><rsup|-c*B>>. The
    residual Gaussian density has this bound by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>. The resolvent identity for each
    compressed inverse gives <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|B>> for its local
    quadratic corrections. Each factor's coefficients decay exponentially
    from its own address, and each address has bounded multiplicity. Thus the
    sum at a coordinate is bounded by <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|B>*<big|sum><rsub|y><math-up|e><rsup|-c<math-up|dist><around|(|y,a|)>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*\<delta\><rsub|B>>,
    uniformly in the period and number of factors. Here <math|y> ranges over
    address locations and <math|a> is the coordinate location. In the linear
    terms, weighted Cauchy\USchwarz at each coordinate gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<ell\><rsub|i>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>,<space|2em><around*|\||\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsub|B>.
    </equation*>

    To specify the linear bound, let <math|w<rsub|i*a>> be the sum of the
    absolute path weights contributing to coordinate <math|a> of
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>>; bounded endpoint multiplicity gives
    <math|sup<rsub|a> <big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i*a>\<leqslant\>C>. Put

    <\equation*>
      \<Lambda\><rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|i><around|(|a|)>|\|><rsup|2>|w<rsub|i*a>>+<tr><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K<rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|)>,
    </equation*>

    with zero summand if both numerator and denominator vanish. This quantity
    assigns the common linear exponential bound to the individual factors. To
    see the first bound, use <math|<around|\||<big|sum><rsub|r>x<rsub|r>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|r><around|\||x<rsub|r>|\|><rsup|2>/w<rsub|r>>
    with the absolute path weights <math|w<rsub|r>>; their sums are the
    preceding bounded row sums. The Schur bound for <math|\<Gamma\>>
    completes the estimate. In particular, this bounds
    <math|<around|\||\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*\<ell\><rsub|0>|\|><rsup|2>> with
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<ell\><rsub|i>>, including all
    terms <math|\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\>*\<ell\><rsub|i<rprime|'>>>
    with <math|i\<ne\>i<rprime|'>>. Include <math|exp
    <around|(|C*\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>> in <math|a<rsub|i>>. Put
    <math|K=<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i>>. Since
    <math|0\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K>, each
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K<rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|\<\|\|\>>>
    is at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1/8>
    for large <math|B>, also after the fixed enlargement needed for two
    moments. Diagonalization and square completion, with <math|-log
    <around|(|1-u|)>\<leqslant\>2*u> for <math|0\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>1/2>,
    give

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<Gamma\>><math-up|e><rsup|2*\<ell\><rsub|0>\<cdot\>z+z<rsup|T>*K*z>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|C*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Under this tilt the covariance is at most <math|2*\<Gamma\>> and its mean
    is <math|<around|(|I-2*\<Gamma\>*K|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|2*\<Gamma\>*\<ell\><rsub|0>|)>>.
    Polynomial moments are bounded by the preceding linear estimate;
    increasing the fixed constant in <math|exp
    <around|(|C*\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>> includes their mean contributions.
    Thus the differentiated factors and likelihood have, after removing the
    warning amplitudes, one <math|L<rsup|2>> majorant bounded by
    <math|P<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d>*<big|prod>a<rsub|i>>.

    We next bound the simultaneous warning event defined above, including all
    derivative supports. Let <math|p<rsub|0>> bound the number of Gaussian
    coordinates per coarse site and <math|m<rsub|0>> the number of distinct
    warning addresses per site. These are fixed once the stage and ratio are
    fixed. For <math|r> distinct warnings, choose one violating coordinate
    for each. There are at most <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>|)><rsup|r>>
    choices. A single coordinate can serve at most
    <math|m<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>> warning addresses. The
    Schur bound gives covariance operator norm at most <math|M<rsub|0>>. For
    a set <math|J> of chosen distinct coordinates, Gaussian exponential
    moments and summation over signs give

    <\equation*>
      Pr <around|{|<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>T*<around|(|i\<in\>J|)>|}>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|<around|\||J|\|>>*exp
      <around|{|<around|(|M<rsub|0>/2-T|)><around|\||J|\|>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Take <math|T=\<xi\>*t/<around|(|2*g|)>> and choose <math|H\<gtr\>0> with
    <math|M<rsub|0>/2+H*m<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>\<leqslant\>T>.
    The multiplicity bound gives

    <\equation*>
      Pr <around|(|E<rsub|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|2*p<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|><math-up|e><rsup|-H>|)><rsup|r>.
    </equation*>

    The factor <math|t/g> grows faster than every fixed power of <math|B> and
    <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>. Consequently <math|H> can exceed
    <math|2*\<tau\><rsub|w>*log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>+log
    <around|(|2*p<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>|)>>, proving the
    claimed bound <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w><rsup|2*r>>.

    For the final majorant, take the finite sum of the absolute
    differentiated smooth expressions and scores, multiplied by the fixed old
    measurable envelopes, the warning amplitudes
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|w>M<rsub|w>>, and the simultaneous warning
    indicator. The product rule bounds every derivative through order
    <math|k> by this same nonnegative function. Its integral is bounded by
    its polynomial <math|L<rsup|2>> bound times <math|Pr
    <around|(|E<rsub|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>|)><rsup|1/2>>. This retains
    precisely <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w><rsup|r>> and gives the displayed
    estimate.

    For the seed suppressions, distribute the local energy of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1> among the selected seed factors before
    differentiating. When <math|A<rsub|c>=\<varnothing\>> the admitted
    support is healthy. The physical-link change holds the fine links and
    selected seed indices fixed; differentiated seed cutoffs retain the
    selected-test inequalities on their supports. The exponential energy is
    retained in each differentiated envelope. Annular affine errors have size
    <math|E<around|(|x|)><around|\||A|\|>> with <math|E> of scale rate
    <math|-3/2>, and the remaining per-seed logarithmic corrections have a
    fixed-power bound <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q>*<around|(|1+log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|d<rsub|0>><around|\||A|\|>>.
    Derivatives of the smooth factors through order <math|k> multiply this
    bound by fixed powers of <math|B>, <math|g<rsup|-1>*s> and the seed
    count. Their logarithms per seed are <math|o<around|(|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>|)>>,
    since <math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>> is comparable to
    <math|<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>, which exceeds every
    fixed power of <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>. Also
    <math|E<around|(|x|)>/t<rsup|2>\<to\>0>. Increase the coupling threshold
    after fixing <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>\<less\>\<kappa\>>;
    absorption into <math|<around|(|\<kappa\>-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>|)>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>
    gives the stated product bound through order <math|k>. The support factor
    <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d>> remains outside this per-seed
    estimate. The determinant and covariance corrections use the exponential
    bounds above. Integrating the independent frozen Haar variables and
    summing the finite tag probabilities preserve the bound.

    For the compulsory component retain its full sector masks and all frozen
    links read by it. The lower bound in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>
    gives <math|\<kappa\>*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>> in its local energy. The
    enlarged neighbourhood used by the component adds annular affine
    remainders bounded by <math|E<around|(|x|)><around|\||A|\|>>, where
    <math|E> has scale rate <math|-3/2>: the row error is
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsup|3>|)>>, and the number of positions per seed is a
    fixed power of <math|B>. The chart-density, tag, local determinant and
    normalized frozen-coordinate factors contribute at most
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|d<rsub|0>><around|\||A|\|>> to the
    upper exponent, for a fixed <math|d<rsub|0>>. Indeed, their read counts
    are at most <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*B<rsup|4><around|\||A|\|>>; the
    logarithm of each inverse Gaussian normalization is
    <math|O<around|(|1+log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)>>, the chart
    logarithms are bounded on the protected chart, and the local determinant
    terms have the bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>. The
    retained value envelope is thus at most

    <\equation*>
      exp <space|-0.17em><around*|[|-\<kappa\>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>\|A\|+<around|{|\<beta\>*E<around|(|x|)>+C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|d<rsub|0>>|}><around|\||A|\|>|]>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|\<beta\>> is in the stated band, <math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>> is
    comparable to <math|<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>. Both
    <math|E<around|(|x|)>/t<rsup|2>> and every fixed power of <math|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>> divided by
    <math|<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>> tend to zero. Choose
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|''><rsub|B>> strictly between
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>> and <math|\<kappa\>> and increase the
    coupling threshold. This bounds the component envelope by
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|''><rsub|B>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>>.
    Use this component envelope and the old measurable supremum envelopes.
    The attached factors and Gaussian scores have exactly the
    <math|L<rsup|2>> bound proved above. Cauchy\USchwarz with their warning
    event retains every attached <math|f<rsub|i>>. Polynomial factors in the
    number of component reads are bounded by a fixed polynomial in
    <math|1+s<rsub|A>> times a fixed power of <math|B>, the latter entering
    <math|P>. Any remaining fixed per-seed logarithmic loss is absorbed by
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|''><rsub|B>-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>>. Frozen
    Haar integration has mass one. This proves the compulsory value bound,
    without differentiating its flag masks or taking a supremum of a Gaussian
    coordinate.

    Finally the Gaussian marginal on <math|I> has covariance
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I>>. Every complete read support contains
    <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> for each coordinate <math|i>
    it reads. The windows are symmetric. Hence if <math|i,j> belong to
    disjoint complete read supports, <math|j\<nin\>\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>
    and <math|i\<nin\>\<Omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>, so
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i*j>=0> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window>. The Gaussian
    characteristic function therefore factorizes over these supports. The
    frozen Haar and reference tag coordinates are disjoint as well, proving
    the asserted exact factorization.
  </proof>

  <section|Path actions and the coupling reset><label|sec:partII-C>

  The path action retains curvature terms through fourth order after each
  integration. We choose its quadratic part so that <math|\<beta\>> is the
  coefficient of the squared Wilson curvature at zero momentum at every
  level. Quadratic terms independent of <math|\<beta\>> have two vanishing
  displacement moments. The marginal part generated by the determinant is
  removed from these terms and absorbed into the coupling.

  The path bounds enter Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>, and the coupling
  formula enters Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>. We evaluate the leading
  action at its stationary background and express its cubic and quartic terms
  in transported curvatures. We compare the coupling coefficient of the
  blocked determinant with that of a regulated fine-lattice determinant, with
  an error uniform in the block ratio. The vector and ghost bubbles give the
  logarithmic coefficient; an affine equation bounds the remaining reset.
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C3>\U<reference|lem:C8> carry out these steps.
  The inputs are the branches, free forms, stationary interpolation, Gaussian
  coordinates, vertex, Ward and insertion estimates of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>\U<reference|lem:B4>, <reference|lem:B7>,
  <reference|lem:B5> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B6>.
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, <reference|lem:D4>\U<reference|lem:D6>
  supply the activity bounds, pre-reset expansion, exact density identity and
  continuity. Their proofs belong to Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>; the reset here bounds and identifies the
  correction in that identity.

  Throughout, the group and metric are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, and the scales are those of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>. At a step <math|m+1\<to\>m>
  put <math|\<ell\>=\<ell\><rsub|m>> and <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>. Arrays depend
  on <math|N,m> and the intervening block ratios, and are chosen on
  <math|<Z><rsup|4>> before periodic evaluation. They are independent of the
  volume index<nbsp><math|p> and of the running coupling. Constants declared
  uniform in<nbsp><math|\<ell\>> are chosen before<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

  The arrays are constructed by descending induction on<nbsp><math|m>. Write
  <math|<around|(|I<rsub|m>|)>> for the path bounds and cancellations of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> at level<nbsp><math|m>.
  The top-level Wilson matching in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>
  starts the induction. Under <math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>,
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>, <reference|lem:C6>\U<reference|lem:C7>
  treat the step <math|m+1\<to\>m>; the joint proof after
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> constructs the output arrays and
  proves<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m>|)>>. Curl division and the gauge
  determinant identity are independent of this induction.

  <paragraph|Curvature coordinates.>

  A path transports curvature to an anchor, where invariant colour
  contractions can be taken. Write <math|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>> for the ordered
  link product along<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>,
  <math|\<frak-p\>=<around|(|a,b|)>>, <math|a\<less\>b>, for an orientation,
  and <math|U<rsub|x;\<frak-p\>>> for a plaquette holonomy. The footprint of
  a slot is the set of vertices visited by its path and plaquette boundary.
  The footprint of an atom is the union of its slot footprints and the
  origin, translated to its anchor. Health means disjointness from
  <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>: no plaquette with a vertex in that
  footprint has chord exceeding<nbsp><math|t<rsub|m>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Curvature slots><label|def:D22> A slot
    <math|s=<around|(|\<gamma\>,\<frak-p\>|)>>, with
    <math|\<gamma\>:0\<to\>z<rsub|s>>, has length
    <math|<around|\||\<gamma\>|\|>+4> and value

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-F\><rsub|s>*<around|(|U,x|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|x+\<gamma\>>|)><math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><frac|U<rsub|x+z<rsub|s>;\<frak-p\>>-U<rsub|x+z<rsub|s>;\<frak-p\>><rsup|-1>|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Transport is from <math|x+z<rsub|s>> to<nbsp><math|x>. Set
    <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>>=<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>>.
    For <math|k=2,3,4>, the atoms <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>>
    are real invariant <math|k>-linear expressions in slots, masked by health
    on their footprints. Atom length sums slot lengths;
    <math|<around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|\|>> sums
    absolute colour coefficients. These arrays retain local terms through
    fourth order.
  </definition>

  Here <math|r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>\<in\><R>> and
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>> denotes the slot assigned to
  row<nbsp><math|\<frak-p\>> and index<nbsp><math|i\<in\><N>>. More
  explicitly, in the orthonormal colour basis
  <math|<around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)><rsub|b>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, an atom has the form

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|U,x|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i>+x<space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*<big|sum><rsub|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|k>>c<rsub|i>*<around|(|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|k>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|k><around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|b<rsub|\<nu\>>><rsup|<g>>,\<cal-F\><rsub|s<rsub|i,\<nu\>>>*<around|(|U,x|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The coefficients are invariant under the simultaneous adjoint action on all
  slots. The finite set <math|X<rsub|i>> is the footprint just defined.

  For each stage<nbsp><math|k>, take <math|\<gamma\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> from
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, so the free plaquette forms for the top and
  both incoming stages are at least <math|2*\<gamma\><rsub|k>*I>. Write
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>> and
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>=c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,J<rsub|m>>>. Whenever
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>, the form
  <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*I> is at least
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*I> and has the positive row factor used
  below. In the relaxed health test, <math|<math-up|dist>> is the
  unit-lattice maximum distance on the torus. This test permits a larger
  chord farther from the anchor.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Path action><label|def:D23> Require
    <math|0\<less\>16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>,1|)>>, and put

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|clip><rsub|t><around|(|u|)>>|<cell|=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*u<rsup|2>/2,>|<cell|u\<leqslant\>t,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*u,>|<cell|u\<gtr\>t,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|hlth>><rsub|m,x><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=\<b-1\>*<around|{|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|y;\<frak-p\>>|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>*e<rsup|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>/R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>><text|
      for every >y,\<frak-p\>|}><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Define

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<pathaction>|)><rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>>|<cell|=\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|x,\<frak-p\>><around*|[|<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|m>><around|(|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|x;\<frak-p\>>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|hlth>><rsub|m,x>*<around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*<around|(|U,x|)>|\|><rsup|2>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|x,i><around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|3|)>>+\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>|)>*<around|(|U,x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|x,i>\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|2|)>><around|(|U,x|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<pathaction><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>>|<cell|=<frac|1|<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<varsigma\>\<in\><hypercubic>><around|(|<pathaction>|)><rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,\<varsigma\><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    This separates the kinetic coefficient from loop corrections.
  </definition>

  The sums over <math|x> are over the torus at level<nbsp><math|m>, and
  <math|i\<in\><N>>. The clip has a downward jump immediately to the right
  of<nbsp><math|t>: its value at<nbsp><math|t> is
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*t<rsup|2>/2>, whereas its right limit is
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t<rsup|2>>. For <math|u\<gtr\>t> its linear
  branch gives <math|<math-up|clip><rsub|t><around|(|u|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*u\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t<rsup|2>>,
  the large-field lower bound used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>. The
  bounds below control actual path lengths on every period.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Path bounds><label|lem:C1> For every <math|N> and
    <math|m\<leqslant\>N>, the chosen arrays are independent of
    <math|p,\<beta\>> and satisfy

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>|\|>*e<rsup|w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>><math-up|length><around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|\|>*e<rsup|w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>><math-up|length><around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>,>|<cell|k=2,>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>,>|<cell|k=3,4.>>>>>>>>>
    </align*>

    All series converge. The same bounds hold at
    <math|w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rprime|'>\<gtr\>w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<gtr\>0>
    with <math|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rprime|'>\<geqslant\>A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<gtr\>0>
    replacing <math|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>, uniformly in
    <math|N>. All constants depend only on stage data.
  </lemma>

  The joint proof of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> follows Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>. To state
  the cancellations, put

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>>=<big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em><math-up|orientation><around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>|)>=\<frak-q\>>r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For fixed ordered orientations <math|\<frak-q\>,\<frak-r\>> and colours
  <math|b<rsub|1>,b<rsub|2>>, let <math|c<rsub|i><rsup|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>;b<rsub|1>*b<rsub|2>>>
  be the corresponding coefficient of the quadratic atom, zero unless its
  slots have those orientations. Write <math|z<rsub|i,1>,z<rsub|i,2>> for
  their offsets. The cancellations remove the kinetic component from the loop
  atoms.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Kinetic and quadratic cancellations><label|lem:C2> For each pair
    of orientations <math|\<frak-q\>,\<frak-r\>>,

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-p\>>s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>>*s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-r\>>=\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For each ordered orientation-colour pair,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i><rsup|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>;b<rsub|1>*b<rsub|2>>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i><rsup|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>;b<rsub|1>*b<rsub|2>>*<around|(|z<rsub|i,2>-z<rsub|i,1>|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All these moment series converge absolutely.
  </lemma>

  The joint proof is given after Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>.

  <paragraph|Fourth-order matching.><label|subsec:C-matching>

  Near the flat field all health indicators equal one. By
  <math|<chord><rsup|2>=2<wilsonterm>> from<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-price-chord>,
  the leading analytic action is obtained from
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> by replacing the clipped term by
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>><wilsonterm>> and omitting the indicators and
  loop atoms. Denote this action, without its factor<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, by
  <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|N,m>>. Its prescribed quadratic part is

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*I+\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<rho\><rsub|N,m>,<space|2em><wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsub|N,m><around|(|0|)>=I<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|d<rsub|1>> is the lattice curl. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>
  gives the exact adjacent free relation and the row factor, with
  <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*I\<geqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*I>.
  Placing its coefficients on coordinate paths gives
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>. The cell volume
  contributes <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> and each curvature contributes
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>>; hence <math|\<ell\><rsup|4><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>|)><rsup|2>=1>,
  preserving the coefficient<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>.

  We need a division that expresses a gauge-invariant Taylor difference in
  curvatures. For a momentum<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> put
  <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=e<rsup|i*\<xi\><rsub|a>>-1>; its
  exterior action <math|d<rsub|1><around|(|\<xi\>|)>*a=q<around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<wedge\>a>
  is the curl symbol. Let <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<xi\><rsub|k-1>> be
  independent momenta and <math|\<xi\><rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|k-1>\<xi\><rsub|\<nu\>>>.
  Write <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<nu\>>=\<iota\><rsub|q<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>>>
  for bilinear contraction in leg<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>. Consider periodic
  analytic kernels <math|f> valued in <math|<big|otimes><rsub|\<nu\>=0><rsup|k-1>\<Lambda\><rsup|1><C><rsup|4>>,
  with <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<nu\>>*f=0> in every leg. On the strip
  <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>\<xi\><rsub|\<nu\>><rsup|a>|\|>\<less\>\<sigma\>>,
  assume each coordinate of<nbsp><math|f> is bounded
  by<nbsp><math|A\<geqslant\>0>. Momentum order<nbsp><math|s> means vanishing
  of all Taylor coefficients of total momentum degree less
  than<nbsp><math|s>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Curl division><label|lem:C3> For <math|k\<geqslant\>3> and
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, there are <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>>
    and <math|K\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on <math|k,\<sigma\>>, such
    that every such <math|f> admits a periodic analytic kernel <math|g>
    valued in <math|<big|otimes><rsub|\<nu\>=0><rsup|k-1>\<Lambda\><rsup|2><C><rsup|4>>,
    with

    <\equation*>
      f=\<delta\><rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|k-1>*g,<space|2em>sup<rsub|\<eta\><text|-strip>><around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\>K*A<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its Fourier coefficients decay exponentially. For <math|k=2> the same
    conclusion holds if <math|f> has momentum order <math|s+2>, with <math|g>
    of order<nbsp><math|s>; <math|\<eta\>,K> are independent of
    <math|s\<geqslant\>0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We give the division for a general transverse kernel. Scalar division
    requires vanishing when the divided leg has zero momentum; positive
    exterior degree requires only closedness. For one leg let
    <math|\<delta\>=\<iota\><rsub|q>> be contraction on exterior tensors.
    Split a <math|\<delta\>>-closed tensor as <math|f=e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>u+v>,
    with <math|u,v> containing no <math|e<rsub|a>>. Define

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|a>*f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<frac|f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>-f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>\|<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|a>=0>|q<rsub|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>,<space|2em>h<rsub|a>*f=e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>D<rsub|a>*v,<space|2em>R<rsub|a>*f=v\|<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|a>=0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The identity <math|\<delta\>*f=0> gives
    <math|\<delta\><rprime|'>*v=-q<rsub|a>*u>, where
    <math|\<delta\><rprime|'>> contracts the other axes. Since
    <math|q<rsub|b>> is independent of <math|\<xi\><rsub|a>> for
    <math|b\<ne\>a>, <math|D<rsub|a>> commutes
    with<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rprime|'>>, and
    <math|D<rsub|a>*<around|(|q<rsub|a>*u|)>=u>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\>*h<rsub|a>*f=q<rsub|a>*D<rsub|a>*v-e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>D<rsub|a>*\<delta\><rprime|'>*v=v-v\|<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|a>=0>+e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>u=f-R<rsub|a>*f<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The remainder <math|R<rsub|a>*f> is again closed and has no component
    containing<nbsp><math|e<rsub|a>>. Applying this in the four axes gives
    the homotopy

    <\equation*>
      f=\<delta\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>h<rsub|a>*R<rsub|a-1>*\<cdots\>*R<rsub|1>*f|)>+R<rsub|4>*R<rsub|3>*R<rsub|2>*R<rsub|1>*f<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The final remainder vanishes in positive exterior degree because it
    contains none of the four axes. In degree zero it is the restriction to
    zero momentum of this leg, which must vanish for scalar division. In that
    case the formula reads <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a>*D<rsub|a>*<around|(|R<rsub|a-1>*\<cdots\>*R<rsub|1>*f|)>>.

    The quotients are analytic even at the zeros of<nbsp><math|q<rsub|a>>. On
    a Fourier monomial the identity

    <\equation*>
      <frac|e<rsup|i*n*\<xi\><rsub|a>>-1|e<rsup|i*\<xi\><rsub|a>>-1>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|v=0><rsup|n-1>e<rsup|i*v*\<xi\><rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<gtr\>0,>>|<row|<cell|-<big|sum><rsub|v=n><rsup|-1>e<rsup|i*v*\<xi\><rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<less\>0,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|n=0>>>>>
    </equation*>

    bounds the quotient on a smaller strip by a constant times
    <math|<around|\||n|\|>> times its original exponential weight. The strip
    loss absorbs <math|<around|\||n|\|>>, so the Fourier series converges
    absolutely. Each division loses at most one momentum order.

    For independent legs, their remaining transverse subspaces do not depend
    on the divided momentum, so each restriction and division preserves them.
    The dependent-leg induction has the following claim: with <math|r>
    independent momenta and dependent exterior degree <math|d\<geqslant\>1>,
    a tensor closed in the dependent leg and transverse in all independent
    legs has a dependent-leg primitive preserving those transverse
    conditions, provided <math|d+2*r\<gtr\>4>. Analytic bounds hold after a
    strip reduction depending only on <math|r,d> and the original strip. For
    <math|r=0> the condition gives <math|d\<gtr\>4>, so the tensor is zero.
    The induction step below reduces <math|<around|(|r,d|)>> to
    <math|<around|(|r-1,d+2|)>>, preserving the condition. Initially
    <math|r=k-1,d=1>, so it applies for <math|k\<geqslant\>3>.

    For the dependent momentum <math|\<xi\><rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k-1>\<xi\><rsub|i>>,
    first shear the coordinates to replace <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>>
    by<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|0>>. The other independent contractions remain
    unchanged. The one-leg formula gives <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*X=f>,
    retaining the contractions in legs <math|2,\<ldots\>,k-1>. Write
    <math|c<rsub|1>> for contraction in leg<nbsp><math|1>. Its defect obeys
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|c<rsub|1>*X|)>=c<rsub|1>*f=0>. Solve
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Y=c<rsub|1>*X> by the same formula. After
    <math|c<rsub|1>>, leg<nbsp><math|1> has exterior degree zero, so
    restoring that leg will require only scalar division.

    On <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>=0>, the restriction <math|Y<rsub|0>> is
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>>-closed, since <math|c<rsub|1>*X> vanishes there.
    The induction hypothesis with one fewer independent momentum and
    dependent degree two higher gives <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Z=Y<rsub|0>>,
    preserving the other transverse conditions. Extend<nbsp><math|Z>
    independently of<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|1>>. Then
    <math|Y<rprime|'>=Y-\<delta\><rsub|0>*Z> vanishes on
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>=0>, and scalar division gives
    <math|c<rsub|1>*D=Y<rprime|'>>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      V=X-\<delta\><rsub|0>*D,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|0>*V=f,<space|2em>c<rsub|1>*V=c<rsub|1>*X-\<delta\><rsub|0>*Y<rprime|'>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All corrections preserve the other independent contractions. The two
    successive solves <math|f\<mapsto\>X> and <math|c<rsub|1>*X\<mapsto\>Y>
    each raise the dependent exterior degree by one; contraction
    <math|c<rsub|1>> changes only leg<nbsp><math|1>. Thus the restricted
    problem for <math|Y<rsub|0>> has one fewer independent momentum and
    dependent degree two higher. For three or more legs it terminates above
    exterior degree four, where the tensor is zero. The independent-leg
    divisions then finish the factorization.

    For two legs, the restriction <math|Y<rsub|0>> is at zero external
    momentum. Starting from order <math|s+2>, the first division loses one
    order, contraction restores one, and the solve for<nbsp><math|Y> leaves
    order <math|s+1\<geqslant\>1>. Hence <math|Y<rsub|0>=0> directly. The
    correction and final division leave order<nbsp><math|s>. This verifies
    the scalar vanishing hypothesis where it is needed. For the Taylor
    differences of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, gauge invariance
    supplies transversality because all lower Taylor differences vanish.
  </proof>

  For <math|z=<around|(|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|k-1>|)>>, contour
  displacement gives <math|<around|\||K<around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-\<zeta\>*<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|><rsub|1>>>.
  Choose the anchor at zero and coordinate paths
  to<nbsp><math|z<rsub|\<nu\>>>. Their total slot length is at most
  <math|4*k+<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  Every path width below<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>> therefore gives a convergent
  weighted sum. Reality and simultaneous adjoint invariance pass to the
  coefficients by conjugation and invariant projection.

  At a step, let <math|v> be the coarse chart variable and <math|z> the fine
  axial variable. Let <math|S*<around|(|v,z|)>> be the fine leading action
  plus <math|<blockpenalty><big|sum><rsub|e><wilsonterm>> of the predictor
  discrepancy, without<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>. It excludes the
  inherited loop action. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> supplies

  <\equation*>
    D<rsub|z>*S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>=0,<space|2em>z<around|(|0|)>=0,<space|2em><math-ss|Q>=D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|0,0|)>\<geqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>*I,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>\<gtr\>0<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Put <math|W*v=<around|(|v,D*z<around|(|0|)>*v|)>> and
  <math|<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><around|[|u|]>=D<rsup|3>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|W*v,W*v,<around|(|0,u|)>|]>>.
  For the bounds use the joint fine-link and centered-frame vertices of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>, before choosing an axial representative. Let
  <math|C<rsub|3>,C<rsub|4>> bound their scalar Fourier entries on the fixed
  smaller strip, both on normalized smooth coarse legs and with one arbitrary
  fine leg of block norm at most one. In particular, the mixed cubic
  functional has dual block norm at most<nbsp><math|C<rsub|3>>. Take
  <math|E=max <around|(|1,C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>> from the
  zeroth-order dual-to-primal bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>. The
  mixed functionals annihilate joint gauge directions, so their pairing
  through this inverse equals their axial pairing
  through<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Q><rsup|-1>>, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>.
  Thus <math|E> is independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>> and of the input path
  amplitude. The constants <math|a<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>> depend only on
  preceding stage data; <math|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>> may
  also depend on the input path bound.

  The next formula constructs the higher arrays while keeping their bound
  independent of the input amplitude after the block ratio has been enlarged.
  The stationary identities hold for every admitted ratio; only the uniform
  array bound requires a larger ratio. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5> gives
  <math|C<rsub|3>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|3>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|3><rprime|'>>
  and <math|C<rsub|4>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|4>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|4><rprime|'>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Classical matching><label|lem:C4> The stationary action
    <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|cl>><around|(|v|)>=S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>>
    satisfies

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsup|3><math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|cl>><around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsup|3>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|3>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|W*v|)><rsup|3>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|D<rsup|4><math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|cl>><around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsup|4>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|4>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|W*v|)><rsup|4>|]>-3<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><math-ss|Q><rsup|-1><math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Its factorial-normalized kernels have bounds <math|C<rsub|3>/6> and
    <math|<around|(|C<rsub|4>+3*C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E|)>/24>. For
    <math|\<ell\>> above a threshold depending on the input path data, cubic
    and quartic arrays satisfying the bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>
    make <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|N,m>> agree with
    <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|cl>>> through degree four. Their bound
    depends only on fixed stage data. The degree-<math|r> fine-minus-Wilson
    vertex has momentum order <math|5-r>, for <math|r=2,3,4>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the block map of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>, <reference|lem:B5>. Differentiating
    <math|D<rsub|z>*S=0> once gives <math|D<rsup|2>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|W*v,<around|(|0,u|)>|]>=0>;
    differentiating twice gives <math|D<rsup|2>*z<around|(|0|)><around|[|v,v|]>=-<math-ss|Q><rsup|-1><math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)>>.
    In the third derivative of <math|S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>>, the
    terms containing <math|D<rsup|2>*z> or <math|D<rsup|3>*z> vanish by these
    two stationary identities. In the fourth derivative the surviving terms
    involving <math|w=D<rsup|2>*z<around|(|0|)><around|[|v,v|]>> are

    <\equation*>
      6<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><around|[|w|]>+3*<around|\<langle\>|w,<math-ss|Q>w|\<rangle\>>=-3<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><math-ss|Q><rsup|-1><math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The other term is <math|D<rsup|4>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|W*v|)><rsup|4>|]>>.
    In the joint coordinates of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>, the cubic and
    quartic stationary kernels have the same identities with smooth
    interpolated legs. Each mixed cubic functional has dual block norm at
    most<nbsp><math|C<rsub|3>>, and the completed inverse has dual-to-primal
    norm at most<nbsp><math|E>. Their pairing is therefore bounded
    by<nbsp><math|C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E>. There are three pairings in the
    symmetric fourth derivative. This gives the kernel estimates after
    division by <math|3>! and<nbsp><math|4>!.

    The fine-minus-Wilson vertices have orders <math|3,2,1> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>. The quadratic order uses
    <math|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><around|(|0|)>=I> and two curls. Differentiated
    gauge invariance then gives the higher orders, including nonlinear
    curvature transport. Wilson has orders <math|2,1,0>: the extra power
    becomes <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>> under free interpolation and rescaling.
    The Wilson vertices and penalty give <math|a<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>>,
    independently of the input atom bound; the differences give
    <math|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>>.

    Increase <math|\<ell\>> until <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,\<ell\><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|4><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3> to the cubic difference between
    <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|cl>>> and the output row action. Subtract
    the resulting full nonlinear cubic atoms before treating degree four. The
    remaining quartic difference is transverse, and a second application
    gives quartic atoms with a fixed bound. This makes the two actions agree
    through degree four. At <math|m=N>, the free form acts as the identity on
    curls, so the same construction matches the Wilson energy through degree
    four and starts with zero loop atoms. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>
    places the remaining top-level difference, including clipping and
    unhealthy configurations, in the initial activities.
  </proof>

  The common factor<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>> in the penalty and fine
  leading action cancels from the stationary equation; hence the arrays are
  independent of<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>. In <math|log det
  <around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'>*M|)>>, the term <math|<around|(|dim M|)>*log
  \<beta\><rprime|'>> is independent of the field and contributes only to the
  vacuum scalar.

  <paragraph|The determinant comparison.><label|subsec:C-determinant>

  The Gaussian change of coordinates in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4> gives
  the one-loop action

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det M<around|(|v|)>-log J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|U<rsub|v>> is the stationary fine configuration,
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1>> is the inherited quadratic loop action, <math|M>
  is the axial Hessian without<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>, and
  <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>> is the Haar chart ratio, normalized to one at
  zero.

  For a quadratic Taylor kernel <math|Y>, including the Taylor
  factor<nbsp><math|1/2>!, define

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*g<around|(|t|)>=<tfrac|1|2>g<rprime|''><around|(|0|)>,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y|)>=2<math-up|Re><around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*Y<rsub|<around|(|2,b|)>,<around|(|2,b|)>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>=\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsup|<math-up|blk>><rsub|N,m>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The kernel <math|Y<rsup|<math-up|blk>><rsub|N,m>> is the infinite-volume
  quadratic kernel of <math|<math-ss|T>>. The prescribed kinetic kernel has
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>=1>. Simplicity makes <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>
  independent of<nbsp><math|b>, as shown in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>; by construction it is independent
  of<nbsp><math|p> and<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>.

  Let <math|<math-ss|J>> be the left-coordinate Hessian of the joint fine
  action and block penalty, including the coarse frame. Let <math|R> insert
  infinitesimal gauge transformations and <math|Q> the axial slice. For
  <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>, put

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|d<rsub|U>*\<phi\>|)><rsub|e>=\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|->|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|e>|)>*\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|+>|)>,<space|2em>\<Delta\><rsub|U>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>,<space|2em>\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|[|\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>|]><rsup|9><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For the rooted frame <math|\<Psi\>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4>,
  define

  <\equation*>
    X=D<rsub|w=0>*<around*|[|-log <around*|(|\<Psi\>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)><rsup|-1>*\<Psi\>*<around|(|e<rsup|w>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>|)>|)>|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Frame covariance gives <math|X*R=I>. With <math|P<rsub|f>> the projection
  to fine links, set

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|\<mu\>>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X,<space|2em><math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>=<math-ss|J>+F<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|\<mu\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The completed Hessian has the following local determinant identity.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Gauge completion><label|lem:C5> For <math|v> in a neighbourhood of
    zero,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-FP><math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
      det <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>-log det
      <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D04> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>\U<reference|lem:B4>. Gauge invariance and
    stationarity give <math|<math-ss|J>R=0>. Ordering the axial tree by
    height makes the gauge block triangular with diagonal entries
    <math|\<pm\>1>, while the slice block is the identity; hence <math|det
    <around|(|R,Q|)><rsup|2>=1>. Since <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*R=<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>,
    in these columns the completed form is

    <\equation*>
      <matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>|<cell|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*Q>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>><rsup|<space|-0.17em>\<ast\>><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|Q<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>Q>>>>><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>|<cell|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*Q>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|det <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|(|det
    <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|2>*det
    <around|(|Q<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>Q|)>>. If <math|s> changes axial
    coordinates to left increments at the stationary point, then

    <\equation*>
      det M=<around|(|det D*s|)><rsup|2>*det
      <around|(|Q<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>Q|)>,<space|2em>J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>=<around|\||det
      D*s|\|><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The second equality follows by comparing the two Haar densities.
    Positivity of <math|M>, <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>> and
    <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>> near zero permits logarithms and
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-FP>.
  </proof>

  The auxiliary fine determinant used for comparison is

  <\equation*>
    \<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>=<tfrac|1|2>log det
    <around|(|<math-ss|A><around|(|U|)>+d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>+<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>|)>-log
    det <around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<math-ss|A>> is the left-coordinate Hessian of the fine leading
  action and <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>> applies
  <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>> to each link component. Let
  <math|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>> be its infinite-volume quadratic Taylor kernel. The
  following comparison controls the coupling coefficient. Write
  <math|u<rsub|j>> for the ratio threshold of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>;
  rows<nbsp>5 and<nbsp>8 of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> choose odd
  ratios above it.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Block-to-fine coefficient comparison><label|lem:C6> For every odd
    <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>> as in rows<nbsp>5 and<nbsp>8 of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, put
    <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>. There is a constant
    <math|d<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on the fixed
    stage bounds, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>-\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|<math-up|cmp>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The constant is independent of <math|N,p,\<ell\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>, <reference|lem:C5> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>, <reference|lem:B5>, <reference|lem:B6>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>. The input arrays satisfy
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> by<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>.
    The full completed Hessian contains a contact term: the second derivative
    of the predictor multiplied by the first derivative of the penalty. Along
    the free interpolation, write <math|<math-ss|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>> for
    this penalty derivative and <math|<math-ss|b><around|(|\<xi\>|)>> for the
    mixed fine/frame trace. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B6> gives
    <math|<math-ss|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>|)>>
    and <math|<math-ss|b><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>|)>>.
    The change in the tested entry on removing the contact term consists of
    <math|<math-ss|ab>>, its reflected counterpart, and a term quadratic
    in<nbsp><math|<math-ss|a>>, with bounded analytic factors. It is
    <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|3>|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>>
    and has zero<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>>.

    First put both operators on the same space. At one coarse momentum let
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|f>> be the fine-link fibre and
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|0>> the coarse-frame fibre, of
    dimension<nbsp><math|<dimG>>. Let <math|P:\<cal-V\><rsub|f>\<oplus\>\<cal-V\><rsub|0>\<to\>\<cal-V\><rsub|f>>
    and <math|Z:\<cal-V\><rsub|f>\<oplus\>\<cal-V\><rsub|0>\<to\>\<cal-V\><rsub|0>>
    be the coordinate projections. With <math|A<rsub|0>=<math-ss|A>+d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>+<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>>,
    set

    <\equation*>
      <wide|A|~><rsub|0>=P<rsup|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|0>*P+Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|0>>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>>,<space|2em>det
      <wide|A|~><rsub|0>=det A<rsub|0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Thus adjoining the frame identity leaves the auxiliary determinant
    unchanged. Let <math|A<rsub|1>> be the contact-removed
    <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>> on this joint space. The frame part
    enters the comparison only through the joint-space difference
    <math|\<Theta\>=A<rsub|1>-<wide|A|~><rsub|0>> below.

    Let <math|H> be the differential of the logarithm of the framed predictor
    discrepancy, and <math|B> its coarse-link penalty Hessian. These are the
    two factors in the second-derivative chain rule after removal of the
    contact term. Put <math|E=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P> and
    <math|K=\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X>. Let <math|J<rsub|a>> select
    direction<nbsp><math|a> from fine links, so
    <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>=<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*J<rsub|a>>.
    The two joint operators are

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsub|1>>|<cell|=P<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|A>P+H<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*H+<around|(|E+K|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|E+K|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<wide|A|~><rsub|0>>|<cell|=P<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|A>P+E<rsup|\<ast\>>*E+P<rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*J<rsub|a>|)>*P+Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z.>>>>
    </align*>

    Consequently their difference is the identity

    <\equation*>
      \<Theta\>=A<rsub|1>-<wide|A|~><rsub|0>=H<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*H-Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z+E<rsup|\<ast\>>*K+K<rsup|\<ast\>>*E+K<rsup|\<ast\>>*K-P<rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*J<rsub|a>|)>*P<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The fine Hessians cancel exactly. The first term factors through coarse
    links, the second through coarse frames. For compatible maps <math|V,W>
    through a coarse space of dimension<nbsp><math|r>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|V*W|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>r<around|\<\|\|\>|V|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em><around|\||<tr><rsub|<math-up|joint>><around|(|V*W|)>|\|>=<around|\||<tr><rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|W*V|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|V|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|r=4<dimG>> or <math|<dimG>>, independently
    of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>. Differentiating these factorizations through
    order two preserves the intermediate coarse spaces.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7> bounds their factors in the mutually dual
    block norms.

    The remaining terms keep a regulator factor. Put
    <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>.
    At zero background its eigenvalues at the fine aliases
    <math|k<rsub|z>=<around|(|\<xi\>+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/\<ell\>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|<around|\||q<around|(|k<rsub|z>|)>|\|><rsup|2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\><frac|C|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C*<big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>><around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-3>\<less\>\<infty\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>2<around|\||k|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>/\<pi\>>
    on the fine momentum cube and <math|<around|\||\<xi\>+2*\<pi\>*z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>\<pi\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    for centered aliases and real <math|\<xi\>\<in\><around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7> gives the same trace bound on a smaller
    complex strip and bounds <math|D<rsup|r>*P<rsub|\<mu\>>> for
    <math|r\<leqslant\>2>. Every regulator is
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9>=\<mu\><rsup|2><around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>.
    Two field derivatives leave one cube undifferentiated. Put that cube in
    trace norm and the other factors in operator norm. The bounds on
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*D<rsup|r><around|(|A<rsup|-1>|)>> in that lemma
    cancel the inverse scale before taking a trace. Applied to the displayed
    factors of<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>>, these estimates give a
    constant<nbsp><math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>> for its trace norm through two field
    insertions, in the dual block norms. It depends only on the fixed stage
    bounds. At each coarse momentum these bounds sum the fine aliases without
    a factor<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|4>>.

    For the logarithmic determinant, write
    <math|R<rsub|1>=A<rsub|1><rsup|-1>> and
    <math|R<rsub|0>=<wide|A|~><rsub|0><rsup|-1>>. The inverse identity is

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|1>-R<rsub|0>=-R<rsub|1>*\<Theta\>*R<rsub|0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|F<rsub|\<ast\>>> bound the inverses in the compatible block
    norms, and let <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>> bound the two operators through two
    normalized field insertions, as supplied by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>
    and the vertex bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>. Set
    <math|D<rsub|\<ast\>>=F<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>*B<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
    <math|E<rsub|\<ast\>>=D<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>+F<rsub|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    For normalized directions <math|v,w>, the logarithmic Hessian is

    <\equation*>
      <tr><around*|(|R<rsub|i>*D<rsub|v*w>*A<rsub|i>-R<rsub|i>*D<rsub|v>*A<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i>*D<rsub|w>*A<rsub|i>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The difference of the first products has trace norm at
    most<nbsp><math|E<rsub|\<ast\>>>. For the second, first subtract
    <math|R<rsub|1>*D<rsub|v>*A<rsub|1>-R<rsub|0>*D<rsub|v>*A<rsub|0>>, then
    the remaining inverse, then <math|D<rsub|w>*A<rsub|1>-D<rsub|w>*A<rsub|0>>.
    This bounds the whole logarithmic-Hessian difference:

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||D<rsub|v>*D<rsub|w>*<around*|(|log det A<rsub|1>-log det
      <wide|A|~><rsub|0>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|\<ast\>>+E<rsub|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>+F<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|\<ast\>>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>+F<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>*B<rsub|\<ast\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All products use one trace-norm factor and bounded operator factors;
    cyclically moving factors identifies the required dual norms. The common
    ghost determinant cancels. Including the Taylor factors gives a
    bound<nbsp><math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> for the analytic difference kernel on a
    strip of width<nbsp><math|\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>, both
    independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>. The inherited loop kernel has order
    four by<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>; pullback by the analytic
    <math|W<rsub|0>> preserves that order, so its <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> is
    zero. The contact term has already been treated above.

    Restrict the tested entry <math|g> of the difference to
    <math|\<xi\>=t*e<rsub|1>>. Cauchy's coefficient inequality on
    <math|<around|\||t|\|>=\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>>/2> gives
    <math|<around|\||<around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*g|\|>\<leqslant\>4*C<rsub|\<ast\>>/\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>>
    and hence <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>8*C<rsub|\<ast\>>/\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>>.
    This gives<nbsp><math|d<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>>.

    Finally, folding the auxiliary operator into coarse momentum does not
    alter<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>>. The symbol of the weights
    <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>> in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-predictor> vanishes at
    every nonzero alias to order at least two in each external leg. These
    aliases contribute <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>>.
    The zero alias in the tested entry is

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsup|4>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*Y<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|\<xi\>/\<ell\>|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|3>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The factors are the cell volume and <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>> per
    interpolated field. Extracting the quadratic coefficient supplies
    another<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>>, so
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>=1>. This proves
    the comparison.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The logarithmic coefficient.><label|subsec:C-coefficient>

  The vertex comparison in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> gives
  momentum orders <math|3,2,1> for the quadratic, cubic and quartic
  differences from Wilson. The input arrays satisfy its hypotheses
  by<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>. The resulting logarithmic term is
  the part of the one-step drift which remains after the block ratio has been
  chosen.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One-loop coefficient><label|lem:C7> There is a constant
    <math|d<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on the fixed stage data,
    such that, for every step <math|m+1\<to\>m> at stage<nbsp><math|j> with
    odd <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>> as in
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|j><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It is independent of <math|N,p,\<ell\><rsub|m>> and the running coupling.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>,
    <reference|lem:C6> and the vertex orders just described. Choose a colour
    basis index <math|b>, external polarization<nbsp><math|e<rsub|2>> and
    external momentum <math|t*e<rsub|1>>. Signed permutations reduce other
    transverse axis pairs to this one. For nonzero loop momentum <math|k>,
    write <math|\<varkappa\>=<around|\||k|\|>>. The leading ghost vertex is
    <math|-2*i*k<rsub|2><math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)>>. The
    vector vertex has the same orbital term on its four components and the
    spin term

    <\equation*>
      2*i*t*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|i*2>*\<delta\><rsub|j*1>-\<delta\><rsub|i*1>*\<delta\><rsub|j*2>|)><math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Simplicity and the trace metric give <math|<tr><rsub|<g>><around|(|<math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)><rsup|2>|)>=-<casimir>>.
    The bubble pairs vertices at <math|t*e<rsub|1>> and <math|-t*e<rsub|1>>.
    The orbital factor <math|-2*i*k<rsub|2>> is unchanged when
    <math|k\<mapsto\>k+t*e<rsub|1>> and <math|t*e<rsub|1>\<mapsto\>-t*e<rsub|1>>.
    Put <math|S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>=E<rsub|21>-E<rsub|12>>. Since
    <math|<tr><space|0.17em>S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>=0>, the orbital\Uspin cross
    terms vanish; the spin product is <math|<around|(|2*i*t*S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>|)>*<around|(|-2*i*t*S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>|)>=4*t<rsup|2>*S<rsub|<math-up|sp>><rsup|2>>,
    with trace <math|-8*t<rsup|2>>. The directional traces of the paired
    vertices are therefore <math|4*<around|(|-2*i*k<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>-8*t<rsup|2>>
    for the vector and <math|<around|(|-2*i*k<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>> for the
    ghost. Include the colour trace, divide by
    <math|\<varkappa\><rsup|2>*<around|\||k+t*e<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|2>> and take
    the coefficient of<nbsp><math|t<rsup|2>>. In <math|<tfrac|1|2>log det
    <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>-log det <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>, the bubble
    terms have weights <math|-1/4> for the vector and <math|+1/2> for the
    ghost. The extraction of <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> supplies the
    factor<nbsp><math|2>. Before taking the coefficient, ghost minus half
    vector is

    <\equation*>
      -<frac|4<casimir><around|(|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>+t<rsup|2>|)>|\<varkappa\><rsup|2>*<around|\||k+t*e<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|2>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its quadratic coefficient is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-pole>h<around|(|k|)>=4<casimir><around*|(|<frac|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>|\<varkappa\><rsup|6>>-<frac|4*k<rsub|2><rsup|2>*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>|\<varkappa\><rsup|8>>-<frac|1|\<varkappa\><rsup|4>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Expanding the denominator gives <math|\<varkappa\><rsup|-4>-2*t*k<rsub|1>*\<varkappa\><rsup|-6>+t<rsup|2>*<around|(|4*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>*\<varkappa\><rsup|-8>-\<varkappa\><rsup|-6>|)>+O<around|(|t<rsup|3>|)>>;
    substitution yields<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-pole> with its sign.

    Let <math|k<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>> be the integrand of
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>, obtained with these
    weights and Taylor factors. Then

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>k<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>*<space|0.17em>d*k<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For constants <math|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>,D<rsub|<math-up|int>>\<geqslant\>0>
    depending only on the fixed fine-stage bounds, uniformly in
    <math|\<mu\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-integrand><around|\||k<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>|<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|4>>,<space|2em><around|\||k<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>-h<around|(|k|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|<math-up|int>>*<around*|(|\<varkappa\><rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<varkappa\><rsup|-6>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<varkappa\>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    To obtain them, use <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>=i*k<rsub|a>+O<around|(|\<varkappa\><rsup|2>|)>>
    and the vertex orders above. For the free inverse
    <math|R<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>>, differentiation of
    <math|R<rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>=I> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\>*R<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-3>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|2>*R<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each first-order vertex has order <math|\<varkappa\>>, with bounded first
    external derivative. In a bubble, two momentum derivatives on one inverse
    give <math|\<varkappa\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2>*<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>>;
    moving derivatives to vertices gives the same bound. On
    <math|\<varkappa\>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>, a lattice-to-leading replacement
    gains one power of<nbsp><math|\<varkappa\>>, giving
    <math|C*\<varkappa\><rsup|-3>>. A regulator insertion contributes at most
    <math|C*\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<varkappa\><rsup|-6>>.

    The terms with one second-order vertex are absent from<nbsp><math|h>.
    Their quadratic coefficients have bounds

    <\equation*>
      C*<around*|(|<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-6>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first term comes from one inverse and a bounded second-order vertex
    derivative; the second retains the regulator
    factor<nbsp><math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> with the differentiated inverse bounds.
    On the cube, <math|\<varkappa\>\<leqslant\>2*\<pi\>> and
    <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>, so this is at most
    <math|C<rprime|'>*<around|(|\<varkappa\>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>>. On
    <math|\<varkappa\>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>, it is at most
    <math|C*<around|(|2*\<pi\>*\<varkappa\><rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<varkappa\><rsup|-6>|)>>.
    Thus these terms satisfy both inequalities
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand>. The product rule gives the same bounds
    for the ninth-power regulator. Summing
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand>.

    On the spherical shell <math|\<mu\>\<less\>\<varkappa\>\<leqslant\>1>,
    rotational symmetry gives angular means
    <math|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>/\<varkappa\><rsup|2>=1/4> and
    <math|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>/\<varkappa\><rsup|4>=1/24>. For
    the latter, write the fourth moments as
    <math|a=\<bbb-E\>*\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|4>> and
    <math|b=\<bbb-E\>*\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|2>*\<theta\><rsub|2><rsup|2>> on
    the unit sphere: rotation by <math|\<pi\>/4> gives <math|a=3*b>, and
    <math|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<theta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>=1>
    gives <math|4*a+12*b=1>. Since the sphere has area
    <math|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\>\<varkappa\>\<leqslant\>1>h<around|(|k|)>*<space|0.17em>d*k>|<cell|=<frac|8*\<pi\><rsup|2><casimir>|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>>*<around*|(|<frac|1|4>-<frac|1|6>-1|)>*log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1><eq-number><label|eq:C-slope>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=-<frac|11<casimir>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1>=-<slope>log \<mu\><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align>

    The errors in<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand> have shell integrals at most
    <math|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*D<rsub|<math-up|int>>> and
    <math|\<pi\><rsup|2>*D<rsub|<math-up|int>>>. On
    <math|\<varkappa\>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>> the bound is
    <math|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>*\<mu\><rsup|-4>> and the volume is
    <math|\<pi\><rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsup|4>/2>; outside
    <math|\<varkappa\>\<leqslant\>1> the bound is
    <math|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>+<slope>log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<around|{|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>*\<pi\><rsup|2>/2+A<rsub|<math-up|int>>*<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>+3*\<pi\><rsup|2>*D<rsub|<math-up|int>>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Combine this bound with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C6> and put
    <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>. Their sum gives<nbsp><math|d<rsub|j>>.
    The computed slope is <math|<slope>=2*b<rsub|0>> of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, with its stated trace metric.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D04>,
    <reference|def:D22>\U<reference|def:D23>, the free forms of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C3>,
    <reference|lem:C4>, <reference|lem:C7>. Start at <math|m=N> and descend.
    The condition <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,k>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|k>>
    supplies the positive row factor at every level. Its zero-momentum
    entries give

    <\equation*>
      <wide|<math-ss|H>|^><around|(|0|)><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-p\>>s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>>*s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-r\>>=\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This proves the first cancellation and also forces
    <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>1> by taking a diagonal
    entry.

    Subtract <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> times the kinetic kernel from the
    quadratic kernel of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>>. The residual is transverse.
    Its colour bilinear form is scalar: its self-adjoint representing
    operator commutes with the adjoint action, and its eigenspaces are
    ideals, so simplicity leaves one eigenvalue. Signed permutations identify
    the diagonal orientation coefficients. Reflection in an axis belonging to
    exactly one of two distinct orientations cancels the off-diagonal
    coefficient. Thus there is one degree-two coefficient, removed by the
    subtraction. Reflection through the origin removes degree three. The
    residual link kernel consequently vanishes to order four.

    Apply the two-leg part of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3>. Removing its two
    curl symbols leaves a plaquette kernel of order two. If its Fourier
    coefficients for a fixed orientation-colour pair are
    <math|a<rsub|<math-up|loop>><around|(|z|)>>, then

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z>a<rsub|<math-up|loop>><around|(|z|)>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|z>z<rsub|a>*a<rsub|<math-up|loop>><around|(|z|)>=0*<space|1em><around|(|a=1,\<ldots\>,4|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Exponential decay permits termwise differentiation. Put the coefficients
    on coordinate paths to obtain the quadratic atoms and their absolutely
    convergent moments. Inherited loop atoms already have these moments,
    hence a link kernel of order four. Pullback by the free interpolation
    preserves this order. Only the quadratic Taylor kernel
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>> is assigned to these atoms; its higher terms
    enter the preparatory expansion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>.

    For the bounds, contour displacement and the path-length estimate after
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3> give a positive common decay rate for the
    top construction and the two possible incoming stages <math|k,k+1>.
    Choose <math|0\<less\>w<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<less\>w<rsub|k><rprime|'>>
    below that rate, and bound the series at <math|w<rsub|k><rprime|'>> when
    choosing <math|A<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|path>>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> permits this choice before the
    corresponding ratios, by making the input-dependent terms at most one.
    Put <math|A<rsub|k><rprime|'>=A<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|path>>>; lowering
    the width gives the stated bounds at <math|w<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|path>>>.
    Make the following loop estimates at <math|w<rsub|k><rprime|'>> as well,
    so that the quadratic bound retains the same strict inequality between
    widths.

    The new loop contribution has a weighted bound
    <math|a<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*\<ell\><rsup|4>+b<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*log
    \<ell\>+c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>>. Here <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> bounds
    the trace dimension per coarse cell, and the logarithm comes from the
    subtracted coefficient. For an inherited order-four link kernel,
    rescaling gives

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsup|4>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>/\<ell\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>=O<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2><around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|4>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its weighted pullback is therefore bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pb>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>.
    Choose <math|\<ell\>> so that <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pb>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1/4>,
    then raise the coupling entry threshold until

    <\equation*>
      2*<around|(|a<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*\<ell\><rsup|4>+b<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*log
      \<ell\>+c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>|)>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>\<leqslant\>2*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The sum is at most <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>+<tfrac|1|4>\<cdot\>2*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>.
    These choices are in the order of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>:
    widths and amplitudes, ratios, then entry thresholds. The first
    inequality is possible because <math|2<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>\<to\>\<infty\>>
    as <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> with
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>,J<rsub|m>> fixed. For the second, antitonicity
    of<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|k>>, <math|J<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1>,
    and <math|\<theta\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1> give, once
    <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>\<gtr\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>|\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<frac|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>\<leqslant\><frac|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>1
    </equation*>

    as <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> with <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>
    fixed. The descent starts with zero loops and the fourth-order Wilson
    matching of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>. The kernels and paths are
    chosen once for all periods. Finally <math|<around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|s>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>
    and <math|<around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|U,x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|k><around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|\|>>
    give absolute convergence of the action.
  </render-proof>

  <paragraph|The scalar reset.><label|subsec:C-reset>

  Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D06>,
  <reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b> and
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>. Write
  <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>=\<beta\><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>.
  Here <math|<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m>> contains the clipped
  term, row squares, and cubic and quartic atoms; <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|m>>
  contains the quadratic atoms, with the hypercubic average in both. For an
  input curve <math|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>> of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, put <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>>
  and <math|b<rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> supplies regular and forced sums
  <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>,\<cal-F\><rsub|u>>, independent of
  <math|\<vartheta\>>, with trial density

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|-<around|(|b<rsub|u>+\<vartheta\>|)><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The flat-field normalizations give <math|q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>><around|(|1|)>=1>.
  Fix <math|r<rsub|<math-up|res>>=1> before
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j>+1> as in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>.

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4> gives a preparatory sum
  <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>> with bound
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/4>, for
  a stage constant <math|c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>\<geqslant\>4>.
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, <reference|lem:D5> give the final
  bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> and
  <math|q<rsub|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>>=\<cal-H\><rsub|u>/\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>>
  for some real <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>. The estimate below proves
  <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>1> by a test
  configuration; the same bound also follows from
  <math|t<rsub|m><rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>>
  after<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-isotropy>. Neither bound is an input to the
  identity.

  The remaining scalar correction can be read from a single configuration
  with a definite kinetic energy.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Coupling reset><label|lem:C8> For each input curve of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, take the families and real
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> supplied by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>, with
    no bound on <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> assumed. Then
    <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, and
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>>
    is continuous and satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>-<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>+e<rsub|u>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|j>+1\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The bound is independent of <math|N,p,u> and the input curve.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D06>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>, <reference|lem:C7>, and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, <reference|lem:D4>\U<reference|lem:D6>.
    Recall <math|t=t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> from row<nbsp>7 of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. Put a group element of chord
    <math|h<rsub|<math-up|pr>>=\<tau\>*t> on direction-<math|1> links whose
    second coordinate is even, and the identity elsewhere. Continuity along a
    nonconstant one-parameter subgroup gives
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> such that every chord in
    <math|<around|[|0,\<varepsilon\><rsub|G>|]>> occurs. Choose

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>min <around*|(|\<varepsilon\><rsub|G>,<frac|1|4>,<frac|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>|512*<around|(|1+A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Every <math|<around|(|1,2|)>> plaquette has chord
    <math|h<rsub|<math-up|pr>>> and all other plaquettes have chord zero.
    Thus this configuration<nbsp><math|V> is everywhere healthy. All periods
    here are even because <math|4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>>. Signed permutations
    preserve the sum of direct plaquette charges, so the direct contribution
    to <math|<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>> is at
    least <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*h<rsub|<math-up|pr>><rsup|2>/2>.
    Row squares are nonnegative. Since <math|<around|\||<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><around|(|U-U<rsup|-1>|)>/2|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|U|)>>
    and adjoint transport is isometric, every slot has norm at most
    <math|h<rsub|<math-up|pr>>\<leqslant\>1/4>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>
    bounds the absolute sum of cubic and quartic terms by

    <\equation*>
      2<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*h<rsub|<math-up|pr>><rsup|3>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*h<rsub|<math-up|pr>><rsup|2>/4<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each signed image of<nbsp><math|V> has the same plaquette chords with a
    single nonzero orientation. Hence the bounds apply to every term of the
    hypercubic average. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|<math-up|pr>>=<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>\<geqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<chi\><rsub|<math-up|pr>>*t<rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<chi\><rsub|<math-up|pr>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*\<tau\><rsup|2>/4\<gtr\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4> gives <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>,\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>\<gtr\>0>.
    Put <math|v<rsub|u>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log
    \<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>> and <math|\<varepsilon\>=\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>.
    Here <math|<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|2,m>> is the nonlinear
    direct and row functional of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>, omitting
    cubic and quartic atoms. Taking the logarithmic contrast gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-prereset>log <frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|2,m><around|(|V|)>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>+E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|V|)>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The pre-reset bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>, summed over anchors
    as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b>, bounds each of the last two
    values by <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<varepsilon\>/4>.
    No vanishing condition at<nbsp><math|1> is needed for this preparatory
    sum.

    On everywhere-healthy configurations every curvature slot has norm at
    most<nbsp><math|t>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> and
    <math|t\<leqslant\>1> therefore give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m>-<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|2,m>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*t<rsup|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    After <math|\<ell\>> is fixed, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> bounds
    <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>|\|>> by a stage constant. Raise the
    coupling entry threshold until <math|2<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>|\|>*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<varepsilon\>/2>.
    This is possible since <math|t<rsup|3>/\<varepsilon\>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-2/5><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|3>\<to\>0>.
    Together with<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-prereset> this yields

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||b<rsub|u>*K<rsub|<math-up|pr>>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>-log
      <frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<varepsilon\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the final regular sum, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|V|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|x><big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|maj>>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<varepsilon\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Since the activity families are fixed as <math|\<vartheta\>> varies, the
    scalar discrepancy is affine:

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f*<around|(|u,\<vartheta\>|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|b<rsub|u>+\<vartheta\>|)>*K<rsub|<math-up|pr>>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>+E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|V|)>-log
      <frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=f*<around|(|u,0|)>+\<vartheta\>*K<rsub|<math-up|pr>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Since <math|V> has no flagged sites, <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>=1>.
    Evaluating the exact identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> at
    <math|V> gives <math|f*<around|(|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>=0>. As
    <math|K<rsub|<math-up|pr>>\<gtr\>0>, this determines

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\><rsub|u>=-f*<around|(|u,0|)>/K<rsub|<math-up|pr>>,<space|2em><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>+1|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|\<chi\><rsub|<math-up|pr>>*t<rsub|m><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Increase the entry threshold to obtain the final inequality, using
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/10><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|-2>\<to\>0>.
    The block integral and activity sums are continuous in<nbsp><math|u> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6>, so <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> is continuous.

    Multiplication by <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>=e<rsup|v<rsub|u><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>>
    gives the density identity at<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>. Its coupling
    is <math|\<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> and <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>
    prove the claimed drift.
  </proof>

  <section|Activities and the inductive step><label|sec:partII-D>

  An exact block integral contains interactions on arbitrarily large
  supports. We express them as a sum of regular activities and a
  disjoint-support sum of forced activities. Regular terms are smooth where
  their supports are healthy; forced terms retain configurations with flagged
  plaquettes. Their norms serve different purposes: derivatives are required
  of the first family, whereas the second is estimated uniformly over
  configurations. The estimates and exact density identity enter
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>. The Wilson representation satisfies
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> with the same classes and supplies the
  initial curve of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>.

  The first difficulty is to estimate products under one Gaussian law.
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> supply the
  local energy and simultaneous integration bounds. Connected components then
  separate the ordinary interactions from those attached to flags. Their
  initial regular bound is too large for iteration. We improve it by
  subtracting the flat value of a finite connected sum and evaluating that
  sum on five reflected tori. A fourth difference cancels the boundary terms
  exactly.

  We next remove the affine Hessian of each regular term. Lattice symmetry
  reduces the sum of these Hessians to one curvature-square coefficient.
  Changing its health masks creates further local terms, which are included
  in the forced sum. This gives the exact density before the scalar estimate
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>. The last part constructs one recursion on
  continuous curves and proves its weighted analytic estimates.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E> uses these estimates for endpoint
  tuning and the full fixed-volume limit. The Wilson initial condition is
  proved at the end.

  Throughout, the group and trace metric are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>. Scales, flags, path actions and
  densities are those of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization> and
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, <reference|def:D22>\U<reference|def:D23>.
  At a step <math|m+1\<to\>m> put <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>,
  <math|\<Lambda\>=\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>>, <math|t=t<rsub|m>>,
  <math|B=B<rsub|m>> and <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>. Set
  <math|h<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\>*<around|{|X\<cap\><flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>|}>>.
  Constants may depend on the fixed stage data and, when indicated, on the
  chosen ratio, but are independent of the volume. Here <math|C<rsub|j>> is a
  generic stage constant; the column bounds <math|C<rsub|j>> of row<nbsp>4
  enter only from Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2> on.

  <paragraph|Local terms and their norms.>

  Locality on <math|X> means dependence only on links with both endpoints in
  <math|X>. Write <math|\<xi\><rsub|o><around|(|y|)>> for the centered lift
  of <math|y-o> to <math|<Z><rsup|4>>. All lattice distances in this section
  use the maximum norm. Set <math|N<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>=<around|{|y:<math-up|dist><around|(|y,X|)>\<leqslant\>R|}>>
  and <math|N<rsub|R><around|(|o|)>=N<rsub|R><around|(|<around|{|o|}>|)>>. A
  link internal to <math|X> has both endpoints in <math|X>. The nonwrapping
  condition is <math|10<math-up|dist><around|(|y,o|)>\<less\>L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>
  for every <math|y\<in\>X>; it permits local affine tests without crossing a
  choice of lift.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Regular activities><label|def:D24> A regular activity is a Borel
    real gauge-invariant function <math|E<rsub|i>>, local on <math|X<rsub|i>>
    and anchored at <math|o<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>>. It satisfies
    <math|E<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>=0> and is
    <math|C<rsup|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>> in exponential left-link
    increments on healthy configurations, with all ordered derivatives. On
    nonwrapping supports the Hessian of <math|A\<mapsto\>E<rsub|i><around|(|exp
    A|)>> at zero vanishes on every pair of affine directions

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|<around|(|y,a|)>>=u<rsub|a>+<big|sum><rsub|c>\<xi\><rsub|o<rsub|i>><around|(|y|)><rsup|c>*v<rsub|a*c>,<space|2em>u<rsub|a>,v<rsub|a*c>\<in\><g><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<around|(|exp A|)><rsub|e>=exp <around|(|A<rsub|e>|)>>. This
    removes the affine quadratic contribution from the error.
  </definition>

  For forced terms the inventory specifies exactly the flags inside the
  support. Additional tests distinguish local alternatives with the same
  support and inventory.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Forced activities><label|def:D25> For
    <math|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I<rsub|i>\<subset\>Y<rsub|i>>, set

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|i>=<around|{|U:Y<rsub|i>\<cap\><flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|i>|}>\<cap\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A forced activity is a Borel real gauge-invariant function
    <math|K<rsub|i>> vanishing off <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|i>>. Both
    <math|K<rsub|i>> and its Borel gauge-invariant extra test depend only on
    internal links and flags in <math|Y<rsub|i>>. These conditions restrict
    the forced sum to the prescribed large-field inventory.
  </definition>

  The address of a regular activity is the pair
  <math|<around|(|X<rsub|i>,o<rsub|i>|)>>; that of a forced activity is the
  triple <math|<around|(|Y<rsub|i>,I<rsub|i>,\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>>|)>>,
  and its eligibility indicator is <math|\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\><rsub|i>>>.

  We use the ordered derivative seminorm and weighted sums of
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>. Regular sums
  are bounded at their anchors; forced sums are bounded at every site of
  their supports. For a function <math|f> on <math|Z> anchored at <math|o>,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,Z>> denotes the seminorm of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a> with that support and anchor.

  Continuity concerns the coefficients at fixed addresses. Health sets remain
  fixed as the curve parameter varies.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Continuity of representations><label|def:D25a> A continuous curve
    has fixed activity addresses and tests, and continuous coupling and
    vacuum scalar. At each <math|u<rsub|0>>, differences of regular
    derivatives and forced values admit nonnegative majorants
    <math|e<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>,k<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>> in the sense of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, with

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*e<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>\<longrightarrow\>0*<space|1em><around|(|u\<to\>u<rsub|0>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Both sums run over all addresses. This is continuity for coupling tuning,
    with no derivative of a flag indicator.
  </definition>

  At fixed volume these sums control the density. Summing regular bounds over
  anchors gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-E\><rsub|m>>.
  Each forced support is nonempty, so summing forced bounds over sites gives
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>sup <around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-K\><rsub|m>>.
  The absolute sum over all finite selections is at most
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+sup
  <around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>exp <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>sup
  <around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>|)>>. Restricting to disjoint supports and exact
  coverage preserves this bound. Thus the density sums of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06> converge. No volume-independent bound
  on the full density follows.

  <subsection|The local expansion>

  Fix a compact parameter interval and a continuous input curve
  <math|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>> in the sense of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>. Its prescribed path satisfies
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>; its regular and forced
  families satisfy Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25>
  and the caps of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>;
  and <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-H\><rsub|u>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>,<space|2em><pathaction><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>=\<beta\><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>><around|(|U|)>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The direct and row part of <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>>
  is denoted by <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>>; it remains
  nonlinear in the links. The coefficient <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> is the
  quadratic coefficient of the determinant comparison in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>. It is independent of the curve
  parameter and the period.

  For <math|a\<less\>b> define the clover curvature at <math|o> by

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U,o|)>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<eta\>=\<pm\>1>\<varepsilon\>*\<eta\><space|0.17em><math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><frac|U<rsub|o;\<varepsilon\>*a,\<eta\>*b>-U<rsub|o;\<varepsilon\>*a,\<eta\>*b><rsup|-1>|2><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The loop starts at <math|o>, takes steps
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*e<rsub|a>,\<eta\>*e<rsub|b>,-\<varepsilon\>*e<rsub|a>,-\<eta\>*e<rsub|b>>,
  and returns to <math|o>. Thus each term transforms by the adjoint action at
  <math|o> and uses links within distance two. For an affine field as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>, differentiating the four link factors
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-clover-linear><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*z>|\|><rsub|z=0>*C<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|e<rsup|z*A>,o|)>=v<rsub|b*a>-v<rsub|a*b><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Each inverse contributes the negative link field; the four orientations
  have the same signed curl.

  Expand each row square into pairs of its slots. Together with the direct
  plaquette terms these give a countable family <math|\<lambda\>>. Let
  <math|r<rsub|\<lambda\>>> be its unclipped quadratic curvature function,
  including its coefficient, and <math|c<rsub|\<lambda\>>> the same function
  with each curvature replaced by the clover curvature of its orientation at
  the anchor. Let <math|L<rsub|\<lambda\>>> be its actual clipped or
  health-tested contribution. A direct term has
  <math|r<rsub|\<lambda\>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>>
  and <math|c<rsub|\<lambda\>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>><around|\||C<rsub|q>|\|><rsup|2>/2>.
  A row pair has coefficient <math|r<rsub|\<alpha\>*i>*r<rsub|\<alpha\>*k>/2>
  multiplying its two curvature factors. Include translations and the
  <math|<hypercubic>> average, with weight
  <math|<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|><rsup|-1>>, in the addresses.

  Choose <math|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>> to contain the slot footprints and the
  radius-<math|B> neighborhood of the anchor needed to decide the relaxed
  health test. Queries outside that neighborhood are automatic once

  <\equation*>
    t*exp <around|(|<around|(|B-2|)>/R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>|)>\<geqslant\>2<space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  since chords are at most two. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
  give absolute convergence and

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>L<rsub|\<lambda\>>+\<cal-A\><rsub|m>,>|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*r<rsub|\<lambda\>>,<label|eq:D-low-sums>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|}>>*c<rsub|\<lambda\>>>|<cell|=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||C<rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\|><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  Here <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|m>> is the cubic and quartic sum. A signed image
  in these formulas uses the pulled-back anchor, support and clover; the
  final equality follows from the coefficient identity of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> and reindexing orientations. On
  <math|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>> healthy, <math|L<rsub|\<lambda\>>=r<rsub|\<lambda\>>>;
  without flags, <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>=<math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>>.

  The local integral is expanded before any regular term is normalized. Its
  elementary factors are the remainders of the stationary action, the
  variation <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-<around|[|\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>|]>>>
  of the inherited loop action about the stationary configuration, the
  determinant and covariance ratios, the Haar Jacobian, the incoming
  activities, and the failures of the fluctuation and chart tests. For a
  factor <math|F<rsub|a>>, write <math|W<rsub|a>=F<rsub|a>-1> when its
  unperturbed value is one. For an incoming forced factor use
  <math|W<rsub|a>=K<rsub|a>> directly. The support includes every field, flag
  and covariance window read by the factor. We join two factors when their
  complete read supports, as defined before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>,
  intersect. A molecule here is a molecule in that sense whose product has
  pairwise disjoint incoming forced supports, satisfies the inventory
  coverage <math|I<rsub|i>\<subset\><big|cup><rsub|p\<in\>A<rsub|f><around|(|M|)>><math-up|corners><around|(|p|)>>
  and the component compatibility there. The selection retains these supports
  and inventories. An ordinary molecule also satisfies the last admitted
  condition: its complete read supports avoid the output flags.

  For an ordinary molecule <math|M> on a healthy output support, its
  integrated weight and an absolute derivative majorant are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-seed-required>z<rsub|M><around|(|U|)>=<big|int><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>M>W<rsub|a>*<around|(|U,\<zeta\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<zeta\>|)>,<space|2em><big|int>g<rsub|M>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>M>f<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<nu\>> is the single sector Gaussian/Haar law of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, <math|g<rsub|M>> majorizes the product and
  its required ordered derivatives, and <math|f<rsub|a>> are that lemma's
  factor bounds. The integer <math|s<rsub|M>> is the sum of the numbers of
  radius-<math|B> blocks covering the complete read supports, including
  retained path lengths in the same units. The function <math|P<rsub|j>> has
  subpower growth after the ratio has been fixed. The formula is used only on
  compatible sectors; their sum retains the exact allocation indicators.
  Terms whose reads meet an output flag component remain attached to that
  component and have a nonempty forced inventory. The ordinary connected
  integrals are taken on the complement, where their read supports are
  disjoint from the output flags. Components with empty inventory are used in
  the regular logarithm on their healthy supports.

  We first give the connected identity with its initial estimates. Write
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|\<tau\>>> and <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<tau\>>> for the
  anchored regular and forced site sums at support exponent <math|\<tau\>>.
  For an extra factor <math|W<rsub|0>> on complete read support
  <math|Z<rsub|0>>, let <math|n<rsub|0>=<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|0>|)>>
  and let <math|v<rsub|0>> bound its integrated majorant, including its
  support weight and required derivatives, with any retained forced seed
  suppression kept separately. Its rooted sum contains each connected term
  with one distinguished occurrence of <math|W<rsub|0>>, counted once; the
  other factors have nonnegative majorants <math|v<rsub|a>> on their complete
  read supports <math|Z<rsub|a>>, including the reserved support weights.
  Denote its absolute regular sum at <math|\<sigma\>+3> by
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|reg>><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)>> and its forced
  sum at <math|\<sigma\>+2> by <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|for>><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)>>.
  For a retained energy exponent <math|r<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0>, set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)>=max <around|{|\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|reg>><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)>,e<rsup|r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>*\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|for>><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)>|}><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For coefficient-valued factors we use the same majorants in a commutative
  coefficient algebra with a submultiplicative weighted norm. This includes
  complex coefficients and finite commuting nilpotent marks. The stronger
  regular estimate needed for subtraction is obtained in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>. The families
  <math|f<rsub|a><rsup|0>> below are local on <math|X<rsub|a>> and regular
  before flat-value and Hessian subtraction; the
  <math|k<rsub|i,u><rsup|<math-up|con>>> are forced activities, with
  exact-cover sum <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|con>>>. Both families
  have fixed countable addresses, are equivariant and continuous in weighted
  norms. The representation holds on all configurations. All bounds are
  coefficientwise and remain valid with every support exponent lowered by
  two.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Connected local expansion><label|lem:D-connected> Every such curve
    admits <math|\<tau\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|old>>\<in\><R>>, families
    <math|f<rsup|0>,k<rsup|<math-up|con>>> and constants
    <math|C<rsub|j>,r<rsub|j>,\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, depending only on stage and
    ratio, with

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-H\><rsub|u>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|{|\<tau\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|old>>\|\<Lambda\>\|-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m>-\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>-<big|sum><rsub|a>h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>|}>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|con>>.
    </equation*>

    Absolute convergence holds;

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|\<sigma\>+3>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|m+1><rsup|-7/16>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|\<sigma\>+2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|W<rsub|0>> satisfying <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>v<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>,
    <math|\<cal-R\><around|(|W<rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|0>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|0>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the kernel and density of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>,
    <reference|def:D06>, the path bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>,
    the local energy of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>, and the simultaneous
    majorant and disjoint-support factorization of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>. Expand finite products first. Fubini's
    theorem applies to each term by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-seed-required>. Disjoint
    complete read supports use disjoint Gaussian marginal blocks and Haar
    variables, so their integrals multiply. This gives a gas of the connected
    weights <math|z<rsub|M>>. Overlapping factors are always integrated
    together.

    Put <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>> and
    <math|g=<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)><rsup|-1/2>>. For a
    healthy local chart write its link logarithm as <math|m<rsub|Y>+g*z>; the
    mean is <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>|)>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>. The following bounds include the
    prescribed finite number of ordered derivatives. A factor
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>> below absorbs fixed powers of
    <math|B> and <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>> after the ratio is fixed;
    read-count powers remain in <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>>
    as in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-seed-required>:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-factor-rates><tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|factor>>|<cell|<text|integrated
      majorant>>>|<row|<cell|<text|stationary-action
      remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*g<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>P<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|centered
      determinant remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>\<leqslant\>P<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|Gaussian-density
      and stationarity residuals>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|res>>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|tail>>*B>>>|<row|<cell|<text|Haar-Jacobian
      remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)><rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|inherited
      loop variation>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>*g\<leqslant\>P<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|incoming
      regular sum>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>>|<row|<cell|<text|incoming
      forced component>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|for>>>>|<row|<cell|<text|warning>>|<cell|M<rsub|w>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>>>>
    </equation>

    For the first row, Taylor's formula after the stationary quadratic part
    starts with <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*<around|(|m<rsub|Y>+g*z|)><rsup|3>>;
    substituting <math|m<rsub|Y>=O<around|(|g<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)>> gives
    the displayed bound. For the determinant, subtract its flat value and the
    quadratic term already assigned to <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>.
    The inverse identity bounds the remaining variation by
    <math|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>>. The covariance and
    stationary residuals are exponentially small by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>; their rescaling costs only the
    displayed fixed power. The chart density has value one and zero first
    derivative at zero, giving its quadratic bound. The inherited loop action
    is quadratic in slot curvatures with weighted coefficient sum at most
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>; on the chart each slot is
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g<around|\||z|\|>|)>> and moves by
    <math|O<around|(|g<around|\||z|\|>|)>>, which gives its row. Incoming
    sums retain their own allowances. Chart-test failures, fine-plaquette and
    mismatch failures, and tag or inventory departures are precisely the
    warning factors of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>.

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> permits every fixed warning exponent
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|w>\<gtr\>0>. Take <math|\<tau\><rsub|w>=1/2>, so
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>> and the simultaneous
    probability bound is <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w><rsup|2*r>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-r>>
    for <math|r> distinct warning addresses, including the coordinate-choice
    multiplicity. The linear Chernoff bound there gives each warning the
    exponent <math|c<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>/g|)>/B<rsup|4>> for some
    <math|c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0>. Since <math|t<rsub|m+1>/g\<geqslant\><cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>/<sqrt|2>>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|t<rsub|m+1>/g|B<rsup|4>*log \<frak-b\>>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The fixed amplitudes <math|M<rsub|w>> enter <math|P<rsub|j>>. All rows
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-factor-rates> are consequently at most a subpower
    factor times <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>. Choose that factor at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|1/32>>, then raise the threshold until
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/32>\<leqslant\>1>. This gives
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>, with <math|1/32> of the power available
    before the last threshold choice. Path lengths are summed with the
    exponential bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. Every fixed
    polynomial in <math|1+s<rsub|M>> is bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<eta\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*s<rsub|M>>>; choose <math|\<eta\>>
    from the unused support exponent. These choices precede the coupling
    threshold.

    A compulsory component is a connected component of the selected seed
    neighbourhoods in the allocation sector of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>. Its seed selection
    <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|f>,A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|c>|)>> consists of the
    selected fine plaquettes, mismatch edges and coarse flags defined there.
    Write <math|\<kappa\><rsub|B>> for that lemma's constant. It retains
    <math|exp <around|(|-\<kappa\><rsub|B>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>|)>>
    before integration. Fix the retained exponent
    <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>\<less\>\<kappa\><rsub|B>> in
    the compulsory-component bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, and
    then choose <math|0\<less\>r<rsub|j>\<less\>\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>>.
    When <math|A<rsub|c>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, keep all reads meeting this
    component in its local factor and apply that bound. Old measurable
    factors use their supremum envelopes, while the Gaussian variables are
    integrated. The frozen Haar integrals are probability integrals. After
    adjoining every seed component met by the reads,
    <math|A<rsub|c>=\<varnothing\>> means that these reads avoid output
    flags. In this case the seed-factor clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> retains
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>>
    through the required derivative order; apply its ordinary molecule bound
    to the remaining factors. With <math|F<rsub|A>> denoting this component
    product, its integrable majorant satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|A>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|A>,<space|2em><big|int>g<rsub|A>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|A>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|a<math-up|attached>>f<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|s<rsub|A>> counts its radius-<math|B> blocks, and
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>> was fixed before <math|r<rsub|j>>. The
    product contains the other attached factors' bounds. Its covariance
    scores and residual quadratic exponentials are estimated by square
    completion under the common Gaussian law there. Thus no supremum bound on
    an unbounded Gaussian coordinate is used. The surrounding ordinary
    factors have supports disjoint from the output flags and use that same
    law. The read-count polynomial uses the unused support exponent: for
    every fixed <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|A>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<delta\>>*e<rsup|\<delta\>*s<rsub|A>>>.
    Choose <math|\<delta\>> within that reserve, as
    for<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-seed-required>; this leaves the output support
    weights intact. The local seed-sector multiplicity uses a fixed part of
    the energy exponent between <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>> and
    <math|r<rsub|j>>. The prescribed derivative costs are already included in
    the read-count polynomial and the subpower factor. A simultaneous set of
    <math|r> warning addresses contributes
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|w>><rsup|2*r>> to the Gaussian
    probability in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>; Cauchy\USchwarz leaves
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|w>><rsup|r>> after the other factors
    have been estimated under that same law. Thus a warning still has its own
    factor in <math|<big|prod>f<rsub|a>>. This is necessary when distinct
    warnings share Gaussian coordinates.

    To sum connected molecules, root at a specified site and order the
    children of a spanning tree by their first support intersection. A
    support requiring <math|n> radius-<math|B> balls has at most
    <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*n> sites. If <math|v<rsub|a>> is the
    resulting factor majorant at a site, the attachment sum is bounded by
    <math|n*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>v<rsub|a>>. The coupling threshold
    makes this quantity at most <math|\<eta\>*n>, with <math|\<eta\>> smaller
    than the reserved exponent. Summing <math|k> children contributes the
    exponential series <math|1/k>!; iteration gives the rooted bound by
    <math|v<rsub|a>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|a>>>. This proves absolute
    summability, including all sectors and all differentiated terms. For a
    distinguished factor <math|W<rsub|0>>, the same child sum starts with
    <math|v<rsub|0>> and at most <math|n<rsub|0>> attachment blocks, giving
    <math|v<rsub|0>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|0>>>. Rooting there counts each
    distinguished occurrence once. A forced component keeps <math|exp
    <around|(|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>|)>>
    in this sum, proving both forced bounds. For coefficient-valued factors
    take their weighted norms before integration. The inequalities
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a*b|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a+b|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>>
    reduce every product and sum to the same nonnegative tree calculation.
    For finitely many commuting marks, products are coefficient convolutions;
    repeated square-zero marks vanish, so deleting these products only
    decreases the majorant. Complex coefficients use absolute values in the
    same calculation. Finite-product identities hold coefficient by
    coefficient; absolute convergence permits passage to their limits. This
    proves the coefficientwise assertions without changing the health or
    inventory tests. Increasing the fine support exponent before choosing the
    ratio leaves the output exponents <math|\<sigma\>+3> and
    <math|\<sigma\>+2>. The same tree calculation applies with two subtracted
    from every support exponent, including the exponent in <math|v<rsub|0>>:
    covering subadditivity still applies and the attachment sum decreases.
    This proves the bounds with the reduced weights.

    Write <math|\<Xi\><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-M\>><big|prod><rsub|M\<in\>\<cal-M\>>z<rsub|M>>,
    where <math|\<cal-M\>> runs over finite selections with disjoint
    supports, including the empty selection. This is the ordinary hard-core
    gas. Its logarithm has the connected coefficients

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-cluster>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|log \<Xi\><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|1|n!>*<big|sum><rsub|M<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,M<rsub|n>>\<phi\><rsup|T>*<around|(|M<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,M<rsub|n>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h=1><rsup|n>z<rsub|M<rsub|h>>,>>|<row|<cell|\<phi\><rsup|T>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\><text|connected>><big|prod><rsub|<around|{|h,k|}>\<in\>\<Gamma\>><around|[|-\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|M<rsub|h>>\<cap\>Z<rsub|M<rsub|k>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>|]><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>

    The graph in this formula has vertex set
    <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>, and the one-vertex coefficient is one.
    Apply<nbsp><cite-detail|Ueltschi2004|Theorem<nbsp>1,
    equations<nbsp>(3)--(4)> to the counting measure with masses
    <math|z<rsub|M>> and interaction <math|\<zeta\>*<around|(|M,M<rprime|'>|)>=-\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|M>\<cap\>Z<rsub|M<rprime|'>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>>.
    Its hypotheses are <math|<around|\||1+\<zeta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, finite
    total variation, finite exponential mass, and

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|M<rprime|'>:Z<rsub|M<rprime|'>>\<cap\>Z<rsub|M>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>><around|\||z<rsub|M<rprime|'>>|\|>*e<rsup|\<frak-w\><around|(|M<rprime|'>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<frak-w\><around|(|M|)>,<space|2em>\<frak-w\><around|(|M|)>=\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|M>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The rooted estimate just proved verifies the last condition. At fixed
    volume the site sum gives finite total exponential mass.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-cluster> and its rooted estimate therefore
    apply. Fixing the distinguished occurrence changes the symmetry factor
    from <math|1/n>! to <math|1/<around|(|n-1|)>>!, matching that theorem's
    rooted sum. Keep the components joined to forced molecules as
    coefficients of the exact-cover sum; take the logarithm only of the
    remaining ordinary gas. Expanding and regrouping finite selections proves
    this separation. Absolute convergence then proves the identity for the
    full integral. The deterministic part is exactly
    <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>:
    the stationary terms and one-loop quadratic term are those of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7>, with the masks
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums> retained.

    For two inputs at fixed addresses, telescope each product with one
    difference factor. Activity differences use their own difference norms
    under Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>. For a coupling change, hold
    physical coordinates fixed and compare the Gaussian densities explicitly.
    On <math|d> active coordinates with fixed mean and positive precision
    <math|<math-ss|A>>, put <math|Q<around|(|a|)>=<around|\<langle\>|a-m,<math-ss|A><around|(|a-m|)>|\<rangle\>>/2>.
    The normalized laws satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <frac|d*\<nu\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|2>>|d*\<nu\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|1>>><around|(|a|)>=<around*|(|<frac|\<beta\><rsub|2>|\<beta\><rsub|1>>|)><rsup|d/2>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|2>-\<beta\><rsub|1>|)>*Q<around|(|a|)>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its derivative in <math|\<beta\><rsub|2>> is the same ratio times
    <math|d/<around|(|2*\<beta\><rsub|2>|)>-Q<around|(|a|)>>. On a small
    relative coupling interval its absolute value is dominated by a
    polynomial times a Gaussian exponential with precision strictly below
    that of the base law. Square completion bounds its integral. Mean and
    precision changes are treated by the inverse difference identity and the
    Schur bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>; their density
    derivatives are again polynomial Gaussian scores. No old measurable
    factor is differentiated in this comparison. The tree bound with the same
    attachment margin controls the weighted discrepancy by a constant times
    the input activity and coupling discrepancies; this constant may depend
    on the fixed volume. Continuity holds locally and, by a finite covering
    of the compact interval, on fixed addresses throughout. Translation and
    signed permutations transport complete read supports, factors and
    sectors; their finite average gives equivariance.
  </proof>

  The terms linear in an incoming regular activity must be treated separately
  from products of small factors. Their supports can be large, so they are
  not replaced by auxiliary tori. The function <math|E<rsub|i>> and its
  derivative norm in this paragraph are at level <math|m+1>. For such an
  address <math|i>, let <math|T<rsub|i>> be its contribution to the negative
  connected logarithm. The local Gaussian substitution of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, restricted to the windows incident to
  <math|X<rsub|i>>, reconstructs a fine configuration
  <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|0>*<around|(|V,z|)>>. With the same inner chart
  protector <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>>, define

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-T\><rsub|i>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<theta\>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|i>><math-up|Ext><rsub|Y<rsub|i>><big|int>\<Gamma\><rsub|i,\<theta\>><around|(|z|)>*E<rsub|i>*<around*|(|U<rsub|i,\<theta\>><rsup|0>*<around|(|V,z|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|i,\<theta\>><around|(|V|)>><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|i>=T<rsub|i>-P<rsub|i><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|i>> is the finite set of rooted frames and tags
  in that local substitution. The operator
  <math|<math-up|Ext><rsub|Y<rsub|i>>> extends the function from the healthy
  domain by its local Borel representative; on that domain it changes no
  value. The coarse support <math|Y<rsub|i>> contains the projected fine
  support and every incident window. This principal Gaussian average retains
  the incoming function itself.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Inherited regular terms><label|lem:D-inherited> The functions
    <math|P<rsub|i>,R<rsub|i>> are regular before flat-value and Hessian
    subtraction, on the same fixed coarse addresses as <math|T<rsub|i>>. At
    exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3> their anchored derivative sums satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><around|(|P|)>\<leqslant\><frac|V<rsub|j>|\<ell\><rsub|m>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><around|(|R|)>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\><rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|inh>><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|V<rsub|j>> is fixed before the ratio and
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\>><rsup|<math-up|inh>>> has scale rate
    <math|-1/2>. The identity <math|T<rsub|i>=P<rsub|i>+R<rsub|i>> and the
    corresponding weighted comparisons hold on the entire input curve.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D05b>,
    <reference|def:D06>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>,
    <reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. For the principal average use the
    same normalized directions as in the incoming norm. The linear background
    lifts a unit coarse curl to fine curvature of size
    <math|O<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>|)>>. Replace the background on
    <math|X<rsub|i>> by its affine part at the anchor. In the Taylor formula
    for <math|E<rsub|i>> the constant vanishes by
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>. The first derivative also vanishes:
    invariance under constant gauge transformations makes its coefficient on
    each link an <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant vector of
    <math|<g>>, which is zero since <math|<g>> is simple and nonabelian
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>). Its Hessian on two such affine
    fields vanishes by Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>. Since
    <math|E<rsub|i>> is gauge invariant, evaluate it in the radial gauge at
    its fine anchor; at fine distance <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>
    the lifted field is then at most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>*<around|(|t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>|)>>
    times a permitted fine direction, using
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<geqslant\>\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>2>.
    Every surviving background term therefore has either three curvature
    factors or one spatial remainder. The third-order integral remainder has
    the product of its three normalized direction sizes. The spatial
    remainder is bounded by the first difference of the prediction kernel and
    supplies one further inverse ratio. In the normalized curvature
    directions, the linear prediction <math|<math-ss|P>> and its affine
    approximation <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|<math-up|aff>>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|P>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|P>-<math-ss|P><rsub|<math-up|aff>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1-r>>
    for <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>2>. Taking its curl and one further
    fine difference gives the two bounds above, with constants fixed before
    the ratio. For the Hessian remainder use one difference and one
    undifferenced direction; for the cubic remainder use three directions.
    Thus their factors before the anchor sum are

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>|)><rsup|3>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-6>,<space|2em>\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-3>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-5><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For every support extending beyond the radius <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>, use
    the incoming covering number <math|d<rsub|i>=<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m+1>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>,
    rather than infer a large covering number from that radius alone. Split
    at <math|d<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<less\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>. In this class
    <math|3*d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>; together with
    <math|B<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m>>, this bounds the inspected fine
    radius by <math|6*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>>. The support
    is nonwrapping because the coarse side is at least
    <math|100*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>|)>>. Perform the radial Taylor expansion
    on this whole support. In the incoming weighted direction norm, each
    differentiated lifted potential decomposes into a constant part, an
    affine part and a remainder with respective bounds

    <\equation*>
      C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-3>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|C> is bounded by a stage constant plus the normalized
    nonlinear and fluctuation errors. After the ratio is fixed these errors
    are made at most one by the entry choice: their bounds are fixed powers
    of <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>,B<rsub|m>> times
    <math|t<rsub|m+1>+g/t<rsub|m+1>>, which tends to zero. The spatial
    weights are retained in this decomposition, using
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<geqslant\>\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>2>
    from row<nbsp>4 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>.

    Assign weights one, two and three to the three parts. In the local
    radial-gauge expansion, a site gauge removes the weight-one part of the
    potential and produces terms of weight at least two; commutators of
    constant parts first occur at weight two. Thus the first possible
    curvature has weight two. The terms through weight four are therefore a
    constant, linear terms in curvature and its spatial derivatives, and a
    quadratic curvature term. The first is zero by flat normalization, the
    second is zero by constant colour invariance, and the last is zero by the
    affine Hessian condition. Equivalently, multilinearity after the
    three-part decomposition cancels every selection of total weight at most
    four. A selection of larger weight has at least five inverse powers of
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>. Taylor's integral formula along the healthy
    radial segment gives the same gain at the evaluation field: integrate the
    next derivative from the flat field, repeating up to the available order
    <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>6>. The ordered chain rule
    has only stage-dependent many placements. The common Gaussian majorant of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> bounds their moments.

    For the complementary class <math|d<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<geqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>,
    including winding supports, apply the derivative bound without an affine
    subtraction. Its direction and read-count losses are polynomial in
    <math|1+d<rsub|i>>. Both cases are expressed by

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|d|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsup|-5>,>|<cell|d<rsup|3>\<less\>\<ell\>,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|d<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>\<ell\>,>>>>><space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsup|-5>*<around|(|1+d|)><rsup|15>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|\<ell\><rsup|5>\<leqslant\>d<rsup|15>> in the second case.
    After the entry choice absorbs factors polynomial in <math|B<rsub|m>>, if
    <math|a<rsub|i>> is an incoming derivative majorant, the integrated
    principal bound is at most <math|C<rsub|j>*a<rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|m>><around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>*<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|q<rsub|j>>>,
    for a fixed integer <math|q<rsub|j>>; the polynomial comes from the
    ordered chain rule and the Gaussian moments just described. No estimate
    <math|d<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>c*\<ell\><rsub|m>> is needed.

    For support compression, fix an integer <math|r> with
    <math|Y<rsub|i>\<subset\>N<rsub|r*B<rsub|m>><around|(|<math-up|parent><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>|)>>
    and put <math|T<rsub|r>=<around|(|2*r+13|)><rsup|4>>. Choose the ratio to
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      20*<around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)>*T<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>.
    </equation*>

    Together with <math|B<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m>>, this leaves half
    the incoming exponent:

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)>*T<rsub|r>+<tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*d<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The remaining half bounds <math|<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|q<rsub|j>+15>>,
    since <math|sup<rsub|d\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+d|)><rsup|q<rsub|j>+15>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*d/2>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    There are <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|4>> fine anchors above a coarse
    anchor. Multiplying their count by the per-address factor
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-5>> proves the bound
    <math|V<rsub|j>/\<ell\><rsub|m>>, with <math|V<rsub|j>> fixed before the
    ratio.

    For <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>>,
    compare the actual local integral with this principal Gaussian average
    before integrating. Subtract their means, covariances and transported
    kernels using the inverse difference identity, and telescope the
    remaining factor product with one marked difference. The inverse
    difference identity <math|C<rsup|-1>-<wide|C|~><rsup|-1>=C<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<wide|C|~>-C|)>*<wide|C|~><rsup|-1>>
    puts a reconstruction difference in one factor. Taylor's formula for the
    link exponential makes that factor at most
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>> in
    each of the requested normalized derivatives; stationarity and window
    residuals contribute <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*g<rsup|-c<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-c<rprime|'><rsub|j>*B>>.
    Consequently their common bound is

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|{|t<rsub|m+1>+g+g<rsup|-c<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-c<rprime|'><rsub|j>*B>|}>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>,<space|2em>\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A second occurrence of the incoming activity has bound
    <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|)><rsup|2>>,
    which is also bounded by a scale-rate-<math|-1/2> factor times
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>. In every term of the
    difference, one of these factors is retained before the common Gaussian
    majorant is applied. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> bounds its remaining
    moments by <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>>,
    where <math|P<rsub|j>> has scale rate zero after the ratio is fixed and
    <math|s<rsub|M>> is the block count defined above. Summing with the
    unused incoming support exponent gives a function
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\>><rsup|<math-up|inh>>> of rate <math|-1/2>.
    This proves the remainder estimate in its weighted norm, rather than only
    a pointwise comparison of integrals. For the remaining supports use the
    unrefined bound for <math|T<rsub|i>> from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> and the same Gaussian majorant for
    <math|P<rsub|i>>. Support compression implies that the incoming covering
    number is at least <math|c*B<rsub|m>-C<rsub|j>>. Retaining a further
    fixed part of its exponent gives an extra <math|e<rsup|-c*B<rsub|m>>> in
    the weighted sum, for some <math|c\<gtr\>0> fixed before the coupling
    threshold. Since <math|B<rsub|m>\<geqslant\><around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>, this factor, times any fixed polynomial in
    <math|B<rsub|m>>, is bounded by <math|<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>> after
    the threshold is raised. This supplies the stated remainder bound on
    large supports without extending the local comparison beyond its domain.

    Local Gaussian marginals read only <math|Y<rsub|i>>. A gauge
    transformation acts orthogonally on their colour coordinates and leaves
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>> invariant; changing Gaussian variables proves
    gauge invariance of <math|P<rsub|i>>. The frame average transports the
    support and anchor under lattice symmetries. The local extension
    preserves these identities, measurability and the healthy derivatives.
    The same properties hold for <math|R<rsub|i>> by subtraction. For a
    parameter difference use that incoming difference in the displayed
    integral and in the marked comparison. The covariance score and
    protected-coordinate change are those of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>,
    so the same weighted estimates give continuity at fixed addresses.
    Finally choose the ratio so <math|V<rsub|j>/\<ell\><rsub|m>> is less than
    the prescribed pre-ratio fraction, and then the threshold so
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\><rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|inh>>> is less than
    that fraction.
  </proof>

  The initial regular estimate does not fit
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>>.
  The following localization gives the additional power. We describe the
  finite-period cancellation because an estimate of each separate local term
  would miss it.

  Write <math|f<rsub|a><rsup|0>> for a regular summand of the negative
  logarithm in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-cluster>, including its coefficient. Keep the
  inherited terms of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited> separately. Call
  a remaining term eligible if it is a connected term with at most two
  molecules of at most two elementary factors each, every path atom having
  length at most <math|B>, with no incoming activity factor or seed
  component, or a path term of length at most <math|B>. Such terms have
  complete read radius at most a fixed stage multiple of <math|B>. Eligible
  terms read only the prescribed path, the coupling and the addresses, not
  the values of the incoming activities. Fix an exhaustion of the eligible
  addresses by finite sets invariant under translations and signed
  permutations, and select the eligible terms in one of these sets. Its index
  and the truncation error <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> are fixed
  in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>. Give an ordinary
  term the shares <math|p<rsub|x*a>=\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|a>><around|(|x|)>/<around|\||X<rsub|a>|\|>>,
  and a path term the share <math|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|a>|}>><around|(|x|)>>.
  Put <math|F<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|a<math-up|selected>>p<rsub|x*a>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>>.
  The remaining terms form <math|f<rsup|<math-up|tail>>>. Let <math|R>
  contain every complete read support of this finite sum relative to a site
  receiving a nonzero share. Its radius is a stage multiple of <math|B>. Set
  <math|w=4*R+100*<around|(|1+B|)>>, <math|e=w+5>, and let
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> have side <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>4>. A configuration on <math|\<Lambda\>>
  gives a configuration <math|V<rsub|i,x>> on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> by
  repeated reflection of its cube about <math|x>. The direction of a
  reflected link is reversed when necessary. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-five-weights>\<lambda\><rsub|i>=<frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>|2<rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>4!>,<space|2em>G<rsub|x><around|(|V|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|y><around|(|V<rsub|i,x>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> is the same finite local formula on the
  auxiliary torus, with its value at <math|1> subtracted at each site. This
  operation recovers a local function from bounds on the total auxiliary
  action.

  For the estimates below put <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>>;
  <math|P<rsub|j>> has subpower growth.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite-period localization><label|lem:D-localization> For a finite
    local connected sum with complete read radius <math|R>, the function
    <math|G<rsub|x>> in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-five-weights> is local on
    <math|N<rsub|e><around|(|x|)>>. Its redistribution has the exact form

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>=c\|\<Lambda\>\|+<big|sum><rsub|a>h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tail>>+<big|sum><rsub|x>h<rsub|N<rsub|e><around|(|x|)>>*G<rsub|x>+<big|sum><rsub|x>L<rsub|x><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each <math|L<rsub|x>> reads links and flags in
    <math|N<rsub|2*e><around|(|x|)>> and vanishes when that set is healthy.

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,N<rsub|e><around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|)>,<space|2em>sup
      <around|\||L<rsub|x>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>\U<reference|def:D06>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C7>,
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Give each connected summand equal
    shares at the sites of its complete read support; give each path term to
    its anchor. These shares sum to one. Let <math|F<rsub|x>> be the
    resulting finite sum at <math|x>, and let <math|f<rsup|<math-up|tail>>>
    be the terms not selected. Define

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-mask-localization>L<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|a<text|selected>>p<rsub|x*a>*h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>h<rsub|N<rsub|e>*<around|(|x-<wide|y|~>|)>>*F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|y><around|(|V<rsub|i,x-<wide|y|~>>|)>,<space|2em>c=F<rsub|0><around|(|1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The coefficients <math|p<rsub|x*a>> are the shares, and <math|<wide|y|~>>
    is the integer representative used in the reflection. Summing over
    <math|x> and changing <math|x> to <math|x-<wide|y|~>> gives exactly the
    asserted identity. Translation symmetry makes
    <math|F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>> independent of <math|x>.

    Use the polynomial period requirement in row 7 of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, in addition to
    <math|100*<around|(|B+1|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>. Write
    <math|n=L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>> and let <math|b> be the fixed neighbourhood
    constant in the eligibility radius. The radius in the local construction
    is

    <\equation*>
      R=18*<around|{|b*B+6*B+3+B|}>+8+2*B.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|e=4*R+100*<around|(|1+B|)>+5> is affine in <math|B>, with
    positive coefficients. Put <math|a<rsub|b>=2*e\|<rsub|B=1>>. The chosen
    stage has integers <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>,h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>
    with <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|b>>,
    <math|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>\<geqslant\>1> and

    <\equation*>
      n\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>.
    </equation*>

    These are the minimum-period choices made after the ratios. Since
    <math|B\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      2*e\<leqslant\>a<rsub|b>*B\<less\>a<rsub|b>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>\<leqslant\>n,<space|2em>4*R+16\<less\>n.
    </equation*>

    The reflection cubes, whose side is at most
    <math|2*<around|(|w+4|)>\<less\>2*e>, therefore embed, as does every
    radius-<math|R> read set. The larger health set
    <math|N<rsub|2*e><around|(|x|)>> is a set on the original torus and need
    not embed; the argument uses only its containment of the queried links
    and flags.

    For the local comparison, suppose that the field is healthy on the
    radius-<math|R> neighbourhood. Translate its cube to
    <math|<around|[|0,2*R|]><rsup|4>>. On integer representatives
    <math|0\<leqslant\>k\<less\>n>, fold each coordinate by
    <math|q<around|(|k|)>=k> for <math|k\<leqslant\>2*R>,
    <math|q<around|(|k|)>=4*R-k> for <math|2*R\<less\>k\<leqslant\>4*R>, and
    <math|q<around|(|k|)>=0> thereafter. Pull back links along this map,
    reversing them on decreasing edges and setting them to the identity on
    collapsed edges. A plaquette becomes a plaquette of the original cube,
    with possible reversal and conjugation, or has identity holonomy. The
    field is therefore globally healthy and agrees with the original field on
    the cube. The bound <math|4*R\<less\>n> permits the construction without
    a divisibility assumption on <math|n>. Locality then permits this
    replacement when comparing the common local formulas on different
    periods.

    We verify the vanishing of <math|L<rsub|x>> on a healthy
    <math|N<rsub|2*e><around|(|x|)>>. The complete read supports then lie in
    that set, every mask in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization> is one, and
    every reflected configuration occurring there is healthy. Classify a
    point of <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> by its distance, through <math|R>,
    from each reflection face. In the <math|y>-summand
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization> the reflection cube is centred at
    <math|x-<wide|y|~>>, so its site <math|y> corresponds to <math|x>. For a
    fixed class the local formula is therefore independent of <math|i>. In
    one coordinate the number of points in an interior class is
    <math|2*<around|(|w-2*R+1|)>+2*i>; for a specified boundary class it is a
    fixed integer. Consequently each class has a multiplicity polynomial in
    <math|i> of degree at most four. Its degree is four only when all four
    coordinates are interior. The identity

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>i<rsup|k>=0*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>k\<less\>4|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>i<rsup|4>=4!
    </equation*>

    cancels every boundary class and retains the interior class with
    coefficient <math|2<rsup|4>*4!/<around|(|2<rsup|4>*4!|)>=1>. Locality and
    gauge invariance identify its value with
    <math|F<rsub|x><around|(|V|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>>. This proves
    <math|L<rsub|x>=0>. It also proves its stated read support: the largest
    displacement is at most <math|w+4\<less\>e>, and each mask reads a
    further radius <math|e>.

    Here are the quantitative estimates on the sum before this localization.
    Put <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>> and
    <math|\<frak-h\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|3/8>>. In the convergent connected sums
    multiply the majorant of each ordinary elementary factor by
    <math|\<frak-h\>>. The rooted attachment parameter still tends to zero,
    since <math|\<frak-h\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/16>>. A
    term with at least three such factors across its molecules gains
    <math|\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>> upon restoring its original weights. A term
    with a retained large-field overlap has the same gain because its
    suppression is at least <math|\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>>. The sum after
    multiplication has size at most a subpower factor times
    <math|\<frak-h\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>. Thus the terms in these two
    classes have bound

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-height-gain>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>*<around|(|\<frak-h\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>|)>=P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-19/16>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>
    </equation>

    after the coupling threshold is raised. The reserve is
    <math|19/16-23/20=3/80>. This estimate also bounds fixed ordered
    derivatives: their placement polynomial was included before the rooted
    sum.

    First retain the inherited terms using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited>; their principal sum is made
    smaller than a prescribed multiple of <math|H<rsub|j>> by choosing the
    ratio, and their comparison remainder by raising the threshold. Among all
    other connected terms retain also those whose total support size exceeds
    the eligibility radius: their support decay is at most
    <math|\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>> after the threshold is raised, so the estimate
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain> applies to them. A term with an incoming
    regular factor and at least one further factor is not eligible. The
    rooted estimate in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>,
    with <math|W<rsub|0>> that factor and at least one attached factor,
    bounds these terms by <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>>.
    The remaining terms are eligible: the selected connected contributions
    and path terms of length at most <math|B>, to which the reflected-period
    construction is applied, and the eligible tail, of weighted norm at most
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>. Long paths have an additional
    factor <math|e<rsup|-w<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*B/4>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>; it converts their
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>> bound to <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>>.
    Eligible connected terms with three or four elementary factors already
    obey<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain>; the remaining connected terms have at
    most two. For the finite sum use the stationary integral on each
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>. Their sides <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>> satisfy
    <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B+1|)>>. The entry
    choice <math|B\<geqslant\>j+4> also gives
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|w+i|)>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>+1|)>>
    for their fine periods. Thus the coarse-period form of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>\U<reference|lem:B-D2> applies, without
    requiring these auxiliary periods to belong to the chosen physical-volume
    sequence. Subtract its flat value and the classical cubic, quartic and
    quadratic one-loop terms of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7>.
    Differentiation of its logarithm gives connected expectations under the
    common Gaussian law. For the terms not involving incoming errors, write
    the normalized local integral as <math|\<bbb-E\>*exp
    <around|(|V<rsub|3>+V<rsub|4>+V<rsub|\<geqslant\>5>|)>>, after the
    stationary action and its Gaussian determinant have been extracted. The
    subscripts refer to the Taylor degree in the link chart. Gaussian parity
    removes a lone odd cubic term. The fourth-order stationary terms are
    already in the classical path action, and the field-dependent quadratic
    part of the determinant is already in <math|\<alpha\>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>
    and the quadratic loop atoms. Subtracting the value at <math|1> removes
    the remaining vacuum terms. Expanding <math|log \<bbb-E\>*e<rsup|V>>
    through two factors gives <math|\<bbb-E\>*V+<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*V<rsup|2>-<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*V|)><rsup|2>|)>>;
    its remaining terms have at least three factors. In normalized
    fluctuation coordinates each nonquadratic factor starts at order
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>. The common Gaussian majorant bounds the
    last terms by <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-3/2>>.
    In the one- and two-factor terms that remain after the stated
    cancellations, Taylor's integral remainder and the healthy field bound
    give

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|{|\<frak-b\><rsup|-3/2>+\<frak-b\>*<space|0.17em>t<rsub|m><rsup|5>+\<frak-b\><rsup|-1>*t<rsub|m>+t<rsub|m><rsup|3>+e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*B>|}>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Every radius and chart loss is included in the subpower factor. Each of
    the four power terms has exponent at most <math|-3/2> before that factor;
    the path exponential decreases faster than any fixed power. Terms
    involving incoming activities are not eligible; apart from the inherited
    terms they obey<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain> or the bound
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>
    above. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> bounds these terms simultaneously,
    so the derivative bound for the centered total auxiliary sum is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-aux-bound>C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<around|{|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>\<gtr\>0> is the chosen
    truncation error. The first term is the centered local integral; the two
    volume terms bound the omitted regular sum at the field and at <math|1>.
    Approaching a boundary point of the healthy domain by radial contraction
    preserves these estimates, so they hold on the closed domain used in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>.

    A direction of that definition, restricted to
    <math|N<rsub|e><around|(|x|)>> and reflected, is bounded by
    <math|2*t*<around|(|1+e|)><rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>>. Rescaling
    each of at most <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>> directions
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-aux-bound> therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,N<rsub|e><around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>*<around|(|1+e|)><rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>*<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<around|[|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<around|(|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|)>|]><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All the factors outside the negative powers are fixed powers of
    <math|1+B>. Also <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|N<rsub|e><around|(|x|)>|)>>
    is bounded by a stage constant. The regular bound divided by
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> tends to zero, since
    <math|23/20\<gtr\>11/10>. Finally,<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization>
    and the uncentered <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>> bound give <math|sup
    <around|\||L<rsub|x>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>,
    with the same polynomial read radius. These logarithmic corrections tend
    to zero in the site norm at exponent <math|\<sigma\>+2> and vanish on
    healthy supports. This proves all the estimates claimed for the
    localization.
  </proof>

  To specify the small coefficient, let <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|sub>>>,
  <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|low>>> and
  <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|high>>> be the operator bounds for local
  subtraction, low path replacement and the part of high path replacement
  proportional to <math|H<rsub|j>>. These are the three linear operations in
  the following subsections; they are fixed from the output derivative norm
  and path bounds. Put <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>=C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|sub>>+C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|low>>+C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|high>>>.
  These constants use only derivative orders, direction powers, support
  exponents, and the output path bounds. For a prescribed
  <math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>, choose before the ratios

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|j>=<frac|1|64*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j><rsup|0>|)>>,<space|2em>H<rsub|j>=\<theta\>*c<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The quantity <math|H<rsub|j>> depends on <math|m> through
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>. The next assertion concerns
  the very same block integral and the masks of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums>.

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=e<rsup|\<tau\><rsub|u>\|\<Lambda\>\|-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|U|)>*f<rsub|i,u><around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0><around|(|U|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:D-early>>>|<row|<cell|sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)><cover><rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*\<frak-e\><rsub|i,u>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>,<space|2em>\<frak-e\><rsub|i,u>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i,u>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,X<rsub|i>>,<eq-number><label|eq:D-early-bound>>>|<row|<cell|sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+1|)><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*\<frak-k\><rsub|i,u>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>\<frak-k\><rsub|i,u>\<geqslant\>sup<rsub|U><around|\||k<rsub|i,u><around|(|U|)>|\|><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:D-preliminary-forced>>>>>
  </align>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Preparatory activities><label|lem:D-preliminary> For every input
    curve above, fixed local families <math|f<rsub|i,u>,k<rsub|i,u>> and a
    continuous scalar <math|\<tau\><rsub|u>>
    satisfy<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early>\U<eqref|eq:D-preliminary-forced>. They
    are equivariant and continuous in the weighted norms with exponents
    <math|\<sigma\>+3> and <math|\<sigma\>+1>, respectively. The regular
    functions have the regularity of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>
    before flat-value and Hessian subtraction; the forced functions satisfy
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25>. Restricting the interval preserves
    the identities and estimates.
  </lemma>

  Here <math|\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>\<gtr\>0> is fixed after
  the ratio, and <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>> is the disjoint-support
  exact-cover sum of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, formed from
  <math|k<rsub|i,u>>.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>\U<reference|def:D06>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>, <reference|lem:B-D2>,
    <reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C7>, <reference|lem:D-connected>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>. First choose the ratio so that
    the inherited principal terms of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited>
    use at most <math|H<rsub|j>/32>, and then raise the threshold so that
    their comparison remainders use at most <math|H<rsub|j>/32>. Bound the
    terms retained in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain> and the long paths
    together by <math|H<rsub|j>/16>. The localization bound is
    <math|o<around|(|H<rsub|j>|)>> with the stronger support exponent. This
    fixes a coupling threshold independent of the period.

    Next fix the truncation index. The eligible tail reads only the
    prescribed path, the coupling and the incoming addresses. For each
    coupling in <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>> summability gives a tail smaller
    than <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>/2>; weighted continuity in
    the coupling gives a neighbourhood on which the same tail is smaller than
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>. Finitely many neighbourhoods
    cover the compact <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>; take the largest index.
    This index depends only on the incoming address lists, so it serves every
    admitted input with these lists. Increasing the index preserves the tail
    bound. Choose the tail bound to be at most <math|H<rsub|j>/16>. The
    regular terms outside the finite sum then have total at most
    <math|<around|(|1/16+2/16|)>*H<rsub|j>>. Choose
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> so that the two localization
    errors in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-aux-bound>, including the direction and
    support losses, have total at most <math|12*H<rsub|j>/16>. The resulting
    bound is <math|15*H<rsub|j>/16\<less\>H<rsub|j>>.

    The change in the scalar is <math|\<tau\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|old>>-F<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>>,
    with the sign fixed by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization>. Multiply the
    preliminary forced sum by <math|exp <around|(|-<big|sum><rsub|x>L<rsub|x>|)>>.
    Expand each <math|e<rsup|-L<rsub|x>>> about one and collect intersecting
    supports, as in the first paragraph of the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion>. The logarithmic factors vanish
    unless their supports contain flags, so each resulting connected
    component still contains a forced term. Their site bound at exponent
    <math|\<sigma\>+2> is a subpower factor times
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>, whereas each forced component retains
    <math|C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>>.
    Choose a smaller positive <math|r<rprime|'><rsub|j>\<less\>r<rsub|j>>.
    The site attachment parameter tends to zero and the remaining exponential
    gives

    <\equation*>
      C<rprime|'><rsub|j>*e<rsup|-r<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>
    </equation*>

    for some <math|\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>\<gtr\>0>, since
    <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>\<geqslant\><refbeta><rsub|m+1>/2>. One
    extra support power is used in this collection, leaving
    <math|\<sigma\>+1>. The rooted-tree induction written in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion> proves the asserted site bound and
    absolute convergence. No scalar depends on the output configuration, and
    all identities hold also when a chord is exactly at a threshold.

    For continuity, the truncation and all reflected tori are now fixed.
    Finite linear combinations preserve the weighted comparison from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. A difference of forced products
    has one marked difference factor; the rooted sum has the same strict
    attachment margin. Dominated convergence proves the weighted comparison
    after the collection. Flat evaluation gives continuity of the scalar. The
    support enlargement, inventories and conjunctions of tests are fixed. The
    same constructions commute with translations and signed permutations by
    reindexing. Every assertion is pointwise in the interval parameter or a
    convergence assertion within the interval, so restriction to a closed
    subinterval preserves it.
  </proof>

  The preparatory regular sum is <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,u>>
  on the healthy domain. Its weighted bound is the anchored sum of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b>, before flat-value and Hessian
  subtraction. The next identity supplies the scalar reset.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Pre-reset expansion><label|lem:D4> For every input curve above and
    every <math|u>, there is a preparatory regular family with sum
    <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>> and weighted bound
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> such that, when
    <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=exp
      <around|{|\<tau\><rsub|u>\|\<Lambda\>\|-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>><around|(|U|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|N,m><math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|U|)>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>,\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>\<gtr\>0>
    on this set.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early>\U<eqref|eq:D-early-bound>. Set
    <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,u>> on
    configurations without flags. Every health mask is one, and nonempty
    forced inventories give <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>=1>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums> gives
    <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>=<math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>>.
    Dropping the extra three support powers gives the stated bound, since
    <math|\<theta\>*c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/64>. Absolute convergence makes
    the exponent finite and real, proving positivity, including at <math|1>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Local quadratic subtraction>

  The subtraction is local and uses no bound on the eventual coupling
  correction. We use the clover curvature of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-clover-linear>
  for the local quadratic subtraction.

  For a preliminary <math|f> on <math|X> anchored at <math|o>, put
  <math|Z=X\<cup\>N<rsub|2><around|(|o|)>>. For <math|q=<around|(|a,b|)>> and
  <math|T\<in\><g>>, set <math|A<rsup|q,T><rsub|<around|(|y,c|)>>=\<b-1\><rsub|c=b>*\<xi\><rsub|o><around|(|y|)><rsup|a>*T>,
  and fix a unit vector <math|T<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\><g>>. Define

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>>|<cell|=D<rsup|2>[f(exp\<cdot\><around|)||]><around|(|0|)><around|[|A<rsup|q,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>,A<rsup|r,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|f><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|q,r>q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)><around|\<langle\>|C<rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>,C<rsub|r>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Set <math|q<rsub|f>=0> when <math|Z> wraps. The matrix extracts the
  response to constant curls; its color independence is proved below. Define
  the regular remainder and the discrepancy of masks by

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|f>>|<cell|=f-f<around|(|1|)>-Q<rsub|f>,<label|eq:D-local-split>>>|<row|<cell|\<zeta\><rsub|f>>|<cell|=\<b-1\><rsub|Z<space|0.17em><math-up|unhealthy>>*<around|{|f<around|(|1|)>+h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|f>-h<rsub|X>*f|}><space|0.17em>.<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  The following identity is checked before any activities are summed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local subtraction><label|lem:D-local-subtraction> For every
    preliminary regular <math|f>,

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|X>*f=f<around|(|1|)>+h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|f>-\<zeta\><rsub|f>+h<rsub|Z>*R<rsub|f><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The function <math|R<rsub|f>> is regular on <math|Z>, vanishes at
    <math|1>, and has zero affine Hessian when <math|Z> is nonwrapping. The
    Borel gauge-invariant function <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> depends only on
    internal links and flags in <math|Z> and vanishes when <math|Z> is
    healthy.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D05a>,
    <reference|def:D24> and the clover formula. The first derivative of
    <math|f> at the flat field vanishes: constant gauge transformations make
    its coefficient on each internal link an adjoint-invariant vector of
    <math|<g>>, hence a central vector, which is zero. Differentiating local
    gauge invariance again makes its Hessian annihilate an infinitesimal
    gradient in either argument. On a nonwrapping support an affine field
    with zero curl is a local gradient. Its constant and symmetric linear
    parts are differences of a quadratic site field, with a constant
    adjustment for the discrete difference. Multiply this site field by a
    periodic cutoff equal to one on <math|Z>. The internal links read by
    <math|f> are unchanged. The second differentiation of gauge invariance
    uses <math|D*f<around|(|1|)>=0>, as proved above, so no second derivative
    of the gauge orbit remains. Hence the affine Hessian depends only on the
    constant curls.

    For each pair of orientations its color form is an invariant bilinear
    form. Represent it by an operator on <math|<g>> using the trace inner
    product. The operator commutes with the adjoint action. Its self-adjoint
    part has ideal eigenspaces, so simplicity makes it scalar. Its
    skew-adjoint part <math|J> commutes with infinitesimal adjoint actions,
    giving

    <\equation*>
      <around|[|J*X,Y|]>=J*<around|[|X,Y|]>=<around|[|X,J*Y|]><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|<math-up|ad><around|(|J*X|)>=J<math-up|ad><around|(|X|)>> is
    both self-adjoint, as a product of commuting skew-adjoint operators, and
    skew-adjoint. It is zero, and <math|J*X> is central. Hence <math|J=0>.
    The color coefficient is therefore the one obtained by testing
    <math|T<rsub|\<ast\>>>.

    The side satisfies <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>\<gtr\>20>,
    which permits the clover loops and affine tests without degeneracies.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-clover-linear> identifies the affine Hessian of
    <math|Q<rsub|f>> with that of <math|f>. Subtracting gives zero diagonal
    affine Hessian for <math|R<rsub|f>>; polarization gives zero on pairs.
    The value and first derivative of <math|R<rsub|f>> vanish at <math|1>. If
    <math|Z> wraps, no Hessian cancellation is required. The clover
    polynomial is smooth, Borel, gauge invariant and local on
    <math|N<rsub|2><around|(|o|)>>, which gives the other regularity
    assertions on <math|Z>.

    All indicators in <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> read flags in <math|Z>. If
    <math|Z> is healthy, every mask in the identity is one and
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>=0>; the identity reduces to the definition of
    <math|R<rsub|f>>. If <math|Z> is unhealthy, <math|h<rsub|Z>*R<rsub|f>=0>
    and the definition of <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> gives the identity
    directly. This proves the equality on every configuration and the stated
    locality and cancellations.
  </proof>

  The same subtraction has a quantitative bound. If <math|a<rsub|f>>
  majorizes <math|f>, the diagonal tests <math|t*A<rsup|q,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
  and their half-sum are permitted directions in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. They give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-projection-bound>t<rsup|2>*<around|\||q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,q|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|f>,<space|2em>t<rsup|2>*<around|\||q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>3*a<rsub|f><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Indeed four times the half-sum's diagonal Hessian is
  <math|t<rsup|2>*<around|(|q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,q|)>+q<rsub|f>*<around|(|r,r|)>+2*q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>|)>>.
  Its absolute value and the two diagonal bounds give the second estimate.
  Product differentiation of the four-link formula bounds every permitted
  derivative of <math|C<rsub|q>> by <math|C<rsub|j>*t> at a healthy anchor.
  For order zero this follows from the plaquette chord bound; at positive
  order the direction factors supply <math|t>, and all undifferentiated group
  matrices are bounded. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-remainder-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|f>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,Z>+sup<rsub|U><around|\||\<zeta\><rsub|f><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*a<rsub|f><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  For <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>>, the terms <math|Q<rsub|f>> and <math|f> occur
  only under their own health indicators, so the estimates still apply when
  <math|Z> is unhealthy.

  Enlargement costs at most <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z|)>\<leqslant\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>+1>:
  one radius-<math|B> ball centered at <math|o\<in\>X> contains
  <math|N<rsub|2><around|(|o|)>> and meets the original connected cover. Also
  <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,o|)>\<leqslant\>C*B<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z|)>>
  for <math|x\<in\>Z>, by following the connected cover. For a support of
  covering number <math|n> through <math|x>, its anchor lies within distance
  <math|C*B*n> of <math|x>, giving at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>> possible
  anchors. The inequality <math|<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|n>>
  converts exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3> at the anchor to exponent
  <math|\<sigma\>+1> at a site: one support power bounds this polynomial and
  one remains unused. At exponent <math|\<sigma\>> the enlargement itself
  costs only a stage constant. Without these extra powers the conversion
  would leave a polynomial in <math|n> at the original exponent.

  Sum <math|q<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>> at each anchor. Translation covariance makes
  the result independent of the anchor. Reflection in an axis belonging to
  exactly one of two distinct orientations changes the sign of the
  off-diagonal entry, so that entry is zero. Permuting axes identifies the
  diagonal entries. Thus a single real <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> satisfies

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=o>q<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>*<around|(|q,r|)>>|<cell|=\<Delta\><rsub|u>*\<delta\><rsub|q*r>,<label|eq:D-isotropy>>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|i>|}>>*Q<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>>|<cell|=<frac|\<Delta\><rsub|u>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||C<rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\|><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  The coefficient sums converge absolutely
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-projection-bound>, and
  <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>>. This
  estimates the subtractions, and already gives
  <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>=\<theta\>*c<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/10><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|-2>\<less\>1>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> obtains <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>
  from a test configuration, which also identifies <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>
  as the unique root of the scalar equation.

  <subsection|Forced fusion and the activity bounds>

  Put <math|\<alpha\>=\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>. The additional regular functions
  and logarithmic corrections are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>|<cell|=\<Delta\><rsub|u>*<around|(|c<rsub|\<lambda\>>-r<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>,<label|eq:D-kinetic-split>>>|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>|<cell|=<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*<around|(|L<rsub|\<lambda\>>-h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*r<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>*<around|(|h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>-h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|}>>|)>*c<rsub|\<lambda\>><space|0.17em>.<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  The regular function is used with mask <math|h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>>.
  Its affine Hessian vanishes because its two terms have the same
  constant-curl response. Add also the regular cubic and quartic functions
  <math|-<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>>,
  with their prescribed masks; they have zero Hessian because
  <math|k\<geqslant\>3>. Every <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>> vanishes on
  healthy <math|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>.

  Write the logarithmic corrections <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>> and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>> as <math|\<ell\><rsub|a>> on nonempty
  supports <math|Z<rsub|a>>. A connected selection <math|P> consists of
  preliminary forced labels and logarithmic labels, contains at least one
  forced label, and has pairwise disjoint preliminary forced supports.
  Connected means connected in the graph joining intersecting supports. Its
  support <math|Y<rsub|P>> is the union of all supports, including
  logarithmic supports; its inventory <math|I<rsub|P>> is the union of the
  preliminary forced inventories. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-fused-term>K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|Y<rsub|P>\<cap\><flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|P>|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P<rsub|<math-up|for>>>k<rsub|i,u><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>P<rsub|<math-up|log>>><around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>>-1|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The extra test is the conjunction of the preliminary forced tests.
  Selections and tests are fixed as <math|u> varies.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Forced fusion><label|lem:D-fusion> Suppose
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>sup<rsub|U><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    For <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>,\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>0> assume
    nonnegative <math|v<rsub|a>> for all forced and logarithmic labels with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||k<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*v<rsub|i>,<space|1em><around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|a>,<space|1em>sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+1|)><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>*v<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|1em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Then the exact-cover gas of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fused-term> equals
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>*exp <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsub|a>|)>>
    and its forced norm of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b> is at most
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06> and the inventories of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25>. Expand <math|exp
    <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsub|a>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|)>|)>>.
    Decompose a finite selection, together with a selection from
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>>, into support-overlap components. A
    nonzero logarithmic factor meets a flag. Exact coverage assigns that flag
    to a preliminary forced label, whose support meets the logarithmic
    support. Every component therefore contains a forced label. Their union
    supports are disjoint; their union inventories cover all flags.
    Conversely the components recover the original selection uniquely. The
    eligibility indicator in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fused-term> equals one on any
    such nonzero product: another component cannot supply a flag in
    <math|Y<rsub|P>>, since its support is disjoint. This proves the identity
    for finite expansions.

    For the absolute bound retain <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>>
    from one forced label per connected selection. Apply the rooted-tree
    bound to the remaining <math|v<rsub|a>>. The total weight of labels
    meeting a support <math|Z> is at most
    <math|<around|\||Z|\|>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z|)>>,
    using the extra exponent in the hypothesis. Successive attachments are
    therefore paid by that exponent. More explicitly, put
    <math|\<frak-v\><rsub|i>=v<rsub|i>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>>>
    and let <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>> sum rooted trees of height
    at most <math|n>, permitting repeated labels. The exponential formula for
    their unordered branches is

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=\<frak-v\><rsub|i>,<space|2em>T<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|n+1|)>>=\<frak-v\><rsub|i>*exp
      <around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|k:Z<rsub|k>\<cap\>Z<rsub|i>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>T<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The hypothesis gives <math|T<rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>\<leqslant\>\<frak-v\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>>>
    by induction. The sum of these upper bounds over roots whose supports
    contain a fixed site is at most <math|\<gamma\>>. Every connected
    selection has a spanning tree and an eligible root, so its sum is at most
    <math|\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1>. The covering number is
    subadditive on intersecting unions, which retains
    <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|P>|)>>> in this
    comparison. Restoring <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>> gives
    the claimed bound. The resulting summable envelope justifies passage from
    finite expansions to the infinite identity.
  </proof>

  Let <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>> be the masked sum of the
  <math|R<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>>, the <math|e<rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>, and the cubic
  and quartic corrections. Let <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u>> be the fused gas.
  Hold these functions fixed while a trial parameter <math|\<vartheta\>>
  varies, and put

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>>=e<rsup|-<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<vartheta\>|)><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The output representation is the following single list: regular addresses
  <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|i>,o<rsub|i>|)>> with functions
  <math|R<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>>, addresses <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>,o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>>
  with functions <math|e<rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>, the cubic and quartic addresses
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D22> with functions
  <math|-<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>>,
  and forced addresses <math|<around|(|Y<rsub|P>,I<rsub|P>,\<cal-E\><rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|extra>>|)>>
  with functions <math|K<rsub|P>>. Each regular function carries its own
  health mask. We prove the inequalities

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-fusion-small>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> before using them for the density
  identity. They impose no condition on the trial coupling.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Activity bounds and covariance><label|lem:D1> For every coarse
    output constructed above and every Wilson representation of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>, both activity families are countable and
    equivariant under translations and <math|<hypercubic>>, including
    supports, anchors, inventories and tests. There exist nonnegative
    majorants as in Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>
    whose weighted series converge and which satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  The proof follows; the Wilson case is treated in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> extends these
  conclusions to all the chosen classes.

  <\render-proof|Proof for a coarse output>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>,
    <reference|lem:D-preliminary>, <reference|lem:D-local-subtraction>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-remainder-bound>
    controls each subtracted function. The low regular terms cost
    <math|C<rsub|j>*H<rsub|j>>, since <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>>.
    The high terms cost <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>+H<rsub|j>|)>>:
    each has at least three curvature factors, and
    <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>*t\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>>.
    A derivative of a word of length <math|l> costs a fixed power of
    <math|1+l>, by the product rule and the allowed spatial growth of the
    directions. The support obeys <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>\<leqslant\>C+l/B>.
    Raise the threshold until <math|\<sigma\>/B\<leqslant\>w<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>/4>.
    A quarter of the path exponent then bounds the support weight, and
    another quarter bounds this polynomial, since
    <math|sup<rsub|l\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+l|)><rsup|k>*e<rsup|-w*l/2>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Summing Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> gives the previously defined
    constant <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>> and a constant <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|1>>
    with

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><rsup|0>*H<rsub|j>+C<rsub|j><rsup|1><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The constant <math|c<rsub|j>=<around|[|64*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j><rsup|0>|)>|]><rsup|-1>>
    was chosen before the ratios in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>.
    After the ratio is fixed, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> bounds
    <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>; raise the entry threshold until
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|1><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/2048>.
    This is possible because <math|t<rsup|3>/\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-2/5><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|3>\<to\>0>.
    Take <math|\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1/32> before the ratio. Since
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>*c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/64>, the sum is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024>. The same argument
    allows any prescribed smaller coefficient by choosing <math|\<theta\>>
    first and raising the threshold for the second term.

    For the logarithmic corrections, conversion from anchored sums to site
    sums costs at most <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>, after the
    extra support powers pay the powers of the covering number. The local
    subtraction costs <math|C<rsub|j>*H<rsub|j>>. The low terms
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-kinetic-split> cost

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<around|{|<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>+H<rsub|j>/t<rsup|2>|)>*t+H<rsub|j>|}>=:m<rsub|j><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The linear clipped tail is bounded by <math|2*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t>
    times its coefficient, since chords are at most two. For a failed relaxed
    health test, the remote threshold supplies
    <math|e<rsup|l/R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>> in a path of
    length <math|l>. It is paid by the path decay because
    <math|R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<geqslant\>12>.
    The common-anchor clover term costs <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>*t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>>.
    These estimates give exactly the displayed bound, including unhealthy
    configurations. Since <math|H<rsub|j>/t=O<rsub|j><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-3/5>/<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)>>
    and <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> is fixed after the ratio,
    <math|<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*m<rsub|j>\<to\>0>.

    Let <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>=\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>/2>.
    The majorants in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-preliminary-forced>, multiplied by
    <math|e<rsup|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>>, have rooted total at most
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>>. For each logarithm use
    <math|<around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|a>|\|>>
    once its individual majorant is at most <math|log 2>; this follows by
    increasing the entry threshold until <math|m<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log 2>.
    Their rooted total is at most <math|2*m<rsub|j>>. Increase the entry
    threshold so that <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/2>
    for <math|\<gamma\>=e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>+2*m<rsub|j>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion> applies. Increase the threshold
    further so that <math|\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024>.
    Indeed <math|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1\<leqslant\>2*\<gamma\>> and
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>\<gg\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|1/5>>.
    This proves the forced inequality and summability
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fusion-small>.

    Every address is a finite combination of countable preliminary and path
    addresses, so the families are countable. Subtraction and path
    replacement transport the entire address under translations. Signed
    permutations transform the curvature orientations with their signs; the
    finite group average includes every transformed term. These permutations
    act on supports, anchors and functions. On a connected selection they
    also transform the union inventory and conjunction of tests.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fused-term> therefore has the required
    covariance. For an inverse link, <math|<around|(|e<rsup|z*A>*U|)><rsup|-1>=e<rsup|-z<math-up|Ad><rsub|U<rsup|-1>><space|0.17em>A>*U<rsup|-1>>.
    Differentiating this identity repeatedly gives only finitely many
    product-rule terms through order <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>; the
    adjoint-invariant norm bounds them by a constant depending on that order.
    This proves the complete address covariance and the convergent weighted
    bounds for a coarse output. The Wilson start is established below;
    induction in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> then gives the class
    assertion.
  </render-proof>

  The identity below fixes the whole density before the scalar estimate of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>. Its proof uses the bounds just established.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exact density representation><label|lem:D5> The final activities
    satisfy Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, vanish at the flat field in their
    regular part, and are independent of <math|\<vartheta\>>. For every
    <math|u> there is <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>\<in\><R>> such that

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>><around|(|U|)>=<frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>><space|2em><text|for
      every >U<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=1> when
    <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>,
    <reference|lem:D-local-subtraction>, <reference|lem:D-fusion>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early>\U<eqref|eq:D-early-bound>,
    including<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fusion-small>. Choose <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-isotropy> and put
    <math|c<rsub|u>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,u><around|(|1|)>>. Local
    subtraction gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|X<rsub|i>>*f<rsub|i,u>=c<rsub|u>+<frac|\<Delta\><rsub|u>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||C<rsub|q>|\|><rsup|2>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<zeta\><rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>+<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|Z<rsub|i>>*R<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All sums converge absolutely. For each low
    term<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-kinetic-split> gives

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*L<rsub|\<lambda\>>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>*h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*<around|(|c<rsub|\<lambda\>>-r<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>-\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>=\<alpha\>*h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*r<rsub|\<lambda\>>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>*h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|}>>*c<rsub|\<lambda\>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Sum and apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums>. The high corrections cancel
    <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*\<cal-A\><rsub|m>>. Thus
    the exponent in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early> becomes

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|u>-c<rsub|u>/<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>|)>\|\<Lambda\>\|-<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>+<big|sum><rsub|i>\<zeta\><rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion> incorporates the last two sums into
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u>>. Hence, on every configuration,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-unnormalized>\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|u>-c<rsub|u>/<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>|)><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>*q<rsub|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    At <math|1> all path and final regular terms vanish. The only exact-cover
    forced selection is empty, so <math|q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>><around|(|1|)>=1>
    for every <math|\<vartheta\>>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-unnormalized>
    proves the quotient identity and positivity of its denominator. The same
    empty-selection argument gives <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u>=1> whenever there
    are no flags.

    The regular cancellations were proved in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-local-subtraction> and
    after<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-kinetic-split>; the weighted bounds are those of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>. The value <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> used in
    the activity construction is already fixed by the preliminary functions.
    The trial variable <math|\<vartheta\>> occurs only in the exponential
    defining <math|q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>>>. This proves the exact identity
    without using Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> or its reset estimate.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Continuity at fixed volume>

  The scalar reset needs continuity of the integral and both final sums. The
  next deduction uses the weighted comparison in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Continuity of the activity sums><label|lem:D6> For input curves of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, the final activity families have fixed
    addresses and are continuous in the weighted sense of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>. For every fixed configuration
    <math|U>,

    <\equation*>
      u\<longmapsto\>\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>u\<longmapsto\>E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>u\<longmapsto\>\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>
    </equation*>

    are continuous.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D06>,
    <reference|def:D25a>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>,
    <reference|lem:D-local-subtraction>, <reference|lem:D-fusion>, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>. The maps
    <math|f\<mapsto\>f<around|(|1|)>,q<rsub|f>,R<rsub|f>,\<zeta\><rsub|f>>
    are linear on functions with fixed address.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-projection-bound> applied to a difference
    bounds its projection coefficients by its derivative majorant divided by
    <math|t<rsup|2>>. Weighted preliminary continuity therefore gives
    continuity of <math|c<rsub|u>>, <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>, and the
    subtracted regular and logarithmic series. The low and high corrections
    are linear in <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>, with fixed summable path
    coefficients, so they retain this continuity. Every mask is a fixed
    function of <math|U>.

    For fusion each coefficient is a finite product and hence continuous in
    supremum norm. To justify summing weighted differences, work first along
    subsequences with one summable majorant. Given
    <math|u<rsub|n>\<to\>u<rsub|0>>, pass to a subsequence whose total
    weighted preliminary discrepancies are at most
    <math|2<rsup|-n>*\<varepsilon\>>. Add their nonnegative majorants to
    those at <math|u<rsub|0>>; the extra mass is at most
    <math|\<varepsilon\>>. The margin <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/2>
    arranged in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> permits this
    addition for small <math|\<varepsilon\>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion> gives one summable envelope for the
    fused coefficients along this subsequence. Dominated convergence gives
    convergence of their weighted differences. If the original sequence
    failed to converge, a subsequence with discrepancies bounded away from
    zero would admit such a further subsequence, a contradiction. This proves
    weighted continuity without assuming a common envelope on an arbitrary
    neighborhood. Constants here may depend on the fixed volume.

    For the pointwise gas, put <math|M<rsub|i>=max <around|(|sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i,u>|\|>,sup <around|\||K<rsub|i,u<rsub|0>>|\|>|)>>.
    Telescoping finite products and summing selections gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-F\><rsub|u<rsub|0>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i>M<rsub|i>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>sup
      <around|\||K<rsub|i,u>-K<rsub|i,u<rsub|0>>|\|><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The restrictions on exact coverage use fixed inventories. Weighted
    continuity makes the right side tend to zero. The regular sum is
    continuous by the analogous sum of order-zero differences. The same
    bounds give continuity in <math|u>, uniform in <math|U>, for the input
    density at fixed volume, since its path action is affine in the
    continuous coupling.

    Finally the kernel of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> is uniformly
    bounded on a compact coupling interval at this volume. The tag masses sum
    to one; its single-link normalizing integrals are positive and continuous
    in the coupling, and its link factors are bounded on the compact group.
    Dominated convergence against product probability Haar measure gives
    continuity of <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>>. This proves all
    three pointwise continuities and weighted continuity, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The specified recursion>

  A choice of output for one representation does not determine a step on
  every continuous input curve. We choose the local construction for an
  entire curve, including its tree gauge and address identifications (the
  truncation index and auxiliary tori depend only on the incoming address
  lists), and then allow restrictions of that choice. The estimates used to
  repeat the step are the following explicit ones. At level <math|m> the path
  is the fixed <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, with the bounds of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>. The activity functions
  satisfy Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>. At an adjacent pair the output is exactly
  the functions in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>, its scalar is
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log \<cal-H\><around|(|1|)>>, and
  its correction obeys

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-retained>t<rsub|m><rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>H<rsub|j>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024,<space|2em><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The first three bounds precede the scalar reset; the last is obtained in
  the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>. The stronger preparatory
  bounds, fixed tests, and whole-interval comparison of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> are retained with the step. The
  input coupling belongs to <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>; no output-band
  condition is imposed. These are the continuation conditions used here.

  Define <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>=<around|{|\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>:\<beta\>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|N>|}>>
  by the Wilson construction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>, and put
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,N,p>=\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>>. Inductively downwards,
  let <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>> contain all outputs of the construction on
  continuous curves in <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m+1,p>> with coupling in
  <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>, retaining their restrictions to closed
  subintervals and their fixed address identifications. Define
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>> to consist of the output\Uinput pairs from
  these same curves and restrictions. The analytic construction below fixes
  these choices simultaneously on the common address spaces; its single step
  map and its restrictions are used for the selected recursion and endpoint
  tuning. Thus an adjacent pair always uses the actual input density and its
  own coupling in the kernel.

  The following statement fixes these choices for the later trajectory
  argument. Its proof also shows that it applies to every admitted continuous
  curve, including curves assembled from previously retained outputs.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Specified recursion><label|lem:D2> For fixed
    <math|G,<blockpenalty>>, the classes just defined consist of
    representations satisfying Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, with

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>\<subset\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,N,p>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>\<subset\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>\<times\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m+1,p><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its trajectories are exactly the tuples satisfying

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|m>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>*<around|(|0\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N|)>,<space|1em>\<rho\><rsub|N>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>,<space|1em><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|m>,\<rho\><rsub|m+1>|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>*<around|(|m\<less\>N|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Every member satisfies the activity bounds in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-retained>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>, <reference|lem:C8>,
    <reference|lem:D-preliminary>, and<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>\U<reference|lem:D6>.
    The Wilson construction at the end supplies the initial curve and both
    caps. At a step, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> uses only the
    prescribed paths, the local regularity and forced eligibility, the bounds
    and the weighted continuity of its input. The proof does not require that
    the input curve coincide with an earlier chosen curve. Its truncation
    index depends only on the incoming address lists, so the same selection
    serves every admitted input curve with these lists. Local subtraction,
    path replacement and fusion preserve the read supports, gauge invariance,
    cancellations and countability. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> proves
    their numerical bounds, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> proves weighted
    continuity on the interval.

    The normalized identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> and the reset of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> identify the same <math|\<Delta\>>. Its
    proof identifies <math|\<Delta\>> with the continuous scalar root and
    gives <math|<around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>; its statement gives
    the drift in Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>. Defining the vacuum scalar
    by flat evaluation gives the exact integral, since the normalized
    representation is one at <math|1>. Hence<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-retained> is
    inherited by the outputs and the prescribed paths are unchanged. Finite
    induction gives the classes just described.

    Restriction preserves every estimate, all supports and tests, and each
    curve choice. Its weighted continuity is continuity in the relative
    topology of the smaller interval, so it is preserved as well. Declare a
    step selected on a larger interval and its restriction to be the same
    choice. Other input curves are treated by the preceding construction,
    rather than by pointwise ancestry. This proves readiness on every
    continuous input curve in the classes. The displayed trajectory
    conditions are exactly the resulting finite recursion.
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E> uses its closure under restriction
    for shooting; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1> below supplies the weighted
    analytic estimates for the selected recursion. The Wilson identity and
    the final one-step assembly are proved after those estimates.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Weighted analytic estimates>

  We compare cutoffs using activity functions on fixed domains. At level
  <math|m> and period <math|p>, put all regular addresses into one countable
  list and all forced addresses into a second list. A missing term has
  coefficient zero. The lists contain every finite support, anchor and
  inventory used above, and every finite conjunction of their eligibility
  tests. The output of a fusion is indexed by its finite selection, so the
  lists are closed under the construction. Keep the same permutations for
  translations and signed permutations. This identifies different cutoffs
  without changing their health domains.

  For a difference <math|A=<around|(|E,K|)>> of activity families set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-common-norm><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,p>=max
    <around*|{|<\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<frac|sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<frac|sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*sup<rsub|U><around|\||K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|\|>|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>>>>>>
    </aligned>|}><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The seminorms in this formula are the least majorants in
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>. Quotient by
  zero seminorm and complete the space with the prescribed local
  cancellations, eligibility and permutations. Denote the resulting real
  Banach space by <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>>. This retains regular
  derivatives on healthy configurations and forced values on every
  configuration. The vacuum scalar is excluded.

  The coupling coordinate must allow relative changes of <math|\<beta\>>
  while keeping a uniform analytic radius. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-coupling-coordinate>\<chi\><rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/32>,<space|1em>b<rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m><refbeta><rsub|m>,<space|1em>x<rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|m>/<refbeta><rsub|m>>,<space|1em>\<beta\><rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m><around|(|\<chi\><rsub|m>*x<rsub|m>|)><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Thus <math|b<rsub|m>> converts a change of <math|x<rsub|m>> into a change
  of <math|\<beta\><rsub|m>>, up to the factor
  <math|2*\<chi\><rsub|m>*x<rsub|m>>. Monotonicity gives
  <math|b<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|0><refbeta><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>.
  Row<nbsp>7 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices> gives
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|224>>; then
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/128>. In the following paragraphs write
  <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<chi\><rsub|0><refbeta><rsub|0>>,
  <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/128> and <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>=1/16>.

  Let <math|F<rsub|N,m>> denote the selected activity map and
  <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>> its scalar correction, so that

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|m>=F<rsub|N,m>*<around|(|x<rsub|m+1>,A<rsub|m+1>|)>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|m>=\<beta\><rsub|m+1>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m><around|(|x<rsub|m+1>,A<rsub|m+1>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The top map is denoted by <math|F<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|x<rsub|N>|)>>.
  The maps are constructed for <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>
  and <math|33/40\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m>*x\<leqslant\>47/40>. All inputs
  in this domain have the common address lists, so the truncation index and
  auxiliary tori of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> are the same at
  every point. Hence <math|F<rsub|N,m>> and <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>> are
  single-valued, and their restriction to a line is the construction on that
  line. We use expansions centred in the smaller domain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-analytic-domain><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/8,<space|2em>17/20\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m>*x\<leqslant\>23/20<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  A ball of radius <math|1/4> about such a centre stays in the larger domain:
  its activity norm is at most <math|3/8>, and the change of
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>*x> is at most <math|1/512\<less\>1/40>. For a
  retained history with <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|0>-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/4>,
  summing the drift and the error bound in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> gives
  <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|m>-<refbeta><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><refbeta><rsub|m>/4>.
  Its square root therefore lies in <math|<around|[|17/20,23/20|]>>. Every
  activity in the history, including the Wilson start, has norm at most
  <math|1/1024> by the proofs of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>. Thus the entire history lies
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-analytic-domain>.

  For a finite stencil with real coefficients <math|c<rsub|\<nu\>>> and nodes
  <math|s<rsub|\<nu\>>\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>, define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-real-stencil>\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<rho\>|)>=sup<rsub|k\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>/\<rho\>|)><rsup|k>|\|><space|0.17em>,<space|2em>\<rho\>\<gtr\>1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  We apply it along <math|<around|(|x<around|(|s|)>,A<around|(|s|)>|)>=<around|(|x+s*v,A<rsub|0>+s*A<rsub|1>|)>>,
  with <math|<around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|0>+s*A<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1> for
  <math|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. Supports, health tests and local
  function domains are fixed along the line. Write
  <math|F<around|(|s|)>=F<rsub|N,m>*<around|(|x<around|(|s|)>,A<around|(|s|)>|)>>
  and <math|\<cal-D\><around|(|s|)>=\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m><around|(|x<around|(|s|)>,A<around|(|s|)>|)>>.
  Let <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|pre>>> be the anchored regular norm at
  exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3>, before division by
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> and before subtraction. Let
  <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|for>>> be the corresponding forced site norm
  at exponent <math|\<sigma\>+1>.

  The coupling varies through a square of an affine function. The next
  estimate controls that dependence at a radius growing with the reference
  coupling; a bound at one fixed field-derivative order would not suffice.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Coupling-line stencils><label|lem:D-coupling-stencil> Put
    <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>> and
    <math|\<frak-R\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|1/256>>. For the lines above centred in
    <math|33/40\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*x\<leqslant\>47/40>, the
    preparatory families satisfy

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|pre>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*f<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/4>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<frak-R\>/2|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|for>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*k<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<frak-R\>/4|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    For any prescribed <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>\<gtr\>0>, the Wilson
    map satisfies the analogous normalized estimate with radius
    <math|\<frak-R\>> and bound <math|4*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>> after
    the entry threshold is raised.
  </lemma>

  The proof keeps physical fields fixed and then applies the connected and
  localization operations.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>\U<reference|def:D06>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>, <reference|lem:B-D2>,
    <reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C7>, <reference|lem:D-connected>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>. Put
    <math|\<upsilon\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/128>> and raise the threshold until

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-coupling-aperture>128*\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>\<upsilon\>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    This is possible since <math|1/256-1/32+1/128=-5/256\<less\>0>. Set
    <math|\<zeta\>=\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*x> and
    <math|L=\<zeta\>-6*\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>>. For
    <math|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\>6*\<frak-R\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><around|(|s|)>=\<frak-b\>*L<rsup|2>*<around*|(|<frac|\<zeta\>|L>+<frac|\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*v|L>*s|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em><frac|3|4>\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*<around|(|x+s*v|)>\<leqslant\><frac|4|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The displayed margins follow from<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-aperture> and
    <math|33/40\<leqslant\>\<zeta\>\<leqslant\>47/40>. In particular the real
    couplings lie in <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>. The relative scale change
    is at most a fixed multiple of <math|\<upsilon\>>.

    To estimate parameter coefficients, use physical link variables in each
    sector integral. All flag, allocation, tag and chart tests are then
    independent of <math|s>: their thresholds are <math|t<rsub|m+1>> and the
    prescribed stage constants, not <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)><rsup|-1/2>>.
    Old measurable forced functions occur as <math|K<rsub|0>+s*K<rsub|1>> at
    the same physical links. Gaussian rescaling is used to estimate the
    coefficients, after these functions have been held fixed. Thus parameter
    coefficients do not differentiate their field arguments or any test.

    Here is the coefficient bound for this change of Gaussian scale. For a
    positive local precision matrix <math|<math-ss|A>> and affine source
    <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|0>>, put <math|m<rsub|0>=-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1><math-ss|b><rsub|0>>
    and <math|Q<around|(|a|)>=<around|\<langle\>|a-m<rsub|0>,<math-ss|A><around|(|a-m<rsub|0>|)>|\<rangle\>>/2>.
    This is the centred quadratic energy in the sector Gaussian density. The
    parameter occurs through <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)>*Q>, its normalizing
    determinant, and the local stationary corrections. Expand the inverse by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<math-ss|A>+H|)><rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>H|)><rsup|n><math-ss|A><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>log
      det <around|(|I+H|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|n+1>|n><math-up|Tr><space|0.17em>H<rsup|n><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In these formulas <math|H> denotes, respectively, the precision
    perturbation and its dimensionless version. The inverse bound and
    exponential Schur sums of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>, together
    with<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-aperture>, make their
    radius-<math|\<frak-R\>> coefficient sums less than a fixed constant. For
    the Gaussian density, expansion of its exponential is dominated by
    <math|e<rsup|c<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>> with <math|c> smaller than half
    its precision lower bound. Indeed the coefficient of
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>> changes by
    <math|O<around|(|\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>|)>>. Integrating this
    majorant bounds every parameter degree at once. The local number of
    coordinates is a fixed power of <math|1+B>; hence its additional loss is
    bounded because <math|<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|C>*\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>\<to\>0>.
    The same Neumann series solves each stationary correction with the
    uniformly bounded inverse. Ordered field derivatives only add the fixed
    read polynomials already present in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>.

    Each incoming factor is affine in <math|s>. For a regular occurrence its
    exponential has radius-<math|\<frak-R\>> majorant <math|exp
    <around|(|\<frak-R\><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>; for a
    forced occurrence the majorant is <math|<around|\||K<rsub|0>|\|>+\<frak-R\><around|\||K<rsub|1>|\|>>.
    Products use Cauchy products of coefficients. Reserve the support powers
    before summing these expressions. The initial small factor
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-factor-rates> loses at most one factor
    <math|\<frak-R\>> for an incoming affine occurrence; the change of
    Gaussian scale has the bounded sums just proved. Thus the weighted
    attachment parameter is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-R\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>=P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-111/256>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The connected logarithm therefore has an absolutely summable parameter
    series by the same rooted estimate as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. A forced component retains a
    smaller fixed portion of the energy in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>;
    coefficient multiplication and sector counting use the rest. Its
    coefficient majorant is <math|C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>>.

    Apply the five reflected tori of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>
    to these same parameter coefficients. A stencil commutes with their
    finite linear combinations. The direction rescaling, auxiliary volumes,
    support conversion and flat subtraction cost a subpower factor. The
    resulting regular coefficient sum has bound
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-3/8>>: the
    preceding attachment bound is smaller, since <math|111/256\<gtr\>3/8>.
    Raise the threshold until this is at most <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/4>>.
    The regular logarithm and the forced collection may use radii
    <math|\<frak-R\>/2> and <math|\<frak-R\>/4>, respectively; their Cauchy
    products then have geometric reserve. Multiplying degree <math|n> by
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*s<rsub|\<nu\>><rsup|n>> proves
    the asserted stencil bounds.

    For the Wilson map use the formulas in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>. Its regular term is
    <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)>> times a fixed fifth-order remainder. Its
    radius-<math|\<frak-R\>> coefficient sum is at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\>*t<rsub|N><rsup|5>/\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,
    which tends to zero. For a forced charge <math|d<rsub|I>\<geqslant\>0>,
    write <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)>=\<beta\><around|(|0|)>+b<rsub|1>*s+b<rsub|2>*s<rsup|2>>.
    Its exponential has coefficient sum at most

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|{|-\<beta\><around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|I>+<around|(|<around|\||b<rsub|1>|\|>*\<frak-R\>+<around|\||b<rsub|2>|\|>*\<frak-R\><rsup|2>|)>*d<rsub|I>|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<beta\><around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|I>/2>
    </equation*>

    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-aperture>. The inventory count in that proof
    is still summable with this half exponent. Both normalized coefficient
    sums can be made at most <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>>; enlarging
    the bound to <math|4*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>> gives the stated
    estimate. All bounds were taken before the period was counted, so they
    are uniform in the period.
  </proof>

  We now reduce the growing radius to one fixed radius chosen before the
  ratios. Choose <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>1>, and then positive
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>/2> and
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>.
  Put <math|\<varphi\><rsub|m>=\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*H<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>>
  and <math|\<varrho\><rsub|m>=2*\<varphi\><rsub|m>>; here
  <math|H<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>=\<theta\>*c<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>
  is the coefficient for this step. The small fraction <math|\<theta\>> is
  chosen below before the ratios.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Real parameter estimates><label|lem:D-real-lines> For every line
    specified above, the selected local maps satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-stencil-bound><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*F<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<rho\>/2|)>,<space|2em><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*\<cal-D\><around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varphi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<rho\>|)>,
    </equation>

    with <math|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<varrho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|m>>.
    The radius <math|\<rho\>> can be arbitrarily large. The Wilson map
    satisfies the first estimate with the same
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D05b>,
    <reference|def:D06> and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>,
    <reference|lem:D1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil>. Recover
    the regular line coefficients from the latter's stencil bound by finite
    differences at zero, as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-stencil-recovery>
    below. Put <math|\<frak-Q\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|1/256>/2>. Split after degree
    <math|300> and interpolate the finite part at <math|301> fixed distinct
    points of <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>. The norm of this finite interpolation
    matrix gives a constant <math|L<rsub|\<rho\>>> independent of the
    coupling and volume. The radius-<math|\<rho\>> coefficient sum is bounded
    by

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|\<rho\>>*H<rsub|j>+<around|(|1+L<rsub|\<rho\>>|)>*<around|(|\<rho\>/\<frak-Q\>|)><rsup|301>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/4><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the tail each degree <math|n\<geqslant\>301> contributes
    <math|<around|(|\<rho\>/\<frak-Q\>|)><rsup|n>>; its geometric denominator
    is bounded once <math|\<frak-Q\>\<geqslant\>2*\<rho\>>. The tail has rate

    <\equation*>
      -301/256-1/4=-365/256\<less\>-11/10<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> with the smaller
    real-interval target <math|H<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|aux>>=\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*H<rsub|j>/<around|[|4*<around|(|1+L<rsub|\<rho\>>|)>|]>>,
    while retaining <math|H<rsub|j>> as its stated upper bound. This changes
    no identity. Choose this fraction before the ratio, and raise the
    threshold until the tail is at most <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*H<rsub|j>/4>.
    The radius-<math|\<rho\>> bound is then at most
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*H<rsub|j>/2>. Projection
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-projection-bound> gives a scalar bound at most
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*H<rsub|j>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>>. The
    scalar reset in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> is exactly
    this extracted coefficient.

    For the regular output, apply its three linear conversions with total
    bound <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>>. Choose <math|\<theta\>> so the
    radius-<math|\<rho\>> preparatory contribution is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024> after this
    conversion, and raise the threshold so the high path term contributes at
    most another <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024>.
    Explicitly it suffices to choose the real-interval coefficient so that
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>*L<rsub|\<rho\>>*\<theta\>*c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/2048>
    and then to use the tail reserve just proved. For the forced output,
    retain its coefficient majorant from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil>;
    subtraction adds logarithmic factors with the bounds in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>. At fixed <math|\<rho\>>, their
    radius-<math|\<rho\>> sums have the same small attachment parameter, with
    a constant depending on <math|\<rho\>>. Fusion therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|m><around|(|K|)><rsub|\<rho\>/2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<rho\>>*exp
      <around|{|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|out>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>|}>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024
    </equation*>

    for a smaller <math|\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|out>>\<gtr\>0> and a
    larger threshold. Here <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|m>> is the forced norm at the
    output exponent <math|\<sigma\>>, and the subscript denotes the parameter
    coefficient sum at that radius. The exponent <math|\<sigma\>+1> of the
    preparatory bound supplies the extra power used in fusion. The maximum
    norm<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-common-norm> is thus at most <math|1/512>, and can
    be made smaller than the prescribed <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>.
    This derives both the order-zero bound and the full stencil bound. For
    Wilson choose <math|4*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil> and reduce its radius to
    <math|\<rho\>/2>.

    Since <math|\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/64>,
    the scalar normalization gives

    <\equation*>
      \<varrho\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>/32|)><refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/10>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<varrho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>><refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|9/10><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The second inequality follows by comparison with an integral, since the
    reference-coupling increments have a fixed positive lower bound. Divide
    by <math|b<rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|31/32>> and raise the entry
    threshold until <math|C<rsub|G>*\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>><rsup|-11/160>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    This proves the weighted scalar bound.
  </proof>

  Uniform line estimates at nearby centres control mixed finite differences.
  This supplies the multilinear coefficients for the endpoint equations. Let
  <math|X,Y> be real normed spaces, with <math|Y> complete, and let <math|f>
  be defined near <math|x\<in\>X>. Suppose that for all centres
  <math|x<rprime|'>> in a neighbourhood of <math|x>, all
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r> and all stencils
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-real-stencil>,

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*f*<around|(|x<rprime|'>+s<rsub|\<nu\>>*w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;R|)>,<space|2em>r\<gtr\>0,<space|1em>R\<gtr\>1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The domain contains all the indicated real segments.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Recovery from real stencils><label|lem:D-stencil-recovery> Under
    these hypotheses there are continuous symmetric multilinear maps
    <math|T<rsub|k>:X<rsup|k>\<to\>Y> such that

    <\equation*>
      f*<around|(|x+h|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>T<rsub|k>*<around|(|h,\<ldots\>,h|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C<around*|(|<frac|4*e|r*R>|)><rsup|k>
    </equation*>

    whenever <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r> and
    <math|4*e<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r*R>. The coefficients
    extend with the same bounds to the completion of <math|X>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Associate to a stencil the sequence <math|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>/R|)><rsup|k>|)><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>>
    with supremum norm. The assumed inequality defines a bounded linear map,
    of norm at most <math|C>, from their span to <math|Y>. Extend it to the
    closure. The <math|k>th coordinate vector lies in that closure: the
    <math|k>th finite difference at <math|0>, divided by
    <math|k!<around|(|a/R|)><rsup|k>>, has zero moments below <math|k>,
    moment one at <math|k>, and higher moments tending uniformly to zero as
    its mesh <math|a\<to\>0>. To see the uniformity, split the higher moments
    at any fixed order and use <math|<around|\||j*a/R|\|><rsup|n>\<leqslant\><around|(|k*a/R|)><rsup|n>>
    for the remaining tail. Evaluation on a single point then gives the exact
    line series. Its coefficient sum at radius <math|R/2> is at most
    <math|2*C>. Uniqueness follows by successively dividing a vanishing
    series by its first possible power.

    Write <math|P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>> for the coefficient of
    <math|s<rsup|k>> in <math|f*<around|(|x+s*h|)>>. Rescaling <math|s> gives
    <math|P<rsub|k>*<around|(|a*h|)>=a<rsup|k>*P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>> and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C<around*|(|<frac|2<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>>|r*R>|)><rsup|k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    We must prove the polynomial identity in the direction <math|h>. Put
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|h>*g<around|(|a|)>=g*<around|(|a+h|)>-g<around|(|a|)>>.
    Uniform line remainders at nearby centres imply

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|s*h><rsup|<space|0.17em>n>*f*<around|(|x+s*a|)>=O<around|(|s<rsup|n>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|s\<to\>0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed insert the line series at <math|x+s*a> into the <math|n>th forward
    difference. The coefficients below degree <math|n> cancel by
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|n><around|(|-1|)><rsup|n-j><binom|n|j>j<rsup|k>=0>
    for <math|k\<less\>n>; the uniformly bounded tail is
    <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|n>|)>>.

    For <math|h<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|n-1>> and
    <math|T\<subset\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>> set

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|T>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>T><frac|h<rsub|i>|n-i>,<space|2em>w<rsub|T>=-<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>T><frac|i*h<rsub|i>|n-i>.
    </equation*>

    There is the exact mixed-difference identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-mixed-difference>\<delta\><rsub|s*h<rsub|0>>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|s*h<rsub|n-1>>*f*<around|(|x+s*a|)>=<big|sum><rsub|T><around|(|-1|)><rsup|n-<around|\||T|\|>>*\<delta\><rsub|s*v<rsub|T>><rsup|<space|0.17em>n>*f*<around|(|x+s*<around|(|a+w<rsub|T>|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Expand the right side as a sum over its <math|n+1> forward-difference
    points. At the point indexed by <math|j\<less\>n>, pairing sets <math|T>
    differing by the index <math|i=j> cancels the terms, since that index has
    coefficient <math|<around|(|j-i|)>/<around|(|n-i|)>=0>. At <math|j=n> the
    coefficient of each <math|h<rsub|i>> is one, leaving precisely the left
    side. Hence every mixed <math|n>th difference above is
    <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|n>|)>>.

    Expand each of its finitely many values in its line series at <math|x>
    and compare powers of <math|s>. It follows that

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|0>>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|n-1>>*P<rsub|k><around|(|a|)>=0*<space|2em><around|(|k\<less\>n|)>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|n=k+1> this makes the <math|k>th difference of <math|P<rsub|k>>
    independent of its base point. Define

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|k>*<around|(|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|k>|)>=<frac|1|k!>*\<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|k>>*P<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>.
    </equation*>

    The identity <math|\<delta\><rsub|u+v>=\<delta\><rsub|u>+\<delta\><rsub|v>+\<delta\><rsub|u>*\<delta\><rsub|v>>
    proves additivity in each slot, since the extra <math|<around|(|k+1|)>>st
    difference vanishes. The displayed bound on <math|P<rsub|k>> makes these
    additive maps locally bounded, hence continuous and real linear in each
    slot. The operators commute, giving symmetry. Homogeneity gives
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|h><rsup|k>*P<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>=k!P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>>,
    so their diagonal is the original line coefficient.

    There are <math|2<rsup|k>> terms in this polarization sum, and the
    argument in each has norm at most <math|k> on unit directions. Thus
    <math|k<rsup|k>/k!\<leqslant\>e<rsup|k>> gives the stated operator bound.
    The diagonal series equals <math|f*<around|(|x+h|)>> by the line
    recovery; the norm bound proves its convergence on the stated domain.
    Continuous multilinear maps extend uniquely through the dense inclusion
    into the completion, with unchanged norm.
  </proof>

  The real parameter estimate yields norm-convergent multilinear expansions
  in the common completed spaces. The strict path reserve also permits their
  comparison across cutoffs.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local analytic estimates><label|lem:D-analytic-step> At every
    point of the real domain above, the centered maps <math|F<rsub|N,m>>,
    <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>> and <math|F<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|W>>> have
    convergent real multilinear expansions on the ball of radius <math|1/4>.
    Their degree-<math|k> coefficients, <math|k\<geqslant\>1>, have norms at
    most <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>>, <math|\<varrho\><rsub|m>> and
    <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>>, respectively, where
    <math|\<varrho\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0> is independent of <math|N,p> and

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<varrho\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|m><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    At fixed <math|m,p> these maps are compatible with cutoff limits in the
    common spaces, including the prescribed paths with a strictly larger path
    exponent.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D05b>,
    <reference|def:D06> and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D-real-lines>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-stencil-recovery>. The larger real domain
    preceding<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-analytic-domain> supplies the neighbouring
    centres required in the recovery lemma. Apply it with <math|r=1/4> and
    <math|R=\<rho\>/2> for the activity map, and <math|R=\<rho\>> for the
    scalar correction. Their coefficient bounds are respectively

    <\equation*>
      2*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>*<around|(|32*e/\<rho\>|)><rsup|k>,<space|2em>2*\<varphi\><rsub|m>*<around|(|16*e/\<rho\>|)><rsup|k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|\<rho\>\<geqslant\>128*e> before the ratios. Since
    <math|2*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
    <math|2*\<varphi\><rsub|m>=\<varrho\><rsub|m>>, these are the asserted
    bounds. The same argument applies to Wilson. Completion preserves each
    coefficient, its norm and its diagonal identity. The fixed permutation
    subspace preserves equivariance.

    For cutoff compatibility, compare first the quadratic rows and then the
    cubic, quartic and quadratic loop kernels in that order. The free forms,
    their row factors and the kernels <math|W<rsub|0>>, <math|T> converge by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>; placing the coefficients of
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>> on coordinate paths
    (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>) gives convergence of the
    quadratic rows. The resolvent identity and the uniform inverse bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> give convergence of the remaining
    fixed-window inverses. In each subsequent comparison fix these rows and
    all preceding kernels. Let <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|a>> be the affine
    coefficient space for the kernel of the current degree, with its
    prescribed symmetries and cancellation identities. Divide its weighted
    coefficient norm by the stage allowance. The corresponding coefficient
    map, denoted <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a>>, obeys

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-affine-kernel>\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a>*<around|(|K+\<lambda\>*<around|(|H-K|)>|)>=\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>+\<lambda\>*<around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|H|)>-\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the cubic coefficient this follows by retaining degree three in the
    stationary substitution: it is linear in the incoming cubic kernel. At
    degree four, terms quadratic in the cubic kernel are fixed, and the
    incoming quartic kernel occurs once. The quadratic loop coefficient is
    affine in its incoming loop kernel, with the preceding classical kernels
    fixed. These are the degree extractions in
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7>.

    Take the ratio threshold of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> for the
    enlarged input path bound <math|64*A<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>,
    and, for the enlarged quadratic loop bound
    <math|64*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>\<leqslant\>128*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>,
    the threshold in the proof of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
    with <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pb>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1/256>,
    so that <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>+<tfrac|1|256>\<cdot\>128*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>.
    These choices precede the coupling threshold. With the preceding kernels
    fixed, the coefficient constructions then accept normalized input size
    <math|64> and give normalized output size at most one. The enlarged input
    must still belong to <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|a>> and satisfy these bounds.
    For admitted inputs <math|K,H> of normalized size at most one put
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>=<around|\<\|\|\>|H-K|\<\|\|\>>>. If
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>\<gtr\>0>, the extrapolated input
    <math|K<rsub|\<ast\>>=K+<around|(|32/\<delta\><rsub|K>|)>*<around|(|H-K|)>>
    belongs to the same affine space and has norm at most
    <math|33\<less\>64>. Its linear cancellation and symmetry conditions hold
    because their homogeneous differences form a linear subspace. Hence its
    stage bound applies. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-affine-kernel> gives

    <\equation*>
      <frac|32|\<delta\><rsub|K>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|H|)>-\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The output difference is at most <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>/16\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|K>/4>;
    at <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>=0> it is zero by the same affine identity.
    This calculation concerns one kernel, with all preceding kernels fixed.

    For two cutoffs, the change of those fixed preceding kernels contributes
    an error <math|e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i>> tending to zero at each fixed
    level <math|i>, once their preceding comparisons have been made. The
    normalized difference of the current kernels therefore satisfies
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i+1>/4+e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i>>.
    Truncate this coefficient recursion at level <math|k>, retaining any
    admitted terminal kernel there. Its terminal difference is at most two,
    and iteration gives

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1/4|)><rsup|k-m>+<big|sum><rsub|i=m><rsup|k-1><around|(|1/4|)><rsup|i-m>*e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    At fixed <math|k> the sum tends to zero; then <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>
    proves the Cauchy property, successively for each degree.

    Retain now finitely many addresses and finite path lengths. Window
    covariances and means on these sets converge with their derivatives by
    the uniform inverse bound and the resolvent identity. Stationary
    corrections use this same inverse, and determinant differences are
    integrals of traces of resolvent differences.
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7> express the path
    coefficients by these kernels. The stronger-weight clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> bounds these coefficients uniformly in
    <math|N> at an exponent <math|w<rprime|'>\<gtr\>w>, with
    <math|w=w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>. A tail of length at
    least <math|l> at exponent <math|w> is bounded by its uniform
    <math|w<rprime|'>> norm times <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|w<rprime|'>-w|)>*l>>.
    Thus finite-coefficient convergence and uniformly small tails prove path
    convergence with a strict reserve.

    For the connected weights, write both integrals in the same fixed
    physical coordinates and subtract their densities. The converging means
    and precisions make these Gaussian densities converge pointwise. Their
    strict positive lower bounds give a common integrable Gaussian
    exponential majorant, including the fixed derivative scores; combine it
    with <math|g<rsub|M>> from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> and the
    weighted input discrepancy. Dominated convergence therefore gives
    convergence for a finite collection. The rooted estimates bound the
    discarded collections uniformly. Localization uses only five fixed
    auxiliary tori, finite linear combinations and such uniformly bounded
    tails. Subtraction is linear, and fusion has a strict attachment margin.
    Consequently input coordinates converging
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-common-norm> and converging normalized couplings give
    outputs and corrections converging in the same common spaces. This proves
    the compatibility assertion.
  </proof>

  For two histories at the same <math|N,p>, let <math|x<rsub|m>,A<rsub|m>>
  and <math|x<rprime|'><rsub|m>,A<rprime|'><rsub|m>> be these coordinates and
  set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-history-distance>\<frak-d\>=max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>
    max <around|{|<around|\||x<rprime|'><rsub|m>-x<rsub|m>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m>-A<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,p>|}>,<space|2em>\<delta\>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|0>-\<beta\><rsub|0><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The parameter series below belong to <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|X|)>=<around|{|<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>z<rsup|k>*X<rsub|k>:<big|sum><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<infty\>|}>>,
  with norm <math|<big|sum><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Evaluation has norm at most one for real
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. This space is complete, and its
  Cauchy product is bounded by the product of the norms.

  For the series equations fix a history reaching
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, whose existence is proved in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.2>, and set
  <math|z<rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m>*x<rsub|m>>. Write <math|u<rsub|m>> for the
  perturbation of <math|x<rsub|m>>, <math|W<rsub|m>> for that of
  <math|A<rsub|m>>, and <math|\<frak-e\>> for the endpoint series. For any
  map <math|F> let <math|<wide|F|^>> be its centered series. The simultaneous
  equations are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|N>>|<cell|=<wide|F|^><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|u<rsub|N>|)>,<label|eq:D-series-map>>>|<row|<cell|W<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<wide|F|^><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|u<rsub|m+1>,W<rsub|m+1>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)>,>>|<row|<cell|u<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<around|(|2*z<rsub|m>|)><rsup|-1>*<around*|[|b<rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<around*|(|\<frak-e\>-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><wide|\<cal-D\>|^><rsub|N,i><around|(|u<rsub|i+1>,W<rsub|i+1>|)>|)>-\<chi\><rsub|m>*u<rsub|m><rsup|2>|]><space|0.17em>.<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  The last line is obtained by expanding the square
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-coordinate>; it retains its quadratic term.

  These right sides define a map on the product of the spaces
  <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|<R>|)>> and <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>|)>>,
  with the maximum of their series norms. The real endpoint interval is

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-J\>=<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|\<ast\>>/4,<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|\<ast\>>/4|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The following is the estimate used by Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>.
  It applies to the selected step maps, their actual Wilson initial family
  and the same fixed address spaces.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Analytic endpoint equations><label|lem:D-E1> The histories above
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-d\>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>/b<rsub|\<ast\>>+8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-e\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-S\><around|(|<R>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|\<ast\>>/4>,
    equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-series-map> preserve the closed
    radius-<math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>> ball in the product of the spaces
    <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|<R>|)>> and <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>|)>>
    and contract by at most <math|1/2>. Their unique solution evaluates to
    the selected real recursion. At fixed <math|m,p>, its scalar couplings
    and activity coordinates converge along the full cutoff sequence for
    every convergent real endpoint sequence in <math|\<frak-J\>>.
  </lemma>

  The maximum estimate and the series contraction are proved next; the cutoff
  conclusion is proved in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level>.

  <\render-proof|Proof of the comparison and series assertions>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D05b>,
    <reference|def:D06>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>, <reference|lem:D2>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-analytic-step>. The latter's centered
    expansions give

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m>-A<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*max
      <around|{|<around|\||x<rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-x<rsub|m+1>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-A<rsub|m+1>|\<\|\|\>>|}>,<label|eq:D-local-lipschitz>>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|N,m>-\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*\<varrho\><rsub|m>*max
      <around|{|<around|\||x<rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-x<rsub|m+1>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-A<rsub|m+1>|\<\|\|\>>|}><space|0.17em>.<eq-number>>>>>
    </align>

    For distances at most <math|1/4> these follow by subtracting the series
    and summing <math|k*<around|(|1/4|)><rsup|k-1>>. For larger distances the
    separate bounds <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>
    and <math|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varrho\><rsub|m>/2>
    give the displayed, weaker constants. The Wilson top map has the first
    bound as well.

    Write <math|z<rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m>*x<rsub|m>> and
    <math|z<rprime|'><rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m>*x<rprime|'><rsub|m>> in this
    proof. Both are in <math|<around|[|17/20,23/20|]>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-coordinate> gives the exact identity

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|m>-\<beta\><rsub|m>=b<rsub|m>*<around|(|x<rprime|'><rsub|m>-x<rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|z<rprime|'><rsub|m>+z<rsub|m>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Summing the scalar recursion from the endpoint cancels
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>> between the two histories, and hence

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|m>*<around|\||x<rprime|'><rsub|m>-x<rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><around|\||\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|N,i>-\<cal-D\><rsub|N,i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>+4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|m>*\<frak-d\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Take the maximum together with the activity estimates and use
    <math|b<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|\<ast\>>>. This proves the asserted
    inequality before absorbing its last term. In particular
    <math|\<delta\>=0> gives <math|\<frak-d\>=0>, and otherwise
    <math|\<frak-d\>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||\<delta\>|\|>/b<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    Thus real evaluation can be identified by uniqueness.

    On the product ball of radius <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>> the series
    pair <math|<around|(|u<rsub|m>,W<rsub|m>|)>> has norm at most
    <math|2*\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>1/4>. A centered series with
    coefficients bounded by <math|c> consequently has norm at most
    <math|c/2>, and the difference of its two evaluations is bounded by
    <math|2*c> times the difference of its arguments. For the scalar prefix
    the latter gives <math|4*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<varrho\><rsub|i>>
    times the product distance, because the two coordinates contribute at
    most twice that distance. Also

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|m><rsup|2>-v<rsub|m><rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|m>-v<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|2*z<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>, the scalar part
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-series-map> has norm at most

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>/4+g<rsub|\<ast\>>+\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>\<less\>\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>
    </equation*>

    and Lipschitz constant at most <math|4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>+2*\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>=5/32\<less\>1/2>.
    The activity part has norm at most <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>/2\<less\>\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>>
    and Lipschitz constant at most <math|4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>1/2>. Thus
    the map preserves the closed ball and contracts there. Starting at zero,
    its successive differences are bounded by a geometric series with ratio
    <math|1/2>; completeness gives its unique fixed point.

    Evaluation commutes with sums, products and each absolutely convergent
    multilinear series. It turns the fixed point into the real recursion and
    Wilson start. The comparison already proved identifies it with the
    selected history. The endpoint width <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|\<ast\>>/4>
    is smaller than the real shooting width:
    <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<chi\><rsub|0>*<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/128>.
    Hence the whole closed real interval stays in that shooting interval.
  </render-proof>

  Only the endpoint coupling is compact in the next argument; no compactness
  of a ball in the activity Banach space is used.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Scalar-free fixed-level limits><label|lem:D-fixed-level> For fixed
    <math|p,m>, the selected scalar couplings and activity coordinates in
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>> converge along every full cutoff sequence with
    convergent endpoints in the interval of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>.
    The prescribed paths converge with the strict reserve of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-analytic-step>. The limits are independent of
    subsequences. No additive vacuum scalar is included.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D05b>,
    <reference|def:D06>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C8>,
    <reference|lem:D-analytic-step> and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>. First
    extract a subsequence on which every scalar coordinate <math|x<rsub|i>>
    converges. Each fixed coordinate is in a compact real interval by
    <math|17/20\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|i>*x<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>23/20>; a
    diagonal selection gives the simultaneous subsequence.

    Fix a stopping index <math|k\<gtr\>m>. At level <math|k> replace the
    activity coordinate by zero and run the same selected maps downward using
    the unchanged scalar coordinates. Write
    <math|A<rsub|m><rsup|<around|[|k|]>>> for the resulting coordinate. The
    first inequality in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-local-lipschitz>, applied
    successively at equal scalar inputs, gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-truncated-orbit><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|m>-A<rsub|m><rsup|<around|[|k|]>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,p>\<leqslant\><around|(|8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|k-m><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The terminal difference is at most one. For fixed <math|k> the truncated
    coordinates converge by a finite downward induction, using cutoff
    compatibility in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-analytic-step>. The
    geometric bound is uniform in the cutoff and tends to zero as
    <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. Given <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose
    <math|k> to make both truncation errors smaller than
    <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>, then choose the cutoffs so that the truncated
    difference is smaller than <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>. Thus the actual
    activity coordinates are Cauchy in their common completed spaces on the
    extracted subsequence. The scalar corrections converge by the second
    inequality in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-local-lipschitz> and cutoff compatibility.

    It remains to identify two possible subsequential limits. Denote them by
    <math|x<rsub|i>,A<rsub|i>,\<cal-D\><rsub|i>> and
    <math|x<rprime|'><rsub|i>,A<rprime|'><rsub|i>,\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>.
    Their common endpoint is <math|\<beta\><rsub|0>>. Passing to the limit in
    the finite scalar recursion gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-limit-prefix>\<beta\><rsub|i>-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|i>=-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>i><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|j>-\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|j>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The limits of <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,j>> cancel because the prescribed
    paths have one limit. Put

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>=sup<rsub|i\<geqslant\>0> max
      <around|{|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i>-A<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|i,p>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This supremum is finite with bound <math|1/4>. Indeed the activity
    difference is at most <math|1/4>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-limit-prefix>
    together with <math|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|j>|\|>,<around|\||\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varrho\><rsub|j>/2>
    gives <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
    after division by <math|b<rsub|i>> as in the previous proof. Passing the
    one-step estimates to the limit gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i>-A<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-limit-prefix>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-local-lipschitz> give
    <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    Hence <math|\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>>,
    so <math|\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>=0>.

    Every subsequence therefore has a further subsequence with these same
    scalar and activity limits. If the full sequence failed to converge at
    some fixed level, a subsequence staying a positive distance from the
    limit would contradict the preceding construction. This proves full
    convergence. The continuous map <math|x\<mapsto\><refbeta><rsub|m><around|(|\<chi\><rsub|m>*x|)><rsup|2>>
    gives convergence of <math|\<beta\><rsub|m>>. The path assertion is
    already part of the cutoff comparison, with a strictly larger exponent
    than the one defining the local charts. None of these arguments compares
    the vacuum scalars, which disappear from the normalized activity and
    coupling equations.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The Wilson start>

  At the finest level the identity free form permits a local choice. Put
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|N>>> and
  <math|F<around|(|u|)>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><around|(|u-u<rsup|-1>|)>/2>.
  To define the quartic term, let <math|\<psi\>> be the local inverse of
  <math|X\<mapsto\>F<around|(|e<rsup|X>|)>> at zero and set

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|4><around|(|Y|)>=<frac|1|4!>*D<rsup|4><around|[|<wilsonterm><around|(|e<rsup|\<psi\><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>>|)>|]><around|(|0|)><around|[|Y,Y,Y,Y|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The inverse exists since <math|D*F<around|(|e<rsup|\<cdot\>>|)><around|(|0|)>=I>.
  This invariant quartic polynomial is chosen so that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-Wilson-remainder>R<around|(|u|)>=<wilsonterm><around|(|u|)>-<tfrac|1|2>\|F<around|(|u|)>\|<rsup|2>-T<rsub|4><around|(|F<around|(|u|)>|)>
  </equation>

  has zero Taylor terms through degree four at <math|1>. This is the case
  <math|m=N> in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>. The path action
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> at <math|m=N> has row
  <math|<sqrt|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>>*<space|0.17em>F>, no cubic or
  loop terms, and quartic term <math|<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*T<rsub|4><around|(|F|)>>
  with its prescribed mask. These finite path sums satisfy
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>.

  The initial activities retain the difference between Wilson energy and this
  action also on flagged configurations. The following proof constructs the
  full curve. The same construction of the classes is used throughout.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Wilson initialization><label|lem:D3> For every <math|N,p> there is
    a continuous curve <math|\<beta\>\<mapsto\>\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>>
    on <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|N>>, with coupling <math|\<beta\>>, vacuum
    scalar zero, and

    <\equation*>
      <density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>=e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The curve has the activity bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> and
    the continuity of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>.
  </lemma>

  <\render-proof|Proof of the initialization identity, bounds and continuity>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, <reference|def:D06>,
    <reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25a>, and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>, <reference|lem:C2>, <reference|lem:C4>.
    Everything here is at level <math|N>: <math|j=J<rsub|N>>,
    <math|t=t<rsub|N>>, <math|B=B<rsub|N>> and
    <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>. For a plaquette <math|q>,
    write <math|\<rho\><rsub|q>=<chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>>,
    <math|k<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|kin>>=<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)><around|\||F<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>|\|><rsup|2>/2>,
    <math|h<rsub|q>=h<rsub|<math-up|vertices><around|(|q|)>>>, and
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|q>> for its relaxed health test. Define its unassigned
    action by

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\><rsub|q><around|(|U|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*\<rho\><rsub|q>,>|<cell|\<rho\><rsub|q>\<gtr\>t,>>|<row|<cell|h<rsub|q>*k<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|kin>>+<around|(|1-h<rsub|q>|)>*<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>-\<chi\><rsub|q>*k<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|kin>>,>|<cell|\<rho\><rsub|q>\<leqslant\>t.>>>>>
    </equation*>

    The relaxed health test <math|\<chi\><rsub|q>=1> implies
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|q>\<leqslant\>t>. The identity
    <math|<around|(|u-u<rsup|-1>|)>/2=<around|(|u-1|)>*<around|(|1+u<rsup|-1>|)>/2>
    and contraction of the projection give
    <math|<around|\||F<around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|u|)>>.
    Together with <math|<chord><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>=2<wilsonterm><around|(|u|)>>
    this gives <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>\<geqslant\>0>. For
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|q>\<gtr\>t>, <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>/16\<leqslant\>1/16>
    gives <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>\<geqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|q><rsup|2>/4\<geqslant\>t<rsup|2>/4>.
    Substitution in the two cases gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-start-identity><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>=L<rsub|q><around|(|U|)>+<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*h<rsub|q>*T<rsub|4><around|(|F<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>|)>+<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*h<rsub|q>*R<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>+\<cal-W\><rsub|q><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|L<rsub|q>> is the direct clipped term plus the health-tested
    row square. The identity includes small plaquettes next to a flag and
    both sides of the clipping threshold.

    Take the regular activities <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*R<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>>
    on their plaquette supports, anchored at the starting vertex, with the
    signed average of the path action. They vanish at <math|1> with their
    Hessians. Through the prescribed order their normalized healthy
    derivatives are bounded by <math|C<rsub|j>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|5>>. To verify
    this without assuming small links, use the plaquette holonomy as one
    coordinate and the other three links as compact parameters. The remainder
    vanishes to order five in the holonomy chart. At derivative order
    <math|k\<leqslant\>5>, Taylor's formula leaves order <math|5-k> and the
    direction factors supply <math|t<rsup|k>>. For <math|k\<gtr\>5>, bounded
    derivatives and <math|t<rsup|k>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|5>> give the same
    estimate. The four-link formula controls the remaining compact factors.
    There are finitely many plaquettes at each anchor, so the regular sum is
    at most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|5>>. On the top band,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<refbeta><rsub|N>t<rsub|N><rsup|5>|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>=<refbeta><rsub|N><rsup|-2/5><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|N>|)><rsup|5>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Increasing the entry threshold gives the regular bound
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024> uniformly in volume.

    Let <math|\<frak-F\>=<flags><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>>. Join two flags when
    their radius-<math|2*B> neighborhoods overlap. For each nonempty
    connected inventory <math|I> use <math|Y<rsub|I>=N<rsub|2*B><around|(|I|)>>
    and

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|Y<rsub|I>\<cap\>\<frak-F\>=I|}>>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*d<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The charge <math|d<rsub|I>> is the signed average of those
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>> whose anchors lie within distance <math|B> of
    <math|I>, in the coordinates of that signed image. Every nonzero
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>> is assigned: a failure of the relaxed health
    test has a witnessing flag within this distance, since <math|t*exp
    <around|(|<around|(|B-2|)>/R<rsub|J<rsub|N>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>|)>\<geqslant\>2>.
    The other cases already meet a flag at a plaquette vertex. The anchors
    lie within <math|B> of <math|I>, and each nonautomatic relaxed health
    test reads within <math|B-2> of its anchor. Thus
    <math|N<rsub|2*B><around|(|I|)>> contains every link and flag used in the
    charge. Distinct actual components have disjoint supports, so each
    nonzero term is assigned once. Hence the sum of the component charges is
    the signed average of <math|<big|sum><rsub|q>\<cal-W\><rsub|q>>.

    Every flag touches a plaquette with <math|\<rho\><rsub|q>\<gtr\>t>, and a
    plaquette has at most four vertices. Retaining the identity orientation
    in the nonnegative signed average gives

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\><frac|t<rsup|2><around|\||I|\|>|16<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>>*<space|2em><around|(|I\<subset\>\<frak-F\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A nonzero exact-cover product of <math|K<rsub|I>> selects precisely the
    overlap components of <math|\<frak-F\>>. Indeed any two flags in one
    selected connected inventory are connected in <math|\<frak-F\>>.
    Conversely a flag joined to a selected inventory cannot lie in another
    selected inventory, since their radius-<math|2*B> supports would
    intersect. Exact coverage then forces every component to be selected. The
    actual components satisfy eligibility. Thus the forced sum has exactly
    one nonzero term, equal to the exponential of minus <math|\<beta\>> times
    the unassigned action. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-start-identity>, summed
    and averaged, proves the Wilson density identity for all configurations
    with scalar zero.

    For the forced norm, a connected inventory of <math|r> sites is encoded
    by a rooted spanning walk of length at most <math|2*<around|(|r-1|)>> in
    the overlap graph. Each step has at most
    <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>> choices, and
    <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|N<rsub|2*B><around|(|I|)>|)>\<leqslant\>5<rsup|4>*r>.
    Counting possible roots for a support through a specified site costs
    another <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>. With
    <math|c=1/<around|(|16<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>|)>>, the weighted sum
    is therefore bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>1><around*|[|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|8>*e<rsup|5<rsup|4>*\<sigma\>-c*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>>|]><rsup|r><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The top band gives <math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>\<geqslant\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|N>|)><rsup|2>/2>.
    This dominates both <math|log <around|(|1+B|)>> and the exponent in
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>, so the geometric sum is
    eventually at most <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024>,
    uniformly in volume. Both normalized bounds can be made arbitrarily
    smaller by increasing the entry threshold.

    Addresses and tests are independent of <math|\<beta\>>. Regular
    differences have the same majorants with <math|\<beta\>> replaced by
    <math|<around|\||\<beta\>-\<beta\><rsub|0>|\|>>. For the forced functions
    <math|d<rsub|I>\<geqslant\>0> is bounded at fixed volume and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||e<rsup|-\<beta\>*d<rsub|I>>-e<rsup|-\<beta\><rsub|0>*d<rsub|I>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<beta\>-\<beta\><rsub|0>|\|>*sup<rsub|U>
      d<rsub|I><around|(|U|)><space|2em><around|(|\<beta\>,\<beta\><rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    There are finitely many subsets of this torus, so the weighted difference
    sums tend to zero. Translations and signed permutations transport
    complete plaquette terms, component supports, inventories and tests. This
    gives covariance as well as continuity. We have proved the Wilson
    representation, both bounds, and its full continuous curve with scalar
    zero. The common one-step assertion follows from the assembly below.
  </render-proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D06>,
    <reference|def:D25a>, and the classes of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2>.
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>\U<reference|lem:D6> give the final
    functions and their exact normalized identity. Put

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|u>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log
      \<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<Delta\><rsub|u><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The denominator is positive by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> gives continuity of <math|v<rsub|u>> and of
    the activity families. The proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>
    identifies this same <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> with its continuous scalar
    root; its drift estimate gives

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>-<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>+e<rsub|u>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Define <math|\<rho\><rsub|u>> by these scalars and the activities in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>. Multiply the identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> by <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>>
    to obtain <math|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>>
    for every configuration. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> gives the activity
    caps and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> gives continuity. The retained
    conditions and the curve construction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2>
    give <math|\<rho\><rsub|u>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>> and
    <math|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|u>,\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>>.
    The Wilson construction above supplies the common initial curve with
    scalar zero.
  </render-proof>

  <section|Trajectories, marked fields and ultraviolet
  convergence><label|sec:partII-E>

  This section constructs the trajectories and the renormalized insertions
  used in Part<nbsp>I, and proves their volume-uniform bounds and full
  fixed-volume limits. The conclusions are Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>,
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> and Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>.
  We also establish the cutoff equation identities, coefficient corrections
  and finite rank comparisons used to construct the quotient fields in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>. All these results concern the same
  Wilson measures of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D02> and the insertions
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>.

  There are three different arguments. A continuous shooting argument first
  chooses the bare coupling to reach one prescribed coarse coupling. Weighted
  estimates for the specified recursion then give uniqueness and analytic
  dependence near that endpoint. Finally, at fixed volume, convergence of the
  effective coordinates at each fixed level is combined with a bound on the
  error made by stopping the insertion transfer there. The additive vacuum
  scalar cancels from normalized expectations; its convergence is
  unnecessary.

  The main difficulty in the bounds is differentiation in position. Each
  lattice difference introduces an inverse spacing, so a bound for
  undifferentiated insertions does not suffice. Local comparison replaces a
  differentiated insertion by one of higher degree, with a small chessboard
  error. Exact equation identities remove its equation component. A finite
  matrix of free Maxwell correlation tests bounds the remaining coefficient.
  This argument takes place at finite cutoff, before any continuum field
  space has been constructed.

  Throughout, the group, metric, penalty, scales and admissible classes are
  those of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. We use the paths of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> and the Wilson
  initialization of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3> and the inductive step of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>. Additional dependence of constants is
  indicated. Write <math|r<around|(|d|)>=<reflevel|d>>,
  <math|M<rsub|N,p>=<latmoment><rsub|N,p>> and
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>=<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>.

  For a token <math|v=<around|(|d,c|)>> put
  <math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y|)>=O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)>>.
  Physical points <math|x> in a moment are read as lattice sites
  <math|x/a<rsub|N>>, modulo <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>. We use the torus
  separation

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>=min<rsub|i\<ne\>j>
    min<rsub|z\<in\>L<rsub|p><Z><rsup|4>><around|\||a<rsub|N>*<around|(|y<rsub|i>-y<rsub|j>|)>+z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>,
  </equation*>

  with value <math|+\<infty\>> for at most one slot. Multilinear
  interpolation on the physical grid means the affine interpolation in each
  coordinate of each grid cube, using its vertex values.

  <subsection|Trajectories and tuning><label|sec:partII-E.1>

  The entry levels allow the ratios and stage constants to increase
  sufficiently slowly. Besides the conditions of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, we use the following quantitative
  choices. Let <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>> be the direct clipping
  coefficient at stage<nbsp><math|j>, and let
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>> be the real dimension of the
  ambient matrix space. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-vacuum-majorant>Q<rsub|j><around|(|b|)>=2*<around|(|2+4*A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>+1+6*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>+<around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>+4*\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>*<around|(|log
    16+<tfrac|1|2>log b|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|m>=Q<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>> is the path bound of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. For the unmarked density on <math|V> sites
  write <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m>=v<rsub|<math-up|vac>,m>*V-<pathaction><rsub|N,m>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>.
  To verify the normalization cost, put <math|<wide|g|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>>
  at this level. The scalar-free unmarked exponent is at most
  <math|V*E<rsub|m>>, and its absolute forced sum is at most
  <math|e<rsup|V*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>>>, where the path
  bounds give <math|E<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2+4*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>.
  Restrict the denominator to links of chord at most
  <math|<wide|g|^><rsub|m>/4>. There are no flags there, the forced sum is
  one, and its scalar-free exponent is at least
  <math|-V*<around|(|E<rsub|m>+6*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>+<around|(|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>|)>>.
  The elementary Haar-ball bound gives this set measure at least
  <math|<around|(|<wide|g|^><rsub|m>/16|)><rsup|4*V*\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>>>.
  Thus

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-vacuum-ratio>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<frac|<big|int>e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\>><big|prod><rsub|Y\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around|\||K<rsub|m,Y><around|(|U|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*U|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m>*<space|0.17em>d*U>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|{|V*<around*|[|2*E<rsub|m>+\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>+6*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>+<around|(|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>+4*\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>*log
      <around|(|16/<wide|g|^><rsub|m>|)>|]>|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|V*q<rsub|m>>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  The common vacuum scalar in numerator and denominator cancels. This
  estimate uses all four links per site without gauge fixing; the sharper
  gauge-fixed calculation in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral> has
  a different purpose. Enlarge <math|Q<rsub|j>> for any finite initial
  levels, so that <math|q<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1> at every level. The choices
  include

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-marker-scales>B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|i+1>=o<around|(|s<rsub|i+1>|)>,<space|2em>\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation>

  Define <math|k<around|(|m|)>> to be the first <math|k\<geqslant\>m> with
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>.

  The short-distance comparison uses the following intervals of levels. For
  <math|b\<gtr\>0> and a level<nbsp><math|t> in stage<nbsp><math|j>, write
  <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-window>h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|<around|(|b+2|)>*log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>|log L>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>v<rsub|t>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|10*log
    B<rsub|t>|log L>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>k<rsub|t>=t-h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>-v<rsub|t>.
  </equation>

  We collect the numerical requirements used in the marked proofs. For each
  stage<nbsp><math|j>, let <math|K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> be a nondecreasing
  bound for the leading coefficient maps and their inverses through that
  stage's finitely many degrees and symmetry averages. These bounds are fixed
  before the column bounds<nbsp><math|C<rsub|j>>. Let
  <math|A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>> bound the finite basis recombination
  in degree<nbsp><math|d>. Let <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> bound the
  local marked error before choosing the ratio. Each step below has
  <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>, <math|K=K<rsub|j>>, <math|S=C<rsub|j>>,
  <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|m>> and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|-3/8>>.
  For the retained incoming regular term, and for the unsubtracted incoming
  contribution in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J>, let
  <math|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>\<in\><N>> bound the enlargement of its
  projected incoming read support in units of <math|B<rsub|m>>, over the
  finitely many adjacent stage pairs with fine stage<nbsp><math|j>. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-support-conversion>\<cal-A\><rsub|m>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|{|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>|}>.
  </equation>

  This factor converts its incoming support weight into the outgoing weight.
  Indeed, for fine and coarse covering numbers <math|s<rsub|f>,s<rsub|c>>,
  the connected-cover bound gives

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L*s<rsub|c>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|L+10*s<rsub|f>|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*s<rsub|c>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>log
    \<cal-A\><rsub|m>+<tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*s<rsub|f>,>>>>
  </align*>

  provided <math|20*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>.
  Thus half the incoming exponent remains for the other support factors. The
  constant <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>2> includes these bounds and
  the finite source comparison constants. In the birth requirement below,
  <math|C<rsub|d>> is the section bound in<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-global-bound>.
  For the source estimates, choose an integer <math|q<rsub|d>> bounding the
  powers of the inverse health threshold in degree<nbsp><math|d>, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<zeta\><rsub|d,i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsub|d>+4|)>>.
  </equation*>

  This allowance controls attachments of other marked factors to a
  distinguished mark. Let <math|A<rsub|j,d>> bound the finite section,
  derivative and support-conversion constants in these attachments at
  stage<nbsp><math|j>, and let <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> be the
  attachment threshold in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> for the step
  at level<nbsp><math|i>. Decrease it so that
  <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/4>. All these constants are fixed before
  the coupling entry. We require

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-attachment-allowance>2*A<rsub|j,d>*<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|q<rsub|d>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>/2,<space|2em>j=J<rsub|i+1>,<space|1em>d\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  The factor <math|<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>> bounds the spatial sum
  from marked anchors to a site; its derivation is in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments>. For transport to finer
  levels we also require

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-source-allowance-transport><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>.
  </equation>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><label|it:E-columns> Choose nondecreasing columns with
    <math|C<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>64*K<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>>, above the derivative
    factorials and finite basis-recombination constants at
    stage<nbsp><math|j>. The support exponent is
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>=100>; we also retain the estimates at
    exponent<nbsp><math|98>.

    <item><label|it:E-graph> Choose <math|L<rsup|3/5>\<gtr\>2*C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>,
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L> and
    <math|20*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>.
    The local marked error <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>> satisfies
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-2/5>>, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-graph-reserve>\<eta\><rsub|m>*<around|(|1+S|)>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>,<space|1em>2*K*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*<around|(|1+S|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>4*S*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>2*K\<leqslant\>S,<space|1em>4*K<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m>>.
    </equation>

    <item><label|it:E-match> For local matching require

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-matching-choices>4*K*<space|0.17em>32*<around|(|1+S|)><rsup|2>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*L<rsup|1/8>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation>

    For each auxiliary section through the stage's degrees, require also
    <math|2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j>|)>*H<rsub|j,d>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>*L<rsup|-1/8>\<leqslant\>1/4>,
    where <math|H<rsub|j,d>> bounds its normalized healthy derivatives.

    <item><label|it:E-log> Choose ratios to
    satisfy<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>, including every constant
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>>.

    <item><label|it:E-entries> On a comparison interval, for an arity bound
    <math|n\<geqslant\>2>, put <math|\<alpha\><rsub|t>=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|1/<around|(|8*n|)>>>.
    Let <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>> be the sum of the
    covariance, rounding, Taylor and localization errors in the fixed finite
    source comparison. Require

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-Gaussian-entry>\<alpha\><rsub|t><rsup|n>*\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>log
      <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<in\><around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|]>*<space|1em><around|(|k<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t|)>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>> is the stage normalization
    constant in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-comparison-cost>, enlarged to cover the
    source modes and amplitudes of the finitely many arity bounds
    <math|n\<geqslant\>2> admitted at stage<nbsp><math|j>, and the second
    bound is required for each of these<nbsp><math|n>. These constants are
    fixed first; the entry is then raised until <math|log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>\<geqslant\>16*n*<space|0.17em>Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>>.
    The first bound is imposed with the stronger smallness used
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-iteration>. The entry also meets the local
    marked bound in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>, the forced-source
    bounds at every exponent between <math|98> and <math|100>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance>\U<eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport>.
    The unmarked attachment sum in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> is
    at most <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>/2>, and <math|64*log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>/B<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/4>.

    <item><label|it:E-birth> At every level<nbsp><math|m> and
    <math|d\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>>, require <math|B<rsub|m>> at least the
    radius of<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and
    <math|C<rsub|d>*t<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>>-1|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>.
    Since <math|t<rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> and
    <math|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|d+1>\<to\>0>,
    this is a lower bound on each stage entry.
  </enumerate>

  The finite comparisons in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> exhaust
  bounded configurations with positive point separation, fixed arities and
  finite derivative orders as<nbsp><math|j> increases. Their errors and
  constants are calculated in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>;
  none depends on the period.

  The following choice gives both the sparse insertion grids and the
  intervals used for free comparison.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Scale and period choices><label|lem:E1.1> All
    requirements<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns>\U<reference|it:E-birth>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> can be met with

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>3<text|
      odd>,<space|2em>log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>,<space|2em>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0,<space|2em>4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The sides <math|L<rsub|p>> increase strictly; every positive integer
    divides infinitely many. For every finite set of exponents
    <math|b\<geqslant\>1> and finite comparison requests, arbitrarily late
    terminal levels<nbsp><math|t> meet the comparison requirements
    simultaneously on their intervals<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>, with
    <math|k<rsub|t>\<to\>\<infty\>> and <math|J<rsub|i>=J<rsub|t>> throughout
    each interval.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the scale definitions of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>
    and the order of choices in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. First
    fix the entire sequence <math|K<rsub|j>> and the finite section bounds.
    Choose <math|C<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>64*K<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>>, and then fix
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>> to include these columns and all source
    constants. Since <math|J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1>, this gives
    <math|4*K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m>>> even at
    a stage transition. Next choose nondecreasing odd stage ratios
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>\<geqslant\>max <around|(|3,j|)>>
    large enough that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-log-cost><frac|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>|log
      \<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>>\<leqslant\>min
      <around|(|<slope>/4,j<rsup|-2>|)>,<space|2em><frac|log
      \<cal-C\><rsub|j>|log \<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>>\<leqslant\>j<rsup|-2>.
    </equation>

    Each denominator can be increased after its numerator is fixed. The ratio
    requirements in items<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>
    and<nbsp><reference|it:E-match> are lower bounds on this same later
    choice: <math|L<rsup|-2/5>/L<rsup|-3/8>=L<rsup|-1/40>\<to\>0> and
    <math|L<rsup|1/8>*L<rsup|-3/8>=L<rsup|-1/4>\<to\>0>. Choose also
    <math|L<rsup|3/5>\<gtr\>2*C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>, so
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>/L\<less\><tfrac|1|2>L<rsup|-2/5>>.
    For this fixed <math|L>, raise the coupling entry until the absolute
    value of the <math|o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>><around|(|1|)>> term
    is at most <math|<tfrac|1|2>L<rsup|-2/5>>. The local error
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>/L+o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>><around|(|1|)>>
    is then at most <math|L<rsup|-2/5>>. The column and ratio choices also
    give the fixed auxiliary-section inequality. The support radii and
    exponents in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion> are fixed
    before<nbsp><math|L>, so its compression condition and
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L> are further fixed lower bounds on
    the ratio. Thus no column is chosen in response to a ratio or to a
    later-stage column.

    Choose consecutive constant-stage intervals, postponing each new stage
    until all its fixed coupling requirements hold. On a fixed stage
    <math|s<rsub|i>> grows linearly, while
    <math|B<rsub|i>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <around|(|2+s<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
    <math|q<rsub|i>=O<around|(|1+log <around|(|2+s<rsub|i>|)>|)>>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|i+1>/s<rsub|i+1>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|1em><text|on
      that stage as its entry is postponed>.
    </equation*>

    For the initial stage, choose <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> after its
    fixed ratio<nbsp><math|L<rsub|0>>. For
    <math|B<around|(|b|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    b|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, the quotient <math|B*<around|(|b+<slope>log
    L<rsub|0>|)><rsup|2>/B<around|(|b|)><rsup|2>> tends to one as
    <math|b\<to\>\<infty\>>. Choose <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> beyond a
    threshold where it is at most two for every
    <math|b\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. This covers every step of
    the first stage, including <math|i=0>. At a transition to a later ratio,
    impose the same bound at its entry and thereafter, with that ratio fixed.
    Impose the second condition of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> with bound
    <math|1/j> after entry to stage<nbsp><math|j>. Impose also
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>/j> and <math|log
    \<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>/j> at its entry.
    These are lower bounds on that entry. By row<nbsp>9 of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>,
    <math|t<rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> and
    <math|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|d+1>\<to\>0>,
    so item<nbsp><reference|it:E-birth>, including the finite stencil-radius
    requirement, is another entry bound. At each fixed ratio, the same
    threshold formula gives <math|t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>\<to\>1> as
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>; impose
    <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2> at entry
    and thereafter, with <math|t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> supplied by
    row<nbsp>7. The preceding entry restrictions give both little-oh
    assertions and <math|log \<ell\><rsub|i>/s<rsub|i>\<to\>0>;
    <math|s<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>i*log 3> at every level.

    For each stage, the length <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> is <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|log log
    <around|(|2+s<rsub|t>|)>|)><rsup|2>+log log
    <around|(|2+s<rsub|t>|)>|)>=o<around|(|t|)>>. Lengthen that
    constant-stage interval to contain a terminal point with the whole
    interval<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> in the same stage and with its left
    endpoint beyond all earlier requested levels. Do this with the longest
    interval <math|b=j>, all arities <math|n\<leqslant\>j> and the finitely
    many comparison orders at stage<nbsp><math|j>, on the compact sets of row
    configurations with point separations at least <math|1/j> and coordinates
    of absolute value at most<nbsp><math|j>. Every
    <math|1\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>j> uses a subinterval of this interval.
    The length and error bounds are uniform for <math|b> in any fixed compact
    subinterval of <math|<around|[|1,j|]>>; take the maximum of the finite
    comparison constants before postponing the common terminal level. Their
    finitely many derivative bounds are uniform on each such compact set. The
    estimates<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-loss>\U<eqref|eq:E-source-iteration>
    show that the first requirement in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-entry> holds
    once that entry is late enough. The second is a lower bound on <math|log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>> after <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>> is
    fixed. The total interval length is <math|o<around|(|t|)>>, so its
    coupling variation is <math|o<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)>> and
    gives the third requirement. These are lower bounds on a later entry or
    interval length. Each fixed request is then met at arbitrarily large
    stages. The source requirements are also entry bounds. On a fixed stage,
    the left side of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance> is a fixed
    constant times <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-4>>
    and tends to zero. In<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport>,
    division by <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>> leaves

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>*<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)><rsup|q<rsub|d>+4>,
    </equation*>

    which tends to zero at fixed ratio. Include both adjacent stages at a
    transition. There are finitely many admitted degrees at each entry. The
    unmarked attachment sum tends to zero by the factor rates in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, so its half-threshold requirement
    is another entry bound. These choices do not alter earlier intervals.
    Taking <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>>
    gives odd ratios, and <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>\<geqslant\>j>
    gives <math|log \<ell\><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    The level <math|k<around|(|m|)>> exists:
    <math|a<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> decreases by at least the factor
    <math|2/3> at each step. If <math|k<around|(|m|)>=u+1\<gtr\>m>,
    minimality gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-first-open>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|u>*B<rsub|u><rsup|2>.
    </equation>

    Consequently<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> gives
    <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)>>.
    The case <math|k<around|(|m|)>=m> satisfies the same conclusion directly
    from <math|q<rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>.

    Choose <math|L<rsub|p>=4*<around|(|M<rsub|<math-up|per>>+p+1|)>>. For
    <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>, the required period inequalities, including row 7
    of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, are

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<rsub|p>*e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>,<space|2em>L<rsub|p>*e<rsup|s<rsub|m+1>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>+1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|L<rsub|p>*e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The first follows by choosing <math|M<rsub|<math-up|per>>> large, since
    <math|<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|m>>> is bounded. After
    canceling <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> in the second, postpone each stage until
    <math|e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|j+4|)>>. For the third,
    <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>> and
    <math|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>> are fixed before entry to
    stage<nbsp><math|j>, and <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|m>>\<to\>0>
    on that stage. Postpone each later stage until this expression is at most
    one thereafter, and enlarge <math|M<rsub|<math-up|per>>> for the finite
    initial interval. This proves all three inequalities. The third supplies
    the period bound for the five-torus localization in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>. In that comparison the field
    is required to be healthy on the radius-<math|R> neighbourhood of the
    anchor, where <math|R> is the localization radius defined in that proof.
    The periods are strictly increasing, divisible by four, and every integer
    divides infinitely many of them.
  </proof>

  The next identities retain the normalization of the original Wilson
  integral through every integration.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exact trajectory identities><label|lem:E1.2> For the trajectories
    constructed below with endpoint <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> and every
    <math|m\<less\>N>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,N>=\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>,<space|2em><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>-\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>=Z<rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    All representations are admissible, and the terminal integral has the
    same value.
  </lemma>

  The following proof also proves Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>.

  <\render-proof|Proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.2> and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>>
    Use the bands and block map of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>
    and<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>, the
    continuous Wilson curve of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>, and the
    continuous step of the specified recursion in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2>. Set

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|m><rsup|->=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<tfrac|1|2><slope>s<rsub|m>,<space|2em>b<rsub|m><rsup|+>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2<slope>s<rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    These intervals lie in <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>>. Suppose the fine
    endpoint couplings are <math|b<rsub|m+1><rsup|->> and
    <math|b<rsub|m+1><rsup|+>>. The drift estimate
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost> give coarse endpoint bounds

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|m><rsup|->\<leqslant\>b<rsub|m><rsup|->-<tfrac|1|4><slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>\<less\>b<rsub|m><rsup|->,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|m><rsup|+>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|m><rsup|+>+<tfrac|3|4><slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>\<gtr\>b<rsub|m><rsup|+>.
    </equation*>

    For a continuous function whose endpoints bracket
    <math|<around|[|b<rsub|m><rsup|->,b<rsub|m><rsup|+>|]>>, restrict between
    its last lower hit before its first upper hit. Its values then remain in
    this interval. Restrict the parameter interval accordingly. Every
    preceding representation is restricted along with the new one, so all
    already obtained identities persist.

    Start at level<nbsp><math|N> with the Wilson curve restricted to
    <math|<around|[|b<rsub|N><rsup|->,b<rsub|N><rsup|+>|]>> and repeat this
    operation down to zero. Since <math|b<rsub|0><rsup|->=b<rsub|0><rsup|+>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>,
    it produces a trajectory with the required endpoint. The case <math|N=0>
    is the Wilson representation at <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. All
    retained representations and adjacent pairs belong to the chosen classes
    and relation because Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> returns admissible
    representations whose adjacent pairs lie in the chosen relation.

    The exact density identity at each step is the one in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, now applied only on the retained
    interval. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>, integration of the block
    kernel in its coarse argument is one. Fubini therefore preserves the
    integral, and the Wilson start gives its displayed value at every level.
    The path arrays were fixed before<nbsp><math|p> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>; restricting the parameter interval does
    not change them.

    Summing the drift from zero gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-running><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-<slope>s<rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>>.
    </equation>

    For a fixed splitting level<nbsp><math|k>, the tail is at most
    <math|sup<rsub|i\<geqslant\>k> J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>*s<rsub|m>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. The initial sum is fixed. Divide by
    <math|s<rsub|m>>, let <math|m\<to\>\<infty\>> and then
    <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. Since <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>,
    this proves the uniform running law in Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>;
    neither bound depends on <math|N\<geqslant\>m> or<nbsp><math|p>. The
    construction, exact identities and running asymptotic asserted there are
    all proved.
  </render-proof>

  The uniqueness estimate concerns the specified recursion, rather than
  arbitrary representations of a density. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>
  supplies its weighted analytic coordinates and their real power-series
  extension. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.3> uses only its
  fixed-level convergence assertion.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Analytic endpoint tuning><label|lem:E1.3> There is
    <math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>\<gtr\>0>, depending only on the construction,
    such that, for every <math|N,p> and <math|s\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>,
    exactly one bare coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>> starts
    a trajectory of the specified recursion ending at
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>*s>. Moreover,

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>=b<rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>*<space|1em><around|(|-1\<less\>s\<less\>1|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|b<rsub|N,p>> is holomorphic on
    <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, <reference|def:D25a> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>. Write <math|\<cal-D\>> for the maximum
    distance between the normalized coordinates of two admitted histories,
    including the coupling coordinate, and <math|\<delta\>> for their coarse
    endpoint difference. That lemma gives constants
    <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>,\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>, independent of
    <math|N,p>, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-analytic-input>\<cal-D\>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>/b<rsub|\<ast\>>+8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<cal-D\>,<space|2em>g<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/128.
    </equation>

    For equal endpoints, <math|\<cal-D\>\<leqslant\>\<cal-D\>/16> implies
    equality of the histories and hence of their bare couplings.

    Take <math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>=\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|\<ast\>>/4>,
    where <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>=1/16> is fixed in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>. The local action is affine in the
    varying coupling; the masks and sector domains are fixed in its analytic
    coordinates. Its power-series map preserves the
    radius-<math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>> ball and contracts by <math|1/2>
    for endpoint series <math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>*z>. Successive
    substitution converges in the norm <math|<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>
    of absolutely summable series. The map preserves real coefficients, so
    evaluation gives an admitted real trajectory for every
    <math|s\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>, pointwise in <math|s>. This supplies
    existence on the whole stated interval, including its endpoints. The
    selected coefficient at <math|s=0> is the trajectory of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.2>, by uniqueness. The bare-coupling series
    is holomorphic in <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>1> and has the claimed
    real values. All clipping and health conditions are covered by the
    admitted analytic neighbourhood in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>; they
    are not differentiated across their boundaries.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Marked fields and volume-uniform
  estimates><label|sec:partII-E.2>

  Fix a degree<nbsp><math|d>. The local section
  <math|P<rsub|d>=<localsection|d>> is that of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>,
  and <math|<factorscale><rsub|t>> is the factor-count scaling of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>. Suppress <math|N,p,d> while describing
  one transfer. Write <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>> for its unmarked representation
  and <math|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>,m>> for its vacuum scalar. Its site torus is
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,m>>>. An anchor is the lattice site at which
  the insertion is placed. Denote the marked regular supports by
  <math|Z<rsub|i>>, to distinguish them from the unmarked
  supports<nbsp><math|X<rsub|i>>. For the moment <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|d>>
  denotes a bare polynomial coordinate. Prescribe

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-marked-evolution>H<rsub|N><around|(|v|)>=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>v|]>,<space|2em>H<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>>H<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation>

  These densities keep track of one insertion under exact integration. The
  formula below describes the coordinates in which they will be represented.
  Write <math|x<rsub|0>> for the unit coefficient of
  <math|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>> and <math|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)><rsup|\<circ\>>=x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>-x<rsub|0>*\<b-1\>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Marked density><label|def:D26> A representation of the
    one-insertion density has the form

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>>|<cell|=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m>*<around*|[|x<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|]>+<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*R<rsub|i>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+e<rsup|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>,m>\|\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,m>>\|-<pathaction><rsub|N,m>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\><math-up|eligible>><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\>>Q<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|for>>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Here <math|R<rsub|i>,Q<rsub|i>> are the marked regular and forced
    remainders; eligibility is that of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>.
    The section and footprint are translated to the anchor. The remainders
    restore the exact density in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-evolution>.
  </definition>

  The marked regular norm below is anchored at the insertion site, taken to
  be zero; it is not anchored separately at each support. For selected
  majorants <math|e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>>,
  <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>sup <around|\||Q<rsub|i>|\|>>, set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-marked-mass>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>>*e<rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>>|<cell|=\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>+<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  The derivative seminorm is Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. A
  coefficient graph at level<nbsp><math|m> is a linear choice
  <math|x\<mapsto\>g<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|R<around|(|x|)>,Q<around|(|x|)>|)>>
  of such remainders, with the supports fixed and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  Its purpose is to use the current polynomial coordinate to bound the entire
  inserted density.

  The following step retains the complete inserted density while reexpressing
  its remainders in the new polynomial coordinate. The inverse estimate is
  essential: a bound in the original bare coordinate would accumulate at
  every step. For <math|m\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>>, write
  <math|u=L<rsub|m>*x> for the extracted polynomial coordinate. Write
  <math|<around|(|w<rsub|i>,k<rsub|i>|)>> for the engineering weight and
  factor count of<nbsp><math|b<rsub|d,i>>. For a coarse radial
  jet<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>>, let <math|U<rsup|W<rsub|0>><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>>
  be the fine stencil configuration obtained by exponentiating the free
  interpolation<nbsp><math|W<rsub|0>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> of the
  coarse potential of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>>. Here the coarse potential is
  the degree-<math|d> Taylor polynomial, in the jet coordinates, of the
  coarse-link logarithms. Let <math|<math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m>b<rsub|d,i>>
  be the label whose derivatives in<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>> at zero
  through order<nbsp><math|d> along <math|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>>
  agree with those of <math|P<rsub|d><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]><around|(|0;U<rsup|W<rsub|0>><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>|)>>,
  and put <math|<around|(|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|eng>><rsub|L>x|)><rsub|i>=L<rsup|w<rsub|i>>*x<rsub|i>>.
  The leading map is

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|m>=<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|m>><math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m><math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|eng>><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|m>><around|(|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|m+1>>|)><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  Taylor matching gives <math|<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m>|)><rsub|b*i>=\<delta\><rsub|b*i>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-w<rsub|i>>>
  for <math|w<rsub|b>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>> and <math|=0> for
  <math|k<rsub|b>\<less\>k<rsub|i>>. To bound the other entries, use the
  kernel form of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>: at averaging order
  <math|j\<geqslant\>d+3>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|m>*y+a,y<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1-r>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>d.
  </equation*>

  The reference-level choice in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>
  ensures this order. A jet potential grows polynomially, and
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>e<rsup|-c<rsub|j><around|\||z|\|>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|d>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  Thus its kernel sum and the required differences converge absolutely.
  Applying the displayed bound to each factor of a homogeneous jet monomial
  gives <math|<around|\||<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m>|)><rsub|b*i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-w<rsub|i>>>,
  with <math|C> depending only on <math|d> and the stage. Periodization gives
  the same bound on a torus by summing over its translates. The threshold
  formula in row<nbsp>9 of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> and the
  entry choices of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> give
  <math|0\<less\>t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2>. Hence
  <math|<around|(|T<rsub|m>|)><rsub|i*i>=<around|(|t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|k<rsub|i>>>,
  and back substitution in increasing weight bounds
  <math|T<rsub|m><rsup|\<pm\>1>> uniformly in <math|N>, <math|p> and <math|m>
  within a stage.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One marked step><label|lem:E-marked-step> The coefficient map
    <math|L<rsub|m>> is invertible. Its leading map <math|T<rsub|m>> sends
    each basis vector to its own and higher weights, with nonzero diagonal.
    Moreover,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||L<rsub|m>*x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*<around|\||L<rsub|m>*x|\|><rsub|1>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m>*<around|(|L<rsub|m>*x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||<around|(|L<rsub|m>*x|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The graphs are linear, vanish on the unit, and respect conjugation and
    signed permutations. Writing <math|u=L<rsub|m>*x>, fine graphs differing
    by at most <math|t*C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>> give
    coarse graphs differing by at most <math|t*C<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>/2>.
  </lemma>

  The proof gives the construction and the bounds, including the contraction
  between two choices of incoming remainders.

  <\proof>
    Use the local sections of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>, the exact
    block map of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, the connected expansion
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, the pre-reset estimates of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>, the subtraction of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-local-subtraction>, and the density identity
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>. Put <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|m>>,
    <math|s=C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>, <math|C=K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>> and
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>=<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>.
    All norms in this proof are coefficient sums or the anchored
    norm<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>.

    <em|Step 1: Marked terms in the local integration.> Introduce
    <math|z<rsup|2>=0>. Replace the regular exponential factor by its product
    with <math|1+z*<around|(|x<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|m+1><rsup|-1>>x<rsup|\<circ\>>|]>+<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*R<rsub|i>|)>>
    and replace a forced factor <math|K<rsub|i>> by
    <math|K<rsub|i>+z*Q<rsub|i>>. The coefficient of <math|z> in the eligible
    covering sum is exactly Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D26>. Apply the
    coefficientwise rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>
    in this nilpotent algebra. Each connected term now contains one
    distinguished factor. For a term with ordinary factors
    <math|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|h>>, its marked coefficient is

    <\equation*>
      <around|[|z|]>*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|h><around|(|a<rsub|i>+z*b<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|h>b<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i>a<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Root its connected support at this distinguished factor. The sums over
    all other factors are bounded by the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>; there is no sum over possible
    translates of the marked anchor. Absolute convergence justifies the
    displayed extraction and integration term by term. A regular mark has its
    derivative majorant <math|e<rsub|i>>; a forced mark has its amplitude
    majorant <math|k<rsub|i>>. The latter retains its original eligibility
    event, so the large-field estimate used in the forced expansion still
    applies.

    The three contributions to a basis input are bounded as follows. Let
    <math|T<rsub|m>> be the leading map defined before the lemma, and
    <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|L>> the diagonal map

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<math-ss|S><rsub|L>x|)><rsub|i>=L<rsup|d-w<rsub|i>>*x<rsub|i>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d,
    </equation*>

    where <math|w<rsub|i>> is the weight of the homogeneous basis polynomial.
    The unit has <math|w<rsub|0>=0>. The map <math|T<rsub|m>> fixes the unit
    and preserves the nonconstant subspace. It is triangular in the
    homogeneous weights: <math|<around|(|T<rsub|m>|)><rsub|i*j>\<ne\>0> and
    <math|i\<ne\>j> imply <math|w<rsub|j>\<less\>w<rsub|i>>. Its diagonal
    entries are nonzero, so inversion proceeds in increasing weight, with the
    finite stage bounds included in<nbsp><math|K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>. Let
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>\<geqslant\>1> bound its two operator norms
    and the following local remainder estimates before symmetry averaging;
    the leading stage bound was chosen with
    <math|K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<geqslant\>2*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>*C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>>.
    Taylor subtraction of a primary mark leaves coefficient error at most
    <math|\<eta\><around|\||x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>> and remainder at
    most <math|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    A regular remainder of mass <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|Z|)>>*e<rsub|i>>
    contributes at most <math|\<eta\>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|Z|)>>*e<rsub|i>>
    to each of these two bounds. A forced remainder contributes at most
    <math|\<eta\>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|Y|)>>*k<rsub|i>/\<kappa\><rsub|m+1>>.

    The common local error bound is <math|\<eta\>=\<eta\><rsub|m>> from
    item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. The
    local polynomial is subtracted through degree<nbsp><math|d> before
    rescaling. Each term left from a nonwrapping regular mark has at least
    one additional scaled jet weight; the block rescaling gives the inverse
    block factor in its norm. The finite connected sums and the ordered
    derivative product rule introduce only stage constants. For a forced
    mark, the support sum keeps half the original forced suppression: its
    normalization is <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m+1>>, not
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|for>>>. The remaining
    suppression absorbs the same derivative and support factors. Wrapping
    terms are assigned to the forced remainder. Thus the bounds before
    choosing parameters have the form <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>/L+o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>><around|(|1|)>>,
    with <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> fixed before <math|L> and the
    second term tending to zero at fixed stage and block factor. Choose
    <math|L<rsup|3/5>\<gtr\>2*C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> first. The first
    term is then less than <math|<tfrac|1|2>L<rsup|-2/5>>; raise the coupling
    entry until the absolute value of the second term is at most
    <math|<tfrac|1|2>L<rsup|-2/5>>. This is item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>
    in the scale choices of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. The
    degree-<math|d> Taylor extraction is on healthy charts; the indicators
    themselves are never differentiated.

    Let <math|u> be the extracted coarse polynomial coordinate, <math|r> the
    incoming anchored remainder norm and <math|r<rprime|'>> the resulting
    coarse remainder norm, including its forced normalization. Summing the
    contributions gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-atom-estimates><around|\||u-T<rsub|m><math-ss|S><rsub|L>x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*<around|(|<around|\||x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>+r|)>,<space|2em>r<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>\|<math-ss|S><rsub|L>x<rsup|\<circ\>>\|<rsub|1>+\<eta\>*r,<space|2em>r=<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m+1><around|(|x|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>.
    </equation>

    The regular-remainder terms in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> use the
    vanishing Taylor coefficients through degree<nbsp><math|d>. Retained
    incoming regular terms use the support conversion
    factor<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion>. The same sums with the
    primary mark canceled give the Lipschitz bound
    <math|\<eta\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|g-<wide|g|~>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>>
    for the coefficient and remainder differences. This cancellation keeps
    the entire forced marked family; estimating the two families separately
    would lose this bound.

    <em|Step 2: Linear choice and inverse bound.> Perform the construction on
    each homogeneous basis vector and on all its signed and conjugated
    images. Take their union of supports, with zero entries where necessary,
    and recombine using the basis coefficients. Average over the finite
    signed-permutation group and over conjugation. This gives a
    complex-linear family with the required symmetries and exact density
    identity. Let <math|A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>\<geqslant\>1> bound the
    sum of absolute recombination coefficients in this finite averaging.
    After averaging, the error in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> is at most
    <math|\<eta\>*<around|(|1+s|)>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>><around|\||x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>,
    and the remainder is at most <math|<around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>+\<eta\>*s|)>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>><around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.

    Use<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-graph-reserve> with <math|s=C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>,
    <math|C=K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>> and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|-3/8>>.
    The cross-stage inequality follows from
    item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns>, fixed before the ratios. Since
    <math|\<eta\>*s\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>>, the
    averaged remainder constant is at most <math|K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>=C>. In
    the notation <math|y=<math-ss|S><rsub|L>x>, the resulting inequalities
    are

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||u-T<rsub|m>*y|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*<around|(|<around|\||x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>+r|)>,<space|2em>r<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>C\|y<rsup|\<circ\>>\|<rsub|1>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*r,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>s<around|\||x<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|d><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>>,
    the first two conditions in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-graph-reserve> imply

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||y|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\||y|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||y<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\||u<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    For example, apply <math|T<rsub|m><rsup|-1>> and use
    <math|2*C*<around|\||u-T<rsub|m>*y|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||y<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>
    to absorb the error; the same argument on the nonconstant subspace gives
    the last bound. Moreover <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*r\<leqslant\>C<around|\||y<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>,
    so

    <\equation*>
      r<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>2*C<around|\||y<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*C<rsup|2><around|\||u<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||u<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    The linear map <math|x\<mapsto\>u=L<rsub|m>*x> is injective by
    <math|<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>s<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>>,
    hence invertible in finite dimension. This defines the new graph by
    <math|g<rsub|m><around|(|u|)>=g<rsub|m>*<around|(|L<rsub|m>*x|)>>. The
    two coefficient estimates now follow. In particular the inverse estimate
    has no factor <math|L<rsup|d>>: <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|L><rsup|-1>> has
    norm at most one.

    For the unit, the primary extraction is exact, <math|T<rsub|m>> fixes it
    and its nonconstant coordinate is zero. All the preceding remainder
    bounds are then zero. It is sent to <math|L<rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>, with zero
    remainders. Finally, when two fine graphs differ by at most
    <math|t*C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>> in the remainder
    norm, the Lipschitz estimate above and rebasing by the just-proved
    inverse map give a coarse difference at most
    <math|<tfrac|1|2>t*C<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>>: the two
    contributions are the changed remainder at fixed input and the changed
    input coordinate, each bounded by <math|t/4>
    using<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-graph-reserve>. This is the graph contraction used
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>.
  </proof>

  Choose <math|r<around|(|d|)>> with <math|J<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>>\<geqslant\>d+3>,
  <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d+1> and
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d> for
  <math|m\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>>. Apply
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> to the Wilson start, and let
  <math|L<rsub|m>> be its coefficient map. Define, before normalizing the
  lattice fields,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-terminal-map><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>=L<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>>*\<cdots\>*L<rsub|N-1>*<space|1em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  An empty product is the identity. For <math|N\<less\>r<around|(|d|)>>
  choose an arbitrary invertible map, as allowed in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Reference normalization><label|lem:E2.1> For every <math|d>,
    <math|N\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>> and <math|p>, the invertible
    maps<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-terminal-map> satisfy

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|x<rsub|N><around|(|v|)>=v,<space|2em>x<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>><around|(|v|)>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>v,>>|<row|<cell|H<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>>H<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>*<space|1em><around|(|r<around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>m\<less\>N|)>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    At the cutoff, <math|H<rsub|N><around|(|v|)>=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>v|]>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D08>,
    <reference|def:D26> and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>. At
    level<nbsp><math|N>, split the unmasked section into its healthy part and
    its complement <math|f<rsub|v>=\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|unhealthy>>*P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>v<rsup|\<circ\>>|]>>.
    For an eligible family<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\>> let
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>> be its forced terms whose supports
    meet<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>>. If
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>=\<varnothing\>>, exact coverage leaves no
    flag in<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and <math|f<rsub|v>=0>. Otherwise
    replace <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>> by one new marked forced address
    with support <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>Y<rsub|i>>,
    inventory <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>I<rsub|i>>,
    zero unmarked activity, and <math|Q=f<rsub|v>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>>
    on the event that the flags in that support are exactly this inventory,
    <math|Q=0> otherwise. An index of the new marked family records the
    cluster<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>, even when two clusters
    have the same support and inventory; retain all original forced factors
    in<nbsp><math|Q>. The remaining supports are disjoint from the merged
    support, since they avoid<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and were disjoint
    from every merged support. This is a bijection between eligible families
    meeting<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and eligible marked families
    containing one new address, so the forced sum restores
    <math|f<rsub|v><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>> exactly. The
    regular remainder is zero and <math|x<rsub|N>=v>.

    The support weight of the merged address is at most
    <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|)>>>
    times the product of the old support weights: their connected covers all
    meet the stencil cover. The activity caps bound the sum of the weighted
    majorants of addresses meeting<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> by
    <math|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>.
    Dropping disjointness and summing their products over nonempty subsets
    gives at most <math|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>-1>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-global-bound>, <math|<around|\||f<rsub|v>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>*t<rsub|N><rsup|-d><around|\||v<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>;
    with<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> and
    <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|N>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*C<rsub|d>*t<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>-1|)><around|\||v<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|\||v<rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    by item<nbsp><reference|it:E-birth>. This is the case <math|m=N> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>.

    Iterate the marked step on the same unmarked trajectory. It gives
    <math|x<rsub|m>=L<rsub|m>*x<rsub|m+1>> and the exact equality
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-evolution>. Each <math|L<rsub|m>> is
    invertible, so their product is invertible and
    <math|x<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>v>. No choice of a
    different unmarked representation is made. The factor
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>> agrees with
    <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|d>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>>.
  </proof>

  The normalization retains the symmetries of the local sections. Its unit
  includes the degree-dependent spacing factor.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Symmetry and the unit><label|lem:E2.1a> For every <math|d>,
    <math|N\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>> and <math|p>, the maps
    <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> commute with conjugation
    and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>. For <math|r<around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>,

    <\equation*>
      x<rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>|)>=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>=a<rsub|r<around|(|d|)>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>
    has insertion one.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the section symmetries in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>, the
    symmetric block map of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, and the
    equivariant coefficient extraction in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>. Complex conjugation and signed
    permutations commute with integration, Taylor extraction and the chosen
    coefficient graph. Each operation therefore commutes with every
    <math|L<rsub|m>> and their product.

    For a unit input, the marked density is
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>>. Exact
    integration and multiplication by <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>> give
    <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|d><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m>> at
    level<nbsp><math|m>. The selected extraction has zero nonconstant
    coordinate and zero remainder for this density. Induction proves the
    displayed identity. In particular <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>.
    Substitute into Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>; factor scaling fixes
    the polynomial unit, so its insertion equals
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*a<rsub|N><rsup|d>=1>.
  </proof>

  The coefficient graph bounds give the following uniform control through the
  trajectory.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked remainders and transport><label|lem:E2.2> For
    <math|N\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>> and
    <math|r<around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>, the coefficients and
    both remainder families are linear in<nbsp><math|v>, with fixed supports,
    and

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>+<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m><around|(|v|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|m\<less\>N>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||x<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><around|\||x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><around|\||x<rsub|m+1><around|(|v|)>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D05b>,
    <reference|def:D08>, <reference|def:D26> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>\U<reference|lem:E2.1a>. The
    graph inequality of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>, in the
    norm<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>, is the first display. Its two
    coefficient inequalities give the second display. Iterating retains the
    graph at every level. For fixed supports, take the union of the addresses
    selected for the finitely many basis inputs and fill unused addresses by
    zero. The basis construction in that lemma then makes the coefficients
    and both remainders linear in<nbsp><math|v>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> states that the selected graph
    vanishes on the unit; the density decomposition alone would not imply
    this.
  </proof>

  These cancellations are the marked format<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation>
  of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked local format><label|lem:E2.2a> For
    <math|N\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>> and
    <math|r<around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>, the complex regular
    remainders are Borel, gauge invariant, local and smooth as in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>. Their supports contain the anchor.
    Their values at the identity, and their radial-jet coefficients through
    degree<nbsp><math|d> on nonwrapping supports, vanish. The forced
    remainders satisfy Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25>, and

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|i>\<ne\>0<space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em><text|the
      anchor belongs to >Y<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D06>,
    <reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D26> and the anchored extraction in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>. Subtracting the value at the
    identity and the radial Taylor polynomial gives exactly the two stated
    regular cancellations. On wrapping supports no radial Taylor cancellation
    is imposed. In the connected expansion every marked component contains
    the original insertion anchor. The support is the union of the supports
    in that component, so it still contains that anchor after enlargement.
    The same argument applies when the component is assigned to a forced
    address. It is assigned only on that address's eligibility event.
    Locality, gauge invariance and Borel measurability are preserved by local
    integration and by these tests. The regular derivatives are taken on
    healthy configurations, as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>; no
    derivative of a health indicator is used. The fixed basis construction
    and its support unions retain these properties under iteration.
  </proof>

  A bond tiling has cell boundaries through link midpoints. Its reflected
  copies place an observable in every cell, with conjugation on odd
  reflections. The copy <math|<around|(|F|)><rsub|C>> is the pullback of
  <math|F> under the composition of bond reflections taking the base cell to
  <math|C>, conjugated when that composition has odd parity.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Chessboard root><label|def:D27> For a tiling
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>> into congruent reflected cells and
    an observable <math|F> in its base cell, set

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>=<around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|(|F|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|><rsup|1/<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>>.
    </equation*>

    Interior means dependence only on links with both endpoints strictly
    inside the base cell. The centered tiling
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|y|)>> has physical
    cell side <math|a<rsub|m>> and base cell centered at<nbsp><math|y>. This
    quantity converts reflected-product estimates into bounds on one
    insertion.
  </definition>

  For interior complex observables, these roots are seminorms and satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-chess><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|(|F<rsub|C>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|C>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>.
  </equation>

  Apply the abstract chessboard theorem, <cite-detail|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978|Theorem<nbsp>4.1>,
  successively in the four axes. The real vector space is the space of
  complex cell functions, and the reflection includes conjugation.
  Translation invariance gives cyclicity; reflection positivity gives the
  required Schwarz inequality. For the Wilson measure this positivity is
  <cite-detail|Seiler1982|Theorem<nbsp>2.1, pp.<nbsp>14--15>, with compact
  matrix group, positive coupling and observables on one side of the bond
  planes. For <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>>, there are
  <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>> cells per axis, an even integer. The cell side
  <math|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N>/D<rsub|m>> is odd, so the centered
  boundaries pass through link midpoints. Bounded Borel cell functions follow
  by approximation in the finite-dimensional configuration measure.

  The centered root estimate must also control anchors near arbitrary,
  separated configurations. Write <math|v=<around|(|d,c|)>> for the insertion
  token. A signed image means the image under<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>;
  conjugation is also allowed. Two tilings are aligned when their cells have
  the same axes and every coarse boundary is a fine boundary.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Interior root bound><label|lem:F1a> Fix
    <math|m\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>, <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>,
    and<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>\<geqslant\>0>. Suppose, for every
    anchor<nbsp><math|y<rprime|'>>, every signed image or image under
    conjugation of<nbsp><math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y<rprime|'>|)>> has root
    at most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>>
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>>
    and stencil within<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>> of<nbsp><math|y<rprime|'>>. In
    every translated bond tiling<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\>> of
    side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>>, an anchor at distance at
    least<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k>> from its cell boundary satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-uncentered>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y|)><text| is
      interior>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  The proof transfers the centered bound first at aligned cell centers, then
  between them.

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, its symmetry in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>, the cell interior condition of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>.
    Since<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N>> is odd, the fine-cell centers are
    lattice sites. At an aligned fine-cell center, reflect all coarse copies
    into distinct fine cells. Their signs and conjugations are among the
    assumed images. Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> with<nbsp><math|1> in every
    unoccupied fine cell. Taking the coarse root bounds it
    by<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>>.

    For intermediate anchors, let<nbsp><math|H<around|(|z|)>> be the coarse
    root after<nbsp><math|z> lattice translations in one axis, with the other
    coordinates fixed. Translation invariance and reflection give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-logconvex>H<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>H*<around|(|z-1|)>*H*<around|(|z+1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Indeed, translate the reflected product by one lattice step. Alternate
    cells then contain shifts<nbsp><math|z-1> and<nbsp><math|z+1>;
    apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>. On a finite interval a nonnegative
    sequence satisfying this inequality cannot exceed its endpoint maximum:
    an interior positive maximum extends to both neighbours and hence to an
    endpoint. This includes zero endpoints.

    Bracket each coordinate of<nbsp><math|y> by its two neighbouring
    fine-center coordinates. All anchors in the resulting rectangle have
    margin at least<nbsp><math|2*a<rsub|k>>, so their stencils are interior.
    Induct on<nbsp><math|i=0,\<ldots\>,4>: the root is at
    most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>> when the first<nbsp><math|i>
    coordinates are arbitrary lattice coordinates in these intervals and the
    remaining coordinates are endpoints. The case<nbsp><math|i=0> is the
    aligned-center bound. In the next axis, the endpoint bounds hold
    uniformly in the coordinates already moved;<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-logconvex>
    supplies the intervening bounds. The case<nbsp><math|i=4>
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-uncentered>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Several-insertion expansion.> For a finite set of slots
  <math|I<rsub|0>>, work in the commutative algebra
  <math|<C><around|[|z<rsub|i>:i\<in\>I<rsub|0>|]>/<around|(|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>:i\<in\>I<rsub|0>|)>>.
  Write <math|z<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>z<rsub|i>>. The exact source
  density starts at

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-multiple-density>\<cal-H\><rsub|N>*<around|(|z;U|)>=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|0>><around|(|1+z<rsub|i>*F<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-H\><rsub|m>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>>\<cal-H\><rsub|m+1>.
  </equation>

  Thus its normalized <math|z<rsub|I>> integral is the cutoff moment of the
  corresponding physical insertions. At level<nbsp><math|m> write its local
  representation as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-source-form>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*f<rsub|i,m><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|X>H<rsub|m,X>*<around|(|z;U|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\><math-up|eligible>><big|prod><rsub|Y\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around*|(|K<rsub|m,Y><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*k<rsub|i,m,Y><around|(|U|)>+K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>*<around|(|z;U|)>|)>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m>> is the unmarked exponent, <math|f<rsub|i,m>>
  and <math|k<rsub|i,m,Y>> are the complete one-insertion exponential and
  forced terms, including their physical spacing factors. The polynomials
  <math|H<rsub|m,X>> and <math|K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>> contain only
  modes <math|z<rsub|I>> with <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2>. Their
  support contains every anchor indexed by a nonzero mode. The regular terms
  include their health masks, and each forced term retains its eligibility
  test. Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> distinguishes the exact
  multiple-source density from the one obtained by keeping only its unmarked
  and singleton data.

  The rooted expansion needs a bound on all the other marked factors. We
  verify it for the primary insertions of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, with their selected coefficient
  graphs, before taking any replicated integral. Fix
  <math|r<around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N> and marked anchors whose
  parents agree and whose separation is at least <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at
  every level <math|i\<in\><around|[|k,N|]>>. For mark<nbsp><math|h> let
  <math|x<rsub|h,i>> be its dimensionless singleton coefficient, and set

  <\equation*>
    b<rsub|h,i>=a<rsub|i><rsup|-d><around|\||x<rsub|h,i>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|h,i+1>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>*\<rho\><rsub|h,i>,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|h,k>*b<rsub|h,k>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>.
  </equation*>

  The initial radii are positive. Let <math|v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>>
  be the weighted integrated majorants of the other marked factors on their
  complete read supports <math|Z<rsub|a,i>>, with derivative and support
  weights as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked attachments><label|lem:E-source-attachments> For these
    sources, at every level <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a,i>>v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>/2.
    </equation*>

    The bound is uniform in the cutoff, the period and the number of marks.
  </lemma>

  The following proof also checks the increase of the marker radius during
  integration.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D06>,
    <reference|def:D08>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>,
    <reference|lem:E-marked-step>, <reference|lem:E2.2a>, and the rooted
    clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>.

    For the local expansion, give a mode <math|z<rsub|I>> the weight
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>w<rsub|i>>, with <math|w<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>.
    The resulting marker coefficient norm is submultiplicative:
    multiplication sums over disjoint splittings of<nbsp><math|I>, exactly
    the splittings in the product of the two weighted norms. The
    coefficientwise rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>
    applies in this algebra, with the attachment bound at the preceding stage
    of the construction. Derivative and support weights are included in the
    separate majorants <math|v<rsub|a,i>>; no submultiplicativity is asserted
    for them. Connected terms with at least two marks have the support
    estimate

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-marked-cover>B<rsub|k><around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>64<cover><rsub|B<rsub|k>><around|(|X|)>,<space|2em><around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2,
    </equation>

    when distinct anchors are separated by <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>>. To
    verify it, take a minimal cover by <math|M> radius-<math|B<rsub|k>> balls
    with connected union. Join their centers by a tree with edges of length
    at most <math|2*B<rsub|k>+1>. Its doubled traversal has length at most
    <math|6*B<rsub|k>*<around|(|M-1|)>>. Assign each anchor to one covering
    center. Distinct anchors have distinct centers, and the distance between
    any two assigned centers is at least <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>-2*B<rsub|k>>.
    Visit these centers in their cyclic order in the traversal. The triangle
    inequality gives <math|<around|\||I|\|>*<around|(|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>-2*B<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>6*B<rsub|k>*<around|(|M-1|)>>.
    For <math|B<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>8> this implies the stated bound, with
    room in the constant. The argument uses the periodic max-distance and
    needs no nonwrapping assumption. This is the marked covering estimate.

    It permits each source weight to be enlarged by
    <math|e<rsup|\<delta\>*B<rsub|k>/64>> when <math|\<delta\>> units of the
    support exponent are removed. In particular, the radius increase from
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|h,i+1>> to <math|\<rho\><rsub|h,i>> costs
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>> on a
    mode<nbsp><math|I>. For <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2>, the same
    covering argument at level<nbsp><math|i> gives

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|{|<frac|64*log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>|B<rsub|i>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|i>><around|(|X|)>|}>.
    </equation*>

    The coefficient of the covering number tends to zero at fixed stage,
    since <math|B<rsub|i>\<asymp\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>> and
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<to\>1>. The
    source entry bounds therefore pay this increase from the support reserve
    at each step. The singleton terms use the coefficient and graph bounds
    established below. This pays for the varying radii on all modes with at
    least two marks.

    The radii <math|\<rho\><rsub|h,i>> estimate the intermediate source
    polynomials; the radius in the final integral remains
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|h,k>>. The inverse estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> gives
    <math|b<rsub|h,i+1>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*b<rsub|h,i>>.
    Therefore<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport> proves by
    induction that

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|h,i>*b<rsub|h,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>*<space|2em><around|(|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N|)>.
    </equation*>

    The graph norm has the same bound with its column constant included in
    <math|A<rsub|j,d>>. The normalized forced terms retain their original
    eligibility events. Their remaining large-field suppression pays the
    fixed derivative factors before the forced normalization is divided out.

    Here is the conversion from a marked-anchor bound to the site sum
    required by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Each support contains
    its marked anchor. If it also contains a site<nbsp><math|x> at distance
    <math|R> from that anchor, its connected covering number is at least
    <math|R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)>>. One reserved unit of support
    exponent thus gives a factor at most <math|e<rsup|-R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)>>>.
    Distinct anchors have occupancy one, so summing this factor over them is
    bounded by

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>e<rsup|-<around|\||z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>/<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>.
    </equation*>

    This estimate also bounds the periodic sum, by lifting the distinct
    anchors to nearest representatives. The finite derivative products and
    polynomial evaluations cost at most <math|A<rsub|j,d>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|q<rsub|d>>>.
    Rooted attachment sums use the other reserved support unit to absorb
    <math|e<rsup|\<eta\><rsub|i>*n<rsub|0>>>; summing their convergent tree
    majorants enlarges the bound by at most two, after decreasing
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>> if necessary. For the weighted majorants
    <math|v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>> of all other marked factors,
    including the connected source terms already formed, this gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a,i>>v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>\<leqslant\>2*A<rsub|j,d>*<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|q<rsub|d>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>/2.
    </equation*>

    Start with finite source products at level<nbsp><math|N>, where there are
    no connected higher modes. In each descending step, apply the rooted
    bound to the input majorants, enlarge the marker radius using the
    covering estimate, and use the displayed site bound for the output
    majorants. This closes the induction, with a bound independent of the
    number of marks and of the truncation. The unmarked factors contribute at
    most the other <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>/2>, by
    item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries>. Thus the complete attachment sum is
    at most <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>> at every level
    <math|i\<in\><around|[|k,N|]>>. Passing to the absolutely convergent
    limit preserves the bound. In particular the rooted clause applies to the
    other marked factors as well as to the ordinary factors.
  </proof>

  The next estimate controls one insertion in every reflected cell. Its
  normalization cost is proportional to the number of marks and to the number
  of coarse sites, rather than to the number of fine sites. We also use it
  for a residual observable<nbsp><math|E> with an exact source
  representation. Call <math|w*E> admitted at level<nbsp><math|k> when its
  weighted singleton exponential bound is at most<nbsp><math|3>, its forced
  singleton bound is at most<nbsp><math|1>, and the connected regular and
  forced bounds contribute at most one each per mark, with the original
  eligibility tests. The source evolution from its initial level down
  to<nbsp><math|k> must retain these allowances and satisfy the attachment
  bounds<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance> at every intervening level.
  This condition concerns the complete source, including its forced terms.
  Fix <math|d> and a bounded coefficient ball. The pairs below satisfy
  <math|r<around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>k> and
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Replicated source bound><label|lem:E-replication> There are radii
    <math|\<frak-r\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>, independent of<nbsp><math|p>, such
    that, for each such pair, some <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|d,<text|ball>,m,k|)>>
    gives, for all <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all<nbsp><math|p>,

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-r\><rsub|k><rsup|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>|\|>><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>><around|(|O<around|(|c|)>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>*q<rsub|k>+6<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>|\|>>.
    </equation*>

    For every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|k<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>> with <math|6-log
    \<frak-r\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<eta\>|)>*s<rsub|k>> for all
    <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>>. The same integral
    bound holds with <math|O<around|(|c|)>,\<frak-r\><rsub|k>> replaced by
    <math|E,w> whenever <math|w*E> is admitted at<nbsp><math|k>.
  </lemma>

  The proof below bounds the complete source before taking its replicated
  integral.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D08>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>, <reference|lem:E-marked-step>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>, and the expansion in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Enlarge the fixed coefficient
    radius to include its signed and conjugated images. Put

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|k>=R<rprime|'>*C<rsub|J<rsub|k>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k>C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>,<space|2em>\<frak-r\><rsub|k>=a<rsub|k><rsup|d>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>/E<rsub|k>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|R<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0> depends on the coefficient ball and
    <math|w<rsub|d>> is a fixed nonnegative integer large enough for its
    finite polynomial evaluations and forced counterparts. The transport
    inequality of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>, iterated from
    <math|r<around|(|d|)>>, bounds the level-<math|k> coefficients by the
    product in <math|E<rsub|k>>. Threshold powers introduce only a fixed
    power of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>; enlarging <math|w<rsub|d>>
    includes them. Increase <math|w<rsub|d>> to at least <math|q<rsub|d>+4>.
    Multiplying every mark by <math|\<frak-r\><rsub|k>> then bounds its full
    singleton size by <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>.

    The reflected anchors at level<nbsp><math|k> have spacing
    <math|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|k><rsup|2>>, and their
    parents agree under each block map. Since
    <math|B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> and
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3>, their spacing is at least
    <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at every finer level. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments> with initial radius
    <math|\<frak-r\><rsub|k>>. Its full-interval bound verifies the
    hypothesis of the rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>.
    Use the marker coefficient norm in that proof; derivative and support
    weights remain in the separate majorants.

    Singleton terms are bounded directly by their one-mark norms. Taylor's
    product rule introduces at most the factorial of the derivative order;
    the stage column constant was chosen above this factorial. With
    <math|j=J<rsub|k+1>>, the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> supplies the derivative bound at
    support exponent <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|k>>+3> and the forced bound
    <math|v<rsub|0>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|k+1><rsup|2>>>
    at exponent <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|k>>+2>, where <math|v<rsub|0>> is
    the integrated majorant of the distinguished factor and <math|n<rsub|0>>
    its covering number. Multiplying by a mode weight changes only the marked
    letter in its tree sum. Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns> pays the
    derivative factorial. The weighted sums therefore retain those bounds;
    decreasing the final exponent from <math|100> to any value in
    <math|<around|[|98,100|]>> only decreases the sums. The forced
    normalization uses half the suppression, leaving the other half for the
    marked derivative and attachment losses. Their spatial anchor is always
    one of the marks; a nonzero mode is assigned only to a support containing
    all its marked anchors. This proves the support assertion
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, as well as its absolute convergence.
    Restricting the set of marks commutes with the construction, since every
    operation takes disjoint unions of their modes.

    At the stopping level, the weighted supremum of each complete singleton
    exponential term is at most <math|3>, and that of its forced profile is
    at most<nbsp><math|1>. Indeed, its polynomial part is at most its
    coefficient bound, its marked regular sum is at most that bound, and the
    normalization of the forced part is at most one;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> and the definition of <math|E<rsub|k>>
    bound the sum by<nbsp><math|3>. The forced profile follows from its
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>> normalization.
    For<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, the local absolute sums give an
    additional two per mark: exponentiation of the connected regular terms
    and the eligible forced sum each contribute at most one. Integrating the
    remaining scalar-free unmarked exponent and dividing by the unmarked
    partition function gives at most <math|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>*q<rsub|k>>>,
    with <math|q<rsub|k>> as in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-majorant>. Thus
    coefficient extraction in the weighted source norm yields

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-r\><rsub|k><rsup|<around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|0>>F<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>*q<rsub|k>+<around|(|3+1+2|)><around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Exact integration preserves these normalized coefficients, proving the
    first assertion. The ratio of site and cell counts is
    <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>>. For a residual source
    admitted at radius<nbsp><math|w>, the same coefficient extraction uses
    <math|w> in place of<nbsp><math|\<frak-r\><rsub|k>>; only the bounds
    <math|3+1+2> and the unmarked ratio enter this calculation. Taking the
    cell root gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-residual-root><around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>w<rsup|-1>*exp
      <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k>+6|}>.
    </equation>

    In particular <math|w=c*w<rsub|0>> gives a bound
    <math|C<rsub|m,k,w<rsub|0>>/c>, uniformly in the cutoff and period. This
    proves the residual clause, without requiring <math|E> to be a primary
    insertion.

    Finally<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>, summed as
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-running>, gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>k>log C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The separate final column is included in this sum for <math|k\<gtr\>0>.
    Hence, for every <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|k<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>> with <math|6-log
    \<frak-r\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<eta\>|)>*s<rsub|k>> for all
    <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>>. All choices precede
    the period, and all the estimates are on full cutoff tails.
  </proof>

  The centered estimate fixes its constant before the physical cell size. A
  sufficiently fine cutoff then places the fixed insertion stencil strictly
  inside that cell.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Centered bound><label|lem:E2.3> For every <math|d> and
    <math|R,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|A*<around|(|d,R,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<geqslant\>0> such that, for
    every <math|m\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>>, some
    <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|d,R,\<varepsilon\>,m|)>\<geqslant\>m> gives,
    for <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>, every <math|p,y> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R>,

    <\equation*>
      O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c,y|)><text| is
      interior>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c,y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|y|)>>\<leqslant\>A*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>.
    The replicated integral and radius bounds, together with the
    stopping-scale estimate in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>, give

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<around|(|c,0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|0|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k>+6-log
      \<frak-r\><rsub|k>|}>,<label|eq:E-centered-input>>>|<row|<cell|6-log
      \<frak-r\><rsub|k>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<eta\>|)>*s<rsub|k>*<space|1em><around|(|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0><around|(|\<eta\>|)>|)>.<eq-number>>>>>
    </align>

    The cutoff threshold is uniform in the coefficient ball and
    in<nbsp><math|p>; <math|k=k<around|(|m|)>> is the first level specified
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>.

    Put <math|\<tau\>=s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>, <math|\<upsilon\>=s<rsub|m>>
    and <math|W=<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>>. Since
    <math|q<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\>-\<upsilon\>=log <around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>W\<leqslant\>W*q<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>*\<tau\>.
    </equation*>

    The exponent in the first line of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-centered-input> is
    therefore at most <math|<around|(|d+2*\<eta\>|)>*\<tau\>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+2*\<eta\>|)>*\<upsilon\>/<around|(|1-\<eta\>|)>>.
    Choose <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<less\>1> so that
    <math|<around|(|d+2*\<eta\>|)>/<around|(|1-\<eta\>|)>\<leqslant\>d+\<varepsilon\>>.
    This proves the required estimate with constant one for all sufficiently
    large<nbsp><math|m>. For the finitely many remaining
    <math|m\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>>, use the first line
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-centered-input> at the selected fixed
    <math|k<around|(|m|)>> and take the maximum of its ratio to
    <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>> and<nbsp>one. This
    fixes<nbsp><math|A> before<nbsp><math|m>.

    Finally the stencil of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08> has fixed
    lattice radius. For fixed<nbsp><math|m> its physical radius tends to zero
    as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>. Translation
    covariance moves the centered calculation to any<nbsp><math|y>. This
    proves interiority and the bound, using precisely the expansion
    estimates<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-centered-input>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Equation and difference corrections.> The following estimates
  concern the insertions of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, with the
  terminal maps<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>. They will be used to identify the quotient
  fields in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>. A statement holds on full
  cutoff tails if it holds for all <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and
  every<nbsp><math|p>. A nonnegative family <math|b<rsub|N,p>> has subpower
  growth when, for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|b<rsub|N,p>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|N><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>> on such a
  tail. A coefficient family is admissible if

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-subpower>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0\<exists\>N<rsub|0>\<forall\>N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>\<forall\>p:<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|N><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>.
  </equation>

  For later volume extraction we also use strictly increasing subsequences
  <math|N=<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>>, <math|p=P<around|(|k|)>> and the
  ordered limit

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E2-tails><math-up|olim><space|0.17em>X<rsub|N,p>=x<space|1em>\<Longleftrightarrow\><space|1em>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0\<exists\>k<rsub|0>\<forall\>k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>\<exists\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>\<forall\>j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>:<around|\||X<rsub|<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>,P<around|(|k|)>>-x|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation>

  The ordered versions of admissibility and smallness replace full cutoff
  tails by these quantifiers. We prove the stronger versions for the bounds
  below. The same constructions work for ordered-admissible families, with
  their ordered thresholds; this is the form used in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>.

  We use the chessboard root and interior condition of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>. Translates of a bond tiling are by
  lattice vectors. An anchor is the lattice site at which an insertion is
  placed. In a base cell of side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>,
  let<nbsp><math|z<rsup|a>> be its integer coordinate measured from the first
  site in direction<nbsp><math|a>, and put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E2-margin>M<rsub|n<rsub|\<ast\>>,k,N>=<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|a<rsub|k>|8*<around|(|n<rsub|\<ast\>>+1|)>*a<rsub|N>>|\<rfloor\>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|n<rsub|\<ast\>>,k,N>\<leqslant\>z<rsup|a>,<space|1em>z<rsup|a>+M<rsub|n<rsub|\<ast\>>,k,N>+1\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  These inequalities specify a relative interior margin.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Small errors><label|def:E2-small> An observable
    family<nbsp><math|E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>> is small if
    some<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|N,p>\<geqslant\>0> with <math|sup<rsub|p>
    \<eta\><rsub|N,p>\<to\>0> as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> satisfies the
    following condition. For every arity<nbsp><math|n<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
    lower level<nbsp><math|m>, there is <math|k\<geqslant\>m> such that, on a
    full cutoff tail, <math|k\<leqslant\>N> and every translated bond
    tiling<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>> of
    side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>> and every base-cell anchor
    satisfying<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-margin> obey

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)><text| is interior>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|N,p><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  Here <math|n<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the number of insertions in a later product
  test; the lower level<nbsp><math|m> requires the chosen cell scale to be
  arbitrarily fine. The tolerance family is chosen
  before<nbsp><math|n<rsub|\<ast\>>,m,k>. All residuals below are translation
  covariant, and their signed and conjugated images obey the same estimates.
  To pass from centered to arbitrary interior roots, choose
  <math|k<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>k> with <math|24*<around|(|n<rsub|\<ast\>>+1|)>*a<rsub|k<rprime|'>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>>
  and apply the finite reflection argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>.
  It uses only fixed stencils and the chessboard inequality, so also applies
  to these residuals on full cutoff tails.

  For a local observable <math|F<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>>, let
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*F<rsub|N,p>> be its dimensionless representative.
  We require it to be continuous on its fixed stencil, regular on healthy
  configurations in the sense of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, with
  derivative and supremum bounds of subpower growth. The supremum bound is on
  all configurations. These two bounds have different uses: Taylor extraction
  uses the first, and the forced complement uses the second.

  Local matching makes no assumption on the size of the physical coefficient
  it produces. It bounds its inverse terminal coordinate, which is the
  quantity in<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-subpower>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local comparison><label|lem:E-local-comparison> For every such
    degree-<math|e> family <math|F>, there are admissible coefficients
    <math|b<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|e>> with
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|e|)>><around|(|b|)>-F> small. For the auxiliary
    section of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4>, there are invertible
    coefficient changes <math|P<rsub|N,p>>, with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>
    eventually uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>, and maps

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|d,N,p>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>P<rsub|N,p><factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N>><math-ss|R><rsub|d>
    </equation*>

    from polynomial labels to <math|V<rsub|d>>, such that
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\>|)>-a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>\<pi\>|]>>
    is small for equation labels with admissible <math|B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\>>.
  </lemma>

  In particular, for <math|\<pi\><rsub|N,p>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> with
  admissible <math|B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\><rsub|N,p>>, the comparison is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-equation-comparison>O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\><rsub|N,p>,y|)>-a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>\<pi\><rsub|N,p>|]><around|(|y|)><space|1em><text|is
    small>.
  </equation>

  We prove both assertions using the same backward coefficient correction.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, <reference|def:D07>,
    <reference|def:D08>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>,
    <reference|lem:E-replication> and the local sections of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>. Write <math|h<rsub|N>=a<rsub|N><rsup|e>*F<rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>>.
    Enlarge its stencil by the section stencil. The radial Taylor readout
    through degree<nbsp><math|e> gives a coefficient <math|v<rsub|N>> and a
    remainder. The readout is bounded by a fixed multiple of the derivative
    bound: it is a linear map on a finite jet space. Subtract the section
    with that coefficient on the healthy part. Its regular remainder has zero
    value and zero radial jet through degree<nbsp><math|e>. Assign the
    complementary observable to the forced supports meeting the enlarged
    stencil. This gives an exact one-mark representation of
    <math|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>*h<rsub|N>>, with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-local-start><around|\||v<rsub|N>|\|><rsub|1>+r<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|N,p>,<space|2em><text|forced
      majorant>\<leqslant\>E<rprime|'><rsub|N,p>,
    </equation>

    where <math|E,E<rprime|'>> have subpower growth. The support weight of
    this fixed enlarged stencil is bounded by
    <math|e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>> eventually; its
    <math|B<rsub|N>> covering number is at most<nbsp><math|81>. Threshold
    powers, the readout bound and this support weight are included in
    <math|E,E<rprime|'>>. The entry conditions make each of them subpower. No
    division by the forced allowance is made on the uncontrolled complement:
    it is retained with its original eligibility tests and the supremum bound
    <math|E<rprime|'>>.

    To compare with the selected graph, carry this representation backwards
    and at the reference level choose the primary coefficient with the same
    polynomial coordinate. Subtract the corresponding selected inserted
    density. Its polynomial coordinate at the reference level is zero. The
    following calculation carries this matching condition towards the fine
    scale.

    For one step, let <math|u,v> be the coarse and fine coordinates,
    <math|R,T> their remainder bounds, <math|Y=<around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>v|\|><rsub|1>>,
    <math|K> the norm bound for the leading map and its inverse,
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|-3/8>> and
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|1/8>>. The marked-step inequalities
    give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-match-cone>Y\<leqslant\>K*<around*|(|<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*<around|(|Y+T|)>|)>,<space|2em>R\<leqslant\>K*Y+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*T.
    </equation>

    Suppose <math|a<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>R>, with
    <math|a=32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>4*K<rsup|2>>.
    Then <math|4*K<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>Y+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*T>.
    Substitution in the first inequality, using
    <math|4*K*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1>, gives
    <math|Y\<leqslant\>4*K*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*T>.
    Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-match> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> gives

    <\equation*>
      4*K*<space|0.17em>32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>|)><rsup|2>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*\<vartheta\><rsub|L>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>|)><rsup|2><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>T>
    and <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|L>*R\<leqslant\>T>. The matching condition
    starts this induction with <math|u=0> at the reference level. Multiplying
    the second inequality through the interval gives the inverse-scale gain,
    while the finest-level bound is closed by absorbing the coefficient
    contribution into its remainder bound. In particular, with subpower
    <math|E<rprime|''>> absorbing <math|E,E<rprime|'>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-backward-match><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|N>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|N>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|N,p>|\<ell\><rsub|N-1><rsup|1/8>>,<space|2em>R<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|N>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|N,p>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|N>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N>> is the difference from the selected fine
    polynomial coordinate. The same bounds hold when a local source is first
    represented at any level <math|n\<leqslant\>N>, with its polynomial
    coordinate matched to the selected graph at a lower reference level. If
    <math|E<rprime|''><rsub|n;N,p>> bounds its starting data after the
    coefficient contribution is absorbed, then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-backward-match-level><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|n>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|n>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|n;N,p>|\<ell\><rsub|n-1><rsup|1/8>>,<space|2em>R<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|n>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|n;N,p>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|n>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8>*<space|1em><around|(|r<around|(|e|)>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>n|)>.
    </equation>

    Indeed the cone starts at the same zero matched coordinate, and its
    induction uses only the steps through<nbsp><math|n>. The boundary
    absorption uses the starting bound there. The product
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=k><rsup|n-1>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-1/8>> is
    exactly <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|n>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8>>. Taking
    <math|n=N> recovers<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match>. Thus this factor
    comes from the matched remainder inequalities, rather than from the
    physical diameter of the original stencil.

    To estimate the replicated residual, for a source of size <math|E>
    started at <math|N>, use at level<nbsp><math|k> the radius

    <\equation*>
      w=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|e,k>/4|a<rsub|k><rsup|-e>*E*exp
      <around|{|-<around|(|s<rsub|N>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8|}>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<zeta\><rsub|e,k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsub|e>+4|)>>>
    is the source allowance fixed before<nbsp><math|N,p>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance>. Its reciprocal is a fixed
    power of the reference coupling. At every finer step multiply this radius
    by the source contraction factor of that step. Its product with the
    dimensionless singleton size is at most one quarter of the available
    source allowance; the other three quarters bound the graph remainder, the
    forced complement and their sum. This follows inductively from the two
    inequalities in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates>. For the first step the
    forced complement is bounded by <math|E<rprime|'>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-local-start>; the large-field suppression absorbs its
    fixed support and derivative factors before division by the new forced
    normalization. The radius transport<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport>
    and the site-sum calculation in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments> verify the attachment
    bound at every finer level. Thus every step is admitted. The singleton
    polynomial coordinate at the reference level is zero, so the
    representation constructed this way is exactly the difference with the
    selected primary coefficient, rather than a new approximation to it.

    Here <math|m> labels the tiling and <math|k> the finer stopping level at
    which its replicated sources are integrated. Replicate the difference at
    an admissible pair <math|m\<leqslant\>k>,
    <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>, and use the
    weighted source representation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>. Coefficient
    extraction contributes <math|w<rsup|-1>> per mark; the complete singleton
    bounds and the normalized unmarked ratio<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-ratio>
    contribute a constant depending on <math|m,k>. Consequently, for every
    fixed such pair,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-local-error><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsup|<around|(|e|)>><around|(|b|)>-F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|0|)>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m,k>*\<frak-e\><rsub|N,p>*a<rsub|N><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,e><rsup|-1>b<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>2*E<rprime|'''><rsub|N,p>,
    </equation>

    where <math|\<frak-e\>,E<rprime|'''>> have subpower growth. The factor
    <math|e<rsup|s<rsub|k>/8>> and the fixed radius
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|e,k><rsup|-1>> belong to <math|C<rsub|m,k>>. The same
    argument applies after each signed permutation and conjugation, with
    translation covariance and the union of their fixed stencils. Absorbing
    two factors <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1/32>> bounds the first expression by
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|1/16>> on every fixed pair. The interior-root
    comparison after Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small> gives the
    common tolerance <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|1/32>>, chosen before all cell
    requests. This proves smallness and admissibility on full cutoff tails.

    For the auxiliary section, perform the construction on all its basis
    polynomials together, with the original Taylor normalization. Let
    <math|u<rsub|j>> be the primary bare coefficient matching its <math|j>th
    basis vector, and set <math|P*x=<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j>*u<rsub|j>>,
    after the finite symmetry averaging. For this fixed section the regular
    derivative bound <math|H<rsub|j,d>> at stage<nbsp><math|j> is fixed
    before its ratio, independently of the later coupling. The sharper
    coefficient estimate in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match> therefore has
    numerator bounded by <math|2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j>|)>*H<rsub|j,d>> on
    each basis input. The ratio condition, including the finite
    basis-recombination factor, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||x-P*x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, before summing over the fixed basis the right side is the
    last-step factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|N-1><rsup|-1/8>> times the stage
    coefficient bound; the stage ratio was chosen after that bound. The
    matrix is therefore invertible and <math|<around|\||P<rsup|-1>*x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>>.
    The construction is common to all later cell requests. Multiplication by
    <math|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N>><math-ss|R><rsub|d>> changes a
    polynomial label into its auxiliary bare insertion coordinate;
    multiplication by <math|<math-ss|Z>> changes that coordinate to the
    reference normalization. These are exactly the maps <math|B> in the
    statement. The inverse bound preserves admissibility,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-local-error> compares the two sections on its
    transported equation ideal. Finite combinations use the same subpower
    factors. The argument only replaces eventual bounds on coefficients, so
    ordered-admissible families obey the corresponding ordered version as
    well.
  </proof>

  The next positional estimate is used before bounds on differentiated fields
  have been established. For a grid configuration<nbsp><math|x> define its
  minimum coordinate separation on the physical torus by

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|p><around|(|x|)>=min<rsub|i\<ne\>j> min<rsub|a=1,\<ldots\>,4>
    min<rsub|z\<in\>L<rsub|p><Z>><around|\||x<rsub|i><rsup|a>-x<rsub|j><rsup|a>+z|\|>,
  </equation*>

  with value <math|+\<infty\>> for one slot. This separation supplies the
  empty slabs for reflection positivity. A coordinate hybrid of <math|x,y>
  chooses each coordinate from either configuration. Assume the insertions
  and their single-axis reflections are interior at a centered
  level<nbsp><math|D\<leqslant\>N>, with all their roots at
  most<nbsp><math|A\<geqslant\>0>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Displacement of correlation tests><label|lem:E-displacement> For
    every <math|r\<gtr\>0> with <math|a<rsub|D>\<less\>r>, a one-coordinate
    grid move <math|x\<mapsto\>y> of size at most<nbsp><math|r> from
    <math|g<rsub|p><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>4*r> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y/a<rsub|N>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsup|n>*<around|\||x-y|\|>/r.
    </equation*>

    If <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<leqslant\>r> and every coordinate hybrid has
    coordinate separation at least<nbsp><math|4*r>, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y/a<rsub|N>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*n*A<rsup|n>*<around|\||x-y|\|>/r.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27> and the reflection
    inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>
    supplies the assumed roots for bounded coefficient lists on full cutoff
    tails. The separation hypothesis gives every directed coordinate distance
    at least<nbsp><math|4*r>, at any period. No fixed row neighbourhood is
    used.

    Fix an axis and all other coordinates. Reflection positivity and
    translation invariance give a positive transfer operator, rescaled to
    <math|0\<leqslant\>T\<leqslant\>1>. On the periodic axis the expectation
    is a normalized trace. If <math|X> denotes the insertion being moved, its
    two adjacent empty slabs have lengths
    <math|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>,n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>> in fine
    sites. A move by <math|l\<geqslant\>0> changes their factor by

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>+l>*X*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l>-T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*X*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>>|<cell|=T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*<around|(|T<rsup|l>*X-X*T<rsup|l>|)>*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l><label|eq:E-trace-displacement>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=-T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*<around|(|1-T<rsup|l>|)>*X*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l>+T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*X*<around|(|1-T<rsup|l>|)>*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l>.<eq-number>>>>>
    </align>

    Both expressions are inserted in the same remaining cyclic product and
    divided by the same partition trace. The stencil is contained in a
    centered cell of lattice side <math|d<rsub|<math-up|cell>>=a<rsub|D>/a<rsub|N>>.
    Since <math|a<rsub|D>\<less\>r>, the directed separations exceed
    <math|2*d<rsub|<math-up|cell>>+2*r/a<rsub|N>>, and
    <math|l*a<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>r> by assumption. Splitting the two
    surrounding empty slabs at their reflection planes leaves
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>+1\<geqslant\>2*r/a<rsub|N>> fine sites next to
    each occurrence of <math|1-T<rsup|l>>. The spectral theorem gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-T<rsup|l>|)>*T<rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>
      l*<around|(|1-t|)>*t<rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>>\<leqslant\>l/<around|(|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>+1|)>\<leqslant\>l*a<rsub|N>/<around|(|2*r|)>.
    </equation*>

    For each normalized trace in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-trace-displacement>,
    reflection Schwarz at the slab ends and the chessboard inequality bound
    the remaining factors by the product of their centered roots, at
    most<nbsp><math|A<rsup|n>>. One applies this inequality to the complete
    cyclic product, so no supremum norm of<nbsp><math|X> is required. The two
    terms together are bounded by <math|A<rsup|n>*l*a<rsub|N>/r>. An
    arity-one moment is translation invariant and has change zero.

    For a negative move reverse the chosen axis. Coordinate separation is
    unchanged and the assumed reflected roots have the same bound, so the
    same slab estimate applies. Replace the <math|4*n> coordinates one at a
    time. Each starting configuration is a coordinate hybrid and hence has
    the required separation. Each move is at most
    <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<leqslant\>r>. Summing proves the second bound.
    The constants depend only on the roots, arity and slab margin, uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|N,p>.
  </proof>

  The equation projections separate the coefficient directions controlled by
  exact cutoff identities. The free equation space consists of polynomial
  labels that vanish on curvature jets satisfying the Maxwell equations. Its
  transported image is the kernel of the projections chosen below; the free
  quotient has dimension<nbsp><math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>. The
  projections and the equation observables in
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.7> are chosen together,
  before the spacing and volume subsequences.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Equation projections><label|lem:E2.6> For every<nbsp><math|d>,
    there are complex linear idempotents <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>>
    and <math|C<rsub|d>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>,<space|2em>dim
      V<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>dim ker \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>+dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    More precisely, <math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>=B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>,
    with the maps <math|B> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the labels of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>, the free
    comparison of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>. Let
    <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>\<subset\>V<rsub|d>> be the polynomials belonging
    to the ideal generated by the linear Maxwell equations in radial jet
    coordinates. Radial coordinates already satisfy the linear Bianchi
    identities. Taking invariant polynomials commutes with the on-shell
    quotient: average any representative over the compact adjoint group to
    obtain an invariant representative. Thus the on-shell map is onto
    <math|<maxwellspace|d>> and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-equation-count>dim V<rsub|d>-dim
      \<cal-I\><rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>.
    </equation>

    This dimension calculation uses invariant averaging and the definition of
    <math|<maxwellspace|d>>; it does not use positivity.

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison> constructs invertible
    coefficient maps <math|B<rsub|d,N,p>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>>. They
    transport <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> to the coefficient equations for
    auxiliary local representatives, including their change to the selected
    insertion coordinates. Set <math|U<rsub|d,N,p>=B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>.
    In the Euclidean norm on the fixed basis take the orthogonal projection
    <math|Q<rsub|d,N,p>> onto <math|U<rsub|d,N,p>> and set
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>=1-Q<rsub|d,N,p>>. Then
    <math|\<Pi\><rsup|2>=\<Pi\>> and <math|ker \<Pi\>=U<rsub|d,N,p>>.
    Equivalence with the fixed coefficient norm bounds
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>|\<\|\|\>>> independently of <math|N,p>,
    even when <math|B> is poorly conditioned.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-count> gives the claimed dimension
    inequality, in fact equality.

    The same local comparison maps will be used in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7>. They and these projections are chosen
    for all cutoffs before any subsequences. This proves the projection and
    rank assertions for the maps used in the equation comparison.
  </proof>

  The next estimate converts a coefficient equation into an observable with
  exact separated moment identities.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Equation observables><label|lem:E2.7> For these same projections,
    every admissible family<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p>\<in\>ker
    \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>> admits a measurable observable
    family<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>>, bounded at each
    anchor and cutoff on a full cutoff tail, for which
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c<rsub|N,p>,y|)>-\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>>
    is small. For every fixed spectator list
    and<nbsp><math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, on a full cutoff tail,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h\<ne\>i>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|h>,y<rsub|h>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>=0*<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D07>\U<reference|def:D08>, the
    coefficient maps of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison> and the
    kernel identity in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>, and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4> supplies
    <math|<wide|P|~><rsub|d>=V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>>> and
    <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>>. Write <math|c<rsub|N,p>=B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\><rsub|N,p>>
    with <math|\<pi\><rsub|N,p>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, and set

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-equation-section>\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>\<pi\><rsub|N,p>|]><around|(|y|)>.
    </equation>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>,
    specifically<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-comparison>, proves that
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c|)>-\<cal-Q\>> is small. Its
    inverse change has subpower growth for admissible<nbsp><math|c>. The
    common smallness tolerance and interior-root comparison were proved there
    and apply to this family.

    The exact zero identity is<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-aux-zero> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4>: every auxiliary equation term is a
    Wilson action derivative whose multiplier and transport avoid the varied
    link, so Haar invariance makes its exterior moment zero. The auxiliary
    section has one finite equation radius. At prescribed positive physical
    separation, every fixed spectator stencil is exterior to it for all
    sufficiently fine cutoffs, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>. Its product is
    bounded and measurable, as required by that lemma. Apply the identity
    with that product as spectator. Finally <math|<wide|P|~><rsub|d>> is
    continuous on a finite product of compact groups. At each fixed cutoff
    its evaluation with the chosen finite coefficients is bounded. No bound
    uniform in those cutoffs is used. This proves the lemma
    from<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-section> and its local comparison
    estimate.
  </proof>

  Changing the degree changes the insertion normalization. The next estimate
  corrects that change before taking limits.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected inclusion><label|lem:E2.8> For every
    <math|d\<leqslant\>e> and <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, there is an admissible
    family<nbsp><math|b<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|e>> such that

    <\equation*>
      O<rsup|<around|(|e|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|b<rsub|N,p>,y|)>-O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)><space|1em><text|is
      small><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>\U<reference|lem:E2.2a>, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>. After multiplication by
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e>>, the target observable is

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|N><rsup|e-d>*P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N><rsup|-1>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]><around|(|y|)>.
    </equation*>

    It is a local continuous function on the fixed degree-<math|d> stencil.
    Its ordered derivatives in the allowed link directions and its supremum
    have subpower bounds. Indeed, the inverse terminal coordinate has this
    growth by the first inequality of the second display in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>; threshold
    powers have subpower growth because <math|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>=o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>>. The factor
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|e-d>\<leqslant\>1> can only decrease these bounds.

    The local matching estimate extracts a degree-<math|e> coefficient
    <math|b<rsub|N,p>> whose inverse terminal coordinate still has subpower
    growth. Its residual root is bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|m,k>*\<frak-e\><rsub|N,p>*a<rsub|N><rsup|1/8>> at every
    fixed admissible pair of levels, with <math|\<frak-e\>> of subpower
    growth. The absorption and interior-root argument in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison> gives one tolerance
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|1/32>> for all the later cell requests. The residual
    is therefore small. For <math|d=e> one may simply take
    <math|b<rsub|N,p>=c>, with zero residual. This proves the assertion,
    including that case.
  </proof>

  The physical difference quotient requires a correction in the next degree.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected difference><label|lem:E2.9> For every
    degree<nbsp><math|d>, <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and axis<nbsp><math|a>,
    there is an admissible family<nbsp><math|b<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|d+1>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      O<rsup|<around|(|d+1|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|b<rsub|N,p>,y|)>-<frac|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y+e<rsub|a>|)>-O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)>|a<rsub|N>><space|1em><text|is
      small><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>. Multiplying the target
    difference by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d+1>> gives exactly

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N><rsup|-1>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]>*<around|(|y+e<rsub|a>|)>-P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|N><rsup|-1>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]><around|(|y|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its stencil is the union of one fixed stencil and its unit translate. It
    is continuous and local. Moving an ordered derivative anchor by one
    lattice site multiplies its spatial weight by at most a stage-dependent
    factor. The factor is at most <math|2<rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>>
    per derivative. The logarithmic condition
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> makes each fixed product of these factors
    subpower. The other bounds are those in the preceding proof. Local
    matching in degree <math|d+1> now supplies an admissible coefficient
    family and the same small residual. The inverse spacing has been removed
    before applying matching, so it causes no further loss in that residual.
    The four axes use translates of this one computation and their fixed
    spatial-weight bounds. This proves the corrected difference.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Short-distance comparison.> Fix<nbsp><math|d> and a real basis
  <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|1>=1,\<frak-m\><rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,\<frak-m\><rsub|q<rsub|d>>>
  of<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>, where <math|q<rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>.
  Choose finitely many one- or two-point tests. In row<nbsp><math|i>, the
  first label is the variable<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>>; the optional
  second label is a fixed basis element. Let
  <math|y<rsup|i>\<in\><Gen|n<rsub|i>>>, <math|n<rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|1,2|}>>,
  be the row's configuration. The free test matrix is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E2-free-matrix>\<cal-G\><rsub|i*j>=<maxwellcorr><rsub|n<rsub|i>><around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>,<text|
    row >i<text| spectator>;y<rsup|i>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The next lemma supplies a finite family that distinguishes every free field
  of degree at most<nbsp><math|d>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free test rows><label|lem:E2-free-tests> For every<nbsp><math|d>
    and real basis<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\><rsub|1>=1,\<frak-m\><rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,\<frak-m\><rsub|q<rsub|d>>>,
    there are finitely many one- or two-point rows at generic configurations,
    with every second label a basis element, for which the
    matrix<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-free-matrix> has rank<nbsp><math|q<rsub|d>>.
  </lemma>

  The proof uses only the Maxwell correlations of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> and the free positivity in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, independently of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>.

  <\proof>
    Let<nbsp><math|c\<ne\>0> in<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> gives a strictly positive reflected
    two-point value for<nbsp><math|c> and the reflection
    of<nbsp><math|<wide|c|\<bar\>>>. Expand that second label in the
    basis<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>|)>>. At least one of the
    resulting two-point functionals is nonzero on<nbsp><math|c>. Each free
    Wick correlation is a finite sum of products of derivatives of
    <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    between distinct sites, hence is smooth there. This permits a
    perturbation of the pair to<nbsp><math|<Gen|2>> preserving this nonzero
    value. Thus the common kernel of all permitted row functionals is zero.
    Starting with<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>, choose a row nonzero on a
    nonzero vector in the current common kernel. Each choice strictly lowers
    its dimension. After finitely many choices the common kernel is zero,
    so<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\>> has rank<nbsp><math|q<rsub|d>>.
  </proof>

  Fix such a family for the rank comparison below.

  For a trial<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>, a common coefficient
  list<nbsp><math|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|j,N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and
  lattice-compatible configuration <math|x<rsup|\<nu\>,i>=r<rsub|\<nu\>>*y<rsup|\<nu\>,i>>
  define the row insertions <math|F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|row>>=O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|j<rsub|h>,N,p>,x<rsub|h><rsup|\<nu\>,i>/a<rsub|N>|)>>,
  with<nbsp><math|j<rsub|1>=j> and the spectator index fixed by
  row<nbsp><math|i>. Physical points are read modulo the lattice period.
  Let<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsub|J><rsup|\<nu\>>> be the Maxwell moment of the
  labels<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j<rsub|h>>|)><rsub|h\<in\>J>>
  at<nbsp><math|<around|(|y<rsub|h><rsup|\<nu\>,i>|)><rsub|h\<in\>J>>, with
  <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<varnothing\>><rsup|\<nu\>>=1>. Define the relative
  errors by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-relative>R<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1,<space|2em>R<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|h\<in\>I>F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|row>>-<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>J\<subseteq\>I>\<cal-G\><rsub|J><rsup|\<nu\>>*R<rsub|I\<setminus\>J><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Only singleton and pair errors occur. The lifts<nbsp><math|b<rsup|\<nu\>>>
  include the scale normalization, so<nbsp><math|F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|row>>>
  carries no additional power of<nbsp><math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>>. A lift is the
  reference coefficient whose Wick-ordered leading polynomial at
  scale<nbsp><math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>> is the specified basis
  element<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>>. The construction uses
  <math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>=a<rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>>|)><rsup|b>>
  for one fixed <math|b\<gtr\>0> chosen from the degree and the finite tests,
  before<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> varies.

  The source estimates below also apply to rows with any fixed arity
  bound<nbsp><math|n\<geqslant\>2>. For a slot set <math|I<rsub|0>> with
  <math|<around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>n>, define

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|G><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|J\<subseteq\>I<rsub|0>>\<cal-G\><rsub|J>*z<rsub|J>,<space|2em><math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>=log
    <math-ss|G><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>=0*<space|1em><around|(|i\<in\>I<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The logarithm is a finite polynomial because the constant term of
  <math|<math-ss|G><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> is one and its nonconstant part is
  nilpotent. Its coefficient on a nonempty<nbsp><math|I> is

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|z<rsub|I>|]><math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-P\><text|
    partition of >I><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||\<cal-P\>|\|>-1>*<around|(|<around|\||\<cal-P\>|\|>-1|)>!<big|prod><rsub|J\<in\>\<cal-P\>>\<cal-G\><rsub|J>.
  </equation*>

  Thus for two slots it is the two singleton means and the connected pair
  moment; the rank comparison uses just this case. In the source estimates,
  the row positions may range over any fixed compact set
  <math|K\<subset\><Conf|<around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>>> with
  <math|<sepinf>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. Constants may depend on
  <math|K,\<delta\>,n> and the finite degree and derivative orders; the lifts
  are common to these positions. The coefficients to be estimated are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-corrected-coefficients>\<frak-c\><rsub|I>=<around|[|z<rsub|I>|]>*<around*|(|e<rsup|-<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|h><around|(|1+z<rsub|h>*F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|row>>|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  For the source comparison, use the intervals<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>. Fix
  <math|b\<gtr\>0> large enough for the degree and the finite tests, put
  <math|l=t-h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>>, <math|k=l-v<rsub|t>> and
  <math|r=a<rsub|t><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|b>>. The interval
  from <math|t> to<nbsp><math|l> separates the reading points from the last
  fluctuation scale; the interval from <math|l> to<nbsp><math|k> permits the
  corrected sources to increase in size while their remainders decrease. With
  <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|J<rsub|t>><rsup|<math-up|st>>>, these intervals
  satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-window-geometry>L<rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>>\<geqslant\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|b+2>,<space|2em>L<rsup|v<rsub|t>>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|t><rsup|10>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<in\><around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|]>*<space|1em><around|(|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t|)>
  </equation>

  on sufficiently late intervals. The last assertion follows from
  <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>=o<around|(|t|)>> at fixed stage and
  the entry choices. All rounded row points lie strictly inside the
  level-<math|k> cell: their distance from the origin is
  <math|O<around|(|r|)>>, while <math|a<rsub|l>/r\<geqslant\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>.
  Rounding changes their rescaled coordinates by
  <math|O<around|(|<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-b>|)>>.

  Fix the arity bound <math|n\<geqslant\>2> and the compact separated
  position set specified above. Put <math|\<cal-M\>=<cutoffsize><rsub|t>>.
  Let <math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>> bound the required derivative orders, and
  let <math|c,\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>\<gtr\>0> be the localization and
  healthy-chart exponents in the estimates below. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-source-initial>\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>=\<cal-M\><rsup|-b>+C<rsub|j>*\<cal-M\><rsup|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>*<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t><rsup|-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>>+e<rsup|-c*B<rsub|t>>|)>.
  </equation>

  The polynomial comparison supplies the initial small error for the
  subsequent source iteration.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Middle-level source comparison><label|lem:E-source-polynomial> For
    each fixed finite label family, sufficiently late intervals admit common
    real lifts, fixing the unit and bounded in <math|N,p> for that interval.
    Uniformly over the prescribed compact position set, all corrected
    nonempty modes at<nbsp><math|l> have total regular and normalized forced
    bound

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>.
    </equation*>

    The assertion holds on a full cutoff tail, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>.
  </lemma>

  The proof gives the Wick correction and the inverse-separation gain.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D07>,
    <reference|def:D11>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>,
    <reference|lem:D-connected>, <reference|lem:E-marked-step>,
    <reference|lem:E-replication> and the covariance estimates of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>. The
    sources have at most <math|n> marks in each cell; the number of cells is
    unrestricted. Use the coefficientwise rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> in their weighted nilpotent
    algebra. Only point separation is needed for the covariance comparison;
    coincident coordinates of distinct points are allowed.

    On a fixed healthy chart, subtract the radial Taylor polynomial of each
    local insertion. The coefficient norm of the remainder is bounded by its
    next ordered derivative times the corresponding power of the coordinate
    radius. All derivatives needed here are among the finitely many stage
    orders. Express each polynomial in curvature and differentiated-curvature
    variables, carrying along their powers of<nbsp><math|r>. For a
    polynomial<nbsp><math|\<pi\>>, the finite Gaussian contraction operator
    is

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|H><rsub|C>\<pi\>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<geqslant\>0><frac|1|2<rsup|j>*j!><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|C>|)><rsup|j>*\<pi\>,<space|2em><math-ss|H><rsub|C><rsup|-1>\<pi\>=<math-ss|H><rsub|-C>\<pi\>.
    </equation*>

    The sums are finite on the chosen polynomial space, since a contraction
    removes two factors. Use the inverse operator for contractions internal
    to a single insertion. This is the Wick-ordering correction; contractions
    between distinct insertions remain. The leading coefficient change
    <math|T<rsub|m>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> and this
    finite contraction operator, in the jet normalization of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>, are invertible and preserve the unit
    and real form. The error in replacing the selected coefficient map by
    this leading map has norm less than <math|1/4> after its inverse, by the
    marked estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> and the entry condition.
    Solving this finite linear system gives one lift for each free basis
    polynomial, common to every row. Transport only between the fixed levels
    <math|r<around|(|d|)>> and<nbsp><math|t> bounds these reference
    coefficients independently of<nbsp><math|N,p> for that interval. All
    matrices are real on the real form, and the unit equation has the exact
    solution<nbsp><math|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>>.

    The covariance calculation involves finitely many derivatives of
    <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    For each pair of coloured plaquette stencils the rescaled Fourier chart
    <math|K<around|(|\<xi\>|)>> is supported in
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>. Its leading symbol is the
    Maxwell curvature symbol <math|H<around|(|\<xi\>|)>>. For every fixed
    required order<nbsp><math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>, the quadratic-form
    calculation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, differentiated in
    frequency, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<partial\><rsup|j>*K<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|-j>,<space|2em><around|\||\<partial\><rsup|j>*<around|(|K-H|)><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|1-j><space|1em><around|(|0\<less\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1,j\<leqslant\>q<rsub|<math-up|der>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    These bounds follow by differentiating the inverse quadratic symbol: its
    curl factors remove the inverse-square singularity; replacing each
    lattice difference by its linear term leaves one extra power
    of<nbsp><math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>>. The regulator and its derivatives
    obey the same fixed bounds; different colours have zero cross covariance.
    To convert the second estimate to position space, decompose a cutoff of
    <math|K-H> into annuli <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<asymp\>2<rsup|-v>>. A
    derivative of order<nbsp><math|s> in position inserts <math|s> powers
    of<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. After <math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>\<gtr\>5+s>
    integrations by parts on each annulus its transform at<nbsp><math|R*x>,
    with <math|x> in a compact separated set, is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C*2<rsup|-v*<around|(|5+s|)>>*min <around|{|1,<around|(|R*2<rsup|-v>|)><rsup|-q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Splitting the sum at <math|2<rsup|v>\<asymp\>R> gives
    <math|C*R<rsup|-5-s>>. The smooth part away from zero decreases faster
    than this. The leading homogeneous symbol is the corresponding curvature
    derivative of<nbsp><math|\<Gamma\>> away from the origin, with the
    Fourier normalization of Part<nbsp>I. Including the curvature scaling
    powers therefore gives relative error <math|O<around|(|R<rsup|-1>|)>>.

    For the finite jet samples, terms of the matching homogeneity give this
    leading derivative exactly. Every other term has at least one extra
    inverse power of<nbsp><math|R>; summing their finitely many absolute
    coefficients preserves the <math|O<around|(|R<rsup|-1>|)>> bound. Here
    <math|R=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|b>>, and rounding in the
    rescaled variables gives an additional <math|O<around|(|R<rsup|-1>|)>>.
    Localizing the sector covariance introduces
    <math|C<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>*e<rsup|-c*B<rsub|t>>>
    by the inverse-covariance bound in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>,
    with smaller <math|c>, since <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2>
    throughout the interval. Its nonlinear healthy-chart error is
    <math|C<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t><rsup|-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>>>
    for some fixed <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>\<gtr\>0>. The derivative
    orders and powers<nbsp><math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>> are fixed by the
    degree and the finite row family; their stage constants are fixed before
    entry. Since <math|log <cutoffsize><rsub|t>=o<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)>> and <math|B<rsub|t>\<asymp\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>, both errors are smaller than any
    prescribed fixed negative power of<nbsp><math|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>> after
    entry. Choose <math|b> so that the inverse-separation error is smaller
    than the finite power required by the source radius below. The chart and
    inspected supports lie inside the nonwrapping comparison interval
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry>, so these local estimates hold on
    every period, with the same constants.

    The finite-polynomial estimate combining these errors is as follows. Let
    <math|U> bound the Gaussian comparison polynomials, <math|T> the
    one-factor error, and <math|Z> the two-factor error in their coefficient
    norm. For the connected two-point term, subtract the product of the
    one-point terms. Expanding that product gives the bound

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-Gaussian-polynomial>T+Z+<around|(|U+T|)>*T+T*U.
    </equation>

    For example, if <math|a<rsub|i>> and <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|0>> are the two
    one-point terms, then <math|a<rsub|1>*a<rsub|2>-a<rsub|1><rsup|0>*a<rsub|2><rsup|0>=<around|(|a<rsub|1>-a<rsub|1><rsup|0>|)>*a<rsub|2>+a<rsub|1><rsup|0>*<around|(|a<rsub|2>-a<rsub|2><rsup|0>|)>>,
    whose norm is at most <math|<around|(|U+T|)>*T+U*T>. The terms <math|T,Z>
    are the errors in the remaining one- and two-mark polynomial terms.
    Gaussian contraction expands into pairings, so the bound for any fixed
    polynomial is a finite polynomial in the bounds for its contractions.
    After <math|b> has been fixed sufficiently large, the preceding
    covariance and Taylor estimates make<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-polynomial>
    smaller than every source bound required at<nbsp><math|l>.

    For more slots, let <math|\<cal-G\><rprime|'><rsub|J>> be the Gaussian
    polynomial moment before replacing its covariance by the Maxwell
    covariance. On the compact separated position set, all these moments are
    bounded by a fixed<nbsp><math|U\<geqslant\>1>, and their discrepancies
    are at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>, a constant times the covariance and
    Taylor error above. For a partition with <math|q> blocks, telescoping its
    product bounds the discrepancy by <math|q*\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|U+\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|q-1>>.
    The partition formula for <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
    therefore bounds the sum of all nonempty corrected coefficients by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|n>*\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|1+U+\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|n-1>.
    </equation*>

    There are finitely many subsets and partitions for fixed<nbsp><math|n>.
    Their constants are included in<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>>.
    Subtracting <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> cancels every leading
    free coefficient in the logarithm; for a pair this is precisely the
    mean-product subtraction in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-polynomial>. A
    support carries a mode only when it contains all its anchors. The
    support-independent scalar correction is retained separately per cell.
    Thus no local term is assigned to a support lacking one of its marks.

    For terms meeting a flagged region, keep that region's complete source
    support and its original eligibility test. At a step <math|i+1\<to\>i> in
    stage<nbsp><math|j>, let <math|v<rsub|0>> be the integrated majorant of
    the distinguished marked factor and <math|n<rsub|0>> its covering number,
    as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Its rooted forced bound is
    <math|v<rsub|0>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>>>
    before division by the forced source allowance. Half this suppression
    pays the derivative and support factors of the finite marks; the other
    half bounds their normalized forced remainder by the error above after
    entry. The finite polynomial contractions, Taylor subtractions and
    connected attachments cost at most <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>> at each of
    the <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>> steps. Their product is the factor in
    the statement. At each step the marker coefficient norm is
    submultiplicative; the derivative and support weights remain in the
    separate integrated majorants. The rooted sums are anchored at marks, so
    their bounds have no factor counting the replicated cells.
  </proof>

  The next estimate increases the source radius while retaining the full
  regular and forced representation.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected Gaussian sources><label|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
    Uniformly on that set, arbitrarily late intervals admit common real lifts
    of the free basis, bounded in <math|N,p> for each fixed interval and
    fixing the unit, such that the flowed corrected source
    <math|e<rsup|-<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|h><around|(|1+z<rsub|h>*F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|row>>|)>>
    has a representation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> at<nbsp><math|k> with
    radius

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\><rsub|t>=exp <around|(|log
      <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|8*n|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    At this radius its regular and forced source sums are each at most one,
    with scalar source bound at most one per cell. For each such interval
    there is <math|N<rsub|0>> such that the assertion holds for all
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all<nbsp><math|p>.
  </lemma>

  Its proof iterates the middle-level error over the second part of the
  interval.

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D05a>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, <reference|lem:E-marked-step>,
    <reference|lem:E-replication> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>.
    Work in the submultiplicative marker coefficient norm defined in the
    proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments>. Keep derivative
    and support weights in separate integrated majorants and retain the
    forced eligibility tests.

    We now bound the loss over all steps; their number grows
    with<nbsp><math|t>. Use<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-initial>. Increasing the
    fixed power <math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>> includes the finite Taylor and
    rounding bounds above. The source radius contributes at most
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|t><rsup|n>=\<cal-M\><rsup|1/8>> to any mode in one
    cell. Enlarge <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>> to include all per-step finite
    polynomial and support constants. From<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost> and the
    two ceilings in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>, on sufficiently late intervals
    with fixed<nbsp><math|b>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-source-loss>\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>+2>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|<around|(|b+6|)>/j<rsup|2>>*B<rsub|t><rsup|10/j<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|1/64>.
    </equation>

    Indeed <math|log B<rsub|t>=2*log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>+o<around|(|1|)>>
    and <math|log \<cal-M\>=<around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>, so
    <math|B<rsub|t>=\<cal-M\><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. The two ceilings and
    the <math|+2> contribute at most <math|L<rsup|4/j<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|4/j<rsup|2>>>,
    since <math|\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\><cutoffsize><rsub|t>>
    by row<nbsp>6 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>; the final
    inequality holds at all sufficiently large stages for each
    fixed<nbsp><math|b>.

    We use the following one-step source estimate. For the fixed lifts of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>, let <math|S<rsub|i>>
    majorize the corrected singleton terms, and <math|P<rsub|i>> the
    corrected terms with at least two marks, in the weighted regular norm at
    radius<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|t>>, including their scalar terms per
    cell. Use the same quantities for the forced norm after division by its
    positive source allowance, and take the larger values. For
    <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<less\>l>, the connected product expansion gives

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>*S<rsub|i+1>+\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>*S<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>,<label|eq:E-source-step>>>|<row|<cell|P<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>*P<rsub|i+1>+\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>*<around|(|S<rsub|i+1>+P<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>.<eq-number>>>>>
    </align>

    Here <math|K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>\<geqslant\>1> is fixed at the
    stage, and <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>+2>.
    To obtain these bounds, separate connected terms linear in the incoming
    corrected sources from those with at least two source factors. The former
    use the one-mark integration bound. In the latter, the product rule
    leaves a product of two source norms; summing the additional attachments
    uses the rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Its
    finite derivative and localization losses are bounded by
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>> after entry. Every higher product is absorbed
    in the same quadratic bound while the source norm is at most
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|-1/2>>. For the forced terms the retained
    eligibility event supplies the forced suppression before its allowance is
    divided out. The entry requirement in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries>
    includes this normalized forced bound. Disjoint cell modes use the
    multiplicative weights of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>; their
    connecting supports keep the same estimate. No factor counts the number
    of cells.

    Set <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>=S<rsub|i>+P<rsub|i>>.
    Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-step> imply
    <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i+1>+2*\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i+1><rsup|2>>.
    The first part of the interval gives <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<alpha\><rsub|t><rsup|n>*\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-loss>, for <math|b\<geqslant\>1> and
    sufficiently late entry,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-source-iteration>\<alpha\><rsub|t><rsup|n>*\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|-3/4>,<space|2em>\<frak-a\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|l-i>*\<frak-a\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|-3/4>\<less\>\<cal-M\><rsup|-1/2>.
    </equation>

    The second inequality follows by backward induction: below
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|-1/2>> the quadratic term in the recurrence is at
    most <math|2*\<frak-a\><rsub|i+1>>. For the first, the covariance
    contribution is at most <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|1/8+1/64-b>>, whose exponent
    is at most <math|-55/64>. The other contributions tend to zero faster
    than every fixed negative power of<nbsp><math|\<cal-M\>>, since <math|log
    \<cal-M\>=o<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)>> and
    <math|B<rsub|t>\<asymp\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>. This proves compatibility of the
    initial-error condition with the whole growing interval, rather than
    treating its constants as a finite product independent of<nbsp><math|t>.

    The extra length <math|v<rsub|t>> also provides the support margin: a
    level-<math|l> neighborhood of radius <math|B<rsub|t><rsup|2>> has
    level-<math|k> radius at most <math|B<rsub|t><rsup|2>/L<rsup|v<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|t><rsup|-8>>.
    It therefore fits inside the protected cell around its rounded reading
    points. The successive kernel neighborhoods add a bounded geometric sum
    in those units; their fixed margin was included in the choice of the
    comparison cell. Thus the inspection and attachment tests used in the
    iteration stay inside the stated interval. The unit has no remainder by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>.

    The support margin in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry> makes every
    replicated inspected stencil fit its own cell. Terms meeting flagged
    regions keep their original forced eligibility and the suppression used
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>; their source amplitude
    replaces only the marked letter's amplitude. Thus the local source sums
    at the enlarged radius are at most one in both regular and forced norms.
    The constant source contribution is at most one per cell. These are
    estimates on the complete source representation, not on its Gaussian
    polynomial alone. They prove the lemma on arbitrarily late intervals,
    with all thresholds independent of<nbsp><math|p>.
  </proof>

  The remaining normalization uses the complete positive unmarked density. In
  particular it cannot discard the forced sum or replace it by its healthy
  part.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Normalized source integral><label|lem:E-source-integral> For the
    corrected sources of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>, put
    <math|u<rsub|t>=log <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>>. For each
    such interval there is <math|N<rsub|0>> such that, for all
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all<nbsp><math|p>, for every nonempty
    mode<nbsp><math|I>,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>><around|(|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>|\|>*q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>>*\<alpha\><rsub|t><rsup|-<around|\||I|\|><around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>|\|>>,<space|2em>q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|t>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06> and positivity of
    <math|<density><space|0.17em>P>, which follows from the density identity
    of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, the positive Wilson start and the
    probability kernels of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>. Let <math|P> be the
    unmarked representation at the width level<nbsp><math|k>,
    <math|V=<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>=<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>|\|>>,
    <math|g=\<beta\><around|(|P|)><rsup|-1/2>>,
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|path>>=A<rsub|J<rsub|k>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>, and
    let <math|\<delta\>,\<Lambda\><rsup|<math-up|loop>>> bound its regular
    and loop terms. The admissibility inequalities give
    <math|0\<less\>g\<leqslant\>t<rsub|k>>, <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|<math-up|loop>>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>
    and <math|\<beta\><around|(|P|)>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>.

    Choose constants <math|s<rsub|H>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>,
    <math|b<rsub|H>\<geqslant\>1> such that small Haar balls have measure at
    least <math|s<rsub|H>*r<rsup|<dimG>>> and the clipped one-link integrals
    are at most <math|b<rsub|H>*<around|(|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|<dimG>>>,
    where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is the smaller of one and the linear
    clip coefficient. These follow from the nonsingular exponential chart and
    compactness of the group, with constants chosen before all levels. Write

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-h\>=log b<rsub|H>-<dimG>log \<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>\<cal-P\>*<around|(|P,X,g|)>=2*E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>+6+\<beta\><around|(|P|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|base>>+X+3*\<frak-h\>-4*log
      s<rsub|H>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>> bounds the loop and regular exponent per
    site, including the exponent discrepancy <math|\<delta\>> in the clipped
    comparison, and on links of size at most <math|g/4>,
    <math|\<beta\><around|(|P|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|base>>=3*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|k>>+H<rsub|<math-up|path>><rsup|2>|)>>.
    For any integrable complex<nbsp><math|F> with
    <math|z<around|\||F<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|P><around|(|U|)>+V*X>>,
    <math|z\<geqslant\>0>, the ratio estimate is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-positive-ratio><frac|z<around|\||<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*U|\|>|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>P*<space|0.17em>d*U>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|V*\<cal-P\>*<around|(|P,X,g|)>-4<dimG>log
      g>.
    </equation>

    For the denominator, fix an axial spanning tree with <math|V-1> links.
    Gauge invariance removes those links, leaving <math|3*V+1> free links.
    Restrict each free link to the ball of chord <math|g/4>. All plaquettes
    are then healthy, so the forced covering sum equals one. The lower action
    bound and the Haar-ball bound give

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><density><space|0.17em>P*<space|0.17em>d*U\<geqslant\>e<rsup|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>>*V-V*<around|(|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>+\<beta\><around|(|P|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|base>>|)>>*<around|(|s<rsub|H>*g<rsup|<dimG>>|)><rsup|3*V+1>.
    </equation*>

    For the numerator, the clipped plaquette inequality is

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><around|(|P|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|plaq>><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\><frac|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>>-1.
    </equation*>

    It follows in the quadratic branch from
    <math|<around|(|a/g-1|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|1,c<rsup|<math-up|dir>>|)>>, and in the linear branch from
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsup|<math-up|lin>>> and
    <math|g> below the threshold. Summing over the six plaquettes per site
    retains the product of <math|e<rsup|-<chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>/<around|(|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>>
    in the upper integrand.

    Keep the <math|3*V-3> plaquette factors selected as follows and discard
    the others, whose values lie in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. The axial tree
    returns each site towards zero in its highest nonzero coordinate. Order
    free links by that coordinate, treating wrap links first in each
    coordinate, except for the four links at zero, and then transverse links
    in decreasing height. Associate to each treated link its plaquette
    towards the tree. That plaquette contains the link once; every other
    remaining selected plaquette is independent of that link in this order.
    Haar invariance therefore replaces its factor by the one-link integral.
    The four untreated links account for the difference
    <math|<around|(|3*V+1|)>-4=3*V-3>. All discarded plaquette factors lie in
    <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, including at the periodic boundary. Thus

    <\equation*>
      z<around|\||<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*U|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>>*V+V*<around|(|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>+6+X|)>>*<around*|(|b<rsub|H>*<around|(|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|<dimG>>|)><rsup|3*V-3>.
    </equation*>

    Divide these inequalities. The powers of <math|g> differ by
    <math|-4<dimG>>. Using <math|3*V-3\<leqslant\>3*V>,
    <math|3*V+1\<leqslant\>4*V>, <math|b<rsub|H>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|s<rsub|H>\<leqslant\>1> gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio>.
    This proves the estimate for the complete positive unmarked denominator
    without removing its forced terms outside the restricted lower-bound set.

    One may use the clipped comparison exponent in this calculation even if
    it is not the exponent used for the source bound. Their difference has
    absolute value at most <math|V*\<delta\>>. Multiplying the upper source
    bound by <math|e<rsup|V*\<delta\>>> and bounding the two positive
    partition integrals in the opposite direction by the same factor accounts
    for this change. These two factors are included in
    <math|2*E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>> because each occurrence of
    <math|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>> includes <math|\<delta\>>.
    Thus<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio> applies to the actual source
    integral on the original exact history.

    The enlarged-radius bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
    give <math|X\<leqslant\>1+n>: one is the scalar bound, and each of at
    most <math|n> marks contributes at most one. The local coefficient
    estimate before integration includes <math|z=\<alpha\><rsub|t><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>*V>>.
    The entry inequalities imply

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>\<leqslant\>8*H<rsub|<math-up|path>>+2,<space|2em>-4<dimG>log
      g/V\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    For the second, <math|-4<dimG>log g=2<dimG>log \<beta\><around|(|P|)>> is
    <math|O<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>>, whereas
    <math|V\<geqslant\>D<rsub|k>=e<rsup|s<rsub|k>>>. The first follows from
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>*t<rsub|k><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>*t<rsub|k><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|loop>>*t<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1>
    on sufficiently late levels. Hence choose a stage constant

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-comparison-cost>Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<geqslant\>2*<around|(|8*A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>+2|)>+6+3*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>+<around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>+<around|(|1+n|)>+3*\<frak-h\><rsub|j>-4*log
      s<rsub|H>+1
    </equation>

    for each arity admitted at stage<nbsp><math|j>. It bounds the exponent
    per cell in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio>. All its quantities are
    fixed before the entry to stage<nbsp><math|j>.
    Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>
    requires <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>> on the chosen intervals. This
    proves <math|q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|t>> and the
    asserted coefficient estimate. In particular the volume appears only
    through the number of cells, so the cutoff threshold is independent
    of<nbsp><math|p>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Window hypotheses.> The proofs of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>\U<reference|lem:E-source-integral>
  use the interval only through these properties of its levels
  <math|k\<less\>l\<less\>t>, with <math|\<cal-M\>=<cutoffsize><rsub|t>>:
  (W1) <math|log <around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|t>|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|b+2|)>*log
  \<cal-M\>>, <math|log <around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|l>|)>\<geqslant\>10*log
  B<rsub|t>>, and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<in\><around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|]>>
  for <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t>; (W2)
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t>\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|1/64>>;
  (W3) every level of <math|<around|[|k,t|]>> is past the entries of
  item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> for the finitely many requests, and
  <math|Q<rsup|<math-up|cmp>><rsub|J<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>log
  \<cal-M\>/<around|(|16*n|)>>. Under (W1)\U(W3) the three lemmas hold with
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+2>> replaced by
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|l\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t>\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>>;
  the interval need not lie in one stage.

  The next estimate uses the corrected source comparison in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>. Each relative error is
  interior whenever its insertions are interior,
  since<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative> is a polynomial in those insertions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Relative rank comparison><label|lem:E2.10> For every such finite
    test family, there are <math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>*\<downarrow\>*0>,
    <math|y<rsup|\<nu\>,i>\<to\>y<rsup|i>>, levels
    <math|k<rsub|\<nu\>>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|\<nu\>>> tending to infinity,
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>\<geqslant\>0> tending to zero, and real
    lifts<nbsp><math|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|j,N,p>> of the free
    basis<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>|)>>, bounded
    in<nbsp><math|N,p> for fixed<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> and common to all rows.
    They satisfy <math|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|1,N,p>=\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>>.
    Each<nbsp><math|x<rsup|\<nu\>,i>> is generic on the
    spacing-<math|a<rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>>> grid. Eventually uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|p>, all row insertions are interior
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k<rsub|\<nu\>>><around|(|0|)>>,
    and, for every nonempty<nbsp><math|I>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k<rsub|\<nu\>>><around|(|0|)>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10>>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral>. Use the coefficients
    <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>> of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-corrected-coefficients>.
    Apply Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>\U<reference|lem:E-source-integral>
    with arity bound <math|n=2>, so <math|\<alpha\><rsub|t>=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|1/16>=e<rsup|2*u<rsub|t>>>
    and <math|u<rsub|t>=log <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/32>. The normalized source
    lemma gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-rank-input><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|(|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>*q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>|}>*\<alpha\><rsub|t><rsup|-<around|\||I|\|><around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>>,<space|2em>q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|t>.
    </equation>

    The cutoff threshold is independent of<nbsp><math|p>.

    Taking the <math|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>>-th root
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-rank-input> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|u<rsub|t>-2<around|\||I|\|>*u<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-u<rsub|t>>=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1/32>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|e<rsup|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>> has coefficient
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|J>> at <math|z<rsub|J>>, multiplication by
    <math|e<rsup|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>> shows that
    <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>> is exactly <math|R<rsub|I>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative>. Choose successive admissible windows with
    both levels increasing, reading radii strictly decreasing to zero, and
    <math|e<rsup|-u<rsub|t>>> decreasing to zero. The scale-selection windows
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> allow these finitely many requests at
    each step. Call the resulting levels <math|k<rsub|\<nu\>>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|\<nu\>>>,
    radii <math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>> and errors
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>=e<rsup|-u<rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>>>>. Rounding
    on the finer grid preserves genericity for each fixed trial once that
    grid is fine enough. Interior insertion supports imply interiority of
    every <math|R<rsub|I>>, by its polynomial formula. This proves the
    relative-error assertion and the common choice of lifts for all rows.
  </render-proof>

  <paragraph|Volume-uniform difference bounds.>

  All the preceding comparison lemmas hold on full cutoff tails, uniformly
  in<nbsp><math|p>. The local matching estimate also applies to any fixed
  iterated difference: after multiplying by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d+h>>, a
  difference of order<nbsp><math|h> is a finite sum of translates of the
  degree-<math|d> section, with subpower derivative bounds. Matching in
  degree <math|d+h> leaves a root error tending to zero at every fixed cell
  size, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>.

  This finite-cutoff argument supplies the difference bounds required in
  Part<nbsp>I.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Difference estimate><label|lem:E2.4> For every arity <math|n>,
    token list <math|v>, difference list <math|\<b-l\>> and <math|r\<gtr\>0>,
    the constants in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> can be chosen
    independently of<nbsp><math|p>:

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|p> sup<rsub|<math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>r><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*<around|(|n,v,\<b-l\>,r|)>*<space|2em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>*<around|(|n,v,\<b-l\>,r|)>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08> and<nbsp><reference|def:D27>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>\U<reference|lem:E2.3>, the local
    matching and equation comparison of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7>\U<reference|lem:E2.9>
    on full cutoff tails, and the free tests and rank comparison of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>\U<reference|lem:E2.10>. We
    first bound the roots of a differentiated insertion at each sufficiently
    fine fixed cell size.

    Let <math|F=O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c|)>>, and let
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F> be a fixed iterated normalized
    difference in its anchor. Local matching gives admissible coefficients
    <math|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>> in degree <math|e=d+<around|\||\<b-a\>|\|>> with
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|e|)>><around|(|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>|)>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F>
    of vanishing root. Write <math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>=\<Pi\><rsub|e,N,p>*c<rsup|\<b-a\>>>.
    For an admissible coefficient in <math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|e>>, the auxiliary
    equation section has root zero. To see this, take all the other reflected
    copies as the spectator in the exact Haar identity in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4>. For a fixed physical cell, its side in
    lattice units eventually exceeds twice the equation radius plus two; all
    those copies are then exterior. The section-comparison residual tends to
    zero in root. In particular it applies to
    <math|z=<around|(|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>-c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>.
    Indeed, <math|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    is admissible, and <math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    has norm at most one. Every bounded coefficient family is admissible,
    because Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,e><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|e>*<big|prod><rsub|i=r<around|(|e|)>><rsup|N-1>C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-o<around|(|1|)>>
    </equation*>

    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. Subtracting these two admissible families
    proves admissibility of<nbsp><math|z>
    before<nbsp><math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>> is bounded.

    It remains to bound<nbsp><math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>>. Use a fixed
    sufficiently short-distance trial in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10> for
    degree<nbsp><math|e>. Let <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> be the minimum
    coordinate separation of the fixed trial on the physical torus. Fix a
    slab margin <math|0\<less\>b\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|0>/8> and a centered
    level<nbsp><math|D> with <math|a<rsub|D>\<less\>b>. Average the variable
    slot of every row over <math|Q<rsub|N>=<around|\<lceil\>|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>/a<rsub|N>|\<rceil\>>>
    sites in each direction occurring in<nbsp><math|\<b-a\>>, where
    <math|0\<less\>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>\<less\>b/<around|[|2*<around|(|<around|\||\<b-a\>|\|>+1|)>|]>>.
    Eventually <math|Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>2*\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>>,
    so the total shift in each coordinate is less than<nbsp><math|b> and
    every coordinate hybrid used in the averaging has coordinate separation
    at least<nbsp><math|4*b>. Denote this linear map on coefficients by
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>>. Telescoping in one direction gives

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|Q<rsub|N>>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|Q<rsub|N>-1><frac|f*<around|(|z+<around|(|j+1|)>*e<rsub|a>|)>-f*<around|(|z+j*e<rsub|a>|)>|a<rsub|N>>=<frac|f*<around|(|z+Q<rsub|N>*e<rsub|a>|)>-f<around|(|z|)>|Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>>,
    iterating bounds the tests of<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F> by
    <math|<around|(|2/\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>|)><rsup|<around|\||\<b-a\>|\|>>>
    times the supremum of tests of<nbsp><math|F>. The latter is bounded by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>, the interior-root comparison
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>. Matching errors tend to zero in these tests:
    put the error and the bounded spectator insertions in distinct cells with
    fixed margins, use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> for their interior
    roots, and apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>. All these bounds hold on full
    cutoff tails and use no positional difference estimate. Thus
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsup|\<b-a\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|test>>>
    eventually, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>.

    The finite-dimensional lower bound is obtained before any cutoff limit.
    The free matrix has a positive lower bound on the unit sphere. The
    relative-error estimate and the equation-root bound put the tests of the
    projected trial lifts within <math|O<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>+o<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>><around|(|1|)>>
    of the free matrix, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>. Choose the fixed
    trial<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> with this error smaller than its matrix lower
    bound. Averaging over sufficiently small<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>>
    preserves the lower bound: the undifferentiated correlation tests have
    uniform positional continuity by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement>,
    with margin<nbsp><math|b>, the centered level<nbsp><math|D> and the
    coordinate-hybrid separations just chosen. The level and its root bounds
    are fixed before reducing<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>>, so the
    displacement bound tends to zero with<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|av>>>.
    The projected lifts are therefore linearly independent. Their number
    equals <math|dim <maxwellspace|e>>, whereas
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6> bounds <math|dim
    <math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<Pi\><rsub|e>> by that number. They
    consequently span the range. Their uniform coefficient bounds give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-detection><around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation>

    with <math|C<rsub|<math-up|det>>> independent of<nbsp><math|N,p> on the
    retained tails. Choose an invertible square minor <math|H<rsub|0>> of the
    free matrix and the corresponding interacting minor<nbsp><math|H>. For
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H-H<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|2<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>,
    the inverse bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>>
    follows. This proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-detection> without assuming a
    continuum rank identity.

    The equation coefficient <math|z=<around|(|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>-c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    is admissible. Its tests tend to zero, so eventually
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>*z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/2>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-detection> yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*C<rsub|<math-up|test>>+<tfrac|1|2><around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*C<rsub|<math-up|test>>+1.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> now bounds the roots
    of<nbsp><math|O<rsup|<around|(|e|)>><around|(|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|)>> at
    each fixed sufficiently fine scale. Now
    <math|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>-c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>> is admissible, so its
    equation residual has vanishing root. Together with the matching
    residual, the triangle inequality for roots gives the same type of bound
    for<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F>. All thresholds and constants
    in this argument are independent of<nbsp><math|p>.

    Distribute the differences in <math|\<b-l\>> among their indicated slots.
    A difference in slot<nbsp><math|i> acts only on that insertion, so the
    resulting moment is exactly the expectation of the product of these
    differentiated insertions. Choose a fixed tiling side smaller
    than<nbsp><math|r> and translate its boundaries away from all anchors. In
    each axis the excluded boundary strips occupy less than one full cell
    length if their widths are chosen below
    <math|a<rsub|m>/<around|[|4*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>. For sufficiently
    large<nbsp><math|N> a lattice translation with these margins exists.
    Distinct anchors occupy distinct cells, since their torus separation
    exceeds the cell side. The interior-root comparison, with a finer
    centered scale, bounds all the factors uniformly.
    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> and put one in every unused cell. Their
    product gives the asserted<nbsp><math|B>. Empty difference lists and
    arities at most one obey the same argument. This proves the lemma and
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Bounded coarse insertions.>

  The later phase argument needs bounded approximations with a bound
  independent of the cutoff. Compactness at each cutoff gives no such bound.
  We obtain it by stopping at one fixed coarse lattice and pulling bounded
  observables back through the probability block kernels.

  In the next two lemmas, a statement holds eventually in the ordered limits
  when it holds for all sufficiently large indices of the chosen volume
  sequence and, at each such volume, all sufficiently fine cutoffs of the
  chosen spacing sequence. No common cutoff threshold in other subsequently
  fixed parameters is asserted. The chessboard roots used below are defined
  in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Bounded coarse approximations><label|lem:E-coarse-bounded> For
    every <math|d>, <math|k\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>>, real
    <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, there are
    measurable real observables <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>>
    and <math|M<rsub|<math-up|bnd>>\<less\>\<infty\>>, independent of
    <math|N,p,y>, with <math|<around|\||\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|<math-up|bnd>>>
    and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>-O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k><around|(|y|)>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>
    </equation*>

    eventually in the ordered limits. Both observables are interior to that
    cell, and the same holds for their signed and reflected images.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D04>, <reference|def:D08>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>, <reference|lem:E2.2a>, and the
    several-source estimate in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>. The
    construction in this proof is independent of the phase decomposition.
    Choose a fixed <math|m\<geqslant\>k> with
    <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|m>>, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. At a finer level <math|h\<geqslant\>m>,
    the bounded coarse polynomial with coordinate<nbsp><math|v> is

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|V><rsub|h><around|(|v;U|)>=a<rsub|h><rsup|-d>*<around*|(|v<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*P<rsub|d><around|[|<factorscale><rsub|t<rsub|h><rsup|-1>>v<rsup|\<circ\>>|]><around|(|U|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its supremum for each basis input is bounded by a constant depending
    on<nbsp><math|d,h>, uniformly in<nbsp><math|N,p>: the healthy polynomial
    uses a fixed compact coordinate range, and the unit term is constant. Its
    inspected stencil has a fixed radius, including the links needed for the
    health test.

    Let <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|N,h><around|(|U,d*V|)>> be the probability
    kernel obtained by composing the block kernels from <math|N>
    to<nbsp><math|h>, and set

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F|)><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F<around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-K\><rsub|N,h><around|(|U,d*V|)>.
    </equation*>

    It preserves real values and does not increase the supremum norm. The
    locality of the block kernels confines its dependence to the fine
    preimage of the inspected stencil. For reflected cells these ancestor
    cones are disjoint once <math|h> has the stated margin. At each
    intermediate level the block kernels are products in the coarse-link
    variables given the preceding field; integrating disjoint ancestor cones
    therefore gives conditional independence, and hence
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|C>\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F<rsub|C>=\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h><around|(|<big|prod><rsub|C>F<rsub|C>|)>>.
    Choose <math|h> sufficiently large that this preimage lies strictly
    inside the centered level-<math|k> cell, including its boundary-link
    margin.

    To compare with the actual insertion, choose trial fine coefficients
    <math|u<rsub|j>> whose selected level-<math|h> polynomial coordinates
    differ by at most <math|1/4> from the basis vectors. They are obtained by
    carrying the pure coarse polynomial back through the inverse graph
    construction in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>.
    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-match-cone> to the difference with this primary
    coarse polynomial. Put <math|S<rsub|h>=C<rsub|J<rsub|h>>> temporarily for
    the column bound, and let <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|h>> be the matrix of the
    selected level-<math|h> coordinates of these trials. For a basis input,
    the cone and the finest-level boundary estimate give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-coarse-inversion>32*<around|(|1+S<rsub|h>|)><rsup|2>*<around|\||e<rsub|j>-\<cal-J\><rsub|h>*e<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|h>,<space|2em>R<rsub|h>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+S<rsub|h>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsub|j>-\<cal-J\><rsub|h>*e<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|1|16*<around|(|1+S<rsub|h>|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|4>.
    </equation>

    Taking the maximum over columns gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-J\><rsub|h>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1/4>,
    which implies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-J\><rsub|h><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>.
    If <math|c> is the required reference coefficient, linear recombination
    therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      O<around|(|c|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j>t<rsub|j>*O<around|(|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>u<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||t<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>h>log
      C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>><around|\||c|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Replace each term on the right by <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h><math-ss|V><rsub|h><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>>.
    This defines a bounded complex observable
    <math|\<cal-B\><rprime|'><rsub|N,p>>; its bound depends on <math|h,d,c>,
    and hence may depend on <math|\<delta\>>, but is independent
    of<nbsp><math|N,p>.

    The discrepancy is controlled by replicating all its reflected copies
    before taking the marginal kernel. Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-multiple-density>
    applies to the fine insertions; at level<nbsp><math|h> multiply the
    source density by the corresponding coarse factors. The conditional
    factorization above identifies the coefficient at each mark with
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h><math-ss|V><rsub|h><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>-O<around|(|<math-ss|Z>u<rsub|j>|)>>.
    The probability kernel supplies no additional supremum factor. Weighted
    coefficient extraction and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-ratio> then give,
    after recombination,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-bounded-error><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rprime|'><rsub|N,p>-O<around|(|c|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k,m,d,c>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>h>log
      C<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|J<rsub|h>>|)>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|h>-s<rsub|m>|)>/8>.
    </equation>

    The prefactor has subpower growth in<nbsp><math|a<rsub|h><rsup|-1>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>; the last factor tends to zero. Choose
    <math|h> so that this expression is at most<nbsp><math|\<delta\>>, before
    choosing the cutoff. This also meets the stencil-fit condition.

    For real<nbsp><math|c>, the original insertion is real by
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:A1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>. Set
    <math|\<cal-B\>=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-B\><rprime|'>>. In the
    centered cell,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\>-O<around|(|c|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<cal-B\><rprime|'>-O<around|(|c|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><wide|\<cal-B\><rprime|'>-O<around|(|c|)>|\<bar\>>.
    </equation*>

    The root seminorm and its invariance under conjugation show that this
    does not increase<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-bounded-error>; the supremum bound
    also does not increase. Translation, signed permutation and conjugation
    use the same finite collection of stencils and bounds. This proves the
    lemma on the stated ordered tails.
  </proof>

  Fix a degree bound<nbsp><math|d>, a lower level<nbsp><math|m> and an
  ordered extraction. A real grid word is a list
  <math|w=<around|(|<around|(|d<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>|)>,x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>>
  with <math|d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d>, <math|<wide|c|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>>
  and distinct spacing-<math|a<rsub|k>> positions. We assume its
  configuration is generic. Write <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|lin>>> for
  linear time reflection on positions and labels, without scalar conjugation.
  Put

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|N,p><around|(|w|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i>O<rsup|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|N>|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>=T*j*a<rsub|k>*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|j,N,p,T>=F<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*w|)>-F<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|lin>>*w|)>.
  </equation*>

  These are the real word differences used in the off-diagonal variance
  estimate in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-H>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Bounded real word approximations><label|lem:E2-bounded-real> There
    is <math|k\<geqslant\>m>, chosen before<nbsp><math|w>, such that every
    such word has, for each <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, measurable real
    approximations <math|g<rsub|j,N,p,T>> and
    <math|R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<less\>\<infty\>> with
    <math|<around|\||g<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>
    for all <math|N,p,T,j>. For each <math|i\<less\>j> and every sufficiently
    large integer<nbsp><math|T>, eventually in the ordered limits,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around|\<langle\>|g<rsub|i,N,p,T>*g<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>-<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i,N,p,T>*f<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, <reference|def:D27>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-coarse-bounded>, <reference|lem:E2.3> and
    the finite reflection argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>. Choose
    <math|k\<geqslant\>max <around|(|m,max<rsub|e\<leqslant\>d>
    r<around|(|e|)>|)>>. It depends only on<nbsp><math|d,m>, before the
    length, labels and positions of<nbsp><math|w>. All its finer comparison
    levels remain available by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>.

    Include every label in<nbsp><math|w> and its signed and conjugated images
    in a finite list. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> gives a bound <math|C>
    for their centered roots at any one fixed sufficiently fine level. Choose
    that level so that its cells and the margins required by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> fit between distinct
    spacing-<math|a<rsub|k>> points. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-coarse-bounded> with
    error<nbsp><math|\<delta\>> at a still finer centered level, preserving
    these margins. Denote its real bounded approximations
    by<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>>. The same family, translated to any
    anchor, is used for every copy of the word. Its roots are at most
    <math|C+\<delta\>>, independently of its possibly much larger supremum
    bound.

    For a product of <math|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>> such insertions at
    distinct grid sites, telescope the products, replacing one factor at a
    time. Put all <math|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>> factors into distinct cells
    with the chosen margins. The interior-root comparison
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-word-error><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>>\<cal-B\><rsub|a>-<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>>O<rsub|a>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|<math-up|word>>*\<delta\>*<around|(|C+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>-1>.
    </equation>

    Only the roots, not the supremum bounds on<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|a>>,
    occur on the right. For each fixed finite configuration, sufficiently
    large periods avoid wrapping identifications; its stencils then lie in
    the prescribed cells on the inner cutoff tail. This explains the order of
    the two eventualities.

    Let <math|G<rsub|j,N,p,T>> be the product of the <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>>
    for the translated word, and let <math|G<rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|j,N,p,T>>
    be the corresponding product for its reflected word. Set
    <math|g<rsub|j>=G<rsub|j>-G<rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|j>>. Choose
    <math|M<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bnd>>> to bound both the original and
    reflected approximations in slot<nbsp><math|i>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||g<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*<big|prod><rsub|i>M<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bnd>>=:R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,
    </equation*>

    independently of<nbsp><math|N,p,T,j>. Every<nbsp><math|g<rsub|j>> is real
    and measurable. Set <math|g<rsub|j>=0> outside the ordered tails on which
    the coarse approximations are defined, so this supremum bound holds for
    all <math|N,p,T,j>. For fixed <math|i\<less\>j>, sufficiently large
    <math|T> separates the two translated finite lists, for each of the four
    combinations of a word and a reflected word. Indeed their first
    coordinates differ by <math|T*<around|(|j-i|)>*a<rsub|k>> plus a fixed
    finite difference. Each list is itself distinct because the original word
    is generic and reflection is injective.

    Expand <math|g<rsub|i>*g<rsub|j>> and <math|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>> into
    their four product terms and apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-word-error> with
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>=2*n>. Choose
    <math|0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1> so that
    <math|4*<around|(|2*n|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|C+1|)><rsup|2*n-1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    Taking real parts cannot increase the error. Intersecting the four
    eventual sets proves the assertion. The empty word has zero difference
    and may be assigned <math|g<rsub|j>=0>. There is no common cutoff
    threshold in <math|T,i,j>; a finite list of pairs is handled by
    intersecting its eventual sets. The choice gives such a grid level above
    every prescribed lower level<nbsp><math|m>, which is the cofinality
    required in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-H>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Full ultraviolet convergence><label|sec:partII-E.3>

  Fix<nbsp><math|p>. The normalized density at a fixed level is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-normalized-density><frac|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>>=<frac|e<rsup|-<pathaction><rsub|N,m>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|N,p,m>|<big|int>e<rsup|-<pathaction><rsub|N,m>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|N,p,m>>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|N,p,m>> is the forced sum of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>. The factor
  <math|e<rsup|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>,m><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,m>>|\|>>>
  has canceled. This identity is needed because no convergence of that factor
  is proved or used.

  For a fixed finite token list and a separated common-grid
  configuration<nbsp><math|x>, let <math|o<rsub|i,m>=x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|m>> be
  its sites at all sufficiently fine levels. They are read modulo the period;
  their parents agree at consecutive levels.
  In<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, form <math|\<cal-H\><rsup|0><rsub|N,p,m>>
  by setting <math|H<rsub|m,X>=K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>=0>, keeping
  the complete singleton functions <math|f<rsub|i,m>>
  and<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i,m,Y>>. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-coarse-integral>C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)>=<frac|<big|int><around|[|z<rsub|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>|]>*\<cal-H\><rsup|0><rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|z;U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>*<space|0.17em>d*U>.
  </equation>

  The numerator is therefore specified by the unmarked representation and the
  one-mark families, rather than by a new choice of a several-insertion
  density. The difference from the exact source is estimated separately.

  The first lemma compares different cutoffs at fixed volume and fixed level.
  It uses the convergence assertion for the unmarked recursion, rather than
  applying a same-cutoff uniqueness inequality to histories of different
  lengths.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fixed-level marked convergence><label|lem:E-fixed-level> For every
    fixed <math|p,v,x> as above and every sufficiently fine fixed
    level<nbsp><math|m>, the scalar-free unmarked density and the complete
    singleton functions in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> are Cauchy in their
    common function spaces as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. Their normalized
    integrals satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-coarse-cauchy>C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)><text| is
      Cauchy as >N\<to\>\<infty\>.
    </equation>

    The scalar-free partition integral is bounded below by a positive
    constant depending on<nbsp><math|p,m>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, <reference|def:D08>,
    <reference|def:D25a> and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>,
    <reference|lem:D-fixed-level>, <reference|lem:E-marked-step>,
    <reference|lem:E2.2a>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1> gives
    full-sequence convergence of the scalar couplings and scalar-free
    activities in fixed weighted spaces at each fixed<nbsp><math|p,m>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level> includes the prescribed paths
    with the strict reserve. Thus the cutoff-dependent path arrays converge
    in the same charts. The vacuum scalar is excluded, as
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-normalized-density>.

    Use <math|\<kappa\><rsub|i>=<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>
    in the remainder norm, and normalize graph differences by
    <math|C<rsub|J<rsub|i>>> and the coefficient sum. The graph bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> bounds this distance between two
    admitted graphs by<nbsp><math|2>. The contraction in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> reduces it by <math|1/2> through
    a step on the same unmarked representation. For two different cutoffs,
    first compare both steps with a step on their limiting unmarked
    coordinates. The one-mark local integrals, their Taylor coefficients and
    the forced regrouping are continuous in these coordinates. To check this
    continuity, retain the common summable majorants
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates>; each finite local integral is
    continuous in its coefficients and chart, and the remaining tails are
    bounded by these same summable majorants. The inverse coefficient maps
    are continuous because their inverses have the common bound already
    proved. Hence for each fixed <math|i> the extra discrepancy
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>> tends to zero as both
    cutoffs tend to infinity.

    Iterating from a remote fixed level<nbsp><math|h> down to<nbsp><math|m>
    gives the genuine cross-cutoff estimate

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-cross-cutoff>d<rsub|m>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|1-<around|(|h-m|)>>+<big|sum><rsub|i=m><rsup|h-1>2<rsup|-<around|(|i-m|)>>*\<delta\><rsub|i>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>.
    </equation>

    This estimate compares graphs after the fixed-level unmarked
    identification, not histories of the same length. First make <math|h-m>
    large. For that fixed <math|h>, the sum tends to zero, proving the Cauchy
    property at<nbsp><math|m>. The finitely many coefficient maps between the
    reference level and<nbsp><math|m> converge as well. Solving their
    fixed-dimensional linear systems therefore gives Cauchy polynomial
    coordinates for each fixed reference label. Substituting in the graph
    gives the complete singleton functions.

    On the fixed finite torus, weighted convergence bounds the sums of the
    regular terms uniformly and bounds the forced terms after grouping
    identical supports and inventories. There are only finitely many such
    supports and inventories. The resulting scalar-free density and the
    singleton functions are uniformly Cauchy on their fixed health domains.
    The health threshold at level<nbsp><math|m> is fixed; no moving indicator
    is being differentiated or replaced. Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>,
    with its higher modes set to zero, is a finite polynomial in the grouped
    functions and their exponentials. Its scalar-free coefficients are
    consequently uniformly Cauchy and integrable against Haar probability
    measure.

    The denominator has a common positive lower bound. Restrict to an axial
    tree gauge with all free links of chord at most <math|g/4>, choosing
    <math|g\<gtr\>0> below every admitted
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m><rsup|-1/2>> on the retained fixed-level tail.
    The forced sum on this set is one, and the scalar-free action is
    uniformly bounded above there. Its product Haar measure is positive. The
    same lower bound applies to all cutoffs on that tail. Dividing the Cauchy
    numerator integrals by these positive Cauchy denominators
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-coarse-cauchy>.
  </proof>

  The source density transfers exactly, but its singleton part does not:
  connected components with two or more marks remain. The next estimate
  bounds the effect of omitting all those components while keeping the bound
  independent of the stopping level.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stopping estimate><label|lem:E-stopping> For fixed <math|p,v,x>,
    there are <math|F<rsub|0>\<less\>\<infty\>> and <math|m<rsub|0>> such
    that, for each <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>> and every sufficiently large
    <math|N>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-stop-error><around|\||M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>.
    </equation>

    The constant <math|F<rsub|0>> is independent of <math|m,N>; the cutoff
    threshold may depend on<nbsp><math|m>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, <reference|def:D08>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>, <reference|lem:E-fixed-level>,
    and the exact source evolution<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-multiple-density>. Choose
    a base level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>> above the reference levels and the
    grid containing<nbsp><math|x>. At all sufficiently fine levels, distinct
    ancestral sites have separation at least <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>>: their
    separation is a fixed positive physical number divided
    by<nbsp><math|a<rsub|i>>, while <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> grows only as a
    power of <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>. Their occupancy is one.
    Enlarge <math|k<rsub|0>> so these assertions and all required positive
    source allowances hold thereafter.

    Choose positive weights <math|w<rsub|i>> at this base level so the
    weighted complete singleton sizes lie below those allowances. This choice
    is independent of<nbsp><math|N>: Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>
    bounds each singleton uniformly along the fixed-level cutoff tail. Let
    <math|s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|for>>> bound
    the weighted regular singleton supremum and the sum of forced singleton
    suprema at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>. Set

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-stopping-constant>F<rsub|0>=<frac|exp
      <around|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k<rsub|0>>>|\|>*q<rsub|k<rsub|0>>+n*<around|(|s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|reg>>+s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|for>>+2|)>|}>|<big|prod><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>>.
    </equation>

    The numerator is the normalized-integral bound of the several-mark
    expansion, now with these fixed base weights. It depends on the base
    level, not on the later stopping level.

    At level<nbsp><math|m\<gtr\>k<rsub|0>>, every nonzero mode of
    <math|H<rsub|m,X>> or <math|K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>> contains at
    least two marks and its support contains their anchors. The covering
    estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-cover> gives

    <\equation*>
      <cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|m>/64.
    </equation*>

    Multiply every one of these higher-mode functions by a common complex
    variable<nbsp><math|z>. For <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>>,
    its amplitude increase is absorbed by decreasing the support exponent by
    two:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||z|\|>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>-2|)><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Singleton functions are not multiplied by<nbsp><math|z>. The source
    representation therefore remains integrable on
    <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>>, with its prescribed
    singleton functions, original forced eligibility tests and two fewer
    units of support exponent. Here <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>=100>.
    For <math|s\<geqslant\>0> and <math|98\<leqslant\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>100>,
    <math|e<rsup|\<tau\>*s>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|100*s>>, so every regular or
    forced weighted sum decreases when its exponent decreases. The
    coefficientwise rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>
    bounds the connected regular terms at exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3> and the
    forced terms at <math|\<sigma\>+2>. The marked product factors are paid
    at the entry chosen in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. Applying these bounds in the weighted
    source norm of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> leaves the two units used
    here, with final exponent <math|98>.

    Carry this modified density back to the same base
    level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>, using the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> with all support exponents lowered
    by two, as permitted in its statement. Every nonzero mode contains only
    marks whose anchors lie in its support, so multiplying modes retains
    their positive weights without adding a mark outside that support. At the
    base level, the singleton exponential and forced factors contribute at
    most <math|n*<around|(|s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|reg>>+s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)>>
    to the logarithmic majorant; the remaining connected regular and forced
    sums contribute at most <math|2*n>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-ratio>
    contributes <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k<rsub|0>>>|\|>*q<rsub|k<rsub|0>>>.
    Coefficient extraction divides by <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>w<rsub|i>>,
    giving exactly<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-stopping-constant>. Absolute convergence
    on <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>> makes the
    normalized full-mode integral <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|z|)>> holomorphic
    there, with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|m><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    At the base level this is precisely the
    bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-stopping-constant>. It avoids the level-<math|m>
    volume factor that would arise from estimating the normalized integral
    only where it was stopped.

    For <math|e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>\<gtr\>2>, the Cauchy derivative estimate
    on circles of radius <math|e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>/2> centered on
    <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|m><around|(|1|)>-F<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>.
    </equation*>

    At <math|z=1> this is the exact multiple-source integral. Its equality to
    the original cutoff moment follows from Fubini and the probability
    normalization of every block kernel in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-multiple-density>.
    At <math|z=0> it is exactly<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-coarse-integral>.
    Substitution proves the estimate. For each fixed<nbsp><math|m>, the
    required bound on its singleton functions holds on a cutoff tail by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>; it changes the threshold but
    leaves<nbsp><math|F<rsub|0>> fixed.
  </proof>

  The fixed-level and stopping estimates, together with the positional bounds
  already proved, give the full limit.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Lattice-point convergence><label|lem:E3.1> For every <math|p,n,v>,
    there is <math|f<rsub|p,v>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>|)>>
    such that, for every <math|\<b-l\>>, compact
    <math|K\<subset\><NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>> and <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
    some <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|p,n,v,\<b-l\>,K,\<varepsilon\>|)>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    at every spacing-<math|a<rsub|N>> point of <math|K> in the periodic lift,
    for every <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D03> and<nbsp><reference|def:D08>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.4>, and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>\U<reference|lem:E-stopping>.
    At a separated configuration on a fixed common grid, the triangle
    inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||M<rsub|N,p>-M<rsub|N<rprime|'>,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>+<around|\||C<rsub|N,p,m>-C<rsub|N<rprime|'>,p,m>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    First take <math|m> large, using <math|B<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>, and
    then take <math|N,N<rprime|'>> large. This proves the Cauchy property at
    every such configuration. Since every ratio is an integer, each coarser
    grid is contained in every finer grid. The union of these grids is dense
    in the periodic noncollision domain.

    We next extend this limit and identify its derivatives. Choose a compact
    neighbourhood <math|K<rprime|'>> of<nbsp><math|K> contained in that
    domain, and a positive lower bound for its torus separation.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.4> bounds the lattice functions and all
    their fixed differences there. One more difference gives, by telescoping
    along coordinate lattice paths,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||G<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*<space|0.17em><around|\||z-z<rprime|'>|\|><rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    for nearby physical grid points, where
    <math|G<rsub|N>=\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>>. For order zero, a
    finite dense grid in <math|K<rprime|'>> and this estimate extend the
    pointwise Cauchy property to uniform convergence on<nbsp><math|K>. The
    multilinear interpolants obey the same bound, with an additional
    <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|N>|)>> displacement to their vertices. The
    resulting limit<nbsp><math|f<rsub|p,v>> is continuous and periodic.

    Suppose <math|G<rsub|N>> already converges locally uniformly
    to<nbsp><math|g>. Bounds for two more differences compare its first
    forward difference with a difference over a fixed physical interval. Put
    <math|h<rsub|N>=Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<to\>h\<gtr\>0>, with
    <math|Q<rsub|N>\<in\><N>>. Summing successive first differences and
    bounding their variation by the second difference gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-discrete-taylor><around*|\||<frac|G<rsub|N>*<around|(|z+h<rsub|N>*e|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>|h<rsub|N>>-\<Delta\><rsub|e>*G<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>B*h<rsub|N>.
    </equation>

    Choose <math|h> small and then let <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. The endpoint
    values converge uniformly, so<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-discrete-taylor> proves
    that <math|\<Delta\><rsub|e>*G<rsub|N>> is uniformly Cauchy
    on<nbsp><math|K>. Call its continuous limit<nbsp><math|g<rsub|e>>. The
    exact telescoping identity on grid segments, followed by Riemann
    summation of the uniformly convergent first differences, gives

    <\equation*>
      g*<around|(|z+t*e|)>-g<around|(|z|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|e>*<around|(|z+u*e|)>*<space|0.17em>d*u.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|\<partial\><rsub|e>*g=g<rsub|e>>. Induct on the length of the
    difference list. Every required additional order is available from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.4>, so all derivative limits exist and
    <math|f<rsub|p,v>> is smooth. Finite covers by such neighbourhoods give
    the asserted uniform statement on<nbsp><math|K>.
  </proof>

  The same limit applies when observation points move across periodic
  representative boundaries. Smearing then follows from a compact-support
  Riemann sum.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Interpolation and smearing><label|lem:E3.2> For fixed
    <math|p,n,v,\<b-l\>> and <math|x<rsub|N>\<to\>x\<in\><NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>>,
    the multilinear interpolants of <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>>
    at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|N>> converge to <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>>.
    For every <math|p>-admissible separated test<nbsp><math|t>,

    <\equation*>
      t*\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v>\<in\>L<rsup|1>,<space|2em>a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>t<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y|)>\<longrightarrow\><big|int>t*\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the placement and moment conventions of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D03>, admissibility from
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:continuum>, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1>. Choose a compact neighbourhood
    of<nbsp><math|x> inside the periodic noncollision domain. Every vertex
    used by the interpolant at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|N>> lies there eventually
    and tends to<nbsp><math|x>. The interpolant is a convex combination of
    the vertex values. Uniform convergence at these vertices and continuity
    of the limiting derivative give the moving-point assertion. Work on the
    periodic lift throughout; no continuity of the choice of centered
    representatives is needed.

    The closed support of<nbsp><math|t> is a compact subset of that domain,
    strictly inside the chosen period box. The smooth limiting derivative is
    bounded there, so its product with<nbsp><math|t> is integrable. On a
    fixed compact neighbourhood, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1> makes the
    supremum of the difference between the lattice values and the limiting
    derivative tend to zero. Its contribution to the sum is at most that
    supremum times <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|t|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> times
    the total volume of the grid cubes meeting the support. This volume is
    bounded independently of<nbsp><math|N>. The remaining sum is the Riemann
    sum for the continuous compactly supported function
    <math|t*\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v>> and converges to its
    integral.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>>
    Set <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>=f<rsub|p,v>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1> gives its smoothness on the full periodic
    noncollision domain, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> gives the
    asserted interpolation and smearing limits for every fixed derivative
    list. Thus every inner limit in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>
    exists at every admissible fixed volume along the full cutoff sequence.
  </render-proof>

  <section|Infinite volume and field calculus><label|sec:partII-F>

  We pass from the fixed-volume kernels of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>
  (UV) to correlations on<nbsp><math|<R><rsup|4>>. The order of limits
  matters: the lattice spacing tends to zero at every fixed admissible
  volume, and only then does the volume increase.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1> uses Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> to choose the volume limit.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F3> passes lattice reflection positivity to the
  limit and uses the centered bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> for
  the global power bound. For derivatives of that bound,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> also uses
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.10>. Together these
  results prove Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV> (IV). The same volume
  refinement supplies the coefficient maps needed for
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> (FC). Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F4>
  controls repeated insertions and will be used to construct the bounded slab
  operators in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>.

  There are two compactness arguments. The bounds of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> (UB), including one additional
  difference, make the ultraviolet kernels and their derivatives
  equicontinuous on compact separated sets. A countable diagonal extraction
  therefore chooses the volume limit for all polynomial fields.
  Finite-dimensional compactness also retains certain corrected coefficient
  maps. These maps are needed because a positional difference changes the
  degree and the insertion normalization; the polynomial embedding between
  two coefficient spaces need not preserve correlations.

  The identification of null coefficients is the additional difficulty. An
  approximation to a derivative may have unbounded coefficients. We normalize
  its projected part and use correlation tests to exclude a nonzero null
  vector in the range of the limiting projection. This produces one fixed
  coefficient vector that represents the derivative against every spectator.
  Finally, chessboard estimates give bounds uniform in the positions,
  including configurations of arbitrarily large diameter. For repeated words,
  two cell sizes keep the cost of the exterior independent of the number of
  repetitions.

  Throughout we use the Wilson family, tokens, differences and separated
  tests of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:continuum>. Write
  <math|f<rsub|p,v>=M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>> and
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>=<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>.
  All constants below may depend on the fixed construction. Any further
  dependence is indicated.

  <subsection|Volume limits>

  Fix a strictly increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>:<N>\<to\><N>>. For
  each<nbsp><math|p,n,v>, let <math|S<rsub|v><rsup|<around|(|p|)>>> be the
  continuous restriction of<nbsp><math|f<rsub|p,v>> to the periodic generic
  domain

  <\equation*>
    <around|{|x:x<rsub|i><rsup|a>-x<rsub|j><rsup|a>\<nin\>L<rsub|p><Z><space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>j,a=1,\<ldots\>,4|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  These restrictions retain the fixed-volume kernels required in IV. The next
  extraction chooses the limiting table and volume sequence together, with
  convergence of all derivatives.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Generic volume kernels><label|lem:F1> There are a strictly
    increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>> and a complex multilinear
    table<nbsp><math|S>. Put <math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>>. For
    every<nbsp><math|n,v,\<b-l\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-conf>S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;\<cdot\>|)>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>,v>\<longrightarrow\>\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;\<cdummy\>|)><space|1em><text|locally
      uniformly on ><Conf|n><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Every compact <math|K\<subset\><Conf|n>> eventually lies
    in<nbsp><math|<NoHit|L<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>>|n>>. For every separated
    test<nbsp><math|f>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-ordered>C<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>\<longrightarrow\><big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
    </equation>

    Both convergences persist under further refinement.
  </lemma>

  The proof below also gives eventual inclusion of every compact generic set
  in the periodic generic domains and uniform convergence
  of<nbsp><math|S<rsub|v><rsup|<around|(|P<around|(|k|)>|)>>> there. After
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>, <math|P> denotes its final refinement.

  <\proof>
    Fix a compact <math|K\<subset\><Conf|n>> and a compact neighbourhood
    <math|K<rprime|'>> contained in<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>. For all
    sufficiently large<nbsp><math|p>, <math|K<rprime|'>> lies
    in<nbsp><math|<NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>>, and its torus separation has a
    positive lower bound <math|2*r> independent of<nbsp><math|p>. UB bounds
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>><latmoment><rsub|N,p>> by
    <math|B*<around|(|v,\<b-l\>,r|)>> on lattice tuples
    near<nbsp><math|K<rprime|'>>, for all sufficiently large<nbsp><math|N>,
    uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>, the
    multilinear interpolants converge at each fixed<nbsp><math|p> to
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v>>. Consequently

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-local>sup<rsub|p\<geqslant\>p<rsub|0>>
      sup<rsub|x\<in\>K<rprime|'>><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*<around|(|v,\<b-l\>,r|)>.
    </equation>

    The ultraviolet threshold for taking this limit may depend
    on<nbsp><math|p>. One more derivative bounds variation along short
    segments in<nbsp><math|K<rprime|'>>, giving equicontinuity
    on<nbsp><math|K>.

    Apply Arzelà\UAscoli, in the form for a pointwise bounded equicontinuous
    sequence of continuous complex functions on a compact metric
    space<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorem<nbsp>7.25>. Diagonalize over
    compact exhaustions of<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>, arities, degree lists, basis
    coefficient lists and derivative lists. These indices are countable.
    Starting from any prescribed strictly increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>>,
    we obtain a strictly increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>> and
    <math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>>. Multilinearity extends the
    limits from basis lists to every token list. Integration by parts against
    a compactly supported test identifies the limit of each first derivative
    with the derivative of the limiting kernel. Repeating this for all
    derivative lists gives a smooth multilinear table<nbsp><math|S>, with
    local convergence of every derivative.

    A compact generic set has bounded diameter and a positive minimum
    coordinate separation, so it eventually lies in the displayed periodic
    generic domain. The asserted uniform convergence
    of<nbsp><math|S<rsub|v><rsup|<around|(|P<around|(|k|)>|)>>> follows from
    the extraction. The same bounded-diameter argument puts every compact
    separated set in<nbsp><math|<NoHit|L<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>>|n>>
    eventually. For every separated test<nbsp><math|f>, UV gives, at every
    admissible<nbsp><math|p>, the full inner limit
    <math|C<rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>=<big|int>f*f<rsub|p,v>>.
    Its support is eventually admissible along<nbsp><math|P>, and local
    uniform convergence proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered>. Thus
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09> holds. Both convergences persist on
    every further volume refinement.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Symmetries of the limit.> We first prove E1\UE3 and E6, using
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>. Permutation
  symmetry, the empty correlation, deletion of the distinguished unit,
  conjugation and signed-permutation covariance hold on the lattice
  once<nbsp><math|N> exceeds the finitely many reference levels. Approximate
  any fixed separated configuration by lattice points and use
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>, then<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>. For
  translations, approximate the translation vector on the same lattice.
  Translation invariance and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-local> pass the identity to
  arbitrary real translations. This proves E1\UE3 independently of the
  coefficient corrections. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> gives E6;
  multilinearity was retained in the extraction.

  <subsection|Interior roots and errors>

  We use the chessboard root of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>. A signed
  image means the image under<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>; conjugation is also
  allowed. Bond tilings, anchors, margins and small errors are specified
  before Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small>. We consider tilings at the
  construction levels. Two such tilings are aligned when their cells have the
  same coordinate axes and every coarse boundary is a fine boundary. Their
  side ratio is an odd integer, since the block ratios are odd.

  For interior functions in the base cell of a bond
  tiling<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\>>, the chessboard inequality reads

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-chess><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-T\>><around|(|F<rsub|C>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-T\>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|C>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Apply the abstract chessboard theorem<nbsp><cite-detail|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978|Theorem<nbsp>4.1>
  axis by axis, on real vector spaces of complex observables, with reflection
  followed by conjugation. The functional has<nbsp><math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|k>>
  factors in each axis, an even number since<nbsp><math|4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>>.
  Lattice translation invariance gives cyclicity. Reflection positivity
  across every translated band cut gives the required Schwarz inequality. For
  the Wilson action this is Osterwalder\USchrader positivity on the periodic
  lattice<nbsp><cite-detail|Seiler1982|Theorem<nbsp>2.1, pp.<nbsp>14--15>,
  applied to a compact matrix gauge group, positive coupling, and observables
  supported on one side of the bond planes. Their boundaries pass through
  link midpoints, as required there.

  The centered root estimate also controls anchors near arbitrary separated
  configurations: this is the interior root bound,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>.
  Write <math|v=<around|(|d,c|)>> for the insertion token.

  Small errors are defined by a root tolerance chosen before the cell scale.
  This order makes them negligible against each fixed spectator test.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Vanishing errors><label|lem:F1b> For every small
    error<nbsp><math|E> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small>, fixed
    token list<nbsp><math|v>, distinguished slot<nbsp><math|i>,
    and<nbsp><math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|C*<around|(|v,\<delta\>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that, eventually
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-small><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h\<ne\>i>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|h>,y<rsub|h>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|v,\<delta\>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|N,p>*<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Consequently its smear against any fixed separated test tends to zero in
    that order.
  </lemma>

  The proof uses distinct cells for the error and each spectator.

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, the centered estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>, Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess>.
    Let<nbsp><math|n> be the arity, and take the arity parameter
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-margin> equal to<nbsp><math|n>. Take<nbsp><math|m>
    with<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>\<delta\>>; the small-error condition
    gives<nbsp><math|k\<geqslant\>m>. Here<nbsp><math|m> only imposes a lower
    bound on<nbsp><math|k>; the tiling has side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>. Choose
    a finer spectator level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>
    with<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/<around|[|8*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> bounds all signed spectator roots and
    their images under conjugation there by a fixed constant.

    In each axis choose the translation of the level-<math|k> tiling so that
    no boundary meets a strip of half-width<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>/<around|[|4*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>
    around any anchor. The excluded length is at
    most<nbsp><math|n*a<rsub|k>/<around|[|2*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>\<less\>a<rsub|k>>,
    so such a lattice translation exists for sufficiently
    large<nbsp><math|N>. It supplies the integer
    margins<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-margin>, including their extra lattice unit,
    and margin<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>> for spectators. Any
    pair of anchors differs in some coordinate by at
    least<nbsp><math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>a<rsub|k>>, so they occupy distinct
    cells. Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> to each spectator with its
    levels<nbsp><math|<around|(|m,k|)>> equal
    to<nbsp><math|<around|(|k,k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>|)>>
    here;<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> then gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-small>. On a
    compact test support, the number of lattice tuples multiplied
    by<nbsp><math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>> is bounded independently
    of<nbsp><math|N,p>. Multiplication by the test supremum therefore proves
    the smeared assertion.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Corrected coefficients>

  We now use the equation projections and corrections of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.9>, and the relative
  rank comparison of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10>. The other inputs are
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB>, Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08>,
  <reference|def:D10> and<nbsp><reference|def:D11>,
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>, <reference|lem:E2.2>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>,
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>, <reference|lem:F1a>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b>, and E1\UE3 established above.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests> supplies the free test rows
  independently of the interacting arguments here. Auxiliary coefficient
  limits may use a common spacing subsequence<nbsp><math|<math-ss|n>> at
  every<nbsp><math|p>; the full ultraviolet kernel limits remain those of UV.

  These estimates distinguish coefficients that vanish in every separated
  correlation. The next lemma constructs the maps asserted in FC and proves
  its dimension equality. The coefficient maps have types
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|d,e>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|e>> for
  <math|d\<leqslant\>e> and <math|\<tau\><rsub|d,a>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d+1>>.
  For a finite-dimensional real or complex coefficient space<nbsp><math|E>, a
  readout <math|R<rsub|N,p>\<in\>E> records finitely many coefficients. It is
  bounded if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B>
  eventually in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>, for some finite<nbsp><math|B>. Its
  cluster limits will retain auxiliary coefficients with the same
  table<nbsp><math|S> and volume sequence<nbsp><math|P>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Quotient compatibility><label|lem:F2> A strictly increasing
    refinement of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>> admits linear maps
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|d,e>,\<tau\><rsub|d,a>> with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-calculus>S*<around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|d,e>*c,\<ldots\>|)>=S*<around|(|c,\<ldots\>|)>,<space|2em>S*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|d,a>*c,\<ldots\>|)>=\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|a>>*S*<around|(|c,\<ldots\>|)>.
    </equation>

    They induce <math|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>,\<partial\><rsub|a>> of FC, and
    <math|dim<rsub|<C>> F<rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>. Inclusions
    preserve correlations and units and compose compatibly. Both commute with
    conjugation, and

    <\equation*>
      h*\<partial\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|b>h<rsub|b*a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*h*<space|2em><around|(|h\<in\><hypercubic>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Each <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>> is injective; every response lies in one
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>*F<rsub|d>>; every field has a token
    representative. The unit is <math|<unitfield|d>=<around|[|a<rsub|<reflevel|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>|]>>.
    Bounded readouts converge along each ultrafilter containing the ordered
    tails.
  </lemma>

  The proof below constructs the inclusions and derivatives asserted in FC,
  proves its dimension equality, and then derives the compatibility
  properties. E1\UE3 are already available.

  <\proof>
    We use the inputs listed above. Bounded coefficient families
    satisfy<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-subpower>. In fact
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> bounds their inverse terminal coordinates
    by a fixed norm factor times <math|<big|prod><rsub|m\<less\>N>C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>,
    and the constant choices in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> give
    <math|log C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>*log
    \<ell\><rsub|m>>. Split the sum at a fixed level. Its finite initial part
    is<nbsp><math|o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>>, while its tail divided
    by<nbsp><math|s<rsub|N>=<big|sum><rsub|m\<less\>N>log \<ell\><rsub|m>> is
    bounded by the largest<nbsp><math|J<rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>> in that tail.
    Sending the splitting level to infinity gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|m\<less\>N>log C<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>>.
    There are countably many projection matrices and comparison coefficient
    lists. The uniform bound in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6> gives
    convergent subsequences by finite-dimensional compactness. The same
    countable diagonal extraction retains every projection, each comparison
    lift and each coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>, <math|k\<in\><N>>,
    which lie in the bounded bands <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|k>> by
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>, first in<nbsp><math|N> at
    every<nbsp><math|p>, then in volume, retaining<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>.
    Thus, in operator norm,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-projections>\<Pi\><rsub|d,<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>,p>\<longrightarrow\>\<Pi\><rsub|d><rsup|<around|(|p|)>>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|d><rsup|<around|(|P<around|(|k|)>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>\<Pi\><rsub|d>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|d><rsup|2>=\<Pi\><rsub|d><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Continuity of composition gives idempotence at both stages. For later
    use, the convergence also holds in the order<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>.

    Let <math|L<rsub|N,p>:V<rsub|d>\<to\><C>> be any fixed one-slot smeared
    correlation map, with arbitrary fixed spectators and test. Its
    operator-norm limit is<nbsp><math|L>, by finite dimensionality
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered>. Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-small> to <math|c-\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>*c>. This gives
    <math|L*<around|(|<around|(|1-\<Pi\><rsub|d>|)>*c|)>=0> for
    every<nbsp><math|L>. Since<nbsp><math|S> is smooth
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-null>ker \<Pi\><rsub|d>\<subset\><nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>.
    </equation>

    For the reverse dimension inequality, use the rank-<math|q<rsub|d>> free
    matrix<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>
    and the common lifts of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10>.
    Rearrange<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative> to
    isolate<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|I>>. Every remaining term
    contains a nonempty<nbsp><math|R<rsub|J>>; its expectation is bounded
    by<nbsp><math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>> using<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess>. Thus

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-rank><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|h\<in\>I>I<rsub|h>|\<rangle\>>-\<cal-G\><rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|I>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<big|sum><rsub|J\<subsetneq\>I><around|\||\<cal-G\><rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|J>|\|>.
    </equation>

    The finite sum stays bounded as <math|y<rsup|\<nu\>,i>\<to\>y<rsup|i>>.
    Pass through both coefficient and kernel limits. Each matrix entry is a
    fixed spectator functional on<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>>, applied to the
    limiting lift of<nbsp><math|m<rsub|j>>. That functional vanishes
    on<nbsp><math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>>, so each matrix factors
    through<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>> and has rank at most<nbsp><math|dim
    F<rsub|d>>. The matrices tend to<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\>>. A nonzero
    <math|q<rsub|d>>-minor therefore gives <math|dim
    F<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>q<rsub|d>>. Ranks of converging idempotents are
    eventually constant: if <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P-Q|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1>,
    <math|P> is injective on<nbsp><math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>Q>, and
    conversely. Passing the dimension inequality in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6> to<nbsp><math|\<Pi\><rsub|d>>
    using<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-projections> and using<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-null> now
    gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-dimension><nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>=ker
      \<Pi\><rsub|d>,<space|2em>dim<rsub|<C>> F<rsub|d>=q<rsub|d>.
    </equation>

    For inclusion, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.8> gives a corrected family
    that has target smeared limit<nbsp><math|<big|int>f*S*<around|(|c,\<ldots\>|)>>.
    For the corrected difference of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.9>, torus
    summation by parts gives exactly

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-parts>a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>f<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|i,a><latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y><frac|f*<around|(|<wide|y|^>-a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|i,a>|)>-f<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)>|a<rsub|N>><latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|e<rsub|i,a>> is the coordinate vector in slot<nbsp><math|i>,
    axis<nbsp><math|a>. The backward quotient tends uniformly
    to<nbsp><math|-\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*f>; its residual is bounded by
    <math|a<rsub|N>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsub|i,a><rsup|2>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>/2>.
    UB at order zero controls the residual sum on a fixed compact
    neighbourhood. Thus the derivative target is
    <math|-<big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*f|)>*S*<around|(|c,\<ldots\>|)>>.

    For either corrected family<nbsp><math|w<rsub|N,p>> in its representing
    degree<nbsp><math|e>, write <math|\<Pi\>=\<Pi\><rsub|e,N,p>>. The
    normalized remainder <math|<around|(|w-\<Pi\>*w|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*w|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    is admissible and belongs to<nbsp><math|ker \<Pi\>>: its first term is a
    bounded scalar multiple of an admissible family, and its second term is
    bounded. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-normalized>L<rsub|N,p>*<around*|(|<around|(|w-\<Pi\>*w|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*w|\<\|\|\>>|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation>

    The ordered tails

    <\equation*>
      <around|{|<around|(|k,j|)>:k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>,j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>|}>
    </equation*>

    form a filter base. An iterated limit <math|x<rsub|k,j>\<to\>x<rsub|k>>
    as <math|j\<to\>\<infty\>>, followed by <math|x<rsub|k>\<to\>x>,
    converges to<nbsp><math|x> on this filter:
    given<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, first make
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k>-x|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>/2> for
    <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>, then make
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x<rsub|k,j>-x<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>/2>
    for <math|j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>>.
    Consequently<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-projections> and the fixed-volume kernel
    limits give <math|\<Pi\><rsub|e,N,p>\<to\>\<Pi\><rsub|e>> and
    <math|L<rsub|N,p>\<to\>L> in norm on this filter, where
    <math|N=<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>>, <math|p=P<around|(|k|)>>. The
    corrected inclusion or difference and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> also
    give <math|L<rsub|N,p><around|(|w|)>\<to\>t<rsub|L>>, its target value,
    for every spectator test.

    Fix any ultrafilter<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> containing the ordered tails.
    Every bounded readout lies eventually in a compact closed ball of its
    coefficient space and therefore has a unique limit
    along<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>>. The same<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> serves all
    readouts; continuous identities and closed inequalities valid eventually
    pass to their limits. The kernel and projection limits already
    established on the ordered tails persist along<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>>, so
    neither<nbsp><math|S> nor<nbsp><math|P> changes.

    The pairs <math|<around|(|<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*w|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>,\<Pi\>*w/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*w|\<\|\|\>>|)>|)>>
    lie in the compact product of<nbsp><math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and the closed
    unit ball of<nbsp><math|V<rsub|e>>, so they converge
    along<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> to some<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<lambda\>,z|)>>.
    Set <math|b<rsub|N,p>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*w|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Passing the identity <math|\<Pi\>*<around|(|b<rsub|N,p>*\<Pi\>*w|)>=b<rsub|N,p>*\<Pi\>*w>
    and the norm identity to the limit gives <math|\<Pi\><rsub|e>*z=z> and
    <math|\<lambda\>+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>. The spectator limits
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-normalized> hold along<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>>, so

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=lim<rsub|\<cal-U\>>
        b<rsub|N,p>*L<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<Pi\>*w|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=lim<rsub|\<cal-U\>><around*|[|b<rsub|N,p>*L<rsub|N,p><around|(|w|)>-L<rsub|N,p>*<around*|(|b<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|w-\<Pi\>*w|)>|)>|]>=\<lambda\>*t<rsub|L>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    If <math|\<lambda\>=0>, then <math|L<around|(|z|)>=0> for every spectator
    test, hence <math|z\<in\><nullspace|e><around|(|S|)>=ker \<Pi\><rsub|e>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension>. Together with <math|\<Pi\><rsub|e>*z=z>,
    this gives <math|z=0>, contradicting <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>.
    Thus <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>, and <math|z/\<lambda\>> represents the
    target for all spectators simultaneously. Choose these representatives on
    a basis and extend linearly, obtaining <math|\<kappa\><rsub|d,e>>
    and<nbsp><math|\<tau\><rsub|d,a>> with<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> converts the distributional identities to
    pointwise identities.

    These maps preserve null coefficients, so they descend
    to<nbsp><math|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>> and<nbsp><math|\<partial\><rsub|a>>. A
    response vanishes precisely on<nbsp><math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>>;
    hence<nbsp><math|\<iota\><rsub|d>> is injective. Equality of responses
    gives injectivity, composition and uniqueness of inclusions. E1 and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a> give preservation
    of<nbsp><math|<around|[|a<rsub|<reflevel|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>|]>>. Any
    finite sum of responses lies in the largest degree involved; quotient
    surjectivity gives token representatives. Finally E2, conjugation and the
    chain rule applied to <math|S*<around|(|h*c;h*x|)>=S*<around|(|c;x|)>>
    give the asserted symmetry identities by injectivity of the response
    maps. The same comparison without differentiation gives
    <math|h*\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>=\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>*h>. Permuting the
    distinguished slot proves FC's positional identity in every slot. This
    proves FC and the lemma using Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.10>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Euclidean properties>

  The lattice symmetries and positivity pass through the ordered limits. The
  centered root estimate gives the additional control as separated points
  approach one another.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Euclidean properties><label|lem:F3> The table supplied by IV is
    complex multilinear and satisfies E1\UE7, including joint-test reflection
    positivity, absolute integrability of its pairings, and bounds for every
    derivative order:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|x><rsup|\<nu\>>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|n,d,c,\<nu\>|)>*r<rsup|-A*<around|(|n,d,c,\<nu\>|)>>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1,<sepinf><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>r|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  The proof below passes reflection positivity to the limit and proves the
  power bound; its positive derivative orders use<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus>.

  <\proof>
    We use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08> and<nbsp><reference|def:D10>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>, <reference|lem:E2.3>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>, equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>, and the
    derivative maps<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus> from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. E1\UE3 and E6 were proved after
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>; only the positive derivative orders of E7
    use the corrected maps.

    We prove E5 and deduce E4. Fix finitely many joint smooth
    tests<nbsp><math|f<rsub|i>> with compact support on positive-side
    separated configurations in one axis. Their lattice
    smears<nbsp><math|F<rsub|i>> are supported strictly inside the positive
    half-torus for large<nbsp><math|p,N>: the support has positive distance
    from the reflection plane, and the physical radius of each fixed stencil
    tends to zero. Use the bond reflection<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|N>-x<rsup|a>>,
    with planes<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>=a<rsub|N>/2>
    and<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>=<around|(|a<rsub|N>+L<rsub|p>|)>/2>. It is a
    lattice symmetry at every cutoff. For large<nbsp><math|p>, the supports
    also avoid a neighbourhood of the second
    plane<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>=L<rsub|p>/2>; for large<nbsp><math|N> their
    stencils avoid both actual bond planes. The periodic Wilson reflection
    positivity cited before<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|z|\<bar\>><rsub|i>*z<rsub|j><around|\<langle\>|<wide|F<rsub|i>\<circ\><math-ss|r><rsub|a,N>|\<bar\>>F<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>\<geqslant\>0.
    </equation*>

    Here<nbsp><math|<math-ss|r><rsub|a,N>> maps a configuration to its
    reflected configuration. Insertion covariance reflects the positions and
    applies<nbsp><math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|refl>>> to their
    labels; conjugation acts on the labels in the first word. The reflected
    joint test converges, with all derivatives,
    to<nbsp><math|<wide|f|\<bar\>><rsub|i>\<otimes\>f<rsub|j>> with its first
    group reflected through<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>=0>. Its support is compact
    and separated, including across the reflection plane. All these supports
    lie in one compact separated set. UB at order zero bounds the difference
    between the moving tests and their limiting test by their uniform
    difference times a bounded weighted site count. It tends to zero. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered> to the
    fixed limiting test in each entry. Smoothness bounds the kernel on the
    support, so every pairing is absolutely integrable. The limiting matrix
    is positive semidefinite. Concentrating normalized smooth bumps at
    prescribed positive-side words gives E4 by continuity. This works in
    every axis with its corresponding reflection.

    For the power bound first consider order zero and a nonempty token list.
    Put <math|d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>=1+max<rsub|i> d<rsub|i>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> with <math|\<varepsilon\>=1>, for all the
    finitely many signed images and images under conjugation, bounds their
    centered roots by <math|C<rsub|<math-up|ctr>>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>>
    for <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>. The constants
    <math|k<rsub|0>,C<rsub|<math-up|ctr>>> precede the choice of separation.
    The scale condition <math|log \<ell\><rsub|u>/s<rsub|u>\<to\>0> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-thickness>a<rsub|u+1>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>*a<rsub|u><rsup|2>.
    </equation>

    Indeed <math|log \<ell\><rsub|u>/log a<rsub|u><rsup|-1>\<to\>0> implies
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|u>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|u><rsup|-1>> eventually;
    choose<nbsp><math|c<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>\<gtr\>0> to include the
    remaining levels. The first <math|u\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>> with
    <math|a<rsub|u>\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1> consequently satisfies
    <math|a<rsub|u>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|mesh>,k<rsub|0>>*\<delta\><rsup|2>>,
    including <math|u=k<rsub|0>>.

    Choose the first <math|m\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>> with
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r/2>, then the first <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>>
    with <math|a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|m>/<around|[|6*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>.
    Thus <math|k\<geqslant\>m> and <math|a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>c<rprime|'><around|(|n|)>*r<rsup|4>>,
    with <math|c<rprime|'><around|(|n|)>\<gtr\>0> also depending
    on<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>. In each coordinate the boundary strips of
    half-width<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k>> excluded by the<nbsp><math|n> anchors
    have total length less than<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>>. Choose a translated
    tiling outside them. All anchors occupy distinct cells.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>, followed by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess>,
    gives the lattice bound <math|<around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|ctr>>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|n>>.
    Passing to the limits by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>, with slightly smaller separation to
    accommodate lattice approximation, gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-power><around|\||S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|n,d,c|)>*r<rsup|-4*d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>*n>.
    </equation>

    All constants are independent of the positions and the volume.

    For higher orders use<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus>: every coordinate
    derivative is a correlation with fixed, degree-raised tokens. At a fixed
    order there are finitely many such lists. Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-power>
    to each, take the largest exponent, and bound the Fréchet operator norm
    by the sum of its coordinate coefficients in an orthonormal basis
    of<nbsp><math|<R><rsup|4*n>>. This proves E7 using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> and hence
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.10>. For arity zero,
    <math|S<rsub|0>=1> and all positive-order derivatives vanish.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV>>
    Compose the volume refinements in Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered> proves
    ordered convergence, retaining UV's full inner limit at every admissible
    volume. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F3> proves E1\UE7. The coefficient
    corrections do not change any kernel limit already obtained.
  </render-proof>

  <subsection|Repeated reflected words>

  Use the OS space of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14>. For any
  <math|s\<gtr\>0> and word<nbsp><math|w> with times in
  <math|<around|(|0,s|)>>, set

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|s*e<rsub|4>>*v,<space|2em>w<rsup|\<dagger\>>=\<tau\><rsub|s*e<rsub|4>><math-ss|s><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|refl>>w<rsup|\<ast\>>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|A<rsub|w>> initially acts on words; boundedness on OS classes is
  not assumed. A buffer<nbsp><math|r> means pairwise sup-distance at
  least<nbsp><math|r> and times in <math|<around|[|r,h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>-r|]>>
  for a slab of width<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>>.

  Fix a degree bound<nbsp><math|d> and coefficient
  bound<nbsp><math|R\<geqslant\>0>, and put <math|Q=4*<around|(|d+1|)>>. The
  next estimate bounds the growth of repeated insertion before taking any
  operator extension.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Repeated reflected words><label|lem:F4> There is
    <math|C<rsub|0>=C<rsub|0>*<around|(|d,R|)>\<gtr\>0> such that every
    nonempty length-<math|n> word<nbsp><math|w> with these bounds and
    buffer<nbsp><math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1> in a slab of
    width<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>> admits
    <math|s=n<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*a<rsub|k>\<in\><around|(|0,h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>|]>>,
    <math|k,n<rsub|<math-up|lat>>\<in\><N>>, for which<nbsp><math|w> has
    buffer<nbsp><math|r/2> in the slab of width<nbsp><math|s>. For every
    separated exterior word<nbsp><math|v> with positive times,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|OS>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|v>*<around|[|C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|Q*n>|]><rsup|4*j>*<space|2em><around|(|j\<geqslant\>0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|B<rsub|v>=B<rsub|v>*<around|(|w,r,s|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> is
    independent of<nbsp><math|j>.
  </lemma>

  The operators in the statement use the chosen width<nbsp><math|s>. Its
  choice and the constants <math|C<rsub|0>,Q> precede<nbsp><math|v>.

  <\proof>
    We use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08> and<nbsp><reference|def:D14>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> and<nbsp><reference|lem:F3>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess>, <eqref|eq:F-conf>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-thickness>. Enlarge the coefficient ball to contain
    every signed image and image under conjugation in degrees at
    most<nbsp><math|d>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>, with
    <math|\<varepsilon\>=1>, gives <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\><N>> and
    <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> such that all these centered roots at
    levels <math|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>> are bounded
    by <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>>,
    where <math|d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>=d+1>. This choice
    precedes<nbsp><math|n,r,w>.

    Choose the first <math|m\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>> with
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r/32>, and put
    <math|s=a<rsub|m>*<around|\<lfloor\>|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>/a<rsub|m>|\<rfloor\>>>.
    The word is nonempty with buffer<nbsp><math|r>, so <math|s\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|s\<leqslant\>h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>>, and it still
    contains<nbsp><math|w> with buffer at least<nbsp><math|r/2>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-thickness> gives
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|1>*r<rsup|2>>. Fix
    <math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>\<leqslant\>1> with
    <math|20*\<alpha\>\<less\>c<rsub|1>>, and choose the first
    <math|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>> with
    <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>\<less\>\<alpha\>*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|2>>.
    Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-repeat-scales>20*n*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>\<less\>a<rsub|m>,<space|2em>a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|2>*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em><around|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|n>\<leqslant\><around|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*c<rsub|2><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|4*d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>*n>.
    </equation>

    These choices precede the exterior. In the statement take <math|k=m> and
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=<around|\<lfloor\>|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>/a<rsub|m>|\<rfloor\>>>.

    Expanding the OS norm gives a correlation of <math|4*j*n> main insertions
    and two copies of<nbsp><math|v>. The main insertions are mutually
    separated, and separated from the exterior, by at least<nbsp><math|r/2>.
    Translations by multiples of<nbsp><math|s> do not change residues
    modulo<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m><Z><rsup|4>>. Thus the main anchors have at
    most<nbsp><math|2*n> residue patterns, those of<nbsp><math|w> and its
    reflection with conjugation, independently of<nbsp><math|j>.

    For nonempty<nbsp><math|v>, let <math|t<rsub|min>\<gtr\>0> be its minimum
    time and choose <math|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>\<geqslant\>k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>
    with <math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<less\>2*t<rsub|min>> and
    <math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> smaller than every distance between
    distinct anchors of<nbsp><math|v>. The two exterior copies have
    times<nbsp><math|\<pm\><around|(|2*j*s+t|)>>, where<nbsp><math|t> is a
    time of<nbsp><math|v>. Their mutual distance is at
    least<nbsp><math|2*t<rsub|min>\<gtr\>a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>>, for
    every<nbsp><math|j>. Next choose <math|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>\<geqslant\>m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>,
    beyond the exterior reference levels, with
    <math|12<around|\||v|\|>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<less\>a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> gives a common finite
    bound<nbsp><math|X\<geqslant\>0> for all exterior roots and their images
    at level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>.
    Both<nbsp><math|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>,k<rsub|<math-up|v>>> are independent
    of<nbsp><math|j>.

    We apply the chessboard estimate at two cell sizes. In each axis, avoid
    strips of half-width<nbsp><math|5*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>> around
    the<nbsp><math|2*n> main residues modulo<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>>. This is
    possible by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-repeat-scales>. Then change the translation
    by less than<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> to place
    all<nbsp><math|2<around|\||v|\|>> exterior anchors at distance at
    least<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> from the finer
    boundaries; <math|12<around|\||v|\|>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<less\>a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>>
    permits this. Since <math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>>,
    the main anchors retain margin at least<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>>.
    Main insertions occupy distinct coarse cells, none containing an exterior
    anchor.

    Several exterior factors may occupy one coarse cell. Reflect their
    product into every coarse cell, and apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> to the
    aligned tiling of side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>>. The fine
    and coarse tilings share an origin and have an odd integer side ratio.
    Coarse reflections preserve the fine grid and its interior margins.
    Within each reflected coarse cell, exterior anchors are separated by more
    than<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> and so occupy distinct fine
    cells; across coarse boundaries their fine cells are disjoint. The
    uncentered estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> bounds every factor
    by<nbsp><math|X>. If the coarse cell contains <math|n<rsub|C>> exterior
    factors, its root is therefore at most<nbsp><math|X<rsup|n<rsub|C>>>.
    Applying the coarse chessboard estimate now gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|OS>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>*<around|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|4*j*n>.
    </equation*>

    For each fixed<nbsp><math|j>, take lattice approximations of all anchors,
    preserving these strict margins and separations, then apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>. No cutoff
    threshold uniform in<nbsp><math|j> is needed. The constants on the right
    are already independent of<nbsp><math|j>. Choose <math|B<rsub|v>=max
    <around|(|1,X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>|)>>,
    <math|C<rsub|0>=B<rsub|\<ast\>>*c<rsub|2><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>>,
    and <math|Q=4*d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-repeat-scales> proves the assertion. For
    empty<nbsp><math|v> omit its factors and take <math|B<rsub|v>=1>.
  </proof>

  <section|Free fields, Ward identities and rotations><label|sec:partII-G>

  The stress tensor is determined by its action on local fields. Conservation
  alone leaves its scale and sign undetermined. We first compare the mixed
  correlations with the Maxwell correlations of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11>. This comparison identifies a
  one-dimensional space of conserved tensors. Integrating a member of that
  space over a small shell gives local translation and angular charges. We
  then identify these charges with the derivatives and rotation generators of
  the fields.

  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>, <reference|lem:G3.2>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.3> prove, respectively,
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>, Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST> and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>. The free calculation in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> also supplies the positivity used by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>. Its proof is independent of the
  interacting quotient and hence of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>.

  Two issues require more than convergence at fixed separated points. The
  lattice comparison must control tests uniformly in their supremum norm,
  including near configurations with a shared coordinate. We remove narrow
  strips around these configurations, using two different scales for the
  bound on the kernel and its variation. Later, conservation removes the
  inner boundaries in the angular identity, but does not by itself remove the
  boundary at infinity. An anisotropic cutoff, reflection positivity and a
  low-energy estimate remove that boundary for collinear configurations.
  Analyticity of one-insertion vectors then extends the identity to arbitrary
  separated configurations.

  Except in the free calculation, fix the table, volume refinement, quotient
  fields and calculus of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D09>,
  <reference|def:D10>, <reference|def:D11> and
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>, <reference|lem:F2>, <reference|lem:F3>.
  Subsequences below change only comparison radii. Write
  <math|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>> for the inclusions and
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>> for field differentiation. The coordinate
  Hilbert spaces used to remove the exterior boundary are obtained from
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> by changing the time axis; their spectral
  measures are defined in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-measure>. Their
  construction uses coordinate reflections and precedes the use of continuous
  rotations.

  <subsection|Free fields><label|sec:partII-G.1>

  Put <math|z=x-y> and <math|<math-ss|h><rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<Gamma\><around|(|z|)>>.
  The curvature covariance in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-curvature>C<rsub|a*b,i*j><around|(|z|)>=-\<delta\><rsub|b*j><math-ss|h><rsub|a*i>+\<delta\><rsub|b*i><math-ss|h><rsub|a*j>+\<delta\><rsub|a*j><math-ss|h><rsub|b*i>-\<delta\><rsub|a*i><math-ss|h><rsub|b*j>,<space|2em><math-ss|h><rsub|a*b>=<frac|8*z<rsub|a>*z<rsub|b>-2*\<delta\><rsub|a*b><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|6>>.
  </equation>

  Different colours have zero covariance. Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-free-tensor>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|a*b,i*j>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<alpha\>>:,<space|2em>P<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|c>Q<rsub|a*c,b*c>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>=<big|sum><rsub|a>P<rsub|a*a>,>>|<row|<cell|<maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>>|<cell|=-P<rsub|a*b>+<tfrac|1|4>\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>,<space|2em><curvaturesquare>=<tfrac|1|4>Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  The minus sign is fixed by the shell convention in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>.

  For the coordinate reflection <math|s<rsub|a>>, strict reflected positivity
  means

  <\equation*>
    <maxwellcorr><rsub|2><around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|s<rsub|a>|)>*<wide|u|\<bar\>>,u;s<rsub|a>*x,x|)>\<gtr\>0*<space|2em><around|(|u\<in\><maxwellspace|d>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>,x=t*e<rsub|a>,t\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|<math-ss|L><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>> for the
  derivative at zero of <math|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>>.
  The two free shell normalizations, with the fluxes of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>, are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-free-shells>\<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>>=S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*u|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-v\><around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|K><rsup|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>>=S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|L><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>*u|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-v\><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  The next lemma gives both the nondegeneracy of free correlation tests and
  the normalization used in the interacting comparison. Its proof follows
  immediately below.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free positivity and stress><label|lem:G1.1> The Maxwell table
    satisfies E1\UE7, the field calculus and <math|O<around|(|4|)>>
    covariance. Every nonzero on-shell invariant Wick polynomial has a
    strictly positive reflected two-point Gram value at distinct reflected
    points. For simple<nbsp><math|<g>>, its centered real symmetric
    <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial conserved degree-four tensors form the line
    of<nbsp><math|<maxwellstress>>, normalized by both shell laws, and

    <\equation*>
      <maxwellcorr><rsub|2><around|(|<curvaturesquare>,<curvaturesquare>;x,y|)>=<frac|3<dimG>|\<pi\><rsup|4>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|8>>*<space|2em><around|(|x\<ne\>y|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11>; no interacting input is needed.
    Wick correlations sum pairings between different insertion sites.
    Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature> satisfies Bianchi and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*C<rsub|a*b,i*j>=0> off zero,
    so these correlations descend to the on-shell quotient. It also gives
    permutation symmetry, reality, units, translations and orthogonal
    covariance. Each differentiated contraction is homogeneous of a fixed
    negative degree and bounded on the unit sphere. A finite product of these
    bounds proves E6\UE7. Differentiating the pairings proves the derivative
    law; inclusions retain the same polynomial.

    Here is the nondegeneracy calculation, including the equations imposed on
    jets. Let <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|j>> be the real space of <math|<g>>-valued
    homogeneous polynomial two-forms of degree<nbsp><math|j> satisfying
    <math|d*f=\<delta\>*f=0>. Its Frobenius inner product identifies it with
    the dual of the order-<math|j> on-shell curvature jets. Use Taylor
    coefficients <math|D<rsup|j>/j>! and the inner product summing both
    ordered form indices and ordered derivative indices;
    <math|p<around|(|\<partial\>|)>> denotes contraction with this normalized
    derivative. For <math|z\<ne\>0>, put <math|I<around|(|z|)>=z/<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>
    and <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|z>=I<rsub|4>-2*z*z<rsup|<math-ss|t>>/<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>.
    Define <math|R<rsub|p><around|(|y|)>> to be the covariance of
    <math|F<around|(|y|)>> with the jet probe<nbsp><math|p> at zero. Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-kelvin>R<rsub|p><around|(|y|)>=s<rsub|j><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-4>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|y>\<otimes\>\<cal-R\><rsub|y>|)>*p<around|(|I<around|(|y|)>|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|j>=<frac|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j>|\<pi\><rsup|2>>.
    </equation>

    Indeed <math|p<around|(|\<partial\>|)>*\<Gamma\>=<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j>*p<around|(|y|)>*\<Gamma\>>;
    induction proves this identity, since traces of the coefficient tensor
    vanish by harmonicity. In taking the two curls, terms containing
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*p<rsub|a*b>> and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*p<rsub|b*c>+\<partial\><rsub|b>*p<rsub|c*a>+\<partial\><rsub|c>*p<rsub|a*b>>
    vanish. The remaining radial derivatives use
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>y<rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*p=j*p> and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j-2>=-<around|(|2*j+2|)>*y<rsub|a><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j-4>>.
    They give

    <\equation*>
      s<rsub|0>=2/\<pi\><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|(|j+1|)>*s<rsub|j+1>=-2*<around|(|j+3|)>*s<rsub|j>,
    </equation*>

    which yields the coefficient in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin>.

    For towers <math|p=<around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>> and
    <math|q=<around|(|q<rsub|j>|)>>, let <math|B<rsub|z>*<around|(|p,q|)>> be
    their two-site covariance at separation<nbsp><math|z>, with
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|z>> acting on all tensor indices of the second
    probe. This is the reflected jet pairing at <math|\<pm\>z/2>. Define the
    triangular map<nbsp><math|\<sigma\><rsub|z>> by replacing a single
    order-<math|j> probe<nbsp><math|p> with the tower whose order-<math|k>
    component is <math|<around|(|s<rsub|j>/s<rsub|k>|)>*T<rsub|k><around|[|p|(>\<cdot\>-I<around|(|z|)>)]>,
    <math|k\<leqslant\>j>, where <math|T<rsub|k>> denotes the homogeneous
    Taylor term at zero. Its generated field is the right side
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin> with <math|p*<around|(|I<around|(|y|)>-I<around|(|z|)>|)>>.
    Since <math|D*I<around|(|z|)>=<around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>*\<cal-R\><rsub|z>>,
    its Taylor terms at<nbsp><math|z> below<nbsp><math|j> vanish, and its top
    term is <math|s<rsub|j><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2*j-4>*\<cal-R\><rsub|z>*p>.
    Symmetry removes the entries above the diagonal as well. Thus

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-pivots>B<rsub|z>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|z>*p,\<sigma\><rsub|z>*q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j><frac|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*2<rsup|j>|\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*j+4>>*<around|\<langle\>|p<rsub|j>,q<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation>

    Evaluating the second order-<math|j> probe supplies
    <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j>>, since its positional derivatives are
    opposite to derivatives of the separation. This cancels the sign
    in<nbsp><math|s<rsub|j>>; reflection acts on all its tensor indices as
    specified above. All pivots are positive. In coordinates diagonalizing
    this form, Wick pairing between two sites gives
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|X<rsup|\<mu\>>,X<rsup|\<nu\>>|\<rangle\>>=\<b-1\><rsub|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>*\<mu\>!<big|prod><rsub|i>b<rsub|i><rsup|\<mu\><rsub|i>>>,
    with <math|b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. Every polynomial uses a finite tower,
    proving strict positivity.

    For several positive-side sites the reflected curvature covariance is
    positive semidefinite: spatial Fourier transformation
    of<nbsp><math|\<Gamma\>> gives <math|e<rsup|-<around|\||p|\|>*<around|(|t+s|)>>/<around|(|2<around|\||p|\|>|)>>,
    and its two curls, with reflection of the form indices, give the sum of
    squares of the two transverse polarizations. Jet derivatives
    differentiate these same factors. Include the contractions within each
    positive-side word in its Fock vector. Its reflected pairing is then the
    symmetric Fock pairing of this positive semidefinite form; hence finite
    sums of words have nonnegative squared norm. On compact separated
    supports every pairing is continuous and bounded, so integration gives E5
    and point evaluation gives E4.

    For the stress line, simplicity makes every invariant linear colour form
    zero and every invariant bilinear form a multiple of the fixed metric.
    Through weight four, only the unit and the quadratics<nbsp><math|Q>
    remain. Sign changes and permutations therefore give the following basis
    of centered real symmetric tensors:

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|a*b>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*P<rsub|a*a>,<space|2em>\<cal-B\><rsub|a*b>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>-2*P<rsub|a*a>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-D\><rsub|a*b>=<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|)>*P<rsub|a*b>.
    </equation*>

    Sign parity reduces the corresponding degree-five vectors to contractions
    of <math|F*\<partial\>*F> with the free index<nbsp><math|b>. The
    equations remove <math|<big|sum><rsub|d>\<partial\><rsub|d>*F<rsub|d*c>>;
    Bianchi gives <math|2*<big|sum><rsub|c,d>F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|c>*F<rsub|b*d>=<big|sum><rsub|c,d>F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|c*d>>.
    The two remaining sums are

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|c>:F<rsub|b*c>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|b*c>\<assign\><tfrac|1|2>\<partial\><rsub|b>*P<rsub|b*b>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|c\<less\>d,c,d\<ne\>b>:F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|c*d>\<assign\><tfrac|1|4>\<partial\><rsub|b>*<around|(|Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>-2*P<rsub|b*b>|)>,
    </equation*>

    with colour sums understood. Test the first divergence component
    at<nbsp><math|e<rsub|1>> against <math|Q<rsub|12,12><around|(|0|)>> and
    <math|Q<rsub|23,23><around|(|0|)>>, dividing by
    <math|<dimG>/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>>. For example,
    <math|S<rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|2>*<around|(|P<rsub|11>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>,Q<rsub|12,12><around|(|0|)>|)>=2<dimG>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|4>*t<rsup|8>|)>>,
    whose derivative gives the first entry below.
    Differentiating<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature> in the other entries gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-divergence-matrix><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-256>|<cell|0>|<cell|128>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|-512>|<cell|-128>>>>>.
    </equation>

    A further test of <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|12>> against <math|Q<rsub|13,23>>
    gives <math|-16> and gives zero for <math|\<cal-A\>,\<cal-B\>>. The three
    tensors are independent, the vector space has dimension two, and
    divergence is onto. Its kernel is the line of
    <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<cal-A\>-<tfrac|1|4>\<cal-B\>+\<cal-D\>=-<maxwellstress>>.

    To compute its charge, polarize the tensor<nbsp><math|<maxwellstress>>
    between a smooth Maxwell wave<nbsp><math|f> and the field generated by a
    probe at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>>. Replace <math|\<Gamma\>> by
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> with
    <math|-\<Delta\>*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,
    where <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> is a smooth approximate
    identity. For a probe <math|F<rsub|i*j>> the divergence of its generated
    field is <math|\<delta\><rsub|b*j>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>-\<delta\><rsub|b*i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>.

    Write <math|g> for the generated field and
    polarize<nbsp><math|<maxwellstress>> as
    <math|t<rsub|a*b>=-<big|sum><rsub|c><around|(|f<rsub|a*c>*g<rsub|b*c>+g<rsub|a*c>*f<rsub|b*c>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<big|sum><rsub|i,j>f<rsub|i*j>*g<rsub|i*j>>.
    Bianchi cancels its derivative terms to give
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*t<rsub|a*b>=-<big|sum><rsub|c>f<rsub|b*c>*<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*g<rsub|a*c>>.
    Hence the minus sign of the shell integral gives
    <math|<big|int>\<chi\>*v<rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*t<rsub|a*b>=-<big|int>\<chi\>*v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*c>*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|c*j>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>-\<delta\><rsub|c*i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>>.
    A second integration by parts gives

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*j>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>-\<partial\><rsub|j>*<around|(|v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*i>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=<around|(|\<cal-L\><rsub|v>*f|)><rsub|i*j><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>.
    </equation*>

    There is no derivative of<nbsp><math|\<chi\>> at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>>.
    The contraction with<nbsp><math|D*v> vanishes by symmetry. For
    <math|v=K*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-x<rsub|0>|)>> the last expression is
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|c><around|(|K<rsub|c*i>*f<rsub|c*j>+K<rsub|c*j>*f<rsub|i*c>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>>,
    the generator of the free action on curvature; for constant<nbsp><math|v>
    it is <math|v\<cdot\>\<nabla\>*f>. Higher probes follow by
    differentiating in<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>> on the compact shell. In a Wick
    contraction, two stress legs ending at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>> have zero
    shell: dilation by<nbsp><math|2> multiplies it by
    <math|2<rsup|3+p-8-j-l>\<ne\>1>, for probe orders<nbsp><math|j,l> and
    weight degree <math|p=0,1>. Two legs ending outside have zero shell by
    conservation. The remaining contractions give the polynomial
    derivation<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\><rsub|v>>,
    proving<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-shells>.

    Finally, at<nbsp><math|z=r*e<rsub|1>> the six unordered curvature
    components have diagonal covariances <math|\<pm\><around|(|\<pi\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|4>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Since <math|<curvaturesquare>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|a\<less\>b,\<alpha\>>:<around|(|F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>|)><rsup|2>>:,
    its two-point Wick sum is <math|<tfrac|1|4>\<cdot\>2\<cdot\>6<dimG>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|4>*r<rsup|8>|)>>.
    Orthogonal covariance gives the asserted formula for
    every<nbsp><math|z\<ne\>0>.
  </proof>

  The next two lemmas concern only the invariant polynomial algebra of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11>, realized by the Taylor sections of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>. A curvature jet of order<nbsp><math|j> has
  weight<nbsp><math|j+2>. Let <math|\<frak-P\>> be the complex polynomial
  algebra on all on-shell jets, and <math|\<frak-P\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j>> its
  subalgebra using orders at most<nbsp><math|j>. The invariant polynomials of
  weight at most<nbsp><math|d> identify with<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>.
  For a fixed spectator degree<nbsp><math|d>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-N\><rsub|d>=<around|{|P\<in\>\<frak-P\>:S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<around|(|0|)>,u<rsub|1><around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|n>|)>|)>=0<text|
    for all >n,u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d>,<around|(|0,x|)>\<in\><Conf|n+1>|}>.
  </equation*>

  All polynomials in this display are Wick ordered at their own site. The
  degree of<nbsp><math|P> is unrestricted. Bounded spectator degree will
  suffice to detect it.

  Let <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|d>> be the real space of complex linear tuples
  <math|P<rsub|a>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\><maxwellspace|d+1>> which commute
  with conjugation, kill the unit, are vector-covariant
  under<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, and send <math|<maxwellspace|e>>
  into<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|e+1>> for <math|e\<leqslant\>d>. For
  <math|P\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|d>>, its Ward sum is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|b><around|(|P;\<b-u\>,x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,P<rsub|b>*u<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|n>;x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The following lemma identifies its kernel using only finitely many tests on
  lines. Its proof first obtains a bounded spectator degree that detects
  arbitrarily high output jets.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite vector Ward tests><label|lem:G1-vector> For all
    sufficiently large<nbsp><math|d>, finitely many positional derivatives of
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b><around|(|P;\<b-u\>,x|)>>, with
    <math|u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d>> and<nbsp><math|x> on a coordinate
    line perpendicular to<nbsp><math|e<rsub|b>>, define a real linear
    map<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|d>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-free-tests>ker \<cal-T\><rsub|d>=<R>\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>><space|1em><text|in
      >\<cal-C\><rsub|d>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>.

    <em|Step 1: detection.> We first establish detection with one bounded
    spectator degree:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-detection>\<frak-N\><rsub|d<rsub|0>>\<cap\>\<frak-P\><rsup|G>=<around|{|0|}><space|2em><text|for
      some >d<rsub|0>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<frak-P\><rsup|G>> is the invariant subalgebra. Fix a jet
    order<nbsp><math|j>. The algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-P\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j>>
    is a polynomial algebra on finitely many variables, hence Noetherian by
    the Hilbert basis theorem<nbsp><cite-detail|Stacks2026|Lemma<nbsp>10.31.1>.
    Its ideal generated by invariant polynomials of positive ordinary
    polynomial degree therefore has finitely many invariant,
    ordinary-homogeneous generators<nbsp><math|f<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Averaging
    coefficients over the compact gauge group and taking homogeneous
    components shows, inductively in ordinary degree, that every invariant
    ordinary-homogeneous polynomial is a sum of products of invariant
    homogeneous polynomials of weight at most some<nbsp><math|b<rsub|j>>.
    Indeed write <math|P=<big|sum>a<rsub|\<alpha\>>*f<rsub|\<alpha\>>>, keep
    only terms of total degree<nbsp><math|deg P>, and replace
    <math|a<rsub|\<alpha\>>> by its gauge average; their degrees are smaller.
    This proves the assertion by induction, including constants.

    These products detect every invariant
    <math|P\<in\>\<frak-P\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j>>. Suppose otherwise and take
    its lowest nonzero ordinary-homogeneous part<nbsp><math|P<rsub|r>>. Split
    a product of homogeneous factors of total ordinary degree<nbsp><math|r>
    into distinct nearby spectator sites. Every leg of<nbsp><math|P> must
    pair with a spectator leg. Since its lowest ordinary degree
    is<nbsp><math|r>, all terms with internal spectator contractions, or with
    higher-degree parts of<nbsp><math|P>, vanish identically. Wick's rule, as
    the spectator sites coalesce, therefore gives the two-site pairing
    of<nbsp><math|P<rsub|r>> with their ordinary product. Each factor has
    weight at most<nbsp><math|b<rsub|j>>, so the assumed annihilation makes
    the limit zero. Choose the product sum to be the reflection of the
    complex conjugation of<nbsp><math|P<rsub|r>>, using the preceding
    generation result. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> makes this pairing
    strictly positive. This contradicts the assumed annihilation, so
    <math|P=0>.

    We now use induction on the weight bound of<nbsp><math|P> to remove the
    restriction on jet order. Put <math|g=<dimG>> and choose
    <math|d<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>max <around|{|b<rsub|g>,2*g+5|}>>. In a real
    colour basis, write <math|X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>> for the jet
    coordinate tested by a scalar order-<math|j> probe<nbsp><math|p>. For
    scalar probes<nbsp><math|p,q> of orders <math|k,l>, the invariant
    quadratic <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:X<rsub|k,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>*X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q|)>>:
    has weight<nbsp><math|k+l+4>. Its collision with a polynomial using jets
    through order<nbsp><math|j> has, in the coefficient where just the
    order-<math|k> leg contracts at that order, the polynomial

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-collision><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q|)>*<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>C<rsub|k,j>*<around|(|p,e<rsub|\<mu\>>;z|)><frac|\<partial\>*P|\<partial\>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>> is a basis of order-<math|j>
    probes, and <math|C<rsub|k,j>> is their differentiated curvature
    covariance. The extraction distinguishes harmonic poles from products of
    poles, rather than comparing the weights of their remaining polynomials.
    Put <math|r=4+k+j>. After testing against fixed exterior spectators, the
    term<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> is harmonic and homogeneous of
    degree<nbsp><math|-r> in<nbsp><math|z>; multiplication by
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-1|)>>> makes it real analytic
    at zero. Taylor expansion of the other terms at each singular order gives
    homogeneous functions which become real analytic after multiplication by
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-2|)>>> at order<nbsp><math|r>.
    In particular, a double contraction is a product of poles of orders
    <math|r<rsub|1>,r<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>2>: its product is cleared by
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>-2|)>>>.
    Taylor terms preserve this two-power improvement. The contraction of the
    order-<math|l> leg alone has order at most <math|4+l+j\<less\>r>.

    A harmonic homogeneous function<nbsp><math|K> of degree<nbsp><math|-r>,
    <math|r\<geqslant\>2>, cannot have the latter improvement unless it is
    zero. Indeed, in four dimensions,

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\>*<around|(|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*s>*K|)>=4*s*<around|(|s+1-r|)><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*s-2>*K.
    </equation*>

    If <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-2|)>>*K> extends
    analytically, successive Laplacians show that<nbsp><math|K> itself
    extends analytically; negative homogeneity then makes it zero. Expand the
    collision through any order exceeding <math|8+k+l+2*j>. If
    <math|P\<in\>\<frak-N\><rsub|d<rsub|0>>>, its correlation with the
    quadratic test and any further bounded-degree spectators is identically
    zero. Comparing successive radial homogeneities in this finite expansion
    makes its order-<math|r> harmonic pole zero by the preceding argument.
    Thus<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> belongs
    to<nbsp><math|\<frak-N\><rsub|d<rsub|0>>>. This conclusion uses only
    <math|l\<less\>k> and <math|k+l+4\<leqslant\>d<rsub|0>>, without a weight
    induction. The induction is used next to make this extracted polynomial
    zero.

    Take <math|k=g+1> and <math|l=0,\<ldots\>,g-1>. For each<nbsp><math|l>
    choose a probe<nbsp><math|q<rsub|l>> and a jet
    value<nbsp><math|v<rsub|l>> with <math|q<rsub|l><around|(|v<rsub|l>|)>=1>.
    The matrix <math|<around|(|X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>|)><rsub|l,\<alpha\>>>
    has nonzero polynomial determinant: assign<nbsp><math|v<rsub|l>> to
    colour<nbsp><math|l> and zero to the other colours in
    order<nbsp><math|l>, making this matrix the identity. For
    <math|j\<gtr\>g> the polynomial<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> replaces an
    order-<math|j> jet by an order-<math|l> jet, so its weight is strictly
    smaller. The induction hypothesis therefore makes it zero. Multiplication
    by the transposed cofactor matrix, in the polynomial domain, shows that
    every one-colour contraction in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> vanishes. As
    <math|z,p> vary, the forms <math|C<rsub|k,j>*<around|(|p,\<cdot\>;z|)>>
    span the dual of the order-<math|j> jets. Their common annihilator
    generates a Maxwell wave whose order-<math|k> jets vanish everywhere.
    That wave is a polynomial of degree below<nbsp><math|k>; its negative
    homogeneity makes it zero. Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin> then makes
    the probe zero. Hence every order-<math|j> partial derivative
    of<nbsp><math|P> vanishes. Starting above<nbsp><math|g> and descending
    removes all these jet orders. The case of orders at most<nbsp><math|g>
    was proved above with spectator bound<nbsp><math|b<rsub|g>>. This closes
    the induction on weight and proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-detection>.

    <em|Step 2: continuation off the lines.> Free correlations are real
    analytic off the diagonals by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature>. If all the
    stated jets vanish, every Ward sum vanishes near each testing line. For
    any separated configuration choose distinct ordered line coordinates
    respecting the strict order of its coordinates in that axis. The straight
    segment to that line configuration stays separated: strict inequalities
    persist, and coordinate ties are separated by the line coordinates.
    Analytic continuation along this segment gives <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>=0>
    on all of<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>.

    <em|Step 3: weight four.> We first determine<nbsp><math|P> on weight
    four. Let <math|V<rsub|j>> be the scalar on-shell order-<math|j> jet
    space and set <math|\<kappa\><rsub|i*j>*<around|(|p,q;z|)>=C<rsub|i,j>*<around|(|p,q;z|)>>
    in one colour. For <math|p\<in\>V<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>> put
    <math|U<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:X<rsub|0,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)><rsup|2>>:.
    Simplicity, the filtration and inversion parity force
    <math|P<rsub|b>*U<around|(|p|)>> to be a colour contraction of one
    order-zero and one order-one jet. Represent it by a linear
    map<nbsp><math|A<rsub|p>:V<rsub|0>\<to\>V<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>. In the
    Ward identity of <math|U<around|(|p|)>,U<around|(|q|)>,U<around|(|w|)>>,
    let the first two points coalesce along<nbsp><math|t*u> and multiply
    by<nbsp><math|t<rsup|5>>. The term with <math|P<rsub|b>> at the distant
    point tends to zero. The remaining two triangular Wick contractions give,
    for every <math|X\<in\>V<rsub|0>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<kappa\><rsub|10>*<around|(|A<rsub|p>*X,q;u|)>*<space|0.17em>q<around|(|X|)>=\<kappa\><rsub|10>*<around|(|A<rsub|q>*X,p;u|)>*<space|0.17em>p<around|(|X|)>.
    </equation*>

    To obtain arbitrary<nbsp><math|X>, choose the distant probe<nbsp><math|w>
    using the invertible order-zero covariance<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin>. A
    common nonzero coefficient in the two surviving terms cancels. If
    <math|p<around|(|X|)>=0>, choose <math|q<around|(|X|)>\<ne\>0>; varying
    <math|q,u> makes <math|A<rsub|p>*X=0>, again by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin>.
    Thus <math|A<rsub|p>*X=p<around|(|X|)>*f<rsub|b>*p>. The same displayed
    identity and nondegeneracy show that <math|f<rsub|b>:V<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>\<to\>V<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>
    is real linear and

    <\equation*>
      \<kappa\><rsub|10>*<around|(|f<rsub|b>*p,q;u|)>=\<kappa\><rsub|10>*<around|(|f<rsub|b>*q,p;u|)>.
    </equation*>

    Here is the signed-covariant calculation of<nbsp><math|f>. Write
    <math|e<rsup|i*j>> for the curvature coordinate and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|h>*e<rsup|i*j>> for its first jet. For
    <math|f<rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>>, sign parity permits only
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>,\<partial\><rsub|3>*e<rsup|23>,\<partial\><rsub|4>*e<rsup|24>>;
    interchange of axes<nbsp><math|3,4> and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|i*2>=0> reduce their
    span to <math|<R>\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>>. For
    <math|f<rsub|1>*e<rsup|23>> the candidates are
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|23>,\<partial\><rsub|2>*e<rsup|13>,\<partial\><rsub|3>*e<rsup|12>>;
    antisymmetrizing axes<nbsp><math|2,3> and Bianchi reduce them to
    <math|<R>\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|23>>. Permutations leave two
    constants. They agree because

    <\equation*>
      \<kappa\><rsub|10>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>,e<rsup|23>;e<rsub|1>+e<rsub|3>|)>=-<frac|1|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>>\<ne\>0,
    </equation*>

    as follows by differentiating<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature>. Therefore
    <math|f<rsub|b>=c<rsub|<math-up|jet>>*\<partial\><rsub|b>> with one
    real<nbsp><math|c<rsub|<math-up|jet>>>. Since
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*U<around|(|p|)>> has two identical terms,
    <math|P<rsub|b>=c*\<partial\><rsub|b>> on weight four, with
    <math|c=c<rsub|<math-up|jet>>/2>.

    <em|Step 4: higher weights.> Set <math|Q<rsub|b>=P<rsub|b>-c*\<partial\><rsub|b>>.
    Translation invariance makes its Ward sums zero, and it kills every free
    stress component. Insert a free stress shell for the dilation field
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>=y-x<rsub|0>> into such a sum. The term where
    <math|Q<rsub|b>> acts on the stress is zero. Evaluate all other shell
    terms by the polarized calculation in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>,
    now with this<nbsp><math|v>. The extra derivative of<nbsp><math|v>
    contracts with the trace of the free stress, which is zero
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-tensor>. Choose the shell to surround only the
    distinguished point, so its Ward sum has just that slot. The local Lie
    derivative of a two-form under dilation is <math|y\<cdot\>\<nabla\>+2>;
    differentiating its jets gives the weight operator
    <math|\<cal-H\>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>=<around|(|j+2|)>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>>;
    subtraction of the Ward sum with the distinguished
    label<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\>*u> shows that
    <math|<around|[|\<cal-H\>,Q<rsub|b>|]>*u\<in\>\<frak-N\><rsub|d>>. For
    <math|d\<geqslant\>d<rsub|0>> Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-detection>
    removes this polynomial. Thus <math|Q<rsub|b>> preserves each homogeneous
    weight. Inversion acts there by<nbsp><math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|<math-up|weight>>>,
    whereas vector covariance makes <math|Q<rsub|b>> odd under inversion. It
    follows that <math|Q<rsub|b>*u=-Q<rsub|b>*u>, hence <math|Q<rsub|b>=0>.

    <em|Step 5: finite selection.> Conversely translation invariance
    annihilates every test on<nbsp><math|\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>.
    Finally the real domain<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsub|d>> is
    finite-dimensional. Successively choose a test nonzero on the current
    kernel modulo this line. Each choice lowers its dimension, so finitely
    many tests have precisely the asserted kernel.
  </proof>

  Let <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|d>> consist of alternating real-bilinear families
  <math|\<Delta\>:<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)><rsup|2>\<to\><math-up|End><rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>
  which commute with conjugation, kill the unit, preserve every lower-degree
  subspace, and satisfy

  <\equation*>
    R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|h|)>*\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>=\<Delta\>*<around|(|h*K*h<rsup|-1>,h*L*h<rsup|-1>|)>*R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|h|)><space|2em><around|(|h\<in\><hypercubic>,det
    h=1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The next result will remove the defect in the rotation commutator.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite internal Ward tests><label|lem:G1-internal> For every
    <math|d\<geqslant\>4>, finitely many sums

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-erasure-test><big|sum><rsub|i>S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>*u<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|n>;x|)>,<space|2em>u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d>,<space|1em>x\<in\><Conf|n>,
    </equation>

    separate<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|d>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>. Suppose all these sums vanish and fix
    <math|<math-ss|Z>=\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>>. On the weight-four space
    let <math|b<rsub|x>*<around|(|u,v|)>=S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|u,v;x,0|)>>.
    The order-zero Kelvin formula and Wick's rule give, for
    <math|<around|\||x|\|>=<around|\||y|\|>\<ne\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|x>*<around|(|u,v|)>=b<rsub|y>*<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|y>*\<cal-R\><rsub|x>|)>*u,v|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both <math|<math-ss|Z>> and the orthogonal action preserve this space.
    Applying <math|b<rsub|x>*<around|(|<math-ss|Z>u,v|)>+b<rsub|x>*<around|(|u,<math-ss|Z>v|)>=0>
    at<nbsp><math|x> and<nbsp><math|y>, then using nondegeneracy
    of<nbsp><math|b<rsub|y>>, shows that<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Z>> commutes
    with <math|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|y>*\<cal-R\><rsub|x>|)>>
    there. In particular it commutes with every coordinate quarter turn.

    Combine this with the signed covariance of<nbsp><math|\<Delta\>>. For
    each weight-four<nbsp><math|u>, the alternating bilinear function
    <math|<around|(|K,L|)>\<mapsto\>\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>*u> is
    invariant under the action of coordinate quarter turns on both arguments.
    Such a function is zero. Indeed in the plane basis
    <math|K<rsub|a*b>=E<rsub|b*a>-E<rsub|a*b>>, overlapping distinct planes
    have opposite sign under a determinant-one diagonal sign change, and
    disjoint planes are interchanged by an even permutation; invariance and
    alternation kill both cases. Thus<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Z>> vanishes on
    weight four, including the stress.

    Insert a free angular stress shell into its Ward sum, as in the dilation
    argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1-vector>. The shell surrounds
    only the distinguished point. Subtracting the Ward sum with its angularly
    differentiated label gives <math|<around|[|<math-ss|L><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|M|)>,<math-ss|Z>|]>*u\<in\>\<frak-N\><rsub|d>>.
    This polynomial has weight at most<nbsp><math|d>, so strict two-point
    positivity in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> already makes it zero.
    Hence <math|<math-ss|Z>> commutes with all infinitesimal rotations.
    Differentiating <math|e<rsup|t<math-ss|L><around|(|M|)>><math-ss|Z>-<math-ss|Z>e<rsup|t<math-ss|L><around|(|M|)>>>
    proves commutation with coordinate quarter turns in every degree.
    Repeating the preceding alternating-plane argument gives
    <math|\<Delta\>=0> in all degrees. Finite-dimensionality selects a finite
    separating subfamily, by successively reducing the common kernel.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Lattice inputs><label|sec:G-lattice>

  The insertions are those of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>; their
  coefficient polynomials are constructed in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D19> and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>. We
  use the inverse coefficient estimate of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>, the corrected sources and
  normalized integral of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral>, and the backward matching
  estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match>. The positional bound is the
  uniform displacement estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement>,
  with coordinate separations at least four times the displacement margin.
  The precise normalizations used below are<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lift-bound>,
  <eqref|eq:G-source-root> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-annular-cost>; the marked
  local format is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>. Roots always mean
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>. Cutoff limits have the
  order<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>, followed by the fixed extraction of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>.

  Fix arity and degree bounds <math|n\<geqslant\>2,D\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|R\<geqslant\>1>. A basis word means a list of members of fixed real
  bases of <math|<maxwellspace|d>>, <math|d\<leqslant\>D>. Put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|R,m>=<around|{|x:<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>R,<sepinf><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>R<rsup|-1>|}>,<space|2em>E<rsub|\<sigma\>>=<around|{|x:<around|\||x<rsub|i><rsup|a>-x<rsub|j><rsup|a>|\|>\<gtr\>\<sigma\>*<around|(|i\<ne\>j,a=1,\<ldots\>,4|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Choose the construction's exponent triple for a degree horizon
  containing<nbsp><math|n,D>, and denote its exponents by
  <math|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>,\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>
  and<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>\<less\>0>. They satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-exponents>\<beta\>+6\<less\>\<alpha\>,<space|1em>\<gamma\>+5\<less\>\<alpha\>+\<zeta\>,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>*D*n+\<zeta\>\<less\>0,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\>-\<gamma\>|)>*D*n-\<alpha\>-\<zeta\>\<less\>0.
  </equation>

  The choice is explicit: with <math|h<rsub|max>=max <around|(|n,D|)>>, take
  <math|v=7*h<rsub|max>*<around|(|h<rsub|max>+2|)>+1>, <math|w=6+v>,
  <math|\<alpha\>=17+h<rsub|max>+v+w*h<rsub|max>*<around|(|h<rsub|max>+2|)>+w>,
  <math|\<beta\>=\<alpha\>-7>, <math|\<gamma\>=\<alpha\>-w> and
  <math|\<zeta\>=-v>. The first two inequalities place the root cells; the
  last two will remove coordinate coincidence strips. Write
  <math|M<rsub|k>=<cutoffsize><rsub|k>> and set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-balance>r<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\>>,<space|2em>\<frak-s\><rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|-\<alpha\>>,<space|2em>\<sigma\><rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|\<zeta\>>,<space|2em>\<frak-a\><rsub|k>=C*M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>*D>,<space|1em>\<frak-b\><rsub|k>=C*M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<gamma\>|)>*D>,
  </equation>

  where <math|C\<geqslant\>1> depends only on <math|n,D,R> and the
  construction and is fixed in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice>. In particular
  <math|r<rsub|k>*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>>: the normalized grid is the
  physical construction grid. For an ordered coefficient cluster limit
  <math|c<rsub|k,d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>> of the lifts in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice>, define

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|k>*<around|(|\<b-u\>;x|)>=S<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|d<rsub|i>>*c<rsub|k,d<rsub|i>>*u<rsub|i>;r<rsub|k>*x|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>> is the quotient of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>.

  The following comparison uses one coefficient family for every word within
  the fixed bounds. Its constants may depend on these bounds, the word and
  the construction. Its estimates hold for every coefficient cluster limit,
  rather than for one specially chosen limit.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Lattice comparison at small radii><label|lem:G-lattice> There are
    arbitrarily large levels<nbsp><math|k>, real
    <math|<hypercubic>>-equivariant lifts
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k,N,p,d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>>
    preserving the degree-zero unit, and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>
    such that every ordered coefficient cluster limit of these lifts
    satisfies, for every basis word with <math|m\<leqslant\>n>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-lattice-input>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||Q<rsub|k>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<frak-a\><rsub|k><rsup|m>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|on
        >K<rsub|R,m>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\>*Q<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*m*\<frak-b\><rsub|k><rsup|m>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|on
        >K<rsub|2*R,m>\<cap\>E<rsub|4*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||Q<rsub|k>-S<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|Max>>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|on
        ><around|(|\<frak-s\><rsub|k><Z>|)><rsup|4*m>\<cap\>K<rsub|2*R,m>\<cap\>E<rsub|0>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>

    At fixed<nbsp><math|k> the lifts are bounded on ordered tails.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Choose a real <math|<hypercubic>>-equivariant polynomial representative
    map <math|\<iota\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|free>>> for the on-shell
    quotient, using the Taylor sections of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1> and
    averaging a real splitting over<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>. Let
    <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|k,N,p,d>> be the marked coefficient readout from
    level<nbsp><math|k> down to<nbsp><math|r<around|(|d|)>>, after the finite
    Gaussian contraction correction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>.
    Write <math|\<delta\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|wt>>> for multiplication of a
    weight-<math|j> monomial by<nbsp><math|t<rsup|j>>; the factor-count
    scaling<nbsp><math|<factorscale><rsub|t>> is
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>. Abbreviate the actual coupling by
    <math|b<rsub|N,p,k>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>. The Gaussian field divided by
    the small-field threshold has scale

    <\equation*>
      \<vartheta\><rsub|k,N,p>=<around|(|t<rsub|k>*<sqrt|b<rsub|N,p,k>>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>0\<less\>\<vartheta\><rsub|k,N,p><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2*M<rsub|k>.
    </equation*>

    The inequality follows from <math|t<rsub|k>=M<rsub|k>/<sqrt|<refbeta><rsub|k>>>
    and <math|b<rsub|N,p,k>\<leqslant\>2<refbeta><rsub|k>>. Here
    <math|t<rsub|k>> is the small-field threshold of Part<nbsp>I. Solve

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-lift-equation><math-ss|C><rsub|k,N,p,d>\<Lambda\><rsub|k,N,p,d>*u=<factorscale><rsub|\<vartheta\><rsub|k,N,p><rsup|-1>>\<delta\><rsub|M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\>>><rsup|<math-up|wt>>*\<iota\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|free>>*u.
    </equation>

    Its solution exists and obeys the inverse estimate

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-lift-bound><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|k,d>*a<rsub|k><rsup|d><around|\||<math-ss|C><rsub|k,N,p,d>v|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|K<rsub|k,d>> is independent of<nbsp><math|N,p> at
    fixed<nbsp><math|k>. To obtain<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lift-bound>, apply the
    inverse estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> at each step
    <math|m+1\<to\>m>, from level<nbsp><math|k> down
    to<nbsp><math|r<around|(|d|)>> and apply the coefficient comparison in
    the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>; its relative
    readout error is less than<nbsp><math|1/4>, so its inverse costs at
    most<nbsp><math|2>. One may take <math|K<rsub|k,d>> bounded by twice the
    product of the finitely many column and block factors up
    to<nbsp><math|k>, enlarged by the fixed basis norms. The readout is
    injective, hence invertible on its finite coefficient space. Its unit,
    conjugation and signed laws give these laws for the inverse. The
    displayed upper bound on<nbsp><math|\<vartheta\><rsup|-1>> bounds the
    right side at fixed<nbsp><math|k>, proving boundedness of the lifts on
    cutoff tails.

    Here is the all-arity estimate. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>\U<reference|lem:E-source-integral>
    are stated for an arity bound<nbsp><math|n\<geqslant\>2>, at
    most<nbsp><math|n> marks per cell and any compact position set with
    positive point separation; we apply them to the
    interval<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> with exponent<nbsp><math|b=\<alpha\>>
    and position set<nbsp><math|K<rsub|2*R,m>>. Their covariance and Taylor
    bounds are uniform on the compact set <math|K<rsub|2*R,m>>, whose point
    separations are at least <math|<around|(|2*R|)><rsup|-1>>; they require
    no lower bound on each coordinate difference. Only the reflected
    placement below requires membership in<nbsp><math|E<rsub|0>>. Write
    <math|I<rsub|i>=O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|k,N,p,d<rsub|i>>*u<rsub|i>,r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|N>|)>>
    at compatible lattice points. For a subset<nbsp><math|J> put
    <math|G<rsub|J>=S<rsub|<around|\||J|\|>><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|<around|(|u<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>J>;<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>J>|)>>
    and define

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1,<space|2em>R<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>I<rsub|i>-<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>J\<subseteq\>I>G<rsub|J>*R<rsub|I\<setminus\>J>.
    </equation*>

    This subtracts the full continuum Gaussian moment on every subset of
    slots. For exact continuum Gaussian variables its nonempty coefficients
    have expectation zero, by induction on<nbsp><math|<around|\||I|\|>>. The
    contraction correction and the <math|\<vartheta\>>-normalized
    equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lift-equation> identify the leading Gaussian
    correlations with these continuum moments at the normalized grid points,
    as calculated in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>.
    Covariance, Taylor and localization errors remain in the regular and
    forced source terms.

    Here is the root estimate for the whole reflected array. Use the
    finite-set clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> to choose
    arbitrarily late common terminal levels<nbsp><math|k> for the three
    exponents <math|\<alpha\>,\<beta\>,\<gamma\>>. First use the
    interval<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> with exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>>.
    Denote its width level by <math|w<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|k|)>=k-h<rsub|k><around|(|\<alpha\>|)>-v<rsub|k>>
    and its centered tiling by <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|w<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)>>,
    as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>. Put <math|q<rsub|k>=log
    M<rsub|k>/<around|(|16*n|)>>. Give each marked slot amplitude
    <math|e<rsup|2*q<rsub|k>>>. This amplitude is at most
    <math|M<rsub|k><rsup|1/16>> because <math|n\<geqslant\>2>. The weighted
    mode norm in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>
    multiplies amplitudes over subsets, so coefficient extraction for a
    nonempty<nbsp><math|I> contributes <math|e<rsup|-2*q<rsub|k><around|\||I|\|>>>
    in each reflected cell. The corrected-source iteration of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources> keeps this radius on the
    selected comparison intervals. For the arity bound<nbsp><math|n>, work in
    <math|<C><around|[|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|m>|]>/<around|(|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>>
    and multiply by the reciprocal of the continuum free moment polynomial.
    Its coefficient of<nbsp><math|z<rsub|I>> is exactly the recursion
    defining<nbsp><math|R<rsub|I>>. Compute the finite Gaussian contractions
    on every subset; the remaining source modes have zero leading Gaussian
    coefficient. In the source iteration, collect their absolute weighted
    norms. Terms linear in these modes cost the finite stage constant; every
    other connected term contains at least two residual modes and is bounded
    by their squared norm times a fixed power of<nbsp><math|M<rsub|k>>. This
    is<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-step> in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>, in the weighted mode norm
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>. The covariance and
    localization errors in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial> are
    chosen below the corresponding finite source tolerance at entry, as
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-iteration>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>, applied to the
    coefficients<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-corrected-coefficients> of these finitely
    many subset modes, gives this source radius; all additional constants
    depend only on <math|n,D,R> and the construction. Its sums are rooted at
    a mark and do not count the replicated cells.

    The remaining cost is the normalized positive integral.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral> bounds its logarithm per
    cell by the stage constant<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|J<rsub|k>><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>>
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-comparison-cost>, which already contains the term
    <math|1+n> for at most<nbsp><math|n> marks.
    Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> imposes
    <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>log
    M<rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>> for each admitted arity bound<nbsp><math|n>,
    hence <math|Q<rsub|J<rsub|k>><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|k>>.
    Its entry is chosen after the finitely many source constants, so the
    bound holds simultaneously for their arity requests. Taking the root over
    the reflected cells gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-source-root><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|w<rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|q<rsub|k>-2*q<rsub|k><around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n|)>>*<space|2em><around|(|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>|)>.
    </equation>

    The smallness here comes from the corrected density at large coupling:
    its source terms remain bounded at an increasing amplitude, while the
    unmarked normalization grows more slowly than that amplitude. The
    estimate applies to the complete reflected array, before absolute values
    are taken, and requires no independence of its insertions.

    The source stencils are interior. Distinct coordinate differences at
    normalized grid points are at least <math|\<frak-s\><rsub|k>>, so their
    physical differences are at least<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>. The reading map
    assigns the corresponding level-<math|k> sites without rounding, and the
    interval margin<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry> contains the inspected
    stencils and their reflected copies. Expanding the recursion, applying
    the chessboard inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> to
    each<nbsp><math|R<rsub|I>>, and bounding the free submoments
    on<nbsp><math|K<rsub|2*R,m>> gives <math|C<rsub|R,\<b-u\>>*M<rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n|)>>>.
    This proves the last line of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lattice-input>.

    Two further root levels bound the same insertions between grid points. At
    its own normalization, the singleton source comparison just proved gives
    a root at most <math|1+<around|\||S<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|u|)>|\|>>
    by the triangle inequality with the unit. For
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, changing normalization
    to<nbsp><math|M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\>>> multiplies a weight-<math|j>
    coefficient by <math|M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>*j>>.
    The finite coefficient sum therefore bounds these roots
    by<nbsp><math|\<frak-a\><rsub|k>>, for<nbsp><math|C> large. The same
    calculation at exponent<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>> bounds the corresponding
    roots by<nbsp><math|\<frak-b\><rsub|k>>. Include every reflected basis
    label and its image under conjugation in these finite maxima.

    The two interval lengths of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> give, with the stage
    block ratio denoted by<nbsp><math|L>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-root-sides><frac|a<rsub|w<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>>|a<rsub|k>>=L<rsup|h<rsub|k><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|2>*M<rsub|k><rsup|b+2>*B<rsub|k><rsup|10>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|k><rsup|b+5>*<space|2em><around|(|b=\<beta\>,\<gamma\>|)>.
    </equation>

    The last inequality holds after the stage entry: <math|L> is fixed there
    and <math|B<rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. In particular the
    <math|\<beta\>> cell is at most <math|a<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<beta\>+6>=o<around|(|r<rsub|k>|)>>,
    and the <math|\<gamma\>> cell is <math|o<around|(|r<rsub|k>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-exponents>. The chessboard inequality with the first
    roots bounds separated continuum moments
    by<nbsp><math|\<frak-a\><rsub|k><rsup|m>>. The second cells have physical
    side less than <math|r<rsub|k>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>. The physical diameter
    on<nbsp><math|r<rsub|k>*K<rsub|2*R,m>> is at most
    <math|4*R*r<rsub|k>\<to\>0>, hence less than<nbsp><math|L<rsub|p>/2> on
    the ordered tails. Thus the torus separation<nbsp><math|g<rsub|p>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement> equals the minimum ordinary
    coordinate separation. On<nbsp><math|E<rsub|4*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>> it
    exceeds <math|4*r<rsub|k>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>. The uniform clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement>, applied with margin
    <math|r<rsub|k>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>> and with the reflected roots included
    in<nbsp><math|\<frak-b\><rsub|k>>, therefore gives
    <math|4*m*\<frak-b\><rsub|k><rsup|m>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>> after rescaling
    positions. These choices use the same coefficient readout and lifts; only
    their weight normalization changes. The common terminal levels chosen
    above serve all three normalizations, and the finitely many cutoff tails
    can be intersected.

    Finally take any convergent coefficient subfilter of these bounded lifts
    in the ordered limit. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1> and
    then<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1> pass the
    moment estimates to the fixed table; the difference bound passes by
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>.
    This proves all three inequalities for every coefficient cluster limit.
    No extraction of the table or volume sequence is made here.
  </proof>

  For the shell estimate take <math|\<varkappa\>\<in\><around|{|0,1|}>>,
  <math|A\<in\>F<rsub|d>>, <math|B\<in\>F<rsub|4>> and
  <math|D=d+1-\<varkappa\>>. A grid test is a separated configuration
  <math|<around|(|0,x<rsub|i>|)>> on <math|a<rsub|j<rsub|0>><Z><rsup|4>> for
  some level<nbsp><math|j<rsub|0>>, generic in each coordinate, with a fixed
  spectator list<nbsp><math|\<b-B\><rsub|i>>. Define its response functional
  and marked response by

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-L\><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=S*<around|(|v<around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<frak-h\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>=S*<around|(|B<around|(|y|)>,A<around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Finite collections may use different grids. The union of all these
  configurations is dense. The estimate below supplies the degree bound
  needed to represent a shrinking shell by a field.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Annular annihilator estimate><label|lem:G-annular> For every fixed
    finite list of grid tests and coefficients <math|g<rsub|i>\<in\><C>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<cal-L\><rsub|i>=0>
    on<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>, there are <math|\<rho\><rsub|l>*\<downarrow\>*0>
    such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-annular-input>sup<rsub|1/4\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|l><rsup|3+\<varkappa\>>*\<frak-h\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|l>*z|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, <reference|def:D10>, the local
    sections of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>, <reference|lem:E2.2a>,
    <reference|lem:E2.3>, together with Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. Put <math|h=3+\<varkappa\>>, so
    <math|D+h=d+4>. Recall <math|s<rsub|m>=log
    <around|(|a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>> from<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-levels>. Use the
    support radius <math|B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> of Part<nbsp>I and take
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>=128*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>*a<rsub|m+1>>. Its
    logarithmic rate is <math|log \<rho\><rsub|m>=-s<rsub|m>+o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>.

    Fix a centered cell around the distinguished point, small enough to be
    disjoint from all spectator stencils, and a finer stopping
    level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>r<around|(|D|)>> at which its
    replicated source estimate applies. Choose a slightly finer
    level<nbsp><math|j<around|(|m|)>> with

    <\equation*>
      <frac|s<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>|s<rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>t=1+<frac|1|64*<around|(|d+12|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Such levels exist since <math|s<rsub|m+1>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>1>, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. At a grid point
    <math|y\<in\>a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>><Z><rsup|4>> with
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>/16\<less\><around|\||y|\|>\<less\>4*\<rho\><rsub|m>>,
    choose representatives <math|a\<in\>V<rsub|d>,b\<in\>V<rsub|4>>
    for<nbsp><math|A,B>, block their two marked insertions at<nbsp><math|y,0>
    together, and retain the Taylor terms of weight at most<nbsp><math|D>.
    Their coefficient vector<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>\<in\>V<rsub|D>>
    is chosen by inverting the marked coefficient map. The exact difference
    is

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>*O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|4|)>><around|(|b,y/a<rsub|N>|)>*O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|a,0|)>-O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|D|)>><around|(|c<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>,0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Estimate this difference in the root of the fixed cell. We give the
    quantitative marked calculation, with the powers that determine its
    smallness. Let <math|u<rsub|m,1>,u<rsub|m,2>> be the available amplitudes
    of the degree-<math|d> and rescaled degree-four marks at
    level<nbsp><math|j<around|(|m|)>>. The one-mark bounds, including the
    factor<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>>, give

    <\equation*>
      log u<rsub|m,1>=<around|(|-d*t+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>,<space|2em>log
      u<rsub|m,2>=<around|(|-4*t+h+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    The support weights, coefficient columns and threshold powers have
    logarithm<nbsp><math|o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>.
    Transporting each of the two sources to level<nbsp><math|m+1> adds the
    four-dimensional volume factor <math|<around|(|a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>/a<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|4>>
    to its available amplitude. Thus the joint
    amplitude<nbsp><math|v<rsub|m>>, and the dimensionless coefficient
    bound<nbsp><math|e<rsub|m>> of its Taylor remainder, satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-annular-cost>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|log v<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<around|[|-D-<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>+o<around|(|1|)>|]>*s<rsub|m>,>>|<row|<cell|e<rsub|m>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>exp
        <around|{|<around|[|<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>+o<around|(|1|)>|]>*s<rsub|m>|}>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>

    Indeed the two transported exponents sum to
    <math|-<around|(|d+4|)>*t+h+8*<around|(|1-t|)>=-D-<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>>.
    Taylor extraction costs the reciprocal joint amplitude
    times<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>>, which cancels the<nbsp><math|D>
    term and gives the second line. This is the two-mark use of the source
    representation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, retaining its forced terms
    and their eligibility tests.

    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match-level>, starting at<nbsp><math|m+1>
    and ending at the fixed stopping level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>. The matched
    remainder gains <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=k<rsub|0>><rsup|m>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|1/8>=e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|m+1>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>>
    in its inverse bound. Since <math|s<rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>s<rsub|m>>, we
    may use the weaker gain <math|e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|m>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>>.
    Put <math|c<rsub|m>=e<rsup|s<rsub|m>/32>>. We require the residual root
    to be <math|O<around|(|c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>>, so multiply its source
    by<nbsp><math|c<rsub|m>> before applying that bound. Its size is then at
    most

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|m>*e<rsub|m>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|m>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|[|-5/64+o<around|(|1|)>|]>*s<rsub|m>|}>,<space|2em><frac|1|32>+<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>-<frac|1|8>=-<frac|5|64>.
    </equation*>

    It eventually fits every fixed source allowance at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>.
    The residual-source estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root>, applied to
    this amplified matched remainder in the same regular and forced source
    norms, has a fixed exponential factor at the fixed cell and
    level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>. It consequently bounds the unamplified
    residual root by<nbsp><math|2*C/c<rsub|m>>, with<nbsp><math|C>
    independent of<nbsp><math|m,N,p>. The inverse coefficient
    estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lift-bound>, applied in degree<nbsp><math|D> at
    the fixed stopping level, also bounds<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>>
    on cutoff tails for each fixed<nbsp><math|m,y>. Interior support follows
    from <math|32*\<rho\><rsub|m>\<to\>0> and the support diameter
    <math|a<rsub|m+1>*<around|(|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>+1+2048*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>|)>\<to\>0>;
    both fit the fixed centered cell.

    Pass to a coefficient cluster limit<nbsp><math|c<around|(|y|)>> and
    project it to<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>. Applying the chessboard
    inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> to the residual and the fixed exterior
    stencils gives, simultaneously for the finite tests,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>*\<frak-h\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|D>*c<around|(|y|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    The same coefficient vector is used for every spectator. Multiplication
    by<nbsp><math|g<rsub|i>> and summation cancels the response term exactly.
    Consequently the sum in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-annular-input> is
    <math|O<around|(|c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>> at all grid points of this
    enlarged annulus whose four coordinates are nonzero.

    We interpolate across the excluded coordinate planes. Let
    <math|f<rsub|m><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>*\<frak-h\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>>.
    For a derivative order<nbsp><math|q>,
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> replaces the moving label by one of
    degree <math|4+q>. The centered and reflected root bounds of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>,
    with<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>, bound a field of fixed degree<nbsp><math|e>
    by <math|exp <around|[|<around|(|e+2+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>|]>>.
    This uses the one-insertion bound with exponent loss two; its constant
    depends on that field but has logarithm<nbsp><math|o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>.
    The finitely many later levels required for the fixed spectator word have
    logarithmic scale <math|s<rsub|m>+o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. Choose degree
    bounds<nbsp><math|e<rsub|i*j>> for its spectator labels and put
    <math|C<rsub|0>=8+d+max<rsub|i> <big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|e<rsub|i*j>+2|)>>.
    Multiplying these root bounds, and using
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>\<leqslant\>1>, gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-annular-derivatives><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|q>*f<rsub|m><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|[|<around|(|q+C<rsub|0>+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>|]>*<space|2em><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|m>/16\<less\><around|\||y|\|>\<less\>4*\<rho\><rsub|m>|)>,
    </equation>

    where <math|C<rsub|0>> depends on the fields and spectators,
    independently of<nbsp><math|q>. Choose <math|q> so
    <math|q*<around|(|t-1|)>\<gtr\>C<rsub|0>>. Since
    <math|a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>=exp <around|[|-<around|(|t+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>|]>>,
    all the <math|q>th derivatives along grid-sized coordinate segments tend
    to zero. Choose <math|2<rsup|q>> nonzero grid coordinates, separated by
    at least one normalized mesh, within distance
    <math|4*<around|(|2<rsup|q>+2|)>*a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>> in each axis.
    On an interval of that fixed normalized length, repeated mean value
    estimates bound a function by a constant times its values at these nodes
    plus its <math|q>th derivative: pair adjacent nodes to bound the
    derivative at intermediate points, repeat<nbsp><math|q> times, and
    integrate back. Apply this to the real and imaginary parts in each of the
    four axes. The constant depends on<nbsp><math|q>, not<nbsp><math|m>.

    These segments stay in the enlarged annulus because
    <math|4*<around|(|2<rsup|q>+2|)>*a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>=o<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|m>|)>>.
    Values with all four coordinates on the nonzero grid tend to zero by the
    preceding cancellation; the derivative remainders tend to zero by the
    choice of<nbsp><math|q>. Four successive interpolations therefore give
    <math|sup<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|m>/4\<leqslant\><around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>2*\<rho\><rsub|m>><around|\||f<rsub|m><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<to\>0>.
    Select a decreasing subsequence of<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|m>>. These
    choices depend on the fixed tests and<nbsp><math|g>, as allowed.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Free comparison and the conserved line><label|sec:partII-G.2>

  The next argument converts Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice> into the
  stable comparison of Part I. Its proof also explains why no volume
  refinement is needed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stable weak comparison><label|lem:G2.1> The fixed family has the
    scaling sequence and estimate of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>. At
    every fixed degree horizon its frames eventually preserve exactly
    conjugation, <math|<hypercubic>> actions, units and inclusions.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D09>, <reference|def:D10>,
    <reference|def:D11>, <reference|def:D12>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>,
    <reference|lem:F2>, <reference|lem:G1.1> and <reference|lem:G-lattice>.
    The set <math|K<rsub|R,m>\<setminus\>E<rsub|8*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>> has
    volume at most <math|C<rsub|m,R>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>: it lies in finitely
    many strips about coordinate coincidence hyperplanes. Its contribution to
    a tested difference is at most <math|C<rsub|m,R><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|k><rsup|m>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<maxwellcorr><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|K<rsub|R,m>>|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>.
    Outside these strips, rounding to the grid moves a point by at most
    <math|4*m*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>>. Eventually
    <math|4*m*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\><rsub|k>>, so the
    segment remains in <math|K<rsub|2*R,m>\<cap\>E<rsub|4*\<sigma\><rsub|k>>>.
    The mean value bound and the free modulus of
    continuity<nbsp><math|\<varpi\>> give total tested error at most

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-tested-error>C<rsub|m,R><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<around*|[|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>+<around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|k><rsup|m>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<maxwellcorr><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|K<rsub|R,m>>|)>*\<sigma\><rsub|k>+\<frak-b\><rsub|k><rsup|m>*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>/\<sigma\><rsub|k>+\<varpi\>*<around|(|4*m*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>|)>|]>.
    </equation>

    Every term tends to zero by the strict exponent inequalities. Expand
    arbitrary fields in the finitely many basis words; their coefficient sums
    are bounded by a constant times the product of their norms.

    At a fixed comparison level the lifts are bounded in a finite-dimensional
    space. Take a convergent subfilter in the ordered cutoff limit and
    project to<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>>. Ordered convergence holds also for
    convergent coefficients, by multilinearity. This
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-tested-error> for linear maps
    <math|U<rsub|k,d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>. Every subfilter uses
    the same table, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>; the bounds apply to all
    its coefficient cluster limits by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice>.

    Choose successively arity, degree and radius bounds tending to infinity,
    tolerances tending to zero, and strictly decreasing radii tending to
    zero. The latter is possible because <math|log M<rsub|k>=o<around|(|log
    a<rsub|k><rsup|-1>|)>> by the scale definitions<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales>
    and the running law<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-running>, whence
    <math|a<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\>>\<to\>0> for every
    fixed<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>>. This selects one sequence before any fixed
    field list. To impose inclusions exactly, choose real <math|<hypercubic>>
    equivariant left inverses <math|L<rsub|d>> of the free
    inclusions<nbsp><math|i<rsub|d>>, by averaging an inner product over the
    finite group. Replace the maps recursively by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-correct-frames><wide|U|~><rsub|k,0>=U<rsub|k,0>,<space|2em><wide|U|~><rsub|k,d+1>=\<jmath\><rsub|d,d+1>*<wide|U|~><rsub|k,d>*L<rsub|d>+U<rsub|k,d+1>*<around|(|I-i<rsub|d>*L<rsub|d>|)>.
    </equation>

    Expansion into the two summands preserves the comparison, since free and
    interacting inclusions preserve correlations. It also preserves reality
    and signed covariance; the degree-zero unit now gives all units. Strict
    free positivity supplies finitely many separating tested correlations in
    each degree. Their matrices converge, so a nonzero full-rank minor makes
    <math|<wide|U|~><rsub|k,d>> eventually injective.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> makes it an isomorphism. Fill the
    finitely many earlier indices in each degree with any of these
    isomorphisms. This changes no eventual assertion.

    Uniformity of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-tested-error> over the supremum-norm unit
    ball of tests gives local <math|L<rsup|1>> convergence by smooth
    approximation to the phase of the difference. This proves
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC> for the single sequence of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D12>.
  </proof>

  We record a consequence needed before identifying the current. For the
  stable frames of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>, put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-derivative-limit><math-ss|d><rsub|k,a>=r<rsub|k><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d+1>|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a><scaleframe|k|d>.
  </equation>

  Then <math|<math-ss|d><rsub|k,a>\<to\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
  in operator norm. To prove this, choose finitely many tested free responses
  injective on<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d+1>>. Their interacting matrices
  converge to this injective matrix by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>.
  Testing <math|<math-ss|d><rsub|k,a>u> gives, by integration by parts, minus
  the correlation tested against <math|\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|a>>*f>.
  These values converge to the tests of <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*u>.
  An injective finite matrix has a bounded left inverse on all sufficiently
  small perturbations, proving the limit on a basis and hence in operator
  norm.

  The chain rule and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> make every positional
  derivative converge locally in<nbsp><math|L<rsup|1>>, also for convergent
  free label lists. For <math|g=\<chi\>*\<cdummy\><around*|(|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d<rsub|i>>u<rsub|k,i>;r<rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>|)>-<maxwellcorr><rsub|n>(u<rsub|i>;\<cdummy\><around*|)||)>|\<nobracket\>>>,
  with <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>>
  equal to one on a compact <math|\<cal-K\>\<subset\><Conf|n>>, and
  <math|M\<gtr\>4*n+j>, integration by parts gives

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<wide|g|^><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|M>*<around|(|1+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|-M>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>M><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>.
  </equation*>

  Fourier inversion<nbsp><cite-detail|Folland1999|Theorem<nbsp>8.26> applies
  since both <math|g> and <math|<wide|g|^>> are integrable. Multiplying
  by<nbsp><math|p> through order<nbsp><math|j> and integrating proves uniform
  convergence of those derivatives on<nbsp><math|\<cal-K\>>. Consequently

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-smooth>S<rsub|n>*<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d<rsub|i>>u<rsub|k,i>;r<rsub|k><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>|)>\<longrightarrow\><maxwellcorr><rsub|n><around|(|u<rsub|i>;\<cdummy\>|)><space|1em><text|in
    >C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|u<rsub|k,i>\<to\>u<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation>

  The rank calculation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-divergence-matrix> now transfers to
  the mixed fields. The following proof also chooses the sign of one fixed
  current.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Conserved line><label|lem:G2.2> The real space of centered real
    symmetric <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial tensors
    <math|J<rsub|a*b>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>=0> is
    one-dimensional. For some nonzero<nbsp><math|J>, a refinement of the
    sequence of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC> and
    positive<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|k>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>\<longrightarrow\><maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>
    </equation*>

    componentwise.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, <reference|def:D11>,
    <reference|def:D12>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>,
    <reference|lem:G1.1>, <reference|lem:G2.1>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit>. Transport a free tensor
    by<nbsp><math|<scaleframe|k|4>> and subtract its mixed one-point value
    times the unit. On centered tensors this is a real
    isomorphism<nbsp><math|C<rsub|k>>; the inverse transports back and
    subtracts the free expectation. Unit preservation makes the two
    composites identities. Let<nbsp><math|V<rsub|k>> be transport
    by<nbsp><math|<scaleframe|k|5>> on real covariant vectors. Then

    <\equation*>
      V<rsub|k><rsup|-1><around|(|r<rsub|k><math-up|div>|)>*C<rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\><math-up|div><rsup|<math-up|Max>>.
    </equation*>

    The centering term disappears since derivatives kill the unit.
    Composition with a right inverse of free divergence tends to the
    identity, so mixed divergence is onto. The domain and target dimensions
    are three and two by these isomorphisms
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-divergence-matrix>; its kernel is a real line.

    Choose a nonzero<nbsp><math|B> on this line and set
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|k>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*B|)>|\<\|\|\>>/<around|\<\|\|\>|<maxwellstress>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    with norms on whole tensors. The normalized preimages have a convergent
    subsequence on this sphere. Stability preserves symmetry, reality and
    signed tensoriality. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth> at arity one gives
    centering of the limit, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> gives zero
    free divergence. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> makes the limit
    <math|<maxwellstress>> or <math|-<maxwellstress>>. Choose <math|J=B> or
    <math|-B> once to obtain the stated limit with
    positive<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|k>>.
  </proof>

  The shell estimate in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-annular> now applies to
  each component of the current.

  The resulting fields represent both fluxes of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>. The proof combines that estimate with
  finite-dimensional duality, conservation, translation covariance and
  continuity of separated responses.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Raw shell charges><label|lem:G2.3> For the chosen<nbsp><math|J>
    there are complex linear maps <math|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>>
    and <math|<math-ss|Y><rsub|K>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>, the latter real
    linear in<nbsp><math|K>, such that, for every allowed cutoff, field,
    spectator list and separated configuration,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|J>=S*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|K><rsup|J>=S*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|Y><rsub|K>A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, <reference|def:D13>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-annular>. More generally use a constant
    Killing field<nbsp><math|v> (<math|\<varkappa\>=0>) or
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>=K*y> (<math|\<varkappa\>=1>). Shell independence
    follows by integration by parts: the divergence vanishes, and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,b><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*v<rsub|b>|)>*J<rsub|a*b>=0>
    by symmetry.

    Fix finitely many grid response tests<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\><rsub|i>> as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-annular>, and
    coefficients<nbsp><math|g<rsub|i>> annihilating<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>,
    where <math|D=d+1-\<varkappa\>>. Shrink a common window to
    <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|y/\<rho\><rsub|l>|)>>, with <math|\<chi\>=1> near
    zero and <math|\<nabla\>*\<chi\>> supported in
    <math|1/2\<leqslant\><around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. Changing variables
    gives the sum of their fluxes as

    <\equation*>
      -<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>|)><around|(|z|)>*v<rsub|b><around|(|z|)>*<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|l><rsup|3+\<varkappa\>>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|l>*z|)>,A<around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z.
    </equation*>

    It is independent of<nbsp><math|l> and tends to zero
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-annular-input>. Thus every linear relation between
    the response tests is also a relation between their flux values.
    Finite-dimensional duality gives a field in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>
    matching these finitely many values: the annihilator of the image of the
    response matrix annihilates the flux vector.

    Choose a finite separating family of grid tests. Such a family exists
    because the common kernel of all separated responses is zero, grid
    configurations are dense, and responses are continuous. A field matching
    this family is unique. Adjoin any additional grid test and apply the
    preceding finite-dimensional argument; uniqueness makes the same field
    match it. Continuity of the responses and of shell integrals, with the
    window fixed locally, extends the identity to all separated
    configurations with distinguished point zero. Translation covariance then
    moves that point to arbitrary<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>>. Linearity of the
    integral and injectivity of responses give complex linearity
    in<nbsp><math|A> and real linearity in<nbsp><math|K>. Taking
    <math|v=e<rsub|b>> and <math|v=K*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-x<rsub|0>|)>>
    proves exactly the two asserted raw shell laws.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Ward identities and rotations><label|sec:partII-G.3>

  Write <math|\<b-q\>=<around|(|A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsub|i>>
  and define the angular sum

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-angular>\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*<around|[|Y,p|]><around|(|\<b-q\>;x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around*|[|S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,Y<rsub|K>*A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|b><around|(|K*x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|b>*S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,p<rsub|b>*A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>|]>.
  </equation>

  The coordinate version of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> gives a
  reflection Hilbert space<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
  and positive generator<nbsp><math|H<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> for
  each coordinate axis<nbsp><math|\<mu\>>; its spectral measure is defined
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-measure>. Here
  <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|\<perp\>><around|(|y|)>> is the unitary translation
  by<nbsp><math|y>, <math|y<rsup|\<mu\>>=0>, on this space. For a point field
  or finite collinear word<nbsp><math|U>, write<nbsp><math|U<rsub|t>> for its
  vector after translating it into the positive half-space by<nbsp><math|t>.
  Its positive spectral measure<nbsp><math|\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>> is
  specified by

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<langle\>|U<rsub|t>,e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>><math-ss|T><rsub|\<perp\>><around|(|y|)>*U<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>e<rsup|-<around|(|2*t+s|)>*\<epsilon\>>*e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>y>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>*<around|(|\<epsilon\>,p|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|t> is larger than the fixed extent of the word; the point-field
  measures are allowed to have infinite total mass before this damping. The
  damped moments of all orders are<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-all-moments>, and mixed
  spectral Cauchy\USchwarz is<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS>. Positive-time
  analyticity is supplied by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>. These results
  use coordinate reflection positivity, E1\UE7,
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> and the coordinate slab operators of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>; conservation enters the tensor estimates,
  and continuous rotations are not used.

  Fix distinct axes<nbsp><math|a,b> and a line axis<nbsp><math|l>. Choose
  even <math|\<eta\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>> equal to
  one on<nbsp><math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> and put
  <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)>=t*\<eta\><around|(|t|)>>. Choose an even
  nonnegative <math|\<psi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>>
  equal to one on<nbsp><math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>; it will dominate the spatial
  Fourier factors on the momentum box. Set <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=-100> for
  <math|j=a,b>, <math|\<omega\><rsub|l>=-150> if
  <math|l\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>, and <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=-300> for
  <math|j\<nin\><around|{|a,b,l|}>>. For <math|\<lambda\>=e<rsup|s>>,
  <math|s\<in\><N>>, <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>>, put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|p|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>\<mu\>>w<rsub|j><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/w<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|U;\<upsilon\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>1><frac|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|p|)>|\<epsilon\>+\<lambda\><rsup|\<upsilon\>>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>,<space|1em>w<rsub|j>=\<lambda\><rsup|1+\<omega\><rsub|j>>.
  </equation*>

  For a word<nbsp><math|B> on<nbsp><math|<R>e<rsub|l>> projected to odd
  parity in axes<nbsp><math|a,b> and even parity in the other axes,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3> gives, for every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-spectral-input>I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|B;-3000|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|u>|)>,<space|1em>u=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|\<mu\>=l,>>|<row|<cell|-\<omega\><rsub|l>,>|<cell|\<mu\>\<ne\>l,>>>>><space|2em>I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>;\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<mu\>\<ne\>b|)>.
  </equation>

  For the diagonal component it gives, with <math|W=\<lambda\><rsup|-99>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-mass-input><big|int><rsub|\<epsilon\>\<leqslant\>1><frac|\<epsilon\>*W<rsup|4>|<around|(|\<epsilon\>+W|)><rsup|4>>*\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|4>|)>.
  </equation>

  Constants may depend on the fields, configuration, axes, profiles
  and<nbsp><math|\<delta\>>, independently of<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>>. These
  bounds include<nbsp><math|\<epsilon\>=0>. The zeroth damped moment and
  <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|900>> imply
  <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>;0|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|900>|)>>.
  For the Fourier\ULaplace test integrals used below,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3> bounds the integral over
  <math|<around|{|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>1|}>\<cup\><around|{|\<exists\>j\<ne\>\<mu\>:<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>w<rsub|j>|}>>
  by <math|C<rsub|M>*\<lambda\><rsup|-M>> for every fixed<nbsp><math|M>,
  after any fixed polynomial prefactor. In the notation of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>: <math|\<mu\>=d>, <math|\<epsilon\>=E>,
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=c<rsub|j>>, <math|\<psi\>=h>,
  <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>=H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>>,
  <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>=I<rsub|d>>, <math|u=\<kappa\><rsub|d>>,
  <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|\<perp\>><around|(|y|)>=U<rsub|y><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>>.

  The free vector and internal tests are those of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G1-vector> and<nbsp><reference|lem:G1-internal>.

  The coordinate bounds remove the angular boundary at infinity before
  continuous rotations are known. The proof below uses an anisotropic cutoff
  so that each reflected column gains an inverse power of its largest spatial
  extent.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Collinear angular identity><label|lem:G-collinear-flux> For every
    separated configuration<nbsp><math|x> on a coordinate line, every field
    list<nbsp><math|\<b-q\>> and every <math|K\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>,
    the raw charges satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-collinear>\<cal-W\><rsub|K>*<around|[|<math-ss|Y>,<math-ss|p>|]><around|(|\<b-q\>;x|)>=0.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Apply the raw shell laws of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3>, with
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13> and the calculus of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>, and the coordinate
    estimates<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-spectral-input>, <eqref|eq:G-mass-input> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>. It suffices to treat
    <math|K=E<rsub|b*a>-E<rsub|a*b>>, <math|a\<ne\>b>. With the profiles
    fixed above, set

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|100>*\<ell\>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-100>*y<rsub|a>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>a>\<ell\><rprime|'>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>>*y<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>V<rsub|b>=f<rsub|a*b>,<space|1em>V<rsub|a>=-f<rsub|b*a>,<space|1em>V<rsub|j>=0*<around|(|j\<ne\>a,b|)>.
    </equation*>

    The vector field<nbsp><math|V> equals<nbsp><math|K*y> near every
    insertion for large<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>>. Disjoint windows and
    conservation give <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|K>=-<big|int><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>,b>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*V<rsub|b>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b><around|(|y|)>,\<b-q\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y>.
    The two terms with <math|\<mu\>=a,b> cancel exactly, because
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a*b>=\<partial\><rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*a>>
    and <math|J<rsub|a*b>=J<rsub|b*a>>.

    Consider the <math|V<rsub|b>> terms with <math|\<mu\>\<ne\>a>. Reflecting
    the integration variable in each axis, including the tensor signs,
    replaces the spectator word by its odd projection in<nbsp><math|a,b> and
    its even projections in the other axes. If the line axis is reflected,
    positions on that line are reflected too; the projection is a finite
    linear combination of collinear words. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>
    applies to each such combination. The differentiated profile is supported
    where <math|<around|\||y<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>>.
    Its Laplace transform therefore has the factor
    <math|e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>/\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>>>. For
    each of the three spatial factors <math|f<rsub|j>=\<ell\>>
    or<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rprime|'>>, Fourier scaling gives, on
    <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<wide|f<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>|)>|^><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|j>><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\>*w<rsub|j><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/w<rsub|j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Their product gives <math|\<lambda\><rsup|3>*\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>>;
    the remaining time integral contributes the displayed
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>> below. For the
    off-diagonal component, <math|e<rsup|-t>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+t|)><rsup|-1>>
    and spectral Cauchy\USchwarz bound the integral, before the
    prefactor<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|100>>, by

    <\equation*>
      C*\<lambda\><rsup|3+\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>*<space|0.17em><around|[|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|B;-3000|)>*I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>;\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|]><rsup|1/2>.
    </equation*>

    Take <math|\<delta\>=1>. For this off-diagonal term
    <math|\<mu\>\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>. Thus if <math|\<mu\>=l> then
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>=-150> and <math|u=1>; otherwise
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>=-300> and <math|u\<leqslant\>150>. The
    exponents are at most <math|-146> and <math|-221.5>, respectively.

    If <math|\<mu\>=b>, split at <math|\<epsilon\>=\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>.
    Above this value use <math|e<rsup|-\<epsilon\>/\<lambda\><rsup|-100>>\<leqslant\>4*<around|[|W/<around|(|\<epsilon\>+W|)>|]><rsup|2>>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-mass-input>; Cauchy\USchwarz gives exponent at most
    <math|3+<around|(|150+1-396|)>/2=-119.5>. Below it,
    <math|\<epsilon\>+\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>\<leqslant\>2*\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>
    supplies a factor <math|\<lambda\><rsup|-1500>> in Cauchy\USchwarz. The
    crude bound <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>;0|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|900>|)>>
    then gives exponent at most <math|3-1500+<around|(|150+900|)>/2=-972>.
    The complementary integration region is
    <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-M>|)>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>, with <math|M\<gtr\>201>. The
    <math|V<rsub|a>> terms are identical after exchanging<nbsp><math|a,b>,
    since <math|\<omega\><rsub|a>=\<omega\><rsub|b>>; this includes their
    diagonal component<nbsp><math|J<rsub|a*a>>. All remaining terms are
    consequently <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-101>|)>> before the
    prefactor, and <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-1>|)>> after it. The
    angular sum is independent of<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>>, so it is zero.
    Linearity in<nbsp><math|K> proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collinear>.
  </proof>

  For a finite-dimensional rotation action write
  <math|<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*t>*R<rsub|d><around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>|\|><rsub|t=0>>
  for its generator. The shell charges can now be identified without assuming
  rotation covariance. The proof first fixes their common scale and then
  constructs an actual orthogonal-group action.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Rotation and charge identification><label|lem:G3.1> There are
    <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>\<in\><R>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>> and continuous
    complex linear <math|O<around|(|4|)>> representations <math|R<rsub|d>>
    extending<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, fixing units and commuting with
    conjugation and inclusions, with

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|p><rsub|b>=c<rsub|\<star\>>*\<partial\><rsub|b>,<space|2em><math-ss|Y><rsub|K>=c<rsub|\<star\>><math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|d+1><around|(|h|)>*\<partial\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|i*a>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*R<rsub|d><around|(|h|)>.
    </equation*>

    For every<nbsp><math|d>, an isomorphism
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>> intertwines the
    free and interacting <math|O<around|(|4|)>> actions.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, <reference|def:D11>,
    <reference|def:D12>, <reference|def:D13>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>
    and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, <reference|lem:G1-vector>,
    <reference|lem:G1-internal>, <reference|lem:G2.1>, <reference|lem:G2.2>,
    <reference|lem:G2.3>. Fix a window and move its distinguished point.
    Differentiating its two shell identities under the compact integral gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-shell-commutators>\<partial\><rsub|b><math-ss|p><rsub|a>=<math-ss|p><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|b>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|b><math-ss|Y><rsub|K>+<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i*b><math-ss|p><rsub|i>=<math-ss|Y><rsub|K>\<partial\><rsub|b>.
    </equation>

    The derivative of <math|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>> gives the second
    term. Testing the unit, applying conjugation or a signed permutation, and
    including a field show, respectively, that the charges kill units, are
    real, have vector or angular covariance, and respect inclusions.
    Injectivity of responses turns these tested identities into field
    identities.

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-collinear-flux>
    gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collinear> for the raw charges.

    Translate a collinear configuration by<nbsp><math|e<rsub|l>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collinear>, with <math|K=E<rsub|b*l>-E<rsub|l*b>>.
    Subtraction gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,<math-ss|p><rsub|b>A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>=0>
    for <math|b\<ne\>l>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-shell-commutators> gives
    every positional jet of these identities. Translation invariance gives
    the same identities for<nbsp><math|\<partial\>>.

    Apply the finite free vector tests<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-tests> to the
    transported mixed vector maps. Their test matrices converge
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth>. Adjoin a real functional nonzero
    on<nbsp><math|\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>; the resulting free matrix
    is injective and remains so under small perturbations. The mixed kernel
    therefore has dimension at most one. It contains
    both<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>> and<nbsp><math|\<partial\>>, so
    <math|<math-ss|p>=c<rsub|d>*\<partial\>> in every sufficiently large
    degree. Inclusions and nonzero free differentiation make the constants
    equal and give the identity also in lower degrees. Rescale the two shell
    laws by<nbsp><math|r<rsub|k>/\<xi\><rsub|k>>
    and<nbsp><math|1/\<xi\><rsub|k>>, respectively. Changing variables
    <math|y=r<rsub|k>*z> and using<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth> yields

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-charge-limits><frac|r<rsub|k>|\<xi\><rsub|k>><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d+1>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|p><rsub|a><scaleframe|k|d>\<to\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>,<space|2em>\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|Y><rsub|K><scaleframe|k|d>\<to\><math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>.
    </equation>

    Finite separating tests recover these operator limits from the shell
    integrals. In particular <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>=c<rsub|d>\<ne\>0> and
    <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>/\<xi\><rsub|k>\<to\>1>. Put
    <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|K>=c<rsub|\<star\>><rsup|-1><math-ss|Y><rsub|K>>.
    Then<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-shell-commutators> reads
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|b><math-ss|D><rsub|K>+<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i*b>*\<partial\><rsub|i>=<math-ss|D><rsub|K>\<partial\><rsub|b>>.

    We next extend the collinear identity. Its angular sum has derivatives of
    the same form, by this commutator. Hence all its jets vanish on a line.
    In a coordinate plane write <math|\<Phi\><rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,s|)>=S*<around|(|u*<around|(|t*e<rsub|a>+s*e<rsub|b>|)>,v<around|(|0|)>|)>>
    and <math|<around|(|\<varrho\>,\<theta\>|)>> for polar coordinates. Put
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,s|)>=S*<around|(|<around|(|e<rsup|-\<theta\><math-ss|D><rsub|K>>*u|)>*<around|(|\<varrho\>*e<rsub|a>|)>,<around|(|e<rsup|-\<theta\><math-ss|D><rsub|K>>*v|)><around|(|0|)>|)>>.
    For positive separation the axial function is a matrix coefficient of
    <math|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>, after fixed
    positive-time damping of its end vectors. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>
    gives its analyticity, so<nbsp><math|\<Psi\>> is analytic. The angular
    equation holds for<nbsp><math|\<Psi\>>, and
    <math|\<cal-F\>=\<Phi\>-\<Psi\>> vanishes on the axis. Its angular jets
    give the recurrence

    <\equation*>
      t*\<partial\><rsub|s><rsup|m+1>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,0|)>=-\<partial\><rsub|s><rsup|m>*<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|<math-ss|D><rsub|K>u,v>+\<cal-F\><rsub|u,<math-ss|D><rsub|K>v>|)>*<around|(|t,0|)>+m*\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<partial\><rsub|s><rsup|m-1>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,0|)>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|m=0> omit the last term. Induction, for all labels at once,
    gives every transverse Taylor coefficient of<nbsp><math|\<cal-F\>> equal
    to zero. In a reflection Hilbert space let <math|v<around|(|s|)>> be a
    one-insertion vector translated transversely. Its Gram kernel depends on
    <math|s<rprime|'>-s> and has just these analytic Taylor coefficients. On
    a compact positive axial interval choose <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> inside a
    common complex analyticity neighbourhood of the finitely many matrix
    entries of<nbsp><math|\<Psi\>>. Cauchy estimates for its Gram kernel give
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsup|<around|(|m|)>><around|(|s|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsup|-2*m>*<around|(|2*m|)>!\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|2/\<rho\>|)><rsup|2*m><around|(|m!|)><rsup|2>>.
    Taylor's remainder proves transverse analyticity, uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|s>. Pairing with a fixed exterior word proves analyticity
    whenever the moving insertion is earliest or latest in some coordinate.

    Every positional derivative of an angular sum is again an angular sum,
    with differentiated labels, by the commutator identity above. Hence if
    the angular sums vanish for all labels at one end of a segment along
    which each of them is analytic, they vanish along it, and so do all their
    jets at its other end. Fix a configuration with distinct first
    coordinates; such configurations are dense. Treat its points one at a
    time. Among the points not yet moved take the one with smallest first
    coordinate; it is strictly earliest in axis<nbsp>1 among all points,
    since every moved point has first coordinate larger than all original
    ones. Move it along<nbsp><math|e<rsub|2>> until its second coordinate
    exceeds all others; then along<nbsp><math|e<rsub|1>> until its first
    coordinate exceeds all others, throughout strictly latest in axis<nbsp>2;
    then set its coordinates <math|2,3,4> to zero in turn, throughout
    strictly latest in axis<nbsp>1. Each segment is separated and transverse
    or axial to an axis in which the moving insertion is strictly extreme, so
    the angular sums are analytic along it. The final configuration lies on
    the first axis, where all jets vanish. Reversing the path proves the
    angular identity at the given configuration, and continuity extends it to
    all of<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>.

    Its commutator gives <math|<around|[|<math-ss|D><rsub|K>,<math-ss|D><rsub|L>|]>=<math-ss|D><rsub|<around|[|K,L|]>>>.
    To justify this inference, its defect has vanishing correlation sums, is
    real, alternating in<nbsp><math|K,L>, covariant under signed permutations
    of determinant one, kills the unit, and preserves the filtration. The
    finite free tests of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-erasure-test> are injective on this
    defect space. Their injectivity transfers by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth>;
    pulling the mixed defect back by the stable frames therefore makes it
    zero in sufficiently large degree. Injective inclusions give the same
    conclusion in all lower degrees.

    We now construct the representation. Let
    <math|C<rsub|i>=<math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K<rsub|i>|)>>
    and <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,i>=<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|D><rsub|K<rsub|i>><scaleframe|k|d>>
    for the six plane generators. By<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-charge-limits>,
    <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k>\<to\>C>. On endomorphisms put
    <math|\<Omega\><around|(|B,C|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around|(|B<rsub|i><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>C<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>>.
    An invariant inner product makes <math|\<Omega\><around|(|C,C|)>>
    self-adjoint, with kernel the common commutant. A real
    polynomial<nbsp><math|z>, with <math|z<around|(|0|)>=1>, gives its kernel
    projection <math|P=z<around|(|\<Omega\><around|(|C,C|)>|)>>. The
    operators <math|z<around|(|\<Omega\><around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|k>,C|)>|)>>
    commute with the relative generators: expanding the commutator with the
    sum of squares cancels the antisymmetric structure constants in pairs. To
    correct them, iterate <math|\<varphi\><around|(|t|)>=3*t<rsup|2>-2*t<rsup|3>>.
    The identities <math|\<varphi\><around|(|t|)>-t=<around|(|1-2*t|)>*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-t|)>>
    and <math|\<varphi\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>-\<varphi\><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|4*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-t|)>-3|)>*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-t|)><rsup|2>>
    show that, near<nbsp><math|P>, the errors square and the displacements
    have a convergent geometric sum. The limits are projections
    <math|P<rsub|k>\<to\>P>, each commuting with its relative generators
    <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,i><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>-<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>C<rsub|i>>.
    The bracket identity just proved makes the relative generators a
    Lie-algebra representation; the compressed operators therefore satisfy
    the same plane commutator relations and tend to zero. Their maximum
    norm<nbsp><math|m<rsub|k>> satisfies <math|m<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*m<rsub|k><rsup|2>>,
    so they vanish eventually. Thus <math|f<rsub|k>=P<rsub|k><around|(|I|)>\<to\>I>
    is invertible and <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,i>f<rsub|k>=f<rsub|k>*C<rsub|i>>.
    All operations preserve the real signed commutant. Set
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>=<scaleframe|k|d>f<rsub|k>> for one sufficiently
    large<nbsp><math|k>, and

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|d><around|(|h|)>=\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|h|)>*\<cal-V\><rsub|d><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    This is continuous, real and extends the signed action, with
    generator<nbsp><math|<math-ss|D>>. The global angular identity makes
    <math|S*<around|(|R<around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>*A;e<rsup|t*K>*x|)>> constant.
    Plane rotations and coordinate reflections
    generate<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>>, proving full covariance.
    Covariance, unit deletion, inclusion and differentiation then imply the
    unit, inclusion and vector laws by injectivity of responses. This proves
    all assertions, with <math|<math-ss|L>=<math-ss|D>> and the
    same<nbsp><math|c<rsub|\<star\>>> in every degree.
  </proof>

  Put <math|J<rsub|0>=c<rsub|\<star\>><rsup|-1>*J> and define, with
  probability Haar measure on<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-average>T<rsub|a*b>=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>><big|sum><rsub|i,j>h<rsub|a*i>*h<rsub|b*j>*R<rsub|4><around|(|h|)><around|(|J<rsub|0>|)><rsub|i*j>*<space|0.17em>d*h.
  </equation>

  This average imposes exact tensor covariance while retaining the normalized
  shell actions. The following proof establishes
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST> for this<nbsp><math|T>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Covariance and normalized tensor><label|lem:G3.2> For every field
    list, <math|x\<in\><Conf|n>> and <math|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>, the table
    and the tensor<nbsp><math|T> defined in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-average> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|n>*<around|(|R<rsub|d<rsub|i>><around|(|h|)>*A<rsub|i>;h*x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|4><around|(|h|)>*T<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>h<rsub|i*a>*h<rsub|j*b>*T<rsub|i*j>.
    </equation*>

    The tensor is centered, nonzero, real, symmetric and conserved and has
    both normalized shell laws of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3>, <reference|lem:G3.1>. Covariance was
    obtained at the end of the preceding proof. This is a finite-dimensional
    integral of a continuous function on a compact group. Haar invariance
    gives the displayed tensor law. Reality and symmetry pass through the
    integral, and the vector law for derivatives gives conservation. Changing
    variables <math|y=h*z> in each transformed shell, and using covariance,
    gives the translation and angular actions <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>> and
    <math|<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>>; for the latter use
    <math|R<around|(|h|)><math-ss|L><around|(|h<rsup|-1>*K*h|)>=<math-ss|L><around|(|K|)>*R<around|(|h|)>>.
    Integration preserves both identities. The positional derivative law in
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> identifies the translation action
    with <math|\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|b>>*S>.
    Thus<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-average> proves precisely
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>, as well as the lemma. The tensor is
    centered: covariance gives <math|S<rsub|1>*<around|(|R<rsub|4><around|(|h|)>*J<rsub|i*j>|)>=S<rsub|1><around|(|J<rsub|i*j>|)>=0>
    in every integrand. If <math|T=0>, its translation shell law would give
    <math|S*<around|(|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b>*A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>=0>
    for every label and separated spectator list.
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10> would then give
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>=0> in degree four,
    contradicting<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> and nonzero free
    differentiation of a quadratic curvature polynomial.
  </proof>

  Finally the charge normalization removes the
  factors<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|k>> from stress insertions. No new choice
  depending on the arity or derivative order is needed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Transported infinitesimal actions><label|lem:G3.3> Along the
    single stable sequence of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>, for every
    fixed <math|d,a,K>,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|k><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d+1>|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a><scaleframe|k|d>\<longrightarrow\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>,<space|2em><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)><scaleframe|k|d>\<longrightarrow\><math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>
    </equation*>

    in operator norm. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC> holds for every
    fixed arity, free list, subset of stress slots, component choices,
    derivative order and separated compact set.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D12>, <reference|def:D13>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>, <reference|lem:G2.2>,
    <reference|lem:G3.1>, <reference|lem:G3.2> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth>.
    The derivative limit is<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit>; the rotation
    limit follows from<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-charge-limits> and
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|k>/c<rsub|\<star\>>\<to\>1>.

    By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2>, the tensor<nbsp><math|T> is centered,
    nonzero, signed-tensorial and conserved. Since <math|J\<ne\>0> spans the
    line of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>, <math|J=c*T> for a real
    <math|c\<ne\>0>. Comparing translation shells gives
    <math|c=c<rsub|\<star\>>>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*T<rsub|a*b>|)>=<frac|\<xi\><rsub|k>|c<rsub|\<star\>>><around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>\<longrightarrow\><maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>.
    </equation*>

    Use these convergent free preimages in any chosen stress slots and use
    the fixed free labels in the others. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth>
    gives convergence of every positional derivative, uniformly on every
    separated compact set. The derivative there differentiates
    <math|x\<mapsto\>S*<around|(|\<b-A\><rsub|k>;r<rsub|k>*x|)>>, so all
    chain-rule factors are included. This is exactly
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>, for the fixed
    tensor<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-average>. The sequence is the refinement already
    chosen in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>; each fixed arity, degree and
    derivative order is covered on a tail of that single sequence.
  </proof>

  <section|Slabs, phases and reconstruction><label|sec:partII-H>

  We prove the phase decomposition of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH> and
  the reconstruction of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>, starting from the
  correlations of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV> and quotient fields of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>. The phase construction uses the
  rotations of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2>
  and the tensor of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>. The rotation proof in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.3> needs coordinate spectral estimates
  before continuous rotations are available. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>
  supplies them; they are not used later in this section.

  Reflection positivity enters at several stages. Repeated reflected
  insertions bound the operator that inserts a word into a time slab.
  Compressing these operators to zero energy gives a commutative algebra; its
  characters define the phases. A lattice variance inequality then makes each
  character invariant under reflection, and small chessboard roots preserve
  the rank of a finite family of field tests in every character. Neither
  conclusion follows from commutativity alone.

  Slab bounds control continuation to complex times and its boundary
  distributions. Vacuum uniqueness initially holds almost everywhere in the
  character space. Locality gives a phase-uniform low-energy estimate;
  continuity of heat pairings and full support remove the exceptional set.
  The selected phase is chosen by its Euclidean ground space before
  reconstruction, and retained throughout. The energy estimate is polynomial;
  no positive lower bound on nonzero energies is used.

  Throughout, a word is <math|w=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>
  with distinct points, and <math|\<tau\><rsub|z>*w=<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>+z|)>>.
  Fix a norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|V<rsub|d>>> on
  each coefficient space. Token bounds refer to representatives in the
  spaces<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>; the
  resulting operators depend only on their classes in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>>.
  Write <math|\<vartheta\>*w> for linear time reflection, acting on labels by
  the hypercubic action of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|s><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|refl>>>,
  and put <math|\<theta\>*w=\<vartheta\>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>> with the order
  reversed. Thus <math|\<theta\>> is the antilinear reflection in the OS form
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14>.

  <subsection|Slabs and phases><label|sec:partII-H.1>

  Put <math|E<rsub|t>=e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>,
  <math|P<rsub|0>=P<rsub|4><rsup|0>> and <math|\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
  in this subsection. Spatial translations on the OS space are denoted
  by<nbsp><math|U<rsub|y>>, <math|y<rsup|4>=0>. The insertion rule and buffer
  are those preceding Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F4>:
  <math|A<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>*e<rsub|4>>*v>,
  and an <math|r>-buffered word in width<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>> has
  mutual sup-distance at least<nbsp><math|r> and times in
  <math|<around|[|r,h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>-r|]>>.

  Write <math|\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*w> for the word with <math|A<rsub|i>>
  replaced by <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|i>>. The reflected-word bound
  eliminates the exterior-dependent constant when one takes arbitrarily high
  powers. This gives the bounded operators needed for compression. Below
  words are <math|r>-buffered, <math|d> bounds degrees, <math|R> bounds
  coefficient norms, and <math|h,k> are admissible slab widths.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Slab operators><label|lem:H1.1> For <math|d\<in\><N>>,
    <math|R\<geqslant\>0>, there are <math|C\<gtr\>0>, <math|Q\<in\><N>> such
    that each admitted length-<math|n> word has a bounded insertion operator
    satisfying

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|Q*n><space|2em><around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>\<gtr\>0,0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>|<cell|=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|h*e<rsub|4>>*\<theta\>*w|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)><math-ss|B><rsub|k><around|(|v|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|h+k><around|(|w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|h*e<rsub|4>>*v|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:H-slab-laws>>>|<row|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>>|<cell|=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*w|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:H-slab-derivative>>>>>
    </align>

    Derivatives are in operator norm; empty words give<nbsp><math|E<rsub|h>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F3>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:F4>, and replace<nbsp><math|R> by <math|max
    <around|(|1,R|)>>. The translation rule defines contractions: on the span
    of positive words,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|t>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|v,E<rsub|2*t>*v|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|2*t>*v|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    E7 bounds the separated orbit; iteration gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|t>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>
    and preserves null vectors. Translation invariance gives symmetry and
    commuting unitary spatial translations; Gram continuity gives strong
    continuity. Since <math|E<rsub|1>*\<psi\>=0> implies
    <math|E<rsub|2<rsup|-j>>*\<psi\>=0>, strong continuity excludes zero
    spectral mass. Spectral calculus<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorem<nbsp>2.5.1
    and Proposition<nbsp>4.2.3> defines <math|H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>=<big|int><rsub|<around|(|0,1|]>>-log
    v*<space|0.17em>d*P<around|(|v|)>> from the spectral measure
    of<nbsp><math|E<rsub|1>>. Positive roots and continuity give
    <math|E<rsub|t>=e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>\<to\>P<rsub|0>>
    strongly.

    For a nonempty word, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F4> supplies a
    width<nbsp><math|s> and <math|D=C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|Q*n>>.
    On the word span put <math|A=A<rsub|w>>,
    <math|B=A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>>, <math|T=B*A>. The OS form gives
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|A*v,u|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|v,B*u|\<rangle\>>>;
    the reflected-word bound gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|j>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|v>*D<rsup|2*j>>.
    Thus <math|d<rsub|j>=D<rsup|-2*j>*<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|j>*v|\<\|\|\>>>
    is bounded and <math|d<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*d<rsub|2*j>>.
    Taking suprema yields <math|sup<rsub|j>
    d<rsub|j>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>, hence
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|v,T*v|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    The bounded extension is unique by density. Right multiplication by
    <math|E<rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>-s>> gives the required width.

    Positive-word matrix entries prove<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws>. FC bounds
    differentiated-label operators in a smaller buffer. Twice integrating
    their matrix entries along segments gives an operator norm Taylor
    remainder; induction proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-derivative> and norm
    smoothness.
  </proof>

  With <math|e<rsub|d>> as time, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> gives
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>,
  <math|E<rsub|t><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>=e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
  and commuting transverse translations <math|U<rsub|x><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>>,
  <math|x<rsup|d>=0>, using only coordinate reflection positivity and signed
  permutations. Their joint projection-valued measure <math|P<rsub|d>> is on
  <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>\<times\><R><rsup|3>>, with variables
  <math|<around|(|E,p|)>>: apply Gelfand theory to the commutative unital
  C<rsup|<math|\<ast\>>>-algebra generated by
  <math|E<rsub|1><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>> and the translation representation of
  <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|<R><rsup|3>|)>><nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorems<nbsp>2.4.1,
  3.7.11, equation<nbsp>(3.89) and Proposition<nbsp>4.2.3>, then take the
  logarithm of the heat coordinate.

  For a word combination<nbsp><math|U>, let <math|v<rsub|d,u><around|(|U|)>>
  be its OS vector translated by<nbsp><math|u*e<rsub|d>> into positive times.
  Undo its damping by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-coordinate-measure>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>*<around|(|E,p|)>=e<rsup|2*u*E>*<space|0.17em>d*<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|d,u><around|(|U|)>,P<rsub|d>*<around|(|E,p|)>*v<rsub|d,u><around|(|U|)>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  This is independent of<nbsp><math|u> and finite on <math|E\<leqslant\>M>.
  Polarize to<nbsp><math|\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U*V>> on integrable tests. For
  nonnegative integrable<nbsp><math|g<rsub|1>,g<rsub|2>>, spectral
  Cauchy\USchwarz gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-coordinate-CS><around*|\||<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U*V>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><big|int>g<rsub|1>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>*<big|int>g<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|V><space|1em><text|if
    ><around|\||f|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|1>*g<rsub|2>.
  </equation>

  For <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>*U=\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*U>,
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>\<in\><around|{|1,-1|}>>, ordinary conjugation
  gives, with <math|x<rsup|d>=0> and <math|t> separating the words,

  <\equation*>
    S*<around*|(|<wide|U|\<bar\>><rsup|<math-up|rev>>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|d>+x>*U|)>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|d>*<big|int>e<rsup|-t*E+i*p\<cdot\>x>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>*<around|(|E,p|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here the bar includes scalar conjugation. Both point fields and collinear
  words are represented.

  Opposite reflection signs compare coordinate spectra. Fix a set of axes
  <math|Q> and a finite word combination<nbsp><math|U> whose offsets have
  zero coordinates in<nbsp><math|Q>, with
  <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>*U=\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*U> for <math|j\<in\>Q>.
  Assume that both signs occur. For even nonnegative
  <math|f<rsub|j>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>>, set
  <math|f<rsub|j,v><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|j>*<around|(|p/v|)>>. The
  next estimate is used in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Opposite reflection signs><label|lem:H-coordinate-signs> For every
    <math|i\<in\>Q>, <math|q\<less\>0>, <math|\<beta\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>0>
    and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>Q\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>f<rsub|j,\<lambda\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|j>>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|E+\<lambda\><rsup|q>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i><rsup|U>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)><space|2em><around|(|\<lambda\>=e<rsup|s>,s\<in\><N>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Constants may depend on <math|U,Q,q,\<beta\>,f,\<delta\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, <reference|def:D14>, E1\UE7 and
    the coordinate derivative law of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>.

    For a placement of margin<nbsp><math|r\<gtr\>0>, E7 bounds the
    <math|2*m>th transverse derivative of the reflected-pair correlation by
    <math|C*r<rsup|-D>>. Its centered difference has spectral integrand
    <math|<around|[|2*sin <around|(|t*p<rsub|j>/2|)>/t|]><rsup|2*m>*e<rsup|-2*u*E>>.
    The integral remainder formula and Fatou give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-coordinate-moments><big|int>e<rsup|-2*u*E><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|-D>.
    </equation>

    Extra heat supplies energy powers. Thus for every
    admissible<nbsp><math|u> and <math|m\<in\><N>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-all-moments><big|int>e<rsup|-2*u*E>*<around|(|1+E+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>\<less\>\<infty\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For offsets perpendicular to<nbsp><math|d>, take <math|u=t/2>,
    <math|r\<asymp\>t>. Integrating <math|C*t<rsup|-D>> against
    <math|t<rsup|D>*<space|0.17em>d*t> on <math|<around|(|0,1|)>> gives,
    enlarging<nbsp><math|D>, <math|<big|int><around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-D><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

    Choose even <math|\<sigma\>\<geqslant\>0> of integral one supported in
    <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>2>. For
    <math|a<rsub|j>=j+2>, let <math|L<rsub|j>>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>m>, be the Lagrange basis polynomials of
    degree <math|m-1> with <math|L<rsub|j><around|(|a<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|i*j>>.
    For radius<nbsp><math|\<varrho\>\<gtr\>0>, define the annular correction
    by

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<around|(|q|)>*g<around|(|q|)>*<space|0.17em>d*q=<big|int>\<sigma\><around|(|v|)>*<big|int>f<around|(|p|)>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>L<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|p/<around|(|\<varrho\>*v|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>*g*<around|(|\<varrho\>*v*a<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p*<space|0.17em>d*v.
    </equation*>

    This gives smooth annular profiles with moment coefficients containing
    <math|\<varrho\><rsup|-2*r>>. Since <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>L<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*a<rsub|j><rsup|2*i>=x<rsup|i>>
    for <math|i\<less\>m>, one has <math|<big|int>p<rsup|2*i>*<around|(|f-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p=0>
    for <math|i\<less\>m>; for even<nbsp><math|f> the residual has <math|2*m>
    vanishing moments. Put <math|<math-up|Lap><space|0.17em>f<around|(|E|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-E<around|\||t|\|>>*<wide|f|^><rsub|+><around|(|t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t>.
    Interpolating <math|<around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> gives its
    residual response

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-residual-response>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<around|(|E|)>=2*E*<big|int><frac|f<around|(|p|)>|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<big|int>\<sigma\><around|(|v|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|<around|(|\<varrho\>*v*a<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>-p<rsup|2>|<around|(|\<varrho\>*v*a<rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*v*<space|0.17em>d*p.
    </equation>

    At <math|E=0> its value is <math|2*\<pi\>*f<around|(|0|)>>. For
    <math|f\<geqslant\>0> supported in <math|<around|[|-\<varrho\>,\<varrho\>|]>>,
    all factors lie in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. For
    <math|\<varrho\>\<geqslant\>1> with <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f<rsub|v>\<subset\><around|[|-\<varrho\>,\<varrho\>|]>>,
    <math|f<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*f*<around|(|p/v|)>> and
    sufficiently large<nbsp><math|m>, the moment bound gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-residual-cost><big|int>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<rsub|v><around|(|E|)><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|v<rsup|-1>+\<varrho\><rsup|D>|)>.
    </equation>

    Choose <math|m\<geqslant\>D/2> after the moment exponent<nbsp><math|D>.
    Indeed, let <math|f\<geqslant\>0> be even, supported in
    <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> and equal to one on
    <math|<around|[|-<tfrac|1|2>,<tfrac|1|2>|]>>, and let
    <math|0\<less\>v\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>\<varrho\>>. Then

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|c<rsub|m>|E+v>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<pi\><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|v>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|\<varrho\>/E|)><rsup|D>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|E\<geqslant\>1,m\<geqslant\>D/2|)>,>>>>
    </align*>

    with <math|C=C*<around|(|m,D,<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>|)>>.
    The upper bounds follow from <math|2*E*<big|int><around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*d*p=2*\<pi\>>
    and from bounding the product by <math|<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|2*\<varrho\>*a<rsub|j>/E|)><rsup|2>>.
    For the lower bound, the product is at least
    <math|2<rsup|-m>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>> for
    <math|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>v>; integrate over
    <math|0\<less\>p\<less\>v*E/<around|(|2*<around|(|E+v|)>|)>>. The value
    at zero follows by continuity.

    Thus <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f=<math-up|Lap><around|(|f-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f|)>>.
    For <math|i,j\<in\>Q>, write <math|<math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=<big|int><math-up|Lap><space|0.17em>g<rsub|i><around|(|E|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>Q\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>g<rsub|j><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i><rsup|U>>.
    For <math|Q> containing all axes, the same product test gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-3>*<big|int>S*<around|(|<wide|U|\<bar\>><rsup|<math-up|rev>>*\<tau\><rsub|x>*U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k><wide|g|^><rsub|k,+><around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=\<varepsilon\><rsub|i><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|j><math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|<big|int>e<rsup|i*p*x>*<wide|g|^><rsub|+><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=2*\<pi\>*g*<around|(|-p|)>=2*\<pi\>*g<around|(|p|)>>
    for the even profiles, whereas the time integral is
    <math|<math-up|Lap><space|0.17em>g<around|(|E|)>>. Both temporal profiles
    have the zero moments required at collisions. For smaller<nbsp><math|Q>,
    choose an even cutoff <math|h\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>>
    equal to one near zero and put <math|g<rsub|k><around|(|p|)>=h*<around|(|p/T|)>>
    in omitted axes, then let <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost>. Hence
    <math|<math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=-<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g|)>>
    when <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=-\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>>. Subtract
    annular corrections <math|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>> from the two head
    profiles. Positivity of the residual responses gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-sign-transfer><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>;g<rsub|\<ne\>i>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>;g<rsub|j>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|j>|)>+<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g<rsub|j>-a<rsub|j>;g<rsub|i>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation>

    Expand the signed identity and discard
    <math|<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g<rsub|j>-a<rsub|j>;g<rsub|\<ne\>j>|)>\<geqslant\>0>.

    Write <math|\<lambda\>=e<rsup|t>>, <math|t\<in\><N>>; the
    index<nbsp><math|s> below counts visits. We prove a stronger response
    bound by backward induction along a finite walk
    <math|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|L>> in<nbsp><math|Q> with opposite signs
    at consecutive visits. Choose the walk so that <math|i<rsub|0>=i>, every
    axis occurs among <math|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|L-1>>, and
    <math|i<rsub|L>> has already occurred. Such a walk exists because both
    signs occur: insert an axis of the opposite sign between any two visits
    of the same sign. Put <math|V<rsub|s>=<around|{|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|s-1>|}>>,
    the axes visited before step<nbsp><math|s>.

    Fix <math|K\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. The induction
    claim is that there is <math|a<rsub|s>\<gtr\>0> such that, for every
    fixed family of even nonnegative compactly supported
    profiles<nbsp><math|f<rsub|j>>, every choice of exponents

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|s>*<space|1em><around|(|j\<in\>V<rsub|s>|)>,<space|2em>-K\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|s>*<space|1em><around|(|j\<in\>Q\<setminus\>V<rsub|s>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and every <math|0\<leqslant\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|s>> with
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|s>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>, fix
    <math|R<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1> so that the head profile is supported in
    <math|<around|[|-R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>,R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>|]>>.
    All sufficiently large subtraction orders<nbsp><math|m> satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-sign-induction>0\<leqslant\><big|int>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>>*f<rsub|i<rsub|s>,\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|s>>>><around|(|E|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>Q\<setminus\><around|{|i<rsub|s>|}>>f<rsub|j,\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i<rsub|s>><rsup|U>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>.
    </equation>

    The constant and the threshold in<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>> may depend on
    the fixed profiles, exponents, <math|R<rsub|0>> and<nbsp><math|m>. In
    particular, the claim includes nonpositive width exponents on all
    unvisited axes; allowing small positive exponents there permits
    subsequent visits.

    At <math|s=L> every width is at least one, so all transverse profile
    suprema are bounded. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost> with
    <math|r=0> bounds the integral by <math|C*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|L>>>+\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>*D>|)>>.
    Choose <math|a<rsub|L>*D\<less\>\<delta\>>; the lower bound
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|L>>\<geqslant\>0> proves the terminal case
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>.

    For the induction step put <math|i=i<rsub|s>>, <math|j=i<rsub|s+1>> and
    <math|P=K*<around|\||Q\<setminus\><around|{|i,j|}>|\|>>. Make the choices
    in this order:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>Take <math|\<alpha\><rprime|'>=a<rsub|s+1>\<gtr\>0> from the next
      case of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>.

      <item>Choose <math|r\<in\><N>> with
      <math|2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>P+max <around|(|K,\<delta\>|)>+1>.

      <item>Take the moment exponent<nbsp><math|D>
      in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost> for
      <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>>. The subtraction orders will
      be taken above the required moment and collision orders.

      <item>Choose <math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>=a<rsub|s>\<less\>min
      <around|(|\<alpha\><rprime|'>,\<delta\>/<around|(|D+1|)>|)>>.
    </enumerate>

    Correct the head<nbsp><math|i> at radius
    <math|R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>> and the next head<nbsp><math|j>
    at radius <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<alpha\><rprime|'>>>. The correction
    in<nbsp><math|i> is bounded in absolute value by a fixed even nonnegative
    annular profile of width<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>>: its
    finitely many coefficients contain only factors
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2*r<rprime|'>*<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|i>-\<eta\>|)>>\<leqslant\>1>.
    The second term of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer> is therefore bounded
    by the next case of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>, since the exponent
    in axis<nbsp><math|i> has become nonnegative. In its first term, the
    correction in<nbsp><math|j> is bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|r>*\<lambda\><rsup|-2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>>;
    its coefficients are bounded because <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>\<less\>\<alpha\><rprime|'>>.
    The other transverse suprema cost at most<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|P>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost> thus bounds this first term by

    <\equation*>
      C*\<lambda\><rsup|P-2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>+max
      <around|(|-\<omega\><rsub|i>,\<eta\>*D|)>>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|P-2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>+max
      <around|(|K,\<alpha\>*D|)>>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both residual responses are nonnegative.
    Applying<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer> with the positive annular
    majorants proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>; the finitely many
    terms may have different subtraction orders, each chosen large enough for
    the preceding integral identities.

    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction> at <math|s=0> with
    <math|K\<geqslant\>max <around|(|-q,max<rsub|j><around|(|-\<beta\><rsub|j>|)>|)>>,
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>=q>, <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=\<beta\><rsub|j>> for
    <math|j\<ne\>i>, and <math|\<eta\>=0>. Take the head profile
    <math|f<rsub|i>> to be the bump used in the lower response bound, and
    keep the given transverse profiles. On <math|E\<leqslant\>1> the lower
    response bound makes the stated integral at most
    <math|c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>> times the integral
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>. End on an already visited axis; all
    widths in<nbsp><math|Q>, including the head, are then at least one. The
    preceding upper and lower response bounds prove the claim.
  </proof>

  Fix <math|a\<ne\>b>, a line axis<nbsp><math|l>, and
  <math|\<lambda\>=e<rsup|s>>, <math|s\<in\><N>>. Set
  <math|c<rsub|a>=c<rsub|b>=-100>, <math|c<rsub|l>=-150> when
  <math|l\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>, and <math|c<rsub|j>=-300> for
  <math|j\<nin\><around|{|a,b,l|}>>. For fixed even nonnegative
  <math|h\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>> put

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|j>=\<lambda\><rsup|1+c<rsub|j>>,<space|1em>H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|p|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>d>w<rsub|j><rsup|-1>*h*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/w<rsub|j>|)>,<space|1em>I<rsub|d>*<around|(|U;q|)>=<big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|p|)>|E+\<lambda\><rsup|q>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U>.
  </equation*>

  Set <math|B=2<rsup|-4>*<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*\<vartheta\><rsub|j>|)>*U>
  for a word combination<nbsp><math|U> with offsets on <math|<R>e<rsub|l>>,
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|a>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|b>=-1> and the other signs
  positive. Let <math|J<rsub|i*j>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> be real, symmetric,
  <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial and conserved:
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>\<partial\><rsub|j>*J<rsub|j*k>=0> for
  every<nbsp><math|k>. The index <math|J<rsub|d*b>> in a measure denotes the
  one-point word <math|J<rsub|d*b><around|(|0|)>>. No Ward normalization is
  assumed.

  For the exterior estimate, consider tests
  <math|\<Psi\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>> such that, for some fixed
  <math|K> and every <math|L\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-scaled-tests><around|\||\<Psi\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L>*\<lambda\><rsup|K>*e<rsup|-\<lambda\><rsup|-c<rsub|d>>*E>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>d><around|(|1+\<lambda\><rsup|-c<rsub|j>><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>|)><rsup|-L><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Write <math|X<rsub|\<lambda\>>=<around|{|E\<gtr\>1|}>\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|j\<ne\>d><around|{|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>w<rsub|j>|}>>.
  The scaled Fourier\ULaplace tests in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.3>,
  with fixed polynomial prefactors, have this bound. All constants below may
  depend on the fixed words, tensor, profiles and exponents.

  These are the coordinate inputs to the rotation proof; their proof uses no
  result of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Coordinate spectral estimates><label|lem:H-G3> For every projected
    collinear<nbsp><math|B> and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I<rsub|d>*<around|(|B;-3000|)>>|<cell|=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<kappa\><rsub|d>>|)>,>|<cell|\<kappa\><rsub|d>>|<cell|=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|d=l,>>|<row|<cell|-c<rsub|l>>|<cell|d\<ne\>l,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|I<rsub|d>*<around|(|J<rsub|d*b>;c<rsub|d>|)>>|<cell|=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|d\<ne\>b|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|E*W<rsup|4>|<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>>*H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|J<rsub|d*d>>>|<cell|=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|4>|)>,>|<cell|W>|<cell|=\<lambda\><rsup|-99>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The pairings obey<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-all-moments>; one-insertion heat matrix elements are
    holomorphic for <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>t\<gtr\>0>. Moreover, for
    <math|M\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|d>*<around|(|J<rsub|d*d>;0|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|900>|)>,<space|2em><big|int><rsub|X<rsub|\<lambda\>>>\<Psi\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|U*V>=O<rsub|M><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-M>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  We prove the sign bounds first, then the diagonal estimate by conservation.

  <\proof>
    In direction<nbsp><math|l>, choose <math|2*u> separating the offsets and
    use <math|R<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-E<around|\||t|\|>>*<around|(|1-\<chi\><around|(|t|)>|)>*\<phi\>*<around|(|v*t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t>,
    where <math|0\<leqslant\>\<chi\>\<leqslant\>1> is compactly supported and
    equals one on <math|<around|[|-2*u,2*u|]>>, and
    <math|\<phi\>=<wide|\<sigma\>\<ast\>\<sigma\>|^><rsub|+>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<phi\><around|(|0|)>=1>. It satisfies
    <math|C/<around|(|E+v|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*v<rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-2*u*E>>
    for <math|E\<leqslant\>1> and small<nbsp><math|v>. For the lower bound
    integrate over <math|R\<less\>t\<less\>R+c/<around|(|E+v|)>> beyond
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>; for the upper bound drop
    <math|1-\<chi\>> and scale. The removed profile has bounded supremum.
    Finite moments<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-moments> permit the
    induction<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer> through an opposite transverse
    sign. Taking <math|\<delta\>=1> in this sign-transfer argument gives
    <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> when <math|d=l>. For <math|d\<ne\>l>,
    omit the <math|l> profile, apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-coordinate-signs>
    on <math|Q=<around|{|j:j\<ne\>l|}>>, and restore its supremum
    <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-1-c<rsub|l>>|)>>. Both signs occur
    in<nbsp><math|Q> for the specified parity. Taking <math|\<delta\>=1>
    gives <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-c<rsub|l>>|)>>. The same lemma
    applied to the point word<nbsp><math|J<rsub|d*b>>, <math|d\<ne\>b>, gives
    the second estimate.

    It remains to treat the diagonal tensor. Write
    <math|C<rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b><around|(|0|)>*J<rsub|c*d><around|(|x|)>|)>>,
    <math|x\<ne\>0>. Choose <math|D<rsub|<math-up|col>>> bounding the
    collision powers of these pairs and their first derivatives. For even
    <math|\<chi\>=1> near zero with compact support put
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|D<rsub|<math-up|col>>>*f<around|(|t|)>=\<chi\><around|(|t|)>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<less\>D<rsub|<math-up|col>>>f<rsup|<around|(|r|)>><around|(|0|)>*t<rsup|r>/r!>,
    and define

    <\equation*>
      R<rsup|0><rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>C<rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|x|)>*<around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|j>f<rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>-<big|prod><rsub|j>\<Pi\><rsub|D<rsub|<math-up|col>>>*f<rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|]>*d*x.
    </equation*>

    Taylor vanishing gives integrability; a zero Taylor factor recovers the
    unsubtracted integral. Average under signed symmetries and pair exchange
    to obtain<nbsp><math|R>. Conservation makes
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*R<rsub|i*b,c*d>> a jet at
    zero. Inversion <math|x\<mapsto\>-x> makes<nbsp><math|R> even,
    eliminating even divergence orders. To remove the linear orders, put
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|0><around|(|x|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j>\<chi\><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>>
    and <math|q<rsub|a*b,c*d>=<big|sum><rsub|i>R<rsub|i*b,c*d>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|0>|)>|)>>,
    and replace the position extension by
    <math|R<rsub|a*b,c*d>-q<rsub|a*b,c*d>*\<delta\><rsub|0>>. Reflections
    kill coefficients unless indices occur in pairs; permuting them proves
    the pair symmetries. The subtraction preserves these and cancels linear
    divergence. The Fourier transform, still<nbsp><math|R>, has divergence
    polynomials with no terms below degree three. Write
    <math|F<rsub|b,c*d>=<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>*R<rsub|i*b,c*d>> for the
    Fourier divergence polynomial. Symmetry gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j,k\<ne\>d>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*R<rsub|k*d,j*d>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<ne\>d>p<rsub|j>*<around|(|F<rsub|d,j*d>-p<rsub|d>*R<rsub|d*d,j*d>|)>=p<rsub|d><rsup|2>*R<rsub|d*d,d*d>+<big|sum><rsub|j\<ne\>d>p<rsub|j>*F<rsub|d,j*d>-p<rsub|d>*F<rsub|d,d*d><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Applying the same two contractions to the other index pair gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-tensor-responses>

      <\eqsplit>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>>|<cell|=R<rsub|d*d,d*d>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|1>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j,k\<ne\>d>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*R<rsub|k*d,j*d>=p<rsub|d><rsup|2>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>+D<rsub|1>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|2>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|j,k,r,s\<ne\>d>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*p<rsub|r>*p<rsub|s>*R<rsub|s*k,r*j>=p<rsub|d><rsup|2>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|1>+D<rsub|2>.>>>>
      </eqsplit>
    </equation>

    Here multiplication by <math|p<rsub|j>> acts on the test, and
    <math|D<rsub|1>,D<rsub|2>> are the polynomial remainders.

    We also need profiles that do not annihilate the remainders. On a fixed
    temporal annulus, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-coordinate-signs>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS> bound each <math|R<rsub|a*b,c*d>> by
    <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>>. For a
    diagonal entry, divide the annular profile by <math|p<rsub|d>> and move
    the derivative to the label, which then has both signs. Moment
    subtraction justifies this integration by parts at the collision. To
    remove the remaining zero-moment restrictions, expand one transverse
    profile into its residual and its fixed annular correction. For an odd
    profile<nbsp><math|f> supported in <math|<around|[|-r<rsub|0>,r<rsub|0>|]>>,
    use <math|p<rsup|-1>*\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*<around|(|p*f|)>> as
    correction; for <math|f<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*f*<around|(|p/v|)>>,
    splitting at <math|E=v> gives <math|<big|int><rsub|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|0>*v><around|\||p*f<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>|\|>/<around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p\<leqslant\>C/<around|(|E+v|)>>
    from <math|<around|\||f<around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\||p|\|>>.
    The kernel factor <math|<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>> makes the even lower
    bound dominate this residual, including zero.
    Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer> control it. Each correction fixes one
    more transverse annulus with bounded coefficients
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2*r*\<beta\>>>, <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>0>.
    Induction ends after at most three coordinates; finite Taylor terms are
    bounded on normalized shrinking profiles.

    Since each transverse momentum costs at most<nbsp><math|W>, the
    fixed-annulus bounds for <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>,\<cal-Q\><rsub|1>,\<cal-Q\><rsub|2>>
    are respectively <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>>,
    <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>
    and <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|4>|)>>.
    The errors cost <math|O<around|(|W<rsup|4>|)>> and
    <math|O<around|(|W<rsup|6>|)>>: divergence has degree at least three, and
    the contractions add one and three powers. Choose one subtraction
    order<nbsp><math|m> above these degrees; its zero temporal moments
    annihilate the errors.

    Put <math|g<rsub|v,r><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*<around|(|p/v|)><rsup|-2*r>*\<sigma\>*<around|(|p/v|)>>,
    so <math|p<rsup|2>*g<rsub|v,r+1>=v<rsup|2>*g<rsub|v,r>>. Keep the
    transverse profile<nbsp><math|H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>>
    fixed and set

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|r><around|(|t|)>=W<rsup|-2*r><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|r><around|(|g<rsub|t*W,r>|)>,<space|2em>d<rsub|r>=W<rsup|-2*r><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|r>*<around*|(|<around|(|1-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,8>|)>*<around|(|g<rsub|W,r>-g<rsub|2*W,r>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Conservation gives <math|c<rsub|r+1><around|(|t|)>=t<rsup|2>*c<rsub|r><around|(|t|)>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>
    for <math|t=1,2>, <math|r=0,1>. The correction of
    <math|g<rsub|W,r>-g<rsub|2*W,r>> has zero zeroth moment and other even
    moments <math|O<around|(|W<rsup|2>|)>>, so the annular bounds give
    <math|d<rsub|r>=c<rsub|r><around|(|1|)>-c<rsub|r><around|(|2|)>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>.
    Reflection positivity for<nbsp><math|J<rsub|d*d>> makes the spectral
    representation of<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>> positive. On corrected
    profiles it gives <math|d<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|d<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>0>, <math|d<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0>, since
    multiplication by <math|p<rsub|d><rsup|2>> becomes <math|-E<rsup|2>>.
    Consequently <math|4*d<rsub|0>=5*d<rsub|1>-d<rsub|2>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>
    and <math|d<rsub|0>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>.

    The response difference is nonnegative. Subtracting
    <math|<around|(|E<rsup|2>+4*W<rsup|2>*v<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> from
    <math|<around|(|E<rsup|2>+W<rsup|2>*v<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-response>, with
    <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>2>, bounds it below by
    <math|c<rsub|m>*E*W<rsup|2>/<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>> for
    <math|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>1>; both vanish at zero. Since the response
    difference and <math|H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>> are
    nonnegative,

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|m>*<big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|E*W<rsup|2>|<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>>*H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|J<rsub|d*d>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|0>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>;
    </equation*>

    multiplying by <math|W<rsup|2>/c<rsub|m>> gives the third estimate.

    On <math|E\<gtr\>1>,<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-scaled-tests> gives
    <math|C<rsub|M>*\<lambda\><rsup|-M>*e<rsup|-t<rsub|0>*E>> for fixed
    admissible<nbsp><math|t<rsub|0>>. On <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>w<rsub|j>>,
    the inequality <math|\<lambda\><rsup|-c<rsub|j>><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>\<lambda\>>
    gives arbitrary Schwartz gain. Integrate by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-all-moments>
    and use<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS> for mixed terms. Finiteness of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|d><rsup|J<rsub|d*d>>*<around|(|E\<leqslant\>1|)>> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|\<lambda\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|897>|)>>
    give the crude bound. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>, a positively
    buffered insertion has matrix elements
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,A*e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>*u|\<rangle\>>>
    with bounded<nbsp><math|A>, holomorphic for
    <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>t\<gtr\>0> by spectral calculus.
  </proof>

  Rotations make spatial translations trivial at zero energy. Compression
  then removes slab width and placement.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Compression algebra><label|lem:H1.2> The operators
    <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>> are independent of admissible slab width
    and placement. They generate a separable commutative unital
    C<rsup|<math|\<ast\>>>-algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> on
    <math|ker H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>, with cyclic and separating
    vector<nbsp><math|\<Omega\>>. Its spectrum<nbsp><math|<phases>> and
    vacuum measure<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<xi\><around|(|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>|)>,<space|2em>S<around|(|w|)>=<big|int><rsub|<phases>>\<xi\><around|(|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The space<nbsp><math|<phases>> is compact metric and <math|\<nu\>> is a
    full-support probability measure.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2>. For fixed words and
    large<nbsp><math|z<rsup|4>>, the correlation is
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,U<rsub|<wide|z|\<vect\>>>*E<rsub|z<rsup|4>-c>*v<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Strong heat convergence makes fixed time shifts negligible, uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|<wide|z|\<vect\>>>.

    Choose an orthogonal reflection<nbsp><math|K> taking <math|y+e<rsub|4>>
    to <math|<around|\||y+e<rsub|4>|\|>*e<rsub|4>>. Since
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|K*<around|(|y+e<rsub|4>|)>,K*e<rsub|4>|\<rangle\>>=1>,
    its ray <math|t*K*e<rsub|4>> has positive time component. Rotating the
    displacements <math|t*e<rsub|4>+y+e<rsub|4>> and<nbsp><math|t*e<rsub|4>>
    makes their difference purely temporal, so their correlations become
    equal in the limit. Removing the extra<nbsp><math|e<rsub|4>> as above
    gives <math|U<rsub|y>*P<rsub|0>=P<rsub|0>> by density.

    The insertion identities <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h+t><around|(|w|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>*E<rsub|t>>
    and <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h+t><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|4>>*w|)>=E<rsub|t><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>>
    give width and placement independence of
    <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>=P<rsub|0><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>, and <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>=P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>>,
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=S<around|(|w|)>>.

    Successive use of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws> proves the cluster identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-clusters>lim<rsub|j\<to\>\<infty\>>
      S*<around*|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|1><around|(|j|)>>*w<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|m><around|(|j|)>>*w<rsub|m>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|m>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation>

    whenever <math|<around|(|z<rsub|i+1><around|(|j|)>-z<rsub|i><around|(|j|)>|)><rsup|4>\<to\>\<infty\>>.
    In the induction, <math|\<psi\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<psi\>> and
    <math|t<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>> imply
    <math|U<rsub|y<rsub|j>>*E<rsub|t<rsub|j>>*\<psi\><rsub|j>\<to\>P<rsub|0>*\<psi\>>
    for arbitrary<nbsp><math|y<rsub|j>>, by contraction and spatial
    invariance on the ground space.

    Place four words at <math|0>, <math|j*<around|(|e<rsub|4>+2*e<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|j*<around|(|2*e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>|)>> and
    <math|3*j*<around|(|e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>|)>>. Exchanging the first and
    fourth axes interchanges the two middle time orders. Covariance
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-clusters> equate the resulting products after
    simultaneous rotation of their labels and points. A second application
    with all displacements along <math|e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>> removes that
    simultaneous rotation. The middle factors therefore commute on the dense
    span <math|P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>>.
    This also gives cyclicity; the empty word gives the unit. If
    <math|a*\<Omega\>=0>, then <math|a*b*\<Omega\>=0> on this dense span, so
    <math|a=0>.

    Basis labels and dense countable position sets
    generate<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> by norm continuity. Gelfand
    theory and the vacuum state give its compact metric spectrum and
    probability measure<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorems<nbsp>2.4.1
    and<nbsp>2.8.2>. Separation gives full support:
    <math|<big|int><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|a|)>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<nu\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|a*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
    vanishes only for <math|a=0>.
  </proof>

  A generic grid word has positions in <math|a<rsub|k><Z><rsup|4>> with
  pairwise distinct coordinates along each axis. For real coefficient
  representatives<nbsp><math|<around|(|d<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>|)>> write
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>> for the Wilson
  expectation of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:wilson>, and put
  <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p><around|(|w|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i>O<rsup|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|N>|)>>.
  The word differences used below are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-real-approx>f<rsub|j,N,p,T>=\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*w|)>-\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*\<vartheta\>*w|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>=T*j*a<rsub|k>*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>.
  </equation>

  For each degree bound and lower grid level<nbsp><math|m>,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-bounded-real> chooses <math|k\<geqslant\>m>
  before<nbsp><math|w>; these are the cofinal grids used below. For
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> it supplies real <math|g<rsub|j,N,p,T>> and a
  finite <math|R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>, independent of <math|N,p,T,j>, such
  that <math|<around|\||g<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>
  eventually in the ordered cutoffs for each fixed<nbsp><math|T,j>. For each
  <math|i\<less\>j> and sufficiently large<nbsp><math|T>, eventually in the
  ordered cutoffs,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<around|\<langle\>|g<rsub|i>*g<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>-<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The threshold may depend on <math|T,i,j>. Bounded<nbsp><math|g<rsub|i>>
  control the diagonal variance terms, for which <math|f<rsub|i><rsup|2>> has
  no uniform bound.

  Lattice variance gives characterwise reflection invariance; chessboard
  roots preserve the quotient fields.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Phase fields and selection><label|lem:H1.3> For every
    <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>, its table satisfies E1\UE7, retains the
    inclusions, derivatives, conjugation, rotations and stress laws of the
    mixture, and

    <\equation*>
      <nullspace|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>=<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>*<space|2em><around|(|d\<in\><N>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each separated value is continuous in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. For
    <math|\<nu\>>-almost every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, the time-axis OS ground
    space is one-dimensional.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We first prove the operator identities

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-reflection>M<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<vartheta\>*w|)>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>,<space|2em>M<rsup|0>*<around|(|h*w|)>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*<space|1em><around|(|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>, the point limits of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>. For a real
    grid word set <math|Z=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>-M<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<vartheta\>*w|)>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws> gives <math|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>=-Z>, so
    <math|a=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,Z<rsup|2>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=-<around|\<\|\|\>|Z*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>0>.
    Lattice variance gives

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\><around*|\<langle\>|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|q>g<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|q+1|)>*R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>+2*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j><around*|(|<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>+\<varepsilon\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For fixed <math|q,T>, intersect the finitely many eventual sets for the
    bounds and pair estimates. Take the ordered cutoffs, then
    <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>. Expand each pair into four separated moments;
    their signed sum tends to<nbsp><math|a> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-clusters>.
    Thus <math|0\<leqslant\><around|(|q+1|)>*<around|[|R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>+q*<around|(|a+\<varepsilon\>|)>|]>>.
    First <math|q\<to\>\<infty\>>, then <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>,
    gives <math|a\<geqslant\>0>. Separation of<nbsp><math|\<Omega\>> gives
    <math|Z=0>. Grid density, norm continuity and multilinearity give all
    words.

    The cluster identity gives simultaneous orthogonal invariance of
    two-factor vacuum entries along rays with positive time before and after
    rotation. Such a ray exists unless time is reversed, when composition
    with<nbsp><math|\<vartheta\>> suffices. Writing
    <math|v=M<rsup|0><around|(|u|)>*\<Omega\>> and
    <math|x=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>>, simultaneous invariance and
    time-reflection invariance give <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,x|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|g<rsup|-1>*v,\<vartheta\>*g<rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|v,g*\<vartheta\>*g<rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>>;
    transformations on vectors act on their words. Density gives every
    hyperplane reflection. Successively fixing basis images expresses an
    orthogonal map as at most four reflections, proving the second identity.

    Use also Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, <reference|def:D14>, FC
    and ST. Testing on positive words gives the permutation, unit and
    linearity identities and annihilates mixed null labels. Thus
    <math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>\<subset\><nullspace|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>>.
    Character evaluation gives reality, covariance and smooth phase values,
    continuous in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>.

    For positive words<nbsp><math|w<rsub|i>> in a common slab set
    <math|Y<rsub|i>=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>. Direct evaluation of the OS form gives

    <\equation*>
      M<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>*w<rsub|j>|)>=Y<rsub|i><rsup|\<ast\>>*Y<rsub|j>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c|\<bar\>><rsub|i>*c<rsub|j>*M<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>*w<rsub|j>|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*Y<rsub|j>|)>\<geqslant\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Spectral permanence and character evaluation preserve
    positivity<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorems<nbsp>2.5.1(1)
    and<nbsp>2.6.4>; coordinate rotations give every axis. Complete this
    continuous positive Gram kernel. Integrating its vectors against compact
    positive-time joint tests expresses the smeared OS form as a norm square.

    Inclusions and derivatives pass through
    <math|T\<mapsto\>\<xi\>*<around|(|P<rsub|0>*T\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>|)>>. Put a stress shell and its insertions
    in one buffered slab. Its weights vanish near collisions, so the operator
    integral exists. Testing against exterior positive words gives ST;
    character evaluation gives both phase shell laws. The bound
    <math|<around|\||S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    also for derivative labels, gives E7 and separated-support integrability.

    For the reverse inclusion use the rows of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>, corrected lifts of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10> and coefficient limits of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. Each row of trial<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>
    consists of a singleton<nbsp><math|u> and optional
    spectator<nbsp><math|v>, placed at the comparison points
    <math|r<rsub|\<ell\>>*y<rsup|\<ell\>,i>>. Write
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|I><rsup|\<ell\>>> for the Maxwell moment of the
    corresponding row labels in slots<nbsp><math|I> at the unscaled
    points<nbsp><math|y<rsup|\<ell\>,i>>. Set
    <math|s=\<cal-G\><rsup|\<ell\>><rsub|<around|{|u|}>>>,
    <math|s<rprime|'>=\<cal-G\><rsup|\<ell\>><rsub|<around|{|v|}>>> and
    <math|c=\<cal-G\><rsup|\<ell\>><rsub|<around|{|u,v|}>>-s*s<rprime|'>>.
    Omit terms involving<nbsp><math|v> in singleton rows.
    In<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> put

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\><rsub|1>=M<rsup|0><around|(|u|)>-s*1,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rsub|1><rprime|'>=M<rsup|0><around|(|v|)>-s<rprime|'>*1,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rsub|2>=M<rsup|0>*<around|(|u*v|)>-s*M<rsup|0><around|(|v|)>-s<rprime|'>*M<rsup|0><around|(|u|)>+<around|(|s*s<rprime|'>-c|)>*1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> to <math|2*j> alternating conjugated copies
    of each polynomial in<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative>, translated by multiples
    of <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ell\>>>*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>>. Translating the
    periodic pattern and reality preserve the centered norm
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>>> under signed
    images and conjugation. Large translations put the copies in distinct
    cells, with bound <math|<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>+\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|2*j>>.
    Expand, take ordered coefficient and point limits,
    then<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-clusters>. Reflections identify the alternating
    copies with adjoints. Let <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0> to obtain

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-moments><big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\>|)>|\|><rsup|2*j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>><rsup|2*j>*<space|2em><around|(|j\<in\><N>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    A value <math|<around|\||\<xi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<Delta\>|)>|\|>\<gtr\>b\<gtr\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>>
    would give an open set<nbsp><math|O> of positive measure with
    <math|\<nu\><around|(|O|)>*b<rsup|2*j>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>><rsup|2*j>>
    for all<nbsp><math|j>, contradicting<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-moments>. Thus
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|1>|)>|\|>,<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|1><rprime|'>|)>|\|>,<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|2>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>>
    in every phase. Expanding <math|\<Delta\><rsub|2>> gives
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\>*<around|(|M<rsup|0>*<around|(|u*v|)>|)>-s*s<rprime|'>-c|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||s|\|>+<around|\||s<rprime|'>|\|>|)>>.

    Phase singleton and pair evaluations of the corrected lifts converge to
    the injective free row map. Its positive minimum on the unit sphere makes
    these maps eventually injective. They factor through
    <math|V<rsub|d>/<nullspace|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>>. The first null
    inclusion and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension> bound its dimension above by
    <math|dim <maxwellspace|d>>; injectivity bounds it below. Thus the null
    spaces coincide.

    Reflection positivity and E7 construct the phase heat semigroup as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>. Write <math|<around|[|b|]><rsub|\<xi\>>>
    for a positive-word class and <math|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
    for its ground projection. Strong heat convergence and the mixture
    identity give, by dominated convergence,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>><around|[|b|]><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0><around|[|b|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\||S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The heat entries decrease from their zero-time bounds. The integrands
    differ by <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>><around|[|b|]><rsub|\<xi\>>-S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    It vanishes almost everywhere. Rational configurations and basis
    coefficients give a countable dense family in every phase OS space, by
    Gram continuity. Outside the union of its null sets the ground range is
    <math|<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>.
  </proof>

  Choose <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> from the full-measure set in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>. Only its ground-space dimension enters
  this choice.

  The slab bound passes to every phase with the same constants. The proof
  transfers the repeated insertion estimate through the character and applies
  the power argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Phase slab operators><label|cor:H-phase-slabs><label|cor:H-phase-slab>
    For every <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>> the operators <math|A<rsub|w>>
    extend to bounded <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>>
    on <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    satisfying<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-derivative>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    In the phase OS space put <math|v<rsub|j>=<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v>.
    The mixed-space map <math|Y<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>=<math-ss|B><around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>> equals
    <math|<around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|)><rsup|j>*Y<around|(|v|)>>.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|v<rsub|j>|]><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<xi\>*<around*|(|Y<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*Y<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<around|(|v|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|4*j><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The same power argument gives <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>>
    of norm at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>>.
    The dense-vector argument gives its identities and norm derivatives, with
    the original constants.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2> gives the spectrum, full-support
    probability measure and mixture formula. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>,
    using Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-bounded-real>, gives E1\UE7, equal
    null spaces, the common FC and ST identities and phase continuity. This
    proves Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>.
  </render-proof>

  <subsection|Reconstruction><label|sec:partII-H.2>

  Use the response labels<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>. With time reflection acting on labels
  through<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, the star is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-star><around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=\<iota\><rsub|d><around*|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|refl>><wide|A|\<bar\>>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The boost generators <math|-i<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K<rsub|4*i>|)>>
  and spatial rotations integrate on <math|S*L*<around|(|2,<C>|)>>, the
  simply connected cover of <math|S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>>. A full
  spatial turn acts trivially, giving descent to
  <math|S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>>. Inclusions intertwine the actions; the
  time-reflection sign of <math|K<rsub|4*i>> and antilinearity
  make<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-star> commute with boosts.

  For <math|\<cal-P\>=S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>\<ltimes\><R><rsup|4>> take
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|P>>=<math-up|id>> and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|lab>>*<around|(|\<Lambda\>,z|)>> the continued
  label action of<nbsp><math|\<Lambda\>>. In the field-word algebra, multiply
  label tensors and joint Schwartz tests by concatenation and tensor product.
  Its antilinear star reverses order and applies label and test conjugation.
  Allow finite sequences.

  Fix a phase and write <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<xi\>>=\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>,
  <math|E<rsub|t><rsup|\<xi\>>=e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
  and <math|U<rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>>> for spatial translation. The next four
  proofs follow the statements: continuation, stress, then comparison and low
  energy. The last proof establishes surjectivity of
  <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>, then the low-energy
  bound for the full zero-energy projection, then vacuum uniqueness, and
  finally irreducibility.

  Positive-height operators give positive boundary distributions without a
  Euclidean extension across collisions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fields and boundary distributions><label|lem:H2.1> For every
    <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>, there is a faithful theory
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>> satisfying W1\UW3, the spectral condition of W0,
    and Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D14f>\U<reference|def:D15>. Its
    word-domain vacuum expectations extend to joint Schwartz distributions
    <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A;\<psi\>|)>>, and

    <\equation*>
      W<rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F|)>\<geqslant\>0
    </equation*>

    for every finite Schwartz test sequence<nbsp><math|F> in the field-word
    algebra. The adjoint and Lorentz label maps are the common maps specified
    above.
  </lemma>

  Surjectivity transfers vacuum uniqueness between the two Hilbert spaces.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|OS comparison and irreducibility><label|lem:H2.2> For
    every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, there is a unique linear isometry
    <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>:\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>\<to\>\<cal-K\><rsub|\<xi\>>>
    with the boundary-word values specified before
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>. It is surjective, hence unitary,
    intertwines spatial translations and heat semigroups, and both
    zero-energy spaces are vacuum lines; in particular the theory of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> satisfies W0. A bounded
    operator<nbsp><math|B> is scalar if and only if, for every smeared
    field<nbsp><math|\<Phi\>> and <math|u,v\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, one
    has <math|u\<in\><math-up|Dom><space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>> and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*u,B*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,B*\<Phi\>*v|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  Locality controls low energies uniformly in the phase; full support then
  extends vacuum uniqueness to every character.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform low-energy bounds><label|lem:H2.3> For every fixed finite
    complex combination<nbsp><math|A> of field words smeared with compactly
    supported smooth tests, there are <math|M<rsub|A>,M<rprime|'><rsub|A>\<geqslant\>0>,
    depending on its words, labels and tests, such that for
    every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>><around|(|<around|{|0\<less\>\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rprime|'><rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|s\<geqslant\>1|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Here <math|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>> projects
    onto<nbsp><math|<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>.
  </lemma>

  For the charge law, set <math|\<eta\>=<math-up|diag><around|(|-1,-1,-1,1|)>>,
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>=1> for <math|i\<leqslant\>3>,
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|4>=-i>, and

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>=-\<zeta\><rsub|a>*\<zeta\><rsub|b>*T<rsub|a*b>,<space|2em>T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b><around|(|f|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>,f|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|K<around|(|x|)>=k+V*x>, <math|V<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*\<eta\>+\<eta\>*V=0>,
  and let <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>> be its infinitesimal Lorentz label action.
  For real <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>> of
  integral one and <math|c\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R><rsup|3>|)>>,
  put <math|Q<rsub|K>*<around|(|f,c|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b>T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|4*b>*<around|(|K<rsup|b>*<around|(|c\<otimes\>f|)>|)>>.
  Suppose <math|m\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>> and
  <math|c=1> on a ball such that all <math|<around|(|y,s|)>> over its
  complement, <math|s\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f>, are spacelike to
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>m>. Then the charge law is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-charge>i*<around|[|Q<rsub|K>*<around|(|f,c|)>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,m|)>|]>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>*\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,m|)>-<big|sum><rsub|a>\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|K<rsup|a>*m|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  A common polynomial bound permits boundary limits under the phase integral
  and continuation of local stress identities.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Phase dependence and continued stress><label|lem:H2.4> For every
    <math|n>, label list<nbsp><math|A> and
    <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n>>, the map
    <math|\<xi\>\<mapsto\>W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A;\<psi\>|)>> is
    continuous and

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|<phases>>W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|A;\<psi\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=W<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|A;\<psi\>|)><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|W<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|mix>>> is the boundary distribution
    of the mixed continuation. In every phase<nbsp><math|T> continues to a
    symmetric Hermitian conserved Lorentz tensor of operator-valued
    distributions, with the local charge law<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-charge> under
    its stated support hypothesis. The selected theory and continuation are
    those at<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\render-proof|Continuation and boundary distributions in
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4>>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D14a>\U<reference|def:D15>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>\U<reference|lem:H1.3> and FC. Vacuum
    uniqueness follows in the comparison proof. For a label<nbsp><math|a> put
    <math|B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|2*\<epsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a*<around|(|\<epsilon\>*e<rsub|4>|)>|)>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> and Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:H-phase-slab>
    give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|a>*\<epsilon\><rsup|-q<rsub|a>>>,
    <math|0\<less\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>1>, uniformly in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>.
    For <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>2*\<epsilon\>> set
    <math|G<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|z,y|)>=e<rsup|-<around|(|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z-2*\<epsilon\>|)>*H<rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>*e<rsup|i<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z*H<rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>*U<rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>>>.
    For <math|n\<geqslant\>1> set

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-continuation><math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;z,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>,B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a<rsub|1>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j=2><rsup|n><around*|[|G<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|z<rsub|j>-z<rsub|j-1>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|j>-<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|j-1>|)>*B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a<rsub|j>|)>|]>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Choose <math|0\<less\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>1> with
    <math|4*\<epsilon\>\<less\>min<rsub|j><math-up|Im><around|(|z<rsub|j+1>-z<rsub|j>|)>>;
    set <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|0><rsup|\<xi\>>=1>. At imaginary times the
    insertion rule gives<nbsp><math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>> independently
    of<nbsp><math|\<epsilon\>>; the identity theorem extends this agreement.
    It is holomorphic in time and continuous in space; extend by zero outside
    ordered times. Taking <math|\<epsilon\>=\<delta\>/8> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-poly><around|\||<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;x<rsup|4>+i*y,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsup|-m>*<space|1em><around*|(|0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1,y<rsub|j+1>-y<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>|)>,
    </equation>

    uniformly in<nbsp><math|x> and<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>.

    To construct the boundary, write <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f|)>=<big|int><math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;x<rsup|4>+i*y,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>,
    let <math|\<rho\><rsub|j>=j>, and put
    <math|\<cal-A\>=-i*<big|sum><rsub|j>\<rho\><rsub|j>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|j><rsup|4>>>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-poly> and holomorphy, integration by parts gives
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+t*\<rho\>;f|)>=\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+t*\<rho\>;\<cal-A\>*f|)>>.
    Repeated integration of this identity gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-boundary>\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|m><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k>|k!>*\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+\<rho\>;\<cal-A\><rsup|k>*f|)>-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><frac|<around|(|-t|)><rsup|m>|m!>*\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+t*\<rho\>;\<cal-A\><rsup|m+1>*f|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The integrand is bounded by <math|C*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><rsup|m+1>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>/m>!,
    uniformly in<nbsp><math|y,\<xi\>>. At <math|y=0> this
    defines<nbsp><math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>. Dominated convergence gives
    every ordered boundary limit and a common Schwartz seminorm bound.

    The mixed operators of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> define
    <math|<math-ss|F><rsup|<math-up|mix>>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-continuation>
    and <math|W<rsup|<math-up|mix>>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary>, with the
    same bound. The mixture formula at imaginary times extends to strict
    times by the identity theorem and integration of Cauchy's formula. For
    continuity, set <math|z<rsub|j>=i*y<rsub|j>+\<varsigma\>*x<rsub|j><rsup|4>>.
    The common strip bound gives a normal family in<nbsp><math|\<varsigma\>>.
    If <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<to\>\<xi\>>, each subsequential limit
    agrees on the imaginary segment by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>, hence
    everywhere. Fubini and the common integrable bound
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary> give the distributional continuity and
    barycenter.

    Display the <math|j>th label in <math|\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f;\<ldots\>,a<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>|)>>,
    keeping the others fixed. Differentiating Euclidean
    <math|<around|(|4,i|)>> covariance and using the time Cauchy\URiemann
    identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j>\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f;\<ldots\>,-i<math-ss|L><around|(|K<rsub|4*i>|)>*a<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>|)>=\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|y;<big|sum><rsub|j><around|[|x<rsub|j><rsup|i>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|j><rsup|4>>+<around|(|x<rsub|j><rsup|4>+i*y<rsub|j>|)>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|j><rsup|i>>|]>*f|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It holds first at imaginary times, then by holomorphy. At the
    boundary<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary> removes<nbsp><math|y>. Along a real
    boost the derivative of simultaneously transformed labels and tests is
    therefore zero. Spatial rotations and translations retain their Euclidean
    identities, proving Poincaré covariance.

    Define <math|<wide|W|~><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>=W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>(<wide|b|^><rsub|+>(-\<cdot\><around|)||)>>.
    For compactly supported smooth frequency tests<nbsp><math|b>,
    differentiating the height and the exponential multiplier gives
    cancellation, hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-Laplace><wide|W|~><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>=\<psi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|y;<wide|e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|j>y<rsub|j>*p<rsub|j><rsup|4>>*b|^><rsub|+>(-\<cdot\><around*|)||)><space|0.17em>.|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation>

    The boundary formula includes the zero endpoint. Sending ordered heights
    to infinity kills negative time-tail momenta. Translation and Lorentz
    covariance therefore give support in

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-V\><rsub|n>=<around*|{|p:<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>=0,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|k=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|k>\<in\><forwardcone><around|(|j\<geqslant\>2|)>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|\<chi\>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<math-up|dist><around|(|p,\<cal-V\><rsub|n>|)>\<less\>2|}>>\<ast\>\<rho\>>,
    where <math|\<rho\>> is a nonnegative smooth unit-integral function
    supported in the unit ball. Then <math|\<chi\>=1>
    near<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>>, has bounded derivatives, and vanishes
    at distance greater than three. For consecutive imaginary differences in
    the open forward cone define

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|z|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4*n>*<wide|W|~><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|\<chi\><around|(|p|)>*e<rsup|i*<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>\<cdot\>z<rsub|j>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Put <math|q<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|k=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|k>>. Then
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>\<cdot\><math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z<rsub|j>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<geqslant\>2>q<rsub|j>\<cdot\><math-up|Im><around|(|z<rsub|j>-z<rsub|j-1>|)>\<geqslant\>c<around|\||p|\|>>
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>>. On <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>
    this holds up to a bounded subtraction, giving a holomorphic Schwartz
    test. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace> identifies this
    with<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-continuation>. On the closed cone the damping
    multipliers act continuously on Schwartz tests. Thus joint tests admit
    continuous partial boundaries as any consecutive imaginary differences
    vanish. Set the arity-zero value to one.

    Tie the heights of two adjacent spacelike points, retaining strict height
    differences elsewhere. For their difference <math|<around|(|v,t|)>>,
    <math|<around|\||t|\|>\<less\><around|\||v|\|>>, an imaginary boost along
    <math|v/<around|\||v|\|>> gives imaginary difference <math|sin
    b*<space|0.17em><around|(|t*<space|0.17em>v/<around|\||v|\|>,<around|\||v|\|>|)>>
    in the forward cone for small <math|b\<gtr\>0>; all other differences
    stay in the cone. The opposite boost treats the exchanged order. Let
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|n><rsup|\<vee\>>> act on label tensors, so
    <math|\<cal-W\>*<around|(|\<Lambda\>*z|)>=\<rho\><rsub|n><rsup|\<vee\>><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*\<cal-W\><around|(|z|)>>.
    Define the two germs by

    <\equation*>
      G<rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|z|)>=\<rho\><rsub|n><rsup|\<vee\>><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|i*\<beta\>>|)><rsup|-1>*\<cal-W\>*<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|i*\<beta\>>*z|)>,<space|2em>N<rsub|\<beta\>>=<around|{|z:\<Lambda\><rsub|i*\<beta\>>*z<text|
      lies in that order's tube>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Choose the sign of<nbsp><math|\<beta\>> for that order. The cone
    inequalities make <math|N<rsub|\<beta\>>> a neighbourhood of the tied
    point. Real covariance and the boost identity theorem identify
    <math|G<rsub|\<beta\>>> with the tube function on overlap. This works
    along the path scaling real times to zero: spacelikeness and the other
    strict height margins persist. At zero real times, Euclidean permutation
    symmetry identifies every derivative on the totally real Wick slice,
    hence the germs. The overlap identities carry this equality through a
    finite chain covering the compact path. Letting the tied heights vanish
    gives equality on Schwartz tests supported in the spacelike set, proving
    locality.

    Apply the propagators and insertions in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-continuation> to
    the vacuum, with an initial heat interval, and smear against a word
    test<nbsp><math|b>. Reflection and the insertion rule identify
    mixed-height Gram entries with <math|<math-ss|F><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>>.
    Their limits as both height lists vanish are
    <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary>. The vectors are therefore Cauchy; their
    limits <math|v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>\<in\>\<cal-K\><rsub|\<xi\>>>
    have this Gram form. The common seminorm bound and product-test density
    extend it to joint tests. Hence <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|b>c<rsub|b>*v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0>.

    Complete the word form modulo null vectors to
    obtain<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, with
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|a,f|)><around|[|b|]>=<around|[|<around|(|a,f|)>*b|]>>.
    For null<nbsp><math|x>, the inserted vector pairs with<nbsp><math|c> as
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|x,<around|(|a<rsup|\<ast\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*c|\<rangle\>>=0>.
    Taking <math|c> to be that vector preserves nullity and gives the adjoint
    law. Test continuity gives strongly continuous covariance unitaries.
    Translations integrate to a nondegenerate representation of
    <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>=C<rsub|0><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>><nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorem<nbsp>3.7.11
    and equation<nbsp>(3.89)>; its spectral measure comes
    from<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Proposition<nbsp>4.2.3>. The Fourier
    support puts its spectrum in<nbsp><math|<forwardcone>>. Locality passes
    to the word domain. A zero-acting label has zero Euclidean correlations
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace>, symmetry and continuity;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3> and response injectivity give
    faithfulness. Only vacuum uniqueness remains.
  </render-proof>

  <\render-proof|Stress assertion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4>>
    Use ST, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3> and the continuation. The factors
    give <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|44>=T<rsub|44>>,
    <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|4*i>=i*T<rsub|4*i>>,
    <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|i*j>=-T<rsub|i*j>>. Euclidean symmetry
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-star> give symmetry and Hermiticity; differentiation
    and exponentiation of covariance give the Lorentz action. Continuing the
    derivative identity gives <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d+1>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*A,f|)>=-\<zeta\><rsub|a><rsup|-1>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,\<partial\><rsub|a>*f|)>>.
    Hence Euclidean conservation gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<eta\><rsub|a*a>*T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*f|)>=0>
    on the common word domain.

    For real<nbsp><math|c>, insert <math|c<around|(|y|)>*h*<around|(|t-t<rsub|0>|)>>
    into ST and integrate conservation on each side of<nbsp><math|t<rsub|0>>.
    The <math|\<nabla\>*c> term stays away from the distinguished point and
    remains bounded, so <math|<big|sum><rsub|b><big|int>c<around|(|y|)>*K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b>*<around|(|y,t|)>*T<rsub|4*b>*<around|(|y,t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y>
    is Cauchy on each side. Its jump is <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>*A+<big|sum><rsub|b>K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*A>,
    where <math|K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b><around|(|y|)>=\<zeta\><rsub|b>*K<rsup|b>*<around|(|<wide|y|\<vect\>>,i*y<rsup|4>|)>>.

    Continue this jump as follows. Fix strictly ordered
    heights<nbsp><math|y<rsub|j>> for all fields other than the current,
    including the distinguished field with index<nbsp><math|0>. For their
    real times<nbsp><math|t<rsub|j>> put <math|z<rsub|j><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>=i*y<rsub|j>+\<varsigma\>*t<rsub|j>>,
    and place the current at <math|z<rsub|0><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>\<pm\>i*\<varepsilon\>>.
    The center follows the distinguished time, retaining Euclidean
    separation<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Write

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<pm\>><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b><big|int>c<around|(|x|)>*K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b>*<around|(|x,-i*z<rsub|0><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>\<pm\>\<varepsilon\>|)><space|0.17em><math-ss|F><rsub|\<pm\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|\<ldots\>,T<rsub|4*b>*<around|(|x,z<rsub|0><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>\<pm\>i*\<varepsilon\>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,
    </equation*>

    where <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|\<pm\>>> inserts the current immediately
    after or before the distinguished field. Choose a common complex
    neighbourhood<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> of <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> preserving
    the other strict height differences. On
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*c> the spacelike germs bound
    the side integrals, uniformly for small<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>> and
    <math|\<varsigma\>> in compact <math|L\<subset\>\<cal-U\>>. Conservation
    gives

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|\<varsigma\>\<in\>L><around|\||I<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<pm\>><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>-I<rsub|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>><rsup|\<pm\>><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L>*<around|\||\<varepsilon\>-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    The limits are holomorphic. At small imaginary<nbsp><math|\<varsigma\>>
    the Euclidean calculation identifies their jump; the identity theorem
    extends it to <math|\<varsigma\>=1>. Test against <math|m,f> and take the
    continuous partial boundaries. Integration by parts differentiates
    <math|K<rsup|a>*m>, giving<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-charge>. Schwartz cutoffs and
    the joint bounds extend from compact spectators. For
    complex<nbsp><math|c>, its imaginary part has charge commuting with the
    distinguished field by locality. Constant and rotational<nbsp><math|K>
    give the required actions, retaining<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  </render-proof>

  <\render-proof|Comparison and low energy: Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3>>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:W0>\U<reference|def:W3>
    and<nbsp><reference|def:D14d>, with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>.

    The isometry <math|J=<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>:<around|[|b|]>\<mapsto\>v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>>
    takes vacuum to vacuum and intertwines spatial translations and heat:
    <math|J*e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>=E<rsub|s><rsup|\<xi\>>*J>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace>. If two linear isometries have these
    boundary-word values, they agree on the dense word span and hence
    everywhere. After surjectivity, strong heat convergence identifies ground
    projections.

    For compact frequency<nbsp><math|b>, let
    <math|f<rsub|y>=<wide|e<rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|j>y<rsub|j>*p<rsub|j><rsup|4>>*b|^><rsub|+><around|(|-\<cdot\>|)>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace> equates the norms and mixed pairing of
    its boundary-word image under<nbsp><math|J> and the corresponding
    positive-height OS vector. They therefore agree. Frequency-test density
    and closed range include every smeared positive-height vector;
    approximate identities give point vectors. Permutation and perturbation
    of equal times give all positive words, proving surjectivity.

    We prove the low-energy estimate for compactly supported words,
    subtracting the full zero-energy projection
    <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>=<math-ss|E><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|<around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>=0|}>|)>>.
    Let <math|A<rsup|\<dagger\>>> be its word adjoint, reversing order and
    applying label and test conjugation, and put
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|z>*A=<math-ss|T><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|z|)>*A<math-ss|T><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|z|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
    Bound all support times and spatial norms by<nbsp><math|r>. Set
    <math|R=4*r+1>, <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>> and
    <math|\<lambda\>=\<mu\><rsub|A<rsup|\<dagger\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>>.
    The common distribution bounds give <math|\<mu\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>+\<lambda\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>\<leqslant\>M>,
    uniformly in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. Covariance and locality imply

    <\equation*>
      a*<around|(|y,t|)>=<wide|\<mu\>|^><rsub|+>*<around|(|y,t|)>-<wide|\<lambda\>|^><rsub|+>*<around|(|-y,-t|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|[|A<rsup|\<dagger\>>,\<alpha\><rsub|<around|(|y,t|)>>*A|]>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=0*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>R,<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<wide|\<mu\>|^><rsub|+><around|(|z|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>z>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|p|)>>,
    and likewise for<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>>. Write <math|E=p<rsup|4>> for
    energy. The time average <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>><around|(|y|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|[|a*<around|(|y,t|)>-a*<around|(|y,-t|)>|]>/<around|(|2*i|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t>
    is bounded by<nbsp><math|M> and supported
    in<nbsp><math|<wide|B|\<bar\>><rsub|R>>. It is the Fourier transform of
    two positive pushforwards: <math|\<pi\><around|(|E|)>*d*\<mu\>> under
    <math|p\<mapsto\><wide|p|\<vect\>>> and
    <math|\<pi\><around|(|E|)>*d*\<lambda\>> under
    <math|p\<mapsto\>-<wide|p|\<vect\>>>, where
    <math|\<pi\><around|(|E|)>=<around|(|1-cos E|)>/E>,
    <math|\<pi\><around|(|0|)>=0>. On <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|\<pi\><around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>E/\<pi\>>.

    Choose a smooth momentum bump <math|b<rsub|u>>, equal to one on
    <math|B<rsub|u>>, supported in<nbsp><math|B<rsub|2*u>> and between zero
    and one. Its transform <math|<fourierplus|b<rsub|u>><around|(|-y|)>> has
    absolute value at most <math|8<around|\||B<rsub|1>|\|>*u<rsup|3>>.
    Fourier inversion and positivity therefore give

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|3><frac|u|2*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><around|(|<around|{|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||<big|int><wide|a|\<bar\>><around|(|y|)><fourierplus|b<rsub|u>><around|(|-y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y|\|>\<leqslant\>8<around|\||B<rsub|1>|\|><around|\||B<rsub|R>|\|>*M*u<rsup|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<around|\||<wide|p|\<vect\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E> on the spectral
    cone makes <math|b<rsub|u>=1> on the indicated shell. Summing shells
    <math|u*2<rsup|-j>> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-low>\<mu\><around|(|<around|{|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|3>*M*u<rsup|2>*<space|2em><around|(|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For <math|u\<gtr\>1> use the total mass bound. Since
    <math|e<rsup|-s*E>=<big|int><rsub|E><rsup|\<infty\>>s*e<rsup|-s*t>*<space|0.17em>d*t>,
    Tonelli gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-heat>0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-Q<rsub|\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|E\<gtr\>0>e<rsup|-s*E>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|A>*s*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>t<rsup|2>*e<rsup|-s*t>*<space|0.17em>d*t=2*C<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The heat pairing is continuous in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>:<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary>
    gives continuous Schwartz frequency cutoffs, with error
    <math|M*e<rsup|-s*N>> outside radius<nbsp><math|2*N>
    on<nbsp><math|<forwardcone>>. Subtract
    <math|<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>>.
    Almost-everywhere uniqueness and full support
    extend<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-heat> to this continuous pairing in every phase.
    Let <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>> to obtain

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>=\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>,
    density of compact word vectors gives
    <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>=P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>>. Surjectivity and heat
    intertwining give the OS vacuum line, completing W0.

    For irreducibility, let <math|B> satisfy the weak relation in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2>. The field adjoint law and iteration move
    words across matrix entries without requiring <math|B> to preserve the
    domain. For a word<nbsp><math|A>, put <math|u=A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>,
    <math|v=A<rsup|\<dagger\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>> and, in the upper
    half-plane,

    <\equation*>
      p<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|u,e<rsup|i*z*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>q<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|B<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,e<rsup|i*z*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*v|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    These functions are bounded and continuous to the boundary, where
    covariance and the weak relation give
    <math|p<around|(|t|)>=q*<around|(|-t|)>>. Morera and Liouville make their
    bounded pasted continuation constant. Hence
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|u,e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Heat convergence gives <math|B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>=b*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>.
    Moving words again gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|A<rprime|'>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,B*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=b*<around|\<langle\>|A<rprime|'>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>,
    so <math|B=b*1> by density. A scalar satisfies the relation by the
    adjoint law. This proves the equivalence in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2>.
  </render-proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>>
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2> prove the
    faithful W0\UW3 theories, Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D14f>\U<reference|def:D15>,
    the unitary comparison, vacuum lines and scalar weak commutant required
    by Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4> supply the uniform rates, phase averages
    and continued stress, retaining<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  </render-proof>

  <section|The trace law and non-free comparisons><label|sec:partII-I>

  The stress tensor constructed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G> has
  a trace whose leading free comparison vanishes. To determine its first
  nonzero term one must retain the next coefficient. We center the trace in
  the phase fixed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:phases>, identify its
  positive spectral measure, and prove Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR> by a
  fourth-order comparison with the free field. The coefficient combines the
  one-loop trace coefficient and the free curvature-square correlation. The
  spectral law distinguishes this field from the finite comparisons of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:NF> and the polynomial and photon
  comparisons of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.2>\U<reference|lem:I2.4>.

  Conservation determines the scalar curvature-square coefficient from the
  divergence of a lattice stress insertion. A two-dimensional linear equation
  removes the other tensor components; the Ward identity fixes the remaining
  normalization. An estimate on the divergence must be more accurate than the
  slowly growing coefficients of this equation. Adjustable stopping levels
  give a subsequence of every prescribed null sequence; positivity then
  removes lattice rounding and gives the full-radius limit.

  Finite mass-shell products have expansions with no negative logarithmic
  powers, which contradict the trace law. In photon Fock space, relative
  locality forces polynomial identities under momentum doubling. The trace
  law permits only sectors with at most three particles; their spectral
  growth is then too small.

  Throughout, retain the fields, inclusions and units of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10> and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>, the tensor of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>, and the selected reconstruction of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>. The symbols
  <math|\<tau\>,e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>,\<Theta\>,<tracetwopoint>> and
  <math|<traceamplitude>> have the meanings of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16>. Write
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>=\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>,f|)>>,
  and use the word domain of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:W1>.

  <subsection|Trace and spectrum><label|sec:partII-I.1>

  Centering must hold on the common operator domain, as well as in separated
  correlations. The proof below identifies the subtraction after
  continuation.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Centering as an operator identity><label|lem:I1.1> For every
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, on the word domain,

    <\equation*>
      \<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>=\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<tau\>,f|)>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>><around*|(|<big|int>f|)><math-up|Id><rsub|\<cal-H\>>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use unit deletion in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E1-E3>, the quotient
    calculus of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>, and the continuation and field
    realization of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>. Deleting the unit
    insertion leaves a Euclidean correlation independent of its position. On
    each ordered-time region its continuation is therefore constant in that
    variable. The identity theorem on the connected continuation domain
    <math|\<cal-Z\><rsub|n>> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D15>, applied
    successively in each time variable, identifies it with the correlation
    containing the unit. Boundary values and smearing give
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|u,\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4><unitfield|4>,f|)>*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|(|<big|int>f|)><around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>
    for word vectors <math|u,v>. Density and linearity give the identity. The
    zero Euclidean one-point function continues to zero and has zero boundary
    distribution, proving the centering assertion.
  </proof>

  We use the Fourier convention of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>:

  <\equation*>
    <fourierplus|f><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*\<b-up-p\>\<cdot\>x>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,<space|2em><around|(|<wavetransform>\<psi\>|)><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*\<b-up-p\>\<cdot\><around|(|y-x|)>>*\<psi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x*<space|0.17em>d*y<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  In particular, <math|<wavetransform><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>=<wide|<fourierplus|f>|\<bar\>><fourierplus|h>>.
  Write <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>
  for the joint tempered boundary distribution of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>. The next lemma constructs its positive
  measure.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Unique trace spectral measure><label|lem:I1.2> There is a unique
    positive tempered-growth Borel measure <math|<tracespectrum>>, supported
    in <math|<forwardcone>>, such that

    <\equation*>
      d*\<mu\><rsub|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>>=<around|\||<fourierplus|f>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum><space|2em><around|(|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It is Lorentz invariant in momentum variables, and

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;\<psi\>|)>>|<cell|=<big|int><wavetransform>\<psi\>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>>|<cell|=<big|int>e<rsup|-r*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|r\<gtr\>0|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the positive boundary distributions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>. For <math|v<rsub|f>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>>,
    translation covariance gives <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>*v<rsub|f>=<wide|f|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>*v<rsub|h>>.
    Here <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>=<big|int><wide|h|^><rsub|+><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<math-ss|E><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>>,
    with <math|<math-ss|E>> the joint translation spectral resolution of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>. Both sides smear the field with
    <math|f\<ast\>h>. Choose tests whose transforms do not vanish on
    successively larger balls. On such a ball define
    <math|d<tracespectrum>=<around|\||<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\|><rsup|-2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|v<rsub|f>>>.
    The displayed identity makes these definitions agree on overlaps.
    Polarization gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|f>,v<rsub|h>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><wide|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\<bar\>><wide|h|^><rsub|+>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum>>.
    Product tests determine the joint distribution.

    The spectrum condition gives cone support. Schwartz continuity, applied
    to inverse transforms of cutoffs equal to one on
    <math|<around|{|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\>R|}>>, gives
    <math|<tracespectrum><around|(|<around|{|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\>R|}>|)>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+R|)><rsup|k>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4> continues <math|T> to a symmetric
    Hermitian Lorentz tensor. Contraction and invariance of the unit
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>) make <math|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>>
    a Hermitian Lorentz scalar. Covariance of <math|W<rsub|2>> and uniqueness
    of the measure give Lorentz invariance.

    Tempered growth and <math|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\><sqrt|2>*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>
    on the cone make the Fourier\ULaplace integral holomorphic at positive
    imaginary time, with boundary <math|W<rsub|2>>. To identify it with the
    Euclidean continuation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>, take enough
    primitives to obtain continuous boundary values, using the polynomial
    boundary bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-poly>. Their zero difference extends by
    Schwarz reflection<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorem<nbsp>11.14>;
    holomorphic uniqueness and differentiation give equality and the Laplace
    formula. In particular <math|<tracetwopoint>> is real, nonnegative and
    nonincreasing.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Comparison at prescribed radii.> Use the scaling frames of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D12>: <math|U<rsub|i><around|(|d|)>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>.
  Insert <math|U<rsub|i><around|(|d|)>*A> at <math|r<rsub|i>*x>, and read
  tensors by <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><around|(|4|)><rsup|-1>>. Write
  <math|x<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> for nearest points to <math|x> in
  <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|n<rsub|i>><Z><rsup|4>>. Jets use positional
  derivatives of smooth interpolants and their normalized lattice
  differences, including derivatives of the inserted fields. A shell flux is
  the integral against <math|-\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>> in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>. Define
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>=lim<rsub|<around|(|N,p|)>>
  \<beta\><rsub|N,p,k<rsub|i>>> along the ordered limits retained in the
  proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>; the radius level <math|n<rsub|i>>
  precedes the insertion level <math|k<rsub|i>>.

  The next lemma extends Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1> to prescribed radii.
  Its proof also transfers the derivative argument
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> to these frames. For an arity
  <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>\<geqslant\>1>, degrees
  <math|d<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>D>, <math|A<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>\<to\>A<rsub|j>>
  and compact <math|K\<subset\><Gen|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>>, the comparison
  below holds for all such choices along one subsequence.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Prescribed radii><label|lem:I-radii> Every positive null sequence
    has a subsequence <math|r<rsub|i>> with frames
    <math|U<rsub|i><around|(|d|)>> making
    <math|<around|(|r<rsub|i>,U<rsub|i>|)>> a stable scaling sequence
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D12>), and levels
    <math|n<rsub|i>,k<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>, such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-adjustable>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>,<space|2em><frac|log
      <around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)>|\<beta\><rsub|i>>\<to\><slope><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|x\<in\>K><around*|\||S<rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>*<around|(|U<rsub|i><around|(|\<b-d\>|)>*\<b-A\><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>;r<rsub|i>*x<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>|)>-<maxwellcorr><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>><around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>|\|>\<to\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The same holds for each fixed jet and translation-shell flux. For
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>=2> without derivatives it holds uniformly over
    the phases.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, the source estimates of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>,
    <reference|lem:E-replication>, the displacement estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement>, and the finite-dimensional
    construction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>. Put
    <math|M<rsub|n>=<cutoffsize><rsub|n>>. At radius level <math|n> allow

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-reading>r*<around|(|n,u|)>=a<rsub|n>*M<rsub|n><rsup|u>,<space|2em>u\<in\><around|[|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|]>,<space|2em>k<around|(|n|)>=min
      <around|{|m\<gtr\>n:log <around|(|a<rsub|n>/a<rsub|m>|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The separation in <math|k<around|(|n|)>> leaves room for all fixed-degree
    marked transports; its logarithmic size is negligible compared with
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|n>>. For degree <math|D> and an arity bound
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>\<geqslant\>2> choose

    <\equation*>
      c\<less\>0,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|1>+6\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|2>+5\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>+c,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>+c\<less\>0,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-c\<less\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Choose the differences <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>\<gtr\>6>,
    then <math|-c\<gtr\><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>,
    then <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>\<gtr\>5-c>, and finally
    increase all three <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>> together until
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>\<geqslant\>D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>+10>. We first
    obtain comparison windows at every sufficiently late radius level, rather
    than only the intervals selected in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. For
    each of the three exponents <math|b=\<alpha\><rsub|j>+u-\<alpha\><rsub|0>>,
    stop backwards from <math|n> when the accumulated logarithmic block
    length first exceeds <math|<around|(|b+2|)>*log M<rsub|n>>, then stop
    again after another <math|10*log B<rsub|n>>. Impose
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log <refbeta><rsub|m>> as below. Each
    overshoot is <math|O<around|(|log log <refbeta><rsub|n>|)>=o<around|(|log
    M<rsub|n>|)>>, so the resulting cell side is at most
    <math|a<rsub|n>*M<rsub|n><rsup|b+5>>. All levels in these windows tend to
    infinity and their couplings have ratio tending to one. The accumulated
    source loss satisfies

    <\equation*>
      log <big|prod><rsub|m<math-up|in the
      window>>\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|sup<rsub|m<math-up|in
      the window>> J<rsub|m><rsup|-2>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|m<math-up|in the
      window>>log \<ell\><rsub|m>=o<around|(|log M<rsub|n>|)>,
    </equation*>

    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. Thus these windows satisfy the window
    hypotheses (W1)\U(W3) after Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral>:
    its initial rounding error is <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|-b>>, its covariance
    and healthy-chart errors are smaller than any required fixed inverse
    power of <math|M<rsub|n>> after entry, and its source
    recurrence<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-step> has total linear loss
    <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. Increase the entry levels for the
    finitely many orders at each stage before selecting the radii. At source
    radius <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|1/<around|(|8*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>> the
    resulting error is at most <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|-3/4>>. The normalized
    integral is bounded by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio>; its stage
    constant is at most <math|log M<rsub|n>/<around|(|16*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>
    after entry. These estimates are uniform in
    <math|u\<in\><around|[|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|]>> and do
    not require a window to remain in one stage.

    At its own normalization a singleton therefore has chessboard root at
    most <math|1+<around|\||<maxwellcorr><rsub|1><around|(|A|)>|\|>>. Indeed
    coefficient extraction at amplitude <math|e<rsup|2*q>>, <math|q=log
    M<rsub|n>/<around|(|16*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>, bounds its centered root
    by <math|e<rsup|q-2*q>\<leqslant\>1>. Expand a degree-<math|D> label in
    weight-homogeneous basis vectors. Changing from exponent
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>+u-\<alpha\><rsub|0>> to <math|u> multiplies a
    weight-<math|j> coordinate by <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*j>>,
    hence bounds this root by <math|C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D>>.
    Include all signed and conjugated basis vectors in the finite comparison
    request. The chessboard inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> then bounds
    their reflected products by the product of these roots. The same
    calculation with <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>> gives roots
    <math|C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D>>.
    Their cell sides are <math|o<around|(|r*<around|(|n,u|)>*\<delta\><rsub|n>|)>>
    for <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>=M<rsub|n><rsup|c>>. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement> only when every coordinate
    hybrid of the two physical configurations has periodic coordinate
    separation at least <math|4*r*<around|(|n,u|)>*\<delta\><rsub|n>>. The
    centered level <math|D<rsub|<math-up|cen>>> can be chosen with
    <math|a<rsub|D<rsub|<math-up|cen>>>\<less\>r*<around|(|n,u|)>*\<delta\><rsub|n>>;
    the insertions and their single-axis reflections are interior
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry>. At small radii these periodic
    separations equal the ordinary coordinate separations on the fixed
    compact set. The lemma bounds the positional difference by the product of
    the roots times <math|4*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>/\<delta\><rsub|n>>. With
    scaled mesh <math|h<rsub|n>=M<rsub|n><rsup|-u>>, the two errors are
    consequently bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>*\<delta\><rsub|n>,<space|2em>C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>*h<rsub|n>/\<delta\><rsub|n><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    These are the contributions of coordinate-coincidence strips of width
    <math|4*\<delta\><rsub|n>> and interpolation across one mesh cell;
    include a strip for each coordinate hybrid, a finite union of volume
    <math|O<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|n>|)>> since
    <math|h<rsub|n>=o<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|n>|)>>. Both vanish uniformly
    in <math|u\<in\><around|[|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|]>>. For
    the remaining error, use the corrected Gaussian source of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources> on every subset of the
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>> slots, as in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-source-root>.
    The weighted mode norm in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication> is submultiplicative at every
    arity. Coefficient extraction at the amplitudes just chosen gives
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>C*M<rsub|n><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>\<to\>0>
    for the complete reflected array. Applied to every repeated reflected
    product, these bounds control the norm of the corresponding zero-energy
    multiplication operator. Evaluation at any character of the phase algebra
    of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH> is bounded by that norm. This proves
    phase-uniformity for the two-point errors, including moving coefficients
    by finite-dimensional multilinearity.

    We impose on the entry bounds of row<nbsp>6 in
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> that both adjacent stage ratios
    obey <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log <refbeta><rsub|m>>. Thus
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|n+1>> eventually. Monotonicity of
    <math|M<rsub|n>> gives <math|r*<around|(|n,\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>r*<around|(|n+1,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|)>>,
    while <math|r*<around|(|n,\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>\<to\>0>. First crossing of
    any prescribed small radius, followed by continuity in <math|u>, puts
    that radius in one of these intervals. Diagonal extraction over degrees,
    arities, compact sets and jet orders gives
    <math|n<rsub|i>,k<rsub|i>=k<around|(|n<rsub|i>|)>> and frames, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a> for symmetries and units and the
    recursive correction<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-correct-frames> for inclusions. Fix
    the exponents before each extraction, and require
    <math|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|)>*log
    M<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>>.

    For jets use Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> and repeat the argument
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> on these frames. Choose finitely
    many tested free responses injective in degree <math|d+1>. Their matrices
    converge by the comparison just proved, so their left inverses stay
    bounded. Testing <math|r<rsub|i>*U<rsub|i>*<around|(|d+1|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*U<rsub|i><around|(|d|)>*A>
    and integrating by parts gives minus the correlation against the
    positional derivative of the test. These values converge to the responses
    of <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*A>; the bounded left
    inverse gives convergence of the coefficient itself. Iteration gives
    bounded coefficients for every fixed derivative order. The
    smooth-convergence argument following<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit>
    now applies. Integration on the fixed compact shell gives the flux limit.
    Minimality of <math|k<around|(|n|)>> gives <math|log
    <around|(|a<rsub|n>/a<rsub|k<around|(|n|)>>|)>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>|)>=o<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|n>|)>>. The
    running law of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>,
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-drift-sum>, gives
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>/<refbeta><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<to\>1>. Since
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|n>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<slope>log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|n>|)>>, the imposed bound on <math|u*log M<rsub|n>>
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-adjustable>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The fourth-order Ward coefficient.> Set
  <math|L=T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>>, <math|D<rsub|a*b>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b><curvaturesquare>>,
  and <math|\<Xi\><rsub|a*a>=0>, <math|\<Xi\><rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|c,\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|b*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>>:
  for <math|a\<ne\>b>. Simplicity excludes invariant linear terms and fixes
  the colour bilinear form. Reflections and permutations then leave exactly
  these three centered real symmetric <math|H<rsub|4>>-tensorial tensors.
  Recall <math|<curvaturesquare>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b,\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>>:.

  Before imposing the Maxwell equation, divergence has three coordinates,
  represented in component <math|b> by

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*c>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*c>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For diagonal entries <math|z<rsub|0>*<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>+z<rsub|1>*<big|sum><rsub|b,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*c>,F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>>
  and off-diagonal entries <math|z<rsub|2>*<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>>,
  the divergence coordinates are <math|<around|(|2*<around|(|z<rsub|0>-z<rsub|2>|)>,2*z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>/2,z<rsub|2>|)>>.
  Indeed Bianchi gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>>.
  Thus the functional <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>*<around|(|x,y,z|)>=-2*x-8*y>
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I-rho>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>B|)><curvaturesquare>=-<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>B,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>L|)>=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>\<Xi\>|)>=0,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>D|)>=-4<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  It annihilates the third coordinate, the Maxwell equation direction. The
  Maxwell quotient of this raw space is two dimensional.

  We construct the lattice insertions from plaquette curvatures. At a site
  <math|x> put <math|f<rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\><around|(|x;a,b|)>>-U<rsub|\<square\><around|(|x;a,b|)>><rsup|-1>|)>/2>
  and

  <\equation*>
    J<rsub|a*b>=<frac|\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|4>*<big|sum><rsub|c,d><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|c*d>,f<rsub|c*d>|\<rangle\>>-<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|a*c>,f<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><around|(|Y<rsub|b><rsup|x>|)><rsub|e>=\<b-1\><rsub|e<rsup|->=x>*f<rsub|b,e<rsup|<math-up|dir>>><around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  These are the initial stress current and left displacement. Let
  <math|<math-ss|S>=<math-ss|S><rsub|N,m>> be the leading analytic path
  action without its coupling, defined in
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:C-matching>. Set
  <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>>,
  where <math|\<nabla\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4>c<rsub|j>*<around|(|T<rsub|a><rsup|j>-T<rsub|a><rsup|-j>|)>>,
  <math|<around|(|c<rsub|1>,c<rsub|2>,c<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>|)>=<around|(|4/5,-1/5,4/105,-1/280|)>>
  and <math|T<rsub|a>*q<around|(|x|)>=q*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>>. Initially
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=0> and <math|J<rsub|a*b>-J<rsub|b*a>=0>.

  The averaging kernels must respect this particular difference. For odd
  block ratio <math|\<ell\>>, define commuting finite sums

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|a>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|\<ell\>-1>T<rsub|a><rsup|j>,<space|2em>B<rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4>c<rsub|j>*<big|sum><rsub|v=-j><rsup|j-1>T<rsub|a><rsup|v>,<space|2em>N<rsub|a>=B<rsub|a><around|(|T<rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>|)>*P<rsub|a>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|0>=<big|prod><rsub|a>P<rsub|a>*N<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|B<rsub|a><around|(|T<rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>|)>> replaces every
  shift by its <math|\<ell\>>th power; <math|B<rsub|a>*<around|(|T<rsub|a>-1|)>=\<nabla\><rsub|a>>.
  Write <math|F<rsub|a>=<big|prod><rsub|c\<ne\>a>P<rsub|c>*N<rsub|c>> and put
  <math|Q<rsub|a>=B<rsub|a>*P<rsub|a>*F<rsub|a>>,
  <math|Q<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|edge>>=N<rsub|a>*F<rsub|a>>. Since
  <math|P<rsub|a>*<around|(|T<rsub|a>-1|)>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|T<rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|0>*\<nabla\><rsub|a>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|coarse>>*Q<rsub|a>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|0>*<around|(|T<rsub|a>-1|)>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|T<rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1|)>*Q<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|edge>>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|a>-Q<rsub|0>=<around|(|T<rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1|)>*Q<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The last quotient is a finite Laurent polynomial:
  <math|B<rsub|a>-B<rsub|a><around|(|T<rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>|)>*P<rsub|a>>
  vanishes modulo <math|T<rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1> after multiplication by
  <math|P<rsub|a>>, because <math|P<rsub|a>> averages all residue classes and
  both <math|B<rsub|a>> factors have mass one. Polynomial division defines
  <math|Q<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>>>. The support radius of <math|Q<rsub|0>>
  is at most <math|56*\<ell\>>, its mass is one and its absolute coefficient
  sum is at most <math|32<rsup|4>>.

  For Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>'s stationary background
  <math|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>>, stationary substitution is
  <math|\<cal-I\>*F<around|(|V|)>=F<around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>|)>>.
  The stationary equation is <math|D<rsub|U>*<around|(|<math-ss|S>+<math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|block>>|)>=0>
  on the gauge-fixed fine variations. Retain the stationary coarse frame
  <math|h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>> from the coordinates preceding
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4>, and write
  <math|Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>=<around|(|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|e>>
  and <math|<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|U,h|)>=<around|(|Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>|)><rsup|h>>
  for the predictor in that frame. Its fine-coordinate differential is

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|f>*\<eta\>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*s>|\|><rsub|s=0>*log
    <around*|(|<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|e<rsup|s*\<eta\>>*U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>*<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)><rsup|-1>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For the root correction, let <math|\<tau\><rsub|x>*<around|(|y;\<eta\>|)>>
  be minus the uniform average of the signed sums of <math|\<eta\>> over the
  frame routes from <math|y> to the patch points, as in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>. Define scalar coefficients
  <math|b<rsub|x,e>> by

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|e>b<rsub|x,e>*\<eta\><rsub|e>=\<tau\><rsub|x>*<around|(|0;\<eta\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|y>w<rsub|y><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<tau\><rsub|x>*<around|(|y;\<eta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|g<rsub|x,e>=h<rsub|<math-up|fr>><around|(|x|)>*U<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|x,e>|)>>,
  where <math|\<gamma\><rsub|x,e>> is the fixed coordinate route from the
  embedded root <math|x> to <math|e<rsup|->>. The root map and its coarse
  gauge variation are

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|R<rsub|0>*\<eta\>|)><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|e>b<rsub|x,e><math-up|Ad><around|(|g<rsub|x,e>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|e>,<space|2em><around|(|d<rsub|V>*\<phi\>|)><rsub|e>=\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|->|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|V<rsub|e>|)>*\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|+>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  These formulas transport a fine displacement to the predictor and correct
  its root frame. Evaluate them at the stationary pair and set
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|\<ast\>>*Y=<around|(|R<rsub|f>+d<rsub|V>*R<rsub|0>|)>*Y<around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>|)>>.
  With <math|\<delta\><rsub|a>=T<rsub|a>-1>, transfer by

  <\equation*>
    J<rprime|'><rsub|a*b>=\<ell\><rsup|4>*Q<rsub|a>*\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|a*b>,<space|1em>Y<rprime|'><rsub|b>=\<ell\><rsup|5>*Q<rsub|0>*\<cal-I\><rsub|\<ast\>>*Y<rsub|b>,<space|1em>\<cal-E\><rprime|'><rsub|b>=\<ell\><rsup|5>*Q<rsub|0>*\<cal-I\>*\<cal-E\><rsub|b>,<space|1em>q<rprime|'><rsub|a>=\<ell\><rsup|3>*Q<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|edge>>*\<cal-I\>*q<rsub|a>+\<ell\><rsup|4>*Q<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>>*\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|a*a><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Starting with <math|q<rsub|a>=0>, this preserves
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>.
  The antisymmetric part, which need not remain zero, is likewise a sum of
  forward differences: its new correction in direction <math|a> is
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*Q<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>>*\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|a*b>>
  and in direction <math|b> is <math|-\<ell\><rsup|4>*Q<rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|tr>>*\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|b*a>>,
  in addition to the transferred old differences.

  For an analytic local function <math|F>, denote its degree-<math|d>
  polynomial read-out by <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|d>*F>: on a radial jet
  <math|<math-ss|j>> it is <math|<big|sum><rsub|v=0><rsup|d><around|[|\<varepsilon\><rsup|v>|]>*F<around|(|U<around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><math-ss|j>|)>|)>>,
  in the basis of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>. The weighted read-out
  is <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|t<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|fac>>\<cal-P\><rsub|d>*F>,
  using Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>. Symmetrize <math|J>, average
  over <math|H<rsub|4>> and conjugation, apply the degree-four read-out and
  marked transport, and subtract the mixture mean. Call the resulting field
  <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|L>>. The same operations on the local contractions
  defining <math|\<Xi\>,D> give <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>,B<rsub|i><rsup|D>>.

  <paragraph|The regulated Ward coefficient.> We first calculate the scalar
  coefficient before marked transport. For a chart function <math|F> define
  its Hessian Fourier kernel by

  <\equation*>
    <wide|F|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>>*<around|(|p,q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x,y>e<rsup|i*<around|(|p\<cdot\>x+q\<cdot\>y|)>>*D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|e<rsub|x,c,\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|y,c,\<alpha\>>|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Fix a colour <math|\<alpha\>>. For a family <math|F<rsub|b>> write

  <\equation*>
    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-L\><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c,b>i*<around|[|z<rsup|2>*t|]><wide|F<rsub|b>|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>><around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>+t*e<rsub|b>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c><around|[|z<rsup|2>|]>*<wide|F|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>>*<around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation*>

  Coefficients use <math|<around|[|z<rsup|r>*t<rsup|s>|]>*F=<around|(|r!s!|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|r>*\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|s>*F<around|(|0|)>>.
  Put <math|\<cal-H\><around|(|F|)>=\<cal-L\><around|(|F|)>-<frac|1|2><math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c><around|[|z<rsup|2>|]><wide|F<rsub|c>|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>><around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>|)>>.
  The Haar coefficient is <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=\<cal-H\><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y|)>>;
  the subtraction corrects the lattice derivative. Define

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<tfrac|1|2>log det
    <around|(|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>-log det
    <around|(|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>,<space|1em>R<rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>=\<cal-Q\><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>,<space|1em>\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>=-\<mu\>*R<rsub|\<Lambda\>><rprime|'><around|(|\<mu\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|H> is the left Hessian of <math|<math-ss|S>> and <math|d> is
  the infinitesimal gauge insertion. The next lemma computes this
  coefficient; its proof follows. For <math|a\<gtr\>0> and <math|r\<in\><N>>,
  with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\||x|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>
  in the kernel's lattice units, put

  <\equation*>
    \<vartheta\><rsub|a,r><around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|a<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|r><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For a chart function anchored at <math|o>, its weighted order-<math|r>
  seminorm is the sum of absolute link coefficients of
  <math|D<rsup|r>*F<around|(|0|)>> against
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|v=1><rsup|r>\<vartheta\><rsub|a,r>*<around|(|e<rsub|v><rsup|->-o|)>>;
  for a displacement use its link components. These weights control the
  spatial moments in the momentum derivatives below. Fix a level <math|k>, a
  weight <math|a\<gtr\>0> and <math|C\<less\>\<infty\>>. For
  <math|\<nu\>\<in\><N>> let <math|N<rsub|\<nu\>>\<gtr\>k> and let
  <math|<around|(|J,Y,\<cal-E\>,q,<math-ss|t>|)><rsup|<around|(|\<nu\>|)>>>
  be translation-invariant local data on the level-<math|k> torus of period
  <math|n<rsub|\<nu\>>\<to\>\<infty\>>, with
  <math|<math-ss|S>=<math-ss|S><rsub|N<rsub|\<nu\>>,k>>, such that

  <\enumerate>
    <item><math|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>\<equiv\><rsub|4><big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-\<cal-E\><rsub|b>>;

    <item><math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>
    and <math|J<rsub|a*b>-J<rsub|b*a>=<big|sum><rsub|c>\<delta\><rsub|c><math-ss|t><rsub|a*b*c>>;

    <item>the <math|a>-weighted seminorms of orders <math|2>\U<math|4> of
    <math|J,Y,q,<math-ss|t>> are at most <math|C>;

    <item>those of <math|\<cal-E\>>, and the deviations of the zero-field
    Hessians of <math|J> and <math|Y> from their free values on tests of
    weight at most <math|4> and <math|5>, are at most
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|\<nu\>>\<to\>0>.
  </enumerate>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Regulated Ward coefficient><label|lem:I-regulator> For every
    colour,

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-h\><around|(|Y<rsup|<around|(|\<nu\>|)>>|)>\<longrightarrow\>-12<slope><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>, <reference|lem:C4>, <reference|lem:C7>.
    We keep the contacts in the determinant identity. Write
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|(|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>,
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|(|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>.
    For <math|\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|s*Y<around|(|U|)>>*U> let
    <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|z|)>=\<partial\><rsub|s>\|<rsub|0>log
    <around|(|\<Psi\><rsub|s>*<around|(|e<rsup|z>*U|)>*\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>|)>>
    and <math|\<cal-V\>=D*\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)>>. If
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|U>> is the mixed derivative of <math|log
    <around|(|<math-up|gauge><rsub|e<rsup|v>><around|(|e<rsup|z>*U|)>*U<rsup|-1>|)>>
    in <math|v,z> at zero, gauge invariance gives <math|H*d=-\<cal-T\>>,
    <math|\<cal-T\>=D<math-ss|S><around|[|\<cal-J\><rsub|U>|]>>, hence

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*d=d*\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>+\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-T\>*\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The product rule for <math|D<rsup|2><around|(|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y|]>|)>>
    and cyclicity of trace now give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-contact><tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*D<rsup|2><around|(|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y|]>|)>|)>-<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y=D*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|Y|]>-\<mu\><rsup|2><math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-V\>|)>+D<math-ss|S><around|[|Z|]>,
    </equation>

    with <math|Z=<frac|1|2><math-up|contr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>,D<rsup|2>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)>|)>-<math-up|contr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|\<cal-V\>+\<cal-V\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>,\<cal-J\><rsub|U>|)>>.
    Contractions sum the indicated matrix indices. Thus the off-shell contact
    is retained.

    Here is the cancellation under <math|\<cal-L\>>. The leading quadratic
    kernel of an invariant scalar action at the required momentum order is a
    colour multiple of <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I-p\<otimes\>p>. Write
    <math|X<rsub|a*c*b>> for the coefficient of momentum <math|e<rsub|a>> in
    the diagonal polarization-<math|c> entry of <math|D*Y<rsub|b>>, with its
    colour indices contracted against the action kernel. Gauge covariance
    makes the mixed polarization term <math|-X<rsub|c*a*c>> and gives
    <math|X<rsub|a*a*a>=0>. In the tested variation the three terms, after
    summing the intermediate polarization, are

    <\equation*>
      -2*X<rsub|a*c*a>-X<rsub|c*a*c>+<big|sum><rsub|b>X<rsub|b*c*b>*<space|2em><around|(|a\<ne\>c|)>.
    </equation*>

    Summing over <math|a\<ne\>c> makes the first and second sums
    <math|-3*<big|sum><rsub|b,c>X<rsub|b*c*b>>, cancelling the third sum.
    Higher momentum orders do not contribute. This applies to
    <math|D*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|Y|]>>,
    <math|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Z|]>> and the further action variation below,
    uniformly on each fixed <math|\<mu\>\<in\><around|[|\<delta\>,1|]>>. For
    the first term on the left of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-contact>, substitute
    <math|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>\<equiv\><rsub|4><big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-\<cal-E\><rsub|b>>.
    The coefficient linear in the total momentum <math|t*e<rsub|b>> selects
    <math|a=b>; summing <math|b> gives <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>.
    Its additional total-momentum factor has zero coefficient linear in
    <math|t>. The defect <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>> tends to zero in the
    weighted seminorms, so its contracted coefficient does too. Here
    <math|\<equiv\><rsub|4>> denotes equality of field derivatives through
    order four. The correction in <math|\<frak-h\>> tends to zero by the same
    identity.

    We compute the surviving regulator term explicitly. For
    <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|x=0>> define
    <math|M<rsub|b>*h<around|(|x|)>=\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>>,
    <math|<around|(|D<rsub|b>*h|)><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=\<delta\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>>
    and <math|<around|(|T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|r|)>>*h|)><around|(|x|)>=\<delta\><rsub|b>*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<around|(|x|)>>
    on <math|r>-forms, <math|r=0,1>. Set

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>=<math-up|sym><around|(|\<cal-V\><rsub|b>-d*M<rsub|b>-D<rsub|b>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,<space|2em>W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=<math-up|sym><around|(|M<rsub|b>*d|)>-<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|<math-up|sym><space|0.17em>A=<around|(|A+A<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2>.
    The inverse identity above yields

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-V\><rsub|b>|)>=>|<cell|<math-up|tr><around*|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|D<rsub|b>-<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>+D<math-ss|S><around|[|Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>|]>+\<cal-W\><rsub|b>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-W\><rsub|b>=>|<cell|<math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)>+<math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>=<math-up|contr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*M<rsub|b>*\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>,\<cal-J\><rsub|U>|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The tested <math|D<rsub|b>> term tends to
    <math|\<cal-Q\><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>;
    each of the four <math|T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|r|)>>> terms tends to
    <math|\<cal-Q\><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>
    or <math|\<cal-Q\><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>
    respectively. This follows by summing <math|\<delta\><rsub|b>*\<chi\>>
    against the two Fourier waves: its symbol is
    <math|e<rsup|-i*t>-1=-i*t+O<around|(|t<rsup|2>|)>>, paired with the
    factor <math|i> in <math|\<cal-L\>>. Translation invariance identifies
    the local anchor sum with the volume-normalized trace in
    <math|\<cal-Q\>>. Consequently their sum is
    <math|-\<cal-Q\><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>+2*\<cal-Q\><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>.
    Differentiation of <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>> in <math|\<mu\>> gives
    the same expression multiplied by <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>, namely
    <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>>. We have proved

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-regulated-ward>\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<cal-L\><around|(|\<cal-W\>|)>+o<around|(|1|)>,
    </equation>

    with error uniform on every fixed <math|<around|[|\<delta\>,1|]>>.

    For the remainder, the inverse-kernel argument in the proof
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand> applies with the scalar regulator
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*I>: <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|r>*R<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|q|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|<around|\||q|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2-r>>.
    Differentiating <math|R<rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>=I> once more gives
    the same bound for <math|r=3>. Hypotheses (c)\U(d) of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator> give bounded weighted derivatives
    of <math|W<rsup|<around|(|r|)>>>. Their zero-background symbols and first
    momentum derivatives tend to zero, as does the zero-momentum first field
    insertion: the free curvature displacement cancels
    <math|d*M<rsub|b>+D<rsub|b>-<frac|1|2>*T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>> in
    the displayed formula. Put <math|z=<around|\||q|\|>+\<mu\>>. Taylor
    expansion gives factors <math|z<rsup|2>,z,1> for the zero-, one- and
    two-field insertions of these symbols. In the product rule they pair with
    inverse bounds <math|C*z<rsup|-4>,C*z<rsup|-3>,C*z<rsup|-2>>,
    respectively. Thus the scaled derivative bound through order three is
    <math|C*<around|(|z<rsup|2>*z<rsup|-4>+2*z*z<rsup|-3>+z<rsup|-2>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*z<rsup|-2>>.
    Unscaling the quadratic coefficient gives
    <math|C*z<rsup|-4>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*z<rsup|-5>>. In four dimensions
    the integral of this bound is at most <math|C/\<mu\>>: the ball
    <math|<around|\||q|\|>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>> costs <math|C/\<mu\>>, and the
    annulus <math|2<rsup|j>*\<mu\>\<less\><around|\||q|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|j+1>*\<mu\>>
    costs <math|C*\<mu\><rsup|-1>*2<rsup|-j>>. At fixed <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>
    the finite sums converge; hence <math|<around|\||\<mu\><rsup|2>*\<cal-L\><around|(|\<cal-W\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\>+o<around|(|1|)>>
    with <math|C> independent of <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>.

    The proof of<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand>, with <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*I> in
    place of its regulator, gives <math|<around|\||R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>-12<slope>log
    \<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\>C> for <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. Here
    <math|R/12> is the coefficient <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> of this
    scalar-regulated kernel: the Hessian is twice the quadratic-action
    kernel, exactly the factor <math|2> in that coefficient. At fixed
    positive <math|\<mu\>>, Riemann sums give
    <math|R<rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>\<to\>R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>>.
    Choose <math|u\<less\>0>, then a length <math|L<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>.
    Once the period controls the endpoints, the mean value theorem on
    <math|<around|[|u-L<rsub|\<ast\>>,u|]>> selects
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<Lambda\>>\<in\><around|[|e<rsup|u-L<rsub|\<ast\>>>,e<rsup|u>|]>>
    with <math|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|\<Lambda\>>|)>+12<slope>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*C+o<around|(|1|)>|)>/L<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-regulated-ward> on this fixed interval, then let
    <math|u\<to\>-\<infty\>> and <math|L<rsub|\<ast\>>\<to\>\<infty\>>. This
    proves the limit without differentiating an asymptotic error.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Transfer to the continuum.> Put
  <math|b<rsub|i>=<refbeta><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>, <math|E<rsub|i>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/5|)>>, <math|R<rsub|i>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/6|)>>, and
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><around|(|4|)><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|K<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>*U<rsub|i><around|(|5|)><rsup|-1><math-up|div>>.
  The image of divergence on centered real symmetric <math|H<rsub|4>> tensors
  is an <math|i>-dependent plane <math|W<rsub|i>\<subset\><around|(|<maxwellspace|5>|)><rsup|4>>:
  its kernel is the conserved line of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>.
  Functionals below act on the ambient real tensorial vector space, with any
  fixed norm.

  This estimate transfers the scalar coefficient to that plane. Its proof
  follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fourth-order Ward transfer><label|lem:I-transfer> There are
    uniformly bounded real functionals <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> and
    <math|C\<less\>\<infty\>>, depending only on the chosen construction,
    such that

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|i>,>|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>>|<cell|\<to\>-<slope>,<eq-number><label|eq:I-divergence>>>|<row|<cell|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|B>>|<cell|\<to\><math-up|div><rsup|<math-up|Max>><space|0.17em>B,>|<cell|E<rsub|i>*<around|[|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|B>|)>-\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>B|)>|]>>|<cell|\<to\>0*<space|1em><around|(|B=\<Xi\>,D|)>.>>>>
    </align>

    Moreover

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-tensor><math-ss|A><rsub|i>B<rsub|i><rsup|B>\<to\>B*<around|(|B=L,\<Xi\>,D|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|i><rsup|p><math-up|tr><around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|i>B<rsub|i><rsup|B>|)>\<to\>0*<around|(|B=L,\<Xi\>;p\<in\><N>|)>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>\<to\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>\U<reference|lem:B6>, <reference|lem:C2>,
    <reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7>, <reference|lem:E2.2>,
    <reference|lem:E2.2a>, and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>,
    <reference|lem:I-regulator>.

    <em|Current estimates.> The transfer identities give (a)\U(b) of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator>; we prove (c)\U(d) below.
    Differentiating the stationary equation eliminates its fine variation.
    Fourth-order matching in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> preserves
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-\<cal-E\><rsub|b>\<equiv\><rsub|4>D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>>.
    The kernel identities above preserve the trace and antisymmetric parts as
    differences. Work after stage <math|j\<geqslant\>7> and choose
    <math|0\<less\>a\<less\>c<rsub|j>>, with <math|c<rsub|j>> the kernel
    decay exponent of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>. Its kernel bound applies
    through polynomial degree three. Divide the order-<math|r> current defect
    by <math|<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|r>>. At orders
    <math|r=3,4>, the linearized stationary fields contribute
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>> each, so transfer costs
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|5-2*r>>. At order two the matched
    low-momentum terms must first be removed. The affine and quadratic parts
    of a stationary test field have weighted sizes
    <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)>/\<ell\><rsup|2>> and
    <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)><rsup|2>/\<ell\><rsup|3>>;
    the remainder is at most <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)><rsup|3>/\<ell\><rsup|4>>,
    by Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>, <reference|lem:B5>. Subtracting the
    affine\Uaffine and affine\Uquadratic products leaves quadratic\Uquadratic
    and affine\Uremainder products, each with factor <math|\<ell\><rsup|-6>>.
    Multiplication by <math|\<ell\><rsup|5>> gives <math|C<rsub|j>/\<ell\>>.
    A bounded matched contribution gives the additional
    <math|C<rsub|j>/<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>. More
    precisely the order-two factor is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j>*<around*|{|<frac|1|<around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>+<frac|<around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>+1|\<ell\><rsub|m><around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Nonlinear chain-rule partitions involve fewer than <math|r> derivatives
    and gain inverse logarithmic powers. Choose each ratio large, then its
    entry coupling large; the resulting triangular map contracts by
    <math|1/2>. After <math|n<rsub|<math-up|step>>> transfers the normalized
    defects are <math|O<around|(|2<rsup|-n<rsub|<math-up|step>>>|)>>.

    <em|The finite coefficient.> At level <math|k> set
    <math|h<rsub|k>=<around|(|log log <refbeta><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>> and use
    <math|M<rsub|k>=<cutoffsize><rsub|k>>. Define
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|5,k>=t<rsub|k><rsup|-2><math-ss|D><rsub|t<rsub|k>><rsup|<math-up|fac>>\<cal-P\><rsub|5>>,
    followed by real <math|H<rsub|4>> averaging. Haar integration by parts in
    the density gives the degree-five coefficient in the form

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-five-pieces>\<Sigma\><rsub|k>=\<cal-P\><rsub|5,k>*<around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y-D*\<cal-L\><rsub|N,k><around|[|Y|]>|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|stat>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tail>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|cl>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|mark>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|N,k>> is the full quadratic loop action of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>. The errors are stationary
    substitution, omitted loop paths, the classical defect and the marked
    remainder. Each has norm at most <math|e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>>>: truncate
    stationary kernels and loop paths at <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>>, using the kernel decay in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> and the path bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. These exponential tails bound
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|stat>>> and
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tail>>> after the finite projection
    losses. The activity caps<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> and the
    threshold<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales> bound
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|cl>>> in the seminorm of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>:

    <\equation*>
      t<rsub|k><rsup|-2>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-1/10>M<rsub|k><rsup|-2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|mark>>>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a> cancels the radial Taylor coefficients
    through degree five on nonwrapping supports. The Taylor remainder has an
    additional small-field factor, at most one after entry. The fixed
    projection and localization powers of <math|M<rsub|k>> therefore give
    <math|C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-\<eta\><rsub|j>>M<rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>>
    with <math|\<eta\><rsub|j>=1/10>. For the forced term,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> leaves
    <math|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>=e<rsup|-M<rsub|k><rsup|1/5>/2>>,
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a> retains the original eligibility
    event. Thus the bound is <math|C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-\<eta\><rsub|j>>M<rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>+C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>e<rsup|-M<rsub|k><rsup|1/5>/2>>
    after the finite projection losses. A wrapping support reaches distance
    <math|n<rsub|p,k>/10> from its anchor, where
    <math|n<rsub|p,k>=L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|k>>, so its covering number is at
    least <math|n<rsub|p,k>/<around|[|10*<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>|]>-1>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>, with support exponent lowered from
    <math|100> to <math|98>, bounds its projected contribution by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>M<rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>*exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|{|-<frac|n<rsub|p,k>|5*<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>>+2|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The wrapping bound vanishes on the ordered period tails at fixed
    <math|k>; the nonwrapping bounds are <math|o<around|(|e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>>|)>>
    after entry.

    The loop term in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-five-pieces> has zero
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> coefficient. If
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|loop>>> is its quadratic link kernel,
    differentiation of <math|D*\<cal-L\><around|[|Y|]>> pairs
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|loop>>> with <math|D*Y>. The cubic momentum test is
    a sum of pairings of constant and quadratic polynomial links and of two
    affine polynomial links. Constant links are pure gauge;
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|loop>>> also kills affine links by both moment
    cancellations of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>. Thus every pairing
    vanishes. The lattice correction in <math|\<frak-h\>> is the
    corresponding correction to this coefficient read-out. Choose the
    transfer depth and period at this fixed <math|k> so that
    <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>> differs from <math|-12<slope>> by at most
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-100>>, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator>. Choose the momentum-Taylor
    approximation to its finite local read-out within
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-30>>. The exact read-out identity gives the
    factor <math|1/12> for the twelve ordered transverse planes. Delayed
    entry bounds the retained loops <math|2*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|k>>>>
    and stage losses by <math|M<rsub|k><rsup|1/6>>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*M<rsub|k><rsup|1/6>,<space|2em><around|\||\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|)>-\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>/12|\|>\<leqslant\>4<around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|\<\|\|\>>*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>>+<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-30>/12<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    At fixed <math|k>, take any joint cluster value of the bounded
    coefficient arrays along the ordered tails<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>, and
    denote its real averaged coefficient by <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>>. All
    closed bounds survive; <math|S> and the maps converging in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> retain their fixed limits. Thus
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|)>\<to\>-<slope>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|i>>.

    <em|The equation direction.> Let <math|s<rsub|i>> be degree-five marked
    transport, followed by <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5>*U<rsub|i><around|(|5|)><rsup|-1>>,
    on the three-dimensional raw vector space. Let <math|s> be the free
    Maxwell quotient map; <math|v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>=<around|(|0,0,1|)>>,
    <math|ker s=<R>v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>> and
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>|)>=0>.
    Let <math|j<rsub|i>> be the raw coefficient of the transferred current's
    divergence after the same threshold normalization. At fixed <math|k>, the
    local current read-out has zero <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>
    coefficient by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-rho>: simplicity makes its degree-three
    trace read-out a unit, whose forward difference vanishes. The stationary
    and marked remainder estimates compare the transported coefficient with
    this read-out within <math|C*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>/4>>. Include the current
    components in the joint cluster choice, denoting their value by
    <math|j<around|(|k|)>>. Closedness preserves
    <math|<around|\||\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|j<around|(|k|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>/4>>
    for every such value. With <math|j<rsub|i>=j<around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>,
    Taylor matching consequently gives

    <\equation*>
      j<rsub|i>\<to\>-v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>,<space|1em><around|\||\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k<rsub|i>>/4>,<space|1em>v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>=\<beta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-j<rsub|i>\<to\>v<rsub|<math-up|eom>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|E<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>\<to\>0>,
    since <math|E<rsub|i>/\<beta\><rsub|i>\<to\>0> and
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>>
    is bounded. For the other rate, localize
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-\<beta\><rsub|i>*j<rsub|i>> before
    transport, with a fixed degree-four spectator. Its exterior moment is
    zero by Haar integration by parts, since the varied link is outside the
    spectator stencil. Here the quantitative estimate uses the norms, not
    merely the local format in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>. To see their
    spatial gain, let <math|v<rsub|X>> be regular majorants rooted at the
    insertion anchor and <math|c<rsub|X>=<cover><rsub|B<rsub|k>><around|(|X|)>>.
    The support weight in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> is
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>=100> by row<nbsp>4 of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. Thus, for every <math|q\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|c<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>q>e<rsup|98*c<rsub|X>>*v<rsub|X>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-2*q>*<big|sum><rsub|X>e<rsup|100*c<rsub|X>>*v<rsub|X>.
    </equation*>

    The same inequality holds for the normalized forced majorants. Since
    every nonzero marked support contains its anchor, a connected term
    reaching a spectator at distance <math|R> in level-<math|k> units has
    <math|c<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>-1>. Shorter terms
    retain the zero exterior moment. For the matched residual
    use<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match-level>, starting at
    <math|k<around|(|n|)>> and ending at <math|n\<geqslant\>r<around|(|5|)>>,
    and replicate by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root>. Across logarithmic
    length <math|H<rsub|n>=<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>> the
    matching gain is <math|e<rsup|-H<rsub|n>/8>>. The fixed degree-five
    projections, threshold factors and transport columns cost
    <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>>>: the logarithms of the threshold
    costs are <math|O<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|n>|)>>, and the column cost
    is bounded by <math|H<rsub|n>*sup<rsub|m\<geqslant\>n>
    J<rsub|m><rsup|-2>>. Fix the tiling level
    <math|m=m<around|(|n|)>\<less\>n> by the first backward crossing of
    <math|B<rsub|n><rsup|2>>; eventually <math|m<around|(|n|)>\<geqslant\>r<around|(|5|)>>.
    If <math|\<ell\>=max<rsub|m\<leqslant\>v\<less\>n> \<ell\><rsub|v>>, then

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>\<ell\>*B<rsub|n><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|n>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|n>+6\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsup|4>*B<rsub|n><rsup|8>*q<rsub|n>+6=o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>log <refbeta><rsub|n>> and
    <math|B<rsub|n>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>>; <math|q<rsub|n>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>> uniformly
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-majorant> and row<nbsp>5 of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. Their product is
    <math|O<around|(|<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|22>|)>=o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>>.
    With the allowances <math|\<zeta\><rsub|e,k>=<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsub|e>+4|)>>>
    fixed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>
    before<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance>, Then <math|log
    \<zeta\><rsub|e,n><rsup|-1>=O<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)>=o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>>, including this additional
    factor in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root>. The support tail just
    displayed is smaller, since <math|R> grows exponentially in
    <math|H<rsub|n>>. At radius level <math|n>, multiplication of the
    physical two-point error by <math|a<rsub|n><rsup|5>> cancels the factor
    <math|a<rsub|n><rsup|-5>> in the replicated source radius. The resulting
    bound is

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|{|-H<rsub|n>/8+o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-H<rsub|n>/100>
    </equation*>

    after entry. To pass to the full radius normalization multiply by
    <math|<around|(|r<rsub|i>/a<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|5>>. We may require
    <math|u<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>log log <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>> in the
    diagonal extraction. The extra scaling factor is then at most <math|exp
    <around|(|5<around|(|log log <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>|)>>,
    while <math|b<rsub|i>/<refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<to\>1>. Thus each
    rescaled evaluation after multiplication by <math|b<rsub|i><rsup|p>> is
    bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C*b<rsub|i><rsup|p>*exp <around|{|5<around|(|log log
      <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>-H<rsub|n<rsub|i>>/100|}>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Finite separating free two-point tests give
    <math|b<rsub|i><rsup|p>*s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>\<to\>0>.

    Construct right inverses <math|t<rsub|i>> of <math|s<rsub|i>> from the
    frames and corrected degree-five projections: lift a free vector by the
    common frame, undo its threshold normalization and marked transport, and
    use the frame inverse to check <math|s<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i>=I>. Their
    coefficient lifts converge on a finite basis by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>, so <math|t<rsub|i>\<to\>t> with
    <math|s*t=I>. These identities give rank two; hence <math|ker s<rsub|i>>
    is a line. Its generator is <math|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>-t<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>>.
    The bases formed by <math|t<rsub|i>> and this kernel generator converge
    to the free basis, giving <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>s>. Define
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>*t<rsub|i>>.
    Decomposing along this line and <math|<math-up|im><space|0.17em>t<rsub|i>>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around*|(|<around|\||\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>|\|>+<around|\<\|\|\>|s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>=o<around|(|E<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The actual divergence satisfies <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>-s<rsub|i>*\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<to\>0>
    by the same equation estimate; for <math|B=\<Xi\>,D>,
    <math|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|B>-s<rsub|i><math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>B=o<around|(|b<rsub|i><rsup|-p>|)>>.
    Since <math|R<rsub|i>/E<rsub|i>\<to\>0>, these estimates
    prove<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-divergence>.

    <em|Tensor traces.> Taylor matching gives
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|i>B<rsub|i><rsup|B>\<to\>B>. The trace of
    <math|\<Xi\>> is zero; the transferred trace of <math|L> is a forward
    difference. Its degree-three projection is a unit, since simplicity
    leaves no nonconstant invariant below degree four. For the degree-three
    stencil and its degree-four difference, fix <math|k<rsub|0>> so late that
    its backward crossing <math|m<rsub|0>=m<around|(|k<rsub|0>|)>> satisfies
    <math|r<around|(|4|)>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|0>\<less\>k<rsub|0>> and
    <math|B<rsub|k<rsub|0>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m<rsub|0>>/a<rsub|k<rsub|0>>>.
    Match at <math|r<around|(|4|)>>, stop at <math|k<rsub|0>> and replicate
    at the fixed pair <math|<around|(|m<rsub|0>,k<rsub|0>|)>>.
    Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match-level>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root> give

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|k<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>=O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|1/8>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The constant includes the inverse allowance, thresholds and <math|exp
    <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m<rsub|0>>/a<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<rsub|0>>+6|}>>.
    Coefficient conversions grow as <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>>,
    giving endpoint error <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>>|)>>
    for any fixed <math|0\<less\>\<eta\><rsub|0>\<less\>1/8> after entry.
    Truncating the support sums above at <math|c<rsub|X>=\<eta\><rsub|0>*s<rsub|k>>
    leaves a tail at most <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|2*\<eta\><rsub|0>>>, and
    decreasing <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>> absorbs all finite losses. This
    estimate is uniform for endpoints in a fixed small neighbourhood of the
    separating tests. Average the degree-four difference over
    <math|m=<around|\<lfloor\>|a<rsub|k><rsup|-1+\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|\<rfloor\>>>
    sites. Telescoping and the position-independent degree-three unit give

    <\equation*>
      <frac|2*C*a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>>|m*a<rsub|k>>=O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|)>,<space|2em>m*a<rsub|k>=O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    The averaged testing functionals converge to the original separating
    functionals by smoothness, so a finite separating matrix still has a
    bounded left inverse. They therefore bound the trace coefficient by
    <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|)>>. Subexponential
    inverse transport preserves <math|o<around|(|b<rsub|i><rsup|-p>|)>> for
    every <math|p>, proving the trace assertion in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-tensor>;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii> gives its coupling assertion.
  </proof>

  Conservation converts these divergence estimates into the scalar
  coefficient. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:I-transfer> give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I-coefficient>\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><around|(|4|)><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\>\<longrightarrow\>-<slope><curvaturesquare><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  <\render-proof|Derivation of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-coefficient>>
    Use the field calculus of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>, the free tensor
    calculation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, the conserved line of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>, and the normalized shell laws of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2>. The free divergences of <math|\<Xi\>,D>
    are independent: a kernel combination would be a multiple of <math|L>,
    impossible in these two tensor coordinates. Thus
    <math|f<rsub|i>:<R><rsup|2>\<to\>W<rsub|i>>,
    <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|c|)>=c<rsub|0>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>+c<rsub|1>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>>,
    are eventually invertible with bounded inverses
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-divergence>. Set <math|c<rsub|i>=-f<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>>.
    Then <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|i>> and

    <\equation*>
      X<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>+c<rsub|i,0>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>+c<rsub|i,1>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>
    </equation*>

    is conserved. Applying <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> to its zero divergence
    gives the exact identity

    <\equation*>
      4*c<rsub|i,1>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>+c<rsub|i,0>*\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>|)>+c<rsub|i,1>*<around*|[|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>|)>+4|]><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|R<rsub|i>/E<rsub|i>\<to\>0>, the last two terms vanish and
    <math|c<rsub|i,1>\<to\>-<slope>/4=d<rsub|2>>, where
    <math|d<rsub|2>=-b<rsub|0>/2> is the trace coefficient of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>.

    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-tensor> and
    <math|R<rsub|i>/\<beta\><rsub|i>\<to\>0> give
    <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i>\<to\>L>. Its
    weighted trace <math|\<beta\><rsub|i><math-up|tr><around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><around|(|4|)><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i>|)>>
    tends to <math|4*d<rsub|2><curvaturesquare>>, since the other traces
    vanish faster than every inverse power. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>
    now gives <math|X<rsub|i>=\<nu\><rsub|i>*T> with
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>\<in\><R>>: the tensors <math|X<rsub|i>> are centered
    in the mixture by construction, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> gives
    the centered, nonzero tensor <math|T>. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, choose <math|A\<in\><maxwellspace|4>>, a
    separating spectator functional <math|o>, and an axis <math|b> with
    <math|y=o<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*A|)>\<ne\>0>.
    For these fixed tests the flux of <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4>*U<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i>>
    tends to the flux of <math|L>, namely <math|y>, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>. The flux of <math|T>, multiplied by
    <math|r<rsub|i>>, tends to <math|y> by the normalized Ward identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> and the jet limit of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>. The first flux is
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>> times the second. Dividing by the latter proves
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>\<to\>1>. This gives the weighted trace limit for
    <math|T>.

    Finally <math|\<Theta\>=<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>><unitfield|4>>
    and <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|-1><unitfield|4>=<unitfield|4>>. The extra term
    has size <math|<around|\||e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>|\|>*\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>\<to\>0>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-adjustable>.
  </render-proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>>
    The phase-table reflection identity E2 of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E1-E3> and the reality of <math|T> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> make <math|e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>> real.
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>, <reference|lem:G3.2>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4> show that <math|\<Theta\>> is real and
    <math|O<around|(|4|)>> invariant and that its continued label is
    Hermitian and Lorentz scalar. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.1> gives
    vacuum centering. For the asymptotic, take any prescribed positive null
    sequence and the subsequence in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-adjustable>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-coefficient>, the phase comparison, and the free
    normalization in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, every generic pair
    <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>> with
    <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<less\>100> and
    <math|<math-up|sep><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>1/100>, with
    <math|x<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>\<in\>r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|n<rsub|i>><Z><rsup|4>>
    nearest to <math|x>, satisfies

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|i><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)><tracetwopoint><around|(|r<rsub|i>*<around|\||x<rsub|0><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>-x<rsub|1><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>|\|>|)>\<longrightarrow\><frac|<traceamplitude>|<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|8>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Translation and <math|O<around|(|4|)>> invariance
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>) give
    <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;r*x<rsub|0>,r*x<rsub|1>|)>=<tracetwopoint><around|(|r*<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>|)>>.
    Here <math|<slope><rsup|-2><around|(|4*d<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>=1>.

    Take <math|x<rsub|0>=0> and <math|x<rsub|1>=<around|(|t/2,t/2,t/2,t/2|)>>,
    with <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2>. This pair is generic and has distance
    <math|t>. Monotonicity from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> bounds the
    lower limit at radius <math|r<rsub|i>> by
    <math|<traceamplitude>/t<rsup|8>> for <math|t\<gtr\>1>, and the upper
    limit by that expression for <math|t\<less\>1>. Let <math|t\<to\>1>. The
    limit along this subsequence is <math|<traceamplitude>>. The subsequence
    criterion gives the full-radius limit. Here
    <math|<traceamplitude>=3<dimG>/\<pi\><rsup|4>\<gtr\>0> since
    <math|<dimG>\<geqslant\>1>; identifying it uses
    <math|d<rsub|2>=-<slope>/4\<ne\>0>. Thus <math|\<Theta\>\<ne\>0>.
  </render-proof>

  The following high-energy law follows from TR and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>, as proved below.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|High-energy trace limit><label|lem:I1.3> For every
    <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c>*<around|(|<R><rsup|4>;<C>|)>> and every
    <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>,

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>\<to\>\<infty\>>
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8><around|(|log
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)><rsup|2>*<big|int>\<varphi\>*<around|(|\<b-up-p\>/R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<frac|<traceamplitude>|1536*\<pi\>>*<big|int><rsub|<forwardcone>><minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)><rsup|2>*\<varphi\><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|4>*\<b-up-p\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>
    and the full-radius conclusion of TR. Put
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>=R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
    R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em><tracespectrum><around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>|)>>.
    For every future timelike Laplace covector <math|u>, with the Euclidean
    pairing <math|u\<cdot\>\<b-up-p\>> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:W0>,
    which is the Minkowski pairing of <math|\<b-up-p\>> with the future
    timelike <math|<around|(|-u<rsup|1>,-u<rsup|2>,-u<rsup|3>,u<rsup|4>|)>>,
    Lorentz invariance reduces its transform to the time-axis transform at
    <math|s=<sqrt|<minksquare><around|(|u|)>>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>e<rsup|-u\<cdot\>\<b-up-p\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>=R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|s/R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)>\<longrightarrow\><traceamplitude>s<rsup|-8><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    To identify the constant, write <math|t=\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>> and integrate
    the spatial radius <math|q>:

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|<forwardcone>>e<rsup|-s*t><minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|4>*\<b-up-p\>=4*\<pi\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsup|-s*t>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>q<rsup|2>*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-q<rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*q*<space|0.17em>d*t=1536*\<pi\>*s<rsup|-8><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The <math|s=1> transform bounds compact masses. Diagonal extraction and
    Riesz representation give vague subsequential
    limits<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorem<nbsp>2.14>, applied to
    positive functionals on <math|C<rsub|c><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>>. The
    <math|u/2> transform controls the <math|u>-damped tail, giving the
    displayed transform for every limit. Uniqueness follows by holomorphic
    uniqueness in each coordinate for timelike real part, then Fourier
    inversion on product Schwartz tests against the finite damped
    measures<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorems<nbsp>10.18 and<nbsp>9.11>;
    finiteness justifies Fubini. Thus every extracted limit is the cone
    density in the statement.

    For Schwartz tests positivity gives

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>*<around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>R|}>\<leqslant\>e*<space|0.17em>R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*R|)><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|8>*<space|2em><around|(|R\<geqslant\>1,R<rsub|<math-up|E>>\<geqslant\>R<rsub|<math-up|E>,0>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Use the trace law at <math|1/<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*R|)>> and
    <math|log R<rsub|<math-up|E>>\<leqslant\>log
    <around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*R|)>>. A Schwartz bound of order greater
    than eight sums the dyadic energy tails uniformly, since
    <math|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\><sqrt|2>*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>
    on the cone. The <math|C<rsub|c>> limit then gives the Schwartz limit.
  </proof>

  For <math|\<psi\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>*<around|(|x,y|)>=R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|8>*\<psi\>*<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*x,R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*y|)>>,
  <math|<wavetransform>\<psi\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<wavetransform>\<psi\>*<around|(|\<b-up-p\>/R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)>>.
  For <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>,
  <math|<wavetransform>\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>. Substitution in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.3> gives the position-space consequence

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
    R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*<space|0.17em>W<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;\<psi\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>|)>\<longrightarrow\><frac|<traceamplitude>|1536*\<pi\>>*<big|int><rsub|<forwardcone>><minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)><rsup|2><wavetransform>\<psi\><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|4>*\<b-up-p\><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  <subsection|Precise non-free comparisons><label|sec:partII-I.2>

  The radial integrals of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17> imply the
  following exclusion; its proof follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite Wick obstruction><label|lem:I2.1> For every finite Wick
    profile <math|W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>>> and every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    there is <math|r\<in\><around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>\<ne\>W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>><around|(|r|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17> and the positive limit in TR. For
    one factor, polar coordinates and <math|\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>>=<sqrt|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>><rsup|2>+q<rsup|2>>>
    give

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>,k<rsub|E>,h<rsub|<math-up|mom>>><around|(|r|)>=<frac|1|2><around*|(|<big|int><rsub|S<rsup|2>>h<rsub|<math-up|mom>>|)>*<big|int><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|\<infty\>>\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|k<rsub|E>>*<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|2>-m<rsub|<math-up|sh>><rsup|2>|)><rsup|<around|(|j<rsub|h>+1|)>/2>*e<rsup|-r*\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>=0> this is proportional to
    <math|r<rsup|-j<rsub|h>-k<rsub|E>-2>>. Otherwise split at
    <math|2*m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>>; the bounded interval is analytic. On the
    tail expand <math|<around|(|1-m<rsub|<math-up|sh>><rsup|2>/\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|2>|)><rsup|<around|(|j<rsub|h>+1|)>/2>>.
    Taking enough terms makes the remainder integrable with any prescribed
    number of energy moments, hence gives a Taylor remainder of the required
    order. The retained terms are <math|I<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>=<big|int><rsub|2*m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|\<infty\>>v<rsup|a>*e<rsup|-r*v>*<space|0.17em>d*v>,
    <math|a\<in\><Z>>. Integrate explicitly for <math|a\<geqslant\>0>;
    <math|I<rsub|-1><rprime|'>=-e<rsup|-2*m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>*r>/r> gives
    the logarithm, and <math|I<rsub|a><rprime|'>=-I<rsub|a+1>> gives all
    lower powers. Only nonnegative logarithmic powers occur. For products,
    choose each remainder order above the other factors' pole orders.

    Thus <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>>=<big|sum><rsub|a,k>c<rsub|a,k>*r<rsup|a>*log<rsup|k>
    r+O<around|(|1|)>> with finitely many terms. After multiplying by
    <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>>, the terms with
    <math|a\<gtr\>-8> vanish. Among the others, the least <math|a>, then
    largest <math|k>, gives a nonzero divergent leading term. A finite limit
    is therefore zero. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> makes
    <math|<tracetwopoint>> real, so equality with
    <math|W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>>> implies equality with its real part. The
    positive trace limit contradicts equality on
    <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>, proving also
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:NF>.
  </proof>

  To describe polynomial mass-shell Wick contractions, put
  <math|q<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=<around|(|\<b-up-k\>,<sqrt|m<rsup|2>+<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|><rsup|2>>|)>>,
  <math|m\<geqslant\>0>. Fix a finite set of elementary field indices
  <math|<math-ss|x>,<math-ss|y>>; words <math|u,u<rprime|'>> list its
  elements. For words of length <math|k>, <math|\<sigma\>> ranges over
  permutations of <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>. Comparison data specify
  finite mass\Upolynomial lists <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|<math-up|pair>>*<around|(|<math-ss|x>,<math-ss|y>|)>>,
  with <math|P\<in\><C><around|[|\<b-up-p\><rsup|1>,\<ldots\>,\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>|]>>,
  and complex contraction coefficients <math|c*<around|(|u,u<rprime|'>,\<sigma\>|)>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Polynomial mass-shell pairings><label|def:D28> Set

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|m,P><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=<frac|P<around|(|q<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>|)>|2*\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>,<space|2em>L<rsub|m,P><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|int>v<rsub|m,P><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|q<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For words <math|u=<around|(|<math-ss|x><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>>,
    <math|u<rprime|'>=<around|(|<math-ss|y><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>>,
    define the convolutions of the functionals <math|L<rsub|m,P>> by

    <\equation*>
      \<Pi\>*<around|(|u,u<rprime|'>;\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<sigma\>>c*<around|(|u,u<rprime|'>,\<sigma\>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|m<rsub|i>,P<rsub|i>|)>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i>\<cal-M\><rsub|<math-up|pair>>*<around|(|<math-ss|x><rsub|i>,<math-ss|y><rsub|\<sigma\><around|(|i|)>>|)>><big|int><big|prod><rsub|i>v<rsub|m<rsub|i>,P<rsub|i>><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>q<rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|i>|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i>d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\><rsub|i><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For unequal lengths put <math|\<Pi\>=0>. A zero denominator at zero
    momentum gives value zero.
  </definition>

  For finite polynomial fields <math|Q=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<alpha\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>>,
  including empty words, the bilinear pairing is
  <math|W<rsup|<math-up|pol>>*<around|(|Q,Q<rprime|'>;\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>\<alpha\><rsub|i>*\<alpha\><rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<Pi\>*<around|(|u<rsub|i>,u<rprime|'><rsub|j>;\<varphi\>|)>>.
  Empty contractions evaluate <math|\<varphi\>> at zero. The shell measure
  has no <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-3>> factor.

  The next lemma separates each comparison by one product test; its proof
  follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Polynomial countertest><label|lem:I2.2> For every comparison data
    and every <math|Q,Q<rprime|'>>, there are <math|f,h\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with
    compactly supported <math|<wavetransform><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<ne\>W<rsup|<math-up|pol>>*<around|(|Q,Q<rprime|'>;<wavetransform><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D17>, <reference|def:D28> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>, <reference|lem:I2.1>. Equality for all
    product tests implies equality for every
    <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>>: choose
    <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|+>=\<varphi\>> and real <math|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>=1>
    on its support. Approximate <math|e<rsup|-r*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>> by
    compact cutoffs. Dominated convergence applies:
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> controls <math|<tracespectrum>>;
    exponential damping controls comparison polynomials at infinity, and
    <math|d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\>/<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>> is integrable at
    zero. Expanding the numerators into monomials factors the integrals into
    the kernels of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17>, with the empty words
    giving constants. Hence <math|<tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>=W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>><around|(|r|)>>
    for all <math|r\<gtr\>0>, contrary to Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.1>.
  </proof>

  For the photon comparison use Hilbert tensor products.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Maxwell Fock space><label|def:D29> For <math|n\<in\><N>> colours
    put

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|d*\<sigma\><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=<frac|d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\>|2<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|a*b><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>-<frac|\<b-up-k\><rsub|a>*\<b-up-k\><rsub|b>|<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|><rsup|2>>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-K\><rsub|n>=L<rsup|2>*<around|(|d*\<sigma\>;<C><rsup|n>\<otimes\><C><rsup|3>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-F\><rsub|n>=<big|oplus><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>\<cal-K\><rsub|n><rsup|\<otimes\>k><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The density and projector are zero at zero momentum. Physical vectors are
    symmetric and transverse in each slot. Translations multiply sector
    <math|k> by <math|exp <around|{|i*z\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>,<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>|)>|}>>.
    The sector-zero unit is <math|\<Omega\><rsub|0>>.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Coherent comparison vectors><label|def:D30> For Schwartz tests
    <math|h=<around|(|h<rsub|\<alpha\>,a>|)>>, put

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|h>*<around|(|\<alpha\>,a;\<b-up-k\>|)>=<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>*<big|sum><rsub|b>\<Pi\><rsub|a*b><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>*<wide|h|^><rsub|\<alpha\>,b,+>*<around|(|\<b-up-k\>,<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>=e<rsup|-<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|h>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>*<big|oplus><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0><frac|v<rsub|h><rsup|\<otimes\>k>|<sqrt|k!>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A comparison <math|U:\<cal-H\>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|n>> is a complex
    linear isometry with physical image, <math|U*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|0>>,
    intertwining translations. Set <math|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>=U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>>.
  </definition>

  Real tests have real components. Causal separation means
  <math|<minksquare><around|(|x-y|)>\<less\>0> for every pair of points in
  their closed supports, where <math|<minksquare><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|x<rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>-<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|3><around|(|x<rsup|a>|)><rsup|2>>.

  Relative locality would impose the equality excluded below. Formally, let
  <math|W<around|(|h|)>> be Weyl operators of the free Maxwell field with
  <math|W<around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>=\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>> and
  <math|W<around|(|h|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=W*<around|(|-h|)>>. If
  <math|U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*U<rsup|\<ast\>>> commuted
  with <math|W<around|(|h|)>> for <math|h> causally separated from <math|f>,
  then, since <math|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>=\<Omega\>> and
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>> is Hermitian,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|\<cal-E\>*<around|(|-h|)>,\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|0>,W<around|(|h|)>*U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*U<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|0>,U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*U<rsup|\<ast\>>*W<around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>,\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Weyl operators enter only this motivation. The proof uses spectral growth
  without restricting particle amplitudes.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fock countertest><label|lem:I2.3> For every <math|n\<geqslant\>0>
    and every comparison <math|U>, for every ball about zero there are
    Schwartz <math|f> compactly supported in that ball and real compactly
    supported Schwartz <math|h<rsub|\<alpha\>,a>>, each causally separated
    from <math|f>, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<cal-E\>*<around|(|-h|)>,\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>|\<rangle\>>\<ne\><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>,\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D29>\U<reference|def:D30>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.1>\U<reference|lem:I1.3> and the
    translation covariance of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>. Fix a ball and
    suppose equality always holds.

    <em|Sector amplitudes.> Set <math|p<rsub|j>=<around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>,<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>|)>>
    and <math|p<rsub|<math-up|tot>>=<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>>. The
    convolution argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> gives sector
    amplitudes <math|Y<rsub|k,s>>, with <math|s> listing colour and spatial
    indices:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-sector>\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)><rsub|k,s>=<wide|f|^><rsub|+><around|(|p<rsub|<math-up|tot>>|)>*Y<rsub|k,s>,<space|2em><tracespectrum>=<around|(|p<rsub|<math-up|tot>>|)><rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|k,s><around|\||Y<rsub|k,s>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Box masses are finite. Cauchy\USchwarz and polynomial spectral growth
    give integrability against sufficiently high inverse polynomials in each
    momentum.

    The common sector unitary <math|i<rsup|k>> preserves the equality,
    physical vectors and translations. Write
    <math|<math-ss|y>=i<rsup|k>*Y<rsub|k,s>>.

    <em|Locality and doubling.> Expand the coherent equality in a real
    parameter multiplying <math|h>, justified by Cauchy\USchwarz and the
    exponential series. Polarization and complexification give independent
    complex slot tests. Transversality removes the projector in contractions
    with physical waves. Shrink nonnegative unit-integral <math|f> to zero.
    Then <math|<around|\||<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>\<to\>1>; dominated convergence gives, for
    <math|g<rsub|j>> supported spacelike to zero,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-remote><big|int><math-ss|y><around|(|\<b-k\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j><around*|[|<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>*<wide|g|^><rsub|j,-><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|]>*<space|0.17em>d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k>=<big|int><wide|<math-ss|y><around|(|\<b-k\>|)>|\<bar\>><big|prod><rsub|j><around*|[|<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>*<wide|g|^><rsub|j,+><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|]>*<space|0.17em>d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|<wide|g|^><rsub|-><around|(|p|)>=<wide|g|^><rsub|+>*<around|(|-p|)>>;
    the sector factors <math|i<rsup|k>> give exactly these signs.

    Fix other slots and use <math|g*<around|(|x,t|)>=a<around|(|x|)>*\<eta\><rsub|\<epsilon\>><rsup|<around|(|b|)>><around|(|t|)>>,
    with <math|\<eta\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>> approximating unit mass and
    <math|b=0,1>. If <math|0\<nin\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>a>, these are
    spacelike for small <math|\<epsilon\>>. Thus both Cauchy data
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-remote> are supported at zero. Weighted integrability
    bounds their order by <math|J>, independently of the other tests.

    Each datum depends only on the <math|J>-jet: multiplication by a cutoff
    in <math|B*<around|(|0,\<epsilon\>|)>> leaves the distribution unchanged.
    For a zero-<math|J>-jet test, Taylor's formula bounds derivatives through
    order <math|J> by <math|O<around|(|\<epsilon\>|)>>; let
    <math|\<epsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>.

    The massless wave equation separates the frequency branches from these
    two Cauchy data away from zero momentum. After smearing other slots, each
    branch is integrable, bounded and holomorphic in its timelike tube, with
    continuous real boundary. Vanishing persists under small translations of
    the other spacelike tests. Schwarz reflection and holomorphic
    uniqueness<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorems<nbsp>11.14
    and<nbsp>10.18> make the zero set invariant under adding <math|<R>u> for
    every future timelike <math|u>. These vectors span <math|<R><rsup|4>>,
    giving vanishing at every real translation. Successive Fourier
    inversion<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorem<nbsp>9.11> of the smeared
    integrable amplitudes gives zero almost everywhere. Countably many
    shrinking bumps remove the other tests: their transforms are eventually
    nonzero at each momentum.

    Let <math|D<rsub|j><math-ss|y><around|(|\<b-k\>|)>=<math-ss|y><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,2*\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>,\<ldots\>,\<b-up-k\><rsub|k>|)>>.
    In the time-order-<math|b> Cauchy datum (<math|b=0,1>), this substitution
    gives <math|2<rsup|-3-b>> times the datum at
    <math|a<around|(|x|)>\<mapsto\>8*a*<around|(|2*x|)>>, from
    <math|d*\<sigma\>*<around|(|2*\<b-up-k\>|)>=4*d*\<sigma\><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>
    and <math|<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|><rsup|1+b>>. Spatial jets of order
    <math|l> scale by <math|2<rsup|3+l>>, giving eigenvalues
    <math|2<rsup|l-b>>. The finite jets and branch argument therefore give,
    for some <math|J<rprime|'>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-doubling><big|prod><rsub|a=0><rsup|J<rprime|'>-1><around|(|D<rsub|j>-2<rsup|a-1>|)><math-ss|y>=0<space|2em><text|in
      every slot >j<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    <em|Growth.> For <math|R=2<rsup|l>> let
    <math|M<rsub|<math-ss|y>><around|(|R|)>=<big|int><rsub|<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>R><around|\||<math-ss|y>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k>>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-sector> and the trace law,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-small-mass>M<rsub|<math-ss|y>><around|(|2<rsup|l>|)>\<leqslant\><tracespectrum><around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\><around|(|k+1|)>*2<rsup|l>|}>,<space|2em>2<rsup|-8*l>*M<rsub|<math-ss|y>><around|(|2<rsup|l>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the limit, bound the energy mass by
    <math|e<space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|<around|(|<around|(|k+1|)>*2<rsup|l>|)><rsup|-1>|)>>.

    Lagrange interpolation in the commuting <math|D<rsub|j>> splits
    <math|<math-ss|y>> into joint eigencomponents
    <math|D<rsub|j><math-ss|y><rsub|\<b-a\>>=2<rsup|a<rsub|j>-1><math-ss|y><rsub|\<b-a\>>>.
    Each is a finite combination of doubled copies, so
    retains<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-small-mass>. Moreover
    <math|M<rsub|<math-ss|y><rsub|\<b-a\>>><around|(|2<rsup|l>|)>=2<rsup|2*l*<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>>*M<rsub|<math-ss|y><rsub|\<b-a\>>><around|(|1|)>>,
    since <math|d*\<sigma\>*<around|(|2*\<b-up-k\>|)>=4*d*\<sigma\><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>.
    If <math|a<rsub|j>=0>, contraction preserves box mass, contradicting
    local square integrability and absence of an atom at zero unless the
    component vanishes. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-small-mass> excludes
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>4>. Thus
    <math|a<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>, <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>a<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>3>,
    and <math|k\<leqslant\>3>, with box mass <math|\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|6>>
    for dyadic <math|R\<geqslant\>1>. The vacuum sector vanishes by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.1> and <math|U*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|0>>.

    Summing<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-sector> gives
    <math|<tracespectrum><around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>R|}>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|6>>
    at dyadic <math|R\<geqslant\>1>. Multiplication by
    <math|R<rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2> R> gives zero in the limit, contradicting
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.3> on a nonnegative compact test in
    <math|<around|{|0\<less\>\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<less\>1,<minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>\<gtr\>0|}>>.
  </proof>

  For a probability space <math|<around|(|Z,\<lambda\>|)>>, let each
  <math|P<rsub|z>:\<cal-F\><rsub|n>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|n>> preserve physical
  inner products and fix <math|U*\<cal-H\>> pointwise, without assuming
  linearity. Nonintegrable scalar functions have integral zero
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14f>). The maps <math|P<rsub|z>> model
  transformations of the free comparison vectors that fix every embedded
  interacting state in <math|U*\<cal-H\>>. Because physical inner products
  are preserved, neither matrix element in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.3>
  changes, so the averaged inequality states that averaging these
  transformations cannot restore the relative-locality identity.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Localized averaged countertest><label|lem:I2.4> For every such
    <math|n,U,<around|(|Z,\<lambda\>|)>,<around|(|P<rsub|z>|)>> and
    <math|r\<gtr\>0>, there are open <math|A,B>, with
    <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>>\<subset\>B*<around|(|0,r|)>> and
    <math|<minksquare><around|(|x-y|)>\<less\>0> on
    <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>>\<times\><wide|B|\<bar\>>>, and tests <math|f,h> as
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.3>, supported respectively in <math|A,B>,
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|Z><around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|z>*\<cal-E\>*<around|(|-h|)>,\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><around|(|z|)>\<ne\><big|int><rsub|Z><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>,P<rsub|z>*\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><around|(|z|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For physical <math|v> and <math|w\<in\>U*\<cal-H\>>
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D30>),
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|z>*v,w|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|z>*v,P<rsub|z>*w|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|v,w|\<rangle\>>>.
    Thus averaging preserves both integrands.

    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.3> in <math|B*<around|(|0,r/2|)>>.
    Compactness and strict spacelike separation give support neighbourhoods
    <math|A,B> with spacelike separated closures and
    <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>>\<subset\>B*<around|(|0,r|)>>.
  </proof>

  <section|Products and contact subtractions><label|sec:partII-J>

  A cluster of separated insertions can be replaced, to any prescribed
  degree, by a finite sum of fields at one point. The coefficients must be
  chosen before the exterior insertions and the phase. We construct them from
  finitely many correlations of the mixed table, impose the rotation and
  reality laws by averaging, and remove the lower degrees successively.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP> follows from this construction.
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.3>\U<reference|lem:J1.6> give its integrated
  and nested forms; they are not used in the proofs of
  Theorems<nbsp><reference|thm:A> and<nbsp><reference|thm:B>.

  The lattice estimate uses the signed local and graded quotient bounds of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:J-graded-quotient>. Blocking loses marker weights
  when insertion points approach one another. Grouping nearby markers bounds
  this loss by a power of the ratio of insertion and fusion scales. Taylor
  subtraction and coefficient transport produce one field and a small
  remainder. Reflection positivity converts its chessboard root into a norm
  bound in the commutative compression algebra, valid in every phase.
  Derivative induction removes the grid restriction; tree induction allows
  several clusters to shrink independently.

  For contacts, a pointwise bound away from collisions is insufficient. We
  subtract normal Taylor jets of the test function, with a different order
  for each retained field degree. Partial collisions require the same
  operation inside each smaller cluster. The resulting finite recursion gives
  the distribution in Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT>; its absolute
  convergence follows by summing normal shells. The contact projections have
  their own probes and clearances, while using the same original fields and
  correlation table as the product expansion.

  Throughout, the lattice insertions and quotient fields are those of
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08> and<nbsp><reference|def:D10>;
  inclusions and derivatives are those of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. We
  use the rotations of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2>, the centered bounds
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>, and the compression
  algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> and phase tables of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>\U<reference|lem:H1.3>. Write
  <math|<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>=<big|sum><rsub|i>deg A<rsub|i>> and
  <math|\<zeta\>\<in\><phases>\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>, as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>.

  <paragraph|Inputs from the marked expansion and the quotient.>

  We first establish two estimates needed when the field to be extracted
  varies with the cutoff. Throughout this subsection, a local marked
  expression has the form of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D26> and the
  regularity, zero radial jets and forced supports of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||x<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>,<space|2em>x<rsup|\<circ\>>=x-x<rsub|0>*\<b-1\>,<space|2em>\<frak-r\><rsub|i>=\<cal-E\><rsub|i>+<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsub|i><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The last quantity is the weighted remainder mass of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>. Fix an output degree<nbsp><math|t>. The
  diagonal rescaling <math|<around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|i>x|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|t-deg
  \<alpha\>>*x<rsub|\<alpha\>>> accounts for the engineering degrees
  inside<nbsp><math|V<rsub|t>>. Put <math|C<rsub|i>=C<rsub|J<rsub|i>>> in
  this subsection. The normalized columns of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>
  are denoted by<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|i><around|(|b|)>>, their
  coefficient maps by<nbsp><math|<math-ss|X><rsub|i>>, and their remainder
  bounds are <math|C<rsub|i>*\<mu\><around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|X><rsub|i>b|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>
  by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>.

  Let <math|K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> be the nondecreasing stage bound fixed
  before item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns> in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. The degree rescaling above is the map
  <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|L>> in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>. For an input
  coefficient<nbsp><math|v> and remainder mass<nbsp><math|q>, its averaged
  marked estimate gives an output coefficient<nbsp><math|u> and remainder
  mass<nbsp><math|r> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-marked-recall>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\>*<around|(|u-M<rsub|i><math-ss|d><rsub|i>v|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|[|\<mu\><around|(|v<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>+q|]>,>>|<row|<cell|r>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*\<mu\><around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|i>v|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>+\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*q,<space|2em>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-3/8>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  Here <math|M<rsub|i>> is the leading polynomial map, fixes the unit,
  preserves the nonconstant subspace, and satisfies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>>.
  These estimates follow from<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> and the
  symmetry averaging in that proof.

  Write <math|C<rsub|l>=C<rsub|J<rsub|l>>>. In the threshold order of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, the construction chooses

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-stage-room>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|64*K<rsub|J<rsub|l>+1><rsup|2>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>,>|<cell|10<rsup|5>*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>+1><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l+1>|)><rsup|2>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/8>,>|<cell|1+2*C<rsub|l>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/40>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  The column choice imposes the first inequality; the ratio choice imposes
  the other two. Stage growth gives <math|J<rsub|l+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|l>+1>.

  The following estimate retains the signed difference produced by
  subtracting a column. For a continuation
  on<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>, let
  <math|x<rsub|l>,r<rsub|l>> be its coefficients and remainder bounds. Write
  <math|q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>> for the combined coefficient and remainder
  bound of the original expression at<nbsp><math|m>, before the affine column
  subtraction. In the next lemma, the interval<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>
  is sufficiently late. A continuation consists of local marked expressions,
  each a normalized column plus a zero-coefficient remainder.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Signed local continuation><label|lem:J-signed-local> (a) Every
    normalized column plus a zero-coefficient remainder at
    level<nbsp><math|m> admits such a continuation
    on<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>. Its densities satisfy
    <math|H<rsub|l>=\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t><blockmap|l><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,l+1>>H<rsub|l+1>>
    <math|<around|(|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>l\<less\>m|)>>, each step
    satisfies<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-recall>,
    <math|r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>+C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>>,
    and the free affine parameter can be chosen so that
    <math|x<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=0>.

    (b) If <math|x<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=0>, then, for
    <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>m>,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|l>+\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  The proof derives the interval bound from the signed one-step inequalities.

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-signed-start>We use the termwise remainder estimates and
    the Lipschitz bound in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>,
    the local format of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>, and the normalized
    columns of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>\U<reference|lem:E2.2>, with
    the insertion normalization of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>.

    Let<nbsp><math|R> have coefficient zero and remainder
    bound<nbsp><math|q<rsub|0>>. Its regular terms have zero radial jets
    through<nbsp><math|t>; its forced terms retain their eligibility events.
    In the marked integration of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>,
    the former gain an inverse block factor before the connected sums, and
    the latter keep half the forced suppression. The stage constants precede
    the ratio and its entry threshold, so both resulting bounds
    <math|C<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>/\<ell\><rsub|i>+o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>><around|(|1|)>>
    are at most<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>>. Sum
    the regular masses<nbsp><math|g<rsub|a>> and forced masses
    <math|k<rsub|b>/<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>
    before taking the output norm. Their sum is at
    most<nbsp><math|q<rsub|0>>. The termwise bounds in that proof, or its
    Lipschitz estimate for the difference between a column plus<nbsp><math|R>
    and the same column, therefore bound the output coefficient and remainder
    by<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>>. The rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, in the marker norm defined below,
    justifies termwise integration and retains the exact density identity.
    The last inequality of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-stage-room> gives
    <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>>.
    If<nbsp><math|Z> denotes the resulting local expression and<nbsp><math|z>
    its coefficient, then

    <\equation*>
      H<around|(|Z|)>=\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|t><blockmap|i><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,i+1>>H<around|(|R|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>,<space|2em>\<frak-r\><rsub|i><around|(|Z|)>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Write the fine affine family as <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|i+1>*<around|(|a+b|)>+R>,
    with<nbsp><math|b> the free parameter. Set
    <math|c=<math-ss|X><rsub|i><rsup|-1>z> and replace it at the coarse level
    by

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-H\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a+c+b|)>+R<rprime|'>,<space|2em>R<rprime|'>=Z-\<cal-H\><rsub|i><around|(|c|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The coefficient of<nbsp><math|R<rprime|'>> is zero, and
    <math|\<frak-r\><rsub|i><around|(|R<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>>.
    Linearity of the column density gives
    <math|H*<around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a+c+b|)>|)>+H<around|(|R<rprime|'>|)>=H*<around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a+b|)>|)>+H<around|(|Z|)>>.
    Together with the exact column identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>, this proves the asserted density
    identity for every<nbsp><math|b>, including all forced terms. The
    supports remain fixed in<nbsp><math|b> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>.

    For the column alone use<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-recall>, with fine
    remainder bound<nbsp><math|q<rsub|<math-up|col>>>. Adding<nbsp><math|Z>
    changes the coefficient by at most<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>>.
    For the remainder, recentering costs at
    most<nbsp><math|C<rsub|i>*\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>>, and shifting the
    column parameter costs at most another<nbsp><math|C<rsub|i>*\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>>.
    The total extra cost is <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>>.
    With<nbsp><math|q=q<rsub|<math-up|col>>+q<rsub|0>>, these are the
    asserted bounds. Repeating this construction from the fine end to the
    chosen coarse end produces the affine family on the whole interval.

    For later use put <math|U=\<mu\><around|(|u|)>>,
    <math|Y=\<mu\><around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|i>v|)>>,
    <math|K=K<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>> and retain<nbsp><math|r,q> for the remainder
    bounds. Applying<nbsp><math|M<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> and using
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|v<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>\<leqslant\>Y> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-signed-input>Y\<leqslant\>K*<around|[|U+\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|(|Y+q|)>|]>,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>K*Y+\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*q<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For an interval<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>, choose the
    free affine parameter to cancel its coefficient
    at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>; this uses the invertibility
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|X><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>. The signed residual
    then starts with coefficient zero. In step<nbsp><math|l>
    put<nbsp><math|K=K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>> and,
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-signed-input>, put
    <math|A<rsub|l>=32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l>|)><rsup|2>> and induct
    from<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> toward<nbsp><math|m>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-stage-room> implies

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>4*K<rsup|2>,<space|1em>4*K*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>4*K*A<rsub|l+1>*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em><around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/8>*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    From <math|A<rsub|l>*U\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>K*Y+\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*q>
    and <math|A<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>4*K<rsup|2>> obtain
    <math|4*K*U\<leqslant\>Y+\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*q>. Substitution
    into<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-signed-input> gives
    <math|Y\<leqslant\>4*K*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*q>. Thus
    <math|A<rsub|l+1>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>q>, since
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>Y>, and
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/8>*r\<leqslant\>q>. Iteration gives the
    discount<nbsp><math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|1/8>>.
    At the finest level the affine subtraction has
    <math|r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>+C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>>.
    The cone gives <math|2*C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|m>>,
    hence <math|r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>>
    and <math|r<rsub|m>+\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>>.
    Applying the iterated discount between any<nbsp><math|l>
    and<nbsp><math|m> proves the interval bound.<label|proof:J-signed-end>
  </proof>

  The next estimate recovers the output-degree power after passing to the
  quotient. Fix any norm on<nbsp><math|F<rsub|t>>. Put
  <math|r<rsub|t>=<reflevel|t>> and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>=\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>|)>>,
  where <math|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>=x<rsub|N,p,m;t><around|(|v|)>> is the
  transferred coefficient of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>. A
  refinement below is a nontrivial refinement of the ordered cutoff
  limits<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>; it may depend on the cluster geometry.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Graded quotient bound><label|lem:J-graded-quotient> For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t> there are
    positive<nbsp><math|C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>>, independent of the
    refinement and label family, with <math|log
    C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>. If
    labels<nbsp><math|v<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|t>> converge
    in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|t>> to<nbsp><math|v> on such a refinement and
    eventually <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>>,
    then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-graded-input><math-up|dist><rsub|F<rsub|t>><around|(|<around|[|v|]>,\<jmath\><rsub|i-1,t>*F<rsub|i-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>*a<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|t-i|)>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|<around|[|v|]>\<in\>F<rsub|t>> is the class of<nbsp><math|v>,
    <math|F<rsub|-1>=0>, and <math|m> is sufficiently large.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-graded-start>We use the leading-map triangularity proved
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>, the marked bounds of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>, and the physical quotient of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10> with the corrected inclusions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. In the notation
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-recall>, that triangularity is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|M<rsub|l>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>=0*<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\>\<ne\>\<beta\>,deg
      \<alpha\>\<leqslant\>deg \<beta\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The diagonal entries are the nonzero fine-to-coarse normalization
    factors. The full coefficient map differs from this leading map by the
    marked error. Write<nbsp><math|P> for coordinate projection onto
    engineering degrees at most<nbsp><math|i-1>, first
    assuming<nbsp><math|i\<gtr\>0>. The leading map<nbsp><math|M<rsub|l>> and
    its inverse preserve<nbsp><math|ker P>: triangularity gives preservation
    by<nbsp><math|M<rsub|l>>, and injectivity on this finite-dimensional
    subspace gives preservation by its inverse. Consequently
    <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*M<rsub|l>*x|)>>.
    The degree rescaling expands<nbsp><math|P*x> by at
    least<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i+1>>, whereas it
    expands<nbsp><math|x-P*x> by at most<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i>>.
    The marked error is bounded by <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>.

    The two resulting estimates are

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l+1>|)>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l>|)>,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|P*x<rsub|l+1>=0>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\>\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    To obtain the first line, put <math|Y=\<mu\><around|(|P<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|)>>.
    The low-cone hypothesis and <math|C<rsub|l+1>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l>>
    give <math|\<mu\><around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*Y>.
    Applying the inverse leading map to the low coordinates and using
    <math|2*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>1>
    gives <math|Y\<leqslant\>2*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l>|)>>.
    The full coefficient is at most <math|3*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*Y>, so
    <math|64*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>,C<rsub|l+1>>
    gives both conclusions. In the second line use
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>>
    and <math|K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>+1\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>>. These numerical
    inequalities follow from<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-stage-room>.

    At the bare level take the plane<nbsp><math|P*V<rsub|t>>. Its transported
    image remains in the low cone by the first line. The map from this plane
    to its low coordinates at any level is injective by its expansion bound,
    hence onto<nbsp><math|P*V<rsub|t>>. Subtracting a transported vector with
    the same low coordinates at level<nbsp><math|m> therefore leaves zero low
    coordinates and increases the full size by at
    most<nbsp><math|1+C<rsub|m>>. Repeat the subtraction at every next
    coarser level, applying the second line between subtractions. This gives
    a vector<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>> in the transported bare low plane with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|N,p>-y<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>G<rsub|m>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|r<rsub|t>>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|t-i>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>,<space|2em>G<rsub|m>=<big|prod><rsub|l\<less\>m><around|(|1+C<rsub|l>|)>*C<rsub|l+1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Allowing a fixed norm-equivalence factor, put
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|m>=<big|prod><rsub|l\<less\>m>C<rsub|l+1>>. The
    first-line expansion, iterated from the bare level, shows that bounded
    vectors<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>> in this plane have bare coefficients
    bounded by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|t-i+1>*e<rsup|o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>>>.
    Multiplying the same polynomial by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|i-1>> instead
    of<nbsp><math|a<rsub|N><rsup|t>> therefore gives a
    degree-<math|<around|(|i-1|)>> local observable with subpower healthy
    derivative and all-configuration supremum bounds. Its stencil is
    unchanged. To match this observable to the selected lower-degree
    insertions, apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>: it gives
    admissible degree-<math|<around|(|i-1|)>>
    coefficients<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p>> whose insertions differ from these
    observables by a small error. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> removes that
    error in every separated spectator test.

    To pass to a physical lower field, let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>> be the
    equation projections of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>. The normalized
    equation remainder

    <\equation*>
      <frac|c<rsub|N,p>-\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsub|N,p>|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>>>
    </equation*>

    remains admissible: its first term is a bounded scalar multiple
    of<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p>> and its second is bounded.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7> and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> make
    every separated spectator test of it tend to zero. Take a simultaneous
    cluster point<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<lambda\>,z|)>> of
    <math|<around|(|<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*c|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>,\<Pi\>*c/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*c|\<\|\|\>>|)>|)>>
    on a further refinement. The projection
    convergence<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-projections> gives
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|i-1>*z=z> and <math|\<lambda\>+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>.
    If<nbsp><math|y> is a limit of the bounded
    labels<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>>, every spectator functional has limit
    <math|L<around|(|z|)>=\<lambda\>*L<around|(|y|)>>. If
    <math|\<lambda\>=0>, the all-spectator identity
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension> put<nbsp><math|z> in<nbsp><math|ker
    \<Pi\><rsub|i-1>>, contradicting <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>.
    Thus <math|z/\<lambda\>\<in\>V<rsub|i-1>> has exactly the limiting
    correlations of<nbsp><math|y>. The corrected inclusions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> and the all-spectator definition of the
    null space now give <math|<around|[|y|]>\<in\>\<jmath\><rsub|i-1,t>*F<rsub|i-1>>.
    Boundedness of<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>> follows from the displayed
    distance bound and convergence of<nbsp><math|v<rsub|N,p>>. If these
    bounds hold only on the chosen refinement, set<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>=0>
    off the set where they hold; this extends the bounded plane family to the
    original tails without changing its limit. Passing to this limit proves
    the distance estimate.

    For<nbsp><math|i=0> no plane is removed. Iterating the reverse
    coefficient estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-J\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|r<rsub|t>>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|t>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>>
    and hence the same bound in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|t>>. Finally <math|log
    \<cal-J\><rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> by row<nbsp>5 of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. Since <math|C<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>2>,
    <math|G<rsub|m>*C<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<cal-J\><rsub|m>|)><rsup|3>*C<rsub|0>>.
    Thus <math|log G<rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> as well. Fixed
    reference-spacing and quotient-norm factors are absorbed
    in<nbsp><math|C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>>. No estimate depends on the
    refinement used to take the limits.<label|proof:J-graded-end>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Products and nested clusters><label|sec:partII-J.1>

  A probe word is a fixed finite list of fields at distinct nonzero points.
  Correlating a field at the origin against such a word gives a linear
  functional on<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>. The intersection of the kernels of all
  these functionals is zero, by the definition of<nbsp><math|<nullspace|j>>.
  Finite dimensionality therefore permits a finite separating collection.

  Choose the probe functionals recursively. Given biorthogonal
  fields<nbsp><math|u<rsub|\<alpha\>>> and probe
  functionals<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> below degree<nbsp><math|j>,
  continue each<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> to<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>
  by the same correlation formula; inclusions preserve that formula by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. The retraction

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|t><rsub|j>A=<big|sum><rsub|deg
    \<alpha\>\<less\>j>\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|A|)>*\<jmath\><rsub|deg
    \<alpha\>,j-1>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|t><rsub|j>\<jmath\><rsub|j-1,j>=1
  </equation*>

  allows a section<nbsp><math|s> of the new quotient to be replaced by
  <math|s-\<jmath\><math-ss|t><rsub|j>s>. Lift a quotient basis with this
  section and realize its dual by finite combinations of probe functionals,
  which span<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>>. The enlarged system is
  biorthogonal. In particular<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> vanishes
  on<nbsp><math|\<jmath\>*F<rsub|deg \<alpha\>-1>>. For <math|j=0> take
  <math|u<rsub|0>=\<b-1\>>. Let<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j>> be the union of
  the finite probe sets used through degree<nbsp><math|j>, with
  index<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>. Write <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<beta\>>m<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>*S*<around|(|A<around|(|0|)>,B<rsub|j,\<beta\>>|)>>
  in this finite collection.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Finite correlation projection><label|def:D31> Define the decoder
    on arbitrary data by

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|deg
      \<alpha\>\<leqslant\>j><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<beta\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>m<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>*y<rsub|\<beta\>>|)>*\<jmath\><rsub|deg
      \<alpha\>,j>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It satisfies <math|D<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|S*<around|(|A<around|(|0|)>,B<rsub|j,\<beta\>>|)>|)><rsub|\<beta\>>|)>=A>
    for<nbsp><math|A\<in\>F<rsub|j>>. For a word<nbsp><math|w> separated from
    these probes, set

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*w=D<rsub|j><around*|(|<around|(|S*<around|(|w,B<rsub|j,\<beta\>>|)>|)><rsub|\<beta\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This extracts a field from finitely many correlations using the fixed
    biorthogonal coordinates.
  </definition>

  Here and below a word is a finite product of labelled insertions; sums of
  words are read linearly. Rotations act on every position and label, and
  conjugation acts on labels and scalar coefficients. Translation of a word
  by<nbsp><math|u> is denoted by<nbsp><math|\<tau\><rsub|u>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Symmetric projection><label|def:D32> When every rotation
    of<nbsp><math|w> is separated from every probe
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>, set

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|j>*w=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>>R<rsub|j><around|(|h|)>*P<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*<around|(|h<rsup|-1>*w|)>*<space|0.17em>d*h,<space|2em>B<rsub|j>*w=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|A<rsub|j>*w+<wide|A<rsub|j>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>|\<bar\>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The Haar measure has mass one. Outside this domain set both maps to zero.
    At center<nbsp><math|o>, apply these formulas
    to<nbsp><math|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w>. These averages impose rotation
    covariance and reality.
  </definition>

  Let<nbsp><math|I<rsub|q>*A=A<around|(|0|)>>
  for<nbsp><math|A\<in\>F<rsub|q>>. The inclusions<nbsp><math|\<jmath\><rsub|i,j>>
  identify the same field in different degrees.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|OPE coefficients><label|def:D33> Put
    <math|L<rsub|i,q>=B<rsub|i>*I<rsub|q>> and define

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|0>=1,<space|2em>Q<rsub|j>=1-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j>\<jmath\><rsub|i,j>*Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>,<space|2em>E<rsub|j>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<less\>j>ker
      <around|(|Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the chosen real basis<nbsp><math|<around|(|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>|)><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>>
    of<nbsp><math|E<rsub|j>>, let<nbsp><math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<lambda\>>:E<rsub|j>\<to\><C>>
    be its coordinate functionals and set

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x,o|)>=\<omega\><rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|Q<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j><space|-0.17em><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-o|)>|]>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The recursion removes lower degrees before reading coordinates and is
    independent of arity and truncation.
  </definition>

  The following splitting makes the coefficients a single choice for all
  later estimates. The proof uses the biorthogonal choices just made.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One channel prescription><label|lem:J1.1> The real
    fields<nbsp><math|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>> form complex bases of
    conjugation-invariant complements:

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|j>=\<jmath\><rsub|j-1,j>*F<rsub|j-1>\<oplus\>E<rsub|j>,<space|2em>E<rsub|j>=<math-up|span><rsub|<C>><around|{|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>:\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>|}>,<space|2em>F<rsub|-1>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The degree-zero vector spans the unit line and need not equal the unit.
    All coefficients are fixed before spectators, states and truncations.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the field calculus of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> and the preceding
    biorthogonal construction. Its reconstruction formula fixes every field
    of<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>. Continuing an old functional means using the
    same finite correlations, since <math|\<iota\><rsub|j>*\<jmath\><rsub|q,j>=\<iota\><rsub|q>>;
    no extension of a functional is chosen arbitrarily.

    For<nbsp><math|i\<geqslant\>q>, this reconstruction and the rotation and
    reality laws give<nbsp><math|L<rsub|i,q>=\<jmath\><rsub|q,i>>. Also
    <math|L<rsub|i,j>*\<jmath\><rsub|q,j>=L<rsub|i,q>>
    for<nbsp><math|i\<less\>q\<leqslant\>j>: both sides evaluate the same
    single-field word before averaging. For all<nbsp><math|q\<leqslant\>j>,
    induction in<nbsp><math|j> now gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-cuts>Q<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j,q>=0*<around|(|q\<less\>j|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<leqslant\>j>\<jmath\><rsub|i,j>*Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,q>=\<jmath\><rsub|q,j><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For<nbsp><math|q=j> the second identity is the recursion;
    for<nbsp><math|q\<less\>j>, compose that recursion
    with<nbsp><math|\<jmath\><rsub|q,j>> and use the compatibility just
    proved and the induction hypothesis. In the same induction assume
    <math|Q<rsub|k>*F<rsub|k>\<subset\>E<rsub|k>> and <math|Q<rsub|k>=1>
    on<nbsp><math|E<rsub|k>> for<nbsp><math|k\<less\>j>.
    Applying<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>> to<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>*A>
    then cancels its matching<nbsp><math|i> term; terms
    with<nbsp><math|k\<less\>i> vanish because the<nbsp><math|i> cut kills
    lower fields, and terms with<nbsp><math|i\<less\>k\<less\>j> vanish
    because<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|k>*A\<in\>E<rsub|k>>.
    Thus<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>*F<rsub|j>\<subset\>E<rsub|j>>.
    On<nbsp><math|E<rsub|j>>, the defining sum for<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>> is
    zero, so<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>=1>. Its kernel is
    exactly<nbsp><math|\<jmath\>*F<rsub|j-1>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-cuts>. Haar
    invariance and simultaneous input and output conjugation show that every
    cut intertwines rotations and conjugation. Thus<nbsp><math|E<rsub|j>> is
    invariant under both. Its fixed real subspace spans it, since
    <math|v=<around|(|v+<wide|v|\<bar\>>|)>/2+i*<around|(|v-<wide|v|\<bar\>>|)>/<around|(|2*i|)>>;
    a real basis is also a complex basis. This proves the stated splitting
    and the simultaneous choice.
  </proof>

  For <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> write <math|f\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|b*T>> when,
  for every<nbsp><math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>, there is<nbsp><math|T<rsub|0>> such
  that for every<nbsp><math|T\<geqslant\>T<rsub|0>> an ordered cutoff tail
  gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|b+\<tau\>|)>*T>>,
  uniformly in the cutoff and volume on that tail. Products add exponents and
  finite sums take their maximum. Factors of exponent zero are called
  subpower. A level<nbsp><math|k> has height<nbsp><math|b>
  when<nbsp><math|s<rsub|k>/T\<to\>b>; thus
  <math|a<rsub|k>=e<rsup|-b*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> supplies these levels by taking the first
  with <math|s<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>b*T>, or
  <math|s<rsub|k>\<geqslant\><sqrt|T>> if<nbsp><math|b=0>.

  Introduce commuting variables<nbsp><math|z<rsub|\<iota\>><rsup|2>=0> for
  the insertions in the reflected cells. A live marker is one distinct
  insertion group of positive weight<nbsp><math|w<rsub|\<iota\>>>. In this
  finite scalar algebra put

  <\equation*>
    c=<big|sum><rsub|A>c<rsub|A>*z<rsub|A>,<space|2em>z<rsub|A>=<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>\<in\>A>z<rsub|\<iota\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>>=<big|sum><rsub|A><around|\||c<rsub|A>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>\<in\>A>w<rsub|\<iota\>>,<space|2em>c<rsub|A>\<in\><C><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Disjoint marker splittings, multiplicativity of the weights and the
  triangle inequality give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c*d|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>><around|\<\|\|\>|d|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>>>.

  If <math|F<rsub|X,A>> is a coefficient with support<nbsp><math|X> and
  marker set<nbsp><math|A>, its weighted majorant is

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|X,A>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|X|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|X,A>|\<\|\|\>>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>\<in\>A>w<rsub|\<iota\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|X,A>|\<\|\|\>>> is the derivative or
  forced majorant of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>;
  at a support ball use its anchored sum. The factors
  <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|X|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|X,A>|\<\|\|\>>>
  enter as<nbsp><math|v<rsub|a>> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>.
  Only the marker norm is used submultiplicatively. A grouped coefficient
  carries the product of its live marker weights, denoted
  by<nbsp><math|\<omega\>>.

  For the marked expansion put

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|l><rsup|<math-up|mark>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|l>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<Xi\><rsub|l>=exp
    <sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1>>,<space|2em>z<rsub|t,l>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|t>>.
  </equation*>

  The fixed integer<nbsp><math|\<nu\><rsub|t>> bounds the local
  degree-<math|t> polynomial losses, and <math|z<rsub|t,l>*C<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1>
  at entry. The four factors pay respectively for support enlargement,
  regular transport, Taylor extraction and insertion normalization. Their
  logarithms and<nbsp><math|log C<rsub|l>> are
  <math|o<around|(|s<rsub|l>|)>>. For an initial
  weight<nbsp><math|\<varpi\>\<gtr\>0> at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> define
  its later weights by

  <\equation*>
    \<varpi\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=\<varpi\>,<space|2em>\<varpi\><rsub|l+1>=\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<varpi\><rsub|l>*<space|1em><around|(|l\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The marked entry choices require <math|\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t+1>*z<rsub|t,l>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|t,l+1>>
  for <math|l\<geqslant\>r<rsub|t>>. This follows the zero-jet regular
  remainder through the integration.

  Write <math|\<frak-b\><rsub|l>> for the support radius <math|B<rsub|l>> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. Fix a reflected cell at
  level<nbsp><math|k> and choose

  <\equation*>
    k<rsub|\<ast\>>=min <around|{|l\<geqslant\>k:\<frak-b\><rsub|l><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|l>|}>,<space|2em>\<cal-S\><rsub|k>=exp
    <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|4><math-ss|v><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>+5|}>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-ss|v><rsub|l>=q<rsub|l>> is the per-site logarithmic
  bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-majorant>. These quantities ensure that the
  marked stencils fit in the cell and bound its density normalization.

  For a grouped coefficient at level<nbsp><math|m+1> of
  weight<nbsp><math|\<omega\>\<gtr\>0>, put

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|m>=4*\<Xi\><rsub|m>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|t>/\<omega\>,<space|2em>\<frak-E\><rsub|m>=8*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|m><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The terminal weight<nbsp><math|\<varpi\>> must satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-marked-weights>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*z<rsub|t,m>/z<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|mark>>*\<omega\>/<around|(|16*\<Xi\><rsub|m>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*\<varpi\>*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|-t>*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|1/8>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>z<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>/2.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  These conditions pay for extraction at<nbsp><math|m> and for the continued
  residual, respectively.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked extraction and insertion costs><label|lem:J-marked-costs> A
    grouped coefficient of weight<nbsp><math|\<omega\>> has a degree-<math|t>
    local extraction with coefficient and remainder each at
    most<nbsp><math|q<rsub|m>>. With <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> as above,
    sufficiently late, and whenever<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> holds,
    the matched field has coefficient size at
    most<nbsp><math|2*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>>. Its interior reflected remainder
    in the level-<math|k> cell has root at
    most<nbsp><math|2*\<cal-S\><rsub|k>/\<varpi\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-costs-start>We use the norms and insertions of
    Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>
    and<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, the marked integration of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>, the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> in the marker norm, the majorants
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, and the local format of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>. Divide a grouped coefficient by its
    available weight<nbsp><math|\<omega\>>. Extract its radial polynomial
    through<nbsp><math|t> and write the regular complement by the integral
    Taylor formula. The finite derivative sums, coefficient conversion and
    remainder constants are at most<nbsp><math|\<Xi\><rsub|m>> after the
    entry levels have been chosen: their logarithms are bounded by stage
    constants and powers of<nbsp><math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>,
    whereas <math|log \<Xi\><rsub|m>=<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>>.
    Multiplication by<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|t>>
    gives<nbsp><math|q<rsub|m>>. The factor four bounds the polynomial and
    complement separately. Forced terms keep their original majorants and the
    forced normalization in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>.

    The combined cost is at most<nbsp><math|2*q<rsub|m>>. Subtract the column
    matching the continuation at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local> gives, for
    <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>m>,

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-r\><rsub|l>+\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>\<frak-E\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|field>>|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    The factor <math|4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>>
    multiplying<nbsp><math|2*q<rsub|m>> includes the finest residual and
    coefficient together. The entry requirement
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t+1>*z<rsub|t,l>\<leqslant\>z<rsub|t,l+1>>
    gives the regular zero-jet gain: iterating
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l+1>=\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<varpi\><rsub|l>> yields
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*z<rsub|t,m>/z<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
    The first condition of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> permits
    extraction at<nbsp><math|m>. For the second, the level-<math|l> ratio of
    required to permitted residual weight is bounded by its
    level-<math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> value times
    <math|<around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|7/8>\<leqslant\>1>:
    use <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*z<rsub|t,l>/z<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>,
    the residual bound <math|\<frak-E\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>>
    and divide by <math|z<rsub|t,l>>. Thus the condition
    at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> permits the signed residual and
    compensating columns throughout the interval. Their density identities
    are exact by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>.

    In the square-zero variables defined above, a marked polynomial is
    measured by the sum of the absolute coefficients times the product of
    their assigned weights. Consequently the coefficient with one residual
    per cell is at most<nbsp><math|\<varpi\><rsup|-1>> per cell times that
    sum. Summing the unmarked majorants and dividing by the two positive
    density normalizations bounds the integral per cell
    by<nbsp><math|\<cal-S\><rsub|k>>. The singleton, forced-profile,
    connected regular and eligible forced sums contribute at most
    <math|2,1,1,1> per cell, giving the additive constant five. Apply this to
    the original local difference and the correction representing its column,
    and use the root triangle inequality, obtained by expanding the reflected
    product and applying<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> to each term. This
    gives<nbsp><math|2*\<cal-S\><rsub|k>/\<varpi\>>.

    To verify that<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> has the required height, let
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|l>=max <around|(|1,<math-ss|v><rsub|l>|)>> bound the
    normalization cost. The entry choices satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-entrance>\<frak-b\><rsub|l+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*\<frak-b\><rsub|l><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|l>*\<frak-b\><rsub|l><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*\<Phi\><rsub|l+1>=o<around|(|s<rsub|l+1>|)>.
    </equation>

    The level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> exists since
    <math|a<rsub|l>*\<frak-b\><rsub|l><rsup|2>> decreases by at
    least<nbsp><math|2/3> at each step. If <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>=k>, the
    spacing ratio is one. Otherwise <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>=l+1>, and
    minimality gives <math|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l>*\<frak-b\><rsub|l><rsup|2>>.
    Therefore <math|<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|4><math-ss|v><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>.
    Also <math|0\<leqslant\>s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>-s<rsub|k>=log
    <around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|4>*\<Phi\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>.
    This proves <math|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>/s<rsub|k>\<to\>1> and <math|log
    \<cal-S\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>. At height zero
    it still gives <math|log \<cal-S\><rsub|k>=o<around|(|T|)>>.
    Finally<nbsp><math|log \<Xi\><rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> follows
    from <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*s<rsub|m>>
    and <math|s<rsub|m+1>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>1>. The other displayed functions
    are fixed powers of<nbsp><math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>> or the subpower column
    cost of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>.<label|proof:J-costs-end>
  </proof>

  Fix nonempty lattice labels<nbsp><math|v<rsub|\<iota\>>\<in\>V<rsub|d<rsub|\<iota\>>>>,
  let<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>> be their number and
  put<nbsp><math|W=<big|sum><rsub|\<iota\>>d<rsub|\<iota\>>>. Choose
  levels<nbsp><math|k,m,j> of heights <math|0\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>.
  The input points lie on the spacing-<math|a<rsub|j>> grid, in the centered
  level-<math|<around|(|m+1|)>> block <math|<around|[|-a<rsub|m+1>/2,a<rsub|m+1>/2|)><rsup|4>>,
  and their separation is at least<nbsp><math|\<frak-b\><rsub|j><rsup|2>*a<rsub|j>>,
  with the support radius<nbsp><math|\<frak-b\><rsub|j>> defined above. The
  configurations belong to<nbsp><math|<Gen|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>>, and all
  stencils are interior to the level-<math|k> cell. These conditions hold
  eventually for clusters of extent <math|e<rsup|-x*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> and
  reciprocal separation at most <math|e<rsup|x*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>, where
  <math|\<theta\>\<less\>x\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>. The
  chosen<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> has height<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|->>
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-entrance>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Lattice cluster replacement><label|lem:J-lattice> For every output
    degree<nbsp><math|t>, the preceding configurations admit
    labels<nbsp><math|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>\<in\>V<rsub|t>> on an
    ordered cutoff tail such that, with <math|\<cal-Z\>=W*\<theta\><rsub|+>+4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|\<cal-Z\>-t*\<theta\>|)>*T>,>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|\<iota\>>,x<rsub|\<iota\>>|)>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>,0|)>|\|><rsub|\<cal-T\><rsub|k><around|(|0|)>>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|[|\<cal-Z\>-<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>|]>*T>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The difference is interior. The bounds are uniform in cutoff and volume
    on that tail.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-lattice-start>Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D26>,
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>\U<reference|lem:E2.2a>, and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs>. In the square-zero variables
    defined above, the coefficient of their product in
    <math|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>><around|(|1+z<rsub|\<iota\>>*O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|\<iota\>>,x<rsub|\<iota\>>|)>|)>>
    is the product insertion times the density. Until level<nbsp><math|j> the
    stencils are disjoint, so their marked integrations are separate.

    Moving a marker to its representative through at most<nbsp><math|h> cover
    balls gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-support-move><cover><around|(|X\<cup\><around|{|y<rsub|\<iota\>>:\<iota\>\<in\>A|}>|)>\<leqslant\><cover><around|(|X|)>+h<around|\||A|\|>.
    </equation>

    If at most<nbsp><math|\<frak-n\>> representatives occupy a ball,
    shrinking each affected weight by <math|\<eta\>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>>
    gives the original term times<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsup|<around|\||A|\|>>>.
    In the anchored sum, terms incident on the old ball use its original
    majorant; any new incidence can be assigned to an old marker on the
    joining path. There are at most<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>> old
    markers for each new representative and<nbsp><math|\<frak-n\>>
    representatives. Taking <math|\<eta\>\<leqslant\><around|[|<around|(|1+\<frak-n\>|)>*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>|]><rsup|-1>>
    pays for these assignments. Unmarked terms keep their supports. Hence the
    enlarged weighted series is bounded by the original majorant series of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>.

    Keep a maximal <math|\<frak-b\><rsub|l><rsup|2>>-separated set of
    representatives at each level, retaining the distinguished one at the
    origin, and attach each discarded point to a retained neighbor. A fusion
    decreases the number of groups, so there are at
    most<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>-1> fusions. The joining-cover bound
    can be taken as <math|h<rsub|l>=16*<around|[|1+\<ell\><rsub|l>*<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsub|l>+\<frak-b\><rsub|l+1>+1|)><rsup|2>|]><rsup|4>>.
    The entry choices give <math|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>+1|)>*h<rsub|l>=o<around|(|s<rsub|l>|)>>
    and subpower occupancy. Choose one<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1>
    below all the resulting enlargement allowances
    for<nbsp><math|m\<leqslant\>l\<less\>j>. It is subpower: the logarithm of
    the running minimum is bounded by the maximum logarithmic cost; split
    this maximum into a fixed initial segment and a tail bounded
    by<nbsp><math|\<tau\>*s<rsub|j>>, then send<nbsp><math|\<tau\>> to zero.
    This uses the entrance requirement on<nbsp><math|h<rsub|l>> in
    rows<nbsp>6\U7 of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. The factor<nbsp><math|<around|[|<around|(|1+\<frak-n\>|)>*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>|]><rsup|-1>>
    above is included in this allowance<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>/8>.

    Between fusions the one-mark integration costs at
    most<nbsp><math|6*\<ell\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|4>> in
    each weight. To derive this bound, let<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>> be an
    incoming marked majorant. Apply the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> coefficientwise in the
    submultiplicative marker norm, with the marked factor fixed. Separate the
    transported regular terms into the zero-jet local integration remainder,
    of total weighted mass<nbsp><math|p<rsub|l>>, and the incoming regular
    contribution carried without Taylor subtraction.

    Use the support conversion factor<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>> defined
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion>. For a fine support<nbsp><math|X>
    of the unsubtracted incoming contribution, let<nbsp><math|Y> be its
    coarse support, including the incident windows. The support conversion
    estimate there gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|l>>+2|)><cover><rsub|\<frak-b\><rsub|l>><around|(|Y|)>\<leqslant\>log
      \<cal-A\><rsub|l>+<tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|l+1>><cover><rsub|\<frak-b\><rsub|l+1>><around|(|X|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Exponentiating bounds this incoming mass
    by<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>*\<cal-L\>>; the remaining half of the
    fine exponent is nonnegative. Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> imposes<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l>>.

    The combined regular mass is thus at most<nbsp><math|p<rsub|l>+\<cal-A\><rsub|l>*\<cal-L\>>.
    Splitting a complex marked term into its real and imaginary parts costs
    at most twice its majorant. For the forced terms,
    let<nbsp><math|f<rsub|l>> denote their total contribution after this
    splitting and the normalization in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>; their
    eligibility tests remain attached. Use the termwise bounds in the proof
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> for the zero-jet local
    remainder: its regular part retains the Taylor gain, and its forced part
    retains half the forced suppression. The unsubtracted incoming
    contribution is bounded by the support conversion above; the
    <math|\<eta\>*r> graph bound in that proof is not used for this
    contribution. Raising the entry coupling
    gives<nbsp><math|2*p<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<cal-L\>>
    and<nbsp><math|f<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<cal-L\>>. Adding the two
    contributions gives

    <\equation*>
      2*<around|(|p<rsub|l>+\<cal-A\><rsub|l>*\<cal-L\>|)>+f<rsub|l>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*\<ell\><rsub|l>+2|)>*\<cal-L\>\<leqslant\>6*\<ell\><rsub|l>*\<cal-L\>.
    </equation*>

    With<nbsp><math|g> groups remaining, their product weight at
    level<nbsp><math|l> is consequently at least

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-marker-weights><around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|j>/8|)><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>-g|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>w<rsub|\<iota\>>.
    </equation>

    Indeed a separated step multiplies the weight
    by<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|-4*g>\<geqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|-4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>>.
    A fusion to<nbsp><math|g<rprime|'>\<less\>g> uses at
    most<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>
    factors<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>/8>, whereas
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|g-g<rprime|'>|)>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>.

    At<nbsp><math|m+1> all representatives have the same ancestor. The
    initial normalized-field weights can be chosen with
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>w<rsub|\<iota\>>=e<rsup|-W*\<theta\><rsub|+>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>. Thus<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marker-weights>
    gives <math|\<omega\>=e<rsup|-\<cal-Z\>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> and
    <math|\<frak-E\><rsub|m>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<cal-Z\>-t*\<theta\>|)>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>.
    Choose

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-terminal-weight>\<varpi\>=exp
      <around|{|<around|[|<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>-\<cal-Z\>|]>*T|}>.
    </equation>

    The second condition of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> has exponent
    <math|-<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>/16\<less\>0>; the first
    has exponent <math|-\<cal-Z\>-15*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>/16>,
    strictly below the exponent<nbsp><math|-\<cal-Z\>> of its right side.
    Both therefore hold, including every subpower factor.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs> gives the coefficient
    bound<nbsp><math|2*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>> and root
    bound<nbsp><math|2*\<cal-S\><rsub|k>/\<varpi\>> asserted here. All
    enlargements fit the prescribed cell margin. Reflection and conjugation
    preserve the majorants, so the same calculation applies to all cells and
    all cutoffs on the common tail.<label|proof:J-lattice-end>
  </proof>

  We next state the bounds when the exterior distances also shrink. Write
  <math|w=<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>,
  <math|W=<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>> and
  <math|V=<around|\||\<b-B\>|\|><rsub|deg>>. As <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>,
  suppose the cluster lies within <math|e<rsup|-\<alpha\>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>
  of<nbsp><math|o>, its pairwise distances are at least
  <math|e<rsup|-\<alpha\>*T-o<around|(|T|)>>>, and <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0>.
  Exterior points are at distance at least
  <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*T-o<around|(|T|)>>> from every cluster point
  and<nbsp><math|o>, and mutually at least
  <math|e<rsup|-\<upsilon\>*T-o<around|(|T|)>>> apart, where
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<less\>\<alpha\>> and
  <math|\<upsilon\>\<geqslant\>\<beta\>>. The field lists are fixed, or range
  over fixed compact sets of coefficients. Call these conditions<nbsp>(G).
  Let <math|u<rsub|<around|(|i,\<kappa\>|)>>>, <math|\<kappa\>> ranging over
  the degree-<math|i> basis fields, be the biorthogonal fields of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J.1>, and put
  <math|w<rsub|o>=\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w>. Set

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|i,\<kappa\>><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<beta\>>m<rsub|<around|(|i,\<kappa\>|)>*\<beta\>>*S*<around|(|w<rsub|o>,B<rsub|i,\<beta\>>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|h>>|<cell|=P<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*w<rsub|o>\<in\>F<rsub|h>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|=M<rsup|0>*<around*|(|w*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|)>-M<rsup|0>*<around*|(|A<rsub|D><around|(|o|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|)>\<in\>\<frak-A\><rsub|0><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Here <math|P<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|raw>>> is the projection of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D31>, and <math|M<rsup|0>> is the
  compression of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>.

  The following estimates retain the exterior distance powers needed for
  nested clusters and contacts.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Power-separated estimates><label|lem:J-power-separated> For every
    <math|i,D\<geqslant\>0> and <math|j\<leqslant\>h>, geometry paths
    obeying<nbsp>(G) satisfy

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|i,\<kappa\>><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|W-i|)>*\<alpha\>*T>,>|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|[|W*\<alpha\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\>|]>*T>,<eq-number><label|eq:J-raw-rates>>>|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j>*w-Q<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j,h>*A<rsub|h>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|W-h-1|)>*\<alpha\>*T>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:J-average-error>>>>>
    </align>

    The final coefficients and tested remainder of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D33> obey the same respective bounds, in
    norm before state evaluation.
  </lemma>

  The proof converts lattice roots to algebra norms, removes the grid
  restriction by derivative descent, and then averages the decoder.

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-power-start>We use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-lattice>,
    the derivative calculus of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>, the root bounds
    of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> and<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>, and the
    compression limit of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>.
    Write<nbsp><math|W=<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>>,
    <math|V=<around|\||\<b-B\>|\|><rsub|deg>>
    and<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>=<around|\||I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>|\|>>.

    First convert roots into algebra norms. If a lattice difference has
    centered root at most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>>, and exterior
    insertions have roots at most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>>,
    apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>
    to<nbsp><math|2*n> widely translated copies, alternately conjugated. Put
    the difference in a coarse cell and the exterior insertions in finer
    disjoint cells. Their stencils fit by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>; the
    bound is<nbsp><math|<around|(|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>|)><rsup|2*n>>.
    For the positive time-axis OS Hamiltonian, let<nbsp><math|E> be its
    spectral resolution. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>-P<rsub|4><rsup|0>|)>*\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int><rsub|<around|(|0,\<infty\>|)>>e<rsup|-2*t*\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>d*<around|\<langle\>|E<rsub|\<lambda\>>*\<psi\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Inserting this strong limit between bounded slabs in the compression
    construction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2> identifies the limit of
    these translated copies with the moment
    of<nbsp><math|<around|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><rsup|n>>. The ordered limits
    and then infinite translation separation give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-moments><big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|><rsup|2*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>|)><rsup|2*n><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here<nbsp><math|U> is the corresponding difference
    in<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>. If<nbsp><math|<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<gtr\>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>>
    anywhere, continuity and the full support of<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH> give an open set of positive measure
    where it exceeds this bound by a fixed factor.
    Letting<nbsp><math|n\<to\>\<infty\>> contradicts<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-moments>.
    Thus<nbsp><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>>.
    The same argument bounds a word by its insertion roots, giving crude
    exponent<nbsp><math|W*\<alpha\>+V*\<upsilon\>> on the stated geometries.

    We give the sharper calculation first for the generic grid configurations
    defined above in a cluster of height<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0>.
    Choose <math|0\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>\<less\>\<alpha\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>,
    with exterior distance exponent<nbsp><math|\<beta\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|->>,
    and set<nbsp><math|\<cal-Z\>=W*\<theta\><rsub|+>+4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>>.
    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-lattice> with an auxiliary
    degree<nbsp><math|t\<geqslant\>D>. For each fixed cluster geometry the
    output coefficients are bounded at fixed<nbsp><math|m>; the reverse
    transfer inequality of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> bounds them also
    at the reference level. Along a refinement of the ordered cutoff tails
    they converge to a field<nbsp><math|A<rsup|<math-up|out>>\<in\>F<rsub|t>>.
    The preceding norm argument bounds the replacement error by exponent

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Z\>-<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>+V*\<upsilon\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Testing the same difference against the fixed probes bounds the raw
    coordinate errors. Reference-level coefficient convergence and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-graded-quotient> apply on this
    geometry-dependent refinement. The degree-<math|i> raw coordinate
    annihilates<nbsp><math|F<rsub|i-1>> by the biorthogonality before
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D31>, and is a fixed bounded functional on
    the quotient. With<nbsp><math|\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|\<cal-Z\>-t*\<theta\>|)>*T>>,
    <eqref|eq:J-graded-input> therefore bounds that coordinate by
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<cal-Z\>-i*\<theta\>>.

    Choose<nbsp><math|t> large enough that
    <math|<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|D+1|)>*\<theta\>>
    and also large enough for every coefficient being tested. Exact
    reconstruction of<nbsp><math|A<rsup|<math-up|out>>> by the prescription
    separates the degrees<nbsp><math|i\<leqslant\>D>
    from<nbsp><math|D\<less\>i\<leqslant\>t>. An omitted degree contributes
    at most

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Z\>-i*\<theta\>+i*\<beta\>+V*\<upsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Z\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The low-degree coordinate errors satisfy the same bound, by the chosen
    auxiliary horizon. We have therefore proved grid
    exponents<nbsp><math|\<cal-Z\>-i*\<theta\>> for raw degree-<math|i>
    coordinates and<nbsp><math|\<cal-Z\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\>>
    for the raw remainder.

    Run the crude, grid and derivative bounds uniformly over all
    characters<nbsp><math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>. The
    algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> is commutative, so
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>=sup<rsub|\<xi\>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|>>.
    To remove the grid restriction, consider these character evaluations for
    every input label list and every positional derivative. A derivative
    replaces its input label by a field of degree one higher, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. For suitable
    constants<nbsp><math|c<rsub|0>,d<rsub|0>> independent of the input
    degrees, the crude bound has exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>*W+c<rsub|0>>
    and the grid bound exponent<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*W+d<rsub|0>>. At
    all<nbsp><math|W> with<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<alpha\>|)>*W\<geqslant\>c<rsub|0>-d<rsub|0>>,
    the crude bound already proves the grid exponent at arbitrary points.
    Descend in<nbsp><math|W>. Move the configuration
    by<nbsp><math|O<around|(|a<rsub|j>|)>> to generic level-<math|j> grid
    points; the intervening segment remains separated at
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>>. The induction hypothesis bounds its
    derivative by<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*<around|(|W+1|)>+d<rsub|0>>,
    and multiplication by<nbsp><math|a<rsub|j>=e<rsup|-\<theta\><rsub|+>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>
    gives<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*W+d<rsub|0>> for the change. This
    proves the descent for every slot and axis.

    Now let<nbsp><math|\<theta\>*\<uparrow\>*\<alpha\>>
    and<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*\<downarrow\>*\<alpha\>>,
    keeping<nbsp><math|\<beta\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>>.
    Translation moves the origin to<nbsp><math|o>. This
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-raw-rates> for arbitrary configurations. The
    remainder estimate is in<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>, and empty
    clusters give zero remainder.

    Finally transfer these estimates to Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D33>.
    Let<nbsp><math|A<rsub|h>\<in\>F<rsub|h>> be the raw truncation at an
    auxiliary degree<nbsp><math|h>. In every rotated probe correlation, the
    difference between the cluster and<nbsp><math|A<rsub|h><around|(|0|)>>
    has exponent<nbsp><math|<around|(|W-h-1|)>*\<alpha\>>. The marked norm
    bounds and multilinearity make<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-raw-rates> uniform on
    compact coefficient sets, hence on the rotation orbit. Haar averaging,
    conjugation and the fixed maps<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>> preserve the
    exponent. This proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-average-error>.
    With<nbsp><math|h=j>, all lower raw modes vanish
    under<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j,j>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-cuts>; the
    remaining modes have exponent<nbsp><math|<around|(|W-j|)>*\<alpha\>>.
    With<nbsp><math|h=D>, use<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-cuts> to
    reconstruct<nbsp><math|A<rsub|D>>, and telescope the raw remainder with
    the errors<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-average-error>
    for<nbsp><math|j\<leqslant\>D>. Testing an error in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>
    costs<nbsp><math|j*\<beta\>+V*\<upsilon\>>, so their largest exponent
    is<nbsp><math|W*\<alpha\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\>>.<label|proof:J-power-end>
  </proof>

  The lattice replacement gives two small-radius estimates with different
  fixed data. For the coefficient fix<nbsp><math|\<b-A\>,M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>,\<eta\>,\<varepsilon\>,j,\<lambda\>>;
  for the remainder fix<nbsp><math|\<b-A\>,\<b-B\>,M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>,\<eta\>,\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>,\<varepsilon\>,D>.
  The parameters<nbsp><math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>> are nonnegative and the
  separation and error parameters are positive, as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>.

  For the algebra-valued remainder put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|D>=<wide|<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|^>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D,<space|0.17em>\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>><space|0.17em><wide|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>><around|(|o|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|^>.
  </equation*>

  Here<nbsp><math|<wide|w|^>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>> is the compressed
  separated word of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>, and its characters and vacuum state give
  the phase and mixed remainders of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Separated estimates><label|lem:J1.2> There are
    thresholds<nbsp><math|r<rsub|0>> for the coefficient bound
    and<nbsp><math|r<rsub|1>> for the remainder bound, preceding positions
    and states, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x,o|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|j-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>>,<space|2em><around|\||\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|D+1-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>>
    </equation*>

    on the respective admissible configurations
    for<nbsp><math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|0>>
    or<nbsp><math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|1>>. The second bound also holds
    in<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> before state evaluation.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J1.2-start>Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated>
    to Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>. Set <math|T=-log r>,
    <math|\<alpha\>=1> and <math|\<beta\>=\<upsilon\>=0>. Every admissible
    geometry has these bounds on extent and reciprocal distances. If either
    claimed threshold failed, choose violating configurations at radii
    tending to zero. The estimates just proved along that sequence, with
    loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>/2>, eventually contradict the violation
    with loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>>. Constants and subpower factors are
    absorbed by<nbsp><math|r<rsup|\<varepsilon\>/2>>.
    Taking<nbsp><math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>*r\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>/2>
    also ensures separation. Character evaluation and the mixed vacuum are
    contractions on<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>. This proves both
    estimates with coefficient one and the stated separate
    dependencies.<label|proof:J1.2-end>
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1> gives the fields and coefficients of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18> before spectators, state indices or
    truncation. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.2> gives its coefficient bound
    and its remainder bound with their respective thresholds. These are
    exactly the two assertions of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>.
  </render-proof>

  Integration requires bounds for the full and truncated expressions
  individually. Fix the two field lists, <math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>\<geqslant\>0>,
  <math|\<eta\>,\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|D\<in\><N>>, a measurable space<nbsp><math|Z>
  and<nbsp><math|B<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>.
  Write<nbsp><math|\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>> for the complex integration weight.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Integrated separated estimate><label|lem:J1.3>
    Some<nbsp><math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> works for all smaller radii,
    measures<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsub|Z>>, integrable weights
    with<nbsp><math|<big|int><around|\||\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|Z>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>>,
    and almost-everywhere measurable geometry and phase maps admissible
    on<nbsp><math|<around|{|\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>\<ne\>0|}>>:

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<big|int><rsub|Z>\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>*\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<xi\><around|(|z|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|Z>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>*r<rsup|D+1-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The weighted full and truncated expressions are integrable. The same
    conclusion holds for the mixed state.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.2>,
    and the phase continuity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>. The root
    argument in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated> also bounds the
    full correlation uniformly over these configurations and states for each
    fixed<nbsp><math|r>. The finite truncated sum is the full expression
    minus the uniformly bounded remainder, so it too is uniformly bounded.
    Separate continuity in geometry and phase gives joint measurability by
    rounding the geometry in its open domain to a countable dense set and
    passing to the pointwise limit. The coefficients are smooth where their
    rotated probes are clear; reducing<nbsp><math|r<rsub|0>> puts all
    admissible geometries there. Their compositions are measurable, and
    multiplication by<nbsp><math|\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>> gives integrable
    majorants. Integrate the remainder bound
    against<nbsp><math|<around|\||\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>|\|>>.
  </proof>

  A nested configuration retains leaf positions and internal centers, so it
  also describes partially replaced words. Call an internal vertex other than
  the root proper, and fix its truncation<nbsp><math|D<rsub|v>\<in\><N>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Nested geometry and rates><label|def:D34> Give each
    vertex<nbsp><math|v> of a finite rooted tree a
    position<nbsp><math|z<rsub|v>>, each leaf a field<nbsp><math|A<rsub|i>>,
    and each internal vertex a scale<nbsp><math|\<varrho\><rsub|v>\<gtr\>0>.
    Its root<nbsp><math|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>> is internal. Require
    <math|\<varrho\><rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>=r>,
    <math|\<varrho\><rsub|v>\<leqslant\>\<varrho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>>,
    <math|<around|\||z<rsub|u>-z<rsub|v>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>*\<varrho\><rsub|v>>
    for every descendant<nbsp><math|u>, and
    <math|<around|\||z<rsub|u>-z<rsub|u<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*\<varrho\><rsub|v>>
    for positions in different child subtrees. Put

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|v>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|i<text|
      below >v>deg A<rsub|i>,>|<cell|q<rsub|v>>|<cell|=\<varrho\><rsub|v>/\<varrho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q><around|(|\<varrho\>|)>>|<cell|=r<rsup|q-W<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>-\<varepsilon\>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>>q<rsub|v><rsup|-W<rsub|v>-\<varepsilon\>>,>|<cell|<math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>><around|(|\<varrho\>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>>q<rsub|v><rsup|D<rsub|v>+1><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  Spectators are<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>-clear of every tree
  position and each other.

  The recursion retains every leaf, including those above a neighboring
  truncation.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Iterated coefficient><label|def:D35> A leaf contributes its field
    and coefficient one. At a proper internal vertex<nbsp><math|v>, replace
    its children by

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D<rsub|v>><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>(<text|child
      fields>;<text|child centers>,z<rsub|v>)O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>,
    </equation*>

    multiplying their coefficients and summing. At the root retain
    only<nbsp><math|<around|(|q,\<lambda\>|)>>,
    giving<nbsp><math|C<rsup|<math-up|it>,\<b-D\>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>>. The
    direct coefficient is <math|C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>=c<rsub|q,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;z<rsub|<math-up|leaves>>,z<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>|)>>.
    This compares successive and direct expansion.
  </definition>

  Fix the tree, leaf list, output<nbsp><math|<around|(|q,\<lambda\>|)>>,
  proper-vertex truncations, <math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>\<geqslant\>0>
  and<nbsp><math|\<eta\>,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. No lower bound on the
  ratios<nbsp><math|q<rsub|v>> is imposed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Nested coefficient estimate><label|lem:J1.4> There
    is<nbsp><math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> such that all geometries of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D34> with<nbsp><math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|0>>
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q>,<space|2em><around|\||C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>-C<rsup|<math-up|it>,\<b-D\>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q><math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Both expressions are smooth in neighborhoods of these geometries.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J1.4-start>Use Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D33>,
    <reference|def:D34> and<nbsp><reference|def:D35> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated>. Along any sequence of
    admissible radii and scales tending to the root collision, put
    <math|T=<big|sum><rsub|v<text| internal>>log
    <around|(|1/\<varrho\><rsub|v>|)>>. Eventually every summand is positive.
    After a subsequence, <math|log <around|(|1/\<varrho\><rsub|v>|)>/T\<to\>a<rsub|v>\<geqslant\>0>,
    with<nbsp><math|<big|sum><rsub|v>a<rsub|v>=1>
    and<nbsp><math|a<rsub|v>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>>.
    Set

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\>=W<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>+<big|sum><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>>W<rsub|v>*<around|(|a<rsub|v>-a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    We prove by induction on the tree that a direct output of
    degree<nbsp><math|q> has exponent<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>-q*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>>,
    and its error against the prescribed finite iteration has that exponent
    minus <math|\<gamma\>=min<rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
    internal>><around|(|D<rsub|v>+1|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|v>-a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>|)>>.
    With no proper internal vertices the two coefficients coincide exactly.

    First flatten the edges<nbsp><math|u\<to\>v>
    with<nbsp><math|a<rsub|u>=a<rsub|v>>: combine their grouping before
    making any field replacement. The word at<nbsp><math|v> then consists of
    original leaves in this equal-height component and outputs from strictly
    finer children; its distinct positions are separated at
    exponent<nbsp><math|a<rsub|v>> by the tree geometry. For a child
    with<nbsp><math|a<rsub|u>\<gtr\>a<rsub|v>>, expand to an auxiliary
    horizon<nbsp><math|H<rsub|u>>. The remainder exponent has the
    improvement<nbsp><math|<around|(|H<rsub|u>+1|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|u>-a<rsub|v>|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-raw-rates>. Choose the children in a fixed order,
    choosing<nbsp><math|H<rsub|u>> only after the outputs already used as
    exterior fields are fixed. Each improvement tends to infinity
    with<nbsp><math|H<rsub|u>>, whereas the exterior degree in that step is
    fixed. It can therefore exceed any prescribed error exponent. The
    identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-telescope><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>X<rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>Y<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|h\<less\>i>Y<rsub|h>|)>*<around|(|X<rsub|i>-Y<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|h\<gtr\>i>X<rsub|h>|)>
    </equation>

    justifies these successive replacements in every correlation and in every
    probe defining a coefficient. It is an identity of separated expressions,
    with their scalar coefficient factors retained.

    For a fixed collection of child outputs<nbsp><math|j<rsub|u>>, the child
    exponents include<nbsp><math|-j<rsub|u>*a<rsub|u>>, and the parent
    coefficient contributes<nbsp><math|j<rsub|u>*a<rsub|v>>. Their sum
    contains<nbsp><math|-j<rsub|u>*<around|(|a<rsub|u>-a<rsub|v>|)>>; the
    remaining leaf weights telescope to<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>-q*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>>.
    Nonnegative output degrees therefore never increase this exponent. If the
    root height is zero, apply the crude estimate to this root word of child
    outputs; its finitely many field degrees cost exponent zero, while their
    coefficients already carry the child contributions
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>>. This proves the direct bound, by the preceding
    arbitrary-accuracy replacements and the induction hypothesis. Remove
    auxiliary outputs above<nbsp><math|D<rsub|u>>. Each discarded term has at
    least one<nbsp><math|j<rsub|u>\<geqslant\>D<rsub|u>+1> and thus gains at
    least<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>. Replacing the remaining direct child
    coefficients by their iterated coefficients
    uses<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-telescope> again and gains<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>
    from one child error. If<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>=0>, the direct and iterated
    bounds themselves give the error exponent.
    If<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>, every proper internal scale increase is
    positive and the auxiliary horizons can be chosen to make the preceding
    replacement errors smaller than this exponent. This proves the induction
    for the difference, with one improvement rather than a product of
    improvements.

    The logarithmic exponent of<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q>>
    is

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\>-q*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>+\<varepsilon\>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>A<rsub|\<ast\>>=a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>+<big|sum><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>><around|(|a<rsub|v>-a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>|)>\<gtr\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Positivity follows because<nbsp><math|<big|sum><rsub|v>a<rsub|v>=1>: if
    every displayed nonnegative term vanished, every<nbsp><math|a<rsub|v>>
    would vanish. The exponent of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>>>
    is<nbsp><math|-\<gamma\>>. Thus the estimates with
    loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>/2> imply the displayed bounds with
    loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>> and coefficient one along this sequence.
    A sequence of violations would have the subsequence just treated, a
    contradiction. This proves a threshold uniform in the scale vector, even
    when some scales decrease faster than every power of<nbsp><math|r>. All
    positions stay separated and, for small<nbsp><math|r>, clear of every
    finite rotated probe family used in the recursion. The averaging
    integrands are smooth on an open neighborhood with that clearance; their
    derivatives are bounded on compact neighborhoods
    times<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>>. Differentiating the finite sums and
    Haar integrals proves smoothness.<label|proof:J1.4-end>
  </proof>

  For tested products, retain the original leaves at the root; only the
  degree of its output is truncated. Fix additionally the root
  truncation<nbsp><math|m\<in\><N>>, a spectator list<nbsp><math|\<b-B\>>
  and<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>\<gtr\>0>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Nested tested estimate><label|lem:J1.5> For
    some<nbsp><math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, every smaller radius, admissible
    nested geometry, admissible spectator configuration and state
    index<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-R\><rsub|m><rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>,z<rsub|<math-up|leaves>>;\<b-B\>,y;z<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,m+1><around|(|\<varrho\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The same bound holds in the norm of<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> before
    evaluation in a character or the mixed vacuum.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18> and the tree induction in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.4>. Keep the spectators in every
    replacement. Their reciprocal distances from every tree position and from
    one another have exponent zero. They therefore add no term
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>>. After the arbitrary-accuracy child
    replacements, the root remainder estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated>, has
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>-<around|(|m+1|)>*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>>.
    When<nbsp><math|a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>=0>, apply the crude bound
    to the root word of child outputs after these replacements. The child
    coefficient powers already account for<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>>; the
    finitely many root output fields and the spectators cost exponent zero.
    The positive quantity<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>> used
    above again absorbs all finite sums and subpower factors. The
    contradiction argument is unchanged. The norm estimate follows
    from<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-moments>; applying a character or the mixed vacuum
    proves the scalar bounds with the same threshold.
  </proof>

  Fix now the data of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.4>
  or<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.5>, a measurable parameter space
  and<nbsp><math|B<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>. A scale
  vector<nbsp><math|\<varrho\>> in the following statement is fixed
  throughout each integral.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Integrated nested estimates><label|lem:J1.6> A threshold preceding
    the measure, weight, scale vector and measurable geometry maps gives the
    respective integral bounds

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|0>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q>,<space|2em>B<rsub|0>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q><math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>>,<space|2em>B<rsub|0>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,m+1>
    </equation*>

    for integrable weights of absolute integral at most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|0>>
    and geometries admissible almost everywhere where the weight is nonzero.
    The weighted compared expressions are integrable. The tested bound
    permits a measurable phase map or the mixed state.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For fixed<nbsp><math|\<varrho\>>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.4> bounds
    the direct coefficient and its difference from the iterated coefficient,
    hence also the latter. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.5> bounds the tested
    difference; the root argument<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-moments> bounds the full
    word for these fixed positive separations and hence its truncated
    expression. Smoothness and the joint measurability argument of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.3> give measurability. Multiply these
    constant bounds by the absolute value of the weight and integrate, as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.3>. The thresholds are independent
    of<nbsp><math|\<varrho\>>, although the majorants depend on this fixed
    vector.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Contact subtractions><label|sec:partII-J.2>

  We work with finite sums of field words,
  identifying<nbsp><math|A<around|(|x|)>>
  with<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>*A|)><around|(|x|)>> and
  imposing linearity in<nbsp><math|A>. Their product is concatenation. The
  correlation of a word is read only at distinct positions; elsewhere give it
  value zero. This convention is used inside the algebraic formulas below,
  before any integration. The underlying prescription and its fields are
  those fixed in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>; the finite probes chosen
  next are independent of its OPE probes.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Contact detector projection><label|def:D36> Choose contact probes
    and decoders as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D31>.
    Choose<nbsp><math|a<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>\<gtr\>0> with every probe
    point at distance at least<nbsp><math|2*a<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>
    from zero. For a word<nbsp><math|w>, set

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|q>*w=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<text|all positions of >w<text| lie
      in ><wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|0,a<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>|)>|}>>*w<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply the averaging and reality formula of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D32> to this clipped word with the contact
    probes; extend linearly to sums, obtaining<nbsp><math|B<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>.
    This extracts contact fields on a fixed region clear of its probes.
  </definition>

  For contact expressions<nbsp><math|I<rsub|q>*A=A<around|(|0|)>> still
  denotes a single field. Unlike the domain restriction in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D32>, clipping here acts on each monomial
  before summing it.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Coherent contact projections><label|def:D37>
    With<nbsp><math|j<rsub|q>=\<jmath\><rsub|q,q+1>>, set

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|0>=B<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|ct>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|q+1>=j<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>+B<rsub|q+1><rsup|<math-up|ct>>-j<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>*I<rsub|q+1>*B<rsub|q+1><rsup|<math-up|ct>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*w=P<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The last term removes the component already measured at lower degrees. In
    particular, <math|P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)><around|[|A<around|(|o|)>|]>=A>
    for<nbsp><math|A\<in\>F<rsub|q>>.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Contact grades and clearance><label|def:D38> The exact-degree
    components and common clearance are

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|P<rsub|=0><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*w>|<cell|=B<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>,>>|<row|<cell|P<rsub|=q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*w>|<cell|=<around|(|1-\<jmath\><rsub|q-1,q>*P<rsub|q-1>*I<rsub|q>|)>*B<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>*<space|1em><around|(|q\<gtr\>0|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<delta\><rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|pres>>>|<cell|=min<rsub|0\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>q>
      a<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|ct>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    These give the graded contact subtractions and their common range of
    separated evaluation.
  </definition>

  The projections are defined before any test function or state. Their
  strata, rather than the OPE strata, provide the real bases used in the
  contact construction.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Real bases><label|lem:J2.1> The contact projections are covariant
    under translations, <math|O<around|(|4|)>> and conjugation. Their
    filtered strata have real bases, with the unit in degree zero.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use the inclusions, rotations and conjugation of Part I, as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>. A ball centered at zero is rotation
    invariant. Haar invariance proves rotation covariance
    of<nbsp><math|B<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>, and its reality formula
    proves conjugation covariance. These maps
    fix<nbsp><math|A<around|(|0|)>>: before averaging the decoder
    returns<nbsp><math|R<rsub|q><around|(|h<rsup|-1>|)>*A>, and after
    multiplication by<nbsp><math|R<rsub|q><around|(|h|)>> it
    returns<nbsp><math|A>. Define<nbsp><math|T<rsub|q>=P<rsub|q>*I<rsub|q+1>:F<rsub|q+1>\<to\>F<rsub|q>>.
    The recursion gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-contact-split>P<rsub|q>*I<rsub|q>=1,<space|2em>T<rsub|q>*j<rsub|q>=1,<space|2em>T<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q+1>=P<rsub|q>,<space|2em>P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>=<big|sum><rsub|d\<leqslant\>q>\<jmath\><rsub|d,q>*P<rsub|=d><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the third identity, apply<nbsp><math|T<rsub|q>> to the recursion and
    cancel<nbsp><math|T<rsub|q>*B<rsub|q+1><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>; the fourth
    follows by iteration. Thus the degree-<math|q> stratum is<nbsp><math|ker
    T<rsub|q-1>> for<nbsp><math|q\<gtr\>0>, and<nbsp><math|F<rsub|0>>
    for<nbsp><math|q=0>. Each is invariant under conjugation and rotations.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1> gives real bases; choose the unit
    in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|0>>. Covariance passes through the recursion, with
    translations following from <math|\<tau\><rsub|-<around|(|o+u|)>>*\<tau\><rsub|u>=\<tau\><rsub|-o>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.2> uses only the single
    length<nbsp><math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>> below.
  </proof>

  We give the finite subtraction formula before proving its convergence.
  Fix<nbsp><math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>>,
  put<nbsp><math|d<rsub|i>=deg A<rsub|i>>,
  <math|W=<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>>, and choose

  <\equation*>
    B<rsub|\<ast\>>=min <around|(|1,\<delta\><rsub|W><rsup|<math-up|pres>>|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|\<ast\>>=B<rsub|\<ast\>>/4<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Expand each input in the contact stratum bases
  using<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-split>, retaining its original degree
  tag<nbsp><math|d<rsub|i>> in every subtraction order.
  For<nbsp><math|h\<subset\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>,
  <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<geqslant\>2>, let<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|h>> be
  its collision diagonal, <math|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>> the orthogonal
  component normal to<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|h>>,
  and<nbsp><math|<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>=<around|\||h|\|><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>h>x<rsub|i>>.
  Thus<nbsp><math|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>> has
  entries<nbsp><math|x<rsub|i>-<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>> on<nbsp><math|h> and
  zero elsewhere. Write

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|h>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>h>d<rsub|i>,<space|2em>q<rsub|h>=4*<around|(|<around|\||h|\|>-1|)>,<space|2em>L<rsub|h>=max
    <around|(|0,d<rsub|h>+1-q<rsub|h>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Choose a smooth radial cutoff<nbsp><math|\<chi\>> equal to one on the unit
  ball and zero outside the ball of radius two. The normal Taylor operator is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-normal-Taylor><around|(|<math-ss|T><rsub|h,k><rsup|\<perp\>>f|)><around|(|x|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>/s<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|l=0><rsup|k><frac|1|l!>*D<rsup|l>*f*<around|(|x-x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*<around|[|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  It subtracts only normal variables, leaving the centroid and all exterior
  variables as parameters.

  An expression<nbsp><math|F\<otimes\>U> means the field
  expression<nbsp><math|F<around|(|x|)>> multiplied
  by<nbsp><math|<around|(|U*f|)><around|(|x|)>> when tested
  against<nbsp><math|f>. Products compose the test operators. On a monomial
  write<nbsp><math|F=F<rsub|h>*F<rsub|h<rsup|c>>> for its factors in and
  outside<nbsp><math|h>. Let<nbsp><math|<math-ss|R><rsub|h,w>> replace the
  subword of<nbsp><math|F<around|(|x|)>> on<nbsp><math|h> by the
  field<nbsp><math|P<rsub|=w><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>|)>*F<rsub|h><around|(|x|)>>
  at<nbsp><math|<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>>, leaving its test operator
  unchanged. Define recursively

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-C\><rsub|<around|{|i|}>><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>|<cell|=A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>\<otimes\>1,>|<cell|\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<pi\><text|
    partition of >h>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>\<geqslant\>2>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|b\<in\>\<pi\>>\<cal-C\><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|ct>>,<eq-number><label|eq:J-contact-recursion>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-C\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|w\<less\>L<rsub|h>><math-ss|R><rsub|h,w>\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>><space|0.17em><math-ss|T><rsub|h,L<rsub|h>-1-w><rsup|\<perp\>>,>|<cell|\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|full>>>|<cell|=\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>+\<cal-C\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||h|\|>\<geqslant\>2|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align>

  Singleton and empty full expressions are
  respectively<nbsp><math|A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>\<otimes\>1> and the
  unit. In a nested term, the parent Taylor operator acts first on the test
  and the child operators act afterwards. Operators on disjoint slot sets
  commute. The recursion terminates because every child has fewer slots;
  expanding it gives forests of disjoint or nested collision subsets.

  Before summing shells we need estimates for complete signed expressions
  after child subtractions. Normalize <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> so that all
  separation heights lie in<nbsp><math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, and fix a finite
  set<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>> of position paths. A finite separated sum of
  cost<nbsp><math|c> at height<nbsp><math|s> has terms

  <\equation*>
    a<around|(|T|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>A<rsub|\<iota\>><around|(|x<rsub|\<iota\>><around|(|T|)>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||a|\|>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|c-s*<big|sum><rsub|\<iota\>>deg
    A<rsub|\<iota\>>|)>*T>,<space|2em><around|\||x<rsub|\<iota\>>-x<rsub|\<iota\><rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-s*T-o<around|(|T|)>>*<space|1em><around|(|\<iota\>\<ne\>\<iota\><rprime|'>|)>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|x<rsub|\<iota\>>\<in\>\<cal-P\>>. This definition permits
  different terms to use overlapping subsets of<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>>.

  Write <math|\<cal-U\>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|s><around|(|c|)>> if it has a
  finite height-one representation of some finite cost and satisfies the
  following approximation property. For every <math|L\<gtr\>0>, there is a
  finite separated sum<nbsp><math|g> of cost<nbsp><math|c> at
  height<nbsp><math|s>, using<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>>, such that for every
  <math|d\<in\><R>> and every exterior finite separated sum<nbsp><math|f> of
  cost<nbsp><math|d> at height<nbsp><math|1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-layer-precision><mid|\|>S<rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>*f|)><mid|\|>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|-L+d|)>*T>.
  </equation>

  Every exterior path must be at distance at least
  <math|e<rsup|-s*T-o<around|(|T|)>>> from every path
  in<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>>. The bound permits any varying
  character<nbsp><math|\<zeta\><around|(|T|)>> and the mixed state. Thus
  exterior internal separations are measured at height one, while its
  distance from<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>> is measured at height<nbsp><math|s>.
  Scalar factors of rate<nbsp><math|b> change the cost to<nbsp><math|c+b>.

  Suppose the paths in<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>> lie within
  <math|e<rsup|-s*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> and
  within<nbsp><math|2*s<rsub|\<ast\>>> of a
  center<nbsp><math|o\<in\>\<cal-P\>>. For contact projections assume
  <math|2*s<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|w><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>. Write
  <math|I<rsub|q><rsup|o>*A=A<around|(|o|)>> for insertion at this center.
  These fixed-distance requirements ensure that the contact probes are clear;
  the exponential distances determine the rates.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Signed layer transfer><label|lem:J-signed-layer> For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<cal-U\>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|s><around|(|c|)>>, every clear
    contact degree<nbsp><math|w> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|=w><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*\<cal-U\>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|c-w*s|)>*T>,<space|2em>\<cal-U\>-I<rsub|w><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>w><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*\<cal-U\>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|u>*<around*|(|c-<around|(|w+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Separated products add their costs; finite signed sums take their
    maximum. Coarsening from<nbsp><math|s> to<nbsp><math|u> without
    subtracting retains cost<nbsp><math|c>.
  </lemma>

  The proof orders the approximation precisions before applying the
  projections.

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-layer-start>For the fields of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, use
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated>, including its uniformity on
    compact label sets, the telescope<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-telescope>, and the
    exact contact identities<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-split>.

    First check products, which explains the strength of the approximation
    property. Let<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>,\<cal-V\>> have costs<nbsp><math|c,d>
    and a separation at height<nbsp><math|s>. Suppose a height-one
    representation of<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\>> has cost<nbsp><math|b>. Choose an
    approximant<nbsp><math|g> to<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> of
    precision<nbsp><math|L+b>. Its own height-one representation has some
    finite cost<nbsp><math|b<rprime|'>>. Next choose an
    approximant<nbsp><math|g<rprime|'>> to<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\>> of
    precision<nbsp><math|L+b<rprime|'>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-U\>*\<cal-V\>-g*g*\<prime\>=<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>*\<cal-V\>+g*<around|(|\<cal-V\>-g*\<prime\>|)>
    </equation*>

    has precision<nbsp><math|L> against every permitted exterior sum. The
    product<nbsp><math|g*g*\<prime\>> has cost<nbsp><math|c+d>, since its
    word positions are separated at height<nbsp><math|s> and its scalar
    coefficient rates add. Iterate in a fixed order for more factors.
    Addition and subtraction follow by taking the maximum cost and making
    every approximation as accurate as required.

    For<nbsp><math|u\<less\>s>, expand a finite separated approximant
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> at<nbsp><math|o> to an auxiliary
    degree<nbsp><math|H>. Applied to each term, <eqref|eq:J-raw-rates> gives
    output fields<nbsp><math|A<rsub|d><around|(|o|)>> whose coefficient rates
    are at most<nbsp><math|c-d*s>, and an error at exterior
    height<nbsp><math|u> of rate at most <math|c-<around|(|H+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>>.
    Increasing<nbsp><math|H> gives any specified precision. The original
    approximation error permits these farther exterior tests. This gives an
    arbitrarily accurate single-point sum<nbsp><math|g> at<nbsp><math|o>, of
    cost at most<nbsp><math|c> at height<nbsp><math|u>. If <math|u=s>, retain
    the original approximant. This proves coarsening.

    For <math|s\<gtr\>0>, approximate with exterior height zero. A contact
    probe of total degree<nbsp><math|p> costs at most<nbsp><math|p> at height
    one; raise the precision by<nbsp><math|p>. Compactness
    of<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>> makes this uniform over rotated and
    conjugated probes, so averaging preserves arbitrary precision. On a
    degree-<math|d> field the degree-<math|w> contact row is zero
    for<nbsp><math|d\<less\>w> and is a fixed linear map
    for<nbsp><math|d\<geqslant\>w>. Its rate is at most
    <math|c-d*s\<leqslant\>c-w*s>, proving the row bound. At<nbsp><math|s=0>
    test directly against the fixed clear probes: their positions have height
    zero and add no exponent. The same conclusion follows.

    For the residual, use <math|u\<less\>s> and the single-point
    sum<nbsp><math|g> just constructed. Every field of
    degree<nbsp><math|d\<leqslant\>w> cancels exactly under
    <math|1-I<rsub|w><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>w><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>.
    For<nbsp><math|d\<geqslant\>w+1> its coefficient rate at the outgoing
    height is<nbsp><math|c-d*s>. Viewed at height<nbsp><math|u>, its cost is
    at most <math|c-d*<around|(|s-u|)>\<leqslant\>c-<around|(|w+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>>.
    The lower fields produced by its contact projection obey the same
    inequality, since their degrees are at most<nbsp><math|d>. Thus
    <math|g-I<rsub|w><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>w><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*g> has
    the required cost. The exact error is
    <math|<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>-I<rsub|w><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>w><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>>.
    The first term has arbitrary precision by choice of<nbsp><math|H>; the
    finite probes defining the second cost only finitely many additional
    exponents. Choose the precision to pay for them and for the requested
    exterior test. This proves the residual's approximation property. Its
    finite height-one representation follows from the initial one and the
    finite probe formulas. At<nbsp><math|u=s> the row bound makes the
    projected finite sum have cost<nbsp><math|c> at height<nbsp><math|s>, so
    subtraction retains that cost directly.<label|proof:J-layer-end>
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Contact extension><label|lem:J2.2> For every
    state<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>>, arity<nbsp><math|n> and field
    list<nbsp><math|\<b-A\>>, the normal subtractions<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-recursion>
    with the contact projections, bases and
    length<nbsp><math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>> fixed above define a complex linear
    continuous functional <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>>>
    on<nbsp><math|C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4*n>|)>>. For
    every<nbsp><math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J2.2-start>Use the original fields of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, the contact projections of
    Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D36>\U<reference|def:D38>, and the
    lattice-derived estimates of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated>.
    We prove absolute integrability of the complete subtracted expression on
    every compact set, with a finite test seminorm.

    At a group<nbsp><math|h>, reorganize the full expression as

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-contact-arms><around*|(|1-<big|sum><rsub|w\<less\>L<rsub|h>><math-ss|R><rsub|h,w>|)>*\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>*f+<big|sum><rsub|w\<less\>L<rsub|h>><math-ss|R><rsub|h,w>\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>*<around|(|1-<math-ss|T><rsub|h,L<rsub|h>-1-w><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*f<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    These are identities of field expressions with test operators; individual
    unsubtracted summands need not be integrable. Every nonzero Taylor term
    lies within<nbsp><math|2*s<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|\<ast\>>> of
    its diagonal, so all its field projections have their required contact
    clearance. If<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>=0>, no projection is subtracted and
    <math|q<rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>1> already gives integrability.
    For<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>\<gtr\>0>, <eqref|eq:J-contact-split> removes
    every degree below<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>> from the first arm. We apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> to the punctured expression,
    including the child subtractions. Here is the induction invariant: after
    fixing the child arms and their test operators, the field expression
    belongs to<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|s><around|(|c|)>> at its outgoing
    height; a projected arm of degree<nbsp><math|w> has coefficient
    rate<nbsp><math|c-w*s>, and a residual arm through<nbsp><math|w> has cost
    <math|c-<around|(|w+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>> at a parent
    height<nbsp><math|u>. Singleton fields start this invariant. Products
    over separated partition blocks preserve it by the product clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer>; its projection and residual
    clauses give the next arms, and its coarsening clause handles equal
    heights. Keep every child Taylor operator attached to its arm throughout
    these operations. Each derivative expansion has finitely many scalar test
    factors of finite rate. The scalar-factor and product clauses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> include these in the
    approximation cost and retain the invariant for each differentiated arm.

    Rescale the patch so all scales are at most one. Fix its finite collision
    tree, write <math|\<rho\><rsub|b>> for its scales and
    <math|R=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|h|)>>>, and set

    <\equation*>
      T=log <around|(|1/\<rho\><rsub|min>|)>,<space|1em>s<rsub|b>=log
      <around|(|1/\<rho\><rsub|b>|)>/T,<space|1em>u=log
      <around|(|1/R|)>/T,<space|1em>\<rho\><rsub|min>=min<rsub|b>
      \<rho\><rsub|b><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    After a subsequence these heights converge
    in<nbsp><math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, with <math|s<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>u>.
    Retain the child size factor <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|h>=<big|prod><rsub|b<text|
    proper below >h><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|b>/\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|b|)>>|)><rsup|-d<rsub|b>>>.
    Before child gains, the incoming cost is
    <math|c=d<rsub|h>*s<rsub|h>+<big|sum><rsub|b<text| proper below
    >h>d<rsub|b>*<around|(|s<rsub|b>-s<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|b|)>>|)>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> gives a degree-<math|w> row of
    rate<nbsp><math|c-w*s<rsub|h>>, and a residual through
    degree<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>-1> of cost
    <math|c-L<rsub|h>*<around|(|s<rsub|h>-u|)>>. Thus, after
    retaining<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|h>> and the outer field costs, their
    physical size bounds are respectively

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-sector-rates>C<rsub|\<tau\>>*\<cal-H\><rsub|h>*\<rho\><rsub|h><rsup|w-d<rsub|h>>*\<rho\><rsub|min><rsup|-\<tau\>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<tau\>>*\<cal-H\><rsub|h>*\<rho\><rsub|h><rsup|-d<rsub|h>>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|h>/R|)><rsup|L<rsub|h>>*\<rho\><rsub|min><rsup|-\<tau\>>.
    </equation>

    With one unresolved scale, <math|\<rho\><rsub|min>=\<rho\><rsub|h>=\<rho\>>,
    giving the row bound <math|C<rsub|\<tau\>>*\<rho\><rsup|w-d<rsub|h>-\<tau\>>>
    and residual gain <math|<around|(|\<rho\>/R|)><rsup|L<rsub|h>>>. With
    several scales, retain the loss at<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|min>> and the
    child gains; the induction below distributes the loss between ancestors
    and descendants. If these estimates failed uniformly in a sector, choose
    a violating sequence and take the convergent height vector just
    described. The signed invariant, including equal heights, gives the rates
    with loss<nbsp><math|\<tau\>/2>, contradicting the violation with
    loss<nbsp><math|\<tau\>>. This proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-sector-rates>
    uniformly over the sector.

    Taylor's integral remainder gives, on a
    shell<nbsp><math|<around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<asymp\>\<rho\>>
    within the subtraction range,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|1-<math-ss|T><rsub|h,L<rsub|h>-1-w><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*f=O<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<rho\><rsup|L<rsub|h>-w>*max<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|h>-w><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Including normal volume, every term of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-arms>
    therefore gains<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|q<rsub|h>+L<rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>-\<tau\>>>,
    whose exponent is at least<nbsp><math|1-\<tau\>>.

    Partial collisions on a normal shell require a further induction. Induct
    on<nbsp><math|<around|\||h|\|>-<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>,
    where<nbsp><math|\<pi\>> is the currently separated partition. The case
    of singleton blocks is a separated correlation against a smooth compactly
    supported test, bounded by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F3>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-moments>. For a nonsingleton block, cut its normal
    coordinate into dyadic shells and rescale
    to<nbsp><math|1/2\<leqslant\><around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>.
    At any point of this compact annulus its equality classes form a proper
    partition. The centroids of nonempty subgroups contained in different
    classes are distinct there. There are finitely many such subgroups;
    continuity gives a neighborhood where their distances have a common
    positive lower bound. A finite smooth partition of unity on the annulus
    thus gives sectors with all subsequent subgroup centroids separated.
    Within a sector apply the induction hypothesis to this strictly finer
    partition. On overlaps the resulting expressions agree: a group crossing
    two sectors' separated blocks has a Taylor jet on a diagonal disjoint
    from the localized test support, and contributes zero. The subdivision
    therefore preserves the full expression and its ordered test operators.

    For the shell estimate, let<nbsp><math|a\<gtr\>0> be the shell radius
    divided by its parent scale. The rescaled Taylor remainder through
    order<nbsp><math|k> obeys

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-shell-Taylor><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-<math-ss|T><rsub|h,k><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|l><text|
      on the rescaled shell>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|l+k+1>><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a<rsup|k+1>,>|<cell|a\<leqslant\>1,>>|<row|<cell|a<rsup|k>,>|<cell|a\<gtr\>1.>>>>>
    </equation>

    For<nbsp><math|a\<leqslant\>1>, differentiate the integral Taylor
    remainder, including the cutoff; for<nbsp><math|a\<gtr\>1>, use the
    bounded test and the degree-<math|k> polynomial. The cutoff derivatives
    have fixed bounds at the parent scale. For a projected
    component<nbsp><math|w>, use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> to
    transfer the signed field expression and put<nbsp><math|k=L<rsub|h>-1-w>.
    Its volume and coefficient power<nbsp><math|a<rsup|q<rsub|h>+w-d<rsub|h>>>
    times the Taylor factor is at most<nbsp><math|a>
    when<nbsp><math|a\<leqslant\>1>, and at most<nbsp><math|1>
    when<nbsp><math|a\<gtr\>1>. Indeed<nbsp><math|q<rsub|h>+w-d<rsub|h>+k=0>
    whenever<nbsp><math|w\<less\>L<rsub|h>>. For the unprojected remainder
    the power is<nbsp><math|q<rsub|h>+L<rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>1>; its
    support restricts<nbsp><math|a> above by a fixed geometric constant.
    Thus, for every<nbsp><math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>, the combined
    estimate is at most<nbsp><math|C*a<rsup|\<theta\>>>.

    Choose<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> with<nbsp><math|<around|(|3<rsup|<around|\||h|\|>-<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>+1|)>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1>.
    At a finer partition with<nbsp><math|d<rprime|'>> remaining splits, the
    just-proved signed invariant remains valid for every projected and
    residual arm. Allocate loss<nbsp><math|3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>> to
    its coefficient and test estimates. The induction hypothesis is:

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|H><rsub|d<rprime|'>>|)><space|1em>>|<cell|<text|each
        projected or residual arm, on a shell>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<asymp\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>R\<leqslant\>1,<text|
        contributes at most>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|C*<around|(|\<rho\>/R|)><rsup|\<gamma\>>*R<rsup|-3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|<around|(|W+2|)>*<around|(|d<rprime|'>+1|)>>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Apply the preceding shell estimate with<nbsp><math|\<tau\>=3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>>
    and<nbsp><math|\<theta\>=\<gamma\>+\<tau\>\<leqslant\>1>. One split
    strictly decreases the remaining number of splits,
    and<nbsp><math|3<rsup|d<rprime|'>+1>*\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>2\<cdot\>3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>+\<gamma\>>
    leaves a factor<nbsp><math|a<rsup|\<gamma\>>> after the preceding
    estimate. To see that this is uniform in the parent scale<nbsp><math|R>,
    combine the child loss<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|-\<tau\>>> with its ratio
    improvement by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<rho\>/R|)><rsup|\<gamma\>+\<tau\>>*\<rho\><rsup|-\<tau\>>=\<rho\><rsup|\<gamma\>>*R<rsup|-<around|(|\<gamma\>+\<tau\>|)>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The last factor is charged to the parent's allocated loss. Finite sector
    sums change only the constant. For shells<nbsp><math|\<rho\>\<asymp\>2<rsup|-j>>,
    the bound is consequently<nbsp><math|C*2<rsup|-j*\<gamma\>>> times a
    fixed test seminorm. Summing this geometric series completes the
    induction, and gives absolute convergence also for iterated choices of
    disjoint shells.

    Finally cover a given compact set by neighborhoods of the equality
    classes at each of its points. The same separation argument gives
    finitely many patches, including patches outside the total-collision
    subtraction range. On these patches the recursion reduces to the product
    of the full expressions in the separated classes: any crossing Taylor
    term has zero jet. A smooth partition of unity and the Leibniz rule
    combine their bounds. Each descent costs at most<nbsp><math|W+2>
    derivatives by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-shell-Taylor>; there are at
    most<nbsp><math|n> descents. We obtain

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-contact-order><big|int><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|\<Pi\><rsub|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><rsup|<math-up|full>>*f|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<zeta\>><around|(|x|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*x\<leqslant\>C<rsub|K>*max<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|W+2|)>*n><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|1em><around|(|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>K|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Define <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>><around|(|f|)>> by
    this integral. Absolute convergence preserves complex
    linearity;<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-order> proves distribution
    continuity.

    For separated<nbsp><math|f>, every normal jet on a collision diagonal
    vanishes. Each nontrivial term of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-recursion> has
    a largest Taylor operator acting first; disjoint operators commute. Only
    <math|f<around|(|x|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>
    remains, proving the restriction also for empty and singleton
    lists.<label|proof:J2.2-end>
  </proof>

  The compact estimate gives the local distribution order.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local distribution order><label|lem:J2.3> For every
    fixed<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>,\<b-A\>> and
    compact<nbsp><math|K\<subset\><R><rsup|4*n>>, there
    are<nbsp><math|q<rsub|K>\<in\><N>> and<nbsp><math|C<rsub|K>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>><around|(|f|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|K>*max<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|K>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>K|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-order> and the integral triangle inequality,
    take<nbsp><math|q<rsub|K>=<around|(|2+<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>|)>*n>.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT>>
    Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D36>\U<reference|def:D38> fix the contact
    projections on the prescription of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.1> gives the bases;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.2> gives the extension, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.3> its local order.
  </render-proof>

  The conclusions of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT> assert neither
  uniqueness, positivity of the extension nor contact Ward identities.
  Estimates along general collision subspaces are not stated here and are not
  used in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>.

